window.MATERIALS = [{"id": 0, "title": "From Earth Observation Data to Verified Scientific Information", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Lecture01_Foundations.pdf", "pages": 9, "sections": [{"title": "Source transparency", "html": "<p>Primary source: Lecture 1 Introduction (2026), 78-page PDF. The available VTT recordings begin with Week 2; no Lecture 1 transcript was verified. Deeper physics and worked examples are supplementary and not attributed to the lecturer.</p>", "text": "Primary source: Lecture 1 Introduction (2026), 78-page PDF. The available VTT recordings begin with Week 2; no Lecture 1 transcript was verified. Deeper physics and worked examples are supplementary and not attributed to the lecturer."}, {"title": "01 | The intellectual purpose of this course", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 24, 39, 45–46, 56</p><h3>What the lecture actually says</h3><p>The course frames Earth observation (EO) as transforming remotely sensed geospatial data into verified information. The central requirement is to understand the physical environment, sensor and energy source, and ecological or biophysical processes. Its learning aims include advanced processing, large EO data platforms, estimation of biophysical attributes, emerging sources such as spaceborne LiDAR, and complete project design.</p><h3>Deeper explanation</h3><p>A satellite records an instrument response to electromagnetic energy; it does not directly record a habitat label, ecosystem health, biomass or disturbance cause. Every useful EO product therefore involves an inference chain: physical process → measurable signal → sensor response → calibrated data → interpretation/model → validation → decision. The chain can fail even when the map looks convincing. An apparent change in vegetation reflectance might come from illumination, water level, atmospheric conditions, species composition, phenology or genuine disturbance.</p><h3>Why this matters in the exam</h3><p>When asked to critique a workflow, start by identifying the target variable. &#x27;Map forest condition&#x27; is underspecified: is condition canopy greenness, fractional cover, biomass, mortality, recruitment or ecological function? A sensor can support some proxies but not all. A rigorous answer distinguishes observable signal from the scientific claim.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 24, 39, 45–46, 56 What the lecture actually says The course frames Earth observation (EO) as transforming remotely sensed geospatial data into verified information. The central requirement is to understand the physical environment, sensor and energy source, and ecological or biophysical processes. Its learning aims include advanced processing, large EO data platforms, estimation of biophysical attributes, emerging sources such as spaceborne LiDAR, and complete project design. Deeper explanation A satellite records an instrument response to electromagnetic energy; it does not directly record a habitat label, ecosystem health, biomass or disturbance cause. Every useful EO product therefore involves an inference chain: physical process → measurable signal → sensor response → calibrated data → interpretation/model → validation → decision. The chain can fail even when the map looks convincing. An apparent change in vegetation reflectance might come from illumination, water level, atmospheric conditions, species composition, phenology or genuine disturbance. Why this matters in the exam When asked to critique a workflow, start by identifying the target variable. 'Map forest condition' is underspecified: is condition canopy greenness, fractional cover, biomass, mortality, recruitment or ecological function? A sensor can support some proxies but not all. A rigorous answer distinguishes observable signal from the scientific claim."}, {"title": "02 | What an EO measurement physically represents", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 16; supplemental physics clearly distinguished</p><h3>Course-grounded principle</h3><p>Lecture 1 explicitly identifies the interaction of &#x27;light&#x27; with the environment as the crux of remote sensing, involving physical environmental components, the energy source and sensor, and ongoing ecological processes.</p><h3>Supplementary physics: the energy pathway</h3><p>For passive optical EO, solar irradiance travels through the atmosphere, interacts with surfaces and returns through the atmosphere to a sensor. Surface energy may be reflected, absorbed or transmitted; conservation can be represented by R + A + T = 1 for a given wavelength and geometry. In thermal EO, surfaces emit radiation related to temperature and emissivity. In active EO, the sensor transmits energy and measures the return; radar and LiDAR use different wavelengths and scattering mechanisms.</p><h3>Radiance and reflectance are not interchangeable</h3><p>Radiance L is directional radiant power per projected area per solid angle, typically W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1. Reflectance is a dimensionless ratio describing reflected relative to incident energy under specified conditions. At-sensor radiance contains surface and atmospheric contributions; calibrated surface reflectance aims to characterise the surface. Raw digital numbers (DN) are instrument-coded measurements and are not intrinsically physical units.</p><h3>Worked thought experiment</h3><p>Two identical seagrass beds observed on days with different aerosol loading can yield different at-sensor radiance even when the bottom is unchanged. A difference image of uncorrected DN might label that atmospheric difference &#x27;habitat loss&#x27;. Correcting radiometry and atmospheric effects is therefore part of defending a biological conclusion, not merely cosmetic preprocessing.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>Explain why a visually sharp image can still be scientifically unfit for a quantitative time-series analysis. Answer: spatial appearance does not guarantee calibrated, atmospherically comparable, geometrically aligned or temporally representative measurements.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 16; supplemental physics clearly distinguished Course-grounded principle Lecture 1 explicitly identifies the interaction of 'light' with the environment as the crux of remote sensing, involving physical environmental components, the energy source and sensor, and ongoing ecological processes. Supplementary physics: the energy pathway For passive optical EO, solar irradiance travels through the atmosphere, interacts with surfaces and returns through the atmosphere to a sensor. Surface energy may be reflected, absorbed or transmitted; conservation can be represented by R + A + T = 1 for a given wavelength and geometry. In thermal EO, surfaces emit radiation related to temperature and emissivity. In active EO, the sensor transmits energy and measures the return; radar and LiDAR use different wavelengths and scattering mechanisms. Radiance and reflectance are not interchangeable Radiance L is directional radiant power per projected area per solid angle, typically W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1. Reflectance is a dimensionless ratio describing reflected relative to incident energy under specified conditions. At-sensor radiance contains surface and atmospheric contributions; calibrated surface reflectance aims to characterise the surface. Raw digital numbers (DN) are instrument-coded measurements and are not intrinsically physical units. Worked thought experiment Two identical seagrass beds observed on days with different aerosol loading can yield different at-sensor radiance even when the bottom is unchanged. A difference image of uncorrected DN might label that atmospheric difference 'habitat loss'. Correcting radiometry and atmospheric effects is therefore part of defending a biological conclusion, not merely cosmetic preprocessing. Exam checkpoint Explain why a visually sharp image can still be scientifically unfit for a quantitative time-series analysis. Answer: spatial appearance does not guarantee calibrated, atmospherically comparable, geometrically aligned or temporally representative measurements."}, {"title": "03 | Spectral signatures: physics before algorithms", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 23; supplementary elaboration</p><h3>Reflectance as a function of wavelength</h3><p>Different materials interact differently with electromagnetic radiation. Green leaves absorb strongly in blue and red wavelengths because of pigments, often reflect more in green, show strong NIR scattering from leaf internal structure, and show water absorption features in SWIR. Water absorbs strongly in NIR/SWIR, while visible light can penetrate shallow clear water to varying depths. Soil spectra vary with mineralogy, moisture and roughness.</p><h3>Spectral separability is conditional</h3><p>Spectral signatures are not immutable fingerprints. Leaf water stress, canopy structure, illumination angle, shadow, atmospheric path, substrate and pixel mixing all change measured reflectance. A wet soil pixel may resemble a different class more than its dry counterpart. This is why representative training data, feature selection and physical understanding are needed.</p><h3>Indices are transformations, not diagnoses</h3><p>NDVI = (NIR - Red)/(NIR + Red) exploits vegetation spectral contrast. It is sensitive to greenness and canopy characteristics but can saturate in dense canopies, vary with soil background, and respond to atmospheric or viewing effects. A lower NDVI is not by itself proof of mortality or disease.</p><h3>Numerical example</h3><p>If NIR = 0.50 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.667. If NIR = 0.40 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.600. The change is -0.067, but the cause cannot be inferred from these two values alone. Establish acquisition comparability, baseline variability and independent ecological evidence.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>Why might a spectral classifier trained in the dry season perform poorly in the wet season? Because class distributions shift with moisture, phenology and observation conditions; the learned decision boundary may not transfer.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 23; supplementary elaboration Reflectance as a function of wavelength Different materials interact differently with electromagnetic radiation. Green leaves absorb strongly in blue and red wavelengths because of pigments, often reflect more in green, show strong NIR scattering from leaf internal structure, and show water absorption features in SWIR. Water absorbs strongly in NIR/SWIR, while visible light can penetrate shallow clear water to varying depths. Soil spectra vary with mineralogy, moisture and roughness. Spectral separability is conditional Spectral signatures are not immutable fingerprints. Leaf water stress, canopy structure, illumination angle, shadow, atmospheric path, substrate and pixel mixing all change measured reflectance. A wet soil pixel may resemble a different class more than its dry counterpart. This is why representative training data, feature selection and physical understanding are needed. Indices are transformations, not diagnoses NDVI = (NIR - Red)/(NIR + Red) exploits vegetation spectral contrast. It is sensitive to greenness and canopy characteristics but can saturate in dense canopies, vary with soil background, and respond to atmospheric or viewing effects. A lower NDVI is not by itself proof of mortality or disease. Numerical example If NIR = 0.50 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.667. If NIR = 0.40 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.600. The change is -0.067, but the cause cannot be inferred from these two values alone. Establish acquisition comparability, baseline variability and independent ecological evidence. Exam checkpoint Why might a spectral classifier trained in the dry season perform poorly in the wet season? Because class distributions shift with moisture, phenology and observation conditions; the learned decision boundary may not transfer."}, {"title": "04 | Four sensor resolutions and the trade-offs", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 20–23; supplementary definitions and comparisons</p><h3>Spatial resolution</h3><p>Spatial resolution concerns the ground detail a sensor can distinguish. Pixel size is one relevant specification, but the actual point-spread function and effective resolution also matter. A 10 m pixel averages a heterogeneous footprint; a 2 m channel or narrow canopy fringe may occupy only a fraction of that pixel. Upsampling a 10 m product to 1 m creates more cells, not new independent detail.</p><h3>Spectral resolution</h3><p>Spectral resolution concerns how finely and where wavelengths are sampled: broad multispectral bands versus many narrow hyperspectral bands. Narrow bands may isolate diagnostic absorption or red-edge features, but they also demand high signal-to-noise ratio and more careful calibration. More bands are not automatically better if they are noisy, redundant or physically irrelevant.</p><h3>Radiometric resolution</h3><p>Radiometric resolution concerns how finely a measurement is quantised, often described by bit depth. An n-bit sensor supports up to 2^n discrete codes, but usable sensitivity also depends on sensor noise, saturation and calibration. Converting an 8-bit image file to 16-bit does not recover information that was never measured.</p><h3>Temporal resolution</h3><p>Temporal resolution concerns repeat observation frequency and practical availability of usable images. A sensor&#x27;s nominal revisit does not equal cloud-free revisit. A geostationary weather sensor can observe frequently but at coarse spatial resolution, while a higher-resolution satellite may revisit less often. For rapid weather evolution, temporal frequency is crucial; for narrow seagrass patches, spatial resolution and optical water conditions dominate.</p><h3>Sensor of the week: Himawari-8</h3><p>The Lecture 1 example describes the geostationary Himawari-8 Advanced Himawari Imager with 16 channels, approximately 0.5–1 km visible/NIR and 2 km thermal pixels, full-disk observations every 10 minutes and Japan-region observations every 2.5 minutes (PDF p. 20). The example demonstrates the high temporal-frequency/coarse-spatial-detail trade-off; these are slide-era specifications, not a claim about current operational status.</p><h3>Worked selection</h3><p>A typhoon-monitoring task needs frequent broad-area observations: a geostationary meteorological sensor is defensible. Mapping individual 5 m mangrove gaps requires much finer spatial resolution. Neither sensor is universally superior; the question determines the useful resolution.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>A researcher chooses Himawari-8 to map individual 10 m coastal buildings because it has 10-minute observations. Identify the flaw: its pixel footprint is hundreds of metres to kilometres, so the buildings are subpixel and not independently resolved.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 20–23; supplementary definitions and comparisons Spatial resolution Spatial resolution concerns the ground detail a sensor can distinguish. Pixel size is one relevant specification, but the actual point-spread function and effective resolution also matter. A 10 m pixel averages a heterogeneous footprint; a 2 m channel or narrow canopy fringe may occupy only a fraction of that pixel. Upsampling a 10 m product to 1 m creates more cells, not new independent detail. Spectral resolution Spectral resolution concerns how finely and where wavelengths are sampled: broad multispectral bands versus many narrow hyperspectral bands. Narrow bands may isolate diagnostic absorption or red-edge features, but they also demand high signal-to-noise ratio and more careful calibration. More bands are not automatically better if they are noisy, redundant or physically irrelevant. Radiometric resolution Radiometric resolution concerns how finely a measurement is quantised, often described by bit depth. An n-bit sensor supports up to 2^n discrete codes, but usable sensitivity also depends on sensor noise, saturation and calibration. Converting an 8-bit image file to 16-bit does not recover information that was never measured. Temporal resolution Temporal resolution concerns repeat observation frequency and practical availability of usable images. A sensor's nominal revisit does not equal cloud-free revisit. A geostationary weather sensor can observe frequently but at coarse spatial resolution, while a higher-resolution satellite may revisit less often. For rapid weather evolution, temporal frequency is crucial; for narrow seagrass patches, spatial resolution and optical water conditions dominate. Sensor of the week: Himawari-8 The Lecture 1 example describes the geostationary Himawari-8 Advanced Himawari Imager with 16 channels, approximately 0.5–1 km visible/NIR and 2 km thermal pixels, full-disk observations every 10 minutes and Japan-region observations every 2.5 minutes (PDF p. 20). The example demonstrates the high temporal-frequency/coarse-spatial-detail trade-off; these are slide-era specifications, not a claim about current operational status. Worked selection A typhoon-monitoring task needs frequent broad-area observations: a geostationary meteorological sensor is defensible. Mapping individual 5 m mangrove gaps requires much finer spatial resolution. Neither sensor is universally superior; the question determines the useful resolution. Exam checkpoint A researcher chooses Himawari-8 to map individual 10 m coastal buildings because it has 10-minute observations. Identify the flaw: its pixel footprint is hundreds of metres to kilometres, so the buildings are subpixel and not independently resolved."}, {"title": "05 | Why combining sensors can be scientifically powerful", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 23</p><h3>Lecture example</h3><p>The lecture illustrates vegetation function and structure using complementary observations: OCO-3 solar-induced fluorescence and CO2, GEDI LiDAR, HISUI hyperspectral measurements, and ECOSTRESS thermal observations. This is a conceptual example of different sensors addressing different aspects of vegetation.</p><h3>The underlying inference</h3><p>Hyperspectral reflectance can inform biochemical and optical properties; fluorescence may indicate aspects of photosynthetic functioning; thermal emission constrains surface temperature and water-stress-related processes; LiDAR characterises vertical structure. These variables are related but not identical. A single greenness index cannot replace all of them.</p><h3>Fusion is not automatic improvement</h3><p>Fusion introduces different footprints, observation times, angular geometries, measurement physics and uncertainty. A GEDI footprint cannot be treated as a pixel-perfect label for every 10 m optical pixel without considering support and geolocation. Combining data at incompatible scales may introduce more bias than it removes.</p><h3>Design example</h3><p>To estimate mangrove biomass, field plots and appropriate allometric models establish reference biomass, LiDAR contributes structural predictors, and optical imagery helps extrapolate spatial patterns. Validate independently across biomass ranges and forest types. The resulting biomass is model-estimated, not directly measured by the optical sensor.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>Explain why matching coordinate systems is insufficient for fusing a 25 m LiDAR footprint and a 10 m multispectral grid. They have different spatial support, geolocation uncertainty and observation footprints; one sample may span multiple pixels.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 23 Lecture example The lecture illustrates vegetation function and structure using complementary observations: OCO-3 solar-induced fluorescence and CO2, GEDI LiDAR, HISUI hyperspectral measurements, and ECOSTRESS thermal observations. This is a conceptual example of different sensors addressing different aspects of vegetation. The underlying inference Hyperspectral reflectance can inform biochemical and optical properties; fluorescence may indicate aspects of photosynthetic functioning; thermal emission constrains surface temperature and water-stress-related processes; LiDAR characterises vertical structure. These variables are related but not identical. A single greenness index cannot replace all of them. Fusion is not automatic improvement Fusion introduces different footprints, observation times, angular geometries, measurement physics and uncertainty. A GEDI footprint cannot be treated as a pixel-perfect label for every 10 m optical pixel without considering support and geolocation. Combining data at incompatible scales may introduce more bias than it removes. Design example To estimate mangrove biomass, field plots and appropriate allometric models establish reference biomass, LiDAR contributes structural predictors, and optical imagery helps extrapolate spatial patterns. Validate independently across biomass ranges and forest types. The resulting biomass is model-estimated, not directly measured by the optical sensor. Exam checkpoint Explain why matching coordinate systems is insufficient for fusing a 25 m LiDAR footprint and a 10 m multispectral grid. They have different spatial support, geolocation uncertainty and observation footprints; one sample may span multiple pixels."}, {"title": "06 | The image processing sequence is a reasoning framework", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 45–46</p><h3>The lecturer&#x27;s sequence</h3><p>Statement of the problem → image data acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → extraction of thematic or quantitative information → error assessment → multi-date analyses (change and trend detection) → GIS integration → solve the problem. External/contextual data enter the process.</p><h3>Why the order matters</h3><p>The scientific question determines required variables and spatial/temporal support. Data acquisition must therefore be justified before processing. Preprocessing makes measurements geometrically and radiometrically defensible. Enhancement may aid interpretation but must not corrupt quantitative inputs. Information extraction turns data into labels or estimates; error assessment determines whether the outputs are reliable enough for use.</p><h3>Important nuance</h3><p>The lecture sequence is a conceptual workflow, not a claim that every algorithm must be run in exactly that order. In practice, validation design should be planned before modelling; multi-date comparability is enforced during acquisition and preprocessing; GIS contextual data can be used throughout. This nuance is supplementary interpretation.</p><h3>A plausible flawed workflow</h3><p>A team acquires a single cloudy image, applies a strong contrast stretch, classifies the stretched values, compares with an uncorrected image from another year, validates on training sites and publishes exact habitat-loss area. Multiple failures compound: missing data, inconsistent physical units, changed pixel distributions, leakage and uncertainty omission.</p><h3>Better workflow</h3><p>Define habitat and change; choose appropriate temporal/spectral/spatial data; apply consistent quality and physical corrections; develop representative training and independent reference samples; extract information; estimate errors and area uncertainty; analyse temporal differences with comparable support; report limitations.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>When a workflow says &#x27;classification accuracy = 98%&#x27; but validation samples were also used for training, what fails? The independence requirement: the estimate is optimistic and cannot establish out-of-sample performance.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 45–46 The lecturer's sequence Statement of the problem → image data acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → extraction of thematic or quantitative information → error assessment → multi-date analyses (change and trend detection) → GIS integration → solve the problem. External/contextual data enter the process. Why the order matters The scientific question determines required variables and spatial/temporal support. Data acquisition must therefore be justified before processing. Preprocessing makes measurements geometrically and radiometrically defensible. Enhancement may aid interpretation but must not corrupt quantitative inputs. Information extraction turns data into labels or estimates; error assessment determines whether the outputs are reliable enough for use. Important nuance The lecture sequence is a conceptual workflow, not a claim that every algorithm must be run in exactly that order. In practice, validation design should be planned before modelling; multi-date comparability is enforced during acquisition and preprocessing; GIS contextual data can be used throughout. This nuance is supplementary interpretation. A plausible flawed workflow A team acquires a single cloudy image, applies a strong contrast stretch, classifies the stretched values, compares with an uncorrected image from another year, validates on training sites and publishes exact habitat-loss area. Multiple failures compound: missing data, inconsistent physical units, changed pixel distributions, leakage and uncertainty omission. Better workflow Define habitat and change; choose appropriate temporal/spectral/spatial data; apply consistent quality and physical corrections; develop representative training and independent reference samples; extract information; estimate errors and area uncertainty; analyse temporal differences with comparable support; report limitations. Exam checkpoint When a workflow says 'classification accuracy = 98%' but validation samples were also used for training, what fails? The independence requirement: the estimate is optimistic and cannot establish out-of-sample performance."}, {"title": "07 | Data cubes, ENVI and reproducibility", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 27–33</p><h3>The practical structure</h3><p>Weeks 1–3 introduce Python and Digital Earth Australia/Sandbox and multitemporal data; Weeks 4–7 use ENVI for image types, preprocessing, supervised/unsupervised classification and accuracy assessment; later practicals return to online tools and project support.</p><h3>What changes with the computing platform</h3><p>A graphical desktop workflow and a Python/data-cube workflow can implement similar scientific methods. The interface does not determine validity. Both require documented product versions, coordinate reference systems, processing parameters, masks, band choices, dates, model settings and reference-data provenance.</p><h3>A minimal processing log</h3><p>Record: problem and target; sensor/product identifier; scene/date; processing level; CRS/resampling; cloud/water masks; atmospheric and geometric corrections; features; training and test sample provenance; model hyperparameters; accuracy metrics; code/software version; exported file names and checksums when useful.</p><h3>The lecturer&#x27;s instruction</h3><p>The slide explicitly tells students to keep a log of processing operations and file names, and to back up data and reports (PDF p. 29). The principle is not merely administrative: without a processing history, apparent trends may be irreproducible or scientifically uninterpretable.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>If a student says &#x27;I used machine learning in the cloud, therefore the map is reliable,&#x27; explain why this is invalid. Computational scale says nothing about training quality, physical comparability, independent validation or ecological interpretation.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 27–33 The practical structure Weeks 1–3 introduce Python and Digital Earth Australia/Sandbox and multitemporal data; Weeks 4–7 use ENVI for image types, preprocessing, supervised/unsupervised classification and accuracy assessment; later practicals return to online tools and project support. What changes with the computing platform A graphical desktop workflow and a Python/data-cube workflow can implement similar scientific methods. The interface does not determine validity. Both require documented product versions, coordinate reference systems, processing parameters, masks, band choices, dates, model settings and reference-data provenance. A minimal processing log Record: problem and target; sensor/product identifier; scene/date; processing level; CRS/resampling; cloud/water masks; atmospheric and geometric corrections; features; training and test sample provenance; model hyperparameters; accuracy metrics; code/software version; exported file names and checksums when useful. The lecturer's instruction The slide explicitly tells students to keep a log of processing operations and file names, and to back up data and reports (PDF p. 29). The principle is not merely administrative: without a processing history, apparent trends may be irreproducible or scientifically uninterpretable. Exam checkpoint If a student says 'I used machine learning in the cloud, therefore the map is reliable,' explain why this is invalid. Computational scale says nothing about training quality, physical comparability, independent validation or ecological interpretation."}, {"title": "08 | Designing a research project from the question backward", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 38–44</p><h3>Course project planning</h3><p>The lecture lists objectives/deliverables, resource constraints, processing phases and budget, schedule, checks against user specifications, responsibilities, implementation, review and reporting. It asks directly: &#x27;What question are you trying to answer?&#x27;</p><h3>Translate a vague topic into an estimand</h3><p>Vague: &#x27;monitor mangrove health&#x27;. Better: &#x27;Estimate the annual area of persistent canopy loss in mangrove stands between 2018 and 2026 at 10–30 m mapping support, with uncertainty.&#x27; This clarifies target, spatial extent, time, observable signal and validation requirement.</p><h3>Match method to objective</h3><p>If the target is a categorical habitat map, use a suitable classification design and class-specific accuracy. If the target is continuous canopy height, use a quantitative model and independent residual metrics. If the target is early warning, use a baseline and test detection lead time against independently dated disturbances.</p><h3>Scope and feasibility</h3><p>A project constrained by limited fieldwork should not claim that a large number of inaccessible habitats were validated. Prioritise representative accessible sites, independent high-resolution references where appropriate, clearly state sampling limitations, and restrict claims to the observable/validated domain.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>Compare these goals: (A) map vegetation greenness; (B) estimate canopy mortality. They are not equivalent. A greenness index may support A, whereas B needs a defensible link to mortality and independent ecological evidence.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 38–44 Course project planning The lecture lists objectives/deliverables, resource constraints, processing phases and budget, schedule, checks against user specifications, responsibilities, implementation, review and reporting. It asks directly: 'What question are you trying to answer?' Translate a vague topic into an estimand Vague: 'monitor mangrove health'. Better: 'Estimate the annual area of persistent canopy loss in mangrove stands between 2018 and 2026 at 10–30 m mapping support, with uncertainty.' This clarifies target, spatial extent, time, observable signal and validation requirement. Match method to objective If the target is a categorical habitat map, use a suitable classification design and class-specific accuracy. If the target is continuous canopy height, use a quantitative model and independent residual metrics. If the target is early warning, use a baseline and test detection lead time against independently dated disturbances. Scope and feasibility A project constrained by limited fieldwork should not claim that a large number of inaccessible habitats were validated. Prioritise representative accessible sites, independent high-resolution references where appropriate, clearly state sampling limitations, and restrict claims to the observable/validated domain. Exam checkpoint Compare these goals: (A) map vegetation greenness; (B) estimate canopy mortality. They are not equivalent. A greenness index may support A, whereas B needs a defensible link to mortality and independent ecological evidence."}, {"title": "09 | The examination is about defending choices", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 56</p><h3>Lecture statement</h3><p>The lecture states that the exam builds on lectures, practicals and project knowledge. Unlike the semester project, the exam tests underlying understanding under time constraints: recognising sound and flawed practice and justifying choices.</p><h3>A four-part critique pattern</h3><p>1. Identify the problematic decision. 2. Explain the physical/statistical reason it fails. 3. State the likely effect on the scientific result. 4. Propose a correction that fits the study&#x27;s constraints. An answer that merely names &#x27;atmospheric correction&#x27; or &#x27;Random Forest&#x27; earns less explanatory value than one that shows why the choice matters.</p><h3>Worked answer</h3><p>Prompt: &#x27;A team uses raw DN from two satellites to estimate 10-year NDVI change.&#x27; Critique: DN are sensor-specific and not directly comparable; differences in calibration, spectral response, atmosphere and acquisition conditions can appear as vegetation change. Use harmonised calibrated reflectance, comparable bands, geometry and seasons, plus independent validation and uncertainty. If cross-sensor harmonisation is not feasible, restrict the inference.</p><h3>Trade-off reasoning</h3><p>No method is universally best. A high-frequency coarse sensor is useful for regional dynamics but poor for narrow features; a very-high-resolution image may capture features but have limited historical archive or high cost. A defensible answer identifies the decisive constraint and explains why alternatives are less suitable.</p><h3>Exam checkpoint</h3><p>Write a 120-word critique of a proposed &#x27;single-date, no-reference, global habitat map&#x27; using the four-part structure. Then design a revised workflow in 150 words.</p>", "text": "Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 56 Lecture statement The lecture states that the exam builds on lectures, practicals and project knowledge. Unlike the semester project, the exam tests underlying understanding under time constraints: recognising sound and flawed practice and justifying choices. A four-part critique pattern 1. Identify the problematic decision. 2. Explain the physical/statistical reason it fails. 3. State the likely effect on the scientific result. 4. Propose a correction that fits the study's constraints. An answer that merely names 'atmospheric correction' or 'Random Forest' earns less explanatory value than one that shows why the choice matters. Worked answer Prompt: 'A team uses raw DN from two satellites to estimate 10-year NDVI change.' Critique: DN are sensor-specific and not directly comparable; differences in calibration, spectral response, atmosphere and acquisition conditions can appear as vegetation change. Use harmonised calibrated reflectance, comparable bands, geometry and seasons, plus independent validation and uncertainty. If cross-sensor harmonisation is not feasible, restrict the inference. Trade-off reasoning No method is universally best. A high-frequency coarse sensor is useful for regional dynamics but poor for narrow features; a very-high-resolution image may capture features but have limited historical archive or high cost. A defensible answer identifies the decisive constraint and explains why alternatives are less suitable. Exam checkpoint Write a 120-word critique of a proposed 'single-date, no-reference, global habitat map' using the four-part structure. Then design a revised workflow in 150 words."}, {"title": "10 | Full worked integrative exercise", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original teaching example, based on Lecture 1 workflow</p><h3>Scenario</h3><p>An agency wants to detect mangrove decline along a 2–8 m-wide estuarine fringe from 2019–2026. It proposes a single 30 m image per year, uncorrected NDVI differencing, a fixed threshold, and reports exact hectares lost without reference data.</p><h3>Issue 1: spatial support</h3><p>The fringe is narrower than the 30 m pixel, so many observations mix mangrove, mud, water and adjacent land. Tide-driven changes in water fraction can alter NDVI. Choose finer imagery where available or redefine the reporting scale and minimum detectable patch; acknowledge mixed pixels.</p><h3>Issue 2: temporal sampling</h3><p>One image per year may capture season, cloud shadow, tide or temporary stress. Use multiple quality-screened dates and comparable seasonal/tidal windows; consider time-series models and persistent anomalies.</p><h3>Issue 3: measurement comparability</h3><p>Use consistent surface-reflectance products and geometric alignment, control cloud/shadow and consider sensor spectral response and view geometry. Raw or inconsistently corrected data can create false changes.</p><h3>Issue 4: ecological inference</h3><p>An NDVI decline is a spectral observation, not a diagnosis of dieback cause or mortality. Combine independent field/very-high-resolution observations and, where useful, complementary SAR or structural information.</p><h3>Issue 5: validation and uncertainty</h3><p>Design independent stratified reference samples for changed and unchanged areas, estimate omission/commission and area uncertainty. Avoid reporting exact area loss without accounting for classification and reference errors.</p><h3>Defended solution</h3><p>A credible workflow would define persistent canopy loss, build a comparable multi-date optical time series at adequate spatial resolution, use independent ecological evidence, apply a justified change-detection model and report uncertainty. This design is more expensive or data-intensive, but it supports a much stronger claim.</p>", "text": "Original teaching example, based on Lecture 1 workflow Scenario An agency wants to detect mangrove decline along a 2–8 m-wide estuarine fringe from 2019–2026. It proposes a single 30 m image per year, uncorrected NDVI differencing, a fixed threshold, and reports exact hectares lost without reference data. Issue 1: spatial support The fringe is narrower than the 30 m pixel, so many observations mix mangrove, mud, water and adjacent land. Tide-driven changes in water fraction can alter NDVI. Choose finer imagery where available or redefine the reporting scale and minimum detectable patch; acknowledge mixed pixels. Issue 2: temporal sampling One image per year may capture season, cloud shadow, tide or temporary stress. Use multiple quality-screened dates and comparable seasonal/tidal windows; consider time-series models and persistent anomalies. Issue 3: measurement comparability Use consistent surface-reflectance products and geometric alignment, control cloud/shadow and consider sensor spectral response and view geometry. Raw or inconsistently corrected data can create false changes. Issue 4: ecological inference An NDVI decline is a spectral observation, not a diagnosis of dieback cause or mortality. Combine independent field/very-high-resolution observations and, where useful, complementary SAR or structural information. Issue 5: validation and uncertainty Design independent stratified reference samples for changed and unchanged areas, estimate omission/commission and area uncertainty. Avoid reporting exact area loss without accounting for classification and reference errors. Defended solution A credible workflow would define persistent canopy loss, build a comparable multi-date optical time series at adequate spatial resolution, use independent ecological evidence, apply a justified change-detection model and report uncertainty. This design is more expensive or data-intensive, but it supports a much stronger claim."}, {"title": "11 | Self-test: answer before revealing", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original practice questions</p><h3>Q1</h3><p>Why is a high-resolution image not necessarily a high-quality scientific measurement? Consider atmosphere, calibration, geometry and validation.</p><h3>Q2</h3><p>Explain the difference between radiance, reflectance and DN; identify which is suitable for cross-date vegetation index comparison and why.</p><h3>Q3</h3><p>Why does Himawari-8 illustrate a trade-off rather than a universal sensor advantage?</p><h3>Q4</h3><p>Name two reasons NDVI could decline without a decrease in mangrove biomass.</p><h3>Q5</h3><p>What additional measurements would you use to link optical greenness to vegetation function and structure?</p><h3>Q6</h3><p>Why should reference-data design be planned before running a classifier?</p><h3>Q7</h3><p>Explain why a project that maps 10 m pixels cannot automatically validate them with GPS points recorded at 10 m positional uncertainty.</p><h3>Q8</h3><p>Give a complete critique of a workflow that uses training data to validate its own classifier.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer guide</summary><p>Q1: effective detail is only one quality dimension. Q2: DN coded measurement, radiance directional energy, reflectance normalised surface response; harmonised surface reflectance is generally preferable. Q3: 10-minute frequency vs kilometre-scale pixels. Q4: tide/mixed pixels, seasonal stress, atmospheric or illumination differences. Q5: LiDAR, fluorescence, thermal, field observations. Q6: avoid biased or non-independent validation. Q7: position error comparable to pixel width may create incorrect labels. Q8: leakage inflates accuracy; separate independent test data and report class-specific uncertainty.</p></details>", "text": "Original practice questions Q1 Why is a high-resolution image not necessarily a high-quality scientific measurement? Consider atmosphere, calibration, geometry and validation. Q2 Explain the difference between radiance, reflectance and DN; identify which is suitable for cross-date vegetation index comparison and why. Q3 Why does Himawari-8 illustrate a trade-off rather than a universal sensor advantage? Q4 Name two reasons NDVI could decline without a decrease in mangrove biomass. Q5 What additional measurements would you use to link optical greenness to vegetation function and structure? Q6 Why should reference-data design be planned before running a classifier? Q7 Explain why a project that maps 10 m pixels cannot automatically validate them with GPS points recorded at 10 m positional uncertainty. Q8 Give a complete critique of a workflow that uses training data to validate its own classifier. Reveal answer guideQ1: effective detail is only one quality dimension. Q2: DN coded measurement, radiance directional energy, reflectance normalised surface response; harmonised surface reflectance is generally preferable. Q3: 10-minute frequency vs kilometre-scale pixels. Q4: tide/mixed pixels, seasonal stress, atmospheric or illumination differences. Q5: LiDAR, fluorescence, thermal, field observations. Q6: avoid biased or non-independent validation. Q7: position error comparable to pixel width may create incorrect labels. Q8: leakage inflates accuracy; separate independent test data and report class-specific uncertainty."}, {"title": "12 | Sources, transcript coverage and boundaries", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Source audit</p><h3>Direct primary source</h3><p>geom3001_7001_L1_introduction_2026.pdf (78 PDF pages). Citations throughout this chapter refer to physical PDF page numbers; the printed slide number can differ.</p><h3>Transcript check</h3><p>The available VTT files begin on 5 August 2026, and the earliest recording explicitly opens as &#x27;Week 2, Lecture 2&#x27;. Therefore no corresponding Lecture 1 spoken transcript was verified in the available uploads. This Lecture 1 chapter is slide-grounded; the deeper physics and worked exercises are explicitly marked as supplementary. Future chapters will integrate matched VTT transcripts where available.</p><h3>What has not been claimed</h3><p>The supplementary examples are not attributed to the lecturer, and the chapter does not imply that every concept or equation appeared verbatim in Lecture 1. The purpose is to preserve the lecture&#x27;s structure while giving the scientific foundation needed for the exam.</p>", "text": "Source audit Direct primary source geom3001_7001_L1_introduction_2026.pdf (78 PDF pages). Citations throughout this chapter refer to physical PDF page numbers; the printed slide number can differ. Transcript check The available VTT files begin on 5 August 2026, and the earliest recording explicitly opens as 'Week 2, Lecture 2'. Therefore no corresponding Lecture 1 spoken transcript was verified in the available uploads. This Lecture 1 chapter is slide-grounded; the deeper physics and worked exercises are explicitly marked as supplementary. Future chapters will integrate matched VTT transcripts where available. What has not been claimed The supplementary examples are not attributed to the lecturer, and the chapter does not imply that every concept or equation appeared verbatim in Lecture 1. The purpose is to preserve the lecture's structure while giving the scientific foundation needed for the exam."}], "text": ["Source transparency Primary source: Lecture 1 Introduction (2026), 78-page PDF. The available VTT recordings begin with Week 2; no Lecture 1 transcript was verified. Deeper physics and worked examples are supplementary and not attributed to the lecturer.", "01 | The intellectual purpose of this course Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 24, 39, 45–46, 56 What the lecture actually says The course frames Earth observation (EO) as transforming remotely sensed geospatial data into verified information. The central requirement is to understand the physical environment, sensor and energy source, and ecological or biophysical processes. Its learning aims include advanced processing, large EO data platforms, estimation of biophysical attributes, emerging sources such as spaceborne LiDAR, and complete project design. Deeper explanation A satellite records an instrument response to electromagnetic energy; it does not directly record a habitat label, ecosystem health, biomass or disturbance cause. Every useful EO product therefore involves an inference chain: physical process → measurable signal → sensor response → calibrated data → interpretation/model → validation → decision. The chain can fail even when the map looks convincing. An apparent change in vegetation reflectance might come from illumination, water level, atmospheric conditions, species composition, phenology or genuine disturbance. Why this matters in the exam When asked to critique a workflow, start by identifying the target variable. 'Map forest condition' is underspecified: is condition canopy greenness, fractional cover, biomass, mortality, recruitment or ecological function? A sensor can support some proxies but not all. A rigorous answer distinguishes observable signal from the scientific claim.", "02 | What an EO measurement physically represents Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 16; supplemental physics clearly distinguished Course-grounded principle Lecture 1 explicitly identifies the interaction of 'light' with the environment as the crux of remote sensing, involving physical environmental components, the energy source and sensor, and ongoing ecological processes. Supplementary physics: the energy pathway For passive optical EO, solar irradiance travels through the atmosphere, interacts with surfaces and returns through the atmosphere to a sensor. Surface energy may be reflected, absorbed or transmitted; conservation can be represented by R + A + T = 1 for a given wavelength and geometry. In thermal EO, surfaces emit radiation related to temperature and emissivity. In active EO, the sensor transmits energy and measures the return; radar and LiDAR use different wavelengths and scattering mechanisms. Radiance and reflectance are not interchangeable Radiance L is directional radiant power per projected area per solid angle, typically W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1. Reflectance is a dimensionless ratio describing reflected relative to incident energy under specified conditions. At-sensor radiance contains surface and atmospheric contributions; calibrated surface reflectance aims to characterise the surface. Raw digital numbers (DN) are instrument-coded measurements and are not intrinsically physical units. Worked thought experiment Two identical seagrass beds observed on days with different aerosol loading can yield different at-sensor radiance even when the bottom is unchanged. A difference image of uncorrected DN might label that atmospheric difference 'habitat loss'. Correcting radiometry and atmospheric effects is therefore part of defending a biological conclusion, not merely cosmetic preprocessing. Exam checkpoint Explain why a visually sharp image can still be scientifically unfit for a quantitative time-series analysis. Answer: spatial appearance does not guarantee calibrated, atmospherically comparable, geometrically aligned or temporally representative measurements.", "03 | Spectral signatures: physics before algorithms Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 16, 23; supplementary elaboration Reflectance as a function of wavelength Different materials interact differently with electromagnetic radiation. Green leaves absorb strongly in blue and red wavelengths because of pigments, often reflect more in green, show strong NIR scattering from leaf internal structure, and show water absorption features in SWIR. Water absorbs strongly in NIR/SWIR, while visible light can penetrate shallow clear water to varying depths. Soil spectra vary with mineralogy, moisture and roughness. Spectral separability is conditional Spectral signatures are not immutable fingerprints. Leaf water stress, canopy structure, illumination angle, shadow, atmospheric path, substrate and pixel mixing all change measured reflectance. A wet soil pixel may resemble a different class more than its dry counterpart. This is why representative training data, feature selection and physical understanding are needed. Indices are transformations, not diagnoses NDVI = (NIR - Red)/(NIR + Red) exploits vegetation spectral contrast. It is sensitive to greenness and canopy characteristics but can saturate in dense canopies, vary with soil background, and respond to atmospheric or viewing effects. A lower NDVI is not by itself proof of mortality or disease. Numerical example If NIR = 0.50 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.667. If NIR = 0.40 and Red = 0.10, NDVI = 0.600. The change is -0.067, but the cause cannot be inferred from these two values alone. Establish acquisition comparability, baseline variability and independent ecological evidence. Exam checkpoint Why might a spectral classifier trained in the dry season perform poorly in the wet season? Because class distributions shift with moisture, phenology and observation conditions; the learned decision boundary may not transfer.", "04 | Four sensor resolutions and the trade-offs Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 20–23; supplementary definitions and comparisons Spatial resolution Spatial resolution concerns the ground detail a sensor can distinguish. Pixel size is one relevant specification, but the actual point-spread function and effective resolution also matter. A 10 m pixel averages a heterogeneous footprint; a 2 m channel or narrow canopy fringe may occupy only a fraction of that pixel. Upsampling a 10 m product to 1 m creates more cells, not new independent detail. Spectral resolution Spectral resolution concerns how finely and where wavelengths are sampled: broad multispectral bands versus many narrow hyperspectral bands. Narrow bands may isolate diagnostic absorption or red-edge features, but they also demand high signal-to-noise ratio and more careful calibration. More bands are not automatically better if they are noisy, redundant or physically irrelevant. Radiometric resolution Radiometric resolution concerns how finely a measurement is quantised, often described by bit depth. An n-bit sensor supports up to 2^n discrete codes, but usable sensitivity also depends on sensor noise, saturation and calibration. Converting an 8-bit image file to 16-bit does not recover information that was never measured. Temporal resolution Temporal resolution concerns repeat observation frequency and practical availability of usable images. A sensor's nominal revisit does not equal cloud-free revisit. A geostationary weather sensor can observe frequently but at coarse spatial resolution, while a higher-resolution satellite may revisit less often. For rapid weather evolution, temporal frequency is crucial; for narrow seagrass patches, spatial resolution and optical water conditions dominate. Sensor of the week: Himawari-8 The Lecture 1 example describes the geostationary Himawari-8 Advanced Himawari Imager with 16 channels, approximately 0.5–1 km visible/NIR and 2 km thermal pixels, full-disk observations every 10 minutes and Japan-region observations every 2.5 minutes (PDF p. 20). The example demonstrates the high temporal-frequency/coarse-spatial-detail trade-off; these are slide-era specifications, not a claim about current operational status. Worked selection A typhoon-monitoring task needs frequent broad-area observations: a geostationary meteorological sensor is defensible. Mapping individual 5 m mangrove gaps requires much finer spatial resolution. Neither sensor is universally superior; the question determines the useful resolution. Exam checkpoint A researcher chooses Himawari-8 to map individual 10 m coastal buildings because it has 10-minute observations. Identify the flaw: its pixel footprint is hundreds of metres to kilometres, so the buildings are subpixel and not independently resolved.", "05 | Why combining sensors can be scientifically powerful Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 23 Lecture example The lecture illustrates vegetation function and structure using complementary observations: OCO-3 solar-induced fluorescence and CO2, GEDI LiDAR, HISUI hyperspectral measurements, and ECOSTRESS thermal observations. This is a conceptual example of different sensors addressing different aspects of vegetation. The underlying inference Hyperspectral reflectance can inform biochemical and optical properties; fluorescence may indicate aspects of photosynthetic functioning; thermal emission constrains surface temperature and water-stress-related processes; LiDAR characterises vertical structure. These variables are related but not identical. A single greenness index cannot replace all of them. Fusion is not automatic improvement Fusion introduces different footprints, observation times, angular geometries, measurement physics and uncertainty. A GEDI footprint cannot be treated as a pixel-perfect label for every 10 m optical pixel without considering support and geolocation. Combining data at incompatible scales may introduce more bias than it removes. Design example To estimate mangrove biomass, field plots and appropriate allometric models establish reference biomass, LiDAR contributes structural predictors, and optical imagery helps extrapolate spatial patterns. Validate independently across biomass ranges and forest types. The resulting biomass is model-estimated, not directly measured by the optical sensor. Exam checkpoint Explain why matching coordinate systems is insufficient for fusing a 25 m LiDAR footprint and a 10 m multispectral grid. They have different spatial support, geolocation uncertainty and observation footprints; one sample may span multiple pixels.", "06 | The image processing sequence is a reasoning framework Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 45–46 The lecturer's sequence Statement of the problem → image data acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → extraction of thematic or quantitative information → error assessment → multi-date analyses (change and trend detection) → GIS integration → solve the problem. External/contextual data enter the process. Why the order matters The scientific question determines required variables and spatial/temporal support. Data acquisition must therefore be justified before processing. Preprocessing makes measurements geometrically and radiometrically defensible. Enhancement may aid interpretation but must not corrupt quantitative inputs. Information extraction turns data into labels or estimates; error assessment determines whether the outputs are reliable enough for use. Important nuance The lecture sequence is a conceptual workflow, not a claim that every algorithm must be run in exactly that order. In practice, validation design should be planned before modelling; multi-date comparability is enforced during acquisition and preprocessing; GIS contextual data can be used throughout. This nuance is supplementary interpretation. A plausible flawed workflow A team acquires a single cloudy image, applies a strong contrast stretch, classifies the stretched values, compares with an uncorrected image from another year, validates on training sites and publishes exact habitat-loss area. Multiple failures compound: missing data, inconsistent physical units, changed pixel distributions, leakage and uncertainty omission. Better workflow Define habitat and change; choose appropriate temporal/spectral/spatial data; apply consistent quality and physical corrections; develop representative training and independent reference samples; extract information; estimate errors and area uncertainty; analyse temporal differences with comparable support; report limitations. Exam checkpoint When a workflow says 'classification accuracy = 98%' but validation samples were also used for training, what fails? The independence requirement: the estimate is optimistic and cannot establish out-of-sample performance.", "07 | Data cubes, ENVI and reproducibility Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 27–33 The practical structure Weeks 1–3 introduce Python and Digital Earth Australia/Sandbox and multitemporal data; Weeks 4–7 use ENVI for image types, preprocessing, supervised/unsupervised classification and accuracy assessment; later practicals return to online tools and project support. What changes with the computing platform A graphical desktop workflow and a Python/data-cube workflow can implement similar scientific methods. The interface does not determine validity. Both require documented product versions, coordinate reference systems, processing parameters, masks, band choices, dates, model settings and reference-data provenance. A minimal processing log Record: problem and target; sensor/product identifier; scene/date; processing level; CRS/resampling; cloud/water masks; atmospheric and geometric corrections; features; training and test sample provenance; model hyperparameters; accuracy metrics; code/software version; exported file names and checksums when useful. The lecturer's instruction The slide explicitly tells students to keep a log of processing operations and file names, and to back up data and reports (PDF p. 29). The principle is not merely administrative: without a processing history, apparent trends may be irreproducible or scientifically uninterpretable. Exam checkpoint If a student says 'I used machine learning in the cloud, therefore the map is reliable,' explain why this is invalid. Computational scale says nothing about training quality, physical comparability, independent validation or ecological interpretation.", "08 | Designing a research project from the question backward Source: Lecture 1, PDF pp. 38–44 Course project planning The lecture lists objectives/deliverables, resource constraints, processing phases and budget, schedule, checks against user specifications, responsibilities, implementation, review and reporting. It asks directly: 'What question are you trying to answer?' Translate a vague topic into an estimand Vague: 'monitor mangrove health'. Better: 'Estimate the annual area of persistent canopy loss in mangrove stands between 2018 and 2026 at 10–30 m mapping support, with uncertainty.' This clarifies target, spatial extent, time, observable signal and validation requirement. Match method to objective If the target is a categorical habitat map, use a suitable classification design and class-specific accuracy. If the target is continuous canopy height, use a quantitative model and independent residual metrics. If the target is early warning, use a baseline and test detection lead time against independently dated disturbances. Scope and feasibility A project constrained by limited fieldwork should not claim that a large number of inaccessible habitats were validated. Prioritise representative accessible sites, independent high-resolution references where appropriate, clearly state sampling limitations, and restrict claims to the observable/validated domain. Exam checkpoint Compare these goals: (A) map vegetation greenness; (B) estimate canopy mortality. They are not equivalent. A greenness index may support A, whereas B needs a defensible link to mortality and independent ecological evidence.", "09 | The examination is about defending choices Source: Lecture 1, PDF p. 56 Lecture statement The lecture states that the exam builds on lectures, practicals and project knowledge. Unlike the semester project, the exam tests underlying understanding under time constraints: recognising sound and flawed practice and justifying choices. A four-part critique pattern 1. Identify the problematic decision. 2. Explain the physical/statistical reason it fails. 3. State the likely effect on the scientific result. 4. Propose a correction that fits the study's constraints. An answer that merely names 'atmospheric correction' or 'Random Forest' earns less explanatory value than one that shows why the choice matters. Worked answer Prompt: 'A team uses raw DN from two satellites to estimate 10-year NDVI change.' Critique: DN are sensor-specific and not directly comparable; differences in calibration, spectral response, atmosphere and acquisition conditions can appear as vegetation change. Use harmonised calibrated reflectance, comparable bands, geometry and seasons, plus independent validation and uncertainty. If cross-sensor harmonisation is not feasible, restrict the inference. Trade-off reasoning No method is universally best. A high-frequency coarse sensor is useful for regional dynamics but poor for narrow features; a very-high-resolution image may capture features but have limited historical archive or high cost. A defensible answer identifies the decisive constraint and explains why alternatives are less suitable. Exam checkpoint Write a 120-word critique of a proposed 'single-date, no-reference, global habitat map' using the four-part structure. Then design a revised workflow in 150 words.", "10 | Full worked integrative exercise Original teaching example, based on Lecture 1 workflow Scenario An agency wants to detect mangrove decline along a 2–8 m-wide estuarine fringe from 2019–2026. It proposes a single 30 m image per year, uncorrected NDVI differencing, a fixed threshold, and reports exact hectares lost without reference data. Issue 1: spatial support The fringe is narrower than the 30 m pixel, so many observations mix mangrove, mud, water and adjacent land. Tide-driven changes in water fraction can alter NDVI. Choose finer imagery where available or redefine the reporting scale and minimum detectable patch; acknowledge mixed pixels. Issue 2: temporal sampling One image per year may capture season, cloud shadow, tide or temporary stress. Use multiple quality-screened dates and comparable seasonal/tidal windows; consider time-series models and persistent anomalies. Issue 3: measurement comparability Use consistent surface-reflectance products and geometric alignment, control cloud/shadow and consider sensor spectral response and view geometry. Raw or inconsistently corrected data can create false changes. Issue 4: ecological inference An NDVI decline is a spectral observation, not a diagnosis of dieback cause or mortality. Combine independent field/very-high-resolution observations and, where useful, complementary SAR or structural information. Issue 5: validation and uncertainty Design independent stratified reference samples for changed and unchanged areas, estimate omission/commission and area uncertainty. Avoid reporting exact area loss without accounting for classification and reference errors. Defended solution A credible workflow would define persistent canopy loss, build a comparable multi-date optical time series at adequate spatial resolution, use independent ecological evidence, apply a justified change-detection model and report uncertainty. This design is more expensive or data-intensive, but it supports a much stronger claim.", "11 | Self-test: answer before revealing Original practice questions Q1 Why is a high-resolution image not necessarily a high-quality scientific measurement? Consider atmosphere, calibration, geometry and validation. Q2 Explain the difference between radiance, reflectance and DN; identify which is suitable for cross-date vegetation index comparison and why. Q3 Why does Himawari-8 illustrate a trade-off rather than a universal sensor advantage? Q4 Name two reasons NDVI could decline without a decrease in mangrove biomass. Q5 What additional measurements would you use to link optical greenness to vegetation function and structure? Q6 Why should reference-data design be planned before running a classifier? Q7 Explain why a project that maps 10 m pixels cannot automatically validate them with GPS points recorded at 10 m positional uncertainty. Q8 Give a complete critique of a workflow that uses training data to validate its own classifier. Reveal answer guideQ1: effective detail is only one quality dimension. Q2: DN coded measurement, radiance directional energy, reflectance normalised surface response; harmonised surface reflectance is generally preferable. Q3: 10-minute frequency vs kilometre-scale pixels. Q4: tide/mixed pixels, seasonal stress, atmospheric or illumination differences. Q5: LiDAR, fluorescence, thermal, field observations. Q6: avoid biased or non-independent validation. Q7: position error comparable to pixel width may create incorrect labels. Q8: leakage inflates accuracy; separate independent test data and report class-specific uncertainty.", "12 | Sources, transcript coverage and boundaries Source audit Direct primary source geom3001_7001_L1_introduction_2026.pdf (78 PDF pages). Citations throughout this chapter refer to physical PDF page numbers; the printed slide number can differ. Transcript check The available VTT files begin on 5 August 2026, and the earliest recording explicitly opens as 'Week 2, Lecture 2'. Therefore no corresponding Lecture 1 spoken transcript was verified in the available uploads. This Lecture 1 chapter is slide-grounded; the deeper physics and worked exercises are explicitly marked as supplementary. Future chapters will integrate matched VTT transcripts where available. What has not been claimed The supplementary examples are not attributed to the lecturer, and the chapter does not imply that every concept or equation appeared verbatim in Lecture 1. The purpose is to preserve the lecture's structure while giving the scientific foundation needed for the exam."]}, {"id": 1, "title": "Digital Earth, Cloud EO, Foundation Models & Multi-temporal Analysis", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Lecture02_DigitalEarth_DEA.pdf", "pages": 9, "sections": [{"title": "Source audit", "html": "<p>Primary: Lecture 2 PDF (37 physical pages); 5 August 2026 VTT with Professor Stuart Phinn and Dr Jenna Guffogg; DEA Practical 2 materials. Slide page references refer to physical PDF pages. Supplementary explanations are not presented as verbatim lecture content.</p>", "text": "Primary: Lecture 2 PDF (37 physical pages); 5 August 2026 VTT with Professor Stuart Phinn and Dr Jenna Guffogg; DEA Practical 2 materials. Slide page references refer to physical PDF pages. Supplementary explanations are not presented as verbatim lecture content."}, {"title": "01. What changed in Earth observation—and what did not?", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 2–8; transcript 00:30–00:40</p><p>The lecture&#x27;s objective is to explain foundational shifts in EO data storage, processing and delivery. It contrasts earlier image acquisition and processing with newer, increasingly integrated, cloud-based systems. The shift is architectural: instead of repeatedly downloading isolated scenes and performing every correction locally, researchers can query standardised archives, process where the data live, and publish outputs or services.</p><p>Professor Stuart Phinn emphasises that the fundamental science has not disappeared. You still need to know how measured pixel values become estimated biophysical quantities or inferred land-cover categories. An automated interface can conceal this chain, but cannot remove its assumptions. The scientific validity of a cloud-generated map still depends on calibration, class definition, representativeness and independent validation.</p><p>Exam implication: distinguish computational convenience from measurement quality. A workflow is not sound simply because it is fast, cloud-native, AI-enabled or global. The scientific question, spatial/temporal support, corrected measurements and reference evidence remain decisive.</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 2–8; transcript 00:30–00:40 The lecture's objective is to explain foundational shifts in EO data storage, processing and delivery. It contrasts earlier image acquisition and processing with newer, increasingly integrated, cloud-based systems. The shift is architectural: instead of repeatedly downloading isolated scenes and performing every correction locally, researchers can query standardised archives, process where the data live, and publish outputs or services. Professor Stuart Phinn emphasises that the fundamental science has not disappeared. You still need to know how measured pixel values become estimated biophysical quantities or inferred land-cover categories. An automated interface can conceal this chain, but cannot remove its assumptions. The scientific validity of a cloud-generated map still depends on calibration, class definition, representativeness and independent validation. Exam implication: distinguish computational convenience from measurement quality. A workflow is not sound simply because it is fast, cloud-native, AI-enabled or global. The scientific question, spatial/temporal support, corrected measurements and reference evidence remain decisive."}, {"title": "02. Digital Earth versus digital twin", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 2–10; transcript around 00:45–00:49</p><p>The lecture links Digital Earth concepts to integrated, continuously accessible geospatial information, and discusses digital twins as systems that bring multiple data types together for a particular place or process. A satellite image mosaic alone is not automatically a functioning digital twin.</p><p>Supplementary conceptual distinction: a digital Earth platform typically organises observations and derived products across geographic space and time. A digital twin usually represents a particular real-world system and may integrate observations, process models and scenario exploration. Usage varies between organisations, so avoid rigid claims that every platform satisfies a single universal definition.</p><p>To evaluate a proposed coastal digital twin, ask: Which variables are represented? What is their spatial and temporal resolution? How are observations updated? Are physical models coupled to the observations? What is the validation strategy? Does it support forecasting or only visualisation?</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 2–10; transcript around 00:45–00:49 The lecture links Digital Earth concepts to integrated, continuously accessible geospatial information, and discusses digital twins as systems that bring multiple data types together for a particular place or process. A satellite image mosaic alone is not automatically a functioning digital twin. Supplementary conceptual distinction: a digital Earth platform typically organises observations and derived products across geographic space and time. A digital twin usually represents a particular real-world system and may integrate observations, process models and scenario exploration. Usage varies between organisations, so avoid rigid claims that every platform satisfies a single universal definition. To evaluate a proposed coastal digital twin, ask: Which variables are represented? What is their spatial and temporal resolution? How are observations updated? Are physical models coupled to the observations? What is the validation strategy? Does it support forecasting or only visualisation?"}, {"title": "03. The move from isolated files to integrated EO systems", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 6–8, 12–18; transcript 00:35–00:45</p><p>The lecturer distinguishes storage options: personal disk, personal cloud space, public EO archives and private archives. These options involve trade-offs in cost, transfer, control, reproducibility and computing capacity. The lecture explicitly cautions against assuming all analysis must move to the cloud.</p><p>An archive is principally a collection of data assets; a portal adds discovery or access interfaces; an analytics platform may run processing close to the stored data. The lecture contrasts fixed-product online services with programmable platforms such as Google Earth Engine and Digital Earth Australia.</p><p>Worked scale example (supplementary): 1,000 scenes at 1.5 GB each require about 1.5 TB to transfer before any derived products are created. A cloud workflow can process subsets near the archive and download only results. However, service quotas, proprietary APIs, network dependencies and hidden processing choices can limit reproducibility.</p><p>Critical evaluation: a local workflow can be preferable for restricted data, custom algorithms or offline use. A cloud workflow is advantageous for continental archives and repeatable time-series queries. Neither option eliminates the need to document processing history.</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 6–8, 12–18; transcript 00:35–00:45 The lecturer distinguishes storage options: personal disk, personal cloud space, public EO archives and private archives. These options involve trade-offs in cost, transfer, control, reproducibility and computing capacity. The lecture explicitly cautions against assuming all analysis must move to the cloud. An archive is principally a collection of data assets; a portal adds discovery or access interfaces; an analytics platform may run processing close to the stored data. The lecture contrasts fixed-product online services with programmable platforms such as Google Earth Engine and Digital Earth Australia. Worked scale example (supplementary): 1,000 scenes at 1.5 GB each require about 1.5 TB to transfer before any derived products are created. A cloud workflow can process subsets near the archive and download only results. However, service quotas, proprietary APIs, network dependencies and hidden processing choices can limit reproducibility. Critical evaluation: a local workflow can be preferable for restricted data, custom algorithms or offline use. A cloud workflow is advantageous for continental archives and repeatable time-series queries. Neither option eliminates the need to document processing history."}, {"title": "04. FAIR, open data and scientific reproducibility", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 13; transcript around 00:51–00:53</p><p>The lecture explicitly introduces FAIR data principles and open science. FAIR means Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable. FAIR does not mean that every dataset is unrestricted or free; it concerns metadata, access mechanisms, standards and conditions that make reuse possible.</p><p>Findable: persistent identifiers and discoverable metadata. Accessible: clear protocols and access conditions. Interoperable: documented formats, coordinate systems and shared vocabularies. Reusable: provenance, licences, quality documentation and enough context to assess suitability.</p><p>Suppose a student publishes a land-cover raster but omits its CRS, date range, class definitions, training samples and product version. The file may be downloadable but is not reliably reusable. The lecturer also highlights acknowledging shared data and code; the practical notebooks demonstrate why provenance matters.</p><p>Exam checkpoint: explain why open imagery alone does not guarantee reproducible analysis. Answer: processing code, masks, parameters, versions, reference data and licences are also required.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 13; transcript around 00:51–00:53 The lecture explicitly introduces FAIR data principles and open science. FAIR means Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable. FAIR does not mean that every dataset is unrestricted or free; it concerns metadata, access mechanisms, standards and conditions that make reuse possible. Findable: persistent identifiers and discoverable metadata. Accessible: clear protocols and access conditions. Interoperable: documented formats, coordinate systems and shared vocabularies. Reusable: provenance, licences, quality documentation and enough context to assess suitability. Suppose a student publishes a land-cover raster but omits its CRS, date range, class definitions, training samples and product version. The file may be downloadable but is not reliably reusable. The lecturer also highlights acknowledging shared data and code; the practical notebooks demonstrate why provenance matters. Exam checkpoint: explain why open imagery alone does not guarantee reproducible analysis. Answer: processing code, masks, parameters, versions, reference data and licences are also required."}, {"title": "05. Data volume, scale and the spatial-temporal cube", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 13, 20–21; transcript 00:57–00:59</p><p>The lecture identifies the &#x27;big data&#x27; problem in terms of spatial resolution, area, time and variables, with corresponding challenges for storage, transfer, processing, visualisation and spatial-temporal analytics. Increasing one dimension can greatly increase total data volume.</p><p>An EO data cube organises spatially aligned and corrected observations across time. The lecture defines it as a collection of geometrically and radiometrically corrected datasets designed for spatial-temporal analyses. Think of a variable such as surface reflectance indexed by x, y, time and spectral band; the conceptual cube can be queried as a map at one date or as a time series for one pixel.</p><p>Worked size calculation (supplementary): a 1,000 × 1,000 pixel region, 100 dates, six bands, and 16-bit storage needs 1,000 × 1,000 × 100 × 6 × 2 bytes = 1.2 billion bytes (about 1.2 GB) before masks, metadata or compression. Doubling both spatial dimensions multiplies pixel count by four.</p><p>Professor Phinn explains that consistent geometric and radiometric correction makes it possible to apply the same selection or analysis over time. A cube is not automatically accurate: quality masks, sensor differences and ecological interpretation still matter.</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 13, 20–21; transcript 00:57–00:59 The lecture identifies the 'big data' problem in terms of spatial resolution, area, time and variables, with corresponding challenges for storage, transfer, processing, visualisation and spatial-temporal analytics. Increasing one dimension can greatly increase total data volume. An EO data cube organises spatially aligned and corrected observations across time. The lecture defines it as a collection of geometrically and radiometrically corrected datasets designed for spatial-temporal analyses. Think of a variable such as surface reflectance indexed by x, y, time and spectral band; the conceptual cube can be queried as a map at one date or as a time series for one pixel. Worked size calculation (supplementary): a 1,000 × 1,000 pixel region, 100 dates, six bands, and 16-bit storage needs 1,000 × 1,000 × 100 × 6 × 2 bytes = 1.2 billion bytes (about 1.2 GB) before masks, metadata or compression. Doubling both spatial dimensions multiplies pixel count by four. Professor Phinn explains that consistent geometric and radiometric correction makes it possible to apply the same selection or analysis over time. A cube is not automatically accurate: quality masks, sensor differences and ecological interpretation still matter."}, {"title": "06. ARD: why corrected inputs are the foundation", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 21, 31–32; transcript 00:57–00:58 and 01:35–01:37</p><p>Analysis Ready Data (ARD) are corrected satellite, airborne or drone data prepared so they can be integrated with other corrected observations and geospatial datasets. The lecture specifies geometric correction into standard datum/projection/coordinates and radiometric conversion from relative DN to physical radiance or reflectance. It also lists terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections where needed.</p><p>ARD is a processing level, not a universal guarantee of ecological validity. A surface-reflectance product can still contain residual atmospheric error, cloud shadow, BRDF effects or water-column effects. An ARD product designed for terrestrial analysis may be unsuitable for shallow benthic mapping without additional water-specific treatment.</p><p>Jenna Guffogg describes DEA quality information and metadata that help filter clouds, cloud shadows and positional issues. This means quality screening is part of analysis, not an optional visual clean-up.</p><p>Workflow critique: &#x27;We downloaded ARD, so no other preprocessing or validation is necessary.&#x27; Incorrect. Verify product specification, quality flags, spatial support, spectral compatibility and suitability for the environment and inference.</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 21, 31–32; transcript 00:57–00:58 and 01:35–01:37 Analysis Ready Data (ARD) are corrected satellite, airborne or drone data prepared so they can be integrated with other corrected observations and geospatial datasets. The lecture specifies geometric correction into standard datum/projection/coordinates and radiometric conversion from relative DN to physical radiance or reflectance. It also lists terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections where needed. ARD is a processing level, not a universal guarantee of ecological validity. A surface-reflectance product can still contain residual atmospheric error, cloud shadow, BRDF effects or water-column effects. An ARD product designed for terrestrial analysis may be unsuitable for shallow benthic mapping without additional water-specific treatment. Jenna Guffogg describes DEA quality information and metadata that help filter clouds, cloud shadows and positional issues. This means quality screening is part of analysis, not an optional visual clean-up. Workflow critique: 'We downloaded ARD, so no other preprocessing or validation is necessary.' Incorrect. Verify product specification, quality flags, spatial support, spectral compatibility and suitability for the environment and inference."}, {"title": "07. Interpretation Ready Data: outputs are not the same as ARD", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 25</p><p>The lecture distinguishes corrected input observations from Interpretation Ready Data: processed outputs in which pixels contain either thematic labels (such as land cover) or quantitative estimates (such as temperature or biomass).</p><p>This is a fundamental distinction for evaluation. A thematic map requires reference categories and class-specific confusion-matrix analysis. A continuous biomass map requires independent numeric measurements and residual statistics such as bias and RMSE. The processing method can be complex, but the output type determines what validation is meaningful.</p><p>An NDVI raster is an index of spectral contrast, not automatically a map of ecological health. A classified &#x27;healthy/unhealthy&#x27; product is a thematic inference requiring class definitions and independent reference labels. A biomass raster is a quantitative model estimate requiring biomass reference data and uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 25 The lecture distinguishes corrected input observations from Interpretation Ready Data: processed outputs in which pixels contain either thematic labels (such as land cover) or quantitative estimates (such as temperature or biomass). This is a fundamental distinction for evaluation. A thematic map requires reference categories and class-specific confusion-matrix analysis. A continuous biomass map requires independent numeric measurements and residual statistics such as bias and RMSE. The processing method can be complex, but the output type determines what validation is meaningful. An NDVI raster is an index of spectral contrast, not automatically a map of ecological health. A classified 'healthy/unhealthy' product is a thematic inference requiring class definitions and independent reference labels. A biomass raster is a quantitative model estimate requiring biomass reference data and uncertainty."}, {"title": "08. Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF and partial access", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 23</p><p>The lecture introduces Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) as a GeoTIFF arrangement intended to support efficient partial access over HTTP, rather than downloading the entire raster to read a small region. Internal tiling and overviews support spatial subsets and reduced-resolution display.</p><p>COG is a storage/access format, not a correction method, sensor type or analysis algorithm. Converting a poor-quality image into COG does not improve its geolocation or calibration. It changes how efficiently the file can be accessed.</p><p>Example: a continental image can be read by requesting only tiles overlapping an estuary. This can save transfer and speed visualisation, but analytical consistency still depends on pixel values, masks, resolution and provenance.</p><p>Exam trap: &#x27;The product is a COG, so it is analysis-ready.&#x27; False: file organisation and scientific processing level are independent dimensions.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 23 The lecture introduces Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) as a GeoTIFF arrangement intended to support efficient partial access over HTTP, rather than downloading the entire raster to read a small region. Internal tiling and overviews support spatial subsets and reduced-resolution display. COG is a storage/access format, not a correction method, sensor type or analysis algorithm. Converting a poor-quality image into COG does not improve its geolocation or calibration. It changes how efficiently the file can be accessed. Example: a continental image can be read by requesting only tiles overlapping an estuary. This can save transfer and speed visualisation, but analytical consistency still depends on pixel values, masks, resolution and provenance. Exam trap: 'The product is a COG, so it is analysis-ready.' False: file organisation and scientific processing level are independent dimensions."}, {"title": "09. STAC: discovering the right assets", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 25; transcript about 02:15</p><p>The lecture introduces the SpatioTemporal Asset Catalog (STAC) specification as a shared way to describe and discover geospatial assets. A catalog can expose items with spatial and temporal metadata and links to data assets. This reduces the need for custom discovery code for every provider.</p><p>Conceptual structure (supplementary): a collection describes a family of assets; items describe particular observations or products; assets point to files such as reflectance bands, masks or metadata. In a robust workflow, a STAC query filters by location, dates, product properties and quality before analysis.</p><p>Jenna Guffogg discusses the DEA STAC endpoint in the transcript. This matters because catalog search is the first stage of reproducible data acquisition, not merely a convenience.</p><p>Distinguish STAC from COG: STAC is discovery/metadata; COG is efficient raster access; ARD is the processing quality/standardisation of the observation. They can work together but solve different problems.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 25; transcript about 02:15 The lecture introduces the SpatioTemporal Asset Catalog (STAC) specification as a shared way to describe and discover geospatial assets. A catalog can expose items with spatial and temporal metadata and links to data assets. This reduces the need for custom discovery code for every provider. Conceptual structure (supplementary): a collection describes a family of assets; items describe particular observations or products; assets point to files such as reflectance bands, masks or metadata. In a robust workflow, a STAC query filters by location, dates, product properties and quality before analysis. Jenna Guffogg discusses the DEA STAC endpoint in the transcript. This matters because catalog search is the first stage of reproducible data acquisition, not merely a convenience. Distinguish STAC from COG: STAC is discovery/metadata; COG is efficient raster access; ARD is the processing quality/standardisation of the observation. They can work together but solve different problems."}, {"title": "10. Foundation models and satellite embeddings", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides pp. 26–29; transcript 00:59–01:03</p><p>The lecture surveys EO foundation models and satellite embeddings, including examples from NASA/IBM, ESA and Google. These models aim to learn transferable representations from large geospatial datasets rather than train a narrow classifier from scratch for every problem.</p><p>Professor Phinn describes embeddings as compressed representations of multi-temporal imagery and potentially other data. The idea is that a model encodes patterns into feature vectors useful for downstream tasks. An embedding is not necessarily an interpretable physical measurement; two embedding dimensions do not automatically correspond to two ecological variables.</p><p>Supplementary explanation: a foundation model is typically pre-trained on broad datasets, then adapted or used for tasks such as land-cover mapping or change detection. Transfer can be valuable when labelled data are scarce, but performance may degrade in novel ecosystems, seasons, sensors or geographies.</p><p>Critical risk: black-box outputs can appear precise without transparent calibration or validation. The lecturer explicitly raises validation as a missing issue in some AI examples. A defensible foundation-model workflow must state training domain, input data, adaptation, independent local reference and uncertainty.</p><p>Exam question: &#x27;A foundation model produces a 99% confident mangrove-loss map without field reference; should it be accepted?&#x27; No. Model confidence is not independently measured accuracy. Assess class definitions, geographic transfer, temporal consistency, reference quality and error.</p>", "text": "Slides pp. 26–29; transcript 00:59–01:03 The lecture surveys EO foundation models and satellite embeddings, including examples from NASA/IBM, ESA and Google. These models aim to learn transferable representations from large geospatial datasets rather than train a narrow classifier from scratch for every problem. Professor Phinn describes embeddings as compressed representations of multi-temporal imagery and potentially other data. The idea is that a model encodes patterns into feature vectors useful for downstream tasks. An embedding is not necessarily an interpretable physical measurement; two embedding dimensions do not automatically correspond to two ecological variables. Supplementary explanation: a foundation model is typically pre-trained on broad datasets, then adapted or used for tasks such as land-cover mapping or change detection. Transfer can be valuable when labelled data are scarce, but performance may degrade in novel ecosystems, seasons, sensors or geographies. Critical risk: black-box outputs can appear precise without transparent calibration or validation. The lecturer explicitly raises validation as a missing issue in some AI examples. A defensible foundation-model workflow must state training domain, input data, adaptation, independent local reference and uncertainty. Exam question: 'A foundation model produces a 99% confident mangrove-loss map without field reference; should it be accepted?' No. Model confidence is not independently measured accuracy. Assess class definitions, geographic transfer, temporal consistency, reference quality and error."}, {"title": "11. The lecturer's AI caution: science still matters", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Transcript 00:36–00:39, 00:50–00:51; slides pp. 9–10, 26–29</p><p>Professor Phinn discusses generative geospatial AI systems that accept natural-language prompts to produce maps and change estimates. He cautions that these may hide processing decisions and identifies validation as a crucial missing consideration in examples.</p><p>The scientifically important distinction is between generating a plausible output and establishing a defensible claim. Even a system that chooses data and models automatically must be interrogated about its sensor input, correction level, spatial scale, reference labels, evaluation design and failure modes.</p><p>Worked critique: &#x27;The AI produced a mangrove map for 2020 and 2025, so the pixel difference is verified loss.&#x27; This does not establish whether both maps share the same class definitions, sensor support, training domain, masks or errors. Require independent change reference and uncertainty estimates.</p><p>Good exam response: use AI as a candidate analysis or feature extractor, then validate it with the same scientific discipline as conventional EO workflows.</p>", "text": "Transcript 00:36–00:39, 00:50–00:51; slides pp. 9–10, 26–29 Professor Phinn discusses generative geospatial AI systems that accept natural-language prompts to produce maps and change estimates. He cautions that these may hide processing decisions and identifies validation as a crucial missing consideration in examples. The scientifically important distinction is between generating a plausible output and establishing a defensible claim. Even a system that chooses data and models automatically must be interrogated about its sensor input, correction level, spatial scale, reference labels, evaluation design and failure modes. Worked critique: 'The AI produced a mangrove map for 2020 and 2025, so the pixel difference is verified loss.' This does not establish whether both maps share the same class definitions, sensor support, training domain, masks or errors. Require independent change reference and uncertainty estimates. Good exam response: use AI as a candidate analysis or feature extractor, then validate it with the same scientific discipline as conventional EO workflows."}, {"title": "12. Multi-temporal comparability: spatial requirements", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 31</p><p>The slide requires equal Ground Resolution Element (GRE) dimensions and recommends resampling to the largest common GRE if necessary. This avoids falsely treating one image as more detailed than another. It also requires the same datum, projection and coordinates.</p><p>The slide specifies image-to-image registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel. Misregistration can create false change at edges. RMSE measures control-point fit; it should be supplemented with independent checks across the area, particularly in spatially variable terrain.</p><p>Worked example: a 10 m and 30 m image cannot be compared pixel-for-pixel without defining common support. Aggregating to 30 m loses detail but avoids pretending 10 m variation exists in the 30 m observation. Resampling 30 m to 10 m creates interpolated values, not new measured information.</p><p>Geometric view angle differences can change apparent surface reflectance and object displacement. The lecture therefore says acquisition look angles should match or be standardised.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 31 The slide requires equal Ground Resolution Element (GRE) dimensions and recommends resampling to the largest common GRE if necessary. This avoids falsely treating one image as more detailed than another. It also requires the same datum, projection and coordinates. The slide specifies image-to-image registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel. Misregistration can create false change at edges. RMSE measures control-point fit; it should be supplemented with independent checks across the area, particularly in spatially variable terrain. Worked example: a 10 m and 30 m image cannot be compared pixel-for-pixel without defining common support. Aggregating to 30 m loses detail but avoids pretending 10 m variation exists in the 30 m observation. Resampling 30 m to 10 m creates interpolated values, not new measured information. Geometric view angle differences can change apparent surface reflectance and object displacement. The lecture therefore says acquisition look angles should match or be standardised."}, {"title": "13. Multi-temporal comparability: spectral and radiometric requirements", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide p. 31</p><p>The slide requires matching spectral band centres and widths between dates, and consistent sensor quantisation and calibrated corrected radiance or reflectance. It also stresses knowing the processing history.</p><p>Two sensors both labelled &#x27;red&#x27; may measure slightly different wavelength intervals; vegetation spectra vary across wavelength, so even calibrated reflectance can differ systematically. Harmonisation may require spectral response adjustments or sensor-specific calibration.</p><p>Quantisation differences can affect small changes. The scientific concern is not simply equal bit depth; it is whether observations are calibrated, comparable and sufficiently sensitive to the expected signal. The lecture&#x27;s wording about identical quantisation is a conservative comparability requirement.</p><p>Example: a Landsat and Sentinel-2 NDVI trend may contain a step at the sensor transition. Check spectral response, band definitions, corrections, BRDF, season and cross-sensor bias before interpreting the step as disturbance.</p>", "text": "Slide p. 31 The slide requires matching spectral band centres and widths between dates, and consistent sensor quantisation and calibrated corrected radiance or reflectance. It also stresses knowing the processing history. Two sensors both labelled 'red' may measure slightly different wavelength intervals; vegetation spectra vary across wavelength, so even calibrated reflectance can differ systematically. Harmonisation may require spectral response adjustments or sensor-specific calibration. Quantisation differences can affect small changes. The scientific concern is not simply equal bit depth; it is whether observations are calibrated, comparable and sufficiently sensitive to the expected signal. The lecture's wording about identical quantisation is a conservative comparability requirement. Example: a Landsat and Sentinel-2 NDVI trend may contain a step at the sensor transition. Check spectral response, band definitions, corrections, BRDF, season and cross-sensor bias before interpreting the step as disturbance."}, {"title": "14. What the DEA guest lecture adds", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Transcript 01:30–02:24; Lecture 2 slides plus DEA practical slides</p><p>Dr Jenna Guffogg, from Geoscience Australia&#x27;s Digital Earth Australia team, explains the rationale for making long-term Australian satellite observations accessible and analysis-ready. Her presentation provides practical context for product selection, quality metadata, interfaces and the DEA Sandbox.</p><p>She emphasises that free and open access does not eliminate data-quality challenges. Clouds, cloud shadows and geometric quality need to be filtered using the available metadata and masks. She also points students toward product access and specifications, which are essential for choosing appropriate inputs.</p><p>She discusses STAC as an increasingly adopted way to organise satellite metadata and reminds students to seek information about the accuracy of the product they use. This supports a core principle: products should be judged against their documented intended use, processing and validation.</p><p>The guest lecture is not a substitute for reading product specifications. Different DEA products can have different processing histories, temporal support and quality layers; do not assume one universal recipe works for all.</p>", "text": "Transcript 01:30–02:24; Lecture 2 slides plus DEA practical slides Dr Jenna Guffogg, from Geoscience Australia's Digital Earth Australia team, explains the rationale for making long-term Australian satellite observations accessible and analysis-ready. Her presentation provides practical context for product selection, quality metadata, interfaces and the DEA Sandbox. She emphasises that free and open access does not eliminate data-quality challenges. Clouds, cloud shadows and geometric quality need to be filtered using the available metadata and masks. She also points students toward product access and specifications, which are essential for choosing appropriate inputs. She discusses STAC as an increasingly adopted way to organise satellite metadata and reminds students to seek information about the accuracy of the product they use. This supports a core principle: products should be judged against their documented intended use, processing and validation. The guest lecture is not a substitute for reading product specifications. Different DEA products can have different processing histories, temporal support and quality layers; do not assume one universal recipe works for all."}, {"title": "15. Practical 2: translating architecture into an analysis", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Practical 2 DEA introduction PDF; lecture/practical transcript 5–6 August</p><p>The course practical sequence uses the DEA Sandbox and Python notebooks. The Week 2 practical recording explicitly asks students to log into the Sandbox and revisit the Beginner&#x27;s Guide notebooks; it recommends the lecture recording because it explains the trade-offs behind the tools.</p><p>An analytically defensible Sandbox workflow has six stages: (1) define a spatial-temporal question; (2) identify an appropriate DEA product and its specification; (3) query area and dates; (4) apply quality masks and inspect coverage; (5) compute a physically interpretable feature or statistic; (6) validate, visualise and export with provenance.</p><p>Supplementary pseudocode—not a verbatim notebook: load a corrected optical product over a polygon and time interval; filter quality flags; compute NDVI from calibrated NIR and red bands; aggregate by time using a documented method; inspect observation counts; compare against independent observations.</p><p>Practical critique: an annual median NDVI image may be robust to isolated cloud-contaminated pixels but can conceal short-duration stress or bias seasons with more clear observations. The compositing statistic must match the research question.</p>", "text": "Practical 2 DEA introduction PDF; lecture/practical transcript 5–6 August The course practical sequence uses the DEA Sandbox and Python notebooks. The Week 2 practical recording explicitly asks students to log into the Sandbox and revisit the Beginner's Guide notebooks; it recommends the lecture recording because it explains the trade-offs behind the tools. An analytically defensible Sandbox workflow has six stages: (1) define a spatial-temporal question; (2) identify an appropriate DEA product and its specification; (3) query area and dates; (4) apply quality masks and inspect coverage; (5) compute a physically interpretable feature or statistic; (6) validate, visualise and export with provenance. Supplementary pseudocode—not a verbatim notebook: load a corrected optical product over a polygon and time interval; filter quality flags; compute NDVI from calibrated NIR and red bands; aggregate by time using a documented method; inspect observation counts; compare against independent observations. Practical critique: an annual median NDVI image may be robust to isolated cloud-contaminated pixels but can conceal short-duration stress or bias seasons with more clear observations. The compositing statistic must match the research question."}, {"title": "16. Worked example: annual coastal vegetation anomaly", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Supplementary worked exercise grounded in Lecture 2 principles</p><p>Question: estimate whether a mangrove fringe showed unusually low greenness in 2025 relative to 2018–2024, using DEA-style corrected observations. The target is a spectral anomaly, not a direct diagnosis of mortality.</p><p>Step 1: define a stable mangrove mask and a spatial support appropriate to fringe width. Step 2: select corrected optical data with consistent band definitions. Step 3: remove cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Step 4: restrict to comparable seasons/tides where possible. Step 5: calculate NDVI per valid observation. Step 6: compute annual or seasonal summaries and an observation-count layer.</p><p>Step 7: define a baseline mean and standard deviation for each pixel from reference years. An anomaly z = (NDVI_2025 - mean_baseline)/SD_baseline standardises departure from historical variability. Example: baseline mean 0.70, SD 0.05, 2025 NDVI 0.55 gives z = -3.0. This is an unusually low spectral observation under the baseline model, not automatically dieback.</p><p>Step 8: evaluate persistent decline using multiple observations and independent high-resolution/field evidence. Discuss assumptions: stable baseline, adequate sample size, temporal autocorrelation, sensor harmonisation, tides and masked observation gaps.</p>", "text": "Supplementary worked exercise grounded in Lecture 2 principles Question: estimate whether a mangrove fringe showed unusually low greenness in 2025 relative to 2018–2024, using DEA-style corrected observations. The target is a spectral anomaly, not a direct diagnosis of mortality. Step 1: define a stable mangrove mask and a spatial support appropriate to fringe width. Step 2: select corrected optical data with consistent band definitions. Step 3: remove cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Step 4: restrict to comparable seasons/tides where possible. Step 5: calculate NDVI per valid observation. Step 6: compute annual or seasonal summaries and an observation-count layer. Step 7: define a baseline mean and standard deviation for each pixel from reference years. An anomaly z = (NDVI_2025 - mean_baseline)/SD_baseline standardises departure from historical variability. Example: baseline mean 0.70, SD 0.05, 2025 NDVI 0.55 gives z = -3.0. This is an unusually low spectral observation under the baseline model, not automatically dieback. Step 8: evaluate persistent decline using multiple observations and independent high-resolution/field evidence. Discuss assumptions: stable baseline, adequate sample size, temporal autocorrelation, sensor harmonisation, tides and masked observation gaps."}, {"title": "17. Integrated workflow critique and model answer", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original exam-style exercise</p><p>Scenario: a student queries 2016 and 2026 imagery through STAC, downloads two COGs, assumes they are ARD, uses unmasked pixel differences to classify urban growth, then reports a 98% accurate map based on 20 training points reused for validation.</p><p>Flaw 1: STAC/COG describe catalog and access mechanisms, not calibration quality. Inspect the product processing level and specifications. Flaw 2: two dates may differ in atmosphere, season, illumination, sensor bands or geometry; harmonise and correct inputs. Flaw 3: missing cloud/shadow masks can create false change; apply and audit quality flags.</p><p>Flaw 4: raw pixel difference is not necessarily a valid urban-growth indicator; specify land-cover classes and distinguish impervious surface from dry soil or bare ground. Flaw 5: 20 reused training points are non-independent and inadequate to characterise rare transitions; design independent, spatially representative reference data and report class-specific errors.</p><p>Defended alternative: specify detectable settlement expansion and target resolution; select comparable corrected time series; mask invalid pixels; choose appropriate spectral/texture features and classifier or change algorithm; use separate training, tuning and validation data; assess transition error and report area uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Original exam-style exercise Scenario: a student queries 2016 and 2026 imagery through STAC, downloads two COGs, assumes they are ARD, uses unmasked pixel differences to classify urban growth, then reports a 98% accurate map based on 20 training points reused for validation. Flaw 1: STAC/COG describe catalog and access mechanisms, not calibration quality. Inspect the product processing level and specifications. Flaw 2: two dates may differ in atmosphere, season, illumination, sensor bands or geometry; harmonise and correct inputs. Flaw 3: missing cloud/shadow masks can create false change; apply and audit quality flags. Flaw 4: raw pixel difference is not necessarily a valid urban-growth indicator; specify land-cover classes and distinguish impervious surface from dry soil or bare ground. Flaw 5: 20 reused training points are non-independent and inadequate to characterise rare transitions; design independent, spatially representative reference data and report class-specific errors. Defended alternative: specify detectable settlement expansion and target resolution; select comparable corrected time series; mask invalid pixels; choose appropriate spectral/texture features and classifier or change algorithm; use separate training, tuning and validation data; assess transition error and report area uncertainty."}, {"title": "18. Key distinctions to memorise—and understand", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Lecture synthesis</p><p>ARD = corrected input observations prepared for analysis. Interpretation Ready Data = thematic or quantitative information products. Data cube = spatially and temporally organised corrected observations. COG = efficient partial access to raster data. STAC = discovery and metadata for geospatial assets. Sandbox = computing environment to query and analyse data. Foundation model = broadly pretrained representation/model adaptable to downstream tasks.</p><p>Digital Earth and digital twins are broader integrated systems; they may combine many data and models. FAIR is a set of data stewardship principles. None of these terms alone guarantees accuracy, fitness for purpose or scientific reproducibility.</p><p>An exam answer should connect the term to its function and limitation. For example, &#x27;A COG lets me read the spatial subset efficiently, but I must separately verify that it is corrected and appropriate for the variable being estimated.&#x27;</p>", "text": "Lecture synthesis ARD = corrected input observations prepared for analysis. Interpretation Ready Data = thematic or quantitative information products. Data cube = spatially and temporally organised corrected observations. COG = efficient partial access to raster data. STAC = discovery and metadata for geospatial assets. Sandbox = computing environment to query and analyse data. Foundation model = broadly pretrained representation/model adaptable to downstream tasks. Digital Earth and digital twins are broader integrated systems; they may combine many data and models. FAIR is a set of data stewardship principles. None of these terms alone guarantees accuracy, fitness for purpose or scientific reproducibility. An exam answer should connect the term to its function and limitation. For example, 'A COG lets me read the spatial subset efficiently, but I must separately verify that it is corrected and appropriate for the variable being estimated.'"}, {"title": "19. Self-assessment questions with answers", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original practice</p><details><summary>Q1: Why can cloud processing be more efficient without being more accurate?</summary><p>A: computation near data reduces transfer, but measurement, modelling and validation quality are separate.</p></details><details><summary>Q2: What is the difference between COG and STAC?</summary><p>A: COG is a raster access arrangement; STAC is metadata/catalog discovery.</p></details><details><summary>Q3: Why is ARD not automatically Interpretation Ready Data?</summary><p>A: ARD is corrected observation input; IRD is derived thematic or quantitative information.</p></details><details><summary>Q4: Why does a data cube require spatial and radiometric comparability?</summary><p>A: otherwise time-series differences can be artefacts of registration or calibration.</p></details><details><summary>Q5: Why might an embedding-based land-cover map fail in an estuary?</summary><p>A: domain shift, mixed pixels, tides, water-column and missing local reference labels.</p></details><details><summary>Q6: Why is RMSE &lt;0.5 pixel a meaningful target for multi-date registration?</summary><p>A: edge misalignment can create false change; the threshold limits but does not eliminate that error.</p></details><details><summary>Q7: Can you directly compare 10 m and 30 m pixel change?</summary><p>A: not without common spatial support and an explicit treatment of mixed pixels.</p></details><details><summary>Q8: What evidence is needed to interpret an NDVI anomaly as ecological disturbance?</summary><p>A: comparability, baseline variability, persistence, independent ecological reference and uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Q9: Why might a product&#x27;s QA band matter more than a polished visual composite?</summary><p>A: the mask determines which measurements are valid; hidden cloud/shadow contamination biases inference.</p></details><details><summary>Q10: Why should you read DEA product specifications?</summary><p>A: to verify band definitions, processing level, temporal coverage, QA flags, intended use and accuracy.</p></details>", "text": "Original practice Q1: Why can cloud processing be more efficient without being more accurate?A: computation near data reduces transfer, but measurement, modelling and validation quality are separate. Q2: What is the difference between COG and STAC?A: COG is a raster access arrangement; STAC is metadata/catalog discovery. Q3: Why is ARD not automatically Interpretation Ready Data?A: ARD is corrected observation input; IRD is derived thematic or quantitative information. Q4: Why does a data cube require spatial and radiometric comparability?A: otherwise time-series differences can be artefacts of registration or calibration. Q5: Why might an embedding-based land-cover map fail in an estuary?A: domain shift, mixed pixels, tides, water-column and missing local reference labels. Q6: Why is RMSE <0.5 pixel a meaningful target for multi-date registration?A: edge misalignment can create false change; the threshold limits but does not eliminate that error. Q7: Can you directly compare 10 m and 30 m pixel change?A: not without common spatial support and an explicit treatment of mixed pixels. Q8: What evidence is needed to interpret an NDVI anomaly as ecological disturbance?A: comparability, baseline variability, persistence, independent ecological reference and uncertainty. Q9: Why might a product's QA band matter more than a polished visual composite?A: the mask determines which measurements are valid; hidden cloud/shadow contamination biases inference. Q10: Why should you read DEA product specifications?A: to verify band definitions, processing level, temporal coverage, QA flags, intended use and accuracy."}, {"title": "Transcript evidence samples", "html": "<p><strong>00:50:18</strong> — Stuart Phinn: crux of the issue, which you&#x27;ll notice I did not mention at all when I talked about this example, and that was validation.</p><p><strong>01:06:53</strong> — Stuart Phinn: this was actually something I started looking at yesterday, because I had to go down a bit of a wormhole about validation. We really have an increasing number of companies</p><p><strong>00:31:58</strong> — Stuart Phinn: data cubes, and how they then go into, and form analysis-ready data, which is a term you&#x27;ve heard before, and how those are used to build, you know, digital twins.</p><p><strong>00:57:08</strong> — Stuart Phinn: you know, you need a portal that has all of that data that&#x27;s being updated, but it has to be in a format that can be used effectively. So, the concept of data cubes we talked about before, you know, this is where we essentially take an area where we&#x27;ve got</p><p><strong>00:59:55</strong> — Stuart Phinn: And what they&#x27;re also doing is, in addition to looking at satellite imagery, they&#x27;re trying to put in other forms of data to be compressed into one file, which you can use for multi-temporal analysis. So if you hear people start talking about embeddings.</p><p><strong>01:00:13</strong> — Stuart Phinn: satellite embeddings data, they&#x27;re trying to take these time series over a set period and compress it down. So, Google sort of had the lead on this with something called the Alpha Earth Foundation, trying to produce these</p><p><strong>00:47:15</strong> — Stuart Phinn: airborne systems, satellite systems, in situ water quality measurement, you know, they&#x27;ve got basically vertical profiling in the streams, all of those things. All of that&#x27;s collected and integrated into that.</p><p><strong>01:35:38</strong> — Jenna Guffogg: Having all of this data publicly accessible is great, but as has already been discussed, there&#x27;s a lot of things that can influence the quality of your data that can affect, your analysis if you&#x27;re doing stuff over time.</p><p><strong>01:08:15</strong> — Stuart Phinn: spatial registration in terms of, I guess, the level of accuracy and precision, and the geographic variability of that.</p><p><strong>02:23:16</strong> — Jenna Guffogg: Yes, always seek out information about the accuracy, of whatever product is that you&#x27;re using.</p>", "text": "00:50:18 — Stuart Phinn: crux of the issue, which you'll notice I did not mention at all when I talked about this example, and that was validation. 01:06:53 — Stuart Phinn: this was actually something I started looking at yesterday, because I had to go down a bit of a wormhole about validation. We really have an increasing number of companies 00:31:58 — Stuart Phinn: data cubes, and how they then go into, and form analysis-ready data, which is a term you've heard before, and how those are used to build, you know, digital twins. 00:57:08 — Stuart Phinn: you know, you need a portal that has all of that data that's being updated, but it has to be in a format that can be used effectively. So, the concept of data cubes we talked about before, you know, this is where we essentially take an area where we've got 00:59:55 — Stuart Phinn: And what they're also doing is, in addition to looking at satellite imagery, they're trying to put in other forms of data to be compressed into one file, which you can use for multi-temporal analysis. So if you hear people start talking about embeddings. 01:00:13 — Stuart Phinn: satellite embeddings data, they're trying to take these time series over a set period and compress it down. So, Google sort of had the lead on this with something called the Alpha Earth Foundation, trying to produce these 00:47:15 — Stuart Phinn: airborne systems, satellite systems, in situ water quality measurement, you know, they've got basically vertical profiling in the streams, all of those things. All of that's collected and integrated into that. 01:35:38 — Jenna Guffogg: Having all of this data publicly accessible is great, but as has already been discussed, there's a lot of things that can influence the quality of your data that can affect, your analysis if you're doing stuff over time. 01:08:15 — Stuart Phinn: spatial registration in terms of, I guess, the level of accuracy and precision, and the geographic variability of that. 02:23:16 — Jenna Guffogg: Yes, always seek out information about the accuracy, of whatever product is that you're using."}], "text": ["Source audit Primary: Lecture 2 PDF (37 physical pages); 5 August 2026 VTT with Professor Stuart Phinn and Dr Jenna Guffogg; DEA Practical 2 materials. Slide page references refer to physical PDF pages. Supplementary explanations are not presented as verbatim lecture content.", "01. What changed in Earth observation—and what did not? Slides pp. 2–8; transcript 00:30–00:40 The lecture's objective is to explain foundational shifts in EO data storage, processing and delivery. It contrasts earlier image acquisition and processing with newer, increasingly integrated, cloud-based systems. The shift is architectural: instead of repeatedly downloading isolated scenes and performing every correction locally, researchers can query standardised archives, process where the data live, and publish outputs or services. Professor Stuart Phinn emphasises that the fundamental science has not disappeared. You still need to know how measured pixel values become estimated biophysical quantities or inferred land-cover categories. An automated interface can conceal this chain, but cannot remove its assumptions. The scientific validity of a cloud-generated map still depends on calibration, class definition, representativeness and independent validation. Exam implication: distinguish computational convenience from measurement quality. A workflow is not sound simply because it is fast, cloud-native, AI-enabled or global. The scientific question, spatial/temporal support, corrected measurements and reference evidence remain decisive.", "02. Digital Earth versus digital twin Slides pp. 2–10; transcript around 00:45–00:49 The lecture links Digital Earth concepts to integrated, continuously accessible geospatial information, and discusses digital twins as systems that bring multiple data types together for a particular place or process. A satellite image mosaic alone is not automatically a functioning digital twin. Supplementary conceptual distinction: a digital Earth platform typically organises observations and derived products across geographic space and time. A digital twin usually represents a particular real-world system and may integrate observations, process models and scenario exploration. Usage varies between organisations, so avoid rigid claims that every platform satisfies a single universal definition. To evaluate a proposed coastal digital twin, ask: Which variables are represented? What is their spatial and temporal resolution? How are observations updated? Are physical models coupled to the observations? What is the validation strategy? Does it support forecasting or only visualisation?", "03. The move from isolated files to integrated EO systems Slides pp. 6–8, 12–18; transcript 00:35–00:45 The lecturer distinguishes storage options: personal disk, personal cloud space, public EO archives and private archives. These options involve trade-offs in cost, transfer, control, reproducibility and computing capacity. The lecture explicitly cautions against assuming all analysis must move to the cloud. An archive is principally a collection of data assets; a portal adds discovery or access interfaces; an analytics platform may run processing close to the stored data. The lecture contrasts fixed-product online services with programmable platforms such as Google Earth Engine and Digital Earth Australia. Worked scale example (supplementary): 1,000 scenes at 1.5 GB each require about 1.5 TB to transfer before any derived products are created. A cloud workflow can process subsets near the archive and download only results. However, service quotas, proprietary APIs, network dependencies and hidden processing choices can limit reproducibility. Critical evaluation: a local workflow can be preferable for restricted data, custom algorithms or offline use. A cloud workflow is advantageous for continental archives and repeatable time-series queries. Neither option eliminates the need to document processing history.", "04. FAIR, open data and scientific reproducibility Slide p. 13; transcript around 00:51–00:53 The lecture explicitly introduces FAIR data principles and open science. FAIR means Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable. FAIR does not mean that every dataset is unrestricted or free; it concerns metadata, access mechanisms, standards and conditions that make reuse possible. Findable: persistent identifiers and discoverable metadata. Accessible: clear protocols and access conditions. Interoperable: documented formats, coordinate systems and shared vocabularies. Reusable: provenance, licences, quality documentation and enough context to assess suitability. Suppose a student publishes a land-cover raster but omits its CRS, date range, class definitions, training samples and product version. The file may be downloadable but is not reliably reusable. The lecturer also highlights acknowledging shared data and code; the practical notebooks demonstrate why provenance matters. Exam checkpoint: explain why open imagery alone does not guarantee reproducible analysis. Answer: processing code, masks, parameters, versions, reference data and licences are also required.", "05. Data volume, scale and the spatial-temporal cube Slides pp. 13, 20–21; transcript 00:57–00:59 The lecture identifies the 'big data' problem in terms of spatial resolution, area, time and variables, with corresponding challenges for storage, transfer, processing, visualisation and spatial-temporal analytics. Increasing one dimension can greatly increase total data volume. An EO data cube organises spatially aligned and corrected observations across time. The lecture defines it as a collection of geometrically and radiometrically corrected datasets designed for spatial-temporal analyses. Think of a variable such as surface reflectance indexed by x, y, time and spectral band; the conceptual cube can be queried as a map at one date or as a time series for one pixel. Worked size calculation (supplementary): a 1,000 × 1,000 pixel region, 100 dates, six bands, and 16-bit storage needs 1,000 × 1,000 × 100 × 6 × 2 bytes = 1.2 billion bytes (about 1.2 GB) before masks, metadata or compression. Doubling both spatial dimensions multiplies pixel count by four. Professor Phinn explains that consistent geometric and radiometric correction makes it possible to apply the same selection or analysis over time. A cube is not automatically accurate: quality masks, sensor differences and ecological interpretation still matter.", "06. ARD: why corrected inputs are the foundation Slides pp. 21, 31–32; transcript 00:57–00:58 and 01:35–01:37 Analysis Ready Data (ARD) are corrected satellite, airborne or drone data prepared so they can be integrated with other corrected observations and geospatial datasets. The lecture specifies geometric correction into standard datum/projection/coordinates and radiometric conversion from relative DN to physical radiance or reflectance. It also lists terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections where needed. ARD is a processing level, not a universal guarantee of ecological validity. A surface-reflectance product can still contain residual atmospheric error, cloud shadow, BRDF effects or water-column effects. An ARD product designed for terrestrial analysis may be unsuitable for shallow benthic mapping without additional water-specific treatment. Jenna Guffogg describes DEA quality information and metadata that help filter clouds, cloud shadows and positional issues. This means quality screening is part of analysis, not an optional visual clean-up. Workflow critique: 'We downloaded ARD, so no other preprocessing or validation is necessary.' Incorrect. Verify product specification, quality flags, spatial support, spectral compatibility and suitability for the environment and inference.", "07. Interpretation Ready Data: outputs are not the same as ARD Slide p. 25 The lecture distinguishes corrected input observations from Interpretation Ready Data: processed outputs in which pixels contain either thematic labels (such as land cover) or quantitative estimates (such as temperature or biomass). This is a fundamental distinction for evaluation. A thematic map requires reference categories and class-specific confusion-matrix analysis. A continuous biomass map requires independent numeric measurements and residual statistics such as bias and RMSE. The processing method can be complex, but the output type determines what validation is meaningful. An NDVI raster is an index of spectral contrast, not automatically a map of ecological health. A classified 'healthy/unhealthy' product is a thematic inference requiring class definitions and independent reference labels. A biomass raster is a quantitative model estimate requiring biomass reference data and uncertainty.", "08. Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF and partial access Slide p. 23 The lecture introduces Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) as a GeoTIFF arrangement intended to support efficient partial access over HTTP, rather than downloading the entire raster to read a small region. Internal tiling and overviews support spatial subsets and reduced-resolution display. COG is a storage/access format, not a correction method, sensor type or analysis algorithm. Converting a poor-quality image into COG does not improve its geolocation or calibration. It changes how efficiently the file can be accessed. Example: a continental image can be read by requesting only tiles overlapping an estuary. This can save transfer and speed visualisation, but analytical consistency still depends on pixel values, masks, resolution and provenance. Exam trap: 'The product is a COG, so it is analysis-ready.' False: file organisation and scientific processing level are independent dimensions.", "09. STAC: discovering the right assets Slide p. 25; transcript about 02:15 The lecture introduces the SpatioTemporal Asset Catalog (STAC) specification as a shared way to describe and discover geospatial assets. A catalog can expose items with spatial and temporal metadata and links to data assets. This reduces the need for custom discovery code for every provider. Conceptual structure (supplementary): a collection describes a family of assets; items describe particular observations or products; assets point to files such as reflectance bands, masks or metadata. In a robust workflow, a STAC query filters by location, dates, product properties and quality before analysis. Jenna Guffogg discusses the DEA STAC endpoint in the transcript. This matters because catalog search is the first stage of reproducible data acquisition, not merely a convenience. Distinguish STAC from COG: STAC is discovery/metadata; COG is efficient raster access; ARD is the processing quality/standardisation of the observation. They can work together but solve different problems.", "10. Foundation models and satellite embeddings Slides pp. 26–29; transcript 00:59–01:03 The lecture surveys EO foundation models and satellite embeddings, including examples from NASA/IBM, ESA and Google. These models aim to learn transferable representations from large geospatial datasets rather than train a narrow classifier from scratch for every problem. Professor Phinn describes embeddings as compressed representations of multi-temporal imagery and potentially other data. The idea is that a model encodes patterns into feature vectors useful for downstream tasks. An embedding is not necessarily an interpretable physical measurement; two embedding dimensions do not automatically correspond to two ecological variables. Supplementary explanation: a foundation model is typically pre-trained on broad datasets, then adapted or used for tasks such as land-cover mapping or change detection. Transfer can be valuable when labelled data are scarce, but performance may degrade in novel ecosystems, seasons, sensors or geographies. Critical risk: black-box outputs can appear precise without transparent calibration or validation. The lecturer explicitly raises validation as a missing issue in some AI examples. A defensible foundation-model workflow must state training domain, input data, adaptation, independent local reference and uncertainty. Exam question: 'A foundation model produces a 99% confident mangrove-loss map without field reference; should it be accepted?' No. Model confidence is not independently measured accuracy. Assess class definitions, geographic transfer, temporal consistency, reference quality and error.", "11. The lecturer's AI caution: science still matters Transcript 00:36–00:39, 00:50–00:51; slides pp. 9–10, 26–29 Professor Phinn discusses generative geospatial AI systems that accept natural-language prompts to produce maps and change estimates. He cautions that these may hide processing decisions and identifies validation as a crucial missing consideration in examples. The scientifically important distinction is between generating a plausible output and establishing a defensible claim. Even a system that chooses data and models automatically must be interrogated about its sensor input, correction level, spatial scale, reference labels, evaluation design and failure modes. Worked critique: 'The AI produced a mangrove map for 2020 and 2025, so the pixel difference is verified loss.' This does not establish whether both maps share the same class definitions, sensor support, training domain, masks or errors. Require independent change reference and uncertainty estimates. Good exam response: use AI as a candidate analysis or feature extractor, then validate it with the same scientific discipline as conventional EO workflows.", "12. Multi-temporal comparability: spatial requirements Slide p. 31 The slide requires equal Ground Resolution Element (GRE) dimensions and recommends resampling to the largest common GRE if necessary. This avoids falsely treating one image as more detailed than another. It also requires the same datum, projection and coordinates. The slide specifies image-to-image registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel. Misregistration can create false change at edges. RMSE measures control-point fit; it should be supplemented with independent checks across the area, particularly in spatially variable terrain. Worked example: a 10 m and 30 m image cannot be compared pixel-for-pixel without defining common support. Aggregating to 30 m loses detail but avoids pretending 10 m variation exists in the 30 m observation. Resampling 30 m to 10 m creates interpolated values, not new measured information. Geometric view angle differences can change apparent surface reflectance and object displacement. The lecture therefore says acquisition look angles should match or be standardised.", "13. Multi-temporal comparability: spectral and radiometric requirements Slide p. 31 The slide requires matching spectral band centres and widths between dates, and consistent sensor quantisation and calibrated corrected radiance or reflectance. It also stresses knowing the processing history. Two sensors both labelled 'red' may measure slightly different wavelength intervals; vegetation spectra vary across wavelength, so even calibrated reflectance can differ systematically. Harmonisation may require spectral response adjustments or sensor-specific calibration. Quantisation differences can affect small changes. The scientific concern is not simply equal bit depth; it is whether observations are calibrated, comparable and sufficiently sensitive to the expected signal. The lecture's wording about identical quantisation is a conservative comparability requirement. Example: a Landsat and Sentinel-2 NDVI trend may contain a step at the sensor transition. Check spectral response, band definitions, corrections, BRDF, season and cross-sensor bias before interpreting the step as disturbance.", "14. What the DEA guest lecture adds Transcript 01:30–02:24; Lecture 2 slides plus DEA practical slides Dr Jenna Guffogg, from Geoscience Australia's Digital Earth Australia team, explains the rationale for making long-term Australian satellite observations accessible and analysis-ready. Her presentation provides practical context for product selection, quality metadata, interfaces and the DEA Sandbox. She emphasises that free and open access does not eliminate data-quality challenges. Clouds, cloud shadows and geometric quality need to be filtered using the available metadata and masks. She also points students toward product access and specifications, which are essential for choosing appropriate inputs. She discusses STAC as an increasingly adopted way to organise satellite metadata and reminds students to seek information about the accuracy of the product they use. This supports a core principle: products should be judged against their documented intended use, processing and validation. The guest lecture is not a substitute for reading product specifications. Different DEA products can have different processing histories, temporal support and quality layers; do not assume one universal recipe works for all.", "15. Practical 2: translating architecture into an analysis Practical 2 DEA introduction PDF; lecture/practical transcript 5–6 August The course practical sequence uses the DEA Sandbox and Python notebooks. The Week 2 practical recording explicitly asks students to log into the Sandbox and revisit the Beginner's Guide notebooks; it recommends the lecture recording because it explains the trade-offs behind the tools. An analytically defensible Sandbox workflow has six stages: (1) define a spatial-temporal question; (2) identify an appropriate DEA product and its specification; (3) query area and dates; (4) apply quality masks and inspect coverage; (5) compute a physically interpretable feature or statistic; (6) validate, visualise and export with provenance. Supplementary pseudocode—not a verbatim notebook: load a corrected optical product over a polygon and time interval; filter quality flags; compute NDVI from calibrated NIR and red bands; aggregate by time using a documented method; inspect observation counts; compare against independent observations. Practical critique: an annual median NDVI image may be robust to isolated cloud-contaminated pixels but can conceal short-duration stress or bias seasons with more clear observations. The compositing statistic must match the research question.", "16. Worked example: annual coastal vegetation anomaly Supplementary worked exercise grounded in Lecture 2 principles Question: estimate whether a mangrove fringe showed unusually low greenness in 2025 relative to 2018–2024, using DEA-style corrected observations. The target is a spectral anomaly, not a direct diagnosis of mortality. Step 1: define a stable mangrove mask and a spatial support appropriate to fringe width. Step 2: select corrected optical data with consistent band definitions. Step 3: remove cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Step 4: restrict to comparable seasons/tides where possible. Step 5: calculate NDVI per valid observation. Step 6: compute annual or seasonal summaries and an observation-count layer. Step 7: define a baseline mean and standard deviation for each pixel from reference years. An anomaly z = (NDVI_2025 - mean_baseline)/SD_baseline standardises departure from historical variability. Example: baseline mean 0.70, SD 0.05, 2025 NDVI 0.55 gives z = -3.0. This is an unusually low spectral observation under the baseline model, not automatically dieback. Step 8: evaluate persistent decline using multiple observations and independent high-resolution/field evidence. Discuss assumptions: stable baseline, adequate sample size, temporal autocorrelation, sensor harmonisation, tides and masked observation gaps.", "17. Integrated workflow critique and model answer Original exam-style exercise Scenario: a student queries 2016 and 2026 imagery through STAC, downloads two COGs, assumes they are ARD, uses unmasked pixel differences to classify urban growth, then reports a 98% accurate map based on 20 training points reused for validation. Flaw 1: STAC/COG describe catalog and access mechanisms, not calibration quality. Inspect the product processing level and specifications. Flaw 2: two dates may differ in atmosphere, season, illumination, sensor bands or geometry; harmonise and correct inputs. Flaw 3: missing cloud/shadow masks can create false change; apply and audit quality flags. Flaw 4: raw pixel difference is not necessarily a valid urban-growth indicator; specify land-cover classes and distinguish impervious surface from dry soil or bare ground. Flaw 5: 20 reused training points are non-independent and inadequate to characterise rare transitions; design independent, spatially representative reference data and report class-specific errors. Defended alternative: specify detectable settlement expansion and target resolution; select comparable corrected time series; mask invalid pixels; choose appropriate spectral/texture features and classifier or change algorithm; use separate training, tuning and validation data; assess transition error and report area uncertainty.", "18. Key distinctions to memorise—and understand Lecture synthesis ARD = corrected input observations prepared for analysis. Interpretation Ready Data = thematic or quantitative information products. Data cube = spatially and temporally organised corrected observations. COG = efficient partial access to raster data. STAC = discovery and metadata for geospatial assets. Sandbox = computing environment to query and analyse data. Foundation model = broadly pretrained representation/model adaptable to downstream tasks. Digital Earth and digital twins are broader integrated systems; they may combine many data and models. FAIR is a set of data stewardship principles. None of these terms alone guarantees accuracy, fitness for purpose or scientific reproducibility. An exam answer should connect the term to its function and limitation. For example, 'A COG lets me read the spatial subset efficiently, but I must separately verify that it is corrected and appropriate for the variable being estimated.'", "19. Self-assessment questions with answers Original practice Q1: Why can cloud processing be more efficient without being more accurate?A: computation near data reduces transfer, but measurement, modelling and validation quality are separate. Q2: What is the difference between COG and STAC?A: COG is a raster access arrangement; STAC is metadata/catalog discovery. Q3: Why is ARD not automatically Interpretation Ready Data?A: ARD is corrected observation input; IRD is derived thematic or quantitative information. Q4: Why does a data cube require spatial and radiometric comparability?A: otherwise time-series differences can be artefacts of registration or calibration. Q5: Why might an embedding-based land-cover map fail in an estuary?A: domain shift, mixed pixels, tides, water-column and missing local reference labels. Q6: Why is RMSE <0.5 pixel a meaningful target for multi-date registration?A: edge misalignment can create false change; the threshold limits but does not eliminate that error. Q7: Can you directly compare 10 m and 30 m pixel change?A: not without common spatial support and an explicit treatment of mixed pixels. Q8: What evidence is needed to interpret an NDVI anomaly as ecological disturbance?A: comparability, baseline variability, persistence, independent ecological reference and uncertainty. Q9: Why might a product's QA band matter more than a polished visual composite?A: the mask determines which measurements are valid; hidden cloud/shadow contamination biases inference. Q10: Why should you read DEA product specifications?A: to verify band definitions, processing level, temporal coverage, QA flags, intended use and accuracy.", "Transcript evidence samples 00:50:18 — Stuart Phinn: crux of the issue, which you'll notice I did not mention at all when I talked about this example, and that was validation. 01:06:53 — Stuart Phinn: this was actually something I started looking at yesterday, because I had to go down a bit of a wormhole about validation. We really have an increasing number of companies 00:31:58 — Stuart Phinn: data cubes, and how they then go into, and form analysis-ready data, which is a term you've heard before, and how those are used to build, you know, digital twins. 00:57:08 — Stuart Phinn: you know, you need a portal that has all of that data that's being updated, but it has to be in a format that can be used effectively. So, the concept of data cubes we talked about before, you know, this is where we essentially take an area where we've got 00:59:55 — Stuart Phinn: And what they're also doing is, in addition to looking at satellite imagery, they're trying to put in other forms of data to be compressed into one file, which you can use for multi-temporal analysis. So if you hear people start talking about embeddings. 01:00:13 — Stuart Phinn: satellite embeddings data, they're trying to take these time series over a set period and compress it down. So, Google sort of had the lead on this with something called the Alpha Earth Foundation, trying to produce these 00:47:15 — Stuart Phinn: airborne systems, satellite systems, in situ water quality measurement, you know, they've got basically vertical profiling in the streams, all of those things. All of that's collected and integrated into that. 01:35:38 — Jenna Guffogg: Having all of this data publicly accessible is great, but as has already been discussed, there's a lot of things that can influence the quality of your data that can affect, your analysis if you're doing stuff over time. 01:08:15 — Stuart Phinn: spatial registration in terms of, I guess, the level of accuracy and precision, and the geographic variability of that. 02:23:16 — Jenna Guffogg: Yes, always seek out information about the accuracy, of whatever product is that you're using."]}, {"id": 2, "title": "DEA Sandbox & Hyperspectral UAV Pasture Quality", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week03_DEA_Hyperspectral.pdf", "pages": 10, "sections": [{"title": "1. Source audit: Week 3 is not a conventional lecture", "html": "<p>Week 3 practical VTT, 12 Aug 2026, 00:09:09–00:09:30</p><h3>Verified scheduling</h3><p>The Week 4 lecture recording (19 August) explicitly says there was no lecture the preceding week because of the public holiday. The Week 3 VTT is a practical-room recording. Thus this chapter covers Week 3 practical work and the uploaded &#x27;L3_P2&#x27; hyperspectral UAV slide resource, not a fabricated Lecture 3 spoken lecture.</p><h3>Two separate sources</h3><p>The 12 August practical transcript concerns the DEA Sandbox, cloud masking and notebook exercises. The 21-page hyperspectral pasture-quality deck describes a UAV-to-Sentinel-2 workflow. The VTT does not discuss that hyperspectral deck: they should not be falsely merged as one recorded presentation.</p><h3>How to study this chapter</h3><p>Part A develops the DEA practical and its conceptual implications. Part B reconstructs the UAV hyperspectral resource. Part C integrates the two into critical workflow design, marking clearly where examples and explanations are supplementary.</p>", "text": "Week 3 practical VTT, 12 Aug 2026, 00:09:09–00:09:30 Verified scheduling The Week 4 lecture recording (19 August) explicitly says there was no lecture the preceding week because of the public holiday. The Week 3 VTT is a practical-room recording. Thus this chapter covers Week 3 practical work and the uploaded 'L3_P2' hyperspectral UAV slide resource, not a fabricated Lecture 3 spoken lecture. Two separate sources The 12 August practical transcript concerns the DEA Sandbox, cloud masking and notebook exercises. The 21-page hyperspectral pasture-quality deck describes a UAV-to-Sentinel-2 workflow. The VTT does not discuss that hyperspectral deck: they should not be falsely merged as one recorded presentation. How to study this chapter Part A develops the DEA practical and its conceptual implications. Part B reconstructs the UAV hyperspectral resource. Part C integrates the two into critical workflow design, marking clearly where examples and explanations are supplementary."}, {"title": "2. Week 3 Sandbox practical: from notebook execution to scientific reasoning", "html": "<p>Week 3 practical VTT 00:09:09–00:10:47</p><h3>Lecturer/tutor context</h3><p>The practical-room instructor says this is the last Sandbox practical before four ENVI practicals, although students will return to the Sandbox for their assessments. The class is directed to revisit Beginner&#x27;s Guide notebook functionality, including a 12-question check.</p><h3>Why the interface is secondary</h3><p>The scientific work is to formulate a query, identify an appropriate corrected data product, inspect metadata, filter invalid observations, compute a defensible measurement and document processing. Clicking &#x27;Run&#x27; on a notebook does not prove that an analysis is correct.</p><h3>Reproducibility checklist</h3><p>For each notebook record its source data/product version, area of interest, temporal window, band names, CRS, cloud and shadow flags, transformations, compositing statistic, units, valid observation counts and output format. This checklist is supplementary, developed to operationalise the practical.</p><h3>Exam application</h3><p>A student reports &#x27;the DEA notebook ran without errors, therefore the map is accurate.&#x27; Critique: software execution validates syntax/runtime, not physical calibration, suitability, independent reference or uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Week 3 practical VTT 00:09:09–00:10:47 Lecturer/tutor context The practical-room instructor says this is the last Sandbox practical before four ENVI practicals, although students will return to the Sandbox for their assessments. The class is directed to revisit Beginner's Guide notebook functionality, including a 12-question check. Why the interface is secondary The scientific work is to formulate a query, identify an appropriate corrected data product, inspect metadata, filter invalid observations, compute a defensible measurement and document processing. Clicking 'Run' on a notebook does not prove that an analysis is correct. Reproducibility checklist For each notebook record its source data/product version, area of interest, temporal window, band names, CRS, cloud and shadow flags, transformations, compositing statistic, units, valid observation counts and output format. This checklist is supplementary, developed to operationalise the practical. Exam application A student reports 'the DEA notebook ran without errors, therefore the map is accurate.' Critique: software execution validates syntax/runtime, not physical calibration, suitability, independent reference or uncertainty."}, {"title": "3. Cloud masking and the difference between a pretty composite and a valid time series", "html": "<p>Week 3 practical VTT 00:19:43–00:20:09</p><h3>Transcript evidence</h3><p>The instructor asks students what stands out in the displayed images, and the class identifies clouds. The exercise trains visual recognition of invalid observations, but quantitative analysis needs systematic quality masking.</p><h3>Cloud and shadow errors</h3><p>Bright cloud pixels and dark cloud-shadow pixels can strongly distort indices and spectral classification. Thin cloud and haze may be less obvious than thick clouds. Masks have false positives and false negatives, so inspect them rather than assuming perfect removal.</p><h3>Temporal sampling bias</h3><p>If a location has 30 valid observations in one year and only 3 in another, annual medians may differ in seasonal representativeness. Always inspect observation counts, seasonality and missing-data patterns.</p><h3>Supplementary numerical example</h3><p>Valid NDVI observations [0.62,0.66,0.64] have median 0.64. If an unmasked cloud shadow produces 0.10, a four-value median is 0.63, but the mean falls from 0.64 to 0.505. Different summaries respond differently to contamination; none replaces quality screening.</p>", "text": "Week 3 practical VTT 00:19:43–00:20:09 Transcript evidence The instructor asks students what stands out in the displayed images, and the class identifies clouds. The exercise trains visual recognition of invalid observations, but quantitative analysis needs systematic quality masking. Cloud and shadow errors Bright cloud pixels and dark cloud-shadow pixels can strongly distort indices and spectral classification. Thin cloud and haze may be less obvious than thick clouds. Masks have false positives and false negatives, so inspect them rather than assuming perfect removal. Temporal sampling bias If a location has 30 valid observations in one year and only 3 in another, annual medians may differ in seasonal representativeness. Always inspect observation counts, seasonality and missing-data patterns. Supplementary numerical example Valid NDVI observations [0.62,0.66,0.64] have median 0.64. If an unmasked cloud shadow produces 0.10, a four-value median is 0.63, but the mean falls from 0.64 to 0.505. Different summaries respond differently to contamination; none replaces quality screening."}, {"title": "4. From repeated observations to meaningful time-series analysis", "html": "<p>Week 3 practical VTT; supplementary method explanation</p><h3>Three distinct questions</h3><p>(1) What is the typical condition in a period? Use an appropriately sampled summary. (2) Is there an anomalous event? Compare to seasonally matched baseline. (3) Is there a long-term trend? Model time and seasonality and examine uncertainty.</p><h3>A baseline must be comparable</h3><p>Compare like seasons, sensors, viewing geometries and masks. A trend driven by sensor transition or systematic cloud-screening differences is not ecological change.</p><h3>Worked anomaly</h3><p>Baseline NDVI values have mean 0.68 and standard deviation 0.04. A new observation of 0.56 gives z = (0.56−0.68)/0.04 = −3.0. This is a large spectral anomaly relative to that baseline; it does not identify the ecological cause.</p><h3>Spatial caution</h3><p>A single pixel may mix vegetation and soil/water. If the target is a narrow riparian strip, choose adequate spatial resolution or aggregate to a meaningful mapping unit.</p>", "text": "Week 3 practical VTT; supplementary method explanation Three distinct questions (1) What is the typical condition in a period? Use an appropriately sampled summary. (2) Is there an anomalous event? Compare to seasonally matched baseline. (3) Is there a long-term trend? Model time and seasonality and examine uncertainty. A baseline must be comparable Compare like seasons, sensors, viewing geometries and masks. A trend driven by sensor transition or systematic cloud-screening differences is not ecological change. Worked anomaly Baseline NDVI values have mean 0.68 and standard deviation 0.04. A new observation of 0.56 gives z = (0.56−0.68)/0.04 = −3.0. This is a large spectral anomaly relative to that baseline; it does not identify the ecological cause. Spatial caution A single pixel may mix vegetation and soil/water. If the target is a narrow riparian strip, choose adequate spatial resolution or aggregate to a meaningful mapping unit."}, {"title": "5. Hyperspectral UAV resource: the scientific problem", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 2–4</p><h3>Case study</h3><p>The slide resource describes paddock-scale pasture quality using UAV hyperspectral imagery, laboratory chemistry, machine-learning predictions of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), and subsequent upscaling with Sentinel-2.</p><h3>Why CP and DMD matter</h3><p>The slides explain that cattle require approximately 7% CP to maintain condition, with deterioration below 5%; these are case-study thresholds, not universal nutritional prescriptions. Lab chemistry provides reference measurements but is expensive and spatially sparse.</p><h3>The central measurement gap</h3><p>Optical sensors measure electromagnetic responses, not protein concentration directly. CP and DMD are inferred through empirical relationships between reflectance features and chemically analysed pasture samples.</p><h3>Why a hierarchical approach</h3><p>Laboratory reference is chemically informative but sparse; UAV hyperspectral imagery has rich spectral and spatial detail over limited areas; Sentinel-2 provides larger-area temporal coverage but coarser spatial/spectral information. Each scale addresses a different constraint.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 2–4 Case study The slide resource describes paddock-scale pasture quality using UAV hyperspectral imagery, laboratory chemistry, machine-learning predictions of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), and subsequent upscaling with Sentinel-2. Why CP and DMD matter The slides explain that cattle require approximately 7% CP to maintain condition, with deterioration below 5%; these are case-study thresholds, not universal nutritional prescriptions. Lab chemistry provides reference measurements but is expensive and spatially sparse. The central measurement gap Optical sensors measure electromagnetic responses, not protein concentration directly. CP and DMD are inferred through empirical relationships between reflectance features and chemically analysed pasture samples. Why a hierarchical approach Laboratory reference is chemically informative but sparse; UAV hyperspectral imagery has rich spectral and spatial detail over limited areas; Sentinel-2 provides larger-area temporal coverage but coarser spatial/spectral information. Each scale addresses a different constraint."}, {"title": "6. Hyperspectral sensing: what hundreds of bands add", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 5, 8</p><h3>Source specifications</h3><p>The deck describes the HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 dual VNIR/SWIR pushbroom system, covering approximately 400–2500 nm. Slide 8 specifies 200 VNIR bands around 400–1000 nm and 288 SWIR bands around 950–2500 nm, with ~3 nm and ~5.4 nm sampling; slide 5 rounds the band count to &#x27;500&#x27;. It gives about 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m altitude.</p><h3>Why narrow bands can help</h3><p>Biochemical constituents and leaf water affect spectral absorption and scattering. Narrow spectral channels may capture variations obscured by broad multispectral bands, although signals are also influenced by canopy structure, soil, shadow and atmosphere.</p><h3>Spectral resolution is not a free lunch</h3><p>More bands can mean high dimensionality, redundant predictors and noisy channels. Water-vapour absorption regions may be unsuitable; high-quality calibration and robust validation are necessary.</p><h3>Pushbroom geometry</h3><p>A pushbroom sensor acquires a cross-track line while the platform moves forward. The resulting cube depends on accurate timing, platform attitude, trajectory and scan-line geometry; errors can warp the image.</p><h3>Exam application</h3><p>A student argues that 500 bands guarantee accurate CP estimates. Reject: reference sample quality, model generalisation, calibration, canopy effects and spatial independence remain decisive.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 5, 8 Source specifications The deck describes the HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 dual VNIR/SWIR pushbroom system, covering approximately 400–2500 nm. Slide 8 specifies 200 VNIR bands around 400–1000 nm and 288 SWIR bands around 950–2500 nm, with ~3 nm and ~5.4 nm sampling; slide 5 rounds the band count to '500'. It gives about 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m altitude. Why narrow bands can help Biochemical constituents and leaf water affect spectral absorption and scattering. Narrow spectral channels may capture variations obscured by broad multispectral bands, although signals are also influenced by canopy structure, soil, shadow and atmosphere. Spectral resolution is not a free lunch More bands can mean high dimensionality, redundant predictors and noisy channels. Water-vapour absorption regions may be unsuitable; high-quality calibration and robust validation are necessary. Pushbroom geometry A pushbroom sensor acquires a cross-track line while the platform moves forward. The resulting cube depends on accurate timing, platform attitude, trajectory and scan-line geometry; errors can warp the image. Exam application A student argues that 500 bands guarantee accurate CP estimates. Reject: reference sample quality, model generalisation, calibration, canopy effects and spatial independence remain decisive."}, {"title": "7. Flight planning and spatial overlap", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 6</p><h3>Recorded workflow</h3><p>The slides describe UgCS route planning for an ArduCopter X8 hexacopter carrying the HySpex payload. Parallel flight lines are planned with at least 30% swath overlap and smooth spline turns.</p><h3>Why overlap matters</h3><p>Overlap supports continuous spatial coverage and helps identify mismatches between adjacent strips. Flight height and sensor field of view influence footprint width and ground sampling distance.</p><h3>Flight trade-offs</h3><p>Lower altitude improves ground sampling but reduces area per flight; changing sun angle or clouds alter illumination. Wind and platform motion affect image geometry. A repeat survey should maintain consistent altitude, time, geometry and calibration procedures where feasible.</p><h3>Practical critique</h3><p>A flight plan optimised only for minimum flight time may produce gaps or inconsistent illumination. Explain how that affects orthorectification and quantitative comparisons.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 6 Recorded workflow The slides describe UgCS route planning for an ArduCopter X8 hexacopter carrying the HySpex payload. Parallel flight lines are planned with at least 30% swath overlap and smooth spline turns. Why overlap matters Overlap supports continuous spatial coverage and helps identify mismatches between adjacent strips. Flight height and sensor field of view influence footprint width and ground sampling distance. Flight trade-offs Lower altitude improves ground sampling but reduces area per flight; changing sun angle or clouds alter illumination. Wind and platform motion affect image geometry. A repeat survey should maintain consistent altitude, time, geometry and calibration procedures where feasible. Practical critique A flight plan optimised only for minimum flight time may produce gaps or inconsistent illumination. Explain how that affects orthorectification and quantitative comparisons."}, {"title": "8. Direct georeferencing: GNSS, IMU and SBET", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 7</p><h3>Source pipeline</h3><p>An Emlid Reach RTK base station logs raw GNSS at 1 Hz. Onboard POS/AV provides dual-frequency GNSS and a tactical IMU at 200 Hz. POSPac processing yields a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for positioning scan lines.</p><h3>The physical problem</h3><p>Each spectral measurement must be linked to where the sensor was and where it pointed. GNSS provides position, while IMU provides attitude; lever-arm and boresight alignment matter because a small angular error shifts the ground footprint.</p><h3>Why no GCPs does not mean no validation</h3><p>The slides describe direct georeferencing without ground control points. Nevertheless, independent checkpoints or well-surveyed features are valuable for assessing absolute positional error; otherwise an apparently seamless mosaic may be displaced.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>High-precision RTK positions alone do not guarantee pixel-perfect geolocation if attitude, timing, boresight or terrain model is wrong.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 7 Source pipeline An Emlid Reach RTK base station logs raw GNSS at 1 Hz. Onboard POS/AV provides dual-frequency GNSS and a tactical IMU at 200 Hz. POSPac processing yields a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for positioning scan lines. The physical problem Each spectral measurement must be linked to where the sensor was and where it pointed. GNSS provides position, while IMU provides attitude; lever-arm and boresight alignment matter because a small angular error shifts the ground footprint. Why no GCPs does not mean no validation The slides describe direct georeferencing without ground control points. Nevertheless, independent checkpoints or well-surveyed features are valuable for assessing absolute positional error; otherwise an apparently seamless mosaic may be displaced. Exam trap High-precision RTK positions alone do not guarantee pixel-perfect geolocation if attitude, timing, boresight or terrain model is wrong."}, {"title": "9. Radiance generation and orthorectification", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 8–9</p><h3>NAV to RAD</h3><p>The HySpex NAV processing step merges raw sensor frames with the SBET trajectory to produce navigation-tagged imagery, while the radiometric stage produces a radiance cube.</p><h3>PARGE orthorectification</h3><p>The deck identifies Parametric Orthorectification (PARGE), which ray-casts each sensor pixel through the scene geometry onto a DSM using radiance, SBET, boresight angles and the camera model. This corrects distortions arising from platform movement and terrain.</p><h3>What a DSM contributes</h3><p>A digital surface model supplies the elevation of canopy/building surfaces rather than bare ground alone. Wrong elevation or vertical datum can displace pixel locations, particularly at oblique view angles.</p><h3>What orthorectification does not do</h3><p>It does not turn radiance into reflectance, remove all spectral noise or prove that the location is accurate. Geometry and radiometry are distinct quality dimensions.</p><h3>Worked reasoning</h3><p>If two spectral strips overlap but disagree in feature location, inspect timing, trajectory, attitude, boresight, DSM and orthorectification settings before attributing differences to pasture variability.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 8–9 NAV to RAD The HySpex NAV processing step merges raw sensor frames with the SBET trajectory to produce navigation-tagged imagery, while the radiometric stage produces a radiance cube. PARGE orthorectification The deck identifies Parametric Orthorectification (PARGE), which ray-casts each sensor pixel through the scene geometry onto a DSM using radiance, SBET, boresight angles and the camera model. This corrects distortions arising from platform movement and terrain. What a DSM contributes A digital surface model supplies the elevation of canopy/building surfaces rather than bare ground alone. Wrong elevation or vertical datum can displace pixel locations, particularly at oblique view angles. What orthorectification does not do It does not turn radiance into reflectance, remove all spectral noise or prove that the location is accurate. Geometry and radiometry are distinct quality dimensions. Worked reasoning If two spectral strips overlap but disagree in feature location, inspect timing, trajectory, attitude, boresight, DSM and orthorectification settings before attributing differences to pasture variability."}, {"title": "10. Surface reflectance correction", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 10</p><h3>Recorded tools</h3><p>The slides describe DROACOR converting at-sensor radiance to surface reflectance using atmospheric retrieval and MODTRAN lookup tables tuned for low UAV altitudes. BREFCOR is used in the reflectance-processing chain.</p><h3>Why radiance needs correction</h3><p>At-sensor radiance is influenced by incoming illumination, atmospheric transmission and path effects, as well as surface properties. Quantitative comparison of vegetation across space and time benefits from consistent surface reflectance.</p><h3>SWIR absorption</h3><p>The deck highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm. These bands can contain little reliable surface information under atmospheric conditions and may need exclusion.</p><h3>Caution about &#x27;automatic&#x27; correction</h3><p>An algorithm that estimates atmosphere from the image still makes assumptions. Check reflectance ranges, residual striping, spectral consistency, illumination and available field panels/independent measurements.</p><h3>Exam application</h3><p>A model is trained on uncorrected radiance from one sunny flight and applied to a cloudy flight. Predict poor transfer because illumination and atmospheric differences alter the input feature distributions.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 10 Recorded tools The slides describe DROACOR converting at-sensor radiance to surface reflectance using atmospheric retrieval and MODTRAN lookup tables tuned for low UAV altitudes. BREFCOR is used in the reflectance-processing chain. Why radiance needs correction At-sensor radiance is influenced by incoming illumination, atmospheric transmission and path effects, as well as surface properties. Quantitative comparison of vegetation across space and time benefits from consistent surface reflectance. SWIR absorption The deck highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm. These bands can contain little reliable surface information under atmospheric conditions and may need exclusion. Caution about 'automatic' correction An algorithm that estimates atmosphere from the image still makes assumptions. Check reflectance ranges, residual striping, spectral consistency, illumination and available field panels/independent measurements. Exam application A model is trained on uncorrected radiance from one sunny flight and applied to a cloudy flight. Predict poor transfer because illumination and atmospheric differences alter the input feature distributions."}, {"title": "11. From spectra to CP and DMD: learning the target", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 11–12, 14</p><h3>The target variables</h3><p>CP is crude protein expressed as a percentage; DMD is dry matter digestibility. They are continuous laboratory-derived attributes, so prediction requires quantitative validation (e.g., RMSE, bias and R-squared), not only classification accuracy.</p><h3>Reference data</h3><p>The deck uses leaf/quadrat reference sampling to calibrate across scales. Samples must match imagery in location, date and pasture condition. Wet-chemistry or appropriate laboratory reference methods anchor the target in measured nutrition.</p><h3>Spectral predictors</h3><p>The reflectance cube supplies candidate features; preprocessing may exclude noisy channels, normalise illumination and summarise the relevant canopy footprint. Machine learning maps spectral variation to laboratory CP/DMD, but does not establish direct chemical measurement.</p><h3>Important confounders</h3><p>Species composition, leaf age, water content, canopy density, exposed soil and season may change both spectra and nutrient content. Models should be tested on independent sites and seasons, not just random neighbouring pixels.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 11–12, 14 The target variables CP is crude protein expressed as a percentage; DMD is dry matter digestibility. They are continuous laboratory-derived attributes, so prediction requires quantitative validation (e.g., RMSE, bias and R-squared), not only classification accuracy. Reference data The deck uses leaf/quadrat reference sampling to calibrate across scales. Samples must match imagery in location, date and pasture condition. Wet-chemistry or appropriate laboratory reference methods anchor the target in measured nutrition. Spectral predictors The reflectance cube supplies candidate features; preprocessing may exclude noisy channels, normalise illumination and summarise the relevant canopy footprint. Machine learning maps spectral variation to laboratory CP/DMD, but does not establish direct chemical measurement. Important confounders Species composition, leaf age, water content, canopy density, exposed soil and season may change both spectra and nutrient content. Models should be tested on independent sites and seasons, not just random neighbouring pixels."}, {"title": "12. Masking pasture before nutritional modelling", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 13, 15</p><h3>Recorded method</h3><p>The deck uses a seven-class deep-learning mask and reports 98.6% overall accuracy for that classification. It distinguishes pasture from bare soil, shrubs, trees and other classes before CP/DMD estimation.</p><h3>Why masking is essential</h3><p>A nutrition regression trained on pasture should not be applied to roads, soil or trees. Incorrect masking creates physically meaningless CP values outside the model&#x27;s valid domain.</p><h3>Why overall accuracy is insufficient</h3><p>If the target is pasture, evaluate pasture user&#x27;s and producer&#x27;s accuracy and spatial patterns of omission/commission. A 98.6% overall score can still conceal systematic errors in a rare but important class.</p><h3>Error propagation</h3><p>A wrong pasture mask changes which pixels enter the CP/DMD model; thus the final product inherits both classification and regression errors. These components should be examined separately and, where possible, jointly.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 13, 15 Recorded method The deck uses a seven-class deep-learning mask and reports 98.6% overall accuracy for that classification. It distinguishes pasture from bare soil, shrubs, trees and other classes before CP/DMD estimation. Why masking is essential A nutrition regression trained on pasture should not be applied to roads, soil or trees. Incorrect masking creates physically meaningless CP values outside the model's valid domain. Why overall accuracy is insufficient If the target is pasture, evaluate pasture user's and producer's accuracy and spatial patterns of omission/commission. A 98.6% overall score can still conceal systematic errors in a rare but important class. Error propagation A wrong pasture mask changes which pixels enter the CP/DMD model; thus the final product inherits both classification and regression errors. These components should be examined separately and, where possible, jointly."}, {"title": "13. Neural network pasture mask", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 15</p><h3>Architecture described</h3><p>The slide lists dense hidden blocks of 512 → 256 → 128 → 64 neurons with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4; the final seven-class output uses softmax.</p><h3>How to interpret it</h3><p>ReLU introduces nonlinear transformations; L2 penalises large weights; batch normalisation stabilises intermediate feature distributions; dropout reduces reliance on particular units during training. Softmax converts output scores to class probabilities under the model.</p><h3>What model confidence means</h3><p>A softmax score is not guaranteed to be calibrated probability of correctness. Assess reliability on independent samples and consider geographic or seasonal domain shift.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>A sophisticated architecture does not substitute for representative training, transparent labels and independent evaluation.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 15 Architecture described The slide lists dense hidden blocks of 512 → 256 → 128 → 64 neurons with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4; the final seven-class output uses softmax. How to interpret it ReLU introduces nonlinear transformations; L2 penalises large weights; batch normalisation stabilises intermediate feature distributions; dropout reduces reliance on particular units during training. Softmax converts output scores to class probabilities under the model. What model confidence means A softmax score is not guaranteed to be calibrated probability of correctness. Assess reliability on independent samples and consider geographic or seasonal domain shift. Exam trap A sophisticated architecture does not substitute for representative training, transparent labels and independent evaluation."}, {"title": "14. TabPFN for CP/DMD prediction", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 16</p><h3>Source explanation</h3><p>The slides describe TabPFN as a pretrained tabular transformer trained on synthetic tabular tasks. At inference, it takes a training dataset as context and predicts new rows without conventional task-specific gradient-descent training.</p><h3>What the approach offers</h3><p>It may perform well on small tabular datasets where conventional deep learning needs more examples. In this case spectral and ancillary predictors can be associated with laboratory nutritional measurements.</p><h3>Why validation remains central</h3><p>Model transfer to new properties, rainfall regimes or pasture species cannot be assumed. High training fit is not evidence of performance on independent paddocks.</p><h3>Distinguish source from supplement</h3><p>The detailed transformer internals are not required by the slide to make a sound EO critique. Focus on training context, predictors, targets, leakage and external validation.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 16 Source explanation The slides describe TabPFN as a pretrained tabular transformer trained on synthetic tabular tasks. At inference, it takes a training dataset as context and predicts new rows without conventional task-specific gradient-descent training. What the approach offers It may perform well on small tabular datasets where conventional deep learning needs more examples. In this case spectral and ancillary predictors can be associated with laboratory nutritional measurements. Why validation remains central Model transfer to new properties, rainfall regimes or pasture species cannot be assumed. High training fit is not evidence of performance on independent paddocks. Distinguish source from supplement The detailed transformer internals are not required by the slide to make a sound EO critique. Focus on training context, predictors, targets, leakage and external validation."}, {"title": "15. Spatial leakage and leave-one-property-out validation", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 17</p><h3>Source warning</h3><p>The slide explicitly warns that random 80/20 splitting can leak information because neighbouring pixels are correlated. Its evaluation holds out entire sites (leave-one-property-out) to test spatial transfer.</p><h3>Why random splits overestimate performance</h3><p>Pixels within a field share soils, species, management and illumination. Training and test samples may be nearly identical despite different row indices, so the score measures interpolation within known conditions rather than transfer to a new property.</p><h3>How spatial holdout helps</h3><p>Training on all but one property and evaluating on the withheld property gives a harder and more realistic test of generalisation. Repeating across properties reveals between-site variability.</p><h3>Residual limitations</h3><p>A property-level holdout still may not test a new season, sensor, geographic region or pasture species. A strong evaluation aligns holdout design with the intended deployment domain.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 17 Source warning The slide explicitly warns that random 80/20 splitting can leak information because neighbouring pixels are correlated. Its evaluation holds out entire sites (leave-one-property-out) to test spatial transfer. Why random splits overestimate performance Pixels within a field share soils, species, management and illumination. Training and test samples may be nearly identical despite different row indices, so the score measures interpolation within known conditions rather than transfer to a new property. How spatial holdout helps Training on all but one property and evaluating on the withheld property gives a harder and more realistic test of generalisation. Repeating across properties reveals between-site variability. Residual limitations A property-level holdout still may not test a new season, sensor, geographic region or pasture species. A strong evaluation aligns holdout design with the intended deployment domain."}, {"title": "16. Interpreting the reported performance", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 18</p><h3>Numbers on the slide</h3><p>The slide reports CP/DMD R-squared at three scales: leaf laboratory NIR (0.76/0.47), UAV hyperspectral (0.95/0.88), and Sentinel-2 (0.92/0.73), with Sentinel-2 CP RMSE shown as 0.39 percentage points.</p><h3>What R-squared does and does not say</h3><p>R-squared measures explained variation relative to a reference baseline under its calculation definition. It is not &#x27;95% of CP measurements are correct&#x27;. RMSE reports error in the original target units, while bias reveals systematic over- or underprediction.</p><h3>Potential reporting questions</h3><p>Different scales may involve different sample sizes, filtering and evaluation sets. Scores should not be ranked as pure sensor superiority without confirming comparable targets, samples, splits and uncertainty.</p><h3>Worked calculation</h3><p>Reference CP [6,8,10]% and predicted [6.5,7.5,11]% yield errors [+0.5,−0.5,+1.0] percentage points; RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points, MAE = 0.667 and bias = +0.333. These values are original examples, not reported case-study metrics.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 18 Numbers on the slide The slide reports CP/DMD R-squared at three scales: leaf laboratory NIR (0.76/0.47), UAV hyperspectral (0.95/0.88), and Sentinel-2 (0.92/0.73), with Sentinel-2 CP RMSE shown as 0.39 percentage points. What R-squared does and does not say R-squared measures explained variation relative to a reference baseline under its calculation definition. It is not '95% of CP measurements are correct'. RMSE reports error in the original target units, while bias reveals systematic over- or underprediction. Potential reporting questions Different scales may involve different sample sizes, filtering and evaluation sets. Scores should not be ranked as pure sensor superiority without confirming comparable targets, samples, splits and uncertainty. Worked calculation Reference CP [6,8,10]% and predicted [6.5,7.5,11]% yield errors [+0.5,−0.5,+1.0] percentage points; RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points, MAE = 0.667 and bias = +0.333. These values are original examples, not reported case-study metrics."}, {"title": "17. Hierarchical upscaling from quadrats to satellites", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck p. 14</p><h3>Recorded scales</h3><p>The slide shows approximately 1 m² leaf/quadrat measurements, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 imagery, with the satellite archive extending back to 2018.</p><h3>The change-of-support problem</h3><p>A 10 m satellite pixel represents 100 m², while a 1 m² quadrat samples only part of it. Aggregating fine-resolution predictions requires an explicit support model, and non-pasture fractions must be handled rather than assumed absent.</p><h3>Upscaling is not just resizing</h3><p>Spectral response functions differ between hyperspectral and multispectral sensors. Satellite atmospheric effects, mixed pixels and view geometry differ from UAV imagery. A model may need band simulation/harmonisation and new calibration.</p><h3>Error propagation</h3><p>Uncertainty arises from field chemistry, quadrat geolocation, UAV reflectance, pasture masking, regression, aggregation and satellite retrieval. The final map should not report more precision than the chain supports.</p><h3>Exam application</h3><p>A student trains on 8 cm pixels then directly applies the same coefficients to 10 m Sentinel-2 bands. Critique spectral incompatibility, scale mismatch, mixed pixels and missing transfer validation.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck p. 14 Recorded scales The slide shows approximately 1 m² leaf/quadrat measurements, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 imagery, with the satellite archive extending back to 2018. The change-of-support problem A 10 m satellite pixel represents 100 m², while a 1 m² quadrat samples only part of it. Aggregating fine-resolution predictions requires an explicit support model, and non-pasture fractions must be handled rather than assumed absent. Upscaling is not just resizing Spectral response functions differ between hyperspectral and multispectral sensors. Satellite atmospheric effects, mixed pixels and view geometry differ from UAV imagery. A model may need band simulation/harmonisation and new calibration. Error propagation Uncertainty arises from field chemistry, quadrat geolocation, UAV reflectance, pasture masking, regression, aggregation and satellite retrieval. The final map should not report more precision than the chain supports. Exam application A student trains on 8 cm pixels then directly applies the same coefficients to 10 m Sentinel-2 bands. Critique spectral incompatibility, scale mismatch, mixed pixels and missing transfer validation."}, {"title": "18. Seasonal dynamics and practical decision support", "html": "<p>Hyperspectral deck pp. 19–20</p><h3>Slide findings</h3><p>The case study reports CP peaks approximately 2–3 weeks after rainfall and ranges around 10–12% in wet conditions and 4–6% in dry conditions; DMD changes more modestly, roughly 55–57% wet versus 52–54% dry.</p><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>The temporal relationship can inform grazing decisions, but it is an observed case-study pattern rather than a universal causal rule. Other drivers include pasture type, grazing pressure, soils and temperature.</p><h3>Delivery</h3><p>The deck describes a Pasture Quality Monitor web application where a grazier selects a paddock area and receives mapped predictions, with a field-visit archive and API-backed access.</p><h3>Scientific responsibility</h3><p>A decision-support interface should display observation dates, uncertainty, valid coverage and model domain. A crisp map without those details may encourage overconfident management decisions.</p>", "text": "Hyperspectral deck pp. 19–20 Slide findings The case study reports CP peaks approximately 2–3 weeks after rainfall and ranges around 10–12% in wet conditions and 4–6% in dry conditions; DMD changes more modestly, roughly 55–57% wet versus 52–54% dry. Interpretation The temporal relationship can inform grazing decisions, but it is an observed case-study pattern rather than a universal causal rule. Other drivers include pasture type, grazing pressure, soils and temperature. Delivery The deck describes a Pasture Quality Monitor web application where a grazier selects a paddock area and receives mapped predictions, with a field-visit archive and API-backed access. Scientific responsibility A decision-support interface should display observation dates, uncertainty, valid coverage and model domain. A crisp map without those details may encourage overconfident management decisions."}, {"title": "19. Integrated examination scenario", "html": "<p>Original scenario drawing on the Week 3 resources</p><h3>Question</h3><p>An agency flies a UAV hyperspectral sensor over one cattle property, obtains 500 spectral bands, trains a CP model on random pixels, reports R-squared = 0.97, upscales directly to Sentinel-2, and tells all graziers the map gives exact CP.</p><h3>Critique: physical preprocessing</h3><p>Require documented mission planning, trajectory/IMU and boresight quality, orthorectification with a suitable DSM, radiance calibration, surface reflectance and noisy-band screening.</p><h3>Critique: targets and masks</h3><p>CP is a chemical quantity requiring laboratory reference; mask non-pasture and evaluate pasture-specific classification errors. Inspect temporal alignment and representativeness of quadrats.</p><h3>Critique: validation</h3><p>Random neighbouring pixel splits are spatially leaky. Use leave-one-property-out or other spatial holdout; report RMSE, bias, uncertainty and performance across species and seasons.</p><h3>Critique: scaling</h3><p>8 cm hyperspectral and 10 m multispectral observations differ in spatial support and spectral response. Use defensible feature transfer and independent satellite-scale validation.</p><h3>Defended alternative</h3><p>Establish a hierarchical calibration/validation design across field, UAV and satellite scales, document preprocessing and masks, use independent property-level testing, and communicate uncertainty rather than exact nutritional values.</p>", "text": "Original scenario drawing on the Week 3 resources Question An agency flies a UAV hyperspectral sensor over one cattle property, obtains 500 spectral bands, trains a CP model on random pixels, reports R-squared = 0.97, upscales directly to Sentinel-2, and tells all graziers the map gives exact CP. Critique: physical preprocessing Require documented mission planning, trajectory/IMU and boresight quality, orthorectification with a suitable DSM, radiance calibration, surface reflectance and noisy-band screening. Critique: targets and masks CP is a chemical quantity requiring laboratory reference; mask non-pasture and evaluate pasture-specific classification errors. Inspect temporal alignment and representativeness of quadrats. Critique: validation Random neighbouring pixel splits are spatially leaky. Use leave-one-property-out or other spatial holdout; report RMSE, bias, uncertainty and performance across species and seasons. Critique: scaling 8 cm hyperspectral and 10 m multispectral observations differ in spatial support and spectral response. Use defensible feature transfer and independent satellite-scale validation. Defended alternative Establish a hierarchical calibration/validation design across field, UAV and satellite scales, document preprocessing and masks, use independent property-level testing, and communicate uncertainty rather than exact nutritional values."}, {"title": "20. Revision and source distinctions", "html": "<p>Week 3 practical VTT + hyperspectral deck</p><h3>What is transcript-supported</h3><p>The 12 August practical recording supports Sandbox notebook instruction, the transition to ENVI practicals, and cloud recognition. It does not serve as a transcript for the hyperspectral presentation.</p><h3>What is slide-supported</h3><p>The hyperspectral deck supplies the UAV instrument specifications, navigation/orthorectification/reflectance pipeline, pasture-mask architecture, TabPFN description, site-level validation, numerical performance and seasonal examples.</p><h3>What is supplementary</h3><p>The radiometric physics, detailed sources of error, RMSE numerical calculation and original exam critique expand the sources for learning. They are not presented as verbatim lecturer remarks.</p><h3>Self-test</h3><p>Explain why RTK alone does not ensure correct pixel location; why high band count does not guarantee chemical prediction; why an overall 98.6% mask accuracy is not sufficient; why leave-one-property-out is stronger than random pixels; and why upscaling requires spectral as well as spatial harmonisation.</p>", "text": "Week 3 practical VTT + hyperspectral deck What is transcript-supported The 12 August practical recording supports Sandbox notebook instruction, the transition to ENVI practicals, and cloud recognition. It does not serve as a transcript for the hyperspectral presentation. What is slide-supported The hyperspectral deck supplies the UAV instrument specifications, navigation/orthorectification/reflectance pipeline, pasture-mask architecture, TabPFN description, site-level validation, numerical performance and seasonal examples. What is supplementary The radiometric physics, detailed sources of error, RMSE numerical calculation and original exam critique expand the sources for learning. They are not presented as verbatim lecturer remarks. Self-test Explain why RTK alone does not ensure correct pixel location; why high band count does not guarantee chemical prediction; why an overall 98.6% mask accuracy is not sufficient; why leave-one-property-out is stronger than random pixels; and why upscaling requires spectral as well as spatial harmonisation."}], "text": ["1. Source audit: Week 3 is not a conventional lecture Week 3 practical VTT, 12 Aug 2026, 00:09:09–00:09:30 Verified scheduling The Week 4 lecture recording (19 August) explicitly says there was no lecture the preceding week because of the public holiday. The Week 3 VTT is a practical-room recording. Thus this chapter covers Week 3 practical work and the uploaded 'L3_P2' hyperspectral UAV slide resource, not a fabricated Lecture 3 spoken lecture. Two separate sources The 12 August practical transcript concerns the DEA Sandbox, cloud masking and notebook exercises. The 21-page hyperspectral pasture-quality deck describes a UAV-to-Sentinel-2 workflow. The VTT does not discuss that hyperspectral deck: they should not be falsely merged as one recorded presentation. How to study this chapter Part A develops the DEA practical and its conceptual implications. Part B reconstructs the UAV hyperspectral resource. Part C integrates the two into critical workflow design, marking clearly where examples and explanations are supplementary.", "2. Week 3 Sandbox practical: from notebook execution to scientific reasoning Week 3 practical VTT 00:09:09–00:10:47 Lecturer/tutor context The practical-room instructor says this is the last Sandbox practical before four ENVI practicals, although students will return to the Sandbox for their assessments. The class is directed to revisit Beginner's Guide notebook functionality, including a 12-question check. Why the interface is secondary The scientific work is to formulate a query, identify an appropriate corrected data product, inspect metadata, filter invalid observations, compute a defensible measurement and document processing. Clicking 'Run' on a notebook does not prove that an analysis is correct. Reproducibility checklist For each notebook record its source data/product version, area of interest, temporal window, band names, CRS, cloud and shadow flags, transformations, compositing statistic, units, valid observation counts and output format. This checklist is supplementary, developed to operationalise the practical. Exam application A student reports 'the DEA notebook ran without errors, therefore the map is accurate.' Critique: software execution validates syntax/runtime, not physical calibration, suitability, independent reference or uncertainty.", "3. Cloud masking and the difference between a pretty composite and a valid time series Week 3 practical VTT 00:19:43–00:20:09 Transcript evidence The instructor asks students what stands out in the displayed images, and the class identifies clouds. The exercise trains visual recognition of invalid observations, but quantitative analysis needs systematic quality masking. Cloud and shadow errors Bright cloud pixels and dark cloud-shadow pixels can strongly distort indices and spectral classification. Thin cloud and haze may be less obvious than thick clouds. Masks have false positives and false negatives, so inspect them rather than assuming perfect removal. Temporal sampling bias If a location has 30 valid observations in one year and only 3 in another, annual medians may differ in seasonal representativeness. Always inspect observation counts, seasonality and missing-data patterns. Supplementary numerical example Valid NDVI observations [0.62,0.66,0.64] have median 0.64. If an unmasked cloud shadow produces 0.10, a four-value median is 0.63, but the mean falls from 0.64 to 0.505. Different summaries respond differently to contamination; none replaces quality screening.", "4. From repeated observations to meaningful time-series analysis Week 3 practical VTT; supplementary method explanation Three distinct questions (1) What is the typical condition in a period? Use an appropriately sampled summary. (2) Is there an anomalous event? Compare to seasonally matched baseline. (3) Is there a long-term trend? Model time and seasonality and examine uncertainty. A baseline must be comparable Compare like seasons, sensors, viewing geometries and masks. A trend driven by sensor transition or systematic cloud-screening differences is not ecological change. Worked anomaly Baseline NDVI values have mean 0.68 and standard deviation 0.04. A new observation of 0.56 gives z = (0.56−0.68)/0.04 = −3.0. This is a large spectral anomaly relative to that baseline; it does not identify the ecological cause. Spatial caution A single pixel may mix vegetation and soil/water. If the target is a narrow riparian strip, choose adequate spatial resolution or aggregate to a meaningful mapping unit.", "5. Hyperspectral UAV resource: the scientific problem Hyperspectral deck pp. 2–4 Case study The slide resource describes paddock-scale pasture quality using UAV hyperspectral imagery, laboratory chemistry, machine-learning predictions of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), and subsequent upscaling with Sentinel-2. Why CP and DMD matter The slides explain that cattle require approximately 7% CP to maintain condition, with deterioration below 5%; these are case-study thresholds, not universal nutritional prescriptions. Lab chemistry provides reference measurements but is expensive and spatially sparse. The central measurement gap Optical sensors measure electromagnetic responses, not protein concentration directly. CP and DMD are inferred through empirical relationships between reflectance features and chemically analysed pasture samples. Why a hierarchical approach Laboratory reference is chemically informative but sparse; UAV hyperspectral imagery has rich spectral and spatial detail over limited areas; Sentinel-2 provides larger-area temporal coverage but coarser spatial/spectral information. Each scale addresses a different constraint.", "6. Hyperspectral sensing: what hundreds of bands add Hyperspectral deck pp. 5, 8 Source specifications The deck describes the HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 dual VNIR/SWIR pushbroom system, covering approximately 400–2500 nm. Slide 8 specifies 200 VNIR bands around 400–1000 nm and 288 SWIR bands around 950–2500 nm, with ~3 nm and ~5.4 nm sampling; slide 5 rounds the band count to '500'. It gives about 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m altitude. Why narrow bands can help Biochemical constituents and leaf water affect spectral absorption and scattering. Narrow spectral channels may capture variations obscured by broad multispectral bands, although signals are also influenced by canopy structure, soil, shadow and atmosphere. Spectral resolution is not a free lunch More bands can mean high dimensionality, redundant predictors and noisy channels. Water-vapour absorption regions may be unsuitable; high-quality calibration and robust validation are necessary. Pushbroom geometry A pushbroom sensor acquires a cross-track line while the platform moves forward. The resulting cube depends on accurate timing, platform attitude, trajectory and scan-line geometry; errors can warp the image. Exam application A student argues that 500 bands guarantee accurate CP estimates. Reject: reference sample quality, model generalisation, calibration, canopy effects and spatial independence remain decisive.", "7. Flight planning and spatial overlap Hyperspectral deck p. 6 Recorded workflow The slides describe UgCS route planning for an ArduCopter X8 hexacopter carrying the HySpex payload. Parallel flight lines are planned with at least 30% swath overlap and smooth spline turns. Why overlap matters Overlap supports continuous spatial coverage and helps identify mismatches between adjacent strips. Flight height and sensor field of view influence footprint width and ground sampling distance. Flight trade-offs Lower altitude improves ground sampling but reduces area per flight; changing sun angle or clouds alter illumination. Wind and platform motion affect image geometry. A repeat survey should maintain consistent altitude, time, geometry and calibration procedures where feasible. Practical critique A flight plan optimised only for minimum flight time may produce gaps or inconsistent illumination. Explain how that affects orthorectification and quantitative comparisons.", "8. Direct georeferencing: GNSS, IMU and SBET Hyperspectral deck p. 7 Source pipeline An Emlid Reach RTK base station logs raw GNSS at 1 Hz. Onboard POS/AV provides dual-frequency GNSS and a tactical IMU at 200 Hz. POSPac processing yields a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for positioning scan lines. The physical problem Each spectral measurement must be linked to where the sensor was and where it pointed. GNSS provides position, while IMU provides attitude; lever-arm and boresight alignment matter because a small angular error shifts the ground footprint. Why no GCPs does not mean no validation The slides describe direct georeferencing without ground control points. Nevertheless, independent checkpoints or well-surveyed features are valuable for assessing absolute positional error; otherwise an apparently seamless mosaic may be displaced. Exam trap High-precision RTK positions alone do not guarantee pixel-perfect geolocation if attitude, timing, boresight or terrain model is wrong.", "9. Radiance generation and orthorectification Hyperspectral deck pp. 8–9 NAV to RAD The HySpex NAV processing step merges raw sensor frames with the SBET trajectory to produce navigation-tagged imagery, while the radiometric stage produces a radiance cube. PARGE orthorectification The deck identifies Parametric Orthorectification (PARGE), which ray-casts each sensor pixel through the scene geometry onto a DSM using radiance, SBET, boresight angles and the camera model. This corrects distortions arising from platform movement and terrain. What a DSM contributes A digital surface model supplies the elevation of canopy/building surfaces rather than bare ground alone. Wrong elevation or vertical datum can displace pixel locations, particularly at oblique view angles. What orthorectification does not do It does not turn radiance into reflectance, remove all spectral noise or prove that the location is accurate. Geometry and radiometry are distinct quality dimensions. Worked reasoning If two spectral strips overlap but disagree in feature location, inspect timing, trajectory, attitude, boresight, DSM and orthorectification settings before attributing differences to pasture variability.", "10. Surface reflectance correction Hyperspectral deck p. 10 Recorded tools The slides describe DROACOR converting at-sensor radiance to surface reflectance using atmospheric retrieval and MODTRAN lookup tables tuned for low UAV altitudes. BREFCOR is used in the reflectance-processing chain. Why radiance needs correction At-sensor radiance is influenced by incoming illumination, atmospheric transmission and path effects, as well as surface properties. Quantitative comparison of vegetation across space and time benefits from consistent surface reflectance. SWIR absorption The deck highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm. These bands can contain little reliable surface information under atmospheric conditions and may need exclusion. Caution about 'automatic' correction An algorithm that estimates atmosphere from the image still makes assumptions. Check reflectance ranges, residual striping, spectral consistency, illumination and available field panels/independent measurements. Exam application A model is trained on uncorrected radiance from one sunny flight and applied to a cloudy flight. Predict poor transfer because illumination and atmospheric differences alter the input feature distributions.", "11. From spectra to CP and DMD: learning the target Hyperspectral deck pp. 11–12, 14 The target variables CP is crude protein expressed as a percentage; DMD is dry matter digestibility. They are continuous laboratory-derived attributes, so prediction requires quantitative validation (e.g., RMSE, bias and R-squared), not only classification accuracy. Reference data The deck uses leaf/quadrat reference sampling to calibrate across scales. Samples must match imagery in location, date and pasture condition. Wet-chemistry or appropriate laboratory reference methods anchor the target in measured nutrition. Spectral predictors The reflectance cube supplies candidate features; preprocessing may exclude noisy channels, normalise illumination and summarise the relevant canopy footprint. Machine learning maps spectral variation to laboratory CP/DMD, but does not establish direct chemical measurement. Important confounders Species composition, leaf age, water content, canopy density, exposed soil and season may change both spectra and nutrient content. Models should be tested on independent sites and seasons, not just random neighbouring pixels.", "12. Masking pasture before nutritional modelling Hyperspectral deck pp. 13, 15 Recorded method The deck uses a seven-class deep-learning mask and reports 98.6% overall accuracy for that classification. It distinguishes pasture from bare soil, shrubs, trees and other classes before CP/DMD estimation. Why masking is essential A nutrition regression trained on pasture should not be applied to roads, soil or trees. Incorrect masking creates physically meaningless CP values outside the model's valid domain. Why overall accuracy is insufficient If the target is pasture, evaluate pasture user's and producer's accuracy and spatial patterns of omission/commission. A 98.6% overall score can still conceal systematic errors in a rare but important class. Error propagation A wrong pasture mask changes which pixels enter the CP/DMD model; thus the final product inherits both classification and regression errors. These components should be examined separately and, where possible, jointly.", "13. Neural network pasture mask Hyperspectral deck p. 15 Architecture described The slide lists dense hidden blocks of 512 → 256 → 128 → 64 neurons with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4; the final seven-class output uses softmax. How to interpret it ReLU introduces nonlinear transformations; L2 penalises large weights; batch normalisation stabilises intermediate feature distributions; dropout reduces reliance on particular units during training. Softmax converts output scores to class probabilities under the model. What model confidence means A softmax score is not guaranteed to be calibrated probability of correctness. Assess reliability on independent samples and consider geographic or seasonal domain shift. Exam trap A sophisticated architecture does not substitute for representative training, transparent labels and independent evaluation.", "14. TabPFN for CP/DMD prediction Hyperspectral deck p. 16 Source explanation The slides describe TabPFN as a pretrained tabular transformer trained on synthetic tabular tasks. At inference, it takes a training dataset as context and predicts new rows without conventional task-specific gradient-descent training. What the approach offers It may perform well on small tabular datasets where conventional deep learning needs more examples. In this case spectral and ancillary predictors can be associated with laboratory nutritional measurements. Why validation remains central Model transfer to new properties, rainfall regimes or pasture species cannot be assumed. High training fit is not evidence of performance on independent paddocks. Distinguish source from supplement The detailed transformer internals are not required by the slide to make a sound EO critique. Focus on training context, predictors, targets, leakage and external validation.", "15. Spatial leakage and leave-one-property-out validation Hyperspectral deck p. 17 Source warning The slide explicitly warns that random 80/20 splitting can leak information because neighbouring pixels are correlated. Its evaluation holds out entire sites (leave-one-property-out) to test spatial transfer. Why random splits overestimate performance Pixels within a field share soils, species, management and illumination. Training and test samples may be nearly identical despite different row indices, so the score measures interpolation within known conditions rather than transfer to a new property. How spatial holdout helps Training on all but one property and evaluating on the withheld property gives a harder and more realistic test of generalisation. Repeating across properties reveals between-site variability. Residual limitations A property-level holdout still may not test a new season, sensor, geographic region or pasture species. A strong evaluation aligns holdout design with the intended deployment domain.", "16. Interpreting the reported performance Hyperspectral deck p. 18 Numbers on the slide The slide reports CP/DMD R-squared at three scales: leaf laboratory NIR (0.76/0.47), UAV hyperspectral (0.95/0.88), and Sentinel-2 (0.92/0.73), with Sentinel-2 CP RMSE shown as 0.39 percentage points. What R-squared does and does not say R-squared measures explained variation relative to a reference baseline under its calculation definition. It is not '95% of CP measurements are correct'. RMSE reports error in the original target units, while bias reveals systematic over- or underprediction. Potential reporting questions Different scales may involve different sample sizes, filtering and evaluation sets. Scores should not be ranked as pure sensor superiority without confirming comparable targets, samples, splits and uncertainty. Worked calculation Reference CP [6,8,10]% and predicted [6.5,7.5,11]% yield errors [+0.5,−0.5,+1.0] percentage points; RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points, MAE = 0.667 and bias = +0.333. These values are original examples, not reported case-study metrics.", "17. Hierarchical upscaling from quadrats to satellites Hyperspectral deck p. 14 Recorded scales The slide shows approximately 1 m² leaf/quadrat measurements, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 imagery, with the satellite archive extending back to 2018. The change-of-support problem A 10 m satellite pixel represents 100 m², while a 1 m² quadrat samples only part of it. Aggregating fine-resolution predictions requires an explicit support model, and non-pasture fractions must be handled rather than assumed absent. Upscaling is not just resizing Spectral response functions differ between hyperspectral and multispectral sensors. Satellite atmospheric effects, mixed pixels and view geometry differ from UAV imagery. A model may need band simulation/harmonisation and new calibration. Error propagation Uncertainty arises from field chemistry, quadrat geolocation, UAV reflectance, pasture masking, regression, aggregation and satellite retrieval. The final map should not report more precision than the chain supports. Exam application A student trains on 8 cm pixels then directly applies the same coefficients to 10 m Sentinel-2 bands. Critique spectral incompatibility, scale mismatch, mixed pixels and missing transfer validation.", "18. Seasonal dynamics and practical decision support Hyperspectral deck pp. 19–20 Slide findings The case study reports CP peaks approximately 2–3 weeks after rainfall and ranges around 10–12% in wet conditions and 4–6% in dry conditions; DMD changes more modestly, roughly 55–57% wet versus 52–54% dry. Interpretation The temporal relationship can inform grazing decisions, but it is an observed case-study pattern rather than a universal causal rule. Other drivers include pasture type, grazing pressure, soils and temperature. Delivery The deck describes a Pasture Quality Monitor web application where a grazier selects a paddock area and receives mapped predictions, with a field-visit archive and API-backed access. Scientific responsibility A decision-support interface should display observation dates, uncertainty, valid coverage and model domain. A crisp map without those details may encourage overconfident management decisions.", "19. Integrated examination scenario Original scenario drawing on the Week 3 resources Question An agency flies a UAV hyperspectral sensor over one cattle property, obtains 500 spectral bands, trains a CP model on random pixels, reports R-squared = 0.97, upscales directly to Sentinel-2, and tells all graziers the map gives exact CP. Critique: physical preprocessing Require documented mission planning, trajectory/IMU and boresight quality, orthorectification with a suitable DSM, radiance calibration, surface reflectance and noisy-band screening. Critique: targets and masks CP is a chemical quantity requiring laboratory reference; mask non-pasture and evaluate pasture-specific classification errors. Inspect temporal alignment and representativeness of quadrats. Critique: validation Random neighbouring pixel splits are spatially leaky. Use leave-one-property-out or other spatial holdout; report RMSE, bias, uncertainty and performance across species and seasons. Critique: scaling 8 cm hyperspectral and 10 m multispectral observations differ in spatial support and spectral response. Use defensible feature transfer and independent satellite-scale validation. Defended alternative Establish a hierarchical calibration/validation design across field, UAV and satellite scales, document preprocessing and masks, use independent property-level testing, and communicate uncertainty rather than exact nutritional values.", "20. Revision and source distinctions Week 3 practical VTT + hyperspectral deck What is transcript-supported The 12 August practical recording supports Sandbox notebook instruction, the transition to ENVI practicals, and cloud recognition. It does not serve as a transcript for the hyperspectral presentation. What is slide-supported The hyperspectral deck supplies the UAV instrument specifications, navigation/orthorectification/reflectance pipeline, pasture-mask architecture, TabPFN description, site-level validation, numerical performance and seasonal examples. What is supplementary The radiometric physics, detailed sources of error, RMSE numerical calculation and original exam critique expand the sources for learning. They are not presented as verbatim lecturer remarks. Self-test Explain why RTK alone does not ensure correct pixel location; why high band count does not guarantee chemical prediction; why an overall 98.6% mask accuracy is not sufficient; why leave-one-property-out is stronger than random pixels; and why upscaling requires spectral as well as spatial harmonisation."]}, {"id": 3, "title": "Hyperspectral UAV Remote Sensing", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/04-GEOM7001_Deep_Week04_UAV_Hyperspectral.pdf", "pages": 9, "sections": [{"title": "Source integrity", "html": "<p>The matching 19 August VTT confirms the Week 4 guest lecture. The slide deck filename is L3_P2, but the presentation corresponds to the Week 4 recording. Supplementary examples are clearly distinguished from slide-reported findings.</p>", "text": "The matching 19 August VTT confirms the Week 4 guest lecture. The slide deck filename is L3_P2, but the presentation corresponds to the Week 4 recording. Supplementary examples are clearly distinguished from slide-reported findings."}, {"title": "01. Source reconciliation and the scientific problem", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 2–4; recording 00:25–00:30, 01:32 onward</p><h3>What the source actually is</h3><p>This is the 19 August Week 4 guest lecture, introduced by Will Woodgate and presented by Jason Barnetson. The guest describes the end-to-end process of converting UAV hyperspectral measurements into paddock-scale nutrition products. Although the file name contains L3_P2, the matching spoken lecture is Week 4; this source should not be described as a Week 3 lecture.</p><h3>The inferential challenge</h3><p>Cattle nutrition requires estimates of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), but an imaging spectrometer observes wavelength-dependent energy rather than protein molecules directly. Lab assays supply the reference variable; a statistical model then predicts nutrition from spectral and spatial predictors. This distinction determines what can be claimed about accuracy.</p><h3>Why this is a systems problem</h3><p>The final pasture map is only as reliable as its flight geometry, radiometric calibration, surface reflectance, pasture/non-pasture mask, field chemistry, statistical modelling and scale transfer. The case study is therefore ideal for the lecturer’s exam emphasis on critiquing complete workflows rather than naming algorithms.</p>", "text": "Slides 2–4; recording 00:25–00:30, 01:32 onward What the source actually is This is the 19 August Week 4 guest lecture, introduced by Will Woodgate and presented by Jason Barnetson. The guest describes the end-to-end process of converting UAV hyperspectral measurements into paddock-scale nutrition products. Although the file name contains L3_P2, the matching spoken lecture is Week 4; this source should not be described as a Week 3 lecture. The inferential challenge Cattle nutrition requires estimates of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), but an imaging spectrometer observes wavelength-dependent energy rather than protein molecules directly. Lab assays supply the reference variable; a statistical model then predicts nutrition from spectral and spatial predictors. This distinction determines what can be claimed about accuracy. Why this is a systems problem The final pasture map is only as reliable as its flight geometry, radiometric calibration, surface reflectance, pasture/non-pasture mask, field chemistry, statistical modelling and scale transfer. The case study is therefore ideal for the lecturer’s exam emphasis on critiquing complete workflows rather than naming algorithms."}, {"title": "02. Sensor design: VNIR, SWIR and the pushbroom acquisition", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 5 and 8; recording ~01:32–01:44</p><h3>Instrument specifications</h3><p>The slides describe a HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 system with a VNIR component (200 bands, about 400–1000 nm) and a SWIR component (288 bands, about 950–2500 nm). Band spacings are approximately 3 nm and 5.4 nm, respectively. Slide 5 rounds this to about 500 bands and states roughly 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m. These are case-study specifications, not a universal capability of all UAV hyperspectral sensors.</p><h3>Why narrow bands are useful</h3><p>Spectral absorption and scattering vary with pigment, water, leaf chemistry, canopy structure and soil exposure. Narrow channels can resolve features that broad multispectral bands average together. But high dimensionality brings correlated bands, noisy wavelengths and a need for well-designed reference data.</p><h3>How pushbroom imaging works</h3><p>A pushbroom instrument measures a line across track at each instant. Aircraft movement builds the second spatial dimension. Each line has a timestamp, position and orientation; errors in timing, pitch, roll, yaw or trajectory can displace or distort its pixels. A hyperspectral cube is therefore not simply a photograph with extra bands.</p><h3>Exam-style critique</h3><p>“The UAV produces 500 bands, so pasture protein is measured directly and accurately.” Incorrect: protein is inferred through calibration to reference chemistry, and performance must be independently evaluated on unseen locations and conditions.</p>", "text": "Slides 5 and 8; recording ~01:32–01:44 Instrument specifications The slides describe a HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 system with a VNIR component (200 bands, about 400–1000 nm) and a SWIR component (288 bands, about 950–2500 nm). Band spacings are approximately 3 nm and 5.4 nm, respectively. Slide 5 rounds this to about 500 bands and states roughly 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m. These are case-study specifications, not a universal capability of all UAV hyperspectral sensors. Why narrow bands are useful Spectral absorption and scattering vary with pigment, water, leaf chemistry, canopy structure and soil exposure. Narrow channels can resolve features that broad multispectral bands average together. But high dimensionality brings correlated bands, noisy wavelengths and a need for well-designed reference data. How pushbroom imaging works A pushbroom instrument measures a line across track at each instant. Aircraft movement builds the second spatial dimension. Each line has a timestamp, position and orientation; errors in timing, pitch, roll, yaw or trajectory can displace or distort its pixels. A hyperspectral cube is therefore not simply a photograph with extra bands. Exam-style critique “The UAV produces 500 bands, so pasture protein is measured directly and accurately.” Incorrect: protein is inferred through calibration to reference chemistry, and performance must be independently evaluated on unseen locations and conditions."}, {"title": "03. Flight planning: spatial coverage and consistent acquisition", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 6; recording ~01:35–01:42</p><h3>Source workflow</h3><p>The guest uses UgCS mission planning with an ArduCopter X8 and a hyperspectral payload. Parallel flight lines, at least 30% swath overlap and spline turns are specified in the slides.</p><h3>Trade-offs</h3><p>Lower flight height can improve nominal ground sampling but reduces coverage and increases flight time. Swath overlap helps avoid gaps and allows inspection of strip agreement. Wind, platform motion, cloud changes and solar geometry can change the measured signal within a single flight.</p><h3>Practical design</h3><p>Plan the area, terrain clearance, takeoff/landing, expected battery endurance, overlap, lighting window and reference-panel acquisition before flying. Record flight logs and sensor settings. These are supplementary operational explanations consistent with the slide workflow.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>If a mission yields gaps and striping, do not immediately “fix” it by interpolation. Inspect flight-line spacing, overlap, motion, sensor timing, geometry and illumination. Interpolation may conceal missing observations rather than restore measured data.</p>", "text": "Slide 6; recording ~01:35–01:42 Source workflow The guest uses UgCS mission planning with an ArduCopter X8 and a hyperspectral payload. Parallel flight lines, at least 30% swath overlap and spline turns are specified in the slides. Trade-offs Lower flight height can improve nominal ground sampling but reduces coverage and increases flight time. Swath overlap helps avoid gaps and allows inspection of strip agreement. Wind, platform motion, cloud changes and solar geometry can change the measured signal within a single flight. Practical design Plan the area, terrain clearance, takeoff/landing, expected battery endurance, overlap, lighting window and reference-panel acquisition before flying. Record flight logs and sensor settings. These are supplementary operational explanations consistent with the slide workflow. Exam response If a mission yields gaps and striping, do not immediately “fix” it by interpolation. Inspect flight-line spacing, overlap, motion, sensor timing, geometry and illumination. Interpolation may conceal missing observations rather than restore measured data."}, {"title": "04. Direct georeferencing: GNSS, IMU and sensor geometry", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 7; recording ~01:43–01:50</p><h3>Recorded pipeline</h3><p>The slides specify an Emlid Reach RTK base station logging raw GNSS at 1 Hz and onboard GNSS/IMU measurements at up to 200 Hz. POSPac produces a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for each flight.</p><h3>Geometric logic</h3><p>Position specifies where the sensor is; attitude specifies its orientation. The viewing ray must also account for the lever arm between navigation unit and camera, boresight offsets and camera geometry. Ground location is obtained by intersecting a pixel viewing ray with an elevation surface.</p><h3>Why no GCP does not mean no error</h3><p>Direct georeferencing can avoid the need for ground-control points in the processing workflow, but independent checkpoints remain useful to quantify accuracy. Errors in attitude or DSM height may dominate despite precise RTK positioning.</p><h3>Worked reasoning</h3><p>At 120 m above ground, a 1-degree angular pointing error corresponds to approximately 120 tan(1 degree) = 2.09 m horizontal displacement for a near-nadir ray. This simplified geometry illustrates why attitude calibration matters even with centimetre-scale GNSS positions.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>Explain why two overlapping strips can disagree spatially despite a high-quality base station: boresight, timing, IMU alignment, sensor model and DSM error all influence geolocation.</p>", "text": "Slide 7; recording ~01:43–01:50 Recorded pipeline The slides specify an Emlid Reach RTK base station logging raw GNSS at 1 Hz and onboard GNSS/IMU measurements at up to 200 Hz. POSPac produces a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for each flight. Geometric logic Position specifies where the sensor is; attitude specifies its orientation. The viewing ray must also account for the lever arm between navigation unit and camera, boresight offsets and camera geometry. Ground location is obtained by intersecting a pixel viewing ray with an elevation surface. Why no GCP does not mean no error Direct georeferencing can avoid the need for ground-control points in the processing workflow, but independent checkpoints remain useful to quantify accuracy. Errors in attitude or DSM height may dominate despite precise RTK positioning. Worked reasoning At 120 m above ground, a 1-degree angular pointing error corresponds to approximately 120 tan(1 degree) = 2.09 m horizontal displacement for a near-nadir ray. This simplified geometry illustrates why attitude calibration matters even with centimetre-scale GNSS positions. Exam response Explain why two overlapping strips can disagree spatially despite a high-quality base station: boresight, timing, IMU alignment, sensor model and DSM error all influence geolocation."}, {"title": "05. Raw counts, navigation and radiance calibration", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 8; recording ~01:44–01:55</p><h3>Recorded sequence</h3><p>The HySpex NAV workflow merges raw detector frames with navigation information; subsequent processing creates a radiance cube. The guest explicitly discusses the starting measurements as detector counts, which must be related to physical radiation.</p><h3>Measurement distinction</h3><p>Digital number (DN) is a coded detector response; radiance has physical units, often W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1; reflectance is a dimensionless surface response relative to illumination under defined geometry. Converting DN to radiance is not the same as removing atmospheric effects.</p><h3>Supplementary calibration equation</h3><p>A simplified linear calibration is L_lambda = gain_lambda × DN_lambda + offset_lambda. Real calibration may also require dark-current, nonlinearity, bad-pixel, stray-light and spectral calibration corrections. The exact software implementation is not fully specified in the slide.</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>A model trained on raw DN may respond to instrument gain or exposure settings rather than pasture biochemistry. Calibration makes cross-line and cross-date measurements more interpretable, although further reflectance correction is needed.</p>", "text": "Slide 8; recording ~01:44–01:55 Recorded sequence The HySpex NAV workflow merges raw detector frames with navigation information; subsequent processing creates a radiance cube. The guest explicitly discusses the starting measurements as detector counts, which must be related to physical radiation. Measurement distinction Digital number (DN) is a coded detector response; radiance has physical units, often W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1; reflectance is a dimensionless surface response relative to illumination under defined geometry. Converting DN to radiance is not the same as removing atmospheric effects. Supplementary calibration equation A simplified linear calibration is L_lambda = gain_lambda × DN_lambda + offset_lambda. Real calibration may also require dark-current, nonlinearity, bad-pixel, stray-light and spectral calibration corrections. The exact software implementation is not fully specified in the slide. Why it matters A model trained on raw DN may respond to instrument gain or exposure settings rather than pasture biochemistry. Calibration makes cross-line and cross-date measurements more interpretable, although further reflectance correction is needed."}, {"title": "06. Orthorectification with PARGE and a DSM", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 9; recording around 01:48–01:55</p><h3>Recorded method</h3><p>The slide names PARGE (Parametric Orthorectification), using radiance, SBET, boresight angles, DSM and camera model. The approach ray-casts sensor pixels to ground positions rather than relying solely on image tie points.</p><h3>Conceptual mechanics</h3><p>For each detector sample, calculate the viewing ray in camera coordinates, rotate it into the map reference frame using attitude and boresight, then intersect it with the DSM. Regrid samples to a target map. Resampling is a separate decision that can affect spectral values and sharpness.</p><h3>Geometric versus spectral quality</h3><p>Orthorectification can correct location and terrain displacement but does not convert radiance to surface reflectance or prove that spectra are chemically meaningful. Verify spatial accuracy using independent checkpoints and inspect seams and overlap consistency.</p><h3>Common failure</h3><p>A poor DSM shifts the ray intersection, especially in steep terrain or off-nadir viewing. If canopy surfaces are relevant, a bare-earth DEM may differ from a DSM; the suitable surface depends on geometry and intended output.</p>", "text": "Slide 9; recording around 01:48–01:55 Recorded method The slide names PARGE (Parametric Orthorectification), using radiance, SBET, boresight angles, DSM and camera model. The approach ray-casts sensor pixels to ground positions rather than relying solely on image tie points. Conceptual mechanics For each detector sample, calculate the viewing ray in camera coordinates, rotate it into the map reference frame using attitude and boresight, then intersect it with the DSM. Regrid samples to a target map. Resampling is a separate decision that can affect spectral values and sharpness. Geometric versus spectral quality Orthorectification can correct location and terrain displacement but does not convert radiance to surface reflectance or prove that spectra are chemically meaningful. Verify spatial accuracy using independent checkpoints and inspect seams and overlap consistency. Common failure A poor DSM shifts the ray intersection, especially in steep terrain or off-nadir viewing. If canopy surfaces are relevant, a bare-earth DEM may differ from a DSM; the suitable surface depends on geometry and intended output."}, {"title": "07. Atmospheric and reflectance processing", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 10; recording ~01:54–02:00</p><h3>Recorded tools</h3><p>The slide identifies DROACOR for low-altitude atmospheric correction from radiance to surface reflectance, with MODTRAN lookup tables; BREFCOR is included in the correction chain. The guest describes scene-derived atmospheric information. The slide highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm.</p><h3>Physics</h3><p>Measured radiance is influenced by illumination, atmospheric absorption and scattering, and surface directional reflectance. Surface reflectance correction seeks a more comparable representation of the vegetation signal. The correction model still relies on assumptions about atmosphere and illumination.</p><h3>Band quality</h3><p>Very low-transmission channels around strong atmospheric water absorption may be unreliable for surface retrieval. Inspect bad bands, striping, spectral discontinuities and physically implausible reflectance before modelling.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>“Drone flight height is low, so atmospheric correction is unnecessary.” Not necessarily: short path length reduces some effects, but illumination variation, water vapour, aerosols and directional reflectance may still matter for quantitative spectral prediction.</p>", "text": "Slide 10; recording ~01:54–02:00 Recorded tools The slide identifies DROACOR for low-altitude atmospheric correction from radiance to surface reflectance, with MODTRAN lookup tables; BREFCOR is included in the correction chain. The guest describes scene-derived atmospheric information. The slide highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm. Physics Measured radiance is influenced by illumination, atmospheric absorption and scattering, and surface directional reflectance. Surface reflectance correction seeks a more comparable representation of the vegetation signal. The correction model still relies on assumptions about atmosphere and illumination. Band quality Very low-transmission channels around strong atmospheric water absorption may be unreliable for surface retrieval. Inspect bad bands, striping, spectral discontinuities and physically implausible reflectance before modelling. Exam response “Drone flight height is low, so atmospheric correction is unnecessary.” Not necessarily: short path length reduces some effects, but illumination variation, water vapour, aerosols and directional reflectance may still matter for quantitative spectral prediction."}, {"title": "08. The reference target: CP and DMD", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 11–12; recording from ~02:00 onward</p><h3>Case-study variables</h3><p>CP and DMD are continuous nutrition measurements. The slide indicates maintenance around CP 7% and increased risk below 5%; these are context-specific decision thresholds rather than a universal feeding guideline.</p><h3>Ground truth is a measurement protocol</h3><p>A field quadrat and its laboratory assay must represent the same material and time as the image footprint. GPS mismatch, mixed vegetation, grazing after sampling or laboratory error create label uncertainty that machine learning cannot eliminate.</p><h3>Spectral inference</h3><p>The regression learns an empirical relation between corrected reflectance and reference CP/DMD. Such a model may capture useful chemistry-related spectral patterns but can also exploit correlated species, soil or season signals. Testing outside the training domain is essential.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>If a model predicts “CP” on bare soil, it is extrapolating outside its defined physical target. A pasture mask is a necessary stage, and errors in that mask propagate into the nutritional map.</p>", "text": "Slides 11–12; recording from ~02:00 onward Case-study variables CP and DMD are continuous nutrition measurements. The slide indicates maintenance around CP 7% and increased risk below 5%; these are context-specific decision thresholds rather than a universal feeding guideline. Ground truth is a measurement protocol A field quadrat and its laboratory assay must represent the same material and time as the image footprint. GPS mismatch, mixed vegetation, grazing after sampling or laboratory error create label uncertainty that machine learning cannot eliminate. Spectral inference The regression learns an empirical relation between corrected reflectance and reference CP/DMD. Such a model may capture useful chemistry-related spectral patterns but can also exploit correlated species, soil or season signals. Testing outside the training domain is essential. Exam response If a model predicts “CP” on bare soil, it is extrapolating outside its defined physical target. A pasture mask is a necessary stage, and errors in that mask propagate into the nutritional map."}, {"title": "09. Pasture classification before regression", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 13 and 15</p><h3>Source method</h3><p>A seven-class neural-network classification separates pasture from other surface types. The slides report 98.6% overall accuracy and describe four dense blocks of 512, 256, 128 and 64 units with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4, followed by seven-class softmax.</p><h3>Why overall accuracy is limited</h3><p>Overall accuracy can be dominated by abundant classes. For the application, false pasture labels cause invalid CP predictions, while missed pasture reduces coverage. Report pasture-specific producer and user accuracy, reference sample design and spatial patterns of error.</p><h3>Probabilities versus confidence</h3><p>Softmax scores are model outputs, not automatically calibrated probabilities of correctness. Thresholds and uncertainty should be assessed using independent reference samples.</p><h3>Propagation</h3><p>The final CP map depends on both a categorical mask and a quantitative regression. A low-error regression does not compensate for widespread false-positive pasture pixels.</p>", "text": "Slides 13 and 15 Source method A seven-class neural-network classification separates pasture from other surface types. The slides report 98.6% overall accuracy and describe four dense blocks of 512, 256, 128 and 64 units with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4, followed by seven-class softmax. Why overall accuracy is limited Overall accuracy can be dominated by abundant classes. For the application, false pasture labels cause invalid CP predictions, while missed pasture reduces coverage. Report pasture-specific producer and user accuracy, reference sample design and spatial patterns of error. Probabilities versus confidence Softmax scores are model outputs, not automatically calibrated probabilities of correctness. Thresholds and uncertainty should be assessed using independent reference samples. Propagation The final CP map depends on both a categorical mask and a quantitative regression. A low-error regression does not compensate for widespread false-positive pasture pixels."}, {"title": "10. TabPFN and what the model learns", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 16</p><h3>Source description</h3><p>The deck presents TabPFN as a pre-trained tabular transformer trained on many synthetic tasks; a labelled training dataset is provided as context to predict unseen records without conventional per-task gradient-descent training.</p><h3>Why this may help</h3><p>The model can learn nonlinear relationships between reflectance-derived predictors and chemistry measurements without training a large network from scratch on a limited field dataset.</p><h3>Scientific caution</h3><p>Pretraining does not establish domain transfer to a new pasture species, soil, property or season. Evaluate against simpler baselines and independent spatial holdouts. The deck does not justify claiming that TabPFN is universally superior to Random Forest, PLS regression or other approaches.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>Method choice should be defended by sample size, predictor dimensionality, interpretability, transferability and out-of-sample error, not novelty alone.</p>", "text": "Slide 16 Source description The deck presents TabPFN as a pre-trained tabular transformer trained on many synthetic tasks; a labelled training dataset is provided as context to predict unseen records without conventional per-task gradient-descent training. Why this may help The model can learn nonlinear relationships between reflectance-derived predictors and chemistry measurements without training a large network from scratch on a limited field dataset. Scientific caution Pretraining does not establish domain transfer to a new pasture species, soil, property or season. Evaluate against simpler baselines and independent spatial holdouts. The deck does not justify claiming that TabPFN is universally superior to Random Forest, PLS regression or other approaches. Exam response Method choice should be defended by sample size, predictor dimensionality, interpretability, transferability and out-of-sample error, not novelty alone."}, {"title": "11. Spatial leakage and leave-one-property-out evaluation", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 17</p><h3>Source warning</h3><p>The slides explicitly reject random 80/20 pixel splitting as potentially misleading because adjacent pixels are spatially correlated. They use leave-one-property-out evaluation to hold out entire sites.</p><h3>Why leakage matters</h3><p>Neighbouring pixels share management, species, soil, illumination and often reference labels. Random splits can place near-duplicates in train and test sets, inflating apparent generalisation.</p><h3>Better design</h3><p>Hold out whole properties, then rotate the held-out property. Report the distribution of performance across properties and, where possible, across seasons. Keep hyperparameter tuning inside the training portion of each evaluation fold.</p><h3>Remaining uncertainty</h3><p>Property-level validation tests a stronger form of spatial transfer but may not establish transfer to new continents, sensors, years or management systems. Match the validation unit to the intended deployment.</p>", "text": "Slide 17 Source warning The slides explicitly reject random 80/20 pixel splitting as potentially misleading because adjacent pixels are spatially correlated. They use leave-one-property-out evaluation to hold out entire sites. Why leakage matters Neighbouring pixels share management, species, soil, illumination and often reference labels. Random splits can place near-duplicates in train and test sets, inflating apparent generalisation. Better design Hold out whole properties, then rotate the held-out property. Report the distribution of performance across properties and, where possible, across seasons. Keep hyperparameter tuning inside the training portion of each evaluation fold. Remaining uncertainty Property-level validation tests a stronger form of spatial transfer but may not establish transfer to new continents, sensors, years or management systems. Match the validation unit to the intended deployment."}, {"title": "12. Continuous model accuracy: interpreting R² and RMSE", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 18</p><h3>Reported slide figures</h3><p>The slide reports CP/DMD R² values of 0.76/0.47 for leaf lab NIR, 0.95/0.88 for UAV hyperspectral, and 0.92/0.73 for Sentinel-2. It also shows a Sentinel-2 CP RMSE of 0.39 percentage points. Treat these as the presentation’s reported results; their cross-scale comparability depends on test design.</p><h3>Metric meaning</h3><p>R² describes explained variation relative to a specified baseline; it is not the percentage of predictions that are correct. RMSE = sqrt(mean((prediction − observation)²)) has the same units as CP or DMD. Bias = mean(prediction − observation) shows systematic over- or underprediction.</p><h3>Worked calculation</h3><p>For reference CP [6,8,10]% and predictions [6.5,7.5,11]%, errors are [0.5,−0.5,1.0] percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points; bias = 0.333. This is a supplementary calculation, not a reported case-study result.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>Ask whether reported scores use independent properties, what the CP range is, whether performance changes in low-protein pasture, and whether uncertainties are displayed to the user.</p>", "text": "Slide 18 Reported slide figures The slide reports CP/DMD R² values of 0.76/0.47 for leaf lab NIR, 0.95/0.88 for UAV hyperspectral, and 0.92/0.73 for Sentinel-2. It also shows a Sentinel-2 CP RMSE of 0.39 percentage points. Treat these as the presentation’s reported results; their cross-scale comparability depends on test design. Metric meaning R² describes explained variation relative to a specified baseline; it is not the percentage of predictions that are correct. RMSE = sqrt(mean((prediction − observation)²)) has the same units as CP or DMD. Bias = mean(prediction − observation) shows systematic over- or underprediction. Worked calculation For reference CP [6,8,10]% and predictions [6.5,7.5,11]%, errors are [0.5,−0.5,1.0] percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points; bias = 0.333. This is a supplementary calculation, not a reported case-study result. Exam response Ask whether reported scores use independent properties, what the CP range is, whether performance changes in low-protein pasture, and whether uncertainties are displayed to the user."}, {"title": "13. Upscaling: leaf to UAV to Sentinel-2", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slide 14</p><h3>Recorded hierarchy</h3><p>The slides show approximately 1 m² field quadrats, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 observations, using UAV information to bridge field chemistry and larger-scale satellite monitoring.</p><h3>Change of support</h3><p>A 10 m pixel covers 100 m². A 1 m² quadrat or an 8 cm pixel does not represent the whole 10 m footprint automatically. Mixed pasture, shrubs, shadows and soil create nonlinear aggregation effects.</p><h3>Spectral mismatch</h3><p>A hyperspectral channel and a broad Sentinel-2 band do not measure the same wavelength interval. Proper transfer may require integrating hyperspectral reflectance over satellite spectral response functions and re-estimating the model at satellite support.</p><h3>Independent satellite evaluation</h3><p>Validate the satellite predictions at the support of the satellite product, not solely against the UAV model used to create its labels. Otherwise the final accuracy can inherit untested errors from the intermediate model.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>“Resample the UAV CP raster to 10 m and the model is validated for Sentinel-2.” Incorrect: resampling changes grid support, not the measurement physics or validation domain.</p>", "text": "Slide 14 Recorded hierarchy The slides show approximately 1 m² field quadrats, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 observations, using UAV information to bridge field chemistry and larger-scale satellite monitoring. Change of support A 10 m pixel covers 100 m². A 1 m² quadrat or an 8 cm pixel does not represent the whole 10 m footprint automatically. Mixed pasture, shrubs, shadows and soil create nonlinear aggregation effects. Spectral mismatch A hyperspectral channel and a broad Sentinel-2 band do not measure the same wavelength interval. Proper transfer may require integrating hyperspectral reflectance over satellite spectral response functions and re-estimating the model at satellite support. Independent satellite evaluation Validate the satellite predictions at the support of the satellite product, not solely against the UAV model used to create its labels. Otherwise the final accuracy can inherit untested errors from the intermediate model. Exam response “Resample the UAV CP raster to 10 m and the model is validated for Sentinel-2.” Incorrect: resampling changes grid support, not the measurement physics or validation domain."}, {"title": "14. Seasonality, rainfall and operational delivery", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 19–20</p><h3>Reported pattern</h3><p>The slides describe CP increasing after rain, with a peak approximately two to three weeks later, and DMD changing less dramatically. The case-study numbers include CP around 10–12% in wet conditions versus 4–6% in dry conditions.</p><h3>Temporal interpretation</h3><p>A correlation with rainfall is useful for management but does not alone prove a universal causal relationship. Vegetation species, grazing intensity, soils and management can change the response.</p><h3>Delivery system</h3><p>The deck shows a Pasture Quality Monitor web app, paddock selection and API access. Operational users need date, valid-data coverage, prediction uncertainty, spatial resolution and known domain limits alongside the map.</p><h3>Exam response</h3><p>An elegant web map does not establish scientific accuracy. A decision-support tool should communicate what was measured, what was predicted and how reliable the prediction is.</p>", "text": "Slides 19–20 Reported pattern The slides describe CP increasing after rain, with a peak approximately two to three weeks later, and DMD changing less dramatically. The case-study numbers include CP around 10–12% in wet conditions versus 4–6% in dry conditions. Temporal interpretation A correlation with rainfall is useful for management but does not alone prove a universal causal relationship. Vegetation species, grazing intensity, soils and management can change the response. Delivery system The deck shows a Pasture Quality Monitor web app, paddock selection and API access. Operational users need date, valid-data coverage, prediction uncertainty, spatial resolution and known domain limits alongside the map. Exam response An elegant web map does not establish scientific accuracy. A decision-support tool should communicate what was measured, what was predicted and how reliable the prediction is."}, {"title": "15. Full worked critique: flawed UAV-to-satellite workflow", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original exam-style scenario based on source methods</p><h3>Scenario</h3><p>A team flies one sunny property, converts raw DN directly to CP using a neural network, trains and tests on randomly selected adjacent pixels, resizes 8 cm predictions to 10 m, and reports a precise national CP map without uncertainty.</p><h3>Problem definition</h3><p>CP is not directly observed; it requires temporally and spatially matched laboratory chemistry. National extrapolation from one property is a severe domain-shift problem.</p><h3>Acquisition and preprocessing</h3><p>Require adequate overlap, stable illumination, reliable trajectory/boresight, DSM-based orthorectification, radiance calibration, reflectance correction and quality screening. Each step has a distinct role.</p><h3>Model design</h3><p>Separate pasture from non-pasture and validate the mask; train regression on field references with appropriate feature screening and transparent model comparison.</p><h3>Validation</h3><p>Random pixel splits leak spatial information. Hold out properties, ideally seasons and ecological domains; report RMSE, bias, R², coverage and uncertainty by CP range.</p><h3>Upscaling</h3><p>Satellite transfer requires matching spectral responses, spatial support and timing, with independent satellite-scale validation. Upsampling or downsampling alone is not enough.</p><h3>Defended solution</h3><p>Build a hierarchical field–UAV–satellite calibration pipeline with independent holdouts, reproducible correction and uncertainty reporting; limit operational claims to validated conditions.</p>", "text": "Original exam-style scenario based on source methods Scenario A team flies one sunny property, converts raw DN directly to CP using a neural network, trains and tests on randomly selected adjacent pixels, resizes 8 cm predictions to 10 m, and reports a precise national CP map without uncertainty. Problem definition CP is not directly observed; it requires temporally and spatially matched laboratory chemistry. National extrapolation from one property is a severe domain-shift problem. Acquisition and preprocessing Require adequate overlap, stable illumination, reliable trajectory/boresight, DSM-based orthorectification, radiance calibration, reflectance correction and quality screening. Each step has a distinct role. Model design Separate pasture from non-pasture and validate the mask; train regression on field references with appropriate feature screening and transparent model comparison. Validation Random pixel splits leak spatial information. Hold out properties, ideally seasons and ecological domains; report RMSE, bias, R², coverage and uncertainty by CP range. Upscaling Satellite transfer requires matching spectral responses, spatial support and timing, with independent satellite-scale validation. Upsampling or downsampling alone is not enough. Defended solution Build a hierarchical field–UAV–satellite calibration pipeline with independent holdouts, reproducible correction and uncertainty reporting; limit operational claims to validated conditions."}, {"title": "16. Self-test with model answers", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original study questions</p><h3>Question 1</h3><p>Why does direct georeferencing need both GNSS and IMU? Answer: position alone does not locate a pixel ray; orientation and camera/boresight geometry determine its ground intersection.</p><h3>Question 2</h3><p>What is the difference between radiance and reflectance? Answer: radiance is measured directional energy; reflectance represents surface response relative to illumination and needs correction assumptions.</p><h3>Question 3</h3><p>Why might water-vapour bands be excluded? Answer: strong atmospheric absorption yields low-transmission/noisy surface information.</p><h3>Question 4</h3><p>Why is 98.6% mask accuracy insufficient? Answer: overall accuracy can hide important pasture-specific omission and commission errors.</p><h3>Question 5</h3><p>What does leave-one-property-out test? Answer: generalisation to an independent property, reducing spatial leakage.</p><h3>Question 6</h3><p>Why is R²=0.95 not “95% accurate”? Answer: it is a variance-explanation statistic, not a percent-correct measure.</p><h3>Question 7</h3><p>Why can’t a 10 m Sentinel-2 pixel be treated as one 8 cm UAV observation? Answer: different spatial support, mixed pixels, band response and acquisition conditions.</p><h3>Question 8</h3><p>What would you report for an operational CP map? Answer: reference protocol, processing provenance, independent RMSE/bias, spatial/seasonal transfer, coverage and uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Original study questions Question 1 Why does direct georeferencing need both GNSS and IMU? Answer: position alone does not locate a pixel ray; orientation and camera/boresight geometry determine its ground intersection. Question 2 What is the difference between radiance and reflectance? Answer: radiance is measured directional energy; reflectance represents surface response relative to illumination and needs correction assumptions. Question 3 Why might water-vapour bands be excluded? Answer: strong atmospheric absorption yields low-transmission/noisy surface information. Question 4 Why is 98.6% mask accuracy insufficient? Answer: overall accuracy can hide important pasture-specific omission and commission errors. Question 5 What does leave-one-property-out test? Answer: generalisation to an independent property, reducing spatial leakage. Question 6 Why is R²=0.95 not “95% accurate”? Answer: it is a variance-explanation statistic, not a percent-correct measure. Question 7 Why can’t a 10 m Sentinel-2 pixel be treated as one 8 cm UAV observation? Answer: different spatial support, mixed pixels, band response and acquisition conditions. Question 8 What would you report for an operational CP map? Answer: reference protocol, processing provenance, independent RMSE/bias, spatial/seasonal transfer, coverage and uncertainty."}, {"title": "17. Source notes and boundaries", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 2–20; 19 August VTT</p><h3>Primary sources</h3><p>geom3001_7001_L3_P2_HyperspectralUAVProcessing_pastureQuality_v3.pdf (21 pages) and GMT20260819-013058_Recording.transcript.vtt. Slide references are physical PDF pages. Spoken recording timestamps refer to the complete Zoom VTT timeline.</p><h3>Scope</h3><p>The 19 August recording includes guest material beyond the UAV slide deck. This chapter focuses on the Barnetson hyperspectral processing case study, rather than claiming to reproduce every minute of the session.</p><h3>Supplementary explanations</h3><p>The angular error example, calibration equation, RMSE calculation, exam critique and some methodological qualifications are additional teaching explanations. They are distinguished from source-reported case-study numbers.</p><h3>Sequence correction</h3><p>The separate 60-page preprocessing deck is internally titled Lecture 4, but it is not the spoken topic of the 19 August Week 4 guest recording. It should be treated as its own course source and matched to its correct lecture recording before attributing spoken explanations.</p>", "text": "Slides 2–20; 19 August VTT Primary sources geom3001_7001_L3_P2_HyperspectralUAVProcessing_pastureQuality_v3.pdf (21 pages) and GMT20260819-013058_Recording.transcript.vtt. Slide references are physical PDF pages. Spoken recording timestamps refer to the complete Zoom VTT timeline. Scope The 19 August recording includes guest material beyond the UAV slide deck. This chapter focuses on the Barnetson hyperspectral processing case study, rather than claiming to reproduce every minute of the session. Supplementary explanations The angular error example, calibration equation, RMSE calculation, exam critique and some methodological qualifications are additional teaching explanations. They are distinguished from source-reported case-study numbers. Sequence correction The separate 60-page preprocessing deck is internally titled Lecture 4, but it is not the spoken topic of the 19 August Week 4 guest recording. It should be treated as its own course source and matched to its correct lecture recording before attributing spoken explanations."}, {"title": "Transcript evidence anchors", "html": "<p><strong>01:32:09</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: From hyperspectral UAV capture to paddock-scale nutrition products, which are used for grazing management.</p><p><strong>01:32:46</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on.</p><p><strong>01:44:57</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: So, now we want to geometrically tag everything. So, getting that raw image cube.</p><p><strong>01:46:33</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: When we go to geometrically, correct, they&#x27;re both in the same spectral resolution.</p><p><strong>01:54:51</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: is a bit of software called Drone at Core. It&#x27;s a drone-specific atmospheric correction tool.</p><p><strong>01:54:58</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: And unlike satellite atmospheric corrections, it retrieves the aerosol and water columns from the image itself.</p><p><strong>01:29:46</strong> — Audio shared by Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: From that, computer models learn to read the protein and quality of pasture, straight from a satellite passing overhead. Just as important is how much feed is out there. We paired old-fa</p><p><strong>01:32:46</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on.</p><p><strong>02:02:25</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: We evaluate with stratified, like Peter did, a five-fold cross validation, and the measured overall accuracy on the held-out stat is 98%.</p><p><strong>02:03:56</strong> — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env &amp; Science: The single biggest mistake in a lot of these remote sensing machine learning papers is an optimistic validation. You shuffle your pixels, take a random 80-20 split, and you report the R-squared on the 2</p>", "text": "01:32:09 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: From hyperspectral UAV capture to paddock-scale nutrition products, which are used for grazing management. 01:32:46 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on. 01:44:57 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: So, now we want to geometrically tag everything. So, getting that raw image cube. 01:46:33 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: When we go to geometrically, correct, they're both in the same spectral resolution. 01:54:51 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: is a bit of software called Drone at Core. It's a drone-specific atmospheric correction tool. 01:54:58 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: And unlike satellite atmospheric corrections, it retrieves the aerosol and water columns from the image itself. 01:29:46 — Audio shared by Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: From that, computer models learn to read the protein and quality of pasture, straight from a satellite passing overhead. Just as important is how much feed is out there. We paired old-fa 01:32:46 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on. 02:02:25 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: We evaluate with stratified, like Peter did, a five-fold cross validation, and the measured overall accuracy on the held-out stat is 98%. 02:03:56 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: The single biggest mistake in a lot of these remote sensing machine learning papers is an optimistic validation. You shuffle your pixels, take a random 80-20 split, and you report the R-squared on the 2"}], "text": ["Source integrity The matching 19 August VTT confirms the Week 4 guest lecture. The slide deck filename is L3_P2, but the presentation corresponds to the Week 4 recording. Supplementary examples are clearly distinguished from slide-reported findings.", "01. Source reconciliation and the scientific problem Slides 2–4; recording 00:25–00:30, 01:32 onward What the source actually is This is the 19 August Week 4 guest lecture, introduced by Will Woodgate and presented by Jason Barnetson. The guest describes the end-to-end process of converting UAV hyperspectral measurements into paddock-scale nutrition products. Although the file name contains L3_P2, the matching spoken lecture is Week 4; this source should not be described as a Week 3 lecture. The inferential challenge Cattle nutrition requires estimates of crude protein (CP) and dry matter digestibility (DMD), but an imaging spectrometer observes wavelength-dependent energy rather than protein molecules directly. Lab assays supply the reference variable; a statistical model then predicts nutrition from spectral and spatial predictors. This distinction determines what can be claimed about accuracy. Why this is a systems problem The final pasture map is only as reliable as its flight geometry, radiometric calibration, surface reflectance, pasture/non-pasture mask, field chemistry, statistical modelling and scale transfer. The case study is therefore ideal for the lecturer’s exam emphasis on critiquing complete workflows rather than naming algorithms.", "02. Sensor design: VNIR, SWIR and the pushbroom acquisition Slides 5 and 8; recording ~01:32–01:44 Instrument specifications The slides describe a HySpex Mjolnir VS-620 system with a VNIR component (200 bands, about 400–1000 nm) and a SWIR component (288 bands, about 950–2500 nm). Band spacings are approximately 3 nm and 5.4 nm, respectively. Slide 5 rounds this to about 500 bands and states roughly 8 cm ground sampling at 120 m. These are case-study specifications, not a universal capability of all UAV hyperspectral sensors. Why narrow bands are useful Spectral absorption and scattering vary with pigment, water, leaf chemistry, canopy structure and soil exposure. Narrow channels can resolve features that broad multispectral bands average together. But high dimensionality brings correlated bands, noisy wavelengths and a need for well-designed reference data. How pushbroom imaging works A pushbroom instrument measures a line across track at each instant. Aircraft movement builds the second spatial dimension. Each line has a timestamp, position and orientation; errors in timing, pitch, roll, yaw or trajectory can displace or distort its pixels. A hyperspectral cube is therefore not simply a photograph with extra bands. Exam-style critique “The UAV produces 500 bands, so pasture protein is measured directly and accurately.” Incorrect: protein is inferred through calibration to reference chemistry, and performance must be independently evaluated on unseen locations and conditions.", "03. Flight planning: spatial coverage and consistent acquisition Slide 6; recording ~01:35–01:42 Source workflow The guest uses UgCS mission planning with an ArduCopter X8 and a hyperspectral payload. Parallel flight lines, at least 30% swath overlap and spline turns are specified in the slides. Trade-offs Lower flight height can improve nominal ground sampling but reduces coverage and increases flight time. Swath overlap helps avoid gaps and allows inspection of strip agreement. Wind, platform motion, cloud changes and solar geometry can change the measured signal within a single flight. Practical design Plan the area, terrain clearance, takeoff/landing, expected battery endurance, overlap, lighting window and reference-panel acquisition before flying. Record flight logs and sensor settings. These are supplementary operational explanations consistent with the slide workflow. Exam response If a mission yields gaps and striping, do not immediately “fix” it by interpolation. Inspect flight-line spacing, overlap, motion, sensor timing, geometry and illumination. Interpolation may conceal missing observations rather than restore measured data.", "04. Direct georeferencing: GNSS, IMU and sensor geometry Slide 7; recording ~01:43–01:50 Recorded pipeline The slides specify an Emlid Reach RTK base station logging raw GNSS at 1 Hz and onboard GNSS/IMU measurements at up to 200 Hz. POSPac produces a smoothed best estimate of trajectory (SBET) for each flight. Geometric logic Position specifies where the sensor is; attitude specifies its orientation. The viewing ray must also account for the lever arm between navigation unit and camera, boresight offsets and camera geometry. Ground location is obtained by intersecting a pixel viewing ray with an elevation surface. Why no GCP does not mean no error Direct georeferencing can avoid the need for ground-control points in the processing workflow, but independent checkpoints remain useful to quantify accuracy. Errors in attitude or DSM height may dominate despite precise RTK positioning. Worked reasoning At 120 m above ground, a 1-degree angular pointing error corresponds to approximately 120 tan(1 degree) = 2.09 m horizontal displacement for a near-nadir ray. This simplified geometry illustrates why attitude calibration matters even with centimetre-scale GNSS positions. Exam response Explain why two overlapping strips can disagree spatially despite a high-quality base station: boresight, timing, IMU alignment, sensor model and DSM error all influence geolocation.", "05. Raw counts, navigation and radiance calibration Slide 8; recording ~01:44–01:55 Recorded sequence The HySpex NAV workflow merges raw detector frames with navigation information; subsequent processing creates a radiance cube. The guest explicitly discusses the starting measurements as detector counts, which must be related to physical radiation. Measurement distinction Digital number (DN) is a coded detector response; radiance has physical units, often W m^-2 sr^-1 um^-1; reflectance is a dimensionless surface response relative to illumination under defined geometry. Converting DN to radiance is not the same as removing atmospheric effects. Supplementary calibration equation A simplified linear calibration is L_lambda = gain_lambda × DN_lambda + offset_lambda. Real calibration may also require dark-current, nonlinearity, bad-pixel, stray-light and spectral calibration corrections. The exact software implementation is not fully specified in the slide. Why it matters A model trained on raw DN may respond to instrument gain or exposure settings rather than pasture biochemistry. Calibration makes cross-line and cross-date measurements more interpretable, although further reflectance correction is needed.", "06. Orthorectification with PARGE and a DSM Slide 9; recording around 01:48–01:55 Recorded method The slide names PARGE (Parametric Orthorectification), using radiance, SBET, boresight angles, DSM and camera model. The approach ray-casts sensor pixels to ground positions rather than relying solely on image tie points. Conceptual mechanics For each detector sample, calculate the viewing ray in camera coordinates, rotate it into the map reference frame using attitude and boresight, then intersect it with the DSM. Regrid samples to a target map. Resampling is a separate decision that can affect spectral values and sharpness. Geometric versus spectral quality Orthorectification can correct location and terrain displacement but does not convert radiance to surface reflectance or prove that spectra are chemically meaningful. Verify spatial accuracy using independent checkpoints and inspect seams and overlap consistency. Common failure A poor DSM shifts the ray intersection, especially in steep terrain or off-nadir viewing. If canopy surfaces are relevant, a bare-earth DEM may differ from a DSM; the suitable surface depends on geometry and intended output.", "07. Atmospheric and reflectance processing Slide 10; recording ~01:54–02:00 Recorded tools The slide identifies DROACOR for low-altitude atmospheric correction from radiance to surface reflectance, with MODTRAN lookup tables; BREFCOR is included in the correction chain. The guest describes scene-derived atmospheric information. The slide highlights water-vapour absorption regions near 1400 and 1900 nm. Physics Measured radiance is influenced by illumination, atmospheric absorption and scattering, and surface directional reflectance. Surface reflectance correction seeks a more comparable representation of the vegetation signal. The correction model still relies on assumptions about atmosphere and illumination. Band quality Very low-transmission channels around strong atmospheric water absorption may be unreliable for surface retrieval. Inspect bad bands, striping, spectral discontinuities and physically implausible reflectance before modelling. Exam response “Drone flight height is low, so atmospheric correction is unnecessary.” Not necessarily: short path length reduces some effects, but illumination variation, water vapour, aerosols and directional reflectance may still matter for quantitative spectral prediction.", "08. The reference target: CP and DMD Slides 11–12; recording from ~02:00 onward Case-study variables CP and DMD are continuous nutrition measurements. The slide indicates maintenance around CP 7% and increased risk below 5%; these are context-specific decision thresholds rather than a universal feeding guideline. Ground truth is a measurement protocol A field quadrat and its laboratory assay must represent the same material and time as the image footprint. GPS mismatch, mixed vegetation, grazing after sampling or laboratory error create label uncertainty that machine learning cannot eliminate. Spectral inference The regression learns an empirical relation between corrected reflectance and reference CP/DMD. Such a model may capture useful chemistry-related spectral patterns but can also exploit correlated species, soil or season signals. Testing outside the training domain is essential. Exam response If a model predicts “CP” on bare soil, it is extrapolating outside its defined physical target. A pasture mask is a necessary stage, and errors in that mask propagate into the nutritional map.", "09. Pasture classification before regression Slides 13 and 15 Source method A seven-class neural-network classification separates pasture from other surface types. The slides report 98.6% overall accuracy and describe four dense blocks of 512, 256, 128 and 64 units with ReLU, L2 regularisation, batch normalisation and dropout 0.4, followed by seven-class softmax. Why overall accuracy is limited Overall accuracy can be dominated by abundant classes. For the application, false pasture labels cause invalid CP predictions, while missed pasture reduces coverage. Report pasture-specific producer and user accuracy, reference sample design and spatial patterns of error. Probabilities versus confidence Softmax scores are model outputs, not automatically calibrated probabilities of correctness. Thresholds and uncertainty should be assessed using independent reference samples. Propagation The final CP map depends on both a categorical mask and a quantitative regression. A low-error regression does not compensate for widespread false-positive pasture pixels.", "10. TabPFN and what the model learns Slide 16 Source description The deck presents TabPFN as a pre-trained tabular transformer trained on many synthetic tasks; a labelled training dataset is provided as context to predict unseen records without conventional per-task gradient-descent training. Why this may help The model can learn nonlinear relationships between reflectance-derived predictors and chemistry measurements without training a large network from scratch on a limited field dataset. Scientific caution Pretraining does not establish domain transfer to a new pasture species, soil, property or season. Evaluate against simpler baselines and independent spatial holdouts. The deck does not justify claiming that TabPFN is universally superior to Random Forest, PLS regression or other approaches. Exam response Method choice should be defended by sample size, predictor dimensionality, interpretability, transferability and out-of-sample error, not novelty alone.", "11. Spatial leakage and leave-one-property-out evaluation Slide 17 Source warning The slides explicitly reject random 80/20 pixel splitting as potentially misleading because adjacent pixels are spatially correlated. They use leave-one-property-out evaluation to hold out entire sites. Why leakage matters Neighbouring pixels share management, species, soil, illumination and often reference labels. Random splits can place near-duplicates in train and test sets, inflating apparent generalisation. Better design Hold out whole properties, then rotate the held-out property. Report the distribution of performance across properties and, where possible, across seasons. Keep hyperparameter tuning inside the training portion of each evaluation fold. Remaining uncertainty Property-level validation tests a stronger form of spatial transfer but may not establish transfer to new continents, sensors, years or management systems. Match the validation unit to the intended deployment.", "12. Continuous model accuracy: interpreting R² and RMSE Slide 18 Reported slide figures The slide reports CP/DMD R² values of 0.76/0.47 for leaf lab NIR, 0.95/0.88 for UAV hyperspectral, and 0.92/0.73 for Sentinel-2. It also shows a Sentinel-2 CP RMSE of 0.39 percentage points. Treat these as the presentation’s reported results; their cross-scale comparability depends on test design. Metric meaning R² describes explained variation relative to a specified baseline; it is not the percentage of predictions that are correct. RMSE = sqrt(mean((prediction − observation)²)) has the same units as CP or DMD. Bias = mean(prediction − observation) shows systematic over- or underprediction. Worked calculation For reference CP [6,8,10]% and predictions [6.5,7.5,11]%, errors are [0.5,−0.5,1.0] percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((0.25+0.25+1)/3) = 0.707 percentage points; bias = 0.333. This is a supplementary calculation, not a reported case-study result. Exam response Ask whether reported scores use independent properties, what the CP range is, whether performance changes in low-protein pasture, and whether uncertainties are displayed to the user.", "13. Upscaling: leaf to UAV to Sentinel-2 Slide 14 Recorded hierarchy The slides show approximately 1 m² field quadrats, 8 cm UAV hyperspectral imagery and 10 m Sentinel-2 observations, using UAV information to bridge field chemistry and larger-scale satellite monitoring. Change of support A 10 m pixel covers 100 m². A 1 m² quadrat or an 8 cm pixel does not represent the whole 10 m footprint automatically. Mixed pasture, shrubs, shadows and soil create nonlinear aggregation effects. Spectral mismatch A hyperspectral channel and a broad Sentinel-2 band do not measure the same wavelength interval. Proper transfer may require integrating hyperspectral reflectance over satellite spectral response functions and re-estimating the model at satellite support. Independent satellite evaluation Validate the satellite predictions at the support of the satellite product, not solely against the UAV model used to create its labels. Otherwise the final accuracy can inherit untested errors from the intermediate model. Exam response “Resample the UAV CP raster to 10 m and the model is validated for Sentinel-2.” Incorrect: resampling changes grid support, not the measurement physics or validation domain.", "14. Seasonality, rainfall and operational delivery Slides 19–20 Reported pattern The slides describe CP increasing after rain, with a peak approximately two to three weeks later, and DMD changing less dramatically. The case-study numbers include CP around 10–12% in wet conditions versus 4–6% in dry conditions. Temporal interpretation A correlation with rainfall is useful for management but does not alone prove a universal causal relationship. Vegetation species, grazing intensity, soils and management can change the response. Delivery system The deck shows a Pasture Quality Monitor web app, paddock selection and API access. Operational users need date, valid-data coverage, prediction uncertainty, spatial resolution and known domain limits alongside the map. Exam response An elegant web map does not establish scientific accuracy. A decision-support tool should communicate what was measured, what was predicted and how reliable the prediction is.", "15. Full worked critique: flawed UAV-to-satellite workflow Original exam-style scenario based on source methods Scenario A team flies one sunny property, converts raw DN directly to CP using a neural network, trains and tests on randomly selected adjacent pixels, resizes 8 cm predictions to 10 m, and reports a precise national CP map without uncertainty. Problem definition CP is not directly observed; it requires temporally and spatially matched laboratory chemistry. National extrapolation from one property is a severe domain-shift problem. Acquisition and preprocessing Require adequate overlap, stable illumination, reliable trajectory/boresight, DSM-based orthorectification, radiance calibration, reflectance correction and quality screening. Each step has a distinct role. Model design Separate pasture from non-pasture and validate the mask; train regression on field references with appropriate feature screening and transparent model comparison. Validation Random pixel splits leak spatial information. Hold out properties, ideally seasons and ecological domains; report RMSE, bias, R², coverage and uncertainty by CP range. Upscaling Satellite transfer requires matching spectral responses, spatial support and timing, with independent satellite-scale validation. Upsampling or downsampling alone is not enough. Defended solution Build a hierarchical field–UAV–satellite calibration pipeline with independent holdouts, reproducible correction and uncertainty reporting; limit operational claims to validated conditions.", "16. Self-test with model answers Original study questions Question 1 Why does direct georeferencing need both GNSS and IMU? Answer: position alone does not locate a pixel ray; orientation and camera/boresight geometry determine its ground intersection. Question 2 What is the difference between radiance and reflectance? Answer: radiance is measured directional energy; reflectance represents surface response relative to illumination and needs correction assumptions. Question 3 Why might water-vapour bands be excluded? Answer: strong atmospheric absorption yields low-transmission/noisy surface information. Question 4 Why is 98.6% mask accuracy insufficient? Answer: overall accuracy can hide important pasture-specific omission and commission errors. Question 5 What does leave-one-property-out test? Answer: generalisation to an independent property, reducing spatial leakage. Question 6 Why is R²=0.95 not “95% accurate”? Answer: it is a variance-explanation statistic, not a percent-correct measure. Question 7 Why can’t a 10 m Sentinel-2 pixel be treated as one 8 cm UAV observation? Answer: different spatial support, mixed pixels, band response and acquisition conditions. Question 8 What would you report for an operational CP map? Answer: reference protocol, processing provenance, independent RMSE/bias, spatial/seasonal transfer, coverage and uncertainty.", "17. Source notes and boundaries Slides 2–20; 19 August VTT Primary sources geom3001_7001_L3_P2_HyperspectralUAVProcessing_pastureQuality_v3.pdf (21 pages) and GMT20260819-013058_Recording.transcript.vtt. Slide references are physical PDF pages. Spoken recording timestamps refer to the complete Zoom VTT timeline. Scope The 19 August recording includes guest material beyond the UAV slide deck. This chapter focuses on the Barnetson hyperspectral processing case study, rather than claiming to reproduce every minute of the session. Supplementary explanations The angular error example, calibration equation, RMSE calculation, exam critique and some methodological qualifications are additional teaching explanations. They are distinguished from source-reported case-study numbers. Sequence correction The separate 60-page preprocessing deck is internally titled Lecture 4, but it is not the spoken topic of the 19 August Week 4 guest recording. It should be treated as its own course source and matched to its correct lecture recording before attributing spoken explanations.", "Transcript evidence anchors 01:32:09 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: From hyperspectral UAV capture to paddock-scale nutrition products, which are used for grazing management. 01:32:46 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on. 01:44:57 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: So, now we want to geometrically tag everything. So, getting that raw image cube. 01:46:33 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: When we go to geometrically, correct, they're both in the same spectral resolution. 01:54:51 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: is a bit of software called Drone at Core. It's a drone-specific atmospheric correction tool. 01:54:58 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: And unlike satellite atmospheric corrections, it retrieves the aerosol and water columns from the image itself. 01:29:46 — Audio shared by Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: From that, computer models learn to read the protein and quality of pasture, straight from a satellite passing overhead. Just as important is how much feed is out there. We paired old-fa 01:32:46 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: UAV hyperspectral is the bridge that lets us calibrate the satellite. What we deliver is paddock scale, crude protein, and dry matter digestibility maps that a grazer can actually act on. 02:02:25 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: We evaluate with stratified, like Peter did, a five-fold cross validation, and the measured overall accuracy on the held-out stat is 98%. 02:03:56 — Jason Barnetson - QLD Department Env & Science: The single biggest mistake in a lot of these remote sensing machine learning papers is an optimistic validation. You shuffle your pixels, take a random 80-20 split, and you report the R-squared on the 2"]}, {"id": 4, "title": "Image Preprocessing: Geometry, Radiometry, Atmosphere, Terrain & BRDF", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week05_Preprocessing.pdf", "pages": 11, "sections": [{"title": "Source conventions", "html": "<p>Primary: 60-page preprocessing slide deck and 26 August 2026 VTT recording. Slide references use physical PDF pages; recording timestamps are approximate. Supplementary explanations and original exercises are clearly distinguished.</p>", "text": "Primary: 60-page preprocessing slide deck and 26 August 2026 VTT recording. Slide references use physical PDF pages; recording timestamps are approximate. Supplementary explanations and original exercises are clearly distinguished."}, {"title": "1. What this lecture actually covers", "html": "<p>Slides 1–12; transcript 00:31–00:43</p><h3>Course sequence</h3><p>Will Woodgate introduces Week 5 as the start of three weeks of core image processing. The guest lecturer is Professor Stuart Phinn. This chapter follows the 60-page preprocessing slide deck, whose footer calls it Lecture 4 despite its Week 5 placement. The lecture recording is GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt.</p><h3>Learning objective</h3><p>The slides define preprocessing as making EO observations usable across space and time. A scientifically defensible image has known geometry, a suitable datum/projection, documented radiometric calibration and correction for atmospheric and other environmental influences where required. The goal is not simply a visually attractive raster; it is a defensible measurement.</p><h3>The core causal chain</h3><p>Scene and illumination → sensor observation → DN → calibrated radiance → atmospheric/terrain/view-angle adjustments → comparable surface measurement → classification or quantitative model → independently validated information. Each transformation addresses a different source of uncertainty. Applying a later step cannot necessarily repair an earlier error.</p><h3>Exam technique</h3><p>For every proposed preprocessing step, answer: (a) what error source does it address? (b) what input metadata or ancillary data does it require? (c) how can we test whether it worked? (d) what inference becomes invalid if it is skipped?</p>", "text": "Slides 1–12; transcript 00:31–00:43 Course sequence Will Woodgate introduces Week 5 as the start of three weeks of core image processing. The guest lecturer is Professor Stuart Phinn. This chapter follows the 60-page preprocessing slide deck, whose footer calls it Lecture 4 despite its Week 5 placement. The lecture recording is GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt. Learning objective The slides define preprocessing as making EO observations usable across space and time. A scientifically defensible image has known geometry, a suitable datum/projection, documented radiometric calibration and correction for atmospheric and other environmental influences where required. The goal is not simply a visually attractive raster; it is a defensible measurement. The core causal chain Scene and illumination → sensor observation → DN → calibrated radiance → atmospheric/terrain/view-angle adjustments → comparable surface measurement → classification or quantitative model → independently validated information. Each transformation addresses a different source of uncertainty. Applying a later step cannot necessarily repair an earlier error. Exam technique For every proposed preprocessing step, answer: (a) what error source does it address? (b) what input metadata or ancillary data does it require? (c) how can we test whether it worked? (d) what inference becomes invalid if it is skipped?"}, {"title": "2. Acquisition: do not correct what has already been corrected", "html": "<p>Slides 13–18; transcript ~00:43–01:03</p><h3>Source warning</h3><p>The slides explicitly say to ALWAYS check the type and level of corrections before processing. Many public and commercial products already have geometric and radiometric corrections. Product specifications and metadata, often XML, are primary evidence. The slides list Planet, Sentinel-2, Landsat 9, Google Earth Engine and AWS as examples of product documentation sources.</p><h3>Processing levels</h3><p>A raw DN image, calibrated top-of-atmosphere radiance product and surface-reflectance product are not interchangeable. The same band may be stored as integer values with scale and offset; multiplying or correcting it a second time can produce nonsense. Check whether the published values are scaled reflectance, radiance, brightness temperature or classification labels.</p><h3>ARD is conditional</h3><p>The lecture defines Analysis Ready Data as data processed into a form suitable for integration with other corrected observations and geospatial information. Standard corrections include geometric and radiometric operations; terrain, view/illumination and environment-specific operations may still be needed. ARD does not mean the image is ready for every ecological question.</p><h3>Practical audit</h3><p>Before a change-detection project, write down product name and processing level, acquisition date/time, bands and spectral response, scale/offset, CRS, geolocation accuracy, QA flags, sun/view angles, cloud fraction, correction algorithm/version and intended-use limitations.</p><h3>Failure example</h3><p>A student takes a surface-reflectance image, applies a DN-to-radiance gain from the raw product handbook and compares it with an unmodified surface-reflectance image. This mixes units and produces a spurious difference. The remedy is to select matching product levels and correctly interpret scale/offset metadata.</p>", "text": "Slides 13–18; transcript ~00:43–01:03 Source warning The slides explicitly say to ALWAYS check the type and level of corrections before processing. Many public and commercial products already have geometric and radiometric corrections. Product specifications and metadata, often XML, are primary evidence. The slides list Planet, Sentinel-2, Landsat 9, Google Earth Engine and AWS as examples of product documentation sources. Processing levels A raw DN image, calibrated top-of-atmosphere radiance product and surface-reflectance product are not interchangeable. The same band may be stored as integer values with scale and offset; multiplying or correcting it a second time can produce nonsense. Check whether the published values are scaled reflectance, radiance, brightness temperature or classification labels. ARD is conditional The lecture defines Analysis Ready Data as data processed into a form suitable for integration with other corrected observations and geospatial information. Standard corrections include geometric and radiometric operations; terrain, view/illumination and environment-specific operations may still be needed. ARD does not mean the image is ready for every ecological question. Practical audit Before a change-detection project, write down product name and processing level, acquisition date/time, bands and spectral response, scale/offset, CRS, geolocation accuracy, QA flags, sun/view angles, cloud fraction, correction algorithm/version and intended-use limitations. Failure example A student takes a surface-reflectance image, applies a DN-to-radiance gain from the raw product handbook and compares it with an unmodified surface-reflectance image. This mixes units and produces a spurious difference. The remedy is to select matching product levels and correctly interpret scale/offset metadata."}, {"title": "3. Geometry: datum, projection, coordinate system and pixel position", "html": "<p>Slides 20–24; transcript ~01:08–01:25</p><h3>Definitions from the slides</h3><p>A datum supplies a reference origin/surface; a projection transforms the curved Earth to a plane; a coordinate system specifies positions relative to an origin, often as x/y/z. Raw image line/column coordinates are not automatically ground coordinates.</p><h3>Why this matters</h3><p>Two rasters can visually overlap yet have differing horizontal datums, pixel grid origins or resolutions. Even if their CRS labels match, misregistration of a few metres can generate false change at shorelines, narrow roads and habitat boundaries.</p><h3>Systematic versus non-systematic distortion</h3><p>Systematic distortions may be described with known sensor/earth geometry; non-systematic effects arise from changing platform attitude, terrain or other departures. The slides introduce image-to-map, image-to-image and image-to-ground methods using sensor geometry/ephemeris.</p><h3>Rectification versus registration</h3><p>Rectification assigns an image to a geographic map system. Registration aligns one image to another, possibly without fully establishing absolute ground accuracy. Image-to-image registration can support change detection while both images still share a common absolute offset.</p><h3>Exam scenario</h3><p>If a researcher georeferences each year independently to different base images, apparent change may include reference-map error. Prefer a stable, well-validated common reference and independent checkpoints across the study area.</p>", "text": "Slides 20–24; transcript ~01:08–01:25 Definitions from the slides A datum supplies a reference origin/surface; a projection transforms the curved Earth to a plane; a coordinate system specifies positions relative to an origin, often as x/y/z. Raw image line/column coordinates are not automatically ground coordinates. Why this matters Two rasters can visually overlap yet have differing horizontal datums, pixel grid origins or resolutions. Even if their CRS labels match, misregistration of a few metres can generate false change at shorelines, narrow roads and habitat boundaries. Systematic versus non-systematic distortion Systematic distortions may be described with known sensor/earth geometry; non-systematic effects arise from changing platform attitude, terrain or other departures. The slides introduce image-to-map, image-to-image and image-to-ground methods using sensor geometry/ephemeris. Rectification versus registration Rectification assigns an image to a geographic map system. Registration aligns one image to another, possibly without fully establishing absolute ground accuracy. Image-to-image registration can support change detection while both images still share a common absolute offset. Exam scenario If a researcher georeferences each year independently to different base images, apparent change may include reference-map error. Prefer a stable, well-validated common reference and independent checkpoints across the study area."}, {"title": "4. Ground control points and coordinate transforms", "html": "<p>Slides 25–30; transcript ~01:22–01:54</p><h3>GCP selection</h3><p>The slides recommend features that can be identified to a single pixel, stable over time (such as street corners) and well distributed. Vegetation boundaries and shifting riverbanks are poor controls when their geometry changes. Avoid clustering all points in one corner.</p><h3>Affine transform</h3><p>The slide gives first-order polynomial equations x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y and y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y. Coefficients allow translation, rotation, scale and skew. Higher-order polynomials can fit more complex warps but risk overfitting or unstable extrapolation.</p><h3>Solving the transform</h3><p>For n GCPs, each contributes observed source and target coordinates. A least-squares fit estimates the coefficients; a first-order 2D affine transform has six coefficients, requiring at least three non-collinear control points. Use more than the minimum and reserve independent checkpoints.</p><h3>Worked affine example</h3><p>Suppose x′ = 100 + 2x − 0.1y and y′ = 200 + 0.05x + 2y. A raw pixel at (x,y)=(10,20) maps to (118,240.5). This is an illustrative coordinate transformation, not an equation reported as a worked numerical example by the lecturer.</p><h3>Residuals</h3><p>A GCP residual is the difference between reference coordinates and transformed coordinates. Small fit residuals can result from excessive polynomial flexibility or from overfitting. Report independent spatial error as well as the fit statistic.</p>", "text": "Slides 25–30; transcript ~01:22–01:54 GCP selection The slides recommend features that can be identified to a single pixel, stable over time (such as street corners) and well distributed. Vegetation boundaries and shifting riverbanks are poor controls when their geometry changes. Avoid clustering all points in one corner. Affine transform The slide gives first-order polynomial equations x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y and y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y. Coefficients allow translation, rotation, scale and skew. Higher-order polynomials can fit more complex warps but risk overfitting or unstable extrapolation. Solving the transform For n GCPs, each contributes observed source and target coordinates. A least-squares fit estimates the coefficients; a first-order 2D affine transform has six coefficients, requiring at least three non-collinear control points. Use more than the minimum and reserve independent checkpoints. Worked affine example Suppose x′ = 100 + 2x − 0.1y and y′ = 200 + 0.05x + 2y. A raw pixel at (x,y)=(10,20) maps to (118,240.5). This is an illustrative coordinate transformation, not an equation reported as a worked numerical example by the lecturer. Residuals A GCP residual is the difference between reference coordinates and transformed coordinates. Small fit residuals can result from excessive polynomial flexibility or from overfitting. Report independent spatial error as well as the fit statistic."}, {"title": "5. RMSE: formula, thresholds and a crucial distinction", "html": "<p>Slide 30; transcript ~01:49–01:58</p><h3>What the slide says</h3><p>The slide states a common tolerance below 0.5 pixel and cautions that RMSE assesses GCP locations, NOT the entire image. It displays a formula whose formatting can be read as an average of Euclidean distances; that is not the conventional root-mean-square formula.</p><h3>Conventional calculation — supplementary clarification</h3><p>For n checkpoints with residuals dx_i and dy_i, planar RMSE = sqrt[(Σ(dx_i² + dy_i²))/n]. The mean radial error instead equals [Σ sqrt(dx_i²+dy_i²)]/n. These quantities are generally different; do not silently treat them as identical.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>Residuals for three checkpoints are (0.2,0.1), (−0.3,0.2), (0.1,−0.4) pixels. Squared radial errors are 0.05, 0.13 and 0.17. Conventional RMSE = sqrt(0.35/3) = 0.342 pixel. The mean radial distance is (0.224+0.361+0.412)/3 = 0.332 pixel.</p><h3>Why 0.5 pixel matters</h3><p>If a 10 m image has 0.5-pixel registration RMSE, that corresponds to 5 m RMS positional disagreement at sampled points. At an abrupt vegetation/water boundary, this can cause false change in adjacent pixels. The threshold is a guideline, not a guarantee of accuracy across the whole scene.</p><h3>Better evaluation</h3><p>Use independent, spatially distributed checkpoints and visual edge overlays; examine whether residuals vary with terrain or image position. Do not discard high-error GCPs merely to improve the number without investigating the cause.</p>", "text": "Slide 30; transcript ~01:49–01:58 What the slide says The slide states a common tolerance below 0.5 pixel and cautions that RMSE assesses GCP locations, NOT the entire image. It displays a formula whose formatting can be read as an average of Euclidean distances; that is not the conventional root-mean-square formula. Conventional calculation — supplementary clarification For n checkpoints with residuals dx_i and dy_i, planar RMSE = sqrt[(Σ(dx_i² + dy_i²))/n]. The mean radial error instead equals [Σ sqrt(dx_i²+dy_i²)]/n. These quantities are generally different; do not silently treat them as identical. Worked example Residuals for three checkpoints are (0.2,0.1), (−0.3,0.2), (0.1,−0.4) pixels. Squared radial errors are 0.05, 0.13 and 0.17. Conventional RMSE = sqrt(0.35/3) = 0.342 pixel. The mean radial distance is (0.224+0.361+0.412)/3 = 0.332 pixel. Why 0.5 pixel matters If a 10 m image has 0.5-pixel registration RMSE, that corresponds to 5 m RMS positional disagreement at sampled points. At an abrupt vegetation/water boundary, this can cause false change in adjacent pixels. The threshold is a guideline, not a guarantee of accuracy across the whole scene. Better evaluation Use independent, spatially distributed checkpoints and visual edge overlays; examine whether residuals vary with terrain or image position. Do not discard high-error GCPs merely to improve the number without investigating the cause."}, {"title": "6. Spatial interpolation versus intensity interpolation", "html": "<p>Slides 25–31; transcript ~01:52–02:00</p><h3>Two distinct operations</h3><p>Spatial interpolation determines where corrected pixels belong. Intensity interpolation (resampling) assigns the pixel value at each new grid location. The lecture explicitly separates these stages.</p><h3>Nearest neighbour</h3><p>Assigns the nearest source pixel value without averaging. It preserves original class codes and is usually appropriate for categorical rasters. It can introduce blocky edges and does not improve positional accuracy.</p><h3>Bilinear interpolation</h3><p>Uses a distance-weighted average of four nearby source pixels. Suitable for continuous data in many settings; it smooths abrupt transitions and changes original measured values.</p><h3>Cubic convolution</h3><p>Uses a larger neighbourhood for smoother interpolation and potentially sharper appearance; may overshoot and create values outside the physical range. Check if outputs remain valid reflectance or temperature.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>Four source reflectances are 0.10, 0.20, 0.30 and 0.50. At the exact centre, bilinear interpolation gives (0.10+0.20+0.30+0.50)/4 = 0.275. Nearest neighbour instead returns one original sample. If the four values are class IDs 1,2,3,4, the bilinear result 2.5 has no meaningful class interpretation.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>A team resamples a classified habitat map using cubic convolution and then counts class 2.5 as a new habitat. This is a category error: use nearest neighbour for class labels and consider the spatial support and area consequences of reprojection.</p>", "text": "Slides 25–31; transcript ~01:52–02:00 Two distinct operations Spatial interpolation determines where corrected pixels belong. Intensity interpolation (resampling) assigns the pixel value at each new grid location. The lecture explicitly separates these stages. Nearest neighbour Assigns the nearest source pixel value without averaging. It preserves original class codes and is usually appropriate for categorical rasters. It can introduce blocky edges and does not improve positional accuracy. Bilinear interpolation Uses a distance-weighted average of four nearby source pixels. Suitable for continuous data in many settings; it smooths abrupt transitions and changes original measured values. Cubic convolution Uses a larger neighbourhood for smoother interpolation and potentially sharper appearance; may overshoot and create values outside the physical range. Check if outputs remain valid reflectance or temperature. Worked example Four source reflectances are 0.10, 0.20, 0.30 and 0.50. At the exact centre, bilinear interpolation gives (0.10+0.20+0.30+0.50)/4 = 0.275. Nearest neighbour instead returns one original sample. If the four values are class IDs 1,2,3,4, the bilinear result 2.5 has no meaningful class interpretation. Exam critique A team resamples a classified habitat map using cubic convolution and then counts class 2.5 as a new habitat. This is a category error: use nearest neighbour for class labels and consider the spatial support and area consequences of reprojection."}, {"title": "7. Radiometric calibration: digital numbers to physical units", "html": "<p>Slides 33–37; transcript ~00:33–00:47 and later processing discussion</p><h3>Source equation</h3><p>The lecture gives at-sensor spectral radiance Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ. Gains and offsets are band-specific, established during calibration and sometimes updated after launch.</p><h3>Why DN is not radiance</h3><p>DN is a sensor-coded number, whose magnitude depends on gain, quantisation, sensor settings and processing. A DN of 1000 in one image is not necessarily twice the physical brightness of a DN of 500 from another sensor.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>Suppose gain=0.012 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹ per DN and offset=−1 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=800, Lλ=0.012×800−1=8.6 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=500, Lλ=5.0 in the same units. These are illustrative coefficients, not sensor-specific published values.</p><h3>Top-of-atmosphere reflectance</h3><p>A supplementary common form is ρ_TOA = π Lλ d²/(ESUNλ cos θs), where d is Earth–Sun distance in astronomical units under the matching irradiance convention, ESUNλ is band solar irradiance and θs is solar zenith angle. Modern product metadata may supply a direct reflectance scaling equation; follow that product specification.</p><h3>Critical distinction</h3><p>Calibrated TOA radiance/reflectance still contains atmospheric effects. A physical unit does not imply that the value represents the surface. Calibration and atmospheric correction solve different problems.</p>", "text": "Slides 33–37; transcript ~00:33–00:47 and later processing discussion Source equation The lecture gives at-sensor spectral radiance Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ. Gains and offsets are band-specific, established during calibration and sometimes updated after launch. Why DN is not radiance DN is a sensor-coded number, whose magnitude depends on gain, quantisation, sensor settings and processing. A DN of 1000 in one image is not necessarily twice the physical brightness of a DN of 500 from another sensor. Worked example Suppose gain=0.012 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹ per DN and offset=−1 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=800, Lλ=0.012×800−1=8.6 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=500, Lλ=5.0 in the same units. These are illustrative coefficients, not sensor-specific published values. Top-of-atmosphere reflectance A supplementary common form is ρ_TOA = π Lλ d²/(ESUNλ cos θs), where d is Earth–Sun distance in astronomical units under the matching irradiance convention, ESUNλ is band solar irradiance and θs is solar zenith angle. Modern product metadata may supply a direct reflectance scaling equation; follow that product specification. Critical distinction Calibrated TOA radiance/reflectance still contains atmospheric effects. A physical unit does not imply that the value represents the surface. Calibration and atmospheric correction solve different problems."}, {"title": "8. Atmospheric physics: absorption, scattering and path radiance", "html": "<p>Slides 38–43; transcript ~00:35–00:47 and later lecture</p><h3>Source framing</h3><p>The lecture treats atmospheric attenuation as absorption plus scattering of electromagnetic radiation. The sensor measures not only the target contribution but also additions and reductions along the Sun–surface–sensor path.</p><h3>Absorption</h3><p>Atmospheric gases absorb at characteristic wavelengths; water vapour, ozone and other gases affect different bands. The loss is wavelength-dependent, so an atmospheric effect can change spectral shape, not merely overall brightness.</p><h3>Scattering</h3><p>Molecules and aerosols redirect light. Some photons from outside the target reach the sensor as path radiance; some surface-reflected photons scatter away. Haze may increase apparent visible brightness while reducing contrast.</p><h3>A simplified radiance model — supplementary</h3><p>One schematic model is L_sensor ≈ L_path + T_up × (ρ_surface E_down/π), ignoring adjacency and multiple scattering. Rearranging shows why one needs estimates of path radiance, transmission and downwelling irradiance to infer surface reflectance. Real radiative-transfer methods are more complex.</p><h3>Why water is difficult</h3><p>Dark water has low reflectance, so a modest path-radiance error can be a large fraction of the retrieved signal. For benthic mapping, additional sun-glint, water-column and air–water interface effects arise after atmospheric correction.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>A student subtracts the same constant from every band and calls the image surface reflectance. Atmospheric absorption and scattering vary by wavelength, geometry and conditions, so a single arbitrary constant is generally inadequate.</p>", "text": "Slides 38–43; transcript ~00:35–00:47 and later lecture Source framing The lecture treats atmospheric attenuation as absorption plus scattering of electromagnetic radiation. The sensor measures not only the target contribution but also additions and reductions along the Sun–surface–sensor path. Absorption Atmospheric gases absorb at characteristic wavelengths; water vapour, ozone and other gases affect different bands. The loss is wavelength-dependent, so an atmospheric effect can change spectral shape, not merely overall brightness. Scattering Molecules and aerosols redirect light. Some photons from outside the target reach the sensor as path radiance; some surface-reflected photons scatter away. Haze may increase apparent visible brightness while reducing contrast. A simplified radiance model — supplementary One schematic model is L_sensor ≈ L_path + T_up × (ρ_surface E_down/π), ignoring adjacency and multiple scattering. Rearranging shows why one needs estimates of path radiance, transmission and downwelling irradiance to infer surface reflectance. Real radiative-transfer methods are more complex. Why water is difficult Dark water has low reflectance, so a modest path-radiance error can be a large fraction of the retrieved signal. For benthic mapping, additional sun-glint, water-column and air–water interface effects arise after atmospheric correction. Exam critique A student subtracts the same constant from every band and calls the image surface reflectance. Atmospheric absorption and scattering vary by wavelength, geometry and conditions, so a single arbitrary constant is generally inadequate."}, {"title": "9. Absolute atmospheric correction and radiative transfer", "html": "<p>Slides 44–47; transcript atmospheric discussion</p><h3>What the lecture specifies</h3><p>Absolute correction uses acquisition-time atmospheric state and illumination/viewing geometry to estimate wavelength-dependent absorption and scattering. The slide names MODTRAN and 6S as radiative-transfer models and lists aerosol model, visibility, water-vapour column, ozone and CO₂ mixing ratio among relevant parameters.</p><h3>How it works conceptually</h3><p>Radiative-transfer software models the propagation of radiation from the Sun through atmosphere to the surface and back to the sensor. It estimates quantities needed to invert the measured radiance into surface reflectance, subject to atmospheric and surface assumptions.</p><h3>Strengths</h3><p>When atmospheric information and sensor calibration are credible, absolute correction supports comparison across dates and with field spectra in physically interpretable units.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Atmospheric inputs can be uncertain, aerosols vary spatially, clouds and adjacency effects can violate assumptions, and bright land next to dark water can contaminate coastal pixels. An automated correction is still a model, not an independent truth.</p><h3>Validation</h3><p>Compare corrected spectra to field spectrometer observations or stable pseudo-invariant targets when possible. Inspect negative or greater-than-one reflectances, residual haze, band discontinuities and scene-edge artefacts; recognise that some values can exceed one under special reflectance definitions/conditions.</p>", "text": "Slides 44–47; transcript atmospheric discussion What the lecture specifies Absolute correction uses acquisition-time atmospheric state and illumination/viewing geometry to estimate wavelength-dependent absorption and scattering. The slide names MODTRAN and 6S as radiative-transfer models and lists aerosol model, visibility, water-vapour column, ozone and CO₂ mixing ratio among relevant parameters. How it works conceptually Radiative-transfer software models the propagation of radiation from the Sun through atmosphere to the surface and back to the sensor. It estimates quantities needed to invert the measured radiance into surface reflectance, subject to atmospheric and surface assumptions. Strengths When atmospheric information and sensor calibration are credible, absolute correction supports comparison across dates and with field spectra in physically interpretable units. Limitations Atmospheric inputs can be uncertain, aerosols vary spatially, clouds and adjacency effects can violate assumptions, and bright land next to dark water can contaminate coastal pixels. An automated correction is still a model, not an independent truth. Validation Compare corrected spectra to field spectrometer observations or stable pseudo-invariant targets when possible. Inspect negative or greater-than-one reflectances, residual haze, band discontinuities and scene-edge artefacts; recognise that some values can exceed one under special reflectance definitions/conditions."}, {"title": "10. Relative atmospheric normalisation", "html": "<p>Slide 42 contrasts absolute and relative correction; supplementary methodological detail</p><h3>What the slides distinguish</h3><p>The processing sequence shows absolute correction using a model atmosphere versus relative correction using regression applied to radiance or DN. The latter seeks relative comparability rather than full physical retrieval.</p><h3>Pseudo-invariant features</h3><p>A common supplementary approach identifies targets expected to be stable across dates, such as certain roads or bare surfaces, then fits bandwise transformations to align their measured values. Stability must be assessed rather than assumed.</p><h3>Worked regression</h3><p>Suppose stable features follow reference reflectance ≈ 0.90 × target-date value + 0.015. A target-date pixel value of 0.20 normalises to 0.195. This can reduce systematic differences but does not prove that 0.195 is physically accurate surface reflectance.</p><h3>When relative correction is appropriate</h3><p>It can be useful for change detection when absolute atmospheric information is unavailable and robust invariant targets exist. It is less appropriate if the entire landscape changes or if the analysis requires absolute biophysical values.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>A student uses all pixels—including deforested areas—to fit relative normalisation between two dates. True change may be absorbed into the regression, biasing the final change estimate. Use defensible stable targets and validate independently.</p>", "text": "Slide 42 contrasts absolute and relative correction; supplementary methodological detail What the slides distinguish The processing sequence shows absolute correction using a model atmosphere versus relative correction using regression applied to radiance or DN. The latter seeks relative comparability rather than full physical retrieval. Pseudo-invariant features A common supplementary approach identifies targets expected to be stable across dates, such as certain roads or bare surfaces, then fits bandwise transformations to align their measured values. Stability must be assessed rather than assumed. Worked regression Suppose stable features follow reference reflectance ≈ 0.90 × target-date value + 0.015. A target-date pixel value of 0.20 normalises to 0.195. This can reduce systematic differences but does not prove that 0.195 is physically accurate surface reflectance. When relative correction is appropriate It can be useful for change detection when absolute atmospheric information is unavailable and robust invariant targets exist. It is less appropriate if the entire landscape changes or if the analysis requires absolute biophysical values. Exam critique A student uses all pixels—including deforested areas—to fit relative normalisation between two dates. True change may be absorbed into the regression, biasing the final change estimate. Use defensible stable targets and validate independently."}, {"title": "11. Terrain illumination: slope, aspect and solar geometry", "html": "<p>Slides 49–52; transcript later lecture</p><h3>Lecture principle</h3><p>The same land-cover type can have different recorded brightness because its surface faces toward or away from the Sun. Terrain effects combine slope/aspect and illumination/viewing geometry.</p><h3>Cosine of incidence — supplementary</h3><p>For terrain slope β, solar zenith θs and relative azimuth Δφ, cos i = cos θs cos β + sin θs sin β cos Δφ. A slope facing the Sun has larger cos i and receives more direct irradiance than an opposing slope.</p><h3>Illustration</h3><p>At solar zenith 40° and slope 20°, a sun-facing slope with Δφ=0° has cos i=cos(20°)=0.940; a slope facing away with Δφ=180° has cos i=cos(60°)=0.500. Even with identical surface material, direct illumination differs markedly.</p><h3>Slide approaches</h3><p>The lecture mentions scheduling near higher solar elevation and band ratios as approaches to reduce illumination effects. It also discusses terrain correction approaches; no single method removes all shadow or BRDF effects.</p><h3>Caveat on ratios</h3><p>A ratio may suppress a common multiplicative brightness factor, but not additive path radiance, cast shadow, wavelength-dependent scattering or differences in spectral response. Ratios do not make physically distinct observations universally comparable.</p>", "text": "Slides 49–52; transcript later lecture Lecture principle The same land-cover type can have different recorded brightness because its surface faces toward or away from the Sun. Terrain effects combine slope/aspect and illumination/viewing geometry. Cosine of incidence — supplementary For terrain slope β, solar zenith θs and relative azimuth Δφ, cos i = cos θs cos β + sin θs sin β cos Δφ. A slope facing the Sun has larger cos i and receives more direct irradiance than an opposing slope. Illustration At solar zenith 40° and slope 20°, a sun-facing slope with Δφ=0° has cos i=cos(20°)=0.940; a slope facing away with Δφ=180° has cos i=cos(60°)=0.500. Even with identical surface material, direct illumination differs markedly. Slide approaches The lecture mentions scheduling near higher solar elevation and band ratios as approaches to reduce illumination effects. It also discusses terrain correction approaches; no single method removes all shadow or BRDF effects. Caveat on ratios A ratio may suppress a common multiplicative brightness factor, but not additive path radiance, cast shadow, wavelength-dependent scattering or differences in spectral response. Ratios do not make physically distinct observations universally comparable."}, {"title": "12. BRDF: why the same surface looks different from different angles", "html": "<p>Slides 53–56; transcript ~02:19–02:21</p><h3>Definition</h3><p>Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes how surface reflection varies with incoming illumination and outgoing viewing direction. Vegetation canopies and rough surfaces are not perfectly Lambertian.</p><h3>Geometry</h3><p>Differences in sun elevation/azimuth and satellite view angle can alter the balance of illuminated and shadowed canopy, producing reflectance differences without land-cover change.</p><h3>Practical response</h3><p>Use acquisition geometries that are as comparable as possible, BRDF-adjusted products where appropriate, and document angular differences. For time series, sensor geometry and seasonal sun angles can create systematic bias.</p><h3>Distinction from topographic correction</h3><p>Terrain correction addresses slope/aspect illumination at landscape scale. BRDF concerns directional reflectance properties of the surface itself, although the phenomena interact. Correcting one does not necessarily eliminate the other.</p><h3>Exam question</h3><p>Why might two surface-reflectance images of unchanged forest differ? Because atmospheric correction does not automatically standardise canopy BRDF, view geometry, terrain shading or phenology.</p>", "text": "Slides 53–56; transcript ~02:19–02:21 Definition Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes how surface reflection varies with incoming illumination and outgoing viewing direction. Vegetation canopies and rough surfaces are not perfectly Lambertian. Geometry Differences in sun elevation/azimuth and satellite view angle can alter the balance of illuminated and shadowed canopy, producing reflectance differences without land-cover change. Practical response Use acquisition geometries that are as comparable as possible, BRDF-adjusted products where appropriate, and document angular differences. For time series, sensor geometry and seasonal sun angles can create systematic bias. Distinction from topographic correction Terrain correction addresses slope/aspect illumination at landscape scale. BRDF concerns directional reflectance properties of the surface itself, although the phenomena interact. Correcting one does not necessarily eliminate the other. Exam question Why might two surface-reflectance images of unchanged forest differ? Because atmospheric correction does not automatically standardise canopy BRDF, view geometry, terrain shading or phenology."}, {"title": "13. Environment-specific correction: sun glint and shallow water", "html": "<p>Slides 57–59; related transcript discussion</p><h3>Source concept</h3><p>The lecture explicitly includes sun-glint from specular reflection off wave faces as an environment-specific distortion. It also identifies air–sea interface refraction and water-column effects in its overall preprocessing list.</p><h3>Why glint matters</h3><p>The sensor can receive sunlight reflected from the water surface rather than light emerging from the water column or seabed. The resulting bright streaks can mimic shallow substrate differences or obscure benthic signal.</p><h3>Supplementary correction logic</h3><p>A common empirical strategy relates visible-band glint to NIR brightness over deep water, where water-leaving NIR signal is small, then subtracts an estimated glint contribution. The assumptions can fail in turbid or shallow water, where NIR may not be negligible.</p><h3>Water-column effect</h3><p>Bottom reflectance is attenuated as light travels down and up through water. Depth, turbidity, dissolved matter and wavelength affect what reaches the sensor. Even perfectly atmospherically corrected imagery may not yield comparable benthic spectra at different depths.</p><h3>Coastal exam critique</h3><p>A team maps seagrass from optical surface reflectance without tidal, glint or depth considerations. A changed water level can alter apparent bottom brightness and area. Choose suitable tides, glint removal, water-column treatment and independent underwater validation.</p>", "text": "Slides 57–59; related transcript discussion Source concept The lecture explicitly includes sun-glint from specular reflection off wave faces as an environment-specific distortion. It also identifies air–sea interface refraction and water-column effects in its overall preprocessing list. Why glint matters The sensor can receive sunlight reflected from the water surface rather than light emerging from the water column or seabed. The resulting bright streaks can mimic shallow substrate differences or obscure benthic signal. Supplementary correction logic A common empirical strategy relates visible-band glint to NIR brightness over deep water, where water-leaving NIR signal is small, then subtracts an estimated glint contribution. The assumptions can fail in turbid or shallow water, where NIR may not be negligible. Water-column effect Bottom reflectance is attenuated as light travels down and up through water. Depth, turbidity, dissolved matter and wavelength affect what reaches the sensor. Even perfectly atmospherically corrected imagery may not yield comparable benthic spectra at different depths. Coastal exam critique A team maps seagrass from optical surface reflectance without tidal, glint or depth considerations. A changed water level can alter apparent bottom brightness and area. Choose suitable tides, glint removal, water-column treatment and independent underwater validation."}, {"title": "14. Building a defensible preprocessing workflow", "html": "<p>Synthesis of slides 9–59 and the matched VTT</p><h3>Step 1: define the target</h3><p>Is the goal visual interpretation, categorical mapping, physical reflectance comparison, water quality, or quantitative change? The necessary correction level depends on this target.</p><h3>Step 2: inspect products</h3><p>Read product specifications and metadata before applying operations. Determine whether the data are already geometrically corrected, radiometrically calibrated or atmospherically corrected.</p><h3>Step 3: ensure geometry</h3><p>Confirm CRS, datum, pixel size and grid origin; test registration with stable features and independent checkpoints. Use resampling suited to continuous or categorical values.</p><h3>Step 4: ensure radiometry</h3><p>Apply correct band-specific scale, offset and calibration only if needed. Compare physical units and band definitions; do not mix raw DN with corrected reflectance.</p><h3>Step 5: address atmosphere and geometry</h3><p>Apply appropriate atmospheric correction, terrain/BRDF adjustment and environment-specific corrections as required. Keep QA masks and record uncertain or invalid pixels.</p><h3>Step 6: test fitness</h3><p>Inspect spectral plausibility, cloud/shadow, residual geometry and temporal consistency. Only then classify, model or detect change. Reserve independent reference data for validation.</p><h3>Step 7: report uncertainty</h3><p>A processing pipeline can reduce systematic artefacts but not eliminate all error. Document residual limitations, thresholds, masks and how these affect conclusions.</p>", "text": "Synthesis of slides 9–59 and the matched VTT Step 1: define the target Is the goal visual interpretation, categorical mapping, physical reflectance comparison, water quality, or quantitative change? The necessary correction level depends on this target. Step 2: inspect products Read product specifications and metadata before applying operations. Determine whether the data are already geometrically corrected, radiometrically calibrated or atmospherically corrected. Step 3: ensure geometry Confirm CRS, datum, pixel size and grid origin; test registration with stable features and independent checkpoints. Use resampling suited to continuous or categorical values. Step 4: ensure radiometry Apply correct band-specific scale, offset and calibration only if needed. Compare physical units and band definitions; do not mix raw DN with corrected reflectance. Step 5: address atmosphere and geometry Apply appropriate atmospheric correction, terrain/BRDF adjustment and environment-specific corrections as required. Keep QA masks and record uncertain or invalid pixels. Step 6: test fitness Inspect spectral plausibility, cloud/shadow, residual geometry and temporal consistency. Only then classify, model or detect change. Reserve independent reference data for validation. Step 7: report uncertainty A processing pipeline can reduce systematic artefacts but not eliminate all error. Document residual limitations, thresholds, masks and how these affect conclusions."}, {"title": "15. Full examination scenario: false mangrove change", "html": "<p>Original exam-style problem grounded in lecture concepts</p><h3>Scenario</h3><p>A researcher compares 2018 raw Landsat DN and 2026 Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, resamples the Landsat image to 10 m, calculates NDVI, thresholds a decline of 0.1, and reports 2.3 ha mangrove loss. There is no cloud mask, no registration test and no field reference.</p><h3>Error 1: incompatible units</h3><p>DN and surface reflectance are not comparable. Select harmonised corrected products and verify band scale/offset, wavelength response and calibration.</p><h3>Error 2: false spatial detail</h3><p>Upsampling 30 m pixels to 10 m creates more grid cells but not more measured detail. Establish common spatial support and a minimum mapping unit appropriate to mangrove width.</p><h3>Error 3: geometric and atmospheric artefacts</h3><p>Coastlines are sensitive to pixel misalignment, tides, glint and atmospheric differences. Check registration, cloud/shadow QA, comparable season/tide and water-adjacent pixels.</p><h3>Error 4: threshold and inference</h3><p>A −0.1 NDVI difference may arise from mixed pixels, phenology or transient stress, not necessarily mortality. Use time-series persistence and independent ecological observations.</p><h3>Defended alternative</h3><p>Define persistent canopy loss; obtain comparable calibrated observations; harmonise bands and spatial grids; quality-mask; verify co-registration; consider coastal water effects; model multi-date change; validate independently; report area and uncertainty rather than unsupported precision.</p>", "text": "Original exam-style problem grounded in lecture concepts Scenario A researcher compares 2018 raw Landsat DN and 2026 Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, resamples the Landsat image to 10 m, calculates NDVI, thresholds a decline of 0.1, and reports 2.3 ha mangrove loss. There is no cloud mask, no registration test and no field reference. Error 1: incompatible units DN and surface reflectance are not comparable. Select harmonised corrected products and verify band scale/offset, wavelength response and calibration. Error 2: false spatial detail Upsampling 30 m pixels to 10 m creates more grid cells but not more measured detail. Establish common spatial support and a minimum mapping unit appropriate to mangrove width. Error 3: geometric and atmospheric artefacts Coastlines are sensitive to pixel misalignment, tides, glint and atmospheric differences. Check registration, cloud/shadow QA, comparable season/tide and water-adjacent pixels. Error 4: threshold and inference A −0.1 NDVI difference may arise from mixed pixels, phenology or transient stress, not necessarily mortality. Use time-series persistence and independent ecological observations. Defended alternative Define persistent canopy loss; obtain comparable calibrated observations; harmonise bands and spatial grids; quality-mask; verify co-registration; consider coastal water effects; model multi-date change; validate independently; report area and uncertainty rather than unsupported precision."}, {"title": "16. Examination scenario: hillside vegetation", "html": "<p>Original applied reasoning</p><h3>Scenario</h3><p>A classifier trained on sunlit forest labels shaded forest as a different species. The team proposes increasing model complexity without changing imagery.</p><h3>Diagnosis</h3><p>The classifier may be responding to illumination and topographic effects, not species. More complex classification alone cannot repair a systematic input measurement problem.</p><h3>Correction</h3><p>Check solar geometry, terrain slope/aspect, cast-shadow masks and suitable topographic/BRDF correction. Include representative training across illumination conditions and independent stratified validation.</p><h3>Limits</h3><p>Deep shadow may have low signal-to-noise ratio; no correction can fully recover missing information. Mask or qualify the areas rather than inventing reliable spectra.</p>", "text": "Original applied reasoning Scenario A classifier trained on sunlit forest labels shaded forest as a different species. The team proposes increasing model complexity without changing imagery. Diagnosis The classifier may be responding to illumination and topographic effects, not species. More complex classification alone cannot repair a systematic input measurement problem. Correction Check solar geometry, terrain slope/aspect, cast-shadow masks and suitable topographic/BRDF correction. Include representative training across illumination conditions and independent stratified validation. Limits Deep shadow may have low signal-to-noise ratio; no correction can fully recover missing information. Mask or qualify the areas rather than inventing reliable spectra."}, {"title": "17. Calculation and reasoning practice", "html": "<p>Original questions with answers</p><h3>Q1 — radiance</h3><p>With gain=0.02 and offset=−2, DN=600 yields L=10 in the matching radiance units. Do not interpret this as surface reflectance.</p><h3>Q2 — RMSE</h3><p>Residuals (0.3,0.4) and (0.0,0.5) pixels give squared distances 0.25 and 0.25; RMSE = sqrt(0.5/2)=0.5 pixel. This is exactly the threshold, not below it.</p><h3>Q3 — interpolation</h3><p>For a land-cover raster choose nearest neighbour; bilinear would create intermediate, invalid category values.</p><h3>Q4 — atmosphere</h3><p>An image calibrated to TOA radiance still contains path radiance and absorption/scattering. It is not equivalent to surface reflectance.</p><h3>Q5 — relative correction</h3><p>Relative normalisation can improve consistency but does not necessarily retrieve absolute physical surface reflectance.</p><h3>Q6 — terrain</h3><p>Band ratios can reduce multiplicative illumination differences but cannot reliably fix cast shadows or all wavelength-dependent effects.</p><h3>Q7 — water</h3><p>Atmospheric correction alone does not remove sun glint or water-column attenuation.</p><h3>Q8 — metadata</h3><p>The correct first step with a downloaded ARD product is to inspect processing specifications and QA layers, not to blindly repeat every correction.</p>", "text": "Original questions with answers Q1 — radiance With gain=0.02 and offset=−2, DN=600 yields L=10 in the matching radiance units. Do not interpret this as surface reflectance. Q2 — RMSE Residuals (0.3,0.4) and (0.0,0.5) pixels give squared distances 0.25 and 0.25; RMSE = sqrt(0.5/2)=0.5 pixel. This is exactly the threshold, not below it. Q3 — interpolation For a land-cover raster choose nearest neighbour; bilinear would create intermediate, invalid category values. Q4 — atmosphere An image calibrated to TOA radiance still contains path radiance and absorption/scattering. It is not equivalent to surface reflectance. Q5 — relative correction Relative normalisation can improve consistency but does not necessarily retrieve absolute physical surface reflectance. Q6 — terrain Band ratios can reduce multiplicative illumination differences but cannot reliably fix cast shadows or all wavelength-dependent effects. Q7 — water Atmospheric correction alone does not remove sun glint or water-column attenuation. Q8 — metadata The correct first step with a downloaded ARD product is to inspect processing specifications and QA layers, not to blindly repeat every correction."}, {"title": "18. Source integrity and study priorities", "html": "<p>Source audit</p><h3>Directly used</h3><p>geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf, 60 pages; GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt, 26 August 2026 lecture. Slide references in this chapter use physical PDF page numbers; timestamps refer to the VTT recording.</p><h3>Label mismatch</h3><p>The slide footer identifies Lecture 4 while the recording introduction calls it the fourth lecture held in Week 5, because Week 3 had no lecture. This chapter uses Week 5 to avoid confusion.</p><h3>Supplementary material</h3><p>Numerical exercises, conventional RMSE clarification, simplified atmospheric equation, topographic incidence equation and exam scenarios are explanatory additions. They are not attributed verbatim to the lecturer.</p><h3>High-priority revision</h3><p>Memorise distinctions between DN/radiance/TOA/surface reflectance, rectification/registration, geometric/intensity interpolation, absolute/relative atmospheric correction, topographic/BRDF correction and ARD/fitness for purpose. Then practise explaining the consequence of each omission.</p>", "text": "Source audit Directly used geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf, 60 pages; GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt, 26 August 2026 lecture. Slide references in this chapter use physical PDF page numbers; timestamps refer to the VTT recording. Label mismatch The slide footer identifies Lecture 4 while the recording introduction calls it the fourth lecture held in Week 5, because Week 3 had no lecture. This chapter uses Week 5 to avoid confusion. Supplementary material Numerical exercises, conventional RMSE clarification, simplified atmospheric equation, topographic incidence equation and exam scenarios are explanatory additions. They are not attributed verbatim to the lecturer. High-priority revision Memorise distinctions between DN/radiance/TOA/surface reflectance, rectification/registration, geometric/intensity interpolation, absolute/relative atmospheric correction, topographic/BRDF correction and ARD/fitness for purpose. Then practise explaining the consequence of each omission."}], "text": ["Source conventions Primary: 60-page preprocessing slide deck and 26 August 2026 VTT recording. Slide references use physical PDF pages; recording timestamps are approximate. Supplementary explanations and original exercises are clearly distinguished.", "1. What this lecture actually covers Slides 1–12; transcript 00:31–00:43 Course sequence Will Woodgate introduces Week 5 as the start of three weeks of core image processing. The guest lecturer is Professor Stuart Phinn. This chapter follows the 60-page preprocessing slide deck, whose footer calls it Lecture 4 despite its Week 5 placement. The lecture recording is GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt. Learning objective The slides define preprocessing as making EO observations usable across space and time. A scientifically defensible image has known geometry, a suitable datum/projection, documented radiometric calibration and correction for atmospheric and other environmental influences where required. The goal is not simply a visually attractive raster; it is a defensible measurement. The core causal chain Scene and illumination → sensor observation → DN → calibrated radiance → atmospheric/terrain/view-angle adjustments → comparable surface measurement → classification or quantitative model → independently validated information. Each transformation addresses a different source of uncertainty. Applying a later step cannot necessarily repair an earlier error. Exam technique For every proposed preprocessing step, answer: (a) what error source does it address? (b) what input metadata or ancillary data does it require? (c) how can we test whether it worked? (d) what inference becomes invalid if it is skipped?", "2. Acquisition: do not correct what has already been corrected Slides 13–18; transcript ~00:43–01:03 Source warning The slides explicitly say to ALWAYS check the type and level of corrections before processing. Many public and commercial products already have geometric and radiometric corrections. Product specifications and metadata, often XML, are primary evidence. The slides list Planet, Sentinel-2, Landsat 9, Google Earth Engine and AWS as examples of product documentation sources. Processing levels A raw DN image, calibrated top-of-atmosphere radiance product and surface-reflectance product are not interchangeable. The same band may be stored as integer values with scale and offset; multiplying or correcting it a second time can produce nonsense. Check whether the published values are scaled reflectance, radiance, brightness temperature or classification labels. ARD is conditional The lecture defines Analysis Ready Data as data processed into a form suitable for integration with other corrected observations and geospatial information. Standard corrections include geometric and radiometric operations; terrain, view/illumination and environment-specific operations may still be needed. ARD does not mean the image is ready for every ecological question. Practical audit Before a change-detection project, write down product name and processing level, acquisition date/time, bands and spectral response, scale/offset, CRS, geolocation accuracy, QA flags, sun/view angles, cloud fraction, correction algorithm/version and intended-use limitations. Failure example A student takes a surface-reflectance image, applies a DN-to-radiance gain from the raw product handbook and compares it with an unmodified surface-reflectance image. This mixes units and produces a spurious difference. The remedy is to select matching product levels and correctly interpret scale/offset metadata.", "3. Geometry: datum, projection, coordinate system and pixel position Slides 20–24; transcript ~01:08–01:25 Definitions from the slides A datum supplies a reference origin/surface; a projection transforms the curved Earth to a plane; a coordinate system specifies positions relative to an origin, often as x/y/z. Raw image line/column coordinates are not automatically ground coordinates. Why this matters Two rasters can visually overlap yet have differing horizontal datums, pixel grid origins or resolutions. Even if their CRS labels match, misregistration of a few metres can generate false change at shorelines, narrow roads and habitat boundaries. Systematic versus non-systematic distortion Systematic distortions may be described with known sensor/earth geometry; non-systematic effects arise from changing platform attitude, terrain or other departures. The slides introduce image-to-map, image-to-image and image-to-ground methods using sensor geometry/ephemeris. Rectification versus registration Rectification assigns an image to a geographic map system. Registration aligns one image to another, possibly without fully establishing absolute ground accuracy. Image-to-image registration can support change detection while both images still share a common absolute offset. Exam scenario If a researcher georeferences each year independently to different base images, apparent change may include reference-map error. Prefer a stable, well-validated common reference and independent checkpoints across the study area.", "4. Ground control points and coordinate transforms Slides 25–30; transcript ~01:22–01:54 GCP selection The slides recommend features that can be identified to a single pixel, stable over time (such as street corners) and well distributed. Vegetation boundaries and shifting riverbanks are poor controls when their geometry changes. Avoid clustering all points in one corner. Affine transform The slide gives first-order polynomial equations x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y and y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y. Coefficients allow translation, rotation, scale and skew. Higher-order polynomials can fit more complex warps but risk overfitting or unstable extrapolation. Solving the transform For n GCPs, each contributes observed source and target coordinates. A least-squares fit estimates the coefficients; a first-order 2D affine transform has six coefficients, requiring at least three non-collinear control points. Use more than the minimum and reserve independent checkpoints. Worked affine example Suppose x′ = 100 + 2x − 0.1y and y′ = 200 + 0.05x + 2y. A raw pixel at (x,y)=(10,20) maps to (118,240.5). This is an illustrative coordinate transformation, not an equation reported as a worked numerical example by the lecturer. Residuals A GCP residual is the difference between reference coordinates and transformed coordinates. Small fit residuals can result from excessive polynomial flexibility or from overfitting. Report independent spatial error as well as the fit statistic.", "5. RMSE: formula, thresholds and a crucial distinction Slide 30; transcript ~01:49–01:58 What the slide says The slide states a common tolerance below 0.5 pixel and cautions that RMSE assesses GCP locations, NOT the entire image. It displays a formula whose formatting can be read as an average of Euclidean distances; that is not the conventional root-mean-square formula. Conventional calculation — supplementary clarification For n checkpoints with residuals dx_i and dy_i, planar RMSE = sqrt[(Σ(dx_i² + dy_i²))/n]. The mean radial error instead equals [Σ sqrt(dx_i²+dy_i²)]/n. These quantities are generally different; do not silently treat them as identical. Worked example Residuals for three checkpoints are (0.2,0.1), (−0.3,0.2), (0.1,−0.4) pixels. Squared radial errors are 0.05, 0.13 and 0.17. Conventional RMSE = sqrt(0.35/3) = 0.342 pixel. The mean radial distance is (0.224+0.361+0.412)/3 = 0.332 pixel. Why 0.5 pixel matters If a 10 m image has 0.5-pixel registration RMSE, that corresponds to 5 m RMS positional disagreement at sampled points. At an abrupt vegetation/water boundary, this can cause false change in adjacent pixels. The threshold is a guideline, not a guarantee of accuracy across the whole scene. Better evaluation Use independent, spatially distributed checkpoints and visual edge overlays; examine whether residuals vary with terrain or image position. Do not discard high-error GCPs merely to improve the number without investigating the cause.", "6. Spatial interpolation versus intensity interpolation Slides 25–31; transcript ~01:52–02:00 Two distinct operations Spatial interpolation determines where corrected pixels belong. Intensity interpolation (resampling) assigns the pixel value at each new grid location. The lecture explicitly separates these stages. Nearest neighbour Assigns the nearest source pixel value without averaging. It preserves original class codes and is usually appropriate for categorical rasters. It can introduce blocky edges and does not improve positional accuracy. Bilinear interpolation Uses a distance-weighted average of four nearby source pixels. Suitable for continuous data in many settings; it smooths abrupt transitions and changes original measured values. Cubic convolution Uses a larger neighbourhood for smoother interpolation and potentially sharper appearance; may overshoot and create values outside the physical range. Check if outputs remain valid reflectance or temperature. Worked example Four source reflectances are 0.10, 0.20, 0.30 and 0.50. At the exact centre, bilinear interpolation gives (0.10+0.20+0.30+0.50)/4 = 0.275. Nearest neighbour instead returns one original sample. If the four values are class IDs 1,2,3,4, the bilinear result 2.5 has no meaningful class interpretation. Exam critique A team resamples a classified habitat map using cubic convolution and then counts class 2.5 as a new habitat. This is a category error: use nearest neighbour for class labels and consider the spatial support and area consequences of reprojection.", "7. Radiometric calibration: digital numbers to physical units Slides 33–37; transcript ~00:33–00:47 and later processing discussion Source equation The lecture gives at-sensor spectral radiance Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ. Gains and offsets are band-specific, established during calibration and sometimes updated after launch. Why DN is not radiance DN is a sensor-coded number, whose magnitude depends on gain, quantisation, sensor settings and processing. A DN of 1000 in one image is not necessarily twice the physical brightness of a DN of 500 from another sensor. Worked example Suppose gain=0.012 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹ per DN and offset=−1 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=800, Lλ=0.012×800−1=8.6 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹. For DN=500, Lλ=5.0 in the same units. These are illustrative coefficients, not sensor-specific published values. Top-of-atmosphere reflectance A supplementary common form is ρ_TOA = π Lλ d²/(ESUNλ cos θs), where d is Earth–Sun distance in astronomical units under the matching irradiance convention, ESUNλ is band solar irradiance and θs is solar zenith angle. Modern product metadata may supply a direct reflectance scaling equation; follow that product specification. Critical distinction Calibrated TOA radiance/reflectance still contains atmospheric effects. A physical unit does not imply that the value represents the surface. Calibration and atmospheric correction solve different problems.", "8. Atmospheric physics: absorption, scattering and path radiance Slides 38–43; transcript ~00:35–00:47 and later lecture Source framing The lecture treats atmospheric attenuation as absorption plus scattering of electromagnetic radiation. The sensor measures not only the target contribution but also additions and reductions along the Sun–surface–sensor path. Absorption Atmospheric gases absorb at characteristic wavelengths; water vapour, ozone and other gases affect different bands. The loss is wavelength-dependent, so an atmospheric effect can change spectral shape, not merely overall brightness. Scattering Molecules and aerosols redirect light. Some photons from outside the target reach the sensor as path radiance; some surface-reflected photons scatter away. Haze may increase apparent visible brightness while reducing contrast. A simplified radiance model — supplementary One schematic model is L_sensor ≈ L_path + T_up × (ρ_surface E_down/π), ignoring adjacency and multiple scattering. Rearranging shows why one needs estimates of path radiance, transmission and downwelling irradiance to infer surface reflectance. Real radiative-transfer methods are more complex. Why water is difficult Dark water has low reflectance, so a modest path-radiance error can be a large fraction of the retrieved signal. For benthic mapping, additional sun-glint, water-column and air–water interface effects arise after atmospheric correction. Exam critique A student subtracts the same constant from every band and calls the image surface reflectance. Atmospheric absorption and scattering vary by wavelength, geometry and conditions, so a single arbitrary constant is generally inadequate.", "9. Absolute atmospheric correction and radiative transfer Slides 44–47; transcript atmospheric discussion What the lecture specifies Absolute correction uses acquisition-time atmospheric state and illumination/viewing geometry to estimate wavelength-dependent absorption and scattering. The slide names MODTRAN and 6S as radiative-transfer models and lists aerosol model, visibility, water-vapour column, ozone and CO₂ mixing ratio among relevant parameters. How it works conceptually Radiative-transfer software models the propagation of radiation from the Sun through atmosphere to the surface and back to the sensor. It estimates quantities needed to invert the measured radiance into surface reflectance, subject to atmospheric and surface assumptions. Strengths When atmospheric information and sensor calibration are credible, absolute correction supports comparison across dates and with field spectra in physically interpretable units. Limitations Atmospheric inputs can be uncertain, aerosols vary spatially, clouds and adjacency effects can violate assumptions, and bright land next to dark water can contaminate coastal pixels. An automated correction is still a model, not an independent truth. Validation Compare corrected spectra to field spectrometer observations or stable pseudo-invariant targets when possible. Inspect negative or greater-than-one reflectances, residual haze, band discontinuities and scene-edge artefacts; recognise that some values can exceed one under special reflectance definitions/conditions.", "10. Relative atmospheric normalisation Slide 42 contrasts absolute and relative correction; supplementary methodological detail What the slides distinguish The processing sequence shows absolute correction using a model atmosphere versus relative correction using regression applied to radiance or DN. The latter seeks relative comparability rather than full physical retrieval. Pseudo-invariant features A common supplementary approach identifies targets expected to be stable across dates, such as certain roads or bare surfaces, then fits bandwise transformations to align their measured values. Stability must be assessed rather than assumed. Worked regression Suppose stable features follow reference reflectance ≈ 0.90 × target-date value + 0.015. A target-date pixel value of 0.20 normalises to 0.195. This can reduce systematic differences but does not prove that 0.195 is physically accurate surface reflectance. When relative correction is appropriate It can be useful for change detection when absolute atmospheric information is unavailable and robust invariant targets exist. It is less appropriate if the entire landscape changes or if the analysis requires absolute biophysical values. Exam critique A student uses all pixels—including deforested areas—to fit relative normalisation between two dates. True change may be absorbed into the regression, biasing the final change estimate. Use defensible stable targets and validate independently.", "11. Terrain illumination: slope, aspect and solar geometry Slides 49–52; transcript later lecture Lecture principle The same land-cover type can have different recorded brightness because its surface faces toward or away from the Sun. Terrain effects combine slope/aspect and illumination/viewing geometry. Cosine of incidence — supplementary For terrain slope β, solar zenith θs and relative azimuth Δφ, cos i = cos θs cos β + sin θs sin β cos Δφ. A slope facing the Sun has larger cos i and receives more direct irradiance than an opposing slope. Illustration At solar zenith 40° and slope 20°, a sun-facing slope with Δφ=0° has cos i=cos(20°)=0.940; a slope facing away with Δφ=180° has cos i=cos(60°)=0.500. Even with identical surface material, direct illumination differs markedly. Slide approaches The lecture mentions scheduling near higher solar elevation and band ratios as approaches to reduce illumination effects. It also discusses terrain correction approaches; no single method removes all shadow or BRDF effects. Caveat on ratios A ratio may suppress a common multiplicative brightness factor, but not additive path radiance, cast shadow, wavelength-dependent scattering or differences in spectral response. Ratios do not make physically distinct observations universally comparable.", "12. BRDF: why the same surface looks different from different angles Slides 53–56; transcript ~02:19–02:21 Definition Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes how surface reflection varies with incoming illumination and outgoing viewing direction. Vegetation canopies and rough surfaces are not perfectly Lambertian. Geometry Differences in sun elevation/azimuth and satellite view angle can alter the balance of illuminated and shadowed canopy, producing reflectance differences without land-cover change. Practical response Use acquisition geometries that are as comparable as possible, BRDF-adjusted products where appropriate, and document angular differences. For time series, sensor geometry and seasonal sun angles can create systematic bias. Distinction from topographic correction Terrain correction addresses slope/aspect illumination at landscape scale. BRDF concerns directional reflectance properties of the surface itself, although the phenomena interact. Correcting one does not necessarily eliminate the other. Exam question Why might two surface-reflectance images of unchanged forest differ? Because atmospheric correction does not automatically standardise canopy BRDF, view geometry, terrain shading or phenology.", "13. Environment-specific correction: sun glint and shallow water Slides 57–59; related transcript discussion Source concept The lecture explicitly includes sun-glint from specular reflection off wave faces as an environment-specific distortion. It also identifies air–sea interface refraction and water-column effects in its overall preprocessing list. Why glint matters The sensor can receive sunlight reflected from the water surface rather than light emerging from the water column or seabed. The resulting bright streaks can mimic shallow substrate differences or obscure benthic signal. Supplementary correction logic A common empirical strategy relates visible-band glint to NIR brightness over deep water, where water-leaving NIR signal is small, then subtracts an estimated glint contribution. The assumptions can fail in turbid or shallow water, where NIR may not be negligible. Water-column effect Bottom reflectance is attenuated as light travels down and up through water. Depth, turbidity, dissolved matter and wavelength affect what reaches the sensor. Even perfectly atmospherically corrected imagery may not yield comparable benthic spectra at different depths. Coastal exam critique A team maps seagrass from optical surface reflectance without tidal, glint or depth considerations. A changed water level can alter apparent bottom brightness and area. Choose suitable tides, glint removal, water-column treatment and independent underwater validation.", "14. Building a defensible preprocessing workflow Synthesis of slides 9–59 and the matched VTT Step 1: define the target Is the goal visual interpretation, categorical mapping, physical reflectance comparison, water quality, or quantitative change? The necessary correction level depends on this target. Step 2: inspect products Read product specifications and metadata before applying operations. Determine whether the data are already geometrically corrected, radiometrically calibrated or atmospherically corrected. Step 3: ensure geometry Confirm CRS, datum, pixel size and grid origin; test registration with stable features and independent checkpoints. Use resampling suited to continuous or categorical values. Step 4: ensure radiometry Apply correct band-specific scale, offset and calibration only if needed. Compare physical units and band definitions; do not mix raw DN with corrected reflectance. Step 5: address atmosphere and geometry Apply appropriate atmospheric correction, terrain/BRDF adjustment and environment-specific corrections as required. Keep QA masks and record uncertain or invalid pixels. Step 6: test fitness Inspect spectral plausibility, cloud/shadow, residual geometry and temporal consistency. Only then classify, model or detect change. Reserve independent reference data for validation. Step 7: report uncertainty A processing pipeline can reduce systematic artefacts but not eliminate all error. Document residual limitations, thresholds, masks and how these affect conclusions.", "15. Full examination scenario: false mangrove change Original exam-style problem grounded in lecture concepts Scenario A researcher compares 2018 raw Landsat DN and 2026 Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, resamples the Landsat image to 10 m, calculates NDVI, thresholds a decline of 0.1, and reports 2.3 ha mangrove loss. There is no cloud mask, no registration test and no field reference. Error 1: incompatible units DN and surface reflectance are not comparable. Select harmonised corrected products and verify band scale/offset, wavelength response and calibration. Error 2: false spatial detail Upsampling 30 m pixels to 10 m creates more grid cells but not more measured detail. Establish common spatial support and a minimum mapping unit appropriate to mangrove width. Error 3: geometric and atmospheric artefacts Coastlines are sensitive to pixel misalignment, tides, glint and atmospheric differences. Check registration, cloud/shadow QA, comparable season/tide and water-adjacent pixels. Error 4: threshold and inference A −0.1 NDVI difference may arise from mixed pixels, phenology or transient stress, not necessarily mortality. Use time-series persistence and independent ecological observations. Defended alternative Define persistent canopy loss; obtain comparable calibrated observations; harmonise bands and spatial grids; quality-mask; verify co-registration; consider coastal water effects; model multi-date change; validate independently; report area and uncertainty rather than unsupported precision.", "16. Examination scenario: hillside vegetation Original applied reasoning Scenario A classifier trained on sunlit forest labels shaded forest as a different species. The team proposes increasing model complexity without changing imagery. Diagnosis The classifier may be responding to illumination and topographic effects, not species. More complex classification alone cannot repair a systematic input measurement problem. Correction Check solar geometry, terrain slope/aspect, cast-shadow masks and suitable topographic/BRDF correction. Include representative training across illumination conditions and independent stratified validation. Limits Deep shadow may have low signal-to-noise ratio; no correction can fully recover missing information. Mask or qualify the areas rather than inventing reliable spectra.", "17. Calculation and reasoning practice Original questions with answers Q1 — radiance With gain=0.02 and offset=−2, DN=600 yields L=10 in the matching radiance units. Do not interpret this as surface reflectance. Q2 — RMSE Residuals (0.3,0.4) and (0.0,0.5) pixels give squared distances 0.25 and 0.25; RMSE = sqrt(0.5/2)=0.5 pixel. This is exactly the threshold, not below it. Q3 — interpolation For a land-cover raster choose nearest neighbour; bilinear would create intermediate, invalid category values. Q4 — atmosphere An image calibrated to TOA radiance still contains path radiance and absorption/scattering. It is not equivalent to surface reflectance. Q5 — relative correction Relative normalisation can improve consistency but does not necessarily retrieve absolute physical surface reflectance. Q6 — terrain Band ratios can reduce multiplicative illumination differences but cannot reliably fix cast shadows or all wavelength-dependent effects. Q7 — water Atmospheric correction alone does not remove sun glint or water-column attenuation. Q8 — metadata The correct first step with a downloaded ARD product is to inspect processing specifications and QA layers, not to blindly repeat every correction.", "18. Source integrity and study priorities Source audit Directly used geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf, 60 pages; GMT20260826-012440_Recording.transcript.vtt, 26 August 2026 lecture. Slide references in this chapter use physical PDF page numbers; timestamps refer to the VTT recording. Label mismatch The slide footer identifies Lecture 4 while the recording introduction calls it the fourth lecture held in Week 5, because Week 3 had no lecture. This chapter uses Week 5 to avoid confusion. Supplementary material Numerical exercises, conventional RMSE clarification, simplified atmospheric equation, topographic incidence equation and exam scenarios are explanatory additions. They are not attributed verbatim to the lecturer. High-priority revision Memorise distinctions between DN/radiance/TOA/surface reflectance, rectification/registration, geometric/intensity interpolation, absolute/relative atmospheric correction, topographic/BRDF correction and ARD/fitness for purpose. Then practise explaining the consequence of each omission."]}, {"id": 5, "title": "Image Classification & Object-Based Image Analysis", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week06_Classification_OBIA.pdf", "pages": 10, "sections": [{"title": "1. Where classification fits in the EO workflow", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 1–8; transcript 00:22:39–00:33:00</p><h3>Course framing</h3><p>Will Woodgate introduces Associate Professor Chris Roelfsema as the guest lecturer for image classification. The slides position classification after image acquisition and preprocessing, and before information products and evaluation. A visually pleasing image is not yet a thematic map: classification translates measured spectral variation into defined information categories.</p><h3>Thematic versus quantitative outputs</h3><p>A thematic product assigns a category such as sand, seagrass or coral. A quantitative product estimates a continuous property such as percentage cover or biomass. The question “what is the output variable?” determines the appropriate reference observations, algorithm and validation metric.</p><h3>The inverse problem</h3><p>A sensor measures radiance or reflectance, not land-cover labels. Different covers may share similar spectra, and the same cover may vary with illumination, moisture, canopy structure and atmosphere. Therefore classification is inference from imperfect observations, not direct identification.</p><h3>Exam defence</h3><p>Define the mapped classes, sensor and resolution, time of observation, spatial unit, intended use and independent reference data before choosing an algorithm. An unsupported claim that a method is “most accurate” is weaker than a defensible choice matched to the problem.</p>", "text": "Slides 1–8; transcript 00:22:39–00:33:00 Course framing Will Woodgate introduces Associate Professor Chris Roelfsema as the guest lecturer for image classification. The slides position classification after image acquisition and preprocessing, and before information products and evaluation. A visually pleasing image is not yet a thematic map: classification translates measured spectral variation into defined information categories. Thematic versus quantitative outputs A thematic product assigns a category such as sand, seagrass or coral. A quantitative product estimates a continuous property such as percentage cover or biomass. The question “what is the output variable?” determines the appropriate reference observations, algorithm and validation metric. The inverse problem A sensor measures radiance or reflectance, not land-cover labels. Different covers may share similar spectra, and the same cover may vary with illumination, moisture, canopy structure and atmosphere. Therefore classification is inference from imperfect observations, not direct identification. Exam defence Define the mapped classes, sensor and resolution, time of observation, spatial unit, intended use and independent reference data before choosing an algorithm. An unsupported claim that a method is “most accurate” is weaker than a defensible choice matched to the problem."}, {"title": "2. Univariate image statistics", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 9–14; lecture section on image statistics</p><h3>Frequency distributions</h3><p>A single-band histogram summarises pixel values. The mode is most frequent, median is central ordered value, mean is arithmetic average, and range spans minimum to maximum. These measures are descriptive, not land-cover labels.</p><h3>Variance and standard deviation</h3><p>Variance describes squared spread around the mean; standard deviation returns to original band units. A class with broad spread may contain multiple substrates, illumination conditions or mixed pixels. A low spread does not establish class uniqueness.</p><h3>Worked calculation</h3><p>For reflectance values 0.10, 0.12, 0.13, 0.15, 0.50, the mean is 0.20 while the median is 0.13. The bright outlier raises the mean substantially. Inspect whether outliers represent real bright cover, cloud, glint or processing errors before removing them.</p><h3>Why statistics matter</h3><p>Classification relies on whether within-class variation is small enough relative to between-class differences. Histograms can reveal skewness, multimodality, saturation and overlap that complicate a single Gaussian class model.</p>", "text": "Slides 9–14; lecture section on image statistics Frequency distributions A single-band histogram summarises pixel values. The mode is most frequent, median is central ordered value, mean is arithmetic average, and range spans minimum to maximum. These measures are descriptive, not land-cover labels. Variance and standard deviation Variance describes squared spread around the mean; standard deviation returns to original band units. A class with broad spread may contain multiple substrates, illumination conditions or mixed pixels. A low spread does not establish class uniqueness. Worked calculation For reflectance values 0.10, 0.12, 0.13, 0.15, 0.50, the mean is 0.20 while the median is 0.13. The bright outlier raises the mean substantially. Inspect whether outliers represent real bright cover, cloud, glint or processing errors before removing them. Why statistics matter Classification relies on whether within-class variation is small enough relative to between-class differences. Histograms can reveal skewness, multimodality, saturation and overlap that complicate a single Gaussian class model."}, {"title": "3. Multivariate statistics and spectral feature space", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 10–17</p><h3>Pixels as vectors</h3><p>With B bands, one pixel is a B-dimensional vector x = [x1, x2, …, xB]. Two classes may overlap in one band but separate when several bands are considered jointly.</p><h3>Covariance</h3><p>Covariance captures how two bands vary together. Strong correlations mean many channels may contribute redundant information. Covariance matrices also describe the shape and orientation of a class cloud in feature space.</p><h3>Why units matter</h3><p>Euclidean distances depend on variable scale: a 0–10000 reflectance integer band can dominate a 0–1 index unless predictors are consistently scaled. This is a supplementary modelling caution, not a slide-specific numerical rule.</p><h3>Worked spectral separation</h3><p>Suppose sand has mean [0.30, 0.35] and vegetation [0.08, 0.42] in red/NIR reflectance. The Euclidean separation of means is sqrt((0.22)^2+(−0.07)^2) ≈ 0.231. Whether this is useful depends on each class’s spread and covariance, not the mean difference alone.</p>", "text": "Slides 10–17 Pixels as vectors With B bands, one pixel is a B-dimensional vector x = [x1, x2, …, xB]. Two classes may overlap in one band but separate when several bands are considered jointly. Covariance Covariance captures how two bands vary together. Strong correlations mean many channels may contribute redundant information. Covariance matrices also describe the shape and orientation of a class cloud in feature space. Why units matter Euclidean distances depend on variable scale: a 0–10000 reflectance integer band can dominate a 0–1 index unless predictors are consistently scaled. This is a supplementary modelling caution, not a slide-specific numerical rule. Worked spectral separation Suppose sand has mean [0.30, 0.35] and vegetation [0.08, 0.42] in red/NIR reflectance. The Euclidean separation of means is sqrt((0.22)^2+(−0.07)^2) ≈ 0.231. Whether this is useful depends on each class’s spread and covariance, not the mean difference alone."}, {"title": "4. Spectral classes versus information classes", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 15–22</p><h3>Spectral class</h3><p>A spectral class groups pixels that are similar in feature space. It may combine distinct ecological substrates with similar reflectance, or divide one ecological type into sunny/shaded spectral subclasses.</p><h3>Information class</h3><p>An information class is defined by the mapping objective, such as live coral, macroalgae, seagrass, sand or deep water. It requires an explicit definition and appropriate ground evidence.</p><h3>Mapping between the two</h3><p>Unsupervised clustering first yields spectral groups that must be interpreted and assigned to information classes. Supervised learning associates training examples with information classes before prediction. Neither approach escapes class-definition ambiguity.</p><h3>Mixed pixels</h3><p>At coarse resolution a pixel may include coral, sand and algae. A hard label loses within-pixel fractions. Subpixel approaches instead estimate class abundance or membership, but require additional assumptions and validation.</p>", "text": "Slides 15–22 Spectral class A spectral class groups pixels that are similar in feature space. It may combine distinct ecological substrates with similar reflectance, or divide one ecological type into sunny/shaded spectral subclasses. Information class An information class is defined by the mapping objective, such as live coral, macroalgae, seagrass, sand or deep water. It requires an explicit definition and appropriate ground evidence. Mapping between the two Unsupervised clustering first yields spectral groups that must be interpreted and assigned to information classes. Supervised learning associates training examples with information classes before prediction. Neither approach escapes class-definition ambiguity. Mixed pixels At coarse resolution a pixel may include coral, sand and algae. A hard label loses within-pixel fractions. Subpixel approaches instead estimate class abundance or membership, but require additional assumptions and validation."}, {"title": "5. A complete classification sequence", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 17–29</p><h3>Define categories</h3><p>Start with classes that are observable, relevant and mutually interpretable at the chosen scale. A hierarchical legend can separate broad geomorphic zones first and detailed benthic categories second.</p><h3>Select source imagery</h3><p>Check wavelength coverage, spatial resolution, acquisition timing, tides, depth, atmospheric correction and geometric alignment. In aquatic imagery, water depth and water-column effects can dominate substrate signals.</p><h3>Collect training observations</h3><p>Use representative reference sites across the full range of class conditions, avoiding exclusive sampling of the easiest textbook examples. Keep training and independent assessment samples distinct.</p><h3>Evaluate features and classes</h3><p>Inspect statistics and spectral separability, then choose predictors and an algorithm. If two classes remain inseparable, reconsider the legend, sensor or scale rather than forcing a high-confidence distinction.</p><h3>Predict, postprocess and validate</h3><p>Apply the trained method, inspect spatial artefacts and confusion, and estimate accuracy with independent samples. Document what was masked, smoothed or merged so the final map is reproducible.</p>", "text": "Slides 17–29 Define categories Start with classes that are observable, relevant and mutually interpretable at the chosen scale. A hierarchical legend can separate broad geomorphic zones first and detailed benthic categories second. Select source imagery Check wavelength coverage, spatial resolution, acquisition timing, tides, depth, atmospheric correction and geometric alignment. In aquatic imagery, water depth and water-column effects can dominate substrate signals. Collect training observations Use representative reference sites across the full range of class conditions, avoiding exclusive sampling of the easiest textbook examples. Keep training and independent assessment samples distinct. Evaluate features and classes Inspect statistics and spectral separability, then choose predictors and an algorithm. If two classes remain inseparable, reconsider the legend, sensor or scale rather than forcing a high-confidence distinction. Predict, postprocess and validate Apply the trained method, inspect spatial artefacts and confusion, and estimate accuracy with independent samples. Document what was masked, smoothed or merged so the final map is reproducible."}, {"title": "6. Training regions of interest (ROIs)", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 27–36; transcript around 00:47:00</p><h3>What an ROI represents</h3><p>A region of interest contains pixels assumed to belong to one information class. Its sample statistics become a model of that class in spectral feature space. The lecture explicitly discusses assessing training sites rather than treating every polygon as suitable.</p><h3>Homogeneity and representativeness</h3><p>An ROI may be internally heterogeneous because of shade, substrate mixtures or geolocation error. Conversely, an extremely pure ROI can underrepresent real-world class variability. Aim for known labels and coverage of legitimate variability.</p><h3>Training sample leakage</h3><p>Adjacent pixels are autocorrelated. Randomly distributing neighbouring pixels between training and validation can overstate transfer accuracy. Separate sites, reef zones, transects or acquisition dates when the intended deployment requires independence.</p><h3>Practical inspection</h3><p>Plot class histograms and pairwise band scatterplots; review outliers against source imagery; compare within-class spread with between-class separation; and check that each ROI matches the stated class definition.</p>", "text": "Slides 27–36; transcript around 00:47:00 What an ROI represents A region of interest contains pixels assumed to belong to one information class. Its sample statistics become a model of that class in spectral feature space. The lecture explicitly discusses assessing training sites rather than treating every polygon as suitable. Homogeneity and representativeness An ROI may be internally heterogeneous because of shade, substrate mixtures or geolocation error. Conversely, an extremely pure ROI can underrepresent real-world class variability. Aim for known labels and coverage of legitimate variability. Training sample leakage Adjacent pixels are autocorrelated. Randomly distributing neighbouring pixels between training and validation can overstate transfer accuracy. Separate sites, reef zones, transects or acquisition dates when the intended deployment requires independence. Practical inspection Plot class histograms and pairwise band scatterplots; review outliers against source imagery; compare within-class spread with between-class separation; and check that each ROI matches the stated class definition."}, {"title": "7. Spectral separability and Jeffries–Matusita distance", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 30–36; transcript about 01:02:50</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p>Separability measures assess whether training classes occupy distinguishable regions of feature space. The transcript explicitly mentions Jeffries–Matusita distance while discussing training class evaluation.</p><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>A high separability score indicates distinct distributions under the chosen bands and statistical assumptions; a low score warns that the classifier may confuse the classes. The score is not independent map accuracy.</p><h3>How to respond to poor separation</h3><p>Inspect labels and reference data, try physically meaningful alternative bands or indices, include texture or context, stratify by habitat zone, or merge categories that cannot be resolved with available data.</p><h3>Avoid circular reasoning</h3><p>Selecting bands based on all labelled data and then testing on the same labels produces optimistic results. Feature selection should be conducted within training data, with an independent holdout for evaluation.</p>", "text": "Slides 30–36; transcript about 01:02:50 Purpose Separability measures assess whether training classes occupy distinguishable regions of feature space. The transcript explicitly mentions Jeffries–Matusita distance while discussing training class evaluation. Interpretation A high separability score indicates distinct distributions under the chosen bands and statistical assumptions; a low score warns that the classifier may confuse the classes. The score is not independent map accuracy. How to respond to poor separation Inspect labels and reference data, try physically meaningful alternative bands or indices, include texture or context, stratify by habitat zone, or merge categories that cannot be resolved with available data. Avoid circular reasoning Selecting bands based on all labelled data and then testing on the same labels produces optimistic results. Feature selection should be conducted within training data, with an independent holdout for evaluation."}, {"title": "8. Supervised algorithms: parallelepiped and minimum distance", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30</p><h3>Parallelepiped</h3><p>Each class is represented by band-wise lower and upper thresholds, forming a box in feature space. It is transparent and fast but can leave unclassified pixels or assign overlapping boxes ambiguously.</p><h3>Minimum distance to means</h3><p>For each pixel calculate distance to each class mean vector and assign the nearest class. This is straightforward but ignores the direction and shape of each class distribution.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>Pixel x = [0.14,0.38], sand mean [0.30,0.35], vegetation mean [0.08,0.42]. Distances are approximately 0.163 and 0.072, respectively; minimum distance selects vegetation. This calculation does not prove the vegetation label is correct.</p><h3>When not to use</h3><p>If classes have strongly unequal covariance or elongated spectral distributions, nearest-mean decisions can be inappropriate. Inspect class scatter and consider a covariance-aware or flexible model.</p>", "text": "Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30 Parallelepiped Each class is represented by band-wise lower and upper thresholds, forming a box in feature space. It is transparent and fast but can leave unclassified pixels or assign overlapping boxes ambiguously. Minimum distance to means For each pixel calculate distance to each class mean vector and assign the nearest class. This is straightforward but ignores the direction and shape of each class distribution. Worked example Pixel x = [0.14,0.38], sand mean [0.30,0.35], vegetation mean [0.08,0.42]. Distances are approximately 0.163 and 0.072, respectively; minimum distance selects vegetation. This calculation does not prove the vegetation label is correct. When not to use If classes have strongly unequal covariance or elongated spectral distributions, nearest-mean decisions can be inappropriate. Inspect class scatter and consider a covariance-aware or flexible model."}, {"title": "9. Maximum likelihood classification", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30</p><h3>Statistical model</h3><p>Maximum likelihood classification typically models the spectral distribution of each class with a multivariate normal distribution. It uses class means and covariance matrices to calculate the relative likelihood of a pixel under each class.</p><h3>What covariance adds</h3><p>Unlike minimum distance, the method accounts for spread and correlation. A pixel may be far from a class mean along a high-variance direction yet still be plausible for that class.</p><h3>Assumptions and failure</h3><p>Small training samples, strongly non-normal or multimodal distributions, and unstable covariance estimates can undermine predictions. Mixed pixels and changing water depth also challenge class distributions.</p><h3>Defend the choice</h3><p>Use maximum likelihood when the class distributions and sample sizes justify its assumptions, not merely because it is a traditional EO algorithm. Compare alternatives with the same independent reference design.</p>", "text": "Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30 Statistical model Maximum likelihood classification typically models the spectral distribution of each class with a multivariate normal distribution. It uses class means and covariance matrices to calculate the relative likelihood of a pixel under each class. What covariance adds Unlike minimum distance, the method accounts for spread and correlation. A pixel may be far from a class mean along a high-variance direction yet still be plausible for that class. Assumptions and failure Small training samples, strongly non-normal or multimodal distributions, and unstable covariance estimates can undermine predictions. Mixed pixels and changing water depth also challenge class distributions. Defend the choice Use maximum likelihood when the class distributions and sample sizes justify its assumptions, not merely because it is a traditional EO algorithm. Compare alternatives with the same independent reference design."}, {"title": "10. Random forest and modern supervised methods", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 37–50; transcript around 01:05:34</p><h3>Random forest</h3><p>An ensemble of decision trees predicts from spectral bands and potentially indices, texture and spatial variables. It can learn nonlinear class boundaries and interactions without assuming multivariate normal classes.</p><h3>Hyperparameters</h3><p>Tree count, maximum depth, minimum leaf size and feature sampling affect complexity and runtime. Tune on training data using spatially appropriate cross-validation, not the final independent test set.</p><h3>Limits</h3><p>Random forest can still learn site-specific shadows, depth or acquisition artefacts. Feature importance is not causal attribution, and probability-like vote fractions may need calibration.</p><h3>Foundation models</h3><p>The deck also introduces EO foundation models as a newer approach. Pretrained representations can help downstream tasks, but benchmark performance, transferability, reference quality and independent validation remain essential.</p>", "text": "Slides 37–50; transcript around 01:05:34 Random forest An ensemble of decision trees predicts from spectral bands and potentially indices, texture and spatial variables. It can learn nonlinear class boundaries and interactions without assuming multivariate normal classes. Hyperparameters Tree count, maximum depth, minimum leaf size and feature sampling affect complexity and runtime. Tune on training data using spatially appropriate cross-validation, not the final independent test set. Limits Random forest can still learn site-specific shadows, depth or acquisition artefacts. Feature importance is not causal attribution, and probability-like vote fractions may need calibration. Foundation models The deck also introduces EO foundation models as a newer approach. Pretrained representations can help downstream tasks, but benchmark performance, transferability, reference quality and independent validation remain essential."}, {"title": "11. Unsupervised clustering and interpretation", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 44–50</p><h3>Clustering objective</h3><p>Unsupervised algorithms group spectrally similar pixels without starting from labelled information classes. The operator must select or evaluate cluster number and later interpret the groups.</p><h3>Typical use</h3><p>Clustering can reveal unexpected spectral patterns, support exploratory mapping and help design training samples. It is not automatically a habitat map.</p><h3>Interpretation challenge</h3><p>One cluster may contain multiple ecological classes; one ecological class may split into sunlit and shaded clusters. Assign information labels using imagery, field data and ecological context.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>“No training labels means no need for ground truth” is incorrect. Labels and independent validation are still needed to establish what clusters mean and how reliable the thematic map is.</p>", "text": "Slides 44–50 Clustering objective Unsupervised algorithms group spectrally similar pixels without starting from labelled information classes. The operator must select or evaluate cluster number and later interpret the groups. Typical use Clustering can reveal unexpected spectral patterns, support exploratory mapping and help design training samples. It is not automatically a habitat map. Interpretation challenge One cluster may contain multiple ecological classes; one ecological class may split into sunlit and shaded clusters. Assign information labels using imagery, field data and ecological context. Exam critique “No training labels means no need for ground truth” is incorrect. Labels and independent validation are still needed to establish what clusters mean and how reliable the thematic map is."}, {"title": "12. Pixel-based versus subpixel mapping", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 21–26</p><h3>Hard classification</h3><p>A per-pixel hard map assigns one dominant category. This is simple to display and analyse but obscures mixtures and boundary uncertainty.</p><h3>Subpixel approach</h3><p>Fractional cover or spectral unmixing estimates the contribution of endmembers within a pixel. Such models depend on endmember representativeness, mixture assumptions and signal-to-noise ratio.</p><h3>Aquatic caution</h3><p>In shallow water, water depth and optical properties can change observed substrate spectra, violating naive linear mixtures of bottom reflectance at the sensor.</p><h3>Scale selection</h3><p>Use a categorical map for discrete management classes when spatial resolution and validation support them. Use fractional outputs when the management question concerns percentage cover and mixtures are substantial.</p>", "text": "Slides 21–26 Hard classification A per-pixel hard map assigns one dominant category. This is simple to display and analyse but obscures mixtures and boundary uncertainty. Subpixel approach Fractional cover or spectral unmixing estimates the contribution of endmembers within a pixel. Such models depend on endmember representativeness, mixture assumptions and signal-to-noise ratio. Aquatic caution In shallow water, water depth and optical properties can change observed substrate spectra, violating naive linear mixtures of bottom reflectance at the sensor. Scale selection Use a categorical map for discrete management classes when spatial resolution and validation support them. Use fractional outputs when the management question concerns percentage cover and mixtures are substantial."}, {"title": "13. Why object-based image analysis?", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 52–63; transcript approximately 01:35–01:42</p><h3>Core concept</h3><p>Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) groups neighbouring pixels into segments, then classifies those objects using spectral, shape, size, texture, context and relational properties.</p><h3>Motivation</h3><p>High-resolution imagery can show substantial within-object texture, shadow and pixel-scale noise. Object-based classification can represent coherent habitat patches and reduce salt-and-pepper patterns.</p><h3>Not automatically superior</h3><p>Poor segmentation may merge different habitats or fragment one habitat. OBIA performance depends on the mapping unit, segmentation parameters, available features and class hierarchy.</p><h3>When pixel methods win</h3><p>If target features are smaller than segments, spectral variation is already highly distinctive, or boundaries must be resolved precisely, a pixel approach may be simpler and more faithful.</p>", "text": "Slides 52–63; transcript approximately 01:35–01:42 Core concept Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) groups neighbouring pixels into segments, then classifies those objects using spectral, shape, size, texture, context and relational properties. Motivation High-resolution imagery can show substantial within-object texture, shadow and pixel-scale noise. Object-based classification can represent coherent habitat patches and reduce salt-and-pepper patterns. Not automatically superior Poor segmentation may merge different habitats or fragment one habitat. OBIA performance depends on the mapping unit, segmentation parameters, available features and class hierarchy. When pixel methods win If target features are smaller than segments, spectral variation is already highly distinctive, or boundaries must be resolved precisely, a pixel approach may be simpler and more faithful."}, {"title": "14. Segmentation as a modelling decision", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 77–85; transcript around 01:38:49</p><h3>What segmentation does</h3><p>Segmentation partitions an image into spatially contiguous regions based on similarity criteria. It creates analysis units but does not itself establish semantic classes.</p><h3>Top-down versus bottom-up</h3><p>Top-down segmentation subdivides large regions into smaller objects; bottom-up segmentation merges pixels or small objects into larger ones. The slides explicitly contrast these strategies.</p><h3>Scale parameter</h3><p>Coarse segmentation creates larger, more homogeneous-in-context objects but risks mixing classes; fine segmentation follows detail but can fragment habitats and amplify noise.</p><h3>Assess quality</h3><p>Compare object boundaries with independent reference features and ecological patch sizes. Do not select scale solely because the resulting polygons look neat.</p>", "text": "Slides 77–85; transcript around 01:38:49 What segmentation does Segmentation partitions an image into spatially contiguous regions based on similarity criteria. It creates analysis units but does not itself establish semantic classes. Top-down versus bottom-up Top-down segmentation subdivides large regions into smaller objects; bottom-up segmentation merges pixels or small objects into larger ones. The slides explicitly contrast these strategies. Scale parameter Coarse segmentation creates larger, more homogeneous-in-context objects but risks mixing classes; fine segmentation follows detail but can fragment habitats and amplify noise. Assess quality Compare object boundaries with independent reference features and ecological patch sizes. Do not select scale solely because the resulting polygons look neat."}, {"title": "15. Hierarchical coral-reef mapping", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 63–79; reef examples</p><h3>Hierarchy</h3><p>The lecture uses reef examples to illustrate large-scale geomorphic zones and finer benthic communities. A broad reef-flat, lagoon or crest zone can constrain which finer categories are plausible.</p><h3>Physical context</h3><p>The same bottom material can have different image signatures with depth, waves and illumination. Mapping zones first can help avoid comparing physically dissimilar pixels as if they were identical.</p><h3>Context and relationships</h3><p>Object features can include adjacency, relative position, size, shape, mean reflectance and texture. For example, a bright shallow feature next to a reef crest may be interpreted differently from an equally bright isolated offshore patch.</p><h3>Caution</h3><p>A hierarchy may encode prior expectations incorrectly. Unexpected habitats or changed geomorphology can be forced into unsuitable categories unless exceptions and validation are allowed.</p>", "text": "Slides 63–79; reef examples Hierarchy The lecture uses reef examples to illustrate large-scale geomorphic zones and finer benthic communities. A broad reef-flat, lagoon or crest zone can constrain which finer categories are plausible. Physical context The same bottom material can have different image signatures with depth, waves and illumination. Mapping zones first can help avoid comparing physically dissimilar pixels as if they were identical. Context and relationships Object features can include adjacency, relative position, size, shape, mean reflectance and texture. For example, a bright shallow feature next to a reef crest may be interpreted differently from an equally bright isolated offshore patch. Caution A hierarchy may encode prior expectations incorrectly. Unexpected habitats or changed geomorphology can be forced into unsuitable categories unless exceptions and validation are allowed."}, {"title": "16. OBIA rule sets and class hierarchy", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 84–96</p><h3>Rule set</h3><p>An OBIA workflow can use sequential rules: segment large zones, calculate object features, apply thresholds or classifiers, refine subclasses and merge or split objects as needed.</p><h3>Process trees</h3><p>The slides show process-tree structure and parent/child relationships. These support reproducible multistage logic, but every threshold needs a rationale and ideally sensitivity analysis.</p><h3>Example</h3><p>First separate water from land, then segment shallow reef regions, then classify benthic objects using spectral and texture features. This is a supplementary illustrative workflow, not a claim of the exact lecture rule set.</p><h3>Auditability</h3><p>Record segmentation scale, feature definitions, threshold values, training samples, postprocessing rules and software version. An undocumented expert-tuned rule set is difficult to reproduce.</p>", "text": "Slides 84–96 Rule set An OBIA workflow can use sequential rules: segment large zones, calculate object features, apply thresholds or classifiers, refine subclasses and merge or split objects as needed. Process trees The slides show process-tree structure and parent/child relationships. These support reproducible multistage logic, but every threshold needs a rationale and ideally sensitivity analysis. Example First separate water from land, then segment shallow reef regions, then classify benthic objects using spectral and texture features. This is a supplementary illustrative workflow, not a claim of the exact lecture rule set. Auditability Record segmentation scale, feature definitions, threshold values, training samples, postprocessing rules and software version. An undocumented expert-tuned rule set is difficult to reproduce."}, {"title": "17. Critical comparison: pixel versus object methods", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Slides 52–96</p><h3>Pixel method advantages</h3><p>Directly relates each measurement to a prediction, is simple to train and often computationally efficient. It preserves local spectral variation and avoids segmentation parameter choices.</p><h3>Pixel method limitations</h3><p>Mixed pixels, high-frequency noise, geometric misalignment and fragmented output can reduce interpretability, especially in very high-resolution imagery.</p><h3>Object method advantages</h3><p>Spatial coherence, shape, texture and contextual relations can better represent landscape or reef patches. It may reduce effects of slight image misregistration, as highlighted in the deck.</p><h3>Object method limitations</h3><p>Segmentation errors propagate into class labels; a polygon may contain multiple habitats; segmentation and rule sets can be subjective and computationally demanding.</p><h3>Decision rule</h3><p>Select based on target patch size, sensor resolution, class separability, geometric quality, desired map unit and reference sampling. Test both on a common independent assessment set if practical.</p>", "text": "Slides 52–96 Pixel method advantages Directly relates each measurement to a prediction, is simple to train and often computationally efficient. It preserves local spectral variation and avoids segmentation parameter choices. Pixel method limitations Mixed pixels, high-frequency noise, geometric misalignment and fragmented output can reduce interpretability, especially in very high-resolution imagery. Object method advantages Spatial coherence, shape, texture and contextual relations can better represent landscape or reef patches. It may reduce effects of slight image misregistration, as highlighted in the deck. Object method limitations Segmentation errors propagate into class labels; a polygon may contain multiple habitats; segmentation and rule sets can be subjective and computationally demanding. Decision rule Select based on target patch size, sensor resolution, class separability, geometric quality, desired map unit and reference sampling. Test both on a common independent assessment set if practical."}, {"title": "18. Worked case: reef benthic map", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original synthesis grounded in lecture methods</p><h3>Scenario</h3><p>You need to classify live coral, rubble, macroalgae, sand and deep water from high-resolution multispectral imagery of a reef. Reference points are concentrated in clear shallow water.</p><h3>Step 1: revise the design</h3><p>Define classes and minimum mapping unit; assess tide, water clarity, sunglint, depth and spatial resolution. Decide whether deep water should be masked or modelled separately.</p><h3>Step 2: collect representative reference</h3><p>Stratify observations across depth, reef zones, substrate types and illumination conditions. Separate independent validation sites or transects from training ROIs.</p><h3>Step 3: choose modelling unit</h3><p>Start with spectral pixel classification as a baseline. Test OBIA where coherent patches and geomorphic context provide meaningful information; document segmentation scale and object features.</p><h3>Step 4: evaluate</h3><p>Report class-specific errors and spatial patterns, not just overall accuracy. Inspect confusion between coral, rubble and algae, particularly across depth zones.</p><h3>Step 5: communicate</h3><p>Describe the acquisition conditions, mapped categories, unclassified/uncertain areas, intended scale and independent validation limitations.</p>", "text": "Original synthesis grounded in lecture methods Scenario You need to classify live coral, rubble, macroalgae, sand and deep water from high-resolution multispectral imagery of a reef. Reference points are concentrated in clear shallow water. Step 1: revise the design Define classes and minimum mapping unit; assess tide, water clarity, sunglint, depth and spatial resolution. Decide whether deep water should be masked or modelled separately. Step 2: collect representative reference Stratify observations across depth, reef zones, substrate types and illumination conditions. Separate independent validation sites or transects from training ROIs. Step 3: choose modelling unit Start with spectral pixel classification as a baseline. Test OBIA where coherent patches and geomorphic context provide meaningful information; document segmentation scale and object features. Step 4: evaluate Report class-specific errors and spatial patterns, not just overall accuracy. Inspect confusion between coral, rubble and algae, particularly across depth zones. Step 5: communicate Describe the acquisition conditions, mapped categories, unclassified/uncertain areas, intended scale and independent validation limitations."}, {"title": "19. Worked accuracy example and interpretation", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Supplementary exam exercise; not a claimed Week 6 lecture result</p><h3>Confusion matrix</h3><p>Suppose 100 independent reference samples: 40 coral and 60 non-coral. The map predicts coral for 35 true coral and 10 non-coral; predicts non-coral for 5 coral and 50 non-coral.</p><h3>Overall accuracy</h3><p>Correct = 35+50 = 85 of 100, so overall accuracy = 85%. This number hides different omission and commission errors.</p><h3>Coral producer’s accuracy</h3><p>35/40 = 87.5%. Of reference coral samples, 12.5% were omitted by the map.</p><h3>Coral user’s accuracy</h3><p>35/(35+10) = 77.8%. Of mapped coral samples, 22.2% are actually non-coral in this reference set.</p><h3>Design warning</h3><p>These proportions are simple sample calculations. For area estimates under stratified sampling, use appropriate design weights; do not assume a non-probability reference sample gives unbiased population accuracy.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exam exercise; not a claimed Week 6 lecture result Confusion matrix Suppose 100 independent reference samples: 40 coral and 60 non-coral. The map predicts coral for 35 true coral and 10 non-coral; predicts non-coral for 5 coral and 50 non-coral. Overall accuracy Correct = 35+50 = 85 of 100, so overall accuracy = 85%. This number hides different omission and commission errors. Coral producer’s accuracy 35/40 = 87.5%. Of reference coral samples, 12.5% were omitted by the map. Coral user’s accuracy 35/(35+10) = 77.8%. Of mapped coral samples, 22.2% are actually non-coral in this reference set. Design warning These proportions are simple sample calculations. For area estimates under stratified sampling, use appropriate design weights; do not assume a non-probability reference sample gives unbiased population accuracy."}, {"title": "20. Exam-style critique and model answer", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Original integrated question</p><h3>Question</h3><p>A researcher chooses 30 coral pixels and 30 sand pixels from the same reef image, obtains 99% training accuracy with random forest, then segments the image into large objects and claims the final reef map is 99% accurate.</p><h3>Issue 1: labels</h3><p>Two classes may be insufficient if rubble, algae, seagrass, shadow and deep water occur. The legend is incomplete and predictions outside the training domain may be misleading.</p><h3>Issue 2: sample design</h3><p>Thirty nearby pixels per class may not capture within-class variability. Training accuracy is not independent accuracy, and adjacent pixels cause spatial leakage.</p><h3>Issue 3: preprocessing</h3><p>Depth, sunglint, atmosphere, sensor geometry and water conditions can create systematic spectral differences unrelated to benthic class.</p><h3>Issue 4: segmentation</h3><p>Large segments may mix coral and sand, and segmentation changes the prediction unit. Training-pixel performance does not validate final object labels.</p><h3>Defensible answer</h3><p>Define a complete hierarchy, obtain representative field reference across zones and depths, independently assess pixel and object alternatives, report class-specific accuracy and spatial uncertainty, and justify the final map scale.</p>", "text": "Original integrated question Question A researcher chooses 30 coral pixels and 30 sand pixels from the same reef image, obtains 99% training accuracy with random forest, then segments the image into large objects and claims the final reef map is 99% accurate. Issue 1: labels Two classes may be insufficient if rubble, algae, seagrass, shadow and deep water occur. The legend is incomplete and predictions outside the training domain may be misleading. Issue 2: sample design Thirty nearby pixels per class may not capture within-class variability. Training accuracy is not independent accuracy, and adjacent pixels cause spatial leakage. Issue 3: preprocessing Depth, sunglint, atmosphere, sensor geometry and water conditions can create systematic spectral differences unrelated to benthic class. Issue 4: segmentation Large segments may mix coral and sand, and segmentation changes the prediction unit. Training-pixel performance does not validate final object labels. Defensible answer Define a complete hierarchy, obtain representative field reference across zones and depths, independently assess pixel and object alternatives, report class-specific accuracy and spatial uncertainty, and justify the final map scale."}, {"title": "21. Quick oral examination prompts", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Course-grounded questions with concise answers</p><h3>Why does training-site separability matter?</h3><p>Because overlapping class distributions imply expected confusion; separability is a diagnostic, not a substitute for independent accuracy assessment.</p><h3>What does maximum likelihood assume?</h3><p>Usually a multivariate normal distribution for each spectral class, estimated using mean and covariance.</p><h3>What is the main difference between classification and segmentation?</h3><p>Segmentation creates spatial units; classification assigns information labels to pixels or objects.</p><h3>Why can OBIA reduce salt-and-pepper noise?</h3><p>It predicts coherent segments using aggregated and contextual properties instead of independently labelling each pixel.</p><h3>What is the key weakness of overall accuracy?</h3><p>It can conceal severe errors for a particular class and depends on reference sampling.</p><h3>How do you defend a model choice?</h3><p>Match the model to physical measurement conditions, class definitions, training design, spatial unit and independent validation rather than choosing solely by a high reported score.</p>", "text": "Course-grounded questions with concise answers Why does training-site separability matter? Because overlapping class distributions imply expected confusion; separability is a diagnostic, not a substitute for independent accuracy assessment. What does maximum likelihood assume? Usually a multivariate normal distribution for each spectral class, estimated using mean and covariance. What is the main difference between classification and segmentation? Segmentation creates spatial units; classification assigns information labels to pixels or objects. Why can OBIA reduce salt-and-pepper noise? It predicts coherent segments using aggregated and contextual properties instead of independently labelling each pixel. What is the key weakness of overall accuracy? It can conceal severe errors for a particular class and depends on reference sampling. How do you defend a model choice? Match the model to physical measurement conditions, class definitions, training design, spatial unit and independent validation rather than choosing solely by a high reported score."}, {"title": "22. Source notes and boundaries", "html": "<p class=\"source\">Primary sources</p><h3>Lecture deck</h3><p>geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf, 105 physical PDF pages, including pixel classification, training-site statistics, separability, supervised and unsupervised methods, foundation models, and GEOBIA coral-reef examples.</p><h3>Recording</h3><p>GMT20260902-012925_Recording.transcript.vtt, 2 September 2026. Speaker introduction near 00:22:39; training-site discussion around 00:47; Jeffries–Matusita around 01:02:50; algorithm list around 01:05:30; segmentation around 01:38:49.</p><h3>Separate future chapter</h3><p>geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf is a distinct 81-page slide deck and will be treated separately in the next chapter. This chapter does not claim to cover its quantitative modelling, change detection and error-assessment content.</p><h3>Additional explanation</h3><p>All explicitly marked worked numerical examples, methodological cautions and exam scenarios are supplementary teaching material, not verbatim quotes or claimed numerical results from the lecturer.</p>", "text": "Primary sources Lecture deck geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf, 105 physical PDF pages, including pixel classification, training-site statistics, separability, supervised and unsupervised methods, foundation models, and GEOBIA coral-reef examples. Recording GMT20260902-012925_Recording.transcript.vtt, 2 September 2026. Speaker introduction near 00:22:39; training-site discussion around 00:47; Jeffries–Matusita around 01:02:50; algorithm list around 01:05:30; segmentation around 01:38:49. Separate future chapter geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf is a distinct 81-page slide deck and will be treated separately in the next chapter. This chapter does not claim to cover its quantitative modelling, change detection and error-assessment content. Additional explanation All explicitly marked worked numerical examples, methodological cautions and exam scenarios are supplementary teaching material, not verbatim quotes or claimed numerical results from the lecturer."}], "text": ["1. Where classification fits in the EO workflow Slides 1–8; transcript 00:22:39–00:33:00 Course framing Will Woodgate introduces Associate Professor Chris Roelfsema as the guest lecturer for image classification. The slides position classification after image acquisition and preprocessing, and before information products and evaluation. A visually pleasing image is not yet a thematic map: classification translates measured spectral variation into defined information categories. Thematic versus quantitative outputs A thematic product assigns a category such as sand, seagrass or coral. A quantitative product estimates a continuous property such as percentage cover or biomass. The question “what is the output variable?” determines the appropriate reference observations, algorithm and validation metric. The inverse problem A sensor measures radiance or reflectance, not land-cover labels. Different covers may share similar spectra, and the same cover may vary with illumination, moisture, canopy structure and atmosphere. Therefore classification is inference from imperfect observations, not direct identification. Exam defence Define the mapped classes, sensor and resolution, time of observation, spatial unit, intended use and independent reference data before choosing an algorithm. An unsupported claim that a method is “most accurate” is weaker than a defensible choice matched to the problem.", "2. Univariate image statistics Slides 9–14; lecture section on image statistics Frequency distributions A single-band histogram summarises pixel values. The mode is most frequent, median is central ordered value, mean is arithmetic average, and range spans minimum to maximum. These measures are descriptive, not land-cover labels. Variance and standard deviation Variance describes squared spread around the mean; standard deviation returns to original band units. A class with broad spread may contain multiple substrates, illumination conditions or mixed pixels. A low spread does not establish class uniqueness. Worked calculation For reflectance values 0.10, 0.12, 0.13, 0.15, 0.50, the mean is 0.20 while the median is 0.13. The bright outlier raises the mean substantially. Inspect whether outliers represent real bright cover, cloud, glint or processing errors before removing them. Why statistics matter Classification relies on whether within-class variation is small enough relative to between-class differences. Histograms can reveal skewness, multimodality, saturation and overlap that complicate a single Gaussian class model.", "3. Multivariate statistics and spectral feature space Slides 10–17 Pixels as vectors With B bands, one pixel is a B-dimensional vector x = [x1, x2, …, xB]. Two classes may overlap in one band but separate when several bands are considered jointly. Covariance Covariance captures how two bands vary together. Strong correlations mean many channels may contribute redundant information. Covariance matrices also describe the shape and orientation of a class cloud in feature space. Why units matter Euclidean distances depend on variable scale: a 0–10000 reflectance integer band can dominate a 0–1 index unless predictors are consistently scaled. This is a supplementary modelling caution, not a slide-specific numerical rule. Worked spectral separation Suppose sand has mean [0.30, 0.35] and vegetation [0.08, 0.42] in red/NIR reflectance. The Euclidean separation of means is sqrt((0.22)^2+(−0.07)^2) ≈ 0.231. Whether this is useful depends on each class’s spread and covariance, not the mean difference alone.", "4. Spectral classes versus information classes Slides 15–22 Spectral class A spectral class groups pixels that are similar in feature space. It may combine distinct ecological substrates with similar reflectance, or divide one ecological type into sunny/shaded spectral subclasses. Information class An information class is defined by the mapping objective, such as live coral, macroalgae, seagrass, sand or deep water. It requires an explicit definition and appropriate ground evidence. Mapping between the two Unsupervised clustering first yields spectral groups that must be interpreted and assigned to information classes. Supervised learning associates training examples with information classes before prediction. Neither approach escapes class-definition ambiguity. Mixed pixels At coarse resolution a pixel may include coral, sand and algae. A hard label loses within-pixel fractions. Subpixel approaches instead estimate class abundance or membership, but require additional assumptions and validation.", "5. A complete classification sequence Slides 17–29 Define categories Start with classes that are observable, relevant and mutually interpretable at the chosen scale. A hierarchical legend can separate broad geomorphic zones first and detailed benthic categories second. Select source imagery Check wavelength coverage, spatial resolution, acquisition timing, tides, depth, atmospheric correction and geometric alignment. In aquatic imagery, water depth and water-column effects can dominate substrate signals. Collect training observations Use representative reference sites across the full range of class conditions, avoiding exclusive sampling of the easiest textbook examples. Keep training and independent assessment samples distinct. Evaluate features and classes Inspect statistics and spectral separability, then choose predictors and an algorithm. If two classes remain inseparable, reconsider the legend, sensor or scale rather than forcing a high-confidence distinction. Predict, postprocess and validate Apply the trained method, inspect spatial artefacts and confusion, and estimate accuracy with independent samples. Document what was masked, smoothed or merged so the final map is reproducible.", "6. Training regions of interest (ROIs) Slides 27–36; transcript around 00:47:00 What an ROI represents A region of interest contains pixels assumed to belong to one information class. Its sample statistics become a model of that class in spectral feature space. The lecture explicitly discusses assessing training sites rather than treating every polygon as suitable. Homogeneity and representativeness An ROI may be internally heterogeneous because of shade, substrate mixtures or geolocation error. Conversely, an extremely pure ROI can underrepresent real-world class variability. Aim for known labels and coverage of legitimate variability. Training sample leakage Adjacent pixels are autocorrelated. Randomly distributing neighbouring pixels between training and validation can overstate transfer accuracy. Separate sites, reef zones, transects or acquisition dates when the intended deployment requires independence. Practical inspection Plot class histograms and pairwise band scatterplots; review outliers against source imagery; compare within-class spread with between-class separation; and check that each ROI matches the stated class definition.", "7. Spectral separability and Jeffries–Matusita distance Slides 30–36; transcript about 01:02:50 Purpose Separability measures assess whether training classes occupy distinguishable regions of feature space. The transcript explicitly mentions Jeffries–Matusita distance while discussing training class evaluation. Interpretation A high separability score indicates distinct distributions under the chosen bands and statistical assumptions; a low score warns that the classifier may confuse the classes. The score is not independent map accuracy. How to respond to poor separation Inspect labels and reference data, try physically meaningful alternative bands or indices, include texture or context, stratify by habitat zone, or merge categories that cannot be resolved with available data. Avoid circular reasoning Selecting bands based on all labelled data and then testing on the same labels produces optimistic results. Feature selection should be conducted within training data, with an independent holdout for evaluation.", "8. Supervised algorithms: parallelepiped and minimum distance Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30 Parallelepiped Each class is represented by band-wise lower and upper thresholds, forming a box in feature space. It is transparent and fast but can leave unclassified pixels or assign overlapping boxes ambiguously. Minimum distance to means For each pixel calculate distance to each class mean vector and assign the nearest class. This is straightforward but ignores the direction and shape of each class distribution. Worked example Pixel x = [0.14,0.38], sand mean [0.30,0.35], vegetation mean [0.08,0.42]. Distances are approximately 0.163 and 0.072, respectively; minimum distance selects vegetation. This calculation does not prove the vegetation label is correct. When not to use If classes have strongly unequal covariance or elongated spectral distributions, nearest-mean decisions can be inappropriate. Inspect class scatter and consider a covariance-aware or flexible model.", "9. Maximum likelihood classification Slides 37–44; transcript around 01:05:30 Statistical model Maximum likelihood classification typically models the spectral distribution of each class with a multivariate normal distribution. It uses class means and covariance matrices to calculate the relative likelihood of a pixel under each class. What covariance adds Unlike minimum distance, the method accounts for spread and correlation. A pixel may be far from a class mean along a high-variance direction yet still be plausible for that class. Assumptions and failure Small training samples, strongly non-normal or multimodal distributions, and unstable covariance estimates can undermine predictions. Mixed pixels and changing water depth also challenge class distributions. Defend the choice Use maximum likelihood when the class distributions and sample sizes justify its assumptions, not merely because it is a traditional EO algorithm. Compare alternatives with the same independent reference design.", "10. Random forest and modern supervised methods Slides 37–50; transcript around 01:05:34 Random forest An ensemble of decision trees predicts from spectral bands and potentially indices, texture and spatial variables. It can learn nonlinear class boundaries and interactions without assuming multivariate normal classes. Hyperparameters Tree count, maximum depth, minimum leaf size and feature sampling affect complexity and runtime. Tune on training data using spatially appropriate cross-validation, not the final independent test set. Limits Random forest can still learn site-specific shadows, depth or acquisition artefacts. Feature importance is not causal attribution, and probability-like vote fractions may need calibration. Foundation models The deck also introduces EO foundation models as a newer approach. Pretrained representations can help downstream tasks, but benchmark performance, transferability, reference quality and independent validation remain essential.", "11. Unsupervised clustering and interpretation Slides 44–50 Clustering objective Unsupervised algorithms group spectrally similar pixels without starting from labelled information classes. The operator must select or evaluate cluster number and later interpret the groups. Typical use Clustering can reveal unexpected spectral patterns, support exploratory mapping and help design training samples. It is not automatically a habitat map. Interpretation challenge One cluster may contain multiple ecological classes; one ecological class may split into sunlit and shaded clusters. Assign information labels using imagery, field data and ecological context. Exam critique “No training labels means no need for ground truth” is incorrect. Labels and independent validation are still needed to establish what clusters mean and how reliable the thematic map is.", "12. Pixel-based versus subpixel mapping Slides 21–26 Hard classification A per-pixel hard map assigns one dominant category. This is simple to display and analyse but obscures mixtures and boundary uncertainty. Subpixel approach Fractional cover or spectral unmixing estimates the contribution of endmembers within a pixel. Such models depend on endmember representativeness, mixture assumptions and signal-to-noise ratio. Aquatic caution In shallow water, water depth and optical properties can change observed substrate spectra, violating naive linear mixtures of bottom reflectance at the sensor. Scale selection Use a categorical map for discrete management classes when spatial resolution and validation support them. Use fractional outputs when the management question concerns percentage cover and mixtures are substantial.", "13. Why object-based image analysis? Slides 52–63; transcript approximately 01:35–01:42 Core concept Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) groups neighbouring pixels into segments, then classifies those objects using spectral, shape, size, texture, context and relational properties. Motivation High-resolution imagery can show substantial within-object texture, shadow and pixel-scale noise. Object-based classification can represent coherent habitat patches and reduce salt-and-pepper patterns. Not automatically superior Poor segmentation may merge different habitats or fragment one habitat. OBIA performance depends on the mapping unit, segmentation parameters, available features and class hierarchy. When pixel methods win If target features are smaller than segments, spectral variation is already highly distinctive, or boundaries must be resolved precisely, a pixel approach may be simpler and more faithful.", "14. Segmentation as a modelling decision Slides 77–85; transcript around 01:38:49 What segmentation does Segmentation partitions an image into spatially contiguous regions based on similarity criteria. It creates analysis units but does not itself establish semantic classes. Top-down versus bottom-up Top-down segmentation subdivides large regions into smaller objects; bottom-up segmentation merges pixels or small objects into larger ones. The slides explicitly contrast these strategies. Scale parameter Coarse segmentation creates larger, more homogeneous-in-context objects but risks mixing classes; fine segmentation follows detail but can fragment habitats and amplify noise. Assess quality Compare object boundaries with independent reference features and ecological patch sizes. Do not select scale solely because the resulting polygons look neat.", "15. Hierarchical coral-reef mapping Slides 63–79; reef examples Hierarchy The lecture uses reef examples to illustrate large-scale geomorphic zones and finer benthic communities. A broad reef-flat, lagoon or crest zone can constrain which finer categories are plausible. Physical context The same bottom material can have different image signatures with depth, waves and illumination. Mapping zones first can help avoid comparing physically dissimilar pixels as if they were identical. Context and relationships Object features can include adjacency, relative position, size, shape, mean reflectance and texture. For example, a bright shallow feature next to a reef crest may be interpreted differently from an equally bright isolated offshore patch. Caution A hierarchy may encode prior expectations incorrectly. Unexpected habitats or changed geomorphology can be forced into unsuitable categories unless exceptions and validation are allowed.", "16. OBIA rule sets and class hierarchy Slides 84–96 Rule set An OBIA workflow can use sequential rules: segment large zones, calculate object features, apply thresholds or classifiers, refine subclasses and merge or split objects as needed. Process trees The slides show process-tree structure and parent/child relationships. These support reproducible multistage logic, but every threshold needs a rationale and ideally sensitivity analysis. Example First separate water from land, then segment shallow reef regions, then classify benthic objects using spectral and texture features. This is a supplementary illustrative workflow, not a claim of the exact lecture rule set. Auditability Record segmentation scale, feature definitions, threshold values, training samples, postprocessing rules and software version. An undocumented expert-tuned rule set is difficult to reproduce.", "17. Critical comparison: pixel versus object methods Slides 52–96 Pixel method advantages Directly relates each measurement to a prediction, is simple to train and often computationally efficient. It preserves local spectral variation and avoids segmentation parameter choices. Pixel method limitations Mixed pixels, high-frequency noise, geometric misalignment and fragmented output can reduce interpretability, especially in very high-resolution imagery. Object method advantages Spatial coherence, shape, texture and contextual relations can better represent landscape or reef patches. It may reduce effects of slight image misregistration, as highlighted in the deck. Object method limitations Segmentation errors propagate into class labels; a polygon may contain multiple habitats; segmentation and rule sets can be subjective and computationally demanding. Decision rule Select based on target patch size, sensor resolution, class separability, geometric quality, desired map unit and reference sampling. Test both on a common independent assessment set if practical.", "18. Worked case: reef benthic map Original synthesis grounded in lecture methods Scenario You need to classify live coral, rubble, macroalgae, sand and deep water from high-resolution multispectral imagery of a reef. Reference points are concentrated in clear shallow water. Step 1: revise the design Define classes and minimum mapping unit; assess tide, water clarity, sunglint, depth and spatial resolution. Decide whether deep water should be masked or modelled separately. Step 2: collect representative reference Stratify observations across depth, reef zones, substrate types and illumination conditions. Separate independent validation sites or transects from training ROIs. Step 3: choose modelling unit Start with spectral pixel classification as a baseline. Test OBIA where coherent patches and geomorphic context provide meaningful information; document segmentation scale and object features. Step 4: evaluate Report class-specific errors and spatial patterns, not just overall accuracy. Inspect confusion between coral, rubble and algae, particularly across depth zones. Step 5: communicate Describe the acquisition conditions, mapped categories, unclassified/uncertain areas, intended scale and independent validation limitations.", "19. Worked accuracy example and interpretation Supplementary exam exercise; not a claimed Week 6 lecture result Confusion matrix Suppose 100 independent reference samples: 40 coral and 60 non-coral. The map predicts coral for 35 true coral and 10 non-coral; predicts non-coral for 5 coral and 50 non-coral. Overall accuracy Correct = 35+50 = 85 of 100, so overall accuracy = 85%. This number hides different omission and commission errors. Coral producer’s accuracy 35/40 = 87.5%. Of reference coral samples, 12.5% were omitted by the map. Coral user’s accuracy 35/(35+10) = 77.8%. Of mapped coral samples, 22.2% are actually non-coral in this reference set. Design warning These proportions are simple sample calculations. For area estimates under stratified sampling, use appropriate design weights; do not assume a non-probability reference sample gives unbiased population accuracy.", "20. Exam-style critique and model answer Original integrated question Question A researcher chooses 30 coral pixels and 30 sand pixels from the same reef image, obtains 99% training accuracy with random forest, then segments the image into large objects and claims the final reef map is 99% accurate. Issue 1: labels Two classes may be insufficient if rubble, algae, seagrass, shadow and deep water occur. The legend is incomplete and predictions outside the training domain may be misleading. Issue 2: sample design Thirty nearby pixels per class may not capture within-class variability. Training accuracy is not independent accuracy, and adjacent pixels cause spatial leakage. Issue 3: preprocessing Depth, sunglint, atmosphere, sensor geometry and water conditions can create systematic spectral differences unrelated to benthic class. Issue 4: segmentation Large segments may mix coral and sand, and segmentation changes the prediction unit. Training-pixel performance does not validate final object labels. Defensible answer Define a complete hierarchy, obtain representative field reference across zones and depths, independently assess pixel and object alternatives, report class-specific accuracy and spatial uncertainty, and justify the final map scale.", "21. Quick oral examination prompts Course-grounded questions with concise answers Why does training-site separability matter? Because overlapping class distributions imply expected confusion; separability is a diagnostic, not a substitute for independent accuracy assessment. What does maximum likelihood assume? Usually a multivariate normal distribution for each spectral class, estimated using mean and covariance. What is the main difference between classification and segmentation? Segmentation creates spatial units; classification assigns information labels to pixels or objects. Why can OBIA reduce salt-and-pepper noise? It predicts coherent segments using aggregated and contextual properties instead of independently labelling each pixel. What is the key weakness of overall accuracy? It can conceal severe errors for a particular class and depends on reference sampling. How do you defend a model choice? Match the model to physical measurement conditions, class definitions, training design, spatial unit and independent validation rather than choosing solely by a high reported score.", "22. Source notes and boundaries Primary sources Lecture deck geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf, 105 physical PDF pages, including pixel classification, training-site statistics, separability, supervised and unsupervised methods, foundation models, and GEOBIA coral-reef examples. Recording GMT20260902-012925_Recording.transcript.vtt, 2 September 2026. Speaker introduction near 00:22:39; training-site discussion around 00:47; Jeffries–Matusita around 01:02:50; algorithm list around 01:05:30; segmentation around 01:38:49. Separate future chapter geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf is a distinct 81-page slide deck and will be treated separately in the next chapter. This chapter does not claim to cover its quantitative modelling, change detection and error-assessment content. Additional explanation All explicitly marked worked numerical examples, methodological cautions and exam scenarios are supplementary teaching material, not verbatim quotes or claimed numerical results from the lecturer."]}, {"id": 6, "title": "Biophysical Modelling, Change Detection & Accuracy Assessment", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week07_Biophysical_Change_Accuracy.pdf", "pages": 10, "sections": [{"title": "1. Where this lecture sits in the course", "html": "<p>Slides 1–6; recording 00:12:53–00:17:25</p><h3>Speaker framing</h3><p>Stuart Phinn describes three linked tasks: extracting biophysical variables from imagery, detecting environmental change across dates, and assessing the errors in resulting maps. He distinguishes uncertainty in raw imagery (position and radiometry) from validation of derived information.</p><h3>The key conceptual contrast</h3><p>A thematic map labels pixels or objects as categories, whereas a biophysical map estimates a quantity such as canopy cover, surface temperature or suspended sediment. The error model and validation metric should match the type of output.</p><h3>What students actually do</h3><p>The speaker connects the lecture to the ENVI practical: assess thematic classification accuracy and compare classified images from two dates. These activities are not equivalent to testing continuous-variable models or analysing long time series.</p><h3>Decision question</h3><p>Before choosing an algorithm, specify the target quantity or class, the spatial/temporal support, the purpose of the map, the reference measurement and the acceptable uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Slides 1–6; recording 00:12:53–00:17:25 Speaker framing Stuart Phinn describes three linked tasks: extracting biophysical variables from imagery, detecting environmental change across dates, and assessing the errors in resulting maps. He distinguishes uncertainty in raw imagery (position and radiometry) from validation of derived information. The key conceptual contrast A thematic map labels pixels or objects as categories, whereas a biophysical map estimates a quantity such as canopy cover, surface temperature or suspended sediment. The error model and validation metric should match the type of output. What students actually do The speaker connects the lecture to the ENVI practical: assess thematic classification accuracy and compare classified images from two dates. These activities are not equivalent to testing continuous-variable models or analysing long time series. Decision question Before choosing an algorithm, specify the target quantity or class, the spatial/temporal support, the purpose of the map, the reference measurement and the acceptable uncertainty."}, {"title": "2. Worked case: field to drone to satellite woody vegetation cover", "html": "<p>Slides 7–13; recording approximately 00:21–00:35</p><h3>Why the example matters</h3><p>The deck develops woody vegetation cover as a chain from field measurements through drone observations to satellite maps. This is a quantitative estimation task, not merely a woody/non-woody classification.</p><h3>Support and scale</h3><p>A ground plot measures vegetation in a finite area; a UAV image resolves individual crowns or canopy gaps; a satellite pixel averages a much larger footprint. A model calibrated at one scale is not automatically valid at another.</p><h3>Field protocol</h3><p>Define woody cover consistently (e.g. projected canopy fraction or foliage projective cover, depending on the study). Record plot dimensions, coordinates, dates, instrument/protocol, species/structure and measurement uncertainty. Do not conflate distinct vegetation-cover definitions.</p><h3>Spatial-temporal matching</h3><p>At 00:32:30–00:34:07, the speaker stresses that validation observations must match the image spatially and ideally temporally. A plot centre coordinate is not enough when its footprint spans heterogeneous pixels.</p><h3>Critique</h3><p>An analyst calibrates 10 m woody-cover pixels with 1 m² field observations taken two years earlier. Major problems: scale mismatch, geolocation error, changing vegetation and unrepresentative sampling.</p>", "text": "Slides 7–13; recording approximately 00:21–00:35 Why the example matters The deck develops woody vegetation cover as a chain from field measurements through drone observations to satellite maps. This is a quantitative estimation task, not merely a woody/non-woody classification. Support and scale A ground plot measures vegetation in a finite area; a UAV image resolves individual crowns or canopy gaps; a satellite pixel averages a much larger footprint. A model calibrated at one scale is not automatically valid at another. Field protocol Define woody cover consistently (e.g. projected canopy fraction or foliage projective cover, depending on the study). Record plot dimensions, coordinates, dates, instrument/protocol, species/structure and measurement uncertainty. Do not conflate distinct vegetation-cover definitions. Spatial-temporal matching At 00:32:30–00:34:07, the speaker stresses that validation observations must match the image spatially and ideally temporally. A plot centre coordinate is not enough when its footprint spans heterogeneous pixels. Critique An analyst calibrates 10 m woody-cover pixels with 1 m² field observations taken two years earlier. Major problems: scale mismatch, geolocation error, changing vegetation and unrepresentative sampling."}, {"title": "3. Thematic versus biophysical mapping", "html": "<p>Slides 14–16; recording 00:15:14–00:16:19</p><h3>Thematic output</h3><p>Assign a label (water, urban, mangrove, etc.) to each mapping unit. Validation asks whether assigned classes agree with reference labels, commonly using a confusion matrix.</p><h3>Biophysical output</h3><p>Estimate a continuous property such as cover percentage, temperature, biomass or water-column depth. Validation asks about residuals, bias, RMSE, calibration and prediction intervals.</p><h3>Why the distinction changes modelling</h3><p>A vegetation classifier may accurately identify mangroves but say nothing about their canopy density or health. An NDVI regression may estimate cover but not identify vegetation species.</p><h3>Model choice</h3><p>Use a classification model for categorical labels and regression or physically based inversion for continuous variables. A workflow can combine them: mask vegetation first, then model cover within valid vegetation pixels.</p>", "text": "Slides 14–16; recording 00:15:14–00:16:19 Thematic output Assign a label (water, urban, mangrove, etc.) to each mapping unit. Validation asks whether assigned classes agree with reference labels, commonly using a confusion matrix. Biophysical output Estimate a continuous property such as cover percentage, temperature, biomass or water-column depth. Validation asks about residuals, bias, RMSE, calibration and prediction intervals. Why the distinction changes modelling A vegetation classifier may accurately identify mangroves but say nothing about their canopy density or health. An NDVI regression may estimate cover but not identify vegetation species. Model choice Use a classification model for categorical labels and regression or physically based inversion for continuous variables. A workflow can combine them: mask vegetation first, then model cover within valid vegetation pixels."}, {"title": "4. Preconditions for comparing multiple images", "html": "<p>Slides 18–27; recording around 00:36–00:58</p><h3>Geometric comparability</h3><p>Same projection/grid and sufficiently accurate image-to-image registration. Even subpixel shifts can create artificial change at sharp coastlines, roads or canopy boundaries.</p><h3>Spatial resolution</h3><p>Match ground sampling and point-spread behaviour; resampling a coarse image to a fine grid does not add genuine spatial information. Consider a common analysis grid and appropriate aggregation.</p><h3>Spectral comparability</h3><p>Check band centres, widths and sensor response functions. &#x27;Red&#x27; and &#x27;NIR&#x27; bands from different instruments are not necessarily identical; harmonisation may be required.</p><h3>Radiometric comparability</h3><p>Compare calibrated and corrected measurements, preferably consistent surface reflectance where appropriate. Quantisation, saturation, atmospheric effects and BRDF can masquerade as change.</p><h3>Acquisition geometry</h3><p>Slides 24–26 discuss BRDF and sun/viewing geometry. Anniversary-date acquisitions reduce seasonal geometry differences but do not remove them.</p><h3>Scene conditions</h3><p>Slides 26–27 note clouds, haze and other scene differences. Also assess phenology, tides for aquatic/coastal settings, water levels, shadows and data completeness.</p><h3>Exam answer</h3><p>A defensible change workflow explicitly lists these requirements before image differencing. &#x27;Both images are Landsat&#x27; is not sufficient justification.</p>", "text": "Slides 18–27; recording around 00:36–00:58 Geometric comparability Same projection/grid and sufficiently accurate image-to-image registration. Even subpixel shifts can create artificial change at sharp coastlines, roads or canopy boundaries. Spatial resolution Match ground sampling and point-spread behaviour; resampling a coarse image to a fine grid does not add genuine spatial information. Consider a common analysis grid and appropriate aggregation. Spectral comparability Check band centres, widths and sensor response functions. 'Red' and 'NIR' bands from different instruments are not necessarily identical; harmonisation may be required. Radiometric comparability Compare calibrated and corrected measurements, preferably consistent surface reflectance where appropriate. Quantisation, saturation, atmospheric effects and BRDF can masquerade as change. Acquisition geometry Slides 24–26 discuss BRDF and sun/viewing geometry. Anniversary-date acquisitions reduce seasonal geometry differences but do not remove them. Scene conditions Slides 26–27 note clouds, haze and other scene differences. Also assess phenology, tides for aquatic/coastal settings, water levels, shadows and data completeness. Exam answer A defensible change workflow explicitly lists these requirements before image differencing. 'Both images are Landsat' is not sufficient justification."}, {"title": "5. Multi-date normalisation and BRDF", "html": "<p>Slides 21–26</p><h3>Absolute versus relative</h3><p>Absolute calibration/correction attempts physically comparable reflectance. Relative normalisation adjusts images to a common reference distribution or stable targets, useful when perfect atmospheric comparability is unavailable.</p><h3>Stable-target assumption</h3><p>Pseudo-invariant features must truly be stable. If an apparent reference feature changed, the normalisation can suppress real change or introduce false change.</p><h3>BRDF</h3><p>Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes directional variation in surface reflectance with illumination and viewing geometry. Two images of the same unchanged canopy can differ because their sun and view angles differ.</p><h3>A practical check</h3><p>Plot values from stable reference surfaces across dates. Examine systematic offsets and slopes, not just visual similarity. Keep a record of normalisation method and residual error.</p>", "text": "Slides 21–26 Absolute versus relative Absolute calibration/correction attempts physically comparable reflectance. Relative normalisation adjusts images to a common reference distribution or stable targets, useful when perfect atmospheric comparability is unavailable. Stable-target assumption Pseudo-invariant features must truly be stable. If an apparent reference feature changed, the normalisation can suppress real change or introduce false change. BRDF Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes directional variation in surface reflectance with illumination and viewing geometry. Two images of the same unchanged canopy can differ because their sun and view angles differ. A practical check Plot values from stable reference surfaces across dates. Examine systematic offsets and slopes, not just visual similarity. Keep a record of normalisation method and residual error."}, {"title": "6. Biophysical modelling: the measurement chain", "html": "<p>Slides 29–34; recording approximately 00:58–01:10</p><h3>From pixel values to parameters</h3><p>The slides describe quantitatively relating image measurements to biophysical variables. The mapping function can be empirical (field-calibrated regression) or physically informed (radiative transfer/inversion).</p><h3>Examples in the deck</h3><p>Global products include atmospheric carbon dioxide, land surface properties and other environmental quantities; the Queensland-government examples show operational EO products.</p><h3>Empirical regression</h3><p>Fit a model y=f(x), where y is measured field property and x represents spectral bands, indices, texture or ancillary variables. Document preprocessing, calibration domain and independent testing.</p><h3>Physical model</h3><p>A radiative-transfer model predicts observed radiance/reflectance given surface and atmospheric parameters. Inversion seeks parameters that plausibly produce the observed signal; different parameter combinations may be non-identifiable.</p><h3>Evaluation</h3><p>For continuous outputs, report RMSE and bias in physical units, plus R-squared where meaningful. Show residuals across ranges, seasons and land-cover types. Independent observations should not be recycled from training.</p><h3>Original numeric illustration</h3><p>If measured woody cover is [20,40,60]% and predictions are [25,35,65]%, errors are [+5,−5,+5] percentage points; RMSE=5 percentage points and bias=+1.67. These are invented teaching values, not lecture findings.</p>", "text": "Slides 29–34; recording approximately 00:58–01:10 From pixel values to parameters The slides describe quantitatively relating image measurements to biophysical variables. The mapping function can be empirical (field-calibrated regression) or physically informed (radiative transfer/inversion). Examples in the deck Global products include atmospheric carbon dioxide, land surface properties and other environmental quantities; the Queensland-government examples show operational EO products. Empirical regression Fit a model y=f(x), where y is measured field property and x represents spectral bands, indices, texture or ancillary variables. Document preprocessing, calibration domain and independent testing. Physical model A radiative-transfer model predicts observed radiance/reflectance given surface and atmospheric parameters. Inversion seeks parameters that plausibly produce the observed signal; different parameter combinations may be non-identifiable. Evaluation For continuous outputs, report RMSE and bias in physical units, plus R-squared where meaningful. Show residuals across ranges, seasons and land-cover types. Independent observations should not be recycled from training. Original numeric illustration If measured woody cover is [20,40,60]% and predictions are [25,35,65]%, errors are [+5,−5,+5] percentage points; RMSE=5 percentage points and bias=+1.67. These are invented teaching values, not lecture findings."}, {"title": "7. Change detection: what is actually changing?", "html": "<p>Slides 35–37; recording 00:16:19–00:17:25</p><h3>Definition</h3><p>The slides frame change detection as identifying meaningful changes in surface measurements or derived information over time. Meaningful change must be distinguished from noise, phenology and correction artefacts.</p><h3>Two broad strategies</h3><p>Image-based change analyses compare spectral or biophysical quantities; post-classification comparison identifies transitions among thematic categories. Each produces different answers.</p><h3>The choice depends on the question</h3><p>If asking whether canopy cover declined from 65% to 40%, continuous-variable differencing is natural. If asking whether a mangrove area became mudflat, categorical transition analysis is natural.</p><h3>Causation warning</h3><p>A changed spectral signature is evidence of altered observation, not proof of a particular driver such as drought, clearing or disease.</p>", "text": "Slides 35–37; recording 00:16:19–00:17:25 Definition The slides frame change detection as identifying meaningful changes in surface measurements or derived information over time. Meaningful change must be distinguished from noise, phenology and correction artefacts. Two broad strategies Image-based change analyses compare spectral or biophysical quantities; post-classification comparison identifies transitions among thematic categories. Each produces different answers. The choice depends on the question If asking whether canopy cover declined from 65% to 40%, continuous-variable differencing is natural. If asking whether a mangrove area became mudflat, categorical transition analysis is natural. Causation warning A changed spectral signature is evidence of altered observation, not proof of a particular driver such as drought, clearing or disease."}, {"title": "8. Image differencing", "html": "<p>Slides 38–41</p><h3>Equation</h3><p>For a consistent pixel and band/index, D = X(date 2) − X(date 1). The sign and magnitude depend on the measurement definition; a negative NDVI difference may indicate reduced greenness but not necessarily vegetation loss.</p><h3>Original example</h3><p>NDVI(date 1)=0.72, NDVI(date 2)=0.41 gives D=−0.31. Interpret only after confirming valid pixels, matching seasons, registration and spectral/radiometric consistency.</p><h3>Thresholding</h3><p>A binary changed/unchanged map requires a justified threshold. A threshold may be based on measurement uncertainty, stable-site variation, a distributional method or independent reference change.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Two dates cannot distinguish an abrupt disturbance from normal seasonal fluctuation. Misregistration near class boundaries can generate a strong artificial difference.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>If a researcher thresholds all NDVI decreases below −0.1 as dieback, ask whether −0.1 exceeds the combined noise and seasonal variability and whether independent evidence supports ecological dieback.</p>", "text": "Slides 38–41 Equation For a consistent pixel and band/index, D = X(date 2) − X(date 1). The sign and magnitude depend on the measurement definition; a negative NDVI difference may indicate reduced greenness but not necessarily vegetation loss. Original example NDVI(date 1)=0.72, NDVI(date 2)=0.41 gives D=−0.31. Interpret only after confirming valid pixels, matching seasons, registration and spectral/radiometric consistency. Thresholding A binary changed/unchanged map requires a justified threshold. A threshold may be based on measurement uncertainty, stable-site variation, a distributional method or independent reference change. Limitations Two dates cannot distinguish an abrupt disturbance from normal seasonal fluctuation. Misregistration near class boundaries can generate a strong artificial difference. Exam critique If a researcher thresholds all NDVI decreases below −0.1 as dieback, ask whether −0.1 exceeds the combined noise and seasonal variability and whether independent evidence supports ecological dieback."}, {"title": "9. Temporal ratios and change-vector analysis", "html": "<p>Slides 42–45</p><h3>Ratios</h3><p>A temporal ratio R=X2/X1 can highlight relative proportional change. It becomes unstable when X1 is near zero and behaves differently for negative-valued indices.</p><h3>Original example</h3><p>If a nonnegative canopy metric decreases from 0.60 to 0.30, R=0.5: the second value is half the first. This does not necessarily imply a 50% reduction in biomass.</p><h3>Change vectors</h3><p>With multiple spectral bands, calculate vector difference Δx=x2−x1; its magnitude sqrt(sum(Δband²)) represents spectral-change strength and direction can contain information about the type of change.</p><h3>Scale dependence</h3><p>Vector magnitude depends on band scaling, correlated bands and units. Normalise appropriately and avoid interpreting large magnitudes as automatically important ecological change.</p><h3>Multidate trajectories</h3><p>More than two dates can identify onset, persistence and recovery, although these require temporal sampling sufficient to resolve the event.</p>", "text": "Slides 42–45 Ratios A temporal ratio R=X2/X1 can highlight relative proportional change. It becomes unstable when X1 is near zero and behaves differently for negative-valued indices. Original example If a nonnegative canopy metric decreases from 0.60 to 0.30, R=0.5: the second value is half the first. This does not necessarily imply a 50% reduction in biomass. Change vectors With multiple spectral bands, calculate vector difference Δx=x2−x1; its magnitude sqrt(sum(Δband²)) represents spectral-change strength and direction can contain information about the type of change. Scale dependence Vector magnitude depends on band scaling, correlated bands and units. Normalise appropriately and avoid interpreting large magnitudes as automatically important ecological change. Multidate trajectories More than two dates can identify onset, persistence and recovery, although these require temporal sampling sufficient to resolve the event."}, {"title": "10. Post-classification change and transition matrices", "html": "<p>Slides 46–48; lecture links to ENVI practical</p><h3>Procedure</h3><p>Classify each date using consistent definitions, align the maps, then cross-tabulate the date-1 classes against date-2 classes. Diagonal cells indicate persistence; off-diagonal cells represent mapped transitions.</p><h3>Example</h3><p>If 80 hectares map as mangrove in year 1, and 12 hectares of those map as mudflat in year 2, the mapped mangrove→mudflat transition is 12 ha. That is a map-derived transition, not automatically a validated true change.</p><h3>Error propagation</h3><p>If each classification has error, change detection can compound it. An apparent transition may reflect inconsistent labels or different class-specific errors rather than real land-cover change.</p><h3>Better evaluation</h3><p>Collect independent reference observations for transitions when possible, including stable and changed locations. Report uncertainty of change-area estimates rather than only transition counts.</p><h3>Exam critique</h3><p>An analyst reports a 2% mangrove decline from two maps with 85% overall accuracy each. That small net change could be overwhelmed by map error; investigate class-specific errors and reference-based change estimates.</p>", "text": "Slides 46–48; lecture links to ENVI practical Procedure Classify each date using consistent definitions, align the maps, then cross-tabulate the date-1 classes against date-2 classes. Diagonal cells indicate persistence; off-diagonal cells represent mapped transitions. Example If 80 hectares map as mangrove in year 1, and 12 hectares of those map as mudflat in year 2, the mapped mangrove→mudflat transition is 12 ha. That is a map-derived transition, not automatically a validated true change. Error propagation If each classification has error, change detection can compound it. An apparent transition may reflect inconsistent labels or different class-specific errors rather than real land-cover change. Better evaluation Collect independent reference observations for transitions when possible, including stable and changed locations. Report uncertainty of change-area estimates rather than only transition counts. Exam critique An analyst reports a 2% mangrove decline from two maps with 85% overall accuracy each. That small net change could be overwhelmed by map error; investigate class-specific errors and reference-based change estimates."}, {"title": "11. Time series and trends", "html": "<p>Slides 49–52; recording around 00:20:15–00:20:59</p><h3>Why more dates help</h3><p>The lecturer distinguishes two-date change from series with 3, 4, 5 or hundreds of observations. Cloud platforms and data cubes make large time series tractable.</p><h3>Components</h3><p>A time series can contain long-term trend, seasonal cycle, abrupt events, recovery and observation noise. A linear trend alone can misrepresent abrupt disturbance.</p><h3>Sampling</h3><p>Compare like seasons and inspect valid observation counts. Irregular cloud gaps can bias annual summaries; a median from one season is not equivalent to a full-year median.</p><h3>Original anomaly calculation</h3><p>For a seasonally matched baseline mean 0.68 and SD 0.04, NDVI 0.56 yields z=(0.56−0.68)/0.04=−3.0. It is a strong statistical anomaly under that baseline, not a diagnosis of its cause.</p><h3>Spatial versus point analysis</h3><p>Slides 52 contrast spatially explicit and point-based approaches. Pixelwise models can map heterogeneity but multiply testing and autocorrelation issues; plot/region summaries can reduce noise but conceal local change.</p>", "text": "Slides 49–52; recording around 00:20:15–00:20:59 Why more dates help The lecturer distinguishes two-date change from series with 3, 4, 5 or hundreds of observations. Cloud platforms and data cubes make large time series tractable. Components A time series can contain long-term trend, seasonal cycle, abrupt events, recovery and observation noise. A linear trend alone can misrepresent abrupt disturbance. Sampling Compare like seasons and inspect valid observation counts. Irregular cloud gaps can bias annual summaries; a median from one season is not equivalent to a full-year median. Original anomaly calculation For a seasonally matched baseline mean 0.68 and SD 0.04, NDVI 0.56 yields z=(0.56−0.68)/0.04=−3.0. It is a strong statistical anomaly under that baseline, not a diagnosis of its cause. Spatial versus point analysis Slides 52 contrast spatially explicit and point-based approaches. Pixelwise models can map heterogeneity but multiply testing and autocorrelation issues; plot/region summaries can reduce noise but conceal local change."}, {"title": "12. Understanding error, accuracy and validation", "html": "<p>Slides 54–62; recording around 01:40–01:46</p><h3>Error is not a single number</h3><p>Geometric, spectral, radiometric, model and reference errors affect the final information product. A map can have good geometric accuracy and poor class accuracy, or vice versa.</p><h3>The lecturer&#x27;s caution</h3><p>Around 01:43:25–01:43:56, Stuart Phinn emphasises that ground measurements themselves involve sampling choices and are not perfect truth. Treat reference data as best-available evidence with uncertainty.</p><h3>Reference representativeness</h3><p>Ensure field protocol measures the same variable at the same spatial support and time as the EO product. In a mixed pixel, a single point label may not represent the whole pixel.</p><h3>Independence</h3><p>Training samples used to fit a classifier should not be treated as independent validation. Nearby samples may also share spatial autocorrelation, making nominally separate datasets less independent than assumed.</p><h3>Accuracy versus precision</h3><p>Accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference; precision concerns repeatability or dispersion. A model may be consistently biased yet precise.</p>", "text": "Slides 54–62; recording around 01:40–01:46 Error is not a single number Geometric, spectral, radiometric, model and reference errors affect the final information product. A map can have good geometric accuracy and poor class accuracy, or vice versa. The lecturer's caution Around 01:43:25–01:43:56, Stuart Phinn emphasises that ground measurements themselves involve sampling choices and are not perfect truth. Treat reference data as best-available evidence with uncertainty. Reference representativeness Ensure field protocol measures the same variable at the same spatial support and time as the EO product. In a mixed pixel, a single point label may not represent the whole pixel. Independence Training samples used to fit a classifier should not be treated as independent validation. Nearby samples may also share spatial autocorrelation, making nominally separate datasets less independent than assumed. Accuracy versus precision Accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference; precision concerns repeatability or dispersion. A model may be consistently biased yet precise."}, {"title": "13. Designing a defensible accuracy assessment", "html": "<p>Slides 63–74; recording around 01:52–02:00</p><h3>Source sequence</h3><p>Slides 65 specify sampling design, reference dataset selection, comparison and error matrix/accuracy evaluation. The speaker explains that sample locations, sample size and sample-unit footprint matter.</p><h3>Sampling unit</h3><p>Point, pixel, plot and object are not interchangeable. Select a unit compatible with the map product and expected heterogeneity.</p><h3>Sampling strategies</h3><p>Random, systematic, stratified and stratified random designs are discussed. Stratification can ensure adequate reference samples for rare classes but raw counts may need design weights for area-wide accuracy.</p><h3>Sample-size equation</h3><p>Slide 70 gives N=Z²p(1−p)/E². For Z=1.96, expected accuracy p=0.85 and target margin E=0.05, N≈196; this simple proportion formula is an illustrative starting point, not a complete spatial design.</p><h3>Reference data quality</h3><p>Slides 73–74 discuss existing maps/ancillary information and field data. Prefer independent, higher-quality, contemporaneous sources; document interpreter uncertainty and disagreements.</p><h3>Risk of convenience samples</h3><p>Sampling only accessible roadside plots can bias estimates for remote or dense vegetation. A representative probability design is preferable when reporting population-wide accuracy.</p>", "text": "Slides 63–74; recording around 01:52–02:00 Source sequence Slides 65 specify sampling design, reference dataset selection, comparison and error matrix/accuracy evaluation. The speaker explains that sample locations, sample size and sample-unit footprint matter. Sampling unit Point, pixel, plot and object are not interchangeable. Select a unit compatible with the map product and expected heterogeneity. Sampling strategies Random, systematic, stratified and stratified random designs are discussed. Stratification can ensure adequate reference samples for rare classes but raw counts may need design weights for area-wide accuracy. Sample-size equation Slide 70 gives N=Z²p(1−p)/E². For Z=1.96, expected accuracy p=0.85 and target margin E=0.05, N≈196; this simple proportion formula is an illustrative starting point, not a complete spatial design. Reference data quality Slides 73–74 discuss existing maps/ancillary information and field data. Prefer independent, higher-quality, contemporaneous sources; document interpreter uncertainty and disagreements. Risk of convenience samples Sampling only accessible roadside plots can bias estimates for remote or dense vegetation. A representative probability design is preferable when reporting population-wide accuracy."}, {"title": "14. Confusion matrix: calculations and interpretation", "html": "<p>Slides 75–78</p><h3>Matrix convention</h3><p>Specify whether rows represent map predictions and columns reference labels; conventions vary. This example uses rows=map and columns=reference.</p><h3>Original two-class example</h3><p>Map mangrove: 42 true mangrove and 8 true other; map other: 6 true mangrove and 44 true other. Total=100; diagonal correct=86.</p><h3>Overall accuracy</h3><p>OA=(42+44)/100=0.86 or 86%. This single number can conceal uneven performance between classes.</p><h3>User&#x27;s accuracy for mangrove</h3><p>UA=42/(42+8)=0.84 or 84%. Of the area mapped mangrove in this sample, 84% is supported by reference labels; complement is commission error.</p><h3>Producer&#x27;s accuracy for mangrove</h3><p>PA=42/(42+6)=0.875 or 87.5%. Of reference mangrove samples, 87.5% were correctly mapped; complement is omission error.</p><h3>Why the denominators matter</h3><p>User&#x27;s accuracy conditions on the mapped category; producer&#x27;s accuracy conditions on the reference category. Always state row/column orientation before calculating.</p><h3>Area estimation caveat</h3><p>When sampling is stratified disproportionately, raw confusion-matrix percentages may not estimate map-wide accuracy. Use sampling weights and uncertainty intervals where required.</p>", "text": "Slides 75–78 Matrix convention Specify whether rows represent map predictions and columns reference labels; conventions vary. This example uses rows=map and columns=reference. Original two-class example Map mangrove: 42 true mangrove and 8 true other; map other: 6 true mangrove and 44 true other. Total=100; diagonal correct=86. Overall accuracy OA=(42+44)/100=0.86 or 86%. This single number can conceal uneven performance between classes. User's accuracy for mangrove UA=42/(42+8)=0.84 or 84%. Of the area mapped mangrove in this sample, 84% is supported by reference labels; complement is commission error. Producer's accuracy for mangrove PA=42/(42+6)=0.875 or 87.5%. Of reference mangrove samples, 87.5% were correctly mapped; complement is omission error. Why the denominators matter User's accuracy conditions on the mapped category; producer's accuracy conditions on the reference category. Always state row/column orientation before calculating. Area estimation caveat When sampling is stratified disproportionately, raw confusion-matrix percentages may not estimate map-wide accuracy. Use sampling weights and uncertainty intervals where required."}, {"title": "15. Continuous-variable validation is different", "html": "<p>Slides 14–15, 62; supplementary statistical detail</p><h3>Residuals</h3><p>For each independent plot, residual e_i=prediction_i−reference_i. Inspect systematic bias, scatter and heteroscedasticity (larger errors at higher values).</p><h3>RMSE and MAE</h3><p>RMSE=sqrt(mean(e_i²)) penalises large errors; MAE=mean(|e_i|) is easier to interpret as average absolute error. Both retain the variable&#x27;s physical units.</p><h3>R-squared</h3><p>R² can describe explained variance relative to a baseline but does not replace error units or reveal bias. The evaluation definition and reference distribution matter.</p><h3>Transfer tests</h3><p>For vegetation-cover models, hold out whole field sites, seasons or ecological zones when the intended use is spatial/temporal extrapolation. A random split of neighbouring pixels can be misleading.</p><h3>Report more than a score</h3><p>Provide sample design, sample size, uncertainty intervals, residual plots, performance by subgroup and known limits of applicability.</p>", "text": "Slides 14–15, 62; supplementary statistical detail Residuals For each independent plot, residual e_i=prediction_i−reference_i. Inspect systematic bias, scatter and heteroscedasticity (larger errors at higher values). RMSE and MAE RMSE=sqrt(mean(e_i²)) penalises large errors; MAE=mean(|e_i|) is easier to interpret as average absolute error. Both retain the variable's physical units. R-squared R² can describe explained variance relative to a baseline but does not replace error units or reveal bias. The evaluation definition and reference distribution matter. Transfer tests For vegetation-cover models, hold out whole field sites, seasons or ecological zones when the intended use is spatial/temporal extrapolation. A random split of neighbouring pixels can be misleading. Report more than a score Provide sample design, sample size, uncertainty intervals, residual plots, performance by subgroup and known limits of applicability."}, {"title": "16. Integrated practical: two-date ENVI classification", "html": "<p>Lecture 00:16:54–00:17:34; slides 46–48, 63–78</p><h3>Step 1</h3><p>Prepare two comparable images and consistent class definitions. Check geometry, reflectance/normalisation, seasons, cloud masks and image extent.</p><h3>Step 2</h3><p>Classify each image with appropriate training data. Keep training and validation independent and evaluate class-specific accuracy.</p><h3>Step 3</h3><p>Cross-tabulate classified maps to produce transition counts/areas. Inspect where transitions cluster, especially along boundaries and mixed pixels.</p><h3>Step 4</h3><p>Use independent change reference to test transition claims. If unavailable, acknowledge compounded classification uncertainty and avoid precise area-change claims.</p><h3>Defence</h3><p>If asked &#x27;Why not simply subtract two RGB images?&#x27;, explain that raw band differences conflate radiometry, illumination, phenology and sensor response with real environmental change.</p>", "text": "Lecture 00:16:54–00:17:34; slides 46–48, 63–78 Step 1 Prepare two comparable images and consistent class definitions. Check geometry, reflectance/normalisation, seasons, cloud masks and image extent. Step 2 Classify each image with appropriate training data. Keep training and validation independent and evaluate class-specific accuracy. Step 3 Cross-tabulate classified maps to produce transition counts/areas. Inspect where transitions cluster, especially along boundaries and mixed pixels. Step 4 Use independent change reference to test transition claims. If unavailable, acknowledge compounded classification uncertainty and avoid precise area-change claims. Defence If asked 'Why not simply subtract two RGB images?', explain that raw band differences conflate radiometry, illumination, phenology and sensor response with real environmental change."}, {"title": "17. Integrated exam scenario: mangrove dieback detection", "html": "<p>Original exam exercise based on lecture principles</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>A team compares a cloud-affected Landsat NDVI image from December 2014 with a dry-season image from July 2016, calls all declines greater than 0.1 mangrove dieback, and reports 92% accuracy using the same polygons that trained its classifier.</p><h3>Problem 1: temporal comparability</h3><p>Different seasons and cloud contamination can create false change. Select seasonally comparable observations, robust quality masks and preferably a multiyear baseline.</p><h3>Problem 2: physical comparability</h3><p>Confirm sensor/band equivalence, surface reflectance, geometric registration, BRDF and tidal/water-level effects near coasts.</p><h3>Problem 3: inference</h3><p>NDVI decline is not unique to dieback. Use independent evidence, multiple indices or biophysical variables, persistence tests and ecological context.</p><h3>Problem 4: validation</h3><p>Training polygons are not independent reference samples. Design independent, representative reference data and calculate change-detection performance rather than only land-cover accuracy.</p><h3>Defensible redesign</h3><p>Build 2012–2014 seasonal baseline, examine anomalies and persistence through 2015–2017, mask invalid pixels, compare against independent dieback/reference locations, quantify false positives/negatives and report spatial uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Original exam exercise based on lecture principles Prompt A team compares a cloud-affected Landsat NDVI image from December 2014 with a dry-season image from July 2016, calls all declines greater than 0.1 mangrove dieback, and reports 92% accuracy using the same polygons that trained its classifier. Problem 1: temporal comparability Different seasons and cloud contamination can create false change. Select seasonally comparable observations, robust quality masks and preferably a multiyear baseline. Problem 2: physical comparability Confirm sensor/band equivalence, surface reflectance, geometric registration, BRDF and tidal/water-level effects near coasts. Problem 3: inference NDVI decline is not unique to dieback. Use independent evidence, multiple indices or biophysical variables, persistence tests and ecological context. Problem 4: validation Training polygons are not independent reference samples. Design independent, representative reference data and calculate change-detection performance rather than only land-cover accuracy. Defensible redesign Build 2012–2014 seasonal baseline, examine anomalies and persistence through 2015–2017, mask invalid pixels, compare against independent dieback/reference locations, quantify false positives/negatives and report spatial uncertainty."}, {"title": "18. Self-test and model answers", "html": "<p>Based on slides 18–78</p><h3>Q1</h3><p>Why must two-date images share a grid? A: Misregistration shifts edges, generating artificial pixel differences.</p><h3>Q2</h3><p>When is a temporal ratio unsafe? A: When the denominator is zero/near-zero or the metric contains signed values not suited to proportional interpretation.</p><h3>Q3</h3><p>What does a post-classification matrix measure? A: Mapped class-to-class transitions; it is not automatically a validated change estimate.</p><h3>Q4</h3><p>Why is overall accuracy insufficient? A: Rare classes and asymmetric omission/commission errors may be hidden.</p><h3>Q5</h3><p>How are UA and PA different? A: UA conditions on map label, PA on reference label.</p><h3>Q6</h3><p>What is the risk of validating with training polygons? A: Optimistic, non-independent accuracy estimates.</p><h3>Q7</h3><p>Why does field-reference support matter? A: A field point and satellite pixel can measure different mixtures or areas.</p><h3>Q8</h3><p>How should you defend a small mapped change? A: Compare its magnitude with classification and reference uncertainty; use independent change samples and area-adjusted estimates if appropriate.</p>", "text": "Based on slides 18–78 Q1 Why must two-date images share a grid? A: Misregistration shifts edges, generating artificial pixel differences. Q2 When is a temporal ratio unsafe? A: When the denominator is zero/near-zero or the metric contains signed values not suited to proportional interpretation. Q3 What does a post-classification matrix measure? A: Mapped class-to-class transitions; it is not automatically a validated change estimate. Q4 Why is overall accuracy insufficient? A: Rare classes and asymmetric omission/commission errors may be hidden. Q5 How are UA and PA different? A: UA conditions on map label, PA on reference label. Q6 What is the risk of validating with training polygons? A: Optimistic, non-independent accuracy estimates. Q7 Why does field-reference support matter? A: A field point and satellite pixel can measure different mixtures or areas. Q8 How should you defend a small mapped change? A: Compare its magnitude with classification and reference uncertainty; use independent change samples and area-adjusted estimates if appropriate."}], "text": ["1. Where this lecture sits in the course Slides 1–6; recording 00:12:53–00:17:25 Speaker framing Stuart Phinn describes three linked tasks: extracting biophysical variables from imagery, detecting environmental change across dates, and assessing the errors in resulting maps. He distinguishes uncertainty in raw imagery (position and radiometry) from validation of derived information. The key conceptual contrast A thematic map labels pixels or objects as categories, whereas a biophysical map estimates a quantity such as canopy cover, surface temperature or suspended sediment. The error model and validation metric should match the type of output. What students actually do The speaker connects the lecture to the ENVI practical: assess thematic classification accuracy and compare classified images from two dates. These activities are not equivalent to testing continuous-variable models or analysing long time series. Decision question Before choosing an algorithm, specify the target quantity or class, the spatial/temporal support, the purpose of the map, the reference measurement and the acceptable uncertainty.", "2. Worked case: field to drone to satellite woody vegetation cover Slides 7–13; recording approximately 00:21–00:35 Why the example matters The deck develops woody vegetation cover as a chain from field measurements through drone observations to satellite maps. This is a quantitative estimation task, not merely a woody/non-woody classification. Support and scale A ground plot measures vegetation in a finite area; a UAV image resolves individual crowns or canopy gaps; a satellite pixel averages a much larger footprint. A model calibrated at one scale is not automatically valid at another. Field protocol Define woody cover consistently (e.g. projected canopy fraction or foliage projective cover, depending on the study). Record plot dimensions, coordinates, dates, instrument/protocol, species/structure and measurement uncertainty. Do not conflate distinct vegetation-cover definitions. Spatial-temporal matching At 00:32:30–00:34:07, the speaker stresses that validation observations must match the image spatially and ideally temporally. A plot centre coordinate is not enough when its footprint spans heterogeneous pixels. Critique An analyst calibrates 10 m woody-cover pixels with 1 m² field observations taken two years earlier. Major problems: scale mismatch, geolocation error, changing vegetation and unrepresentative sampling.", "3. Thematic versus biophysical mapping Slides 14–16; recording 00:15:14–00:16:19 Thematic output Assign a label (water, urban, mangrove, etc.) to each mapping unit. Validation asks whether assigned classes agree with reference labels, commonly using a confusion matrix. Biophysical output Estimate a continuous property such as cover percentage, temperature, biomass or water-column depth. Validation asks about residuals, bias, RMSE, calibration and prediction intervals. Why the distinction changes modelling A vegetation classifier may accurately identify mangroves but say nothing about their canopy density or health. An NDVI regression may estimate cover but not identify vegetation species. Model choice Use a classification model for categorical labels and regression or physically based inversion for continuous variables. A workflow can combine them: mask vegetation first, then model cover within valid vegetation pixels.", "4. Preconditions for comparing multiple images Slides 18–27; recording around 00:36–00:58 Geometric comparability Same projection/grid and sufficiently accurate image-to-image registration. Even subpixel shifts can create artificial change at sharp coastlines, roads or canopy boundaries. Spatial resolution Match ground sampling and point-spread behaviour; resampling a coarse image to a fine grid does not add genuine spatial information. Consider a common analysis grid and appropriate aggregation. Spectral comparability Check band centres, widths and sensor response functions. 'Red' and 'NIR' bands from different instruments are not necessarily identical; harmonisation may be required. Radiometric comparability Compare calibrated and corrected measurements, preferably consistent surface reflectance where appropriate. Quantisation, saturation, atmospheric effects and BRDF can masquerade as change. Acquisition geometry Slides 24–26 discuss BRDF and sun/viewing geometry. Anniversary-date acquisitions reduce seasonal geometry differences but do not remove them. Scene conditions Slides 26–27 note clouds, haze and other scene differences. Also assess phenology, tides for aquatic/coastal settings, water levels, shadows and data completeness. Exam answer A defensible change workflow explicitly lists these requirements before image differencing. 'Both images are Landsat' is not sufficient justification.", "5. Multi-date normalisation and BRDF Slides 21–26 Absolute versus relative Absolute calibration/correction attempts physically comparable reflectance. Relative normalisation adjusts images to a common reference distribution or stable targets, useful when perfect atmospheric comparability is unavailable. Stable-target assumption Pseudo-invariant features must truly be stable. If an apparent reference feature changed, the normalisation can suppress real change or introduce false change. BRDF Bidirectional reflectance distribution describes directional variation in surface reflectance with illumination and viewing geometry. Two images of the same unchanged canopy can differ because their sun and view angles differ. A practical check Plot values from stable reference surfaces across dates. Examine systematic offsets and slopes, not just visual similarity. Keep a record of normalisation method and residual error.", "6. Biophysical modelling: the measurement chain Slides 29–34; recording approximately 00:58–01:10 From pixel values to parameters The slides describe quantitatively relating image measurements to biophysical variables. The mapping function can be empirical (field-calibrated regression) or physically informed (radiative transfer/inversion). Examples in the deck Global products include atmospheric carbon dioxide, land surface properties and other environmental quantities; the Queensland-government examples show operational EO products. Empirical regression Fit a model y=f(x), where y is measured field property and x represents spectral bands, indices, texture or ancillary variables. Document preprocessing, calibration domain and independent testing. Physical model A radiative-transfer model predicts observed radiance/reflectance given surface and atmospheric parameters. Inversion seeks parameters that plausibly produce the observed signal; different parameter combinations may be non-identifiable. Evaluation For continuous outputs, report RMSE and bias in physical units, plus R-squared where meaningful. Show residuals across ranges, seasons and land-cover types. Independent observations should not be recycled from training. Original numeric illustration If measured woody cover is [20,40,60]% and predictions are [25,35,65]%, errors are [+5,−5,+5] percentage points; RMSE=5 percentage points and bias=+1.67. These are invented teaching values, not lecture findings.", "7. Change detection: what is actually changing? Slides 35–37; recording 00:16:19–00:17:25 Definition The slides frame change detection as identifying meaningful changes in surface measurements or derived information over time. Meaningful change must be distinguished from noise, phenology and correction artefacts. Two broad strategies Image-based change analyses compare spectral or biophysical quantities; post-classification comparison identifies transitions among thematic categories. Each produces different answers. The choice depends on the question If asking whether canopy cover declined from 65% to 40%, continuous-variable differencing is natural. If asking whether a mangrove area became mudflat, categorical transition analysis is natural. Causation warning A changed spectral signature is evidence of altered observation, not proof of a particular driver such as drought, clearing or disease.", "8. Image differencing Slides 38–41 Equation For a consistent pixel and band/index, D = X(date 2) − X(date 1). The sign and magnitude depend on the measurement definition; a negative NDVI difference may indicate reduced greenness but not necessarily vegetation loss. Original example NDVI(date 1)=0.72, NDVI(date 2)=0.41 gives D=−0.31. Interpret only after confirming valid pixels, matching seasons, registration and spectral/radiometric consistency. Thresholding A binary changed/unchanged map requires a justified threshold. A threshold may be based on measurement uncertainty, stable-site variation, a distributional method or independent reference change. Limitations Two dates cannot distinguish an abrupt disturbance from normal seasonal fluctuation. Misregistration near class boundaries can generate a strong artificial difference. Exam critique If a researcher thresholds all NDVI decreases below −0.1 as dieback, ask whether −0.1 exceeds the combined noise and seasonal variability and whether independent evidence supports ecological dieback.", "9. Temporal ratios and change-vector analysis Slides 42–45 Ratios A temporal ratio R=X2/X1 can highlight relative proportional change. It becomes unstable when X1 is near zero and behaves differently for negative-valued indices. Original example If a nonnegative canopy metric decreases from 0.60 to 0.30, R=0.5: the second value is half the first. This does not necessarily imply a 50% reduction in biomass. Change vectors With multiple spectral bands, calculate vector difference Δx=x2−x1; its magnitude sqrt(sum(Δband²)) represents spectral-change strength and direction can contain information about the type of change. Scale dependence Vector magnitude depends on band scaling, correlated bands and units. Normalise appropriately and avoid interpreting large magnitudes as automatically important ecological change. Multidate trajectories More than two dates can identify onset, persistence and recovery, although these require temporal sampling sufficient to resolve the event.", "10. Post-classification change and transition matrices Slides 46–48; lecture links to ENVI practical Procedure Classify each date using consistent definitions, align the maps, then cross-tabulate the date-1 classes against date-2 classes. Diagonal cells indicate persistence; off-diagonal cells represent mapped transitions. Example If 80 hectares map as mangrove in year 1, and 12 hectares of those map as mudflat in year 2, the mapped mangrove→mudflat transition is 12 ha. That is a map-derived transition, not automatically a validated true change. Error propagation If each classification has error, change detection can compound it. An apparent transition may reflect inconsistent labels or different class-specific errors rather than real land-cover change. Better evaluation Collect independent reference observations for transitions when possible, including stable and changed locations. Report uncertainty of change-area estimates rather than only transition counts. Exam critique An analyst reports a 2% mangrove decline from two maps with 85% overall accuracy each. That small net change could be overwhelmed by map error; investigate class-specific errors and reference-based change estimates.", "11. Time series and trends Slides 49–52; recording around 00:20:15–00:20:59 Why more dates help The lecturer distinguishes two-date change from series with 3, 4, 5 or hundreds of observations. Cloud platforms and data cubes make large time series tractable. Components A time series can contain long-term trend, seasonal cycle, abrupt events, recovery and observation noise. A linear trend alone can misrepresent abrupt disturbance. Sampling Compare like seasons and inspect valid observation counts. Irregular cloud gaps can bias annual summaries; a median from one season is not equivalent to a full-year median. Original anomaly calculation For a seasonally matched baseline mean 0.68 and SD 0.04, NDVI 0.56 yields z=(0.56−0.68)/0.04=−3.0. It is a strong statistical anomaly under that baseline, not a diagnosis of its cause. Spatial versus point analysis Slides 52 contrast spatially explicit and point-based approaches. Pixelwise models can map heterogeneity but multiply testing and autocorrelation issues; plot/region summaries can reduce noise but conceal local change.", "12. Understanding error, accuracy and validation Slides 54–62; recording around 01:40–01:46 Error is not a single number Geometric, spectral, radiometric, model and reference errors affect the final information product. A map can have good geometric accuracy and poor class accuracy, or vice versa. The lecturer's caution Around 01:43:25–01:43:56, Stuart Phinn emphasises that ground measurements themselves involve sampling choices and are not perfect truth. Treat reference data as best-available evidence with uncertainty. Reference representativeness Ensure field protocol measures the same variable at the same spatial support and time as the EO product. In a mixed pixel, a single point label may not represent the whole pixel. Independence Training samples used to fit a classifier should not be treated as independent validation. Nearby samples may also share spatial autocorrelation, making nominally separate datasets less independent than assumed. Accuracy versus precision Accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference; precision concerns repeatability or dispersion. A model may be consistently biased yet precise.", "13. Designing a defensible accuracy assessment Slides 63–74; recording around 01:52–02:00 Source sequence Slides 65 specify sampling design, reference dataset selection, comparison and error matrix/accuracy evaluation. The speaker explains that sample locations, sample size and sample-unit footprint matter. Sampling unit Point, pixel, plot and object are not interchangeable. Select a unit compatible with the map product and expected heterogeneity. Sampling strategies Random, systematic, stratified and stratified random designs are discussed. Stratification can ensure adequate reference samples for rare classes but raw counts may need design weights for area-wide accuracy. Sample-size equation Slide 70 gives N=Z²p(1−p)/E². For Z=1.96, expected accuracy p=0.85 and target margin E=0.05, N≈196; this simple proportion formula is an illustrative starting point, not a complete spatial design. Reference data quality Slides 73–74 discuss existing maps/ancillary information and field data. Prefer independent, higher-quality, contemporaneous sources; document interpreter uncertainty and disagreements. Risk of convenience samples Sampling only accessible roadside plots can bias estimates for remote or dense vegetation. A representative probability design is preferable when reporting population-wide accuracy.", "14. Confusion matrix: calculations and interpretation Slides 75–78 Matrix convention Specify whether rows represent map predictions and columns reference labels; conventions vary. This example uses rows=map and columns=reference. Original two-class example Map mangrove: 42 true mangrove and 8 true other; map other: 6 true mangrove and 44 true other. Total=100; diagonal correct=86. Overall accuracy OA=(42+44)/100=0.86 or 86%. This single number can conceal uneven performance between classes. User's accuracy for mangrove UA=42/(42+8)=0.84 or 84%. Of the area mapped mangrove in this sample, 84% is supported by reference labels; complement is commission error. Producer's accuracy for mangrove PA=42/(42+6)=0.875 or 87.5%. Of reference mangrove samples, 87.5% were correctly mapped; complement is omission error. Why the denominators matter User's accuracy conditions on the mapped category; producer's accuracy conditions on the reference category. Always state row/column orientation before calculating. Area estimation caveat When sampling is stratified disproportionately, raw confusion-matrix percentages may not estimate map-wide accuracy. Use sampling weights and uncertainty intervals where required.", "15. Continuous-variable validation is different Slides 14–15, 62; supplementary statistical detail Residuals For each independent plot, residual e_i=prediction_i−reference_i. Inspect systematic bias, scatter and heteroscedasticity (larger errors at higher values). RMSE and MAE RMSE=sqrt(mean(e_i²)) penalises large errors; MAE=mean(|e_i|) is easier to interpret as average absolute error. Both retain the variable's physical units. R-squared R² can describe explained variance relative to a baseline but does not replace error units or reveal bias. The evaluation definition and reference distribution matter. Transfer tests For vegetation-cover models, hold out whole field sites, seasons or ecological zones when the intended use is spatial/temporal extrapolation. A random split of neighbouring pixels can be misleading. Report more than a score Provide sample design, sample size, uncertainty intervals, residual plots, performance by subgroup and known limits of applicability.", "16. Integrated practical: two-date ENVI classification Lecture 00:16:54–00:17:34; slides 46–48, 63–78 Step 1 Prepare two comparable images and consistent class definitions. Check geometry, reflectance/normalisation, seasons, cloud masks and image extent. Step 2 Classify each image with appropriate training data. Keep training and validation independent and evaluate class-specific accuracy. Step 3 Cross-tabulate classified maps to produce transition counts/areas. Inspect where transitions cluster, especially along boundaries and mixed pixels. Step 4 Use independent change reference to test transition claims. If unavailable, acknowledge compounded classification uncertainty and avoid precise area-change claims. Defence If asked 'Why not simply subtract two RGB images?', explain that raw band differences conflate radiometry, illumination, phenology and sensor response with real environmental change.", "17. Integrated exam scenario: mangrove dieback detection Original exam exercise based on lecture principles Prompt A team compares a cloud-affected Landsat NDVI image from December 2014 with a dry-season image from July 2016, calls all declines greater than 0.1 mangrove dieback, and reports 92% accuracy using the same polygons that trained its classifier. Problem 1: temporal comparability Different seasons and cloud contamination can create false change. Select seasonally comparable observations, robust quality masks and preferably a multiyear baseline. Problem 2: physical comparability Confirm sensor/band equivalence, surface reflectance, geometric registration, BRDF and tidal/water-level effects near coasts. Problem 3: inference NDVI decline is not unique to dieback. Use independent evidence, multiple indices or biophysical variables, persistence tests and ecological context. Problem 4: validation Training polygons are not independent reference samples. Design independent, representative reference data and calculate change-detection performance rather than only land-cover accuracy. Defensible redesign Build 2012–2014 seasonal baseline, examine anomalies and persistence through 2015–2017, mask invalid pixels, compare against independent dieback/reference locations, quantify false positives/negatives and report spatial uncertainty.", "18. Self-test and model answers Based on slides 18–78 Q1 Why must two-date images share a grid? A: Misregistration shifts edges, generating artificial pixel differences. Q2 When is a temporal ratio unsafe? A: When the denominator is zero/near-zero or the metric contains signed values not suited to proportional interpretation. Q3 What does a post-classification matrix measure? A: Mapped class-to-class transitions; it is not automatically a validated change estimate. Q4 Why is overall accuracy insufficient? A: Rare classes and asymmetric omission/commission errors may be hidden. Q5 How are UA and PA different? A: UA conditions on map label, PA on reference label. Q6 What is the risk of validating with training polygons? A: Optimistic, non-independent accuracy estimates. Q7 Why does field-reference support matter? A: A field point and satellite pixel can measure different mixtures or areas. Q8 How should you defend a small mapped change? A: Compare its magnitude with classification and reference uncertainty; use independent change samples and area-adjusted estimates if appropriate."]}, {"id": 7, "title": "Terrestrial LiDAR & Radiative Transfer Modelling", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week08_LiDAR_RTM.pdf", "pages": 8, "sections": [{"title": "1. Lecture structure and what is actually recorded", "html": "<p>Transcript 00:04:10–00:04:48; LiDAR slides 1–2; RTM slides 1–5</p><h3>Two connected halves</h3><p>Will Woodgate introduces Week 8, Lecture 7 as a two-part session. The first part is a recorded guest lecture by Professor Kim Calders on terrestrial LiDAR. Woodgate then presents radiative transfer applications for vegetation. The two topics connect through forest structure: LiDAR describes geometry and RTMs explain how photons interact with that geometry.</p><h3>Three distinct scientific products</h3><p>A point cloud gives sampled 3D surface locations. A structural product estimates attributes such as tree dimensions or canopy gaps. A radiative-transfer simulation predicts the electromagnetic response of a scene under specified geometry and material properties. These are not interchangeable.</p><h3>Central exam question</h3><p>Can you defend each assumption between sensor observations, physical structure, modelled reflectance and the ecological quantity being inferred?</p>", "text": "Transcript 00:04:10–00:04:48; LiDAR slides 1–2; RTM slides 1–5 Two connected halves Will Woodgate introduces Week 8, Lecture 7 as a two-part session. The first part is a recorded guest lecture by Professor Kim Calders on terrestrial LiDAR. Woodgate then presents radiative transfer applications for vegetation. The two topics connect through forest structure: LiDAR describes geometry and RTMs explain how photons interact with that geometry. Three distinct scientific products A point cloud gives sampled 3D surface locations. A structural product estimates attributes such as tree dimensions or canopy gaps. A radiative-transfer simulation predicts the electromagnetic response of a scene under specified geometry and material properties. These are not interchangeable. Central exam question Can you defend each assumption between sensor observations, physical structure, modelled reflectance and the ecological quantity being inferred?"}, {"title": "2. Why measure forests in 3D?", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 3–13; recorded guest introduction around 00:08–00:12</p><h3>The measurement gap</h3><p>The slides contrast broad spatial coverage of satellite remote sensing with detailed but local in-situ measurements. A 2D optical image can show canopy colour and texture but may not directly resolve trunk diameter, branch architecture or canopy gaps.</p><h3>Structural variables</h3><p>Forest monitoring may need stem diameter, height, crown geometry, volume, woody biomass, vertical foliage distribution and structural change. Each requires a defined observation model, not just a visually convincing 3D rendering.</p><h3>Sampling and representativeness</h3><p>A detailed LiDAR plot does not automatically represent a region. Site selection, species composition, slope and stand age affect how reliably plot relationships can be extrapolated.</p><h3>Critical distinction</h3><p>LiDAR measures laser returns and distances. Biomass is a derived quantity, typically requiring geometric reconstruction, wood density and biological assumptions.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 3–13; recorded guest introduction around 00:08–00:12 The measurement gap The slides contrast broad spatial coverage of satellite remote sensing with detailed but local in-situ measurements. A 2D optical image can show canopy colour and texture but may not directly resolve trunk diameter, branch architecture or canopy gaps. Structural variables Forest monitoring may need stem diameter, height, crown geometry, volume, woody biomass, vertical foliage distribution and structural change. Each requires a defined observation model, not just a visually convincing 3D rendering. Sampling and representativeness A detailed LiDAR plot does not automatically represent a region. Site selection, species composition, slope and stand age affect how reliably plot relationships can be extrapolated. Critical distinction LiDAR measures laser returns and distances. Biomass is a derived quantity, typically requiring geometric reconstruction, wood density and biological assumptions."}, {"title": "3. How terrestrial LiDAR works", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 14–20</p><h3>Active ranging</h3><p>Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) is an active sensor: the instrument emits laser energy and measures the return. In time-of-flight ranging, distance is proportional to travel time, d = cΔt/2. The factor of two accounts for the outgoing and returning path.</p><h3>Original calculation</h3><p>For a round-trip time of 20 nanoseconds, d ≈ (3×10^8 m/s × 20×10^-9 s)/2 = 3 m. Real instruments also require timing calibration and beam-direction knowledge.</p><h3>From ranges to XYZ</h3><p>Combine distance with scanner azimuth/elevation and instrument position/orientation to locate points in 3D. A point cloud is a sample of visible surfaces, not a complete solid reconstruction.</p><h3>Sensor configuration</h3><p>Different terrestrial scanners vary in beam divergence, wavelength, ranging technology, field of view and scan density. These properties affect point sampling and material interaction.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 14–20 Active ranging Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) is an active sensor: the instrument emits laser energy and measures the return. In time-of-flight ranging, distance is proportional to travel time, d = cΔt/2. The factor of two accounts for the outgoing and returning path. Original calculation For a round-trip time of 20 nanoseconds, d ≈ (3×10^8 m/s × 20×10^-9 s)/2 = 3 m. Real instruments also require timing calibration and beam-direction knowledge. From ranges to XYZ Combine distance with scanner azimuth/elevation and instrument position/orientation to locate points in 3D. A point cloud is a sample of visible surfaces, not a complete solid reconstruction. Sensor configuration Different terrestrial scanners vary in beam divergence, wavelength, ranging technology, field of view and scan density. These properties affect point sampling and material interaction."}, {"title": "4. Occlusion, scan design and registration", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 20–23, 29–31</p><h3>Occlusion</h3><p>Branches, leaves and trunks block lines of sight. A single scan may miss the far side of a tree or internal canopy; a sparse point cloud can therefore reflect visibility rather than actual absence of material.</p><h3>Multiple scan positions</h3><p>Multiple viewpoints can reduce occlusion but require point-cloud registration. Targets, geometric features or control networks can align scans to a shared coordinate system.</p><h3>Registration uncertainty</h3><p>Even a small rotational misalignment can displace distant points. Check residuals, overlap consistency and independent survey control. A tight visual fit is not proof of correct absolute coordinates.</p><h3>Practical trade-off</h3><p>More scans improve completeness but increase field time, storage, processing and opportunities for registration error. Choose scan density based on the structural metric and plot complexity.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 20–23, 29–31 Occlusion Branches, leaves and trunks block lines of sight. A single scan may miss the far side of a tree or internal canopy; a sparse point cloud can therefore reflect visibility rather than actual absence of material. Multiple scan positions Multiple viewpoints can reduce occlusion but require point-cloud registration. Targets, geometric features or control networks can align scans to a shared coordinate system. Registration uncertainty Even a small rotational misalignment can displace distant points. Check residuals, overlap consistency and independent survey control. A tight visual fit is not proof of correct absolute coordinates. Practical trade-off More scans improve completeness but increase field time, storage, processing and opportunities for registration error. Choose scan density based on the structural metric and plot complexity."}, {"title": "5. From point clouds to woody biomass", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 24–36</p><h3>Quantitative structure models</h3><p>The guest slides discuss detailed tree-structure reconstruction. A quantitative structure model (QSM) approximates woody stems and branches with geometric primitives such as cylinders, enabling estimates of woody volume.</p><h3>Biomass chain</h3><p>An illustrative chain is point cloud → stem/branch segmentation → fitted geometry → woody volume → density-weighted dry mass. Wood density may vary by species, tissue and tree; assumptions should be documented.</p><h3>Why allometry matters</h3><p>Traditional biomass estimates often use equations relating diameter, height and wood density to biomass. QSMs offer a more explicit geometry-based approach but are sensitive to missing branches and segmentation choices.</p><h3>Validation</h3><p>Compare modelled dimensions or biomass against independent reference measurements when feasible. Report systematic errors for small branches, large stems and occluded canopy regions.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>A LiDAR-derived biomass map is not a direct laser measurement of carbon. Conversion to carbon requires further assumptions about biomass composition.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 24–36 Quantitative structure models The guest slides discuss detailed tree-structure reconstruction. A quantitative structure model (QSM) approximates woody stems and branches with geometric primitives such as cylinders, enabling estimates of woody volume. Biomass chain An illustrative chain is point cloud → stem/branch segmentation → fitted geometry → woody volume → density-weighted dry mass. Wood density may vary by species, tissue and tree; assumptions should be documented. Why allometry matters Traditional biomass estimates often use equations relating diameter, height and wood density to biomass. QSMs offer a more explicit geometry-based approach but are sensitive to missing branches and segmentation choices. Validation Compare modelled dimensions or biomass against independent reference measurements when feasible. Report systematic errors for small branches, large stems and occluded canopy regions. Exam trap A LiDAR-derived biomass map is not a direct laser measurement of carbon. Conversion to carbon requires further assumptions about biomass composition."}, {"title": "6. Urban structure and virtual forests", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 37–46</p><h3>Beyond forestry</h3><p>The slides extend terrestrial LiDAR to urban vegetation and 3D modelling, including combinations of terrestrial and airborne LiDAR. Each platform sees different parts of the canopy and ground.</p><h3>Complementary perspectives</h3><p>Airborne scanning tends to observe upper canopy and broad terrain; terrestrial scanning often resolves trunks and lower canopy. Merging them can improve structural completeness but requires consistent registration and sampling models.</p><h3>Virtual forests</h3><p>3D scene models can be used as inputs to environmental simulations. Their usefulness depends on whether geometry, leaf distribution and material properties are realistic at the scale of the model.</p><h3>Connection to RTM</h3><p>A structurally plausible virtual forest provides a more realistic scene for simulating illumination, shadowing, scattering and sensor-view effects than a simple homogeneous canopy.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 37–46 Beyond forestry The slides extend terrestrial LiDAR to urban vegetation and 3D modelling, including combinations of terrestrial and airborne LiDAR. Each platform sees different parts of the canopy and ground. Complementary perspectives Airborne scanning tends to observe upper canopy and broad terrain; terrestrial scanning often resolves trunks and lower canopy. Merging them can improve structural completeness but requires consistent registration and sampling models. Virtual forests 3D scene models can be used as inputs to environmental simulations. Their usefulness depends on whether geometry, leaf distribution and material properties are realistic at the scale of the model. Connection to RTM A structurally plausible virtual forest provides a more realistic scene for simulating illumination, shadowing, scattering and sensor-view effects than a simple homogeneous canopy."}, {"title": "7. Gap fraction and canopy phenology", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 47–56; transcript around 00:10–00:11</p><h3>Definition</h3><p>Gap fraction is the proportion of viewing directions or rays through a canopy that encounter no obstructing vegetation over a defined angular sector. It varies with direction, leaf condition and scan geometry.</p><h3>Link to leaf area</h3><p>Under simplified random-leaf assumptions, canopy gap probability can be represented using an exponential attenuation relationship. Inverting it can estimate plant area or leaf area, but woody elements and nonrandom clumping complicate interpretation.</p><h3>Seasonal monitoring</h3><p>The slides discuss repeated scans to monitor spring phenology. Changes in gap fraction may reflect leaf emergence, but also instrument position, registration and acquisition conditions.</p><h3>Why leaf area is not automatic</h3><p>A laser beam may hit a branch rather than a leaf, and occlusion affects which components are sampled. Leaf area retrieval needs explicit treatment of wood, clumping and angular effects.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 47–56; transcript around 00:10–00:11 Definition Gap fraction is the proportion of viewing directions or rays through a canopy that encounter no obstructing vegetation over a defined angular sector. It varies with direction, leaf condition and scan geometry. Link to leaf area Under simplified random-leaf assumptions, canopy gap probability can be represented using an exponential attenuation relationship. Inverting it can estimate plant area or leaf area, but woody elements and nonrandom clumping complicate interpretation. Seasonal monitoring The slides discuss repeated scans to monitor spring phenology. Changes in gap fraction may reflect leaf emergence, but also instrument position, registration and acquisition conditions. Why leaf area is not automatic A laser beam may hit a branch rather than a leaf, and occlusion affects which components are sampled. Leaf area retrieval needs explicit treatment of wood, clumping and angular effects."}, {"title": "8. Future directions: multi-platform LiDAR and GEDI", "html": "<p>LiDAR slides 57–70</p><h3>Combining platforms</h3><p>The guest slides discuss combining complementary point clouds to reduce occlusion. This requires robust cross-platform alignment and understanding of different sampling densities.</p><h3>GEDI</h3><p>The slides introduce the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI), a spaceborne lidar mission that samples vegetation vertical structure. Spaceborne footprints provide broad geographic coverage but do not constitute a wall-to-wall high-resolution TLS map.</p><h3>Scale-up problem</h3><p>Linking plot-scale TLS, airborne lidar and satellite footprints requires matching spatial support, ecological conditions and uncertainty. Extrapolation beyond sampled environments is a model, not a measurement.</p><h3>Exam defence</h3><p>Explain what each platform measures, which canopy components it misses, and how independent reference data would test regional estimates.</p>", "text": "LiDAR slides 57–70 Combining platforms The guest slides discuss combining complementary point clouds to reduce occlusion. This requires robust cross-platform alignment and understanding of different sampling densities. GEDI The slides introduce the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI), a spaceborne lidar mission that samples vegetation vertical structure. Spaceborne footprints provide broad geographic coverage but do not constitute a wall-to-wall high-resolution TLS map. Scale-up problem Linking plot-scale TLS, airborne lidar and satellite footprints requires matching spatial support, ecological conditions and uncertainty. Extrapolation beyond sampled environments is a model, not a measurement. Exam defence Explain what each platform measures, which canopy components it misses, and how independent reference data would test regional estimates."}, {"title": "9. Radiative transfer: the forward problem", "html": "<p>RTM slides 3–6, 21–29; transcript 01:09:55–01:10:15</p><h3>Definition</h3><p>Woodgate frames radiative transfer as understanding photons of electromagnetic radiation and what a sensor measures after those photons interact with matter. A forward RTM predicts the sensor signal given source, geometry, medium and material properties.</p><h3>Components</h3><p>Specify the radiation source, atmospheric pathway, target scene, sensor viewing geometry, wavelength-dependent absorption/scattering and boundary conditions.</p><h3>Forward versus inverse</h3><p>Forward: given leaf chlorophyll, canopy structure and sun/view angles, simulate reflectance. Inverse: given measured reflectance, estimate chlorophyll or structure. Inversion may be ambiguous because multiple scenes can produce similar spectra.</p><h3>Why models matter</h3><p>RTMs help interpret physical causes of spectral variation, generate synthetic datasets and test sensor configurations; they do not automatically eliminate calibration or validation requirements.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 3–6, 21–29; transcript 01:09:55–01:10:15 Definition Woodgate frames radiative transfer as understanding photons of electromagnetic radiation and what a sensor measures after those photons interact with matter. A forward RTM predicts the sensor signal given source, geometry, medium and material properties. Components Specify the radiation source, atmospheric pathway, target scene, sensor viewing geometry, wavelength-dependent absorption/scattering and boundary conditions. Forward versus inverse Forward: given leaf chlorophyll, canopy structure and sun/view angles, simulate reflectance. Inverse: given measured reflectance, estimate chlorophyll or structure. Inversion may be ambiguous because multiple scenes can produce similar spectra. Why models matter RTMs help interpret physical causes of spectral variation, generate synthetic datasets and test sensor configurations; they do not automatically eliminate calibration or validation requirements."}, {"title": "10. Scattering, absorption, transmission and extinction", "html": "<p>RTM slides 12–20; transcript around 01:17–01:28</p><h3>Energy pathways</h3><p>Incident radiation can be reflected/scattered, absorbed or transmitted. The relative amounts depend on wavelength, material and geometry. The slides distinguish scattering from absorption and introduce extinction as removal of energy from a particular beam.</p><h3>Beer–Lambert intuition</h3><p>For a simple homogeneous medium, transmitted intensity I = I0 exp(−τ), where τ is optical depth. This is a model of beam attenuation, not a universal canopy inversion formula.</p><h3>Original calculation</h3><p>For optical depth τ = 0.8, transmittance exp(−0.8) ≈ 0.449. Approximately 44.9% of the initial beam remains in the direct path; the rest is removed from that beam by extinction, which can include scattering and absorption.</p><h3>Why sky blue?</h3><p>The slide asks why the sky is blue. Shorter visible wavelengths are preferentially scattered by small atmospheric molecules (Rayleigh scattering). The question illustrates wavelength-dependent interaction, not forest-canopy physics alone.</p><h3>Limits</h3><p>Multiple scattering, heterogeneous media, directional leaf distributions and atmospheric layering require more complex modelling than a single exponential expression.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 12–20; transcript around 01:17–01:28 Energy pathways Incident radiation can be reflected/scattered, absorbed or transmitted. The relative amounts depend on wavelength, material and geometry. The slides distinguish scattering from absorption and introduce extinction as removal of energy from a particular beam. Beer–Lambert intuition For a simple homogeneous medium, transmitted intensity I = I0 exp(−τ), where τ is optical depth. This is a model of beam attenuation, not a universal canopy inversion formula. Original calculation For optical depth τ = 0.8, transmittance exp(−0.8) ≈ 0.449. Approximately 44.9% of the initial beam remains in the direct path; the rest is removed from that beam by extinction, which can include scattering and absorption. Why sky blue? The slide asks why the sky is blue. Shorter visible wavelengths are preferentially scattered by small atmospheric molecules (Rayleigh scattering). The question illustrates wavelength-dependent interaction, not forest-canopy physics alone. Limits Multiple scattering, heterogeneous media, directional leaf distributions and atmospheric layering require more complex modelling than a single exponential expression."}, {"title": "11. Leaf optical properties and canopy structure", "html": "<p>RTM slides 30–36</p><h3>Leaf-level effects</h3><p>Leaf pigments, water and dry matter influence absorption and scattering at different wavelengths. The deck includes simulated chlorophyll spectral responses.</p><h3>Canopy-level effects</h3><p>Observed canopy reflectance also depends on leaf area, orientation, clumping, crown geometry, shadowing, soil background and illumination/viewing directions.</p><h3>Equifinality</h3><p>A darker canopy signal could arise from higher pigment absorption, more leaf area, more shadow or different soil exposure. A single band or index may not distinguish these causes.</p><h3>Retrieval strategy</h3><p>Use multiple informative wavelengths, structural constraints and independent observations where possible. Document which parameters are fixed, estimated or assumed.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 30–36 Leaf-level effects Leaf pigments, water and dry matter influence absorption and scattering at different wavelengths. The deck includes simulated chlorophyll spectral responses. Canopy-level effects Observed canopy reflectance also depends on leaf area, orientation, clumping, crown geometry, shadowing, soil background and illumination/viewing directions. Equifinality A darker canopy signal could arise from higher pigment absorption, more leaf area, more shadow or different soil exposure. A single band or index may not distinguish these causes. Retrieval strategy Use multiple informative wavelengths, structural constraints and independent observations where possible. Document which parameters are fixed, estimated or assumed."}, {"title": "12. RTM model families: simple to 3D", "html": "<p>RTM slides 37–43</p><h3>One-dimensional homogeneous models</h3><p>These represent vegetation as horizontally uniform layers. They are computationally efficient and useful when the canopy approximates their assumptions.</p><h3>Heterogeneous and 3D models</h3><p>Spatially explicit models represent individual crowns, gaps and other scene components. They better accommodate complex shadowing and directional effects, at greater computational and parameter cost.</p><h3>Trade-off</h3><p>The most complex model is not automatically best. Choose a model whose scene representation matches the question, spatial scale, available input data and validation resources.</p><h3>Physical consistency</h3><p>A realistic-looking 3D image is not sufficient. Leaf optical properties, geometry, atmosphere and sensor spectral response must be credible.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 37–43 One-dimensional homogeneous models These represent vegetation as horizontally uniform layers. They are computationally efficient and useful when the canopy approximates their assumptions. Heterogeneous and 3D models Spatially explicit models represent individual crowns, gaps and other scene components. They better accommodate complex shadowing and directional effects, at greater computational and parameter cost. Trade-off The most complex model is not automatically best. Choose a model whose scene representation matches the question, spatial scale, available input data and validation resources. Physical consistency A realistic-looking 3D image is not sufficient. Leaf optical properties, geometry, atmosphere and sensor spectral response must be credible."}, {"title": "13. Connecting LiDAR to radiative transfer", "html": "<p>RTM slides 44–50; transcript 01:47:35–01:47:51, 01:52:30–01:52:39</p><h3>Lecture connection</h3><p>Woodgate explains that LiDAR provides measured 3D geometry for ray-tracing models. Known sensor position, solar direction and scene geometry permit explicit simulation of photon pathways.</p><h3>Voxel representation</h3><p>The lecture describes voxels as 3D cubes containing information such as vegetation density. Voxelisation reduces complex point clouds to a model representation; voxel size controls detail and computation.</p><h3>What LiDAR cannot directly provide</h3><p>LiDAR geometry does not automatically reveal leaf reflectance, chlorophyll or biochemical composition. RTMs still require wavelength-dependent optical properties and structural assumptions.</p><h3>Validation</h3><p>Test both the reconstructed geometry and the simulated optical signal. A correct geometry model can still yield wrong reflectance if material properties or atmosphere are misrepresented.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 44–50; transcript 01:47:35–01:47:51, 01:52:30–01:52:39 Lecture connection Woodgate explains that LiDAR provides measured 3D geometry for ray-tracing models. Known sensor position, solar direction and scene geometry permit explicit simulation of photon pathways. Voxel representation The lecture describes voxels as 3D cubes containing information such as vegetation density. Voxelisation reduces complex point clouds to a model representation; voxel size controls detail and computation. What LiDAR cannot directly provide LiDAR geometry does not automatically reveal leaf reflectance, chlorophyll or biochemical composition. RTMs still require wavelength-dependent optical properties and structural assumptions. Validation Test both the reconstructed geometry and the simulated optical signal. A correct geometry model can still yield wrong reflectance if material properties or atmosphere are misrepresented."}, {"title": "14. DART and simulation applications", "html": "<p>RTM slides 47–52; transcript around 01:54:13–01:54:32</p><h3>Source model</h3><p>Woodgate mentions DART (Discrete Anisotropic Radiative Transfer) as a model that can simulate passive optical and LiDAR observations for complex scenes, including urban environments.</p><h3>Experimental design</h3><p>Use RTMs to vary one parameter while holding others fixed: leaf area, chlorophyll, solar angle, canopy clumping or sensor view. Examine which spectral changes are physically attributable to that parameter.</p><h3>Synthetic data warning</h3><p>A model trained on simulated scenes can inherit assumptions and miss real-world variation. Independent real measurements remain necessary to assess domain transfer.</p><h3>Exam question</h3><p>Why combine TLS with DART? Because TLS can constrain scene structure while DART simulates radiation interactions, enabling physically interpretable tests of sensor response.</p>", "text": "RTM slides 47–52; transcript around 01:54:13–01:54:32 Source model Woodgate mentions DART (Discrete Anisotropic Radiative Transfer) as a model that can simulate passive optical and LiDAR observations for complex scenes, including urban environments. Experimental design Use RTMs to vary one parameter while holding others fixed: leaf area, chlorophyll, solar angle, canopy clumping or sensor view. Examine which spectral changes are physically attributable to that parameter. Synthetic data warning A model trained on simulated scenes can inherit assumptions and miss real-world variation. Independent real measurements remain necessary to assess domain transfer. Exam question Why combine TLS with DART? Because TLS can constrain scene structure while DART simulates radiation interactions, enabling physically interpretable tests of sensor response."}, {"title": "15. Integrated exam scenario: biomass and canopy health", "html": "<p>Original supplementary exercise based on both lecture halves</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>A team scans five accessible forest plots with TLS, estimates woody biomass using QSMs, extrapolates to a whole region using satellite NDVI, then claims the same model can identify chlorophyll decline from a single low-NDVI image.</p><h3>Sampling critique</h3><p>Five accessible plots may not represent the full region. Use a stratified design across forest types, structure and environmental gradients, with spatially independent validation.</p><h3>Structural critique</h3><p>QSM biomass depends on occlusion, registration, branch reconstruction and wood density. Propagate these uncertainties before regional mapping.</p><h3>Spectral critique</h3><p>NDVI is affected by multiple factors and may saturate in dense vegetation. Low NDVI alone cannot diagnose chlorophyll decline; illumination, soil, structure and phenology must be considered.</p><h3>Better workflow</h3><p>Calibrate structure-to-satellite relationships with matched footprints and dates, validate on withheld sites, use RTM sensitivity analysis to distinguish plausible optical drivers, and report uncertainty and transfer limits.</p>", "text": "Original supplementary exercise based on both lecture halves Prompt A team scans five accessible forest plots with TLS, estimates woody biomass using QSMs, extrapolates to a whole region using satellite NDVI, then claims the same model can identify chlorophyll decline from a single low-NDVI image. Sampling critique Five accessible plots may not represent the full region. Use a stratified design across forest types, structure and environmental gradients, with spatially independent validation. Structural critique QSM biomass depends on occlusion, registration, branch reconstruction and wood density. Propagate these uncertainties before regional mapping. Spectral critique NDVI is affected by multiple factors and may saturate in dense vegetation. Low NDVI alone cannot diagnose chlorophyll decline; illumination, soil, structure and phenology must be considered. Better workflow Calibrate structure-to-satellite relationships with matched footprints and dates, validate on withheld sites, use RTM sensitivity analysis to distinguish plausible optical drivers, and report uncertainty and transfer limits."}, {"title": "16. Revision questions and concise answers", "html": "<p>Supplementary exam preparation</p><h3>Q1</h3><p>Why is TLS called active? It emits its own laser pulse/beam rather than relying only on sunlight.</p><h3>Q2</h3><p>What is occlusion? Unobserved structure hidden behind foreground surfaces from a given scan position.</p><h3>Q3</h3><p>Why are multiple scans not a perfect fix? Registration error and residual hidden surfaces remain.</p><h3>Q4</h3><p>Does LiDAR measure biomass directly? No; biomass is estimated through geometry and density/allometric assumptions.</p><h3>Q5</h3><p>What is gap fraction? Fraction of specified sight lines through canopy without interception.</p><h3>Q6</h3><p>Forward versus inverse RTM? Forward predicts signal from scene parameters; inverse infers parameters from signal.</p><h3>Q7</h3><p>What is extinction? Removal from a beam through absorption and scattering.</p><h3>Q8</h3><p>Why use LiDAR in a 3D RTM? It constrains scene geometry and photon path interactions.</p><h3>Q9</h3><p>Why not trust the most complex RTM automatically? It can be poorly parameterised and hard to validate.</p><h3>Q10</h3><p>What should a defensible final product report? Sensor/preprocessing, scale, assumptions, independent validation, uncertainty and limits of applicability.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exam preparation Q1 Why is TLS called active? It emits its own laser pulse/beam rather than relying only on sunlight. Q2 What is occlusion? Unobserved structure hidden behind foreground surfaces from a given scan position. Q3 Why are multiple scans not a perfect fix? Registration error and residual hidden surfaces remain. Q4 Does LiDAR measure biomass directly? No; biomass is estimated through geometry and density/allometric assumptions. Q5 What is gap fraction? Fraction of specified sight lines through canopy without interception. Q6 Forward versus inverse RTM? Forward predicts signal from scene parameters; inverse infers parameters from signal. Q7 What is extinction? Removal from a beam through absorption and scattering. Q8 Why use LiDAR in a 3D RTM? It constrains scene geometry and photon path interactions. Q9 Why not trust the most complex RTM automatically? It can be poorly parameterised and hard to validate. Q10 What should a defensible final product report? Sensor/preprocessing, scale, assumptions, independent validation, uncertainty and limits of applicability."}], "text": ["1. Lecture structure and what is actually recorded Transcript 00:04:10–00:04:48; LiDAR slides 1–2; RTM slides 1–5 Two connected halves Will Woodgate introduces Week 8, Lecture 7 as a two-part session. The first part is a recorded guest lecture by Professor Kim Calders on terrestrial LiDAR. Woodgate then presents radiative transfer applications for vegetation. The two topics connect through forest structure: LiDAR describes geometry and RTMs explain how photons interact with that geometry. Three distinct scientific products A point cloud gives sampled 3D surface locations. A structural product estimates attributes such as tree dimensions or canopy gaps. A radiative-transfer simulation predicts the electromagnetic response of a scene under specified geometry and material properties. These are not interchangeable. Central exam question Can you defend each assumption between sensor observations, physical structure, modelled reflectance and the ecological quantity being inferred?", "2. Why measure forests in 3D? LiDAR slides 3–13; recorded guest introduction around 00:08–00:12 The measurement gap The slides contrast broad spatial coverage of satellite remote sensing with detailed but local in-situ measurements. A 2D optical image can show canopy colour and texture but may not directly resolve trunk diameter, branch architecture or canopy gaps. Structural variables Forest monitoring may need stem diameter, height, crown geometry, volume, woody biomass, vertical foliage distribution and structural change. Each requires a defined observation model, not just a visually convincing 3D rendering. Sampling and representativeness A detailed LiDAR plot does not automatically represent a region. Site selection, species composition, slope and stand age affect how reliably plot relationships can be extrapolated. Critical distinction LiDAR measures laser returns and distances. Biomass is a derived quantity, typically requiring geometric reconstruction, wood density and biological assumptions.", "3. How terrestrial LiDAR works LiDAR slides 14–20 Active ranging Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) is an active sensor: the instrument emits laser energy and measures the return. In time-of-flight ranging, distance is proportional to travel time, d = cΔt/2. The factor of two accounts for the outgoing and returning path. Original calculation For a round-trip time of 20 nanoseconds, d ≈ (3×10^8 m/s × 20×10^-9 s)/2 = 3 m. Real instruments also require timing calibration and beam-direction knowledge. From ranges to XYZ Combine distance with scanner azimuth/elevation and instrument position/orientation to locate points in 3D. A point cloud is a sample of visible surfaces, not a complete solid reconstruction. Sensor configuration Different terrestrial scanners vary in beam divergence, wavelength, ranging technology, field of view and scan density. These properties affect point sampling and material interaction.", "4. Occlusion, scan design and registration LiDAR slides 20–23, 29–31 Occlusion Branches, leaves and trunks block lines of sight. A single scan may miss the far side of a tree or internal canopy; a sparse point cloud can therefore reflect visibility rather than actual absence of material. Multiple scan positions Multiple viewpoints can reduce occlusion but require point-cloud registration. Targets, geometric features or control networks can align scans to a shared coordinate system. Registration uncertainty Even a small rotational misalignment can displace distant points. Check residuals, overlap consistency and independent survey control. A tight visual fit is not proof of correct absolute coordinates. Practical trade-off More scans improve completeness but increase field time, storage, processing and opportunities for registration error. Choose scan density based on the structural metric and plot complexity.", "5. From point clouds to woody biomass LiDAR slides 24–36 Quantitative structure models The guest slides discuss detailed tree-structure reconstruction. A quantitative structure model (QSM) approximates woody stems and branches with geometric primitives such as cylinders, enabling estimates of woody volume. Biomass chain An illustrative chain is point cloud → stem/branch segmentation → fitted geometry → woody volume → density-weighted dry mass. Wood density may vary by species, tissue and tree; assumptions should be documented. Why allometry matters Traditional biomass estimates often use equations relating diameter, height and wood density to biomass. QSMs offer a more explicit geometry-based approach but are sensitive to missing branches and segmentation choices. Validation Compare modelled dimensions or biomass against independent reference measurements when feasible. Report systematic errors for small branches, large stems and occluded canopy regions. Exam trap A LiDAR-derived biomass map is not a direct laser measurement of carbon. Conversion to carbon requires further assumptions about biomass composition.", "6. Urban structure and virtual forests LiDAR slides 37–46 Beyond forestry The slides extend terrestrial LiDAR to urban vegetation and 3D modelling, including combinations of terrestrial and airborne LiDAR. Each platform sees different parts of the canopy and ground. Complementary perspectives Airborne scanning tends to observe upper canopy and broad terrain; terrestrial scanning often resolves trunks and lower canopy. Merging them can improve structural completeness but requires consistent registration and sampling models. Virtual forests 3D scene models can be used as inputs to environmental simulations. Their usefulness depends on whether geometry, leaf distribution and material properties are realistic at the scale of the model. Connection to RTM A structurally plausible virtual forest provides a more realistic scene for simulating illumination, shadowing, scattering and sensor-view effects than a simple homogeneous canopy.", "7. Gap fraction and canopy phenology LiDAR slides 47–56; transcript around 00:10–00:11 Definition Gap fraction is the proportion of viewing directions or rays through a canopy that encounter no obstructing vegetation over a defined angular sector. It varies with direction, leaf condition and scan geometry. Link to leaf area Under simplified random-leaf assumptions, canopy gap probability can be represented using an exponential attenuation relationship. Inverting it can estimate plant area or leaf area, but woody elements and nonrandom clumping complicate interpretation. Seasonal monitoring The slides discuss repeated scans to monitor spring phenology. Changes in gap fraction may reflect leaf emergence, but also instrument position, registration and acquisition conditions. Why leaf area is not automatic A laser beam may hit a branch rather than a leaf, and occlusion affects which components are sampled. Leaf area retrieval needs explicit treatment of wood, clumping and angular effects.", "8. Future directions: multi-platform LiDAR and GEDI LiDAR slides 57–70 Combining platforms The guest slides discuss combining complementary point clouds to reduce occlusion. This requires robust cross-platform alignment and understanding of different sampling densities. GEDI The slides introduce the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI), a spaceborne lidar mission that samples vegetation vertical structure. Spaceborne footprints provide broad geographic coverage but do not constitute a wall-to-wall high-resolution TLS map. Scale-up problem Linking plot-scale TLS, airborne lidar and satellite footprints requires matching spatial support, ecological conditions and uncertainty. Extrapolation beyond sampled environments is a model, not a measurement. Exam defence Explain what each platform measures, which canopy components it misses, and how independent reference data would test regional estimates.", "9. Radiative transfer: the forward problem RTM slides 3–6, 21–29; transcript 01:09:55–01:10:15 Definition Woodgate frames radiative transfer as understanding photons of electromagnetic radiation and what a sensor measures after those photons interact with matter. A forward RTM predicts the sensor signal given source, geometry, medium and material properties. Components Specify the radiation source, atmospheric pathway, target scene, sensor viewing geometry, wavelength-dependent absorption/scattering and boundary conditions. Forward versus inverse Forward: given leaf chlorophyll, canopy structure and sun/view angles, simulate reflectance. Inverse: given measured reflectance, estimate chlorophyll or structure. Inversion may be ambiguous because multiple scenes can produce similar spectra. Why models matter RTMs help interpret physical causes of spectral variation, generate synthetic datasets and test sensor configurations; they do not automatically eliminate calibration or validation requirements.", "10. Scattering, absorption, transmission and extinction RTM slides 12–20; transcript around 01:17–01:28 Energy pathways Incident radiation can be reflected/scattered, absorbed or transmitted. The relative amounts depend on wavelength, material and geometry. The slides distinguish scattering from absorption and introduce extinction as removal of energy from a particular beam. Beer–Lambert intuition For a simple homogeneous medium, transmitted intensity I = I0 exp(−τ), where τ is optical depth. This is a model of beam attenuation, not a universal canopy inversion formula. Original calculation For optical depth τ = 0.8, transmittance exp(−0.8) ≈ 0.449. Approximately 44.9% of the initial beam remains in the direct path; the rest is removed from that beam by extinction, which can include scattering and absorption. Why sky blue? The slide asks why the sky is blue. Shorter visible wavelengths are preferentially scattered by small atmospheric molecules (Rayleigh scattering). The question illustrates wavelength-dependent interaction, not forest-canopy physics alone. Limits Multiple scattering, heterogeneous media, directional leaf distributions and atmospheric layering require more complex modelling than a single exponential expression.", "11. Leaf optical properties and canopy structure RTM slides 30–36 Leaf-level effects Leaf pigments, water and dry matter influence absorption and scattering at different wavelengths. The deck includes simulated chlorophyll spectral responses. Canopy-level effects Observed canopy reflectance also depends on leaf area, orientation, clumping, crown geometry, shadowing, soil background and illumination/viewing directions. Equifinality A darker canopy signal could arise from higher pigment absorption, more leaf area, more shadow or different soil exposure. A single band or index may not distinguish these causes. Retrieval strategy Use multiple informative wavelengths, structural constraints and independent observations where possible. Document which parameters are fixed, estimated or assumed.", "12. RTM model families: simple to 3D RTM slides 37–43 One-dimensional homogeneous models These represent vegetation as horizontally uniform layers. They are computationally efficient and useful when the canopy approximates their assumptions. Heterogeneous and 3D models Spatially explicit models represent individual crowns, gaps and other scene components. They better accommodate complex shadowing and directional effects, at greater computational and parameter cost. Trade-off The most complex model is not automatically best. Choose a model whose scene representation matches the question, spatial scale, available input data and validation resources. Physical consistency A realistic-looking 3D image is not sufficient. Leaf optical properties, geometry, atmosphere and sensor spectral response must be credible.", "13. Connecting LiDAR to radiative transfer RTM slides 44–50; transcript 01:47:35–01:47:51, 01:52:30–01:52:39 Lecture connection Woodgate explains that LiDAR provides measured 3D geometry for ray-tracing models. Known sensor position, solar direction and scene geometry permit explicit simulation of photon pathways. Voxel representation The lecture describes voxels as 3D cubes containing information such as vegetation density. Voxelisation reduces complex point clouds to a model representation; voxel size controls detail and computation. What LiDAR cannot directly provide LiDAR geometry does not automatically reveal leaf reflectance, chlorophyll or biochemical composition. RTMs still require wavelength-dependent optical properties and structural assumptions. Validation Test both the reconstructed geometry and the simulated optical signal. A correct geometry model can still yield wrong reflectance if material properties or atmosphere are misrepresented.", "14. DART and simulation applications RTM slides 47–52; transcript around 01:54:13–01:54:32 Source model Woodgate mentions DART (Discrete Anisotropic Radiative Transfer) as a model that can simulate passive optical and LiDAR observations for complex scenes, including urban environments. Experimental design Use RTMs to vary one parameter while holding others fixed: leaf area, chlorophyll, solar angle, canopy clumping or sensor view. Examine which spectral changes are physically attributable to that parameter. Synthetic data warning A model trained on simulated scenes can inherit assumptions and miss real-world variation. Independent real measurements remain necessary to assess domain transfer. Exam question Why combine TLS with DART? Because TLS can constrain scene structure while DART simulates radiation interactions, enabling physically interpretable tests of sensor response.", "15. Integrated exam scenario: biomass and canopy health Original supplementary exercise based on both lecture halves Prompt A team scans five accessible forest plots with TLS, estimates woody biomass using QSMs, extrapolates to a whole region using satellite NDVI, then claims the same model can identify chlorophyll decline from a single low-NDVI image. Sampling critique Five accessible plots may not represent the full region. Use a stratified design across forest types, structure and environmental gradients, with spatially independent validation. Structural critique QSM biomass depends on occlusion, registration, branch reconstruction and wood density. Propagate these uncertainties before regional mapping. Spectral critique NDVI is affected by multiple factors and may saturate in dense vegetation. Low NDVI alone cannot diagnose chlorophyll decline; illumination, soil, structure and phenology must be considered. Better workflow Calibrate structure-to-satellite relationships with matched footprints and dates, validate on withheld sites, use RTM sensitivity analysis to distinguish plausible optical drivers, and report uncertainty and transfer limits.", "16. Revision questions and concise answers Supplementary exam preparation Q1 Why is TLS called active? It emits its own laser pulse/beam rather than relying only on sunlight. Q2 What is occlusion? Unobserved structure hidden behind foreground surfaces from a given scan position. Q3 Why are multiple scans not a perfect fix? Registration error and residual hidden surfaces remain. Q4 Does LiDAR measure biomass directly? No; biomass is estimated through geometry and density/allometric assumptions. Q5 What is gap fraction? Fraction of specified sight lines through canopy without interception. Q6 Forward versus inverse RTM? Forward predicts signal from scene parameters; inverse infers parameters from signal. Q7 What is extinction? Removal from a beam through absorption and scattering. Q8 Why use LiDAR in a 3D RTM? It constrains scene geometry and photon path interactions. Q9 Why not trust the most complex RTM automatically? It can be poorly parameterised and hard to validate. Q10 What should a defensible final product report? Sensor/preprocessing, scale, assumptions, independent validation, uncertainty and limits of applicability."]}, {"id": 8, "title": "Aquatic Remote Sensing & Allen Coral Atlas", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week09_Aquatic_AllenCoralAtlas.pdf", "pages": 8, "sections": [{"title": "1. Lecture framing and the marine observation problem", "html": "<p>Slides 1–8; VTT ~00:17–00:25</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Chris Roelfsema delivers Lecture 8 during calendar Week 9 (the public holiday shifted numbering). The lecture moves from marine optical physics to field validation, marine corrections, coral reef mapping, the Allen Coral Atlas, and bleaching applications. The core question is not &#x27;Which satellite image looks best?&#x27; but &#x27;Which environmental quantity is physically observable under these water, atmosphere and spatial conditions?&#x27;</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Before selecting imagery, specify whether the goal is to map reef geomorphology, benthic habitat, seagrass, water quality or thermal stress. These differ in target, depth dependence, spectral requirements, reference protocol and validation. Optical imagery may observe the benthos only in sufficiently shallow, clear water; a satellite can observe surface temperature without seeing the seabed.</p>", "text": "Slides 1–8; VTT ~00:17–00:25 Lecture material and interpretation Chris Roelfsema delivers Lecture 8 during calendar Week 9 (the public holiday shifted numbering). The lecture moves from marine optical physics to field validation, marine corrections, coral reef mapping, the Allen Coral Atlas, and bleaching applications. The core question is not 'Which satellite image looks best?' but 'Which environmental quantity is physically observable under these water, atmosphere and spatial conditions?' Why it matters / exam application Before selecting imagery, specify whether the goal is to map reef geomorphology, benthic habitat, seagrass, water quality or thermal stress. These differ in target, depth dependence, spectral requirements, reference protocol and validation. Optical imagery may observe the benthos only in sufficiently shallow, clear water; a satellite can observe surface temperature without seeing the seabed."}, {"title": "2. Passive and active sensing over water", "html": "<p>Slides 7–9; VTT ~00:23–00:27</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The lecture distinguishes passive observation of reflected sunlight from active approaches. A passive optical sensor receives a mixture of atmospheric, water-surface, water-column and bottom signals. The mixture varies with wavelength, depth, turbidity, viewing geometry and illumination.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Passive visible-band remote sensing is often suitable for shallow benthic mapping; thermal sensors observe sea-surface thermal emission rather than bottom reflectance; active acoustic and laser methods provide different depth/structure information. No single instrument directly measures all reef properties. A defensible answer states what is observed and what is inferred.</p>", "text": "Slides 7–9; VTT ~00:23–00:27 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture distinguishes passive observation of reflected sunlight from active approaches. A passive optical sensor receives a mixture of atmospheric, water-surface, water-column and bottom signals. The mixture varies with wavelength, depth, turbidity, viewing geometry and illumination. Why it matters / exam application Passive visible-band remote sensing is often suitable for shallow benthic mapping; thermal sensors observe sea-surface thermal emission rather than bottom reflectance; active acoustic and laser methods provide different depth/structure information. No single instrument directly measures all reef properties. A defensible answer states what is observed and what is inferred."}, {"title": "3. Why water changes the spectrum", "html": "<p>Slides 9–16; VTT ~00:24–00:35</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Water absorbs and scatters electromagnetic radiation. The lecturer discusses how organic and inorganic material changes the water-column signal and why colours diminish differently with depth. Slides show spectral signatures of water types and a seagrass depth-effect example.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>The observed radiance is not simply the seagrass or coral reflectance. As depth increases, the bottom contribution is attenuated along the downward and upward light paths. Blue-green light often penetrates clearer water more effectively than red/NIR, while suspended sediment and dissolved organic matter change this pattern. Consequently, the same seagrass meadow can appear spectrally different at different depths.</p>", "text": "Slides 9–16; VTT ~00:24–00:35 Lecture material and interpretation Water absorbs and scatters electromagnetic radiation. The lecturer discusses how organic and inorganic material changes the water-column signal and why colours diminish differently with depth. Slides show spectral signatures of water types and a seagrass depth-effect example. Why it matters / exam application The observed radiance is not simply the seagrass or coral reflectance. As depth increases, the bottom contribution is attenuated along the downward and upward light paths. Blue-green light often penetrates clearer water more effectively than red/NIR, while suspended sediment and dissolved organic matter change this pattern. Consequently, the same seagrass meadow can appear spectrally different at different depths."}, {"title": "4. A conceptual water-column model", "html": "<p>Slides 11–16; supplementary equation</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The slide sequence illustrates absorption and depth dependence but does not prescribe one universal inversion formula. For intuition, a simplified bottom contribution can be written B(λ,z)≈R_bottom(λ)exp[−2K(λ)z], where K is an effective attenuation coefficient and z is depth. This is a teaching approximation, not a verbatim slide formula or complete radiative-transfer solution.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>For example, if K=0.15 m⁻¹ and z=5 m, attenuation factor exp(−1.5)=0.223. A bottom signal of 0.20 would contribute only ~0.045 in these simplified units before other water/atmospheric terms. The two-way optical path is why depth is a serious confounder. The approximation ignores variable sun/view angles, multiple scattering and wavelength-dependent water constituents.</p>", "text": "Slides 11–16; supplementary equation Lecture material and interpretation The slide sequence illustrates absorption and depth dependence but does not prescribe one universal inversion formula. For intuition, a simplified bottom contribution can be written B(λ,z)≈R_bottom(λ)exp[−2K(λ)z], where K is an effective attenuation coefficient and z is depth. This is a teaching approximation, not a verbatim slide formula or complete radiative-transfer solution. Why it matters / exam application For example, if K=0.15 m⁻¹ and z=5 m, attenuation factor exp(−1.5)=0.223. A bottom signal of 0.20 would contribute only ~0.045 in these simplified units before other water/atmospheric terms. The two-way optical path is why depth is a serious confounder. The approximation ignores variable sun/view angles, multiple scattering and wavelength-dependent water constituents."}, {"title": "5. Depth versus benthic class: an identifiability problem", "html": "<p>Slides 13–17</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>A dark pixel could indicate deep water, a dark benthic substrate, suspended material or a shadow. A brighter pixel could indicate shallow sand, surface glint or a different water-column condition. The lecture&#x27;s spectral-library and depth-modelling slides show why bottom-type interpretation requires more than matching raw spectral curves.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>To separate bottom type from depth, consider bathymetry, water-column correction, multiple bands, appropriate depth ranges, and reference observations. A classification model trained in shallow water may label deeper seagrass as another class because attenuation changes the feature distribution. Examine accuracy by depth and turbidity, not only overall accuracy.</p>", "text": "Slides 13–17 Lecture material and interpretation A dark pixel could indicate deep water, a dark benthic substrate, suspended material or a shadow. A brighter pixel could indicate shallow sand, surface glint or a different water-column condition. The lecture's spectral-library and depth-modelling slides show why bottom-type interpretation requires more than matching raw spectral curves. Why it matters / exam application To separate bottom type from depth, consider bathymetry, water-column correction, multiple bands, appropriate depth ranges, and reference observations. A classification model trained in shallow water may label deeper seagrass as another class because attenuation changes the feature distribution. Examine accuracy by depth and turbidity, not only overall accuracy."}, {"title": "6. Field data: photo quadrats and geolocation", "html": "<p>Slides 18–23; VTT ~00:35–00:43</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The deck describes geolocated 1 m² photo quadrats and photo interpretation/automation. Overlapping imagery and structure-from-motion photogrammetry can support detailed reef observations. Field data anchor satellite labels to independently observed ecological conditions.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A good quadrat protocol documents location, date/time, depth, tide, image orientation, footprint and class definition. A GPS coordinate recorded at the surface can be offset from an underwater quadrat due to drift or positioning method. A 1 m² photo is not automatically representative of a 5 m satellite pixel (25 m²). Use appropriate aggregation or sample design, and quantify uncertainty.</p>", "text": "Slides 18–23; VTT ~00:35–00:43 Lecture material and interpretation The deck describes geolocated 1 m² photo quadrats and photo interpretation/automation. Overlapping imagery and structure-from-motion photogrammetry can support detailed reef observations. Field data anchor satellite labels to independently observed ecological conditions. Why it matters / exam application A good quadrat protocol documents location, date/time, depth, tide, image orientation, footprint and class definition. A GPS coordinate recorded at the surface can be offset from an underwater quadrat due to drift or positioning method. A 1 m² photo is not automatically representative of a 5 m satellite pixel (25 m²). Use appropriate aggregation or sample design, and quantify uncertainty."}, {"title": "7. Machine learning for photo annotation", "html": "<p>Slides 19–23, 79</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Slides discuss machine learning to analyse field photos and later show an automated photo-analysis process after model training. Automation accelerates annotation but inherits training-label ambiguity and image-quality limitations.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A coral-cover model should be tested on photos from different reefs, depths, lighting and cameras. Validate the annotation model independently before treating its outputs as ground truth for satellite validation. If an automated photo model is wrong and then used as &#x27;reference&#x27;, the satellite model may be unfairly penalised or rewarded.</p>", "text": "Slides 19–23, 79 Lecture material and interpretation Slides discuss machine learning to analyse field photos and later show an automated photo-analysis process after model training. Automation accelerates annotation but inherits training-label ambiguity and image-quality limitations. Why it matters / exam application A coral-cover model should be tested on photos from different reefs, depths, lighting and cameras. Validate the annotation model independently before treating its outputs as ground truth for satellite validation. If an automated photo model is wrong and then used as 'reference', the satellite model may be unfairly penalised or rewarded."}, {"title": "8. Corrections specific to marine imagery", "html": "<p>Slides 24–35; VTT ~00:38–00:49</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The lecture dedicates a full section to marine image corrections. Atmospheric path radiance, cloud/shadow, sun glint, water-column attenuation, waves and geometric differences can all affect apparent benthic reflectance. The instructor discusses practical visual quality review.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A robust workflow separates atmospheric correction, glint mitigation, water-column/depth treatment and quality masking. These are not interchangeable: correcting atmosphere does not remove bottom-depth effects; glint removal does not make turbid water transparent. Inspect residual artefacts against field observations and avoid treating every correction as universally beneficial.</p>", "text": "Slides 24–35; VTT ~00:38–00:49 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture dedicates a full section to marine image corrections. Atmospheric path radiance, cloud/shadow, sun glint, water-column attenuation, waves and geometric differences can all affect apparent benthic reflectance. The instructor discusses practical visual quality review. Why it matters / exam application A robust workflow separates atmospheric correction, glint mitigation, water-column/depth treatment and quality masking. These are not interchangeable: correcting atmosphere does not remove bottom-depth effects; glint removal does not make turbid water transparent. Inspect residual artefacts against field observations and avoid treating every correction as universally beneficial."}, {"title": "9. Sun glint: mechanism and mitigation", "html": "<p>Slides 25–35; VTT mentions glint ~00:34–00:49</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Specular reflection from the air–water interface adds a strong surface component. Wave facets can reflect direct sunlight toward the sensor, creating bright streaks and obscuring bottom features. Glint is affected by wind, sun/view geometry and surface roughness.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Possible responses include selecting low-glint scenes, masking severe glint and using a justified glint-correction method. Near-infrared bands may help estimate glint in optically deep water, but assumptions can fail in shallow/turbid settings. A correction that subtracts too much can remove real shallow-water signal; inspect corrected spectra and residual spatial patterns.</p>", "text": "Slides 25–35; VTT mentions glint ~00:34–00:49 Lecture material and interpretation Specular reflection from the air–water interface adds a strong surface component. Wave facets can reflect direct sunlight toward the sensor, creating bright streaks and obscuring bottom features. Glint is affected by wind, sun/view geometry and surface roughness. Why it matters / exam application Possible responses include selecting low-glint scenes, masking severe glint and using a justified glint-correction method. Near-infrared bands may help estimate glint in optically deep water, but assumptions can fail in shallow/turbid settings. A correction that subtracts too much can remove real shallow-water signal; inspect corrected spectra and residual spatial patterns."}, {"title": "10. Geometric correction and tidal conditions", "html": "<p>Slides 24–35; supplementary application</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Coastal maps are particularly sensitive to registration errors at narrow reef edges, seagrass boundaries and intertidal areas. Water level changes alter which bottom features are exposed and the optical path length to submerged surfaces.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Compare images at suitable tidal states where possible, or model depth/water level explicitly. A 2 m misregistration along a narrow seagrass fringe can create a false change signal. Tidal effects are especially important for intertidal reef flats and shallow banks; document acquisition time, tide and datum.</p>", "text": "Slides 24–35; supplementary application Lecture material and interpretation Coastal maps are particularly sensitive to registration errors at narrow reef edges, seagrass boundaries and intertidal areas. Water level changes alter which bottom features are exposed and the optical path length to submerged surfaces. Why it matters / exam application Compare images at suitable tidal states where possible, or model depth/water level explicitly. A 2 m misregistration along a narrow seagrass fringe can create a false change signal. Tidal effects are especially important for intertidal reef flats and shallow banks; document acquisition time, tide and datum."}, {"title": "11. What is a coral reef and what can be mapped?", "html": "<p>Slides 36–47; VTT ~00:49–01:05</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The lecture introduces coral ecology, reef geomorphology and the value of habitat maps for conservation. Coral habitats are structured by light, temperature, substrate, wave energy and water chemistry. The slides show reef forms and habitats and why maps need consistent ecological definitions.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Distinguish geomorphic zones (e.g., reef crest, flat, slope, lagoon) from benthic cover (e.g., coral, algae, sand, rubble). A geomorphic map describes reef form; a benthic map describes substrate/community composition. A reef-crest polygon is not equivalent to &#x27;100% live coral&#x27;. Confusing these outputs is a common interpretation error.</p>", "text": "Slides 36–47; VTT ~00:49–01:05 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture introduces coral ecology, reef geomorphology and the value of habitat maps for conservation. Coral habitats are structured by light, temperature, substrate, wave energy and water chemistry. The slides show reef forms and habitats and why maps need consistent ecological definitions. Why it matters / exam application Distinguish geomorphic zones (e.g., reef crest, flat, slope, lagoon) from benthic cover (e.g., coral, algae, sand, rubble). A geomorphic map describes reef form; a benthic map describes substrate/community composition. A reef-crest polygon is not equivalent to '100% live coral'. Confusing these outputs is a common interpretation error."}, {"title": "12. Why global reef mapping is difficult", "html": "<p>Slides 48–58; VTT ~01:05 onward</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The Allen Coral Atlas section asks how to map reefs globally. Slides describe very large volumes of Planet Dove imagery, global geomorphic and benthic categories, extensive field datasets and mapping consistency. Slide 57 reports global map accuracy in the 65–85% range and depth-domain statements for geomorphic and benthic mapping.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A global system must trade local detail for transferable classes, consistent preprocessing, scalable computation and repeatable QA. A single model trained on one reef may fail in another due to water colour, reef shape, season or image quality. &#x27;Globally consistent&#x27; means a common framework, not identical confidence at every reef.</p>", "text": "Slides 48–58; VTT ~01:05 onward Lecture material and interpretation The Allen Coral Atlas section asks how to map reefs globally. Slides describe very large volumes of Planet Dove imagery, global geomorphic and benthic categories, extensive field datasets and mapping consistency. Slide 57 reports global map accuracy in the 65–85% range and depth-domain statements for geomorphic and benthic mapping. Why it matters / exam application A global system must trade local detail for transferable classes, consistent preprocessing, scalable computation and repeatable QA. A single model trained on one reef may fail in another due to water colour, reef shape, season or image quality. 'Globally consistent' means a common framework, not identical confidence at every reef."}, {"title": "13. Allen Coral Atlas inputs and preprocessing", "html": "<p>Slides 51–73</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The deck outlines low-tide Dove mosaics, depth composites, reef/scene masks, mapping regions, image mosaicking and quality review. Slide 69 identifies 5 m daily Planet Dove imagery. The workflow uses Google Earth Engine and region-based processing rather than manually digitising every reef.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Why low tide? In suitable conditions it reduces water-column attenuation and improves visibility of shallow structures, though exposure, wet surfaces and tide-specific class interpretation need care. Mosaicking multiple images reduces cloud/glint gaps but can introduce seams and differing radiometry. Review image quality before classification.</p>", "text": "Slides 51–73 Lecture material and interpretation The deck outlines low-tide Dove mosaics, depth composites, reef/scene masks, mapping regions, image mosaicking and quality review. Slide 69 identifies 5 m daily Planet Dove imagery. The workflow uses Google Earth Engine and region-based processing rather than manually digitising every reef. Why it matters / exam application Why low tide? In suitable conditions it reduces water-column attenuation and improves visibility of shallow structures, though exposure, wet surfaces and tide-specific class interpretation need care. Mosaicking multiple images reduces cloud/glint gaps but can introduce seams and differing radiometry. Review image quality before classification."}, {"title": "14. Geomorphic classification: physical covariates", "html": "<p>Slides 61–68, 88–92</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The workflow combines image information with depth, slope, wave exposure and spatial relationships. The slides show object-based rules and random forest classification for geomorphic zones, followed by smoothing, masking and cleanup.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A reef crest is defined partly by its position and morphology, not just a spectral signature. Two zones may have similar colours but different slope, depth or neighbourhood. Object-based rules help enforce geomorphic plausibility. However, incorrect bathymetry or overly rigid rules can systematically misclassify unusual reef forms.</p>", "text": "Slides 61–68, 88–92 Lecture material and interpretation The workflow combines image information with depth, slope, wave exposure and spatial relationships. The slides show object-based rules and random forest classification for geomorphic zones, followed by smoothing, masking and cleanup. Why it matters / exam application A reef crest is defined partly by its position and morphology, not just a spectral signature. Two zones may have similar colours but different slope, depth or neighbourhood. Object-based rules help enforce geomorphic plausibility. However, incorrect bathymetry or overly rigid rules can systematically misclassify unusual reef forms."}, {"title": "15. Benthic classification: spectra, context and classes", "html": "<p>Slides 63–68, 89–94</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The deck separates geomorphic and benthic mapping and shows random forest plus cleanup for benthic outputs. Benthic classes are inferred from corrected optical imagery, reference data and contextual information.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Benthic classes can be spectrally confused because of mixed cover, depth and turbidity. A 5 m pixel may contain coral, algae and sand; assigning a single dominant label loses fractional composition. Use class definitions that correspond to observable mixtures, validate with geolocated quadrats and interpret class probabilities cautiously.</p>", "text": "Slides 63–68, 89–94 Lecture material and interpretation The deck separates geomorphic and benthic mapping and shows random forest plus cleanup for benthic outputs. Benthic classes are inferred from corrected optical imagery, reference data and contextual information. Why it matters / exam application Benthic classes can be spectrally confused because of mixed cover, depth and turbidity. A 5 m pixel may contain coral, algae and sand; assigning a single dominant label loses fractional composition. Use class definitions that correspond to observable mixtures, validate with geolocated quadrats and interpret class probabilities cautiously."}, {"title": "16. Reference data assembly at global scale", "html": "<p>Slides 74–87</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The slide sequence describes collecting existing field observations and maps from agencies, NGOs, scientists and citizen science; storing/reviewing them in GIS web maps; creating reference quadrats; segmenting images and manually labelling geomorphic/benthic categories. Slide 82 gives example regional reference-quadrat counts.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Reference data vary in age, quality, protocol, position and class vocabulary. Before pooling, harmonise definitions and assign confidence. The deck explicitly illustrates manual segment labelling and expert knowledge; reference sampling is not simply an automatic export from a field database.</p>", "text": "Slides 74–87 Lecture material and interpretation The slide sequence describes collecting existing field observations and maps from agencies, NGOs, scientists and citizen science; storing/reviewing them in GIS web maps; creating reference quadrats; segmenting images and manually labelling geomorphic/benthic categories. Slide 82 gives example regional reference-quadrat counts. Why it matters / exam application Reference data vary in age, quality, protocol, position and class vocabulary. Before pooling, harmonise definitions and assign confidence. The deck explicitly illustrates manual segment labelling and expert knowledge; reference sampling is not simply an automatic export from a field database."}, {"title": "17. Random forest and object-based workflow", "html": "<p>Slides 84–94</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Slides show eCognition-based reference segments and Google Earth Engine modules for classification. Random forest learns nonlinear relationships between class labels and predictors; object-based analysis uses spatially coherent units and contextual rules.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Why not just threshold one band? Reef classes often overlap spectrally and require multiple features. Why not assume RF is correct? Training data may be geographically biased, and a model can overfit regional conditions. Use independent regional tests, class-level errors and inspection of misclassified locations.</p>", "text": "Slides 84–94 Lecture material and interpretation Slides show eCognition-based reference segments and Google Earth Engine modules for classification. Random forest learns nonlinear relationships between class labels and predictors; object-based analysis uses spatially coherent units and contextual rules. Why it matters / exam application Why not just threshold one band? Reef classes often overlap spectrally and require multiple features. Why not assume RF is correct? Training data may be geographically biased, and a model can overfit regional conditions. Use independent regional tests, class-level errors and inspection of misclassified locations."}, {"title": "18. Validation and map quality control", "html": "<p>Slides 95–102</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The lecture explicitly asks what makes a map &#x27;good&#x27;. The Atlas workflow includes overall accuracy, user&#x27;s and producer&#x27;s class accuracies, error matrices, validation, review, feedback and delivery. Slides also distinguish reference-data confidence levels.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Overall accuracy hides rare-class problems. For a coral class, user&#x27;s accuracy answers &#x27;How often is mapped coral actually coral?&#x27; while producer&#x27;s accuracy answers &#x27;How much reference coral did the map capture?&#x27; A credible product should communicate spatial variation in data quality and reference confidence, not just one global accuracy figure.</p>", "text": "Slides 95–102 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture explicitly asks what makes a map 'good'. The Atlas workflow includes overall accuracy, user's and producer's class accuracies, error matrices, validation, review, feedback and delivery. Slides also distinguish reference-data confidence levels. Why it matters / exam application Overall accuracy hides rare-class problems. For a coral class, user's accuracy answers 'How often is mapped coral actually coral?' while producer's accuracy answers 'How much reference coral did the map capture?' A credible product should communicate spatial variation in data quality and reference confidence, not just one global accuracy figure."}, {"title": "19. Worked confusion-matrix example", "html": "<p>Slides 96–100; original calculation</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Suppose 100 independent reference points yield: mapped coral/reference coral=35, mapped coral/reference other=15, mapped other/reference coral=5, mapped other/reference other=45. Rows are mapped classes; columns are reference.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Overall accuracy=(35+45)/100=80%. Coral user&#x27;s accuracy=35/(35+15)=70%; coral producer&#x27;s accuracy=35/(35+5)=87.5%. Thus 30% of mapped coral points are commission errors while 12.5% of true coral points are omitted. This illustrative matrix is not an Allen Coral Atlas result. If samples were disproportionately stratified, area-weighted estimates may be needed.</p>", "text": "Slides 96–100; original calculation Lecture material and interpretation Suppose 100 independent reference points yield: mapped coral/reference coral=35, mapped coral/reference other=15, mapped other/reference coral=5, mapped other/reference other=45. Rows are mapped classes; columns are reference. Why it matters / exam application Overall accuracy=(35+45)/100=80%. Coral user's accuracy=35/(35+15)=70%; coral producer's accuracy=35/(35+5)=87.5%. Thus 30% of mapped coral points are commission errors while 12.5% of true coral points are omitted. This illustrative matrix is not an Allen Coral Atlas result. If samples were disproportionately stratified, area-weighted estimates may be needed."}, {"title": "20. Coral bleaching: map versus monitoring", "html": "<p>Slides 103–114; VTT ~01:45–02:02</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The final application section discusses sea-surface temperature, bleaching prediction and monitoring, including NOAA thermal-stress products and a bleaching detection approach using Allen Coral Atlas habitat context.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>A habitat map indicates where coral-bearing areas are likely to occur, but not whether individual colonies are currently bleached. Sea-surface thermal stress indicates elevated bleaching risk, not confirmed benthic bleaching. Direct optical bleaching detection faces clouds, depth, water clarity and mixed pixels. Combining habitat, temperature and field observations gives stronger inference.</p>", "text": "Slides 103–114; VTT ~01:45–02:02 Lecture material and interpretation The final application section discusses sea-surface temperature, bleaching prediction and monitoring, including NOAA thermal-stress products and a bleaching detection approach using Allen Coral Atlas habitat context. Why it matters / exam application A habitat map indicates where coral-bearing areas are likely to occur, but not whether individual colonies are currently bleached. Sea-surface thermal stress indicates elevated bleaching risk, not confirmed benthic bleaching. Direct optical bleaching detection faces clouds, depth, water clarity and mixed pixels. Combining habitat, temperature and field observations gives stronger inference."}, {"title": "21. SST and thermal stress", "html": "<p>Slides 107–111</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The lecture presents sea-surface temperature and thermal stress as major coral-monitoring inputs. Thermal infrared satellites sense emitted energy from the uppermost ocean surface; temperature retrieval depends on atmospheric correction and other conditions.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Thermal stress products can be spatially coarser than reef-habitat maps. Overlaying a 5 m reef map with a kilometre-scale SST field does not make the temperature data 5 m resolution. A bleaching alert should state the temperature product&#x27;s native scale, temporal averaging and the distinction between hazard and observed ecological impact.</p>", "text": "Slides 107–111 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture presents sea-surface temperature and thermal stress as major coral-monitoring inputs. Thermal infrared satellites sense emitted energy from the uppermost ocean surface; temperature retrieval depends on atmospheric correction and other conditions. Why it matters / exam application Thermal stress products can be spatially coarser than reef-habitat maps. Overlaying a 5 m reef map with a kilometre-scale SST field does not make the temperature data 5 m resolution. A bleaching alert should state the temperature product's native scale, temporal averaging and the distinction between hazard and observed ecological impact."}, {"title": "22. Combining data sources for monitoring", "html": "<p>Slides 112–115</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>The slides ask how remote sensing could monitor reefs and illustrate combining different observations. Reef habitat maps, sea-surface temperature, repeated optical imagery and field/citizen-science records each contribute distinct information.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>For an early-warning system, use thermal stress to prioritise surveys, habitat maps to locate potentially affected areas, and independent in-water observations to verify bleaching and mortality. The logic is a multi-evidence monitoring design rather than a single-index diagnosis.</p>", "text": "Slides 112–115 Lecture material and interpretation The slides ask how remote sensing could monitor reefs and illustrate combining different observations. Reef habitat maps, sea-surface temperature, repeated optical imagery and field/citizen-science records each contribute distinct information. Why it matters / exam application For an early-warning system, use thermal stress to prioritise surveys, habitat maps to locate potentially affected areas, and independent in-water observations to verify bleaching and mortality. The logic is a multi-evidence monitoring design rather than a single-index diagnosis."}, {"title": "23. Worked seagrass workflow critique", "html": "<p>Original exam exercise grounded in lecture concepts</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Scenario: A researcher uses one Planet image acquired at high tide, applies atmospheric correction only, labels dark pixels as seagrass, trains on 20 underwater photos collected a year later, reports 95% accuracy using those same photos and concludes that seagrass has declined by 30% since a second image.</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Problems: depth/tide effects can darken pixels; glint and turbidity are unaddressed; field dates differ; 20 photos may not represent depth and habitat gradients; training/reference leakage inflates accuracy; change maps compound error. A defensible redesign uses low-glint seasonally/tidally comparable scenes, appropriate water-column treatment, independent stratified reference data, depth-specific validation and uncertainty-aware area-change estimation.</p>", "text": "Original exam exercise grounded in lecture concepts Lecture material and interpretation Scenario: A researcher uses one Planet image acquired at high tide, applies atmospheric correction only, labels dark pixels as seagrass, trains on 20 underwater photos collected a year later, reports 95% accuracy using those same photos and concludes that seagrass has declined by 30% since a second image. Why it matters / exam application Problems: depth/tide effects can darken pixels; glint and turbidity are unaddressed; field dates differ; 20 photos may not represent depth and habitat gradients; training/reference leakage inflates accuracy; change maps compound error. A defensible redesign uses low-glint seasonally/tidally comparable scenes, appropriate water-column treatment, independent stratified reference data, depth-specific validation and uncertainty-aware area-change estimation."}, {"title": "24. Worked Allen Coral Atlas workflow defence", "html": "<p>Original exam exercise grounded in slides 61–100</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Question: Why would a global reef-mapping project use low-tide image composites, depth and slope, reference quadrats, random forest, object-based rules and independent QA rather than one universal spectral threshold?</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Answer: Low-tide composites improve shallow-bottom visibility; depth/slope add geomorphic information absent from colour alone; quadrats supply class evidence; random forest models complex predictor combinations; object rules enforce spatial relationships; QA detects implausible outcomes and regional transfer failures. Every stage has uncertainty, and QA cannot replace statistically independent validation.</p>", "text": "Original exam exercise grounded in slides 61–100 Lecture material and interpretation Question: Why would a global reef-mapping project use low-tide image composites, depth and slope, reference quadrats, random forest, object-based rules and independent QA rather than one universal spectral threshold? Why it matters / exam application Answer: Low-tide composites improve shallow-bottom visibility; depth/slope add geomorphic information absent from colour alone; quadrats supply class evidence; random forest models complex predictor combinations; object rules enforce spatial relationships; QA detects implausible outcomes and regional transfer failures. Every stage has uncertainty, and QA cannot replace statistically independent validation."}, {"title": "25. Source-grounded revision questions", "html": "<p>Slides 6–115</p><h3>Lecture material and interpretation</h3><p>Q1: What parts of marine radiance come from the water surface, water column and bottom? Q2: Why does red light disappear faster with depth in many waters? Q3: How can depth mimic a change in benthic class? Q4: Why are photo-quadrat location and footprint critical? Q5: What is the difference between geomorphic and benthic maps? Q6: Why does the Atlas use depth/slope/waves? Q7: What do user&#x27;s and producer&#x27;s accuracy mean? Q8: Why does SST indicate bleaching risk rather than verified bleaching?</p><h3>Why it matters / exam application</h3><p>Model-answer anchors: distinguish observation from inference; identify optical attenuation and mixed signals; match reference spatial/temporal support; explain physical covariates and contextual rules; validate independent samples; separate thermal hazard from biological response. For a high-mark exam response, give a limitation and a mitigation for every processing decision.</p>", "text": "Slides 6–115 Lecture material and interpretation Q1: What parts of marine radiance come from the water surface, water column and bottom? Q2: Why does red light disappear faster with depth in many waters? Q3: How can depth mimic a change in benthic class? Q4: Why are photo-quadrat location and footprint critical? Q5: What is the difference between geomorphic and benthic maps? Q6: Why does the Atlas use depth/slope/waves? Q7: What do user's and producer's accuracy mean? Q8: Why does SST indicate bleaching risk rather than verified bleaching? Why it matters / exam application Model-answer anchors: distinguish observation from inference; identify optical attenuation and mixed signals; match reference spatial/temporal support; explain physical covariates and contextual rules; validate independent samples; separate thermal hazard from biological response. For a high-mark exam response, give a limitation and a mitigation for every processing decision."}], "text": ["1. Lecture framing and the marine observation problem Slides 1–8; VTT ~00:17–00:25 Lecture material and interpretation Chris Roelfsema delivers Lecture 8 during calendar Week 9 (the public holiday shifted numbering). The lecture moves from marine optical physics to field validation, marine corrections, coral reef mapping, the Allen Coral Atlas, and bleaching applications. The core question is not 'Which satellite image looks best?' but 'Which environmental quantity is physically observable under these water, atmosphere and spatial conditions?' Why it matters / exam application Before selecting imagery, specify whether the goal is to map reef geomorphology, benthic habitat, seagrass, water quality or thermal stress. These differ in target, depth dependence, spectral requirements, reference protocol and validation. Optical imagery may observe the benthos only in sufficiently shallow, clear water; a satellite can observe surface temperature without seeing the seabed.", "2. Passive and active sensing over water Slides 7–9; VTT ~00:23–00:27 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture distinguishes passive observation of reflected sunlight from active approaches. A passive optical sensor receives a mixture of atmospheric, water-surface, water-column and bottom signals. The mixture varies with wavelength, depth, turbidity, viewing geometry and illumination. Why it matters / exam application Passive visible-band remote sensing is often suitable for shallow benthic mapping; thermal sensors observe sea-surface thermal emission rather than bottom reflectance; active acoustic and laser methods provide different depth/structure information. No single instrument directly measures all reef properties. A defensible answer states what is observed and what is inferred.", "3. Why water changes the spectrum Slides 9–16; VTT ~00:24–00:35 Lecture material and interpretation Water absorbs and scatters electromagnetic radiation. The lecturer discusses how organic and inorganic material changes the water-column signal and why colours diminish differently with depth. Slides show spectral signatures of water types and a seagrass depth-effect example. Why it matters / exam application The observed radiance is not simply the seagrass or coral reflectance. As depth increases, the bottom contribution is attenuated along the downward and upward light paths. Blue-green light often penetrates clearer water more effectively than red/NIR, while suspended sediment and dissolved organic matter change this pattern. Consequently, the same seagrass meadow can appear spectrally different at different depths.", "4. A conceptual water-column model Slides 11–16; supplementary equation Lecture material and interpretation The slide sequence illustrates absorption and depth dependence but does not prescribe one universal inversion formula. For intuition, a simplified bottom contribution can be written B(λ,z)≈R_bottom(λ)exp[−2K(λ)z], where K is an effective attenuation coefficient and z is depth. This is a teaching approximation, not a verbatim slide formula or complete radiative-transfer solution. Why it matters / exam application For example, if K=0.15 m⁻¹ and z=5 m, attenuation factor exp(−1.5)=0.223. A bottom signal of 0.20 would contribute only ~0.045 in these simplified units before other water/atmospheric terms. The two-way optical path is why depth is a serious confounder. The approximation ignores variable sun/view angles, multiple scattering and wavelength-dependent water constituents.", "5. Depth versus benthic class: an identifiability problem Slides 13–17 Lecture material and interpretation A dark pixel could indicate deep water, a dark benthic substrate, suspended material or a shadow. A brighter pixel could indicate shallow sand, surface glint or a different water-column condition. The lecture's spectral-library and depth-modelling slides show why bottom-type interpretation requires more than matching raw spectral curves. Why it matters / exam application To separate bottom type from depth, consider bathymetry, water-column correction, multiple bands, appropriate depth ranges, and reference observations. A classification model trained in shallow water may label deeper seagrass as another class because attenuation changes the feature distribution. Examine accuracy by depth and turbidity, not only overall accuracy.", "6. Field data: photo quadrats and geolocation Slides 18–23; VTT ~00:35–00:43 Lecture material and interpretation The deck describes geolocated 1 m² photo quadrats and photo interpretation/automation. Overlapping imagery and structure-from-motion photogrammetry can support detailed reef observations. Field data anchor satellite labels to independently observed ecological conditions. Why it matters / exam application A good quadrat protocol documents location, date/time, depth, tide, image orientation, footprint and class definition. A GPS coordinate recorded at the surface can be offset from an underwater quadrat due to drift or positioning method. A 1 m² photo is not automatically representative of a 5 m satellite pixel (25 m²). Use appropriate aggregation or sample design, and quantify uncertainty.", "7. Machine learning for photo annotation Slides 19–23, 79 Lecture material and interpretation Slides discuss machine learning to analyse field photos and later show an automated photo-analysis process after model training. Automation accelerates annotation but inherits training-label ambiguity and image-quality limitations. Why it matters / exam application A coral-cover model should be tested on photos from different reefs, depths, lighting and cameras. Validate the annotation model independently before treating its outputs as ground truth for satellite validation. If an automated photo model is wrong and then used as 'reference', the satellite model may be unfairly penalised or rewarded.", "8. Corrections specific to marine imagery Slides 24–35; VTT ~00:38–00:49 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture dedicates a full section to marine image corrections. Atmospheric path radiance, cloud/shadow, sun glint, water-column attenuation, waves and geometric differences can all affect apparent benthic reflectance. The instructor discusses practical visual quality review. Why it matters / exam application A robust workflow separates atmospheric correction, glint mitigation, water-column/depth treatment and quality masking. These are not interchangeable: correcting atmosphere does not remove bottom-depth effects; glint removal does not make turbid water transparent. Inspect residual artefacts against field observations and avoid treating every correction as universally beneficial.", "9. Sun glint: mechanism and mitigation Slides 25–35; VTT mentions glint ~00:34–00:49 Lecture material and interpretation Specular reflection from the air–water interface adds a strong surface component. Wave facets can reflect direct sunlight toward the sensor, creating bright streaks and obscuring bottom features. Glint is affected by wind, sun/view geometry and surface roughness. Why it matters / exam application Possible responses include selecting low-glint scenes, masking severe glint and using a justified glint-correction method. Near-infrared bands may help estimate glint in optically deep water, but assumptions can fail in shallow/turbid settings. A correction that subtracts too much can remove real shallow-water signal; inspect corrected spectra and residual spatial patterns.", "10. Geometric correction and tidal conditions Slides 24–35; supplementary application Lecture material and interpretation Coastal maps are particularly sensitive to registration errors at narrow reef edges, seagrass boundaries and intertidal areas. Water level changes alter which bottom features are exposed and the optical path length to submerged surfaces. Why it matters / exam application Compare images at suitable tidal states where possible, or model depth/water level explicitly. A 2 m misregistration along a narrow seagrass fringe can create a false change signal. Tidal effects are especially important for intertidal reef flats and shallow banks; document acquisition time, tide and datum.", "11. What is a coral reef and what can be mapped? Slides 36–47; VTT ~00:49–01:05 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture introduces coral ecology, reef geomorphology and the value of habitat maps for conservation. Coral habitats are structured by light, temperature, substrate, wave energy and water chemistry. The slides show reef forms and habitats and why maps need consistent ecological definitions. Why it matters / exam application Distinguish geomorphic zones (e.g., reef crest, flat, slope, lagoon) from benthic cover (e.g., coral, algae, sand, rubble). A geomorphic map describes reef form; a benthic map describes substrate/community composition. A reef-crest polygon is not equivalent to '100% live coral'. Confusing these outputs is a common interpretation error.", "12. Why global reef mapping is difficult Slides 48–58; VTT ~01:05 onward Lecture material and interpretation The Allen Coral Atlas section asks how to map reefs globally. Slides describe very large volumes of Planet Dove imagery, global geomorphic and benthic categories, extensive field datasets and mapping consistency. Slide 57 reports global map accuracy in the 65–85% range and depth-domain statements for geomorphic and benthic mapping. Why it matters / exam application A global system must trade local detail for transferable classes, consistent preprocessing, scalable computation and repeatable QA. A single model trained on one reef may fail in another due to water colour, reef shape, season or image quality. 'Globally consistent' means a common framework, not identical confidence at every reef.", "13. Allen Coral Atlas inputs and preprocessing Slides 51–73 Lecture material and interpretation The deck outlines low-tide Dove mosaics, depth composites, reef/scene masks, mapping regions, image mosaicking and quality review. Slide 69 identifies 5 m daily Planet Dove imagery. The workflow uses Google Earth Engine and region-based processing rather than manually digitising every reef. Why it matters / exam application Why low tide? In suitable conditions it reduces water-column attenuation and improves visibility of shallow structures, though exposure, wet surfaces and tide-specific class interpretation need care. Mosaicking multiple images reduces cloud/glint gaps but can introduce seams and differing radiometry. Review image quality before classification.", "14. Geomorphic classification: physical covariates Slides 61–68, 88–92 Lecture material and interpretation The workflow combines image information with depth, slope, wave exposure and spatial relationships. The slides show object-based rules and random forest classification for geomorphic zones, followed by smoothing, masking and cleanup. Why it matters / exam application A reef crest is defined partly by its position and morphology, not just a spectral signature. Two zones may have similar colours but different slope, depth or neighbourhood. Object-based rules help enforce geomorphic plausibility. However, incorrect bathymetry or overly rigid rules can systematically misclassify unusual reef forms.", "15. Benthic classification: spectra, context and classes Slides 63–68, 89–94 Lecture material and interpretation The deck separates geomorphic and benthic mapping and shows random forest plus cleanup for benthic outputs. Benthic classes are inferred from corrected optical imagery, reference data and contextual information. Why it matters / exam application Benthic classes can be spectrally confused because of mixed cover, depth and turbidity. A 5 m pixel may contain coral, algae and sand; assigning a single dominant label loses fractional composition. Use class definitions that correspond to observable mixtures, validate with geolocated quadrats and interpret class probabilities cautiously.", "16. Reference data assembly at global scale Slides 74–87 Lecture material and interpretation The slide sequence describes collecting existing field observations and maps from agencies, NGOs, scientists and citizen science; storing/reviewing them in GIS web maps; creating reference quadrats; segmenting images and manually labelling geomorphic/benthic categories. Slide 82 gives example regional reference-quadrat counts. Why it matters / exam application Reference data vary in age, quality, protocol, position and class vocabulary. Before pooling, harmonise definitions and assign confidence. The deck explicitly illustrates manual segment labelling and expert knowledge; reference sampling is not simply an automatic export from a field database.", "17. Random forest and object-based workflow Slides 84–94 Lecture material and interpretation Slides show eCognition-based reference segments and Google Earth Engine modules for classification. Random forest learns nonlinear relationships between class labels and predictors; object-based analysis uses spatially coherent units and contextual rules. Why it matters / exam application Why not just threshold one band? Reef classes often overlap spectrally and require multiple features. Why not assume RF is correct? Training data may be geographically biased, and a model can overfit regional conditions. Use independent regional tests, class-level errors and inspection of misclassified locations.", "18. Validation and map quality control Slides 95–102 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture explicitly asks what makes a map 'good'. The Atlas workflow includes overall accuracy, user's and producer's class accuracies, error matrices, validation, review, feedback and delivery. Slides also distinguish reference-data confidence levels. Why it matters / exam application Overall accuracy hides rare-class problems. For a coral class, user's accuracy answers 'How often is mapped coral actually coral?' while producer's accuracy answers 'How much reference coral did the map capture?' A credible product should communicate spatial variation in data quality and reference confidence, not just one global accuracy figure.", "19. Worked confusion-matrix example Slides 96–100; original calculation Lecture material and interpretation Suppose 100 independent reference points yield: mapped coral/reference coral=35, mapped coral/reference other=15, mapped other/reference coral=5, mapped other/reference other=45. Rows are mapped classes; columns are reference. Why it matters / exam application Overall accuracy=(35+45)/100=80%. Coral user's accuracy=35/(35+15)=70%; coral producer's accuracy=35/(35+5)=87.5%. Thus 30% of mapped coral points are commission errors while 12.5% of true coral points are omitted. This illustrative matrix is not an Allen Coral Atlas result. If samples were disproportionately stratified, area-weighted estimates may be needed.", "20. Coral bleaching: map versus monitoring Slides 103–114; VTT ~01:45–02:02 Lecture material and interpretation The final application section discusses sea-surface temperature, bleaching prediction and monitoring, including NOAA thermal-stress products and a bleaching detection approach using Allen Coral Atlas habitat context. Why it matters / exam application A habitat map indicates where coral-bearing areas are likely to occur, but not whether individual colonies are currently bleached. Sea-surface thermal stress indicates elevated bleaching risk, not confirmed benthic bleaching. Direct optical bleaching detection faces clouds, depth, water clarity and mixed pixels. Combining habitat, temperature and field observations gives stronger inference.", "21. SST and thermal stress Slides 107–111 Lecture material and interpretation The lecture presents sea-surface temperature and thermal stress as major coral-monitoring inputs. Thermal infrared satellites sense emitted energy from the uppermost ocean surface; temperature retrieval depends on atmospheric correction and other conditions. Why it matters / exam application Thermal stress products can be spatially coarser than reef-habitat maps. Overlaying a 5 m reef map with a kilometre-scale SST field does not make the temperature data 5 m resolution. A bleaching alert should state the temperature product's native scale, temporal averaging and the distinction between hazard and observed ecological impact.", "22. Combining data sources for monitoring Slides 112–115 Lecture material and interpretation The slides ask how remote sensing could monitor reefs and illustrate combining different observations. Reef habitat maps, sea-surface temperature, repeated optical imagery and field/citizen-science records each contribute distinct information. Why it matters / exam application For an early-warning system, use thermal stress to prioritise surveys, habitat maps to locate potentially affected areas, and independent in-water observations to verify bleaching and mortality. The logic is a multi-evidence monitoring design rather than a single-index diagnosis.", "23. Worked seagrass workflow critique Original exam exercise grounded in lecture concepts Lecture material and interpretation Scenario: A researcher uses one Planet image acquired at high tide, applies atmospheric correction only, labels dark pixels as seagrass, trains on 20 underwater photos collected a year later, reports 95% accuracy using those same photos and concludes that seagrass has declined by 30% since a second image. Why it matters / exam application Problems: depth/tide effects can darken pixels; glint and turbidity are unaddressed; field dates differ; 20 photos may not represent depth and habitat gradients; training/reference leakage inflates accuracy; change maps compound error. A defensible redesign uses low-glint seasonally/tidally comparable scenes, appropriate water-column treatment, independent stratified reference data, depth-specific validation and uncertainty-aware area-change estimation.", "24. Worked Allen Coral Atlas workflow defence Original exam exercise grounded in slides 61–100 Lecture material and interpretation Question: Why would a global reef-mapping project use low-tide image composites, depth and slope, reference quadrats, random forest, object-based rules and independent QA rather than one universal spectral threshold? Why it matters / exam application Answer: Low-tide composites improve shallow-bottom visibility; depth/slope add geomorphic information absent from colour alone; quadrats supply class evidence; random forest models complex predictor combinations; object rules enforce spatial relationships; QA detects implausible outcomes and regional transfer failures. Every stage has uncertainty, and QA cannot replace statistically independent validation.", "25. Source-grounded revision questions Slides 6–115 Lecture material and interpretation Q1: What parts of marine radiance come from the water surface, water column and bottom? Q2: Why does red light disappear faster with depth in many waters? Q3: How can depth mimic a change in benthic class? Q4: Why are photo-quadrat location and footprint critical? Q5: What is the difference between geomorphic and benthic maps? Q6: Why does the Atlas use depth/slope/waves? Q7: What do user's and producer's accuracy mean? Q8: Why does SST indicate bleaching risk rather than verified bleaching? Why it matters / exam application Model-answer anchors: distinguish observation from inference; identify optical attenuation and mixed signals; match reference spatial/temporal support; explain physical covariates and contextual rules; validate independent samples; separate thermal hazard from biological response. For a high-mark exam response, give a limitation and a mitigation for every processing decision."]}, {"id": 9, "title": "From Proposal to Completed Earth Observation Project", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Deep_Week10_Proposal_to_Project.pdf", "pages": 7, "sections": [{"title": "1. What Week 10 is actually about", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 1–7; 7 October VTT 00:06:54–00:10:00</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Will Woodgate introduces this as the proposal-to-project workshop. The aim is to move from a proposed Earth observation study to an implemented analysis and final report, with a guest walkthrough by Maria Prieto, followed by general proposal feedback and planning guidance. The lecture is about scientific project execution and communication rather than introducing a new satellite sensor.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>The important distinction is between a plausible proposal and a reproducible completed investigation. A proposal states what you intend to do; a report must establish what you actually did, why choices were defensible, what results were obtained, how reliable they are, and what the results do and do not mean.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 1–7; 7 October VTT 00:06:54–00:10:00 What the source teaches Will Woodgate introduces this as the proposal-to-project workshop. The aim is to move from a proposed Earth observation study to an implemented analysis and final report, with a guest walkthrough by Maria Prieto, followed by general proposal feedback and planning guidance. The lecture is about scientific project execution and communication rather than introducing a new satellite sensor. Interpretation and exam application The important distinction is between a plausible proposal and a reproducible completed investigation. A proposal states what you intend to do; a report must establish what you actually did, why choices were defensible, what results were obtained, how reliable they are, and what the results do and do not mean."}, {"title": "2. The guest project and its research question", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 1–8; VTT 00:11:22–00:17:25</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Maria Prieto presents her previous project on the effects of Ex-Tropical Cyclone Alfred on Gold Coast coastal ecosystems, using satellite imagery and Digital Earth Australia. The case focuses on water turbidity and mangrove vegetation. The slides and spoken explanation connect March 2025 storm impacts, flooding and erosion to expected changes in water quality and vegetation condition.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Maria frames a testable expectation: turbidity index (NDTI) should increase after the storm, while mangrove greenness measured with NDVI may decrease later. Will Woodgate clarifies around 00:17:27 that not every project requires a hypothesis, but a well-formulated hypothesis can frame results interpretation. Do not present the hypothesis as an observed outcome before analysis.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 1–8; VTT 00:11:22–00:17:25 What the source teaches Maria Prieto presents her previous project on the effects of Ex-Tropical Cyclone Alfred on Gold Coast coastal ecosystems, using satellite imagery and Digital Earth Australia. The case focuses on water turbidity and mangrove vegetation. The slides and spoken explanation connect March 2025 storm impacts, flooding and erosion to expected changes in water quality and vegetation condition. Interpretation and exam application Maria frames a testable expectation: turbidity index (NDTI) should increase after the storm, while mangrove greenness measured with NDVI may decrease later. Will Woodgate clarifies around 00:17:27 that not every project requires a hypothesis, but a well-formulated hypothesis can frame results interpretation. Do not present the hypothesis as an observed outcome before analysis."}, {"title": "3. Start from literature and define a narrow scope", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 4–8; Part 2 slides 4–7, 17</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The guest presentation explicitly asks students to begin with the state of the art and avoid repeating an already answered question without a reason. A research question should arise from a knowledge gap, the available data, and a feasible method. The workshop returns to literature review, research gaps and justified data/method selection.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>A defensible question specifies phenomenon, geography, temporal period and measurable outcome. Compare &#x27;Did the cyclone affect nature?&#x27; with &#x27;How did satellite-derived water turbidity and mangrove vegetation indices change in Gold Coast coastal waters around the March 2025 event?&#x27; The second question constrains the observation and analysis design, although index changes still require careful interpretation.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 4–8; Part 2 slides 4–7, 17 What the source teaches The guest presentation explicitly asks students to begin with the state of the art and avoid repeating an already answered question without a reason. A research question should arise from a knowledge gap, the available data, and a feasible method. The workshop returns to literature review, research gaps and justified data/method selection. Interpretation and exam application A defensible question specifies phenomenon, geography, temporal period and measurable outcome. Compare 'Did the cyclone affect nature?' with 'How did satellite-derived water turbidity and mangrove vegetation indices change in Gold Coast coastal waters around the March 2025 event?' The second question constrains the observation and analysis design, although index changes still require careful interpretation."}, {"title": "4. A hypothesis is a prediction, not proof", "html": "<p>Part 1 slide 8; VTT 00:16:34–00:17:35</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Maria explicitly distinguishes her expectation from the results. She anticipated a sharp increase in NDTI and a decrease in mangrove NDVI after the event. Will Woodgate notes that hypotheses, when used well, help organise interpretation. The slide frames the hypothesis as something justified from literature rather than invented after seeing results.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Do not reverse-engineer a hypothesis from the most striking map. Write the expected direction and time scale before interpreting the figures; then compare the evidence, including alternative explanations and uncertainty. If results contradict the prediction, that is a scientific finding to explain rather than a reason to discard the analysis.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slide 8; VTT 00:16:34–00:17:35 What the source teaches Maria explicitly distinguishes her expectation from the results. She anticipated a sharp increase in NDTI and a decrease in mangrove NDVI after the event. Will Woodgate notes that hypotheses, when used well, help organise interpretation. The slide frames the hypothesis as something justified from literature rather than invented after seeing results. Interpretation and exam application Do not reverse-engineer a hypothesis from the most striking map. Write the expected direction and time scale before interpreting the figures; then compare the evidence, including alternative explanations and uncertainty. If results contradict the prediction, that is a scientific finding to explain rather than a reason to discard the analysis."}, {"title": "5. Choose data because they answer the question", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 9–11; VTT 00:18–00:22</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The guest explains why a multitemporal satellite approach was suitable for the storm event and discusses using DEA products. Field turbidity records from the Gold Coast Water Quality Program were available as potential validation. She mentions 15 selected monitoring sites and sampling every one or two months, with turbidity measured in NTU.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Data choice is a design decision. For an acute event, revisit frequency, clouds, dates before and after, spectral sensitivity, spatial resolution and field timing determine what can be concluded. A field measurement from several weeks after an image may be too far apart to validate short-lived sediment plumes. State the temporal matching window and the limitations of sparse observations.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 9–11; VTT 00:18–00:22 What the source teaches The guest explains why a multitemporal satellite approach was suitable for the storm event and discusses using DEA products. Field turbidity records from the Gold Coast Water Quality Program were available as potential validation. She mentions 15 selected monitoring sites and sampling every one or two months, with turbidity measured in NTU. Interpretation and exam application Data choice is a design decision. For an acute event, revisit frequency, clouds, dates before and after, spectral sensitivity, spatial resolution and field timing determine what can be concluded. A field measurement from several weeks after an image may be too far apart to validate short-lived sediment plumes. State the temporal matching window and the limitations of sparse observations."}, {"title": "6. NDTI and NTU are different quantities", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 10–11, 18–20; VTT 00:13:29–00:13:59 and 00:21:25–00:22:14</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The guest uses NDTI as a satellite-derived turbidity indicator and compares the study to field water-quality measurements reported in nephelometric turbidity units (NTU). The slides ask students to examine what NDTI versus NTU means, rather than equating them.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>An index is a dimensionless spectral calculation; NTU is a measurement of optical turbidity by an instrument. They are not interchangeable units. A correlation between NDTI and NTU may support usefulness of the index in the study conditions, but a conversion to NTU requires an independently calibrated and tested model, with attention to atmospheric correction, bottom reflectance and water depth.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 10–11, 18–20; VTT 00:13:29–00:13:59 and 00:21:25–00:22:14 What the source teaches The guest uses NDTI as a satellite-derived turbidity indicator and compares the study to field water-quality measurements reported in nephelometric turbidity units (NTU). The slides ask students to examine what NDTI versus NTU means, rather than equating them. Interpretation and exam application An index is a dimensionless spectral calculation; NTU is a measurement of optical turbidity by an instrument. They are not interchangeable units. A correlation between NDTI and NTU may support usefulness of the index in the study conditions, but a conversion to NTU requires an independently calibrated and tested model, with attention to atmospheric correction, bottom reflectance and water depth."}, {"title": "7. NDVI and EVI are proxies, not ecological diagnoses", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 7–8, 19–21</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The guest includes NDVI and EVI to explore vegetation response and asks whether patterns might be seasonal. The lecture does not establish that every index decline is mangrove damage; it emphasises interpretation in light of the literature and method limitations.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>NDVI or EVI change can reflect leaf properties, canopy structure, soil/water background, clouds, tides and acquisition conditions. For mangrove analysis, use a stable mangrove mask, comparable seasonal observations and appropriate reference evidence. A decline in greenness alone cannot establish mortality, and a lack of index decline does not prove absence of stress.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 7–8, 19–21 What the source teaches The guest includes NDVI and EVI to explore vegetation response and asks whether patterns might be seasonal. The lecture does not establish that every index decline is mangrove damage; it emphasises interpretation in light of the literature and method limitations. Interpretation and exam application NDVI or EVI change can reflect leaf properties, canopy structure, soil/water background, clouds, tides and acquisition conditions. For mangrove analysis, use a stable mangrove mask, comparable seasonal observations and appropriate reference evidence. A decline in greenness alone cannot establish mortality, and a lack of index decline does not prove absence of stress."}, {"title": "8. How much analysis is enough?", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 12–16; VTT 00:22:29–00:24:30</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Maria says students often ask whether their methodology contains enough work. Her slides answer that an Earth observation workflow should address the research question with a coherent degree of certainty within the available timeframe. The example shows a sequence of time steps and six change maps, not a requirement that every student produce that number.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>The correct amount of analysis is not the largest number of indices, models or maps. It is the minimum defensible chain that addresses the question, checks key assumptions and assesses uncertainty. A focused comparison with meaningful validation is preferable to a dozen unvalidated maps. Additional complexity must earn its place by improving inference.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 12–16; VTT 00:22:29–00:24:30 What the source teaches Maria says students often ask whether their methodology contains enough work. Her slides answer that an Earth observation workflow should address the research question with a coherent degree of certainty within the available timeframe. The example shows a sequence of time steps and six change maps, not a requirement that every student produce that number. Interpretation and exam application The correct amount of analysis is not the largest number of indices, models or maps. It is the minimum defensible chain that addresses the question, checks key assumptions and assesses uncertainty. A focused comparison with meaningful validation is preferable to a dozen unvalidated maps. Additional complexity must earn its place by improving inference."}, {"title": "9. Design the processing sequence before coding", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 10–11 and 20</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The workshop lays out a planning sequence: objectives and deliverables; data and resource identification; tasks and timeline; implementation; and results. Its &#x27;images to information&#x27; sequence moves from statement of the problem through image acquisition, processing, information extraction, verification and interpretation.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>A useful dependency chain is: research question → measurable variable → sensor/product → study period and sample design → preprocessing → analysis → validation → results → limitations. If validation data are unavailable, the workflow must be revised before producing a strong accuracy claim. If images are persistently cloudy during the event, change the temporal window or narrow the question.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 10–11 and 20 What the source teaches The workshop lays out a planning sequence: objectives and deliverables; data and resource identification; tasks and timeline; implementation; and results. Its 'images to information' sequence moves from statement of the problem through image acquisition, processing, information extraction, verification and interpretation. Interpretation and exam application A useful dependency chain is: research question → measurable variable → sensor/product → study period and sample design → preprocessing → analysis → validation → results → limitations. If validation data are unavailable, the workflow must be revised before producing a strong accuracy claim. If images are persistently cloudy during the event, change the temporal window or narrow the question."}, {"title": "10. Plan time, resources and milestones", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 8–12; VTT 01:09:47–01:10:10</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The slides state that project planning helps ensure completion, maintain quality standards, meet a budget and operate within time constraints. The 7 October recording notes the final report deadline of Friday 30 October 2026, leaving roughly four weeks at the time of the lecture.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>The deadline is historical course context, not a future deadline today. The transferable lesson is to reserve explicit time for data download, correction, modelling, validation, troubleshooting, writing and revision. Plan backward from the deliverable. If a processing stage fails, prioritise a smaller validated result over a larger unsupported conclusion.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 8–12; VTT 01:09:47–01:10:10 What the source teaches The slides state that project planning helps ensure completion, maintain quality standards, meet a budget and operate within time constraints. The 7 October recording notes the final report deadline of Friday 30 October 2026, leaving roughly four weeks at the time of the lecture. Interpretation and exam application The deadline is historical course context, not a future deadline today. The transferable lesson is to reserve explicit time for data download, correction, modelling, validation, troubleshooting, writing and revision. Plan backward from the deliverable. If a processing stage fails, prioritise a smaller validated result over a larger unsupported conclusion."}, {"title": "11. Reproducibility: project logs and notebooks", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 20–21</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The workshop explicitly illustrates an ENVI processing log in Word and a Jupyter notebook published on GitHub. These are alternative mechanisms for recording processing choices and outcomes. The slides do not imply that a successful software run is itself evidence of scientific validity.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Record product identifiers, acquisition dates, cloud filters, spatial bounds, CRS, masks, band definitions, index formulas, model parameters, reference sample locations, software versions and intermediate outputs. Keep code and outputs linked. A good log allows another analyst to identify exactly where a map came from and to reproduce or audit it.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 20–21 What the source teaches The workshop explicitly illustrates an ENVI processing log in Word and a Jupyter notebook published on GitHub. These are alternative mechanisms for recording processing choices and outcomes. The slides do not imply that a successful software run is itself evidence of scientific validity. Interpretation and exam application Record product identifiers, acquisition dates, cloud filters, spatial bounds, CRS, masks, band definitions, index formulas, model parameters, reference sample locations, software versions and intermediate outputs. Keep code and outputs linked. A good log allows another analyst to identify exactly where a map came from and to reproduce or audit it."}, {"title": "12. Interpreting a sequence of change maps", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 14–20</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Maria&#x27;s presentation includes seven temporal steps yielding six consecutive change maps. The slides then move to results and ask how to present them clearly and interpret their relationship to hypotheses. A map sequence is evidence of mapped spectral change, not automatic proof of storm causality.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Distinguish event-related departures from ordinary seasonal variation. Compare the pre-event baseline, event period and recovery window. Examine whether changes persist, whether they are spatially coherent with plausible processes, and whether cloud, glint, tide or acquisition timing can explain the signal. Annotate maps with dates, units, colour scale and data gaps.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 14–20 What the source teaches Maria's presentation includes seven temporal steps yielding six consecutive change maps. The slides then move to results and ask how to present them clearly and interpret their relationship to hypotheses. A map sequence is evidence of mapped spectral change, not automatic proof of storm causality. Interpretation and exam application Distinguish event-related departures from ordinary seasonal variation. Compare the pre-event baseline, event period and recovery window. Examine whether changes persist, whether they are spatially coherent with plausible processes, and whether cloud, glint, tide or acquisition timing can explain the signal. Annotate maps with dates, units, colour scale and data gaps."}, {"title": "13. Independent validation and uncertainty", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 11, 17–21</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The guest uses field turbidity data as external evidence and explicitly separates results from interpretation and validation. The source does not provide enough information here to claim a specific numerical correlation or prediction accuracy without inspecting its full results.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Independent validation should match the target, time and spatial support. For NDTI, compare satellite pixels to field NTU only after accounting for the mismatch in units and sampling footprints; evaluate whether a calibrated relationship exists. For vegetation, use independent field observations or high-quality reference imagery where available. Report how few samples, temporal gaps and location error limit certainty.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 11, 17–21 What the source teaches The guest uses field turbidity data as external evidence and explicitly separates results from interpretation and validation. The source does not provide enough information here to claim a specific numerical correlation or prediction accuracy without inspecting its full results. Interpretation and exam application Independent validation should match the target, time and spatial support. For NDTI, compare satellite pixels to field NTU only after accounting for the mismatch in units and sampling footprints; evaluate whether a calibrated relationship exists. For vegetation, use independent field observations or high-quality reference imagery where available. Report how few samples, temporal gaps and location error limit certainty."}, {"title": "14. Reporting results so they can be understood", "html": "<p>Part 1 slides 17–21; Part 2 slides 23, 25–27</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>The two slide decks ask students to present readable results, analyse whether the hypotheses were supported, discuss limitations, and compile a final report according to the assignment brief and template. The general workshop includes review and revision as stages of the process.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Every figure should answer a defined question. Use clear titles, units, colour bars, legends, north/scale where relevant, time labels and captions explaining the comparison. Separate Results (what the evidence shows) from Discussion (why it may have happened and how it relates to prior work). State what is unsupported, not just what succeeded.</p>", "text": "Part 1 slides 17–21; Part 2 slides 23, 25–27 What the source teaches The two slide decks ask students to present readable results, analyse whether the hypotheses were supported, discuss limitations, and compile a final report according to the assignment brief and template. The general workshop includes review and revision as stages of the process. Interpretation and exam application Every figure should answer a defined question. Use clear titles, units, colour bars, legends, north/scale where relevant, time labels and captions explaining the comparison. Separate Results (what the evidence shows) from Discussion (why it may have happened and how it relates to prior work). State what is unsupported, not just what succeeded."}, {"title": "15. Proposal feedback and asking for help", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 3–5, 18; VTT 00:09:01–00:09:15 and 01:17:46–01:18:10</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Will Woodgate says he has compiled generic proposal feedback to share with the class. Later, he explains that there is no dedicated formal draft-report feedback submission, but students are encouraged to show progress during practical sessions. The slide deck also highlights active use of available resources and support.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>A good question to a tutor is specific: &#x27;My surface-reflectance images differ by acquisition month and I observe an NDVI drop only at shoreline pixels. Is my tidal mask adequate?&#x27; That is more productive than &#x27;Does my project look okay?&#x27; Bring an example map, code/log, a stated uncertainty and the decision you need to make.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 3–5, 18; VTT 00:09:01–00:09:15 and 01:17:46–01:18:10 What the source teaches Will Woodgate says he has compiled generic proposal feedback to share with the class. Later, he explains that there is no dedicated formal draft-report feedback submission, but students are encouraged to show progress during practical sessions. The slide deck also highlights active use of available resources and support. Interpretation and exam application A good question to a tutor is specific: 'My surface-reflectance images differ by acquisition month and I observe an NDVI drop only at shoreline pixels. Is my tidal mask adequate?' That is more productive than 'Does my project look okay?' Bring an example map, code/log, a stated uncertainty and the decision you need to make."}, {"title": "16. Original worked design: mangrove stress study", "html": "<p>Supplementary application of the workshop framework</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Question: Can satellite-derived vegetation indicators reveal early mangrove stress before a mapped disturbance event? Define a pre-event baseline, candidate indices, a study region and independent disturbance/reference locations. Choose observations with suitable spatial, temporal and spectral resolution, and specify how invalid pixels and sensor differences will be handled.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Suggested sequence: (1) define event and baseline dates; (2) inspect data completeness and seasonal patterns; (3) apply consistent quality masking; (4) compute indices and seasonal anomalies; (5) compare affected and unaffected sites; (6) test lead time and false alarms; (7) conduct sensitivity analysis; (8) communicate uncertainty. A negative anomaly may be an early signal but is not proof of a unique physiological mechanism.</p>", "text": "Supplementary application of the workshop framework What the source teaches Question: Can satellite-derived vegetation indicators reveal early mangrove stress before a mapped disturbance event? Define a pre-event baseline, candidate indices, a study region and independent disturbance/reference locations. Choose observations with suitable spatial, temporal and spectral resolution, and specify how invalid pixels and sensor differences will be handled. Interpretation and exam application Suggested sequence: (1) define event and baseline dates; (2) inspect data completeness and seasonal patterns; (3) apply consistent quality masking; (4) compute indices and seasonal anomalies; (5) compare affected and unaffected sites; (6) test lead time and false alarms; (7) conduct sensitivity analysis; (8) communicate uncertainty. A negative anomaly may be an early signal but is not proof of a unique physiological mechanism."}, {"title": "17. Original exam critique: an overambitious proposal", "html": "<p>Supplementary exam scenario based on workshop principles</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>A student proposes to map all coral, seagrass, turbidity, sea-surface temperature and mangrove condition over a decade, using four sensors, but has two weeks, no reference data and no plan for harmonising acquisitions. They promise 95% accuracy based on visual inspection.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Critique: the scope exceeds resources; targets have different measurement physics; cross-sensor harmonisation and temporal sampling are unspecified; visual inspection is not an independent accuracy assessment. Defend a revised plan that selects one or two observable variables, defines a realistic time window, documents preprocessing and uses available independent reference data. Explain explicitly what cannot be validated.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exam scenario based on workshop principles What the source teaches A student proposes to map all coral, seagrass, turbidity, sea-surface temperature and mangrove condition over a decade, using four sensors, but has two weeks, no reference data and no plan for harmonising acquisitions. They promise 95% accuracy based on visual inspection. Interpretation and exam application Critique: the scope exceeds resources; targets have different measurement physics; cross-sensor harmonisation and temporal sampling are unspecified; visual inspection is not an independent accuracy assessment. Defend a revised plan that selects one or two observable variables, defines a realistic time window, documents preprocessing and uses available independent reference data. Explain explicitly what cannot be validated."}, {"title": "18. Original exam critique: cyclone attribution", "html": "<p>Supplementary exam scenario based on Maria Prieto&#x27;s case</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>A team finds higher NDTI in one post-cyclone image and lower mangrove NDVI in another. They conclude that the cyclone caused water turbidity and mangrove mortality. Both images have different cloud cover, water levels and seasons.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Critique: a temporal association is not causal identification. Check acquisition conditions and sensor correction, use more pre- and post-event dates, compare unaffected control areas, account for seasonality, and seek independent water-quality and ecological evidence. Report a supported observation (&#x27;index changed&#x27;) separately from a causal hypothesis (&#x27;storm drove the change&#x27;).</p>", "text": "Supplementary exam scenario based on Maria Prieto's case What the source teaches A team finds higher NDTI in one post-cyclone image and lower mangrove NDVI in another. They conclude that the cyclone caused water turbidity and mangrove mortality. Both images have different cloud cover, water levels and seasons. Interpretation and exam application Critique: a temporal association is not causal identification. Check acquisition conditions and sensor correction, use more pre- and post-event dates, compare unaffected control areas, account for seasonality, and seek independent water-quality and ecological evidence. Report a supported observation ('index changed') separately from a causal hypothesis ('storm drove the change')."}, {"title": "19. Mini checklist for final report defence", "html": "<p>Part 2 slides 17–27; supplementary operational checklist</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Can you state the research question in one sentence? Does every output correspond to an objective? Are datasets, dates, processing and sample design documented? Is the model appropriate to the physical variable? Is reference evidence independent? Are maps readable? Do results distinguish measured quantities from proxies? Are limitations explicit?</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>During an oral or written critique, explain not only what you chose but why alternatives were unsuitable. A strong defence connects every major decision to an error it controls or a scientific question it helps answer.</p>", "text": "Part 2 slides 17–27; supplementary operational checklist What the source teaches Can you state the research question in one sentence? Does every output correspond to an objective? Are datasets, dates, processing and sample design documented? Is the model appropriate to the physical variable? Is reference evidence independent? Are maps readable? Do results distinguish measured quantities from proxies? Are limitations explicit? Interpretation and exam application During an oral or written critique, explain not only what you chose but why alternatives were unsuitable. A strong defence connects every major decision to an error it controls or a scientific question it helps answer."}, {"title": "20. Revision questions with concise answers", "html": "<p>Both decks and 7 October recording</p><h3>What the source teaches</h3><p>Q1: What changes between proposal and report? A: planned work becomes documented executed methods, results and critique. Q2: Must every project state a hypothesis? A: No; the lecturer says a well-framed hypothesis can help interpretation. Q3: Is NDTI measured in NTU? A: No, the former is a spectral index and the latter a field turbidity unit. Q4: Why keep a processing log? A: Reproducibility and auditability.</p><h3>Interpretation and exam application</h3><p>Q5: Why not use many indices by default? A: Extra analyses should answer a defined question. Q6: What is a validation mismatch? A: Reference data measure a different time, footprint or variable. Q7: Why are six change maps not necessarily better than one? A: Quantity of maps does not establish quality. Q8: What belongs in discussion? A: Interpretation, relation to literature, alternative explanations and limitations.</p>", "text": "Both decks and 7 October recording What the source teaches Q1: What changes between proposal and report? A: planned work becomes documented executed methods, results and critique. Q2: Must every project state a hypothesis? A: No; the lecturer says a well-framed hypothesis can help interpretation. Q3: Is NDTI measured in NTU? A: No, the former is a spectral index and the latter a field turbidity unit. Q4: Why keep a processing log? A: Reproducibility and auditability. Interpretation and exam application Q5: Why not use many indices by default? A: Extra analyses should answer a defined question. Q6: What is a validation mismatch? A: Reference data measure a different time, footprint or variable. Q7: Why are six change maps not necessarily better than one? A: Quantity of maps does not establish quality. Q8: What belongs in discussion? A: Interpretation, relation to literature, alternative explanations and limitations."}], "text": ["1. What Week 10 is actually about Part 2 slides 1–7; 7 October VTT 00:06:54–00:10:00 What the source teaches Will Woodgate introduces this as the proposal-to-project workshop. The aim is to move from a proposed Earth observation study to an implemented analysis and final report, with a guest walkthrough by Maria Prieto, followed by general proposal feedback and planning guidance. The lecture is about scientific project execution and communication rather than introducing a new satellite sensor. Interpretation and exam application The important distinction is between a plausible proposal and a reproducible completed investigation. A proposal states what you intend to do; a report must establish what you actually did, why choices were defensible, what results were obtained, how reliable they are, and what the results do and do not mean.", "2. The guest project and its research question Part 1 slides 1–8; VTT 00:11:22–00:17:25 What the source teaches Maria Prieto presents her previous project on the effects of Ex-Tropical Cyclone Alfred on Gold Coast coastal ecosystems, using satellite imagery and Digital Earth Australia. The case focuses on water turbidity and mangrove vegetation. The slides and spoken explanation connect March 2025 storm impacts, flooding and erosion to expected changes in water quality and vegetation condition. Interpretation and exam application Maria frames a testable expectation: turbidity index (NDTI) should increase after the storm, while mangrove greenness measured with NDVI may decrease later. Will Woodgate clarifies around 00:17:27 that not every project requires a hypothesis, but a well-formulated hypothesis can frame results interpretation. Do not present the hypothesis as an observed outcome before analysis.", "3. Start from literature and define a narrow scope Part 1 slides 4–8; Part 2 slides 4–7, 17 What the source teaches The guest presentation explicitly asks students to begin with the state of the art and avoid repeating an already answered question without a reason. A research question should arise from a knowledge gap, the available data, and a feasible method. The workshop returns to literature review, research gaps and justified data/method selection. Interpretation and exam application A defensible question specifies phenomenon, geography, temporal period and measurable outcome. Compare 'Did the cyclone affect nature?' with 'How did satellite-derived water turbidity and mangrove vegetation indices change in Gold Coast coastal waters around the March 2025 event?' The second question constrains the observation and analysis design, although index changes still require careful interpretation.", "4. A hypothesis is a prediction, not proof Part 1 slide 8; VTT 00:16:34–00:17:35 What the source teaches Maria explicitly distinguishes her expectation from the results. She anticipated a sharp increase in NDTI and a decrease in mangrove NDVI after the event. Will Woodgate notes that hypotheses, when used well, help organise interpretation. The slide frames the hypothesis as something justified from literature rather than invented after seeing results. Interpretation and exam application Do not reverse-engineer a hypothesis from the most striking map. Write the expected direction and time scale before interpreting the figures; then compare the evidence, including alternative explanations and uncertainty. If results contradict the prediction, that is a scientific finding to explain rather than a reason to discard the analysis.", "5. Choose data because they answer the question Part 1 slides 9–11; VTT 00:18–00:22 What the source teaches The guest explains why a multitemporal satellite approach was suitable for the storm event and discusses using DEA products. Field turbidity records from the Gold Coast Water Quality Program were available as potential validation. She mentions 15 selected monitoring sites and sampling every one or two months, with turbidity measured in NTU. Interpretation and exam application Data choice is a design decision. For an acute event, revisit frequency, clouds, dates before and after, spectral sensitivity, spatial resolution and field timing determine what can be concluded. A field measurement from several weeks after an image may be too far apart to validate short-lived sediment plumes. State the temporal matching window and the limitations of sparse observations.", "6. NDTI and NTU are different quantities Part 1 slides 10–11, 18–20; VTT 00:13:29–00:13:59 and 00:21:25–00:22:14 What the source teaches The guest uses NDTI as a satellite-derived turbidity indicator and compares the study to field water-quality measurements reported in nephelometric turbidity units (NTU). The slides ask students to examine what NDTI versus NTU means, rather than equating them. Interpretation and exam application An index is a dimensionless spectral calculation; NTU is a measurement of optical turbidity by an instrument. They are not interchangeable units. A correlation between NDTI and NTU may support usefulness of the index in the study conditions, but a conversion to NTU requires an independently calibrated and tested model, with attention to atmospheric correction, bottom reflectance and water depth.", "7. NDVI and EVI are proxies, not ecological diagnoses Part 1 slides 7–8, 19–21 What the source teaches The guest includes NDVI and EVI to explore vegetation response and asks whether patterns might be seasonal. The lecture does not establish that every index decline is mangrove damage; it emphasises interpretation in light of the literature and method limitations. Interpretation and exam application NDVI or EVI change can reflect leaf properties, canopy structure, soil/water background, clouds, tides and acquisition conditions. For mangrove analysis, use a stable mangrove mask, comparable seasonal observations and appropriate reference evidence. A decline in greenness alone cannot establish mortality, and a lack of index decline does not prove absence of stress.", "8. How much analysis is enough? Part 1 slides 12–16; VTT 00:22:29–00:24:30 What the source teaches Maria says students often ask whether their methodology contains enough work. Her slides answer that an Earth observation workflow should address the research question with a coherent degree of certainty within the available timeframe. The example shows a sequence of time steps and six change maps, not a requirement that every student produce that number. Interpretation and exam application The correct amount of analysis is not the largest number of indices, models or maps. It is the minimum defensible chain that addresses the question, checks key assumptions and assesses uncertainty. A focused comparison with meaningful validation is preferable to a dozen unvalidated maps. Additional complexity must earn its place by improving inference.", "9. Design the processing sequence before coding Part 2 slides 10–11 and 20 What the source teaches The workshop lays out a planning sequence: objectives and deliverables; data and resource identification; tasks and timeline; implementation; and results. Its 'images to information' sequence moves from statement of the problem through image acquisition, processing, information extraction, verification and interpretation. Interpretation and exam application A useful dependency chain is: research question → measurable variable → sensor/product → study period and sample design → preprocessing → analysis → validation → results → limitations. If validation data are unavailable, the workflow must be revised before producing a strong accuracy claim. If images are persistently cloudy during the event, change the temporal window or narrow the question.", "10. Plan time, resources and milestones Part 2 slides 8–12; VTT 01:09:47–01:10:10 What the source teaches The slides state that project planning helps ensure completion, maintain quality standards, meet a budget and operate within time constraints. The 7 October recording notes the final report deadline of Friday 30 October 2026, leaving roughly four weeks at the time of the lecture. Interpretation and exam application The deadline is historical course context, not a future deadline today. The transferable lesson is to reserve explicit time for data download, correction, modelling, validation, troubleshooting, writing and revision. Plan backward from the deliverable. If a processing stage fails, prioritise a smaller validated result over a larger unsupported conclusion.", "11. Reproducibility: project logs and notebooks Part 2 slides 20–21 What the source teaches The workshop explicitly illustrates an ENVI processing log in Word and a Jupyter notebook published on GitHub. These are alternative mechanisms for recording processing choices and outcomes. The slides do not imply that a successful software run is itself evidence of scientific validity. Interpretation and exam application Record product identifiers, acquisition dates, cloud filters, spatial bounds, CRS, masks, band definitions, index formulas, model parameters, reference sample locations, software versions and intermediate outputs. Keep code and outputs linked. A good log allows another analyst to identify exactly where a map came from and to reproduce or audit it.", "12. Interpreting a sequence of change maps Part 1 slides 14–20 What the source teaches Maria's presentation includes seven temporal steps yielding six consecutive change maps. The slides then move to results and ask how to present them clearly and interpret their relationship to hypotheses. A map sequence is evidence of mapped spectral change, not automatic proof of storm causality. Interpretation and exam application Distinguish event-related departures from ordinary seasonal variation. Compare the pre-event baseline, event period and recovery window. Examine whether changes persist, whether they are spatially coherent with plausible processes, and whether cloud, glint, tide or acquisition timing can explain the signal. Annotate maps with dates, units, colour scale and data gaps.", "13. Independent validation and uncertainty Part 1 slides 11, 17–21 What the source teaches The guest uses field turbidity data as external evidence and explicitly separates results from interpretation and validation. The source does not provide enough information here to claim a specific numerical correlation or prediction accuracy without inspecting its full results. Interpretation and exam application Independent validation should match the target, time and spatial support. For NDTI, compare satellite pixels to field NTU only after accounting for the mismatch in units and sampling footprints; evaluate whether a calibrated relationship exists. For vegetation, use independent field observations or high-quality reference imagery where available. Report how few samples, temporal gaps and location error limit certainty.", "14. Reporting results so they can be understood Part 1 slides 17–21; Part 2 slides 23, 25–27 What the source teaches The two slide decks ask students to present readable results, analyse whether the hypotheses were supported, discuss limitations, and compile a final report according to the assignment brief and template. The general workshop includes review and revision as stages of the process. Interpretation and exam application Every figure should answer a defined question. Use clear titles, units, colour bars, legends, north/scale where relevant, time labels and captions explaining the comparison. Separate Results (what the evidence shows) from Discussion (why it may have happened and how it relates to prior work). State what is unsupported, not just what succeeded.", "15. Proposal feedback and asking for help Part 2 slides 3–5, 18; VTT 00:09:01–00:09:15 and 01:17:46–01:18:10 What the source teaches Will Woodgate says he has compiled generic proposal feedback to share with the class. Later, he explains that there is no dedicated formal draft-report feedback submission, but students are encouraged to show progress during practical sessions. The slide deck also highlights active use of available resources and support. Interpretation and exam application A good question to a tutor is specific: 'My surface-reflectance images differ by acquisition month and I observe an NDVI drop only at shoreline pixels. Is my tidal mask adequate?' That is more productive than 'Does my project look okay?' Bring an example map, code/log, a stated uncertainty and the decision you need to make.", "16. Original worked design: mangrove stress study Supplementary application of the workshop framework What the source teaches Question: Can satellite-derived vegetation indicators reveal early mangrove stress before a mapped disturbance event? Define a pre-event baseline, candidate indices, a study region and independent disturbance/reference locations. Choose observations with suitable spatial, temporal and spectral resolution, and specify how invalid pixels and sensor differences will be handled. Interpretation and exam application Suggested sequence: (1) define event and baseline dates; (2) inspect data completeness and seasonal patterns; (3) apply consistent quality masking; (4) compute indices and seasonal anomalies; (5) compare affected and unaffected sites; (6) test lead time and false alarms; (7) conduct sensitivity analysis; (8) communicate uncertainty. A negative anomaly may be an early signal but is not proof of a unique physiological mechanism.", "17. Original exam critique: an overambitious proposal Supplementary exam scenario based on workshop principles What the source teaches A student proposes to map all coral, seagrass, turbidity, sea-surface temperature and mangrove condition over a decade, using four sensors, but has two weeks, no reference data and no plan for harmonising acquisitions. They promise 95% accuracy based on visual inspection. Interpretation and exam application Critique: the scope exceeds resources; targets have different measurement physics; cross-sensor harmonisation and temporal sampling are unspecified; visual inspection is not an independent accuracy assessment. Defend a revised plan that selects one or two observable variables, defines a realistic time window, documents preprocessing and uses available independent reference data. Explain explicitly what cannot be validated.", "18. Original exam critique: cyclone attribution Supplementary exam scenario based on Maria Prieto's case What the source teaches A team finds higher NDTI in one post-cyclone image and lower mangrove NDVI in another. They conclude that the cyclone caused water turbidity and mangrove mortality. Both images have different cloud cover, water levels and seasons. Interpretation and exam application Critique: a temporal association is not causal identification. Check acquisition conditions and sensor correction, use more pre- and post-event dates, compare unaffected control areas, account for seasonality, and seek independent water-quality and ecological evidence. Report a supported observation ('index changed') separately from a causal hypothesis ('storm drove the change').", "19. Mini checklist for final report defence Part 2 slides 17–27; supplementary operational checklist What the source teaches Can you state the research question in one sentence? Does every output correspond to an objective? Are datasets, dates, processing and sample design documented? Is the model appropriate to the physical variable? Is reference evidence independent? Are maps readable? Do results distinguish measured quantities from proxies? Are limitations explicit? Interpretation and exam application During an oral or written critique, explain not only what you chose but why alternatives were unsuitable. A strong defence connects every major decision to an error it controls or a scientific question it helps answer.", "20. Revision questions with concise answers Both decks and 7 October recording What the source teaches Q1: What changes between proposal and report? A: planned work becomes documented executed methods, results and critique. Q2: Must every project state a hypothesis? A: No; the lecturer says a well-framed hypothesis can help interpretation. Q3: Is NDTI measured in NTU? A: No, the former is a spectral index and the latter a field turbidity unit. Q4: Why keep a processing log? A: Reproducibility and auditability. Interpretation and exam application Q5: Why not use many indices by default? A: Extra analyses should answer a defined question. Q6: What is a validation mismatch? A: Reference data measure a different time, footprint or variable. Q7: Why are six change maps not necessarily better than one? A: Quantity of maps does not establish quality. Q8: What belongs in discussion? A: Interpretation, relation to literature, alternative explanations and limitations."]}, {"id": 10, "title": "Earth Observation Futures, Job-Ready Skills & Exam Synthesis", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Final_Lecture_Exam_Synthesis.pdf", "pages": 8, "sections": [{"title": "1. What the final lecture actually covers", "html": "<p>Slides 1–7; recording 28 October, opening ~00:09</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Will Woodgate closes the course with four themes: course summary and examination; professional pathways; current and future EO directions; and course close. This is a synthesis and career-oriented lecture, not a new image-processing algorithm.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>For revision, treat the course learning outcomes and image-processing sequence as a map of what you should be able to explain. The future-of-EO slides provide context, not a substitute for technical reasoning.</p>", "text": "Slides 1–7; recording 28 October, opening ~00:09 From the lecture Will Woodgate closes the course with four themes: course summary and examination; professional pathways; current and future EO directions; and course close. This is a synthesis and career-oriented lecture, not a new image-processing algorithm. Explanation / application For revision, treat the course learning outcomes and image-processing sequence as a map of what you should be able to explain. The future-of-EO slides provide context, not a substitute for technical reasoning."}, {"title": "2. Course aims: what has been taught", "html": "<p>Slide 8</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Five stated aims: advanced EO processing and interpretation; practical big-data platforms such as Open Data Cube; biophysical attributes from remotely sensed and ground data; advanced data types; and planning/executing a remote-sensing project.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>A strong answer links physics and data characteristics to a reproducible workflow, reference evidence and defensible interpretation. Knowing only the name of an index or algorithm is not enough.</p>", "text": "Slide 8 From the lecture Five stated aims: advanced EO processing and interpretation; practical big-data platforms such as Open Data Cube; biophysical attributes from remotely sensed and ground data; advanced data types; and planning/executing a remote-sensing project. Explanation / application A strong answer links physics and data characteristics to a reproducible workflow, reference evidence and defensible interpretation. Knowing only the name of an index or algorithm is not enough."}, {"title": "3. Learning outcome LO1: resolutions", "html": "<p>Slide 10</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>LO1 requires applying spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal resolution concepts in professional settings.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Example defence: choosing 10 m imagery rather than 30 m may help resolve narrow mangrove fringes, but higher spatial resolution can cost temporal coverage or increase noise and data volume. Spectral resolution affects discriminating vegetation properties; revisit frequency controls how quickly disturbance can be observed.</p>", "text": "Slide 10 From the lecture LO1 requires applying spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal resolution concepts in professional settings. Explanation / application Example defence: choosing 10 m imagery rather than 30 m may help resolve narrow mangrove fringes, but higher spatial resolution can cost temporal coverage or increase noise and data volume. Spectral resolution affects discriminating vegetation properties; revisit frequency controls how quickly disturbance can be observed."}, {"title": "4. Learning outcome LO2: processing chain", "html": "<p>Slides 10–11</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>LO2 covers preprocessing, corrections, enhancement, classification, biophysical modelling and accuracy assessment. Slide 11 presents a problem-to-solution sequence, with expert/reference information and GIS integration.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Explain what error each stage reduces: geometry for spatial alignment, radiometric/atmospheric correction for comparability, feature selection for discriminability, classification or regression for target retrieval, and independent validation for uncertainty. Do not imply that a visually attractive image is an accurate information product.</p>", "text": "Slides 10–11 From the lecture LO2 covers preprocessing, corrections, enhancement, classification, biophysical modelling and accuracy assessment. Slide 11 presents a problem-to-solution sequence, with expert/reference information and GIS integration. Explanation / application Explain what error each stage reduces: geometry for spatial alignment, radiometric/atmospheric correction for comparability, feature selection for discriminability, classification or regression for target retrieval, and independent validation for uncertainty. Do not imply that a visually attractive image is an accurate information product."}, {"title": "5. Learning outcomes LO3–LO6", "html": "<p>Slide 10</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>LO3: execute complete projects integrating field and non-image data. LO4: evaluate datasets for applications. LO5: use big-data EO platforms. LO6: analyse hyperspectral, LiDAR, radar and multitemporal data.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>For a question asking which data to use, justify sensor physics, scale, temporal coverage, corrections, reference availability and computing feasibility. Compare options against the target, not against popularity.</p>", "text": "Slide 10 From the lecture LO3: execute complete projects integrating field and non-image data. LO4: evaluate datasets for applications. LO5: use big-data EO platforms. LO6: analyse hyperspectral, LiDAR, radar and multitemporal data. Explanation / application For a question asking which data to use, justify sensor physics, scale, temporal coverage, corrections, reference availability and computing feasibility. Compare options against the target, not against popularity."}, {"title": "6. The full image-processing sequence", "html": "<p>Slide 11</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The slide lists statement of problem → image acquisition → preprocessing → enhancement → thematic/quantitative information extraction → error assessment → multidate analysis → GIS → solving the problem, with expert and reference data.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>This is a conceptual flow, not an unbreakable chronological law. Validation design should begin before data collection; multitemporal analysis can occur on reflectance or derived classes; and interpretation may require revisiting earlier steps. The scientific question drives the sequence.</p>", "text": "Slide 11 From the lecture The slide lists statement of problem → image acquisition → preprocessing → enhancement → thematic/quantitative information extraction → error assessment → multidate analysis → GIS → solving the problem, with expert and reference data. Explanation / application This is a conceptual flow, not an unbreakable chronological law. Validation design should begin before data collection; multitemporal analysis can occur on reflectance or derived classes; and interpretation may require revisiting earlier steps. The scientific question drives the sequence."}, {"title": "7. Field-to-impact EO chain", "html": "<p>Slides 12–14</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The lecturer connects field measurements, UAV/airborne/satellite observations, images-to-information processing, applications/services and impacts. Example biophysical workflows show how operational information products arise from EO and ground data.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Distinguish a measurement from a service. A canopy-cover raster is a data product; a habitat-risk dashboard is an application; improved management decisions are intended impacts. Each stage needs its own quality and usability criteria.</p>", "text": "Slides 12–14 From the lecture The lecturer connects field measurements, UAV/airborne/satellite observations, images-to-information processing, applications/services and impacts. Example biophysical workflows show how operational information products arise from EO and ground data. Explanation / application Distinguish a measurement from a service. A canopy-cover raster is a data product; a habitat-risk dashboard is an application; improved management decisions are intended impacts. Each stage needs its own quality and usability criteria."}, {"title": "8. Exam: what the slides actually say", "html": "<p>Slide 15; recording final lecture</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The slide identifies two authoritative resources: the Electronic Course Profile (ECP) and the Exam Preparation guide on Blackboard. It calls the exam a hurdle assessment worth 45%. The deck does not supply the full exam questions, precise format, or hurdle pass threshold.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Check the ECP and Blackboard guide for exact conditions. Do not infer a minimum pass mark or exam structure from the slide alone. Prepare to justify processing choices, assumptions, limitations and validation, which are consistent with the stated learning outcomes.</p>", "text": "Slide 15; recording final lecture From the lecture The slide identifies two authoritative resources: the Electronic Course Profile (ECP) and the Exam Preparation guide on Blackboard. It calls the exam a hurdle assessment worth 45%. The deck does not supply the full exam questions, precise format, or hurdle pass threshold. Explanation / application Check the ECP and Blackboard guide for exact conditions. Do not infer a minimum pass mark or exam structure from the slide alone. Prepare to justify processing choices, assumptions, limitations and validation, which are consistent with the stated learning outcomes."}, {"title": "9. How to answer a workflow critique", "html": "<p>Supplementary exam technique grounded in slides 10–11</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Use a five-part response: target and measurement physics; data and sampling fit; corrections and analysis; independent validation; interpretation and uncertainty.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Example: “The two images are not comparable because season and acquisition geometry differ; I would use matched-season surface-reflectance composites, check registration and cloud masks, quantify baseline variability, and validate suspected change with independent reference observations.” This is a model answer, not a verbatim lecturer quotation.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exam technique grounded in slides 10–11 From the lecture Use a five-part response: target and measurement physics; data and sampling fit; corrections and analysis; independent validation; interpretation and uncertainty. Explanation / application Example: “The two images are not comparable because season and acquisition geometry differ; I would use matched-season surface-reflectance composites, check registration and cloud masks, quantify baseline variability, and validate suspected change with independent reference observations.” This is a model answer, not a verbatim lecturer quotation."}, {"title": "10. Worked calculation: accuracy", "html": "<p>Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>A confusion matrix has mapped mangrove/reference mangrove=36, mapped mangrove/reference other=9, mapped other/reference mangrove=4, mapped other/reference other=51. There are 100 independent observations; rows are mapped labels.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Overall accuracy=(36+51)/100=87%; mangrove user’s accuracy=36/(36+9)=80%; producer’s accuracy=36/(36+4)=90%. Thus commission error for mapped mangrove is 20% and omission error for reference mangrove is 10%. If the validation sampling was disproportionately stratified, design weights may be necessary.</p>", "text": "Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material From the lecture A confusion matrix has mapped mangrove/reference mangrove=36, mapped mangrove/reference other=9, mapped other/reference mangrove=4, mapped other/reference other=51. There are 100 independent observations; rows are mapped labels. Explanation / application Overall accuracy=(36+51)/100=87%; mangrove user’s accuracy=36/(36+9)=80%; producer’s accuracy=36/(36+4)=90%. Thus commission error for mapped mangrove is 20% and omission error for reference mangrove is 10%. If the validation sampling was disproportionately stratified, design weights may be necessary."}, {"title": "11. Worked calculation: spectral anomaly", "html": "<p>Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>A seasonally matched 2012–2014 baseline NDVI has mean 0.65 and standard deviation 0.05. A valid 2015 observation is 0.50.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>The standardized anomaly is (0.50−0.65)/0.05=−3.0. This is a strong negative deviation from that baseline but not automatically a causal diagnosis of dieback. Check number of observations, atmospheric/tidal artefacts, seasonal alignment and persistence.</p>", "text": "Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material From the lecture A seasonally matched 2012–2014 baseline NDVI has mean 0.65 and standard deviation 0.05. A valid 2015 observation is 0.50. Explanation / application The standardized anomaly is (0.50−0.65)/0.05=−3.0. This is a strong negative deviation from that baseline but not automatically a causal diagnosis of dieback. Check number of observations, atmospheric/tidal artefacts, seasonal alignment and persistence."}, {"title": "12. Job-ready skills: foundational and human", "html": "<p>Slide 17</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The lecturer emphasises geospatial collection/analysis/communication, effective listening and written/oral/graphic communication, awareness of strengths and weaknesses, workplace values, motivation to learn and a growing professional network. Sector-specific knowledge helps.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>A technically competent EO analyst must explain uncertainty to a non-specialist and listen to what decision-makers need. In interviews, describe one project from problem to validated output, including a failed assumption and how you addressed it.</p>", "text": "Slide 17 From the lecture The lecturer emphasises geospatial collection/analysis/communication, effective listening and written/oral/graphic communication, awareness of strengths and weaknesses, workplace values, motivation to learn and a growing professional network. Sector-specific knowledge helps. Explanation / application A technically competent EO analyst must explain uncertainty to a non-specialist and listen to what decision-makers need. In interviews, describe one project from problem to validated output, including a failed assumption and how you addressed it."}, {"title": "13. Job-ready skills: technical trends", "html": "<p>Slide 18</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>A table lists cloud-native engineering, MLOps and foundation-model fine-tuning, Python ecosystems, product management, responsible AI/data governance, cross-domain fusion, storytelling/visualisation, and DevOps/reproducibility.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>These are the lecturer’s future-oriented skill suggestions, not verified labour-market statistics. Specific numeric claims and regulatory descriptions in the table should not be repeated as independently established facts without external verification. The durable principle is to combine EO science, coding, data engineering and communication.</p>", "text": "Slide 18 From the lecture A table lists cloud-native engineering, MLOps and foundation-model fine-tuning, Python ecosystems, product management, responsible AI/data governance, cross-domain fusion, storytelling/visualisation, and DevOps/reproducibility. Explanation / application These are the lecturer’s future-oriented skill suggestions, not verified labour-market statistics. Specific numeric claims and regulatory descriptions in the table should not be repeated as independently established facts without external verification. The durable principle is to combine EO science, coding, data engineering and communication."}, {"title": "14. Roles and disciplines in EO", "html": "<p>Slides 19–22</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The deck connects sensor engineering, environmental sciences, GIS, EO processing, computing and professional roles. Potential employers include government, defence, established and start-up industry, universities, research institutes, Indigenous organisations, NGOs, community groups and consultancies.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>An EO career may be labelled environmental analyst, spatial data scientist, research assistant, geospatial developer or monitoring specialist rather than “remote sensing scientist.” Job titles and demand vary; the lecture’s job-board examples are snapshots rather than current vacancies.</p>", "text": "Slides 19–22 From the lecture The deck connects sensor engineering, environmental sciences, GIS, EO processing, computing and professional roles. Potential employers include government, defence, established and start-up industry, universities, research institutes, Indigenous organisations, NGOs, community groups and consultancies. Explanation / application An EO career may be labelled environmental analyst, spatial data scientist, research assistant, geospatial developer or monitoring specialist rather than “remote sensing scientist.” Job titles and demand vary; the lecture’s job-board examples are snapshots rather than current vacancies."}, {"title": "15. Professional practice and values", "html": "<p>Slide 20</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The lecturer prompts students to consider what they value in a job, preferred career direction, professionalism, and the workplace culture they want to join.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Professionalism in EO includes transparent methods, ethical data handling, uncertainty communication, reproducibility, acknowledging collaborators and not overstating findings. These are applications of the lecture prompts, not an additional accreditation requirement.</p>", "text": "Slide 20 From the lecture The lecturer prompts students to consider what they value in a job, preferred career direction, professionalism, and the workplace culture they want to join. Explanation / application Professionalism in EO includes transparent methods, ethical data handling, uncertainty communication, reproducibility, acknowledging collaborators and not overstating findings. These are applications of the lecture prompts, not an additional accreditation requirement."}, {"title": "16. Networks, societies and accreditation", "html": "<p>Slides 23–25</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The slides name ASPRS, AGU, IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Society and ISPRS as professional and research organisations.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Membership or accreditation may support networking and professional development, but the deck does not establish that any one certification is mandatory for all EO roles. Assess suitability for the sector and country.</p>", "text": "Slides 23–25 From the lecture The slides name ASPRS, AGU, IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Society and ISPRS as professional and research organisations. Explanation / application Membership or accreditation may support networking and professional development, but the deck does not establish that any one certification is mandatory for all EO roles. Assess suitability for the sector and country."}, {"title": "17. Future EO: observation systems", "html": "<p>Slides 4–6, 26–30</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The lecturer lists satellite miniaturisation, larger constellations, higher resolution, multi-sensor integration and AI/ML as possible trends. Later slides refer to ESA EO strategy and Australian EO priorities: environmental understanding, workforce/training/collaboration, and sustainable data acquisition.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>More imagery does not automatically mean better evidence. Constellations increase coverage but can create calibration, sensor-harmonisation and data-governance challenges. Higher spatial resolution does not necessarily improve spectral resolution or physical retrievability.</p>", "text": "Slides 4–6, 26–30 From the lecture The lecturer lists satellite miniaturisation, larger constellations, higher resolution, multi-sensor integration and AI/ML as possible trends. Later slides refer to ESA EO strategy and Australian EO priorities: environmental understanding, workforce/training/collaboration, and sustainable data acquisition. Explanation / application More imagery does not automatically mean better evidence. Constellations increase coverage but can create calibration, sensor-harmonisation and data-governance challenges. Higher spatial resolution does not necessarily improve spectral resolution or physical retrievability."}, {"title": "18. Future EO: AI and responsible practice", "html": "<p>Slides 4–6, 18; supplementary critical framing</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The slides discuss AI-assisted analysis and include explicitly AI-generated illustrations/content in the introductory trend discussion.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>AI can speed feature extraction but may generalise poorly outside its training domain. Evaluate spatial/temporal transfer, training-data bias, uncertainty and reproducibility. Treat slide-generated forecasts and job statistics as claims to verify rather than settled evidence.</p>", "text": "Slides 4–6, 18; supplementary critical framing From the lecture The slides discuss AI-assisted analysis and include explicitly AI-generated illustrations/content in the introductory trend discussion. Explanation / application AI can speed feature extraction but may generalise poorly outside its training domain. Evaluate spatial/temporal transfer, training-data bias, uncertainty and reproducibility. Treat slide-generated forecasts and job statistics as claims to verify rather than settled evidence."}, {"title": "19. Indigenous approaches and EO", "html": "<p>Slides 31–34</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The lecture highlights Martu art/knowledge, Winyama, Indigenous Mapping Collective, Living Knowledge Network, a cultural-burning project and UQ reconciliation planning.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>A responsible workflow begins with community priorities and governance, not just extracting geospatial observations. Discuss consent, local knowledge, attribution, data sovereignty, benefits and appropriate sharing restrictions. The slides introduce examples; they do not provide a universal consent protocol.</p>", "text": "Slides 31–34 From the lecture The lecture highlights Martu art/knowledge, Winyama, Indigenous Mapping Collective, Living Knowledge Network, a cultural-burning project and UQ reconciliation planning. Explanation / application A responsible workflow begins with community priorities and governance, not just extracting geospatial observations. Discuss consent, local knowledge, attribution, data sovereignty, benefits and appropriate sharing restrictions. The slides introduce examples; they do not provide a universal consent protocol."}, {"title": "20. Continuing study and research", "html": "<p>Slides 37–38</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The slides describe special-project opportunities and potential research directions: radar-derived vegetation structure/carbon dynamics, environmental change detection, coastal habitat/reef mapping and urban temperature. The special-project slide lists proposal 20%, project 70% and engagement 10% for that distinct offering.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>These percentages belong to the advertised special-project assessment, not the GEOM7001 final exam. Project opportunities require confirmation with staff and current enrolment information.</p>", "text": "Slides 37–38 From the lecture The slides describe special-project opportunities and potential research directions: radar-derived vegetation structure/carbon dynamics, environmental change detection, coastal habitat/reef mapping and urban temperature. The special-project slide lists proposal 20%, project 70% and engagement 10% for that distinct offering. Explanation / application These percentages belong to the advertised special-project assessment, not the GEOM7001 final exam. Project opportunities require confirmation with staff and current enrolment information."}, {"title": "21. Integrated mock exam A: mangrove early signal", "html": "<p>Supplementary exercise across course learning outcomes</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Question: Compare NDVI, Sentinel-2 red-edge indices and SIF for detecting a 2015–2016 mangrove disturbance. Your data have differing resolutions, revisit frequencies and seasonal gaps. Design a defensible comparison.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Answer: Define early signal relative to an independently mapped event; harmonise temporal support and spatial aggregation; build pre-event seasonal baselines; mask poor-quality data; calculate anomalies and lead times; compare affected/unaffected reference areas; assess false positives and uncertainty. SIF’s coarse spatial support and different physical meaning must be stated. An anomaly is not a demonstrated physiological mechanism.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exercise across course learning outcomes From the lecture Question: Compare NDVI, Sentinel-2 red-edge indices and SIF for detecting a 2015–2016 mangrove disturbance. Your data have differing resolutions, revisit frequencies and seasonal gaps. Design a defensible comparison. Explanation / application Answer: Define early signal relative to an independently mapped event; harmonise temporal support and spatial aggregation; build pre-event seasonal baselines; mask poor-quality data; calculate anomalies and lead times; compare affected/unaffected reference areas; assess false positives and uncertainty. SIF’s coarse spatial support and different physical meaning must be stated. An anomaly is not a demonstrated physiological mechanism."}, {"title": "22. Integrated mock exam B: choose a sensor", "html": "<p>Supplementary exercise across LO1, LO4 and LO6</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Question: A manager needs (a) flood extent during persistent cloud, (b) detailed canopy height, and (c) shallow coral habitat. Which sensor types and limitations are relevant?</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Answer: (a) SAR is cloud-capable but affected by geometry, surface roughness and vegetation; (b) LiDAR measures 3D returns, subject to occlusion and sampling; (c) optical multispectral/hyperspectral imagery can map shallow benthos in clear water, subject to atmosphere, glint and depth attenuation. No sensor is universally best.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exercise across LO1, LO4 and LO6 From the lecture Question: A manager needs (a) flood extent during persistent cloud, (b) detailed canopy height, and (c) shallow coral habitat. Which sensor types and limitations are relevant? Explanation / application Answer: (a) SAR is cloud-capable but affected by geometry, surface roughness and vegetation; (b) LiDAR measures 3D returns, subject to occlusion and sampling; (c) optical multispectral/hyperspectral imagery can map shallow benthos in clear water, subject to atmosphere, glint and depth attenuation. No sensor is universally best."}, {"title": "23. Integrated mock exam C: small mapped change", "html": "<p>Supplementary exercise across LO2 and LO3</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Question: Two land-cover maps each report 88% overall accuracy, and their difference indicates 2% mangrove loss. Is the loss reliable?</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Answer: Not from those figures alone. Overall accuracy is not change-detection accuracy, and errors may compound. Use independent change-reference samples, class-specific errors, appropriate area estimators, matched-season images and uncertainty intervals. Report whether the inferred change exceeds plausible mapping error.</p>", "text": "Supplementary exercise across LO2 and LO3 From the lecture Question: Two land-cover maps each report 88% overall accuracy, and their difference indicates 2% mangrove loss. Is the loss reliable? Explanation / application Answer: Not from those figures alone. Overall accuracy is not change-detection accuracy, and errors may compound. Use independent change-reference samples, class-specific errors, appropriate area estimators, matched-season images and uncertainty intervals. Report whether the inferred change exceeds plausible mapping error."}, {"title": "24. Final revision checklist", "html": "<p>Slides 8–15; supplementary checklist</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>Can you explain all four resolutions; choose appropriate data; identify preprocessing errors; distinguish classification from regression; calculate and interpret accuracy; justify time-series methods; compare optical, radar, LiDAR and hyperspectral sensing; and explain field/reference matching?</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>For each topic, practise: definition → physical principle → processing choice → assumption → failure mode → mitigation → validation. If you can only define an acronym but cannot defend its use, revise the workflow reasoning.</p>", "text": "Slides 8–15; supplementary checklist From the lecture Can you explain all four resolutions; choose appropriate data; identify preprocessing errors; distinguish classification from regression; calculate and interpret accuracy; justify time-series methods; compare optical, radar, LiDAR and hyperspectral sensing; and explain field/reference matching? Explanation / application For each topic, practise: definition → physical principle → processing choice → assumption → failure mode → mitigation → validation. If you can only define an acronym but cannot defend its use, revise the workflow reasoning."}, {"title": "25. Source boundaries and next steps", "html": "<p>Slides 15, 18, 37–38</p><h3>From the lecture</h3><p>The provided final lecture is a wrap-up, careers and future-direction presentation. It refers students to the ECP and Blackboard exam guide, neither of which is reproduced here. The lecture contains some AI-generated forecasts and job-market assertions that are not independently verified in this chapter.</p><h3>Explanation / application</h3><p>Use this guide for synthesis. For exact exam rules, use the ECP and exam guide. For detailed technical derivations, revisit the earlier weekly lectures. The three integrated mock exams above are original practice questions, not leaked or official examination questions.</p>", "text": "Slides 15, 18, 37–38 From the lecture The provided final lecture is a wrap-up, careers and future-direction presentation. It refers students to the ECP and Blackboard exam guide, neither of which is reproduced here. The lecture contains some AI-generated forecasts and job-market assertions that are not independently verified in this chapter. Explanation / application Use this guide for synthesis. For exact exam rules, use the ECP and exam guide. For detailed technical derivations, revisit the earlier weekly lectures. The three integrated mock exams above are original practice questions, not leaked or official examination questions."}], "text": ["1. What the final lecture actually covers Slides 1–7; recording 28 October, opening ~00:09 From the lecture Will Woodgate closes the course with four themes: course summary and examination; professional pathways; current and future EO directions; and course close. This is a synthesis and career-oriented lecture, not a new image-processing algorithm. Explanation / application For revision, treat the course learning outcomes and image-processing sequence as a map of what you should be able to explain. The future-of-EO slides provide context, not a substitute for technical reasoning.", "2. Course aims: what has been taught Slide 8 From the lecture Five stated aims: advanced EO processing and interpretation; practical big-data platforms such as Open Data Cube; biophysical attributes from remotely sensed and ground data; advanced data types; and planning/executing a remote-sensing project. Explanation / application A strong answer links physics and data characteristics to a reproducible workflow, reference evidence and defensible interpretation. Knowing only the name of an index or algorithm is not enough.", "3. Learning outcome LO1: resolutions Slide 10 From the lecture LO1 requires applying spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal resolution concepts in professional settings. Explanation / application Example defence: choosing 10 m imagery rather than 30 m may help resolve narrow mangrove fringes, but higher spatial resolution can cost temporal coverage or increase noise and data volume. Spectral resolution affects discriminating vegetation properties; revisit frequency controls how quickly disturbance can be observed.", "4. Learning outcome LO2: processing chain Slides 10–11 From the lecture LO2 covers preprocessing, corrections, enhancement, classification, biophysical modelling and accuracy assessment. Slide 11 presents a problem-to-solution sequence, with expert/reference information and GIS integration. Explanation / application Explain what error each stage reduces: geometry for spatial alignment, radiometric/atmospheric correction for comparability, feature selection for discriminability, classification or regression for target retrieval, and independent validation for uncertainty. Do not imply that a visually attractive image is an accurate information product.", "5. Learning outcomes LO3–LO6 Slide 10 From the lecture LO3: execute complete projects integrating field and non-image data. LO4: evaluate datasets for applications. LO5: use big-data EO platforms. LO6: analyse hyperspectral, LiDAR, radar and multitemporal data. Explanation / application For a question asking which data to use, justify sensor physics, scale, temporal coverage, corrections, reference availability and computing feasibility. Compare options against the target, not against popularity.", "6. The full image-processing sequence Slide 11 From the lecture The slide lists statement of problem → image acquisition → preprocessing → enhancement → thematic/quantitative information extraction → error assessment → multidate analysis → GIS → solving the problem, with expert and reference data. Explanation / application This is a conceptual flow, not an unbreakable chronological law. Validation design should begin before data collection; multitemporal analysis can occur on reflectance or derived classes; and interpretation may require revisiting earlier steps. The scientific question drives the sequence.", "7. Field-to-impact EO chain Slides 12–14 From the lecture The lecturer connects field measurements, UAV/airborne/satellite observations, images-to-information processing, applications/services and impacts. Example biophysical workflows show how operational information products arise from EO and ground data. Explanation / application Distinguish a measurement from a service. A canopy-cover raster is a data product; a habitat-risk dashboard is an application; improved management decisions are intended impacts. Each stage needs its own quality and usability criteria.", "8. Exam: what the slides actually say Slide 15; recording final lecture From the lecture The slide identifies two authoritative resources: the Electronic Course Profile (ECP) and the Exam Preparation guide on Blackboard. It calls the exam a hurdle assessment worth 45%. The deck does not supply the full exam questions, precise format, or hurdle pass threshold. Explanation / application Check the ECP and Blackboard guide for exact conditions. Do not infer a minimum pass mark or exam structure from the slide alone. Prepare to justify processing choices, assumptions, limitations and validation, which are consistent with the stated learning outcomes.", "9. How to answer a workflow critique Supplementary exam technique grounded in slides 10–11 From the lecture Use a five-part response: target and measurement physics; data and sampling fit; corrections and analysis; independent validation; interpretation and uncertainty. Explanation / application Example: “The two images are not comparable because season and acquisition geometry differ; I would use matched-season surface-reflectance composites, check registration and cloud masks, quantify baseline variability, and validate suspected change with independent reference observations.” This is a model answer, not a verbatim lecturer quotation.", "10. Worked calculation: accuracy Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material From the lecture A confusion matrix has mapped mangrove/reference mangrove=36, mapped mangrove/reference other=9, mapped other/reference mangrove=4, mapped other/reference other=51. There are 100 independent observations; rows are mapped labels. Explanation / application Overall accuracy=(36+51)/100=87%; mangrove user’s accuracy=36/(36+9)=80%; producer’s accuracy=36/(36+4)=90%. Thus commission error for mapped mangrove is 20% and omission error for reference mangrove is 10%. If the validation sampling was disproportionately stratified, design weights may be necessary.", "11. Worked calculation: spectral anomaly Supplementary synthesis of earlier course material From the lecture A seasonally matched 2012–2014 baseline NDVI has mean 0.65 and standard deviation 0.05. A valid 2015 observation is 0.50. Explanation / application The standardized anomaly is (0.50−0.65)/0.05=−3.0. This is a strong negative deviation from that baseline but not automatically a causal diagnosis of dieback. Check number of observations, atmospheric/tidal artefacts, seasonal alignment and persistence.", "12. Job-ready skills: foundational and human Slide 17 From the lecture The lecturer emphasises geospatial collection/analysis/communication, effective listening and written/oral/graphic communication, awareness of strengths and weaknesses, workplace values, motivation to learn and a growing professional network. Sector-specific knowledge helps. Explanation / application A technically competent EO analyst must explain uncertainty to a non-specialist and listen to what decision-makers need. In interviews, describe one project from problem to validated output, including a failed assumption and how you addressed it.", "13. Job-ready skills: technical trends Slide 18 From the lecture A table lists cloud-native engineering, MLOps and foundation-model fine-tuning, Python ecosystems, product management, responsible AI/data governance, cross-domain fusion, storytelling/visualisation, and DevOps/reproducibility. Explanation / application These are the lecturer’s future-oriented skill suggestions, not verified labour-market statistics. Specific numeric claims and regulatory descriptions in the table should not be repeated as independently established facts without external verification. The durable principle is to combine EO science, coding, data engineering and communication.", "14. Roles and disciplines in EO Slides 19–22 From the lecture The deck connects sensor engineering, environmental sciences, GIS, EO processing, computing and professional roles. Potential employers include government, defence, established and start-up industry, universities, research institutes, Indigenous organisations, NGOs, community groups and consultancies. Explanation / application An EO career may be labelled environmental analyst, spatial data scientist, research assistant, geospatial developer or monitoring specialist rather than “remote sensing scientist.” Job titles and demand vary; the lecture’s job-board examples are snapshots rather than current vacancies.", "15. Professional practice and values Slide 20 From the lecture The lecturer prompts students to consider what they value in a job, preferred career direction, professionalism, and the workplace culture they want to join. Explanation / application Professionalism in EO includes transparent methods, ethical data handling, uncertainty communication, reproducibility, acknowledging collaborators and not overstating findings. These are applications of the lecture prompts, not an additional accreditation requirement.", "16. Networks, societies and accreditation Slides 23–25 From the lecture The slides name ASPRS, AGU, IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Society and ISPRS as professional and research organisations. Explanation / application Membership or accreditation may support networking and professional development, but the deck does not establish that any one certification is mandatory for all EO roles. Assess suitability for the sector and country.", "17. Future EO: observation systems Slides 4–6, 26–30 From the lecture The lecturer lists satellite miniaturisation, larger constellations, higher resolution, multi-sensor integration and AI/ML as possible trends. Later slides refer to ESA EO strategy and Australian EO priorities: environmental understanding, workforce/training/collaboration, and sustainable data acquisition. Explanation / application More imagery does not automatically mean better evidence. Constellations increase coverage but can create calibration, sensor-harmonisation and data-governance challenges. Higher spatial resolution does not necessarily improve spectral resolution or physical retrievability.", "18. Future EO: AI and responsible practice Slides 4–6, 18; supplementary critical framing From the lecture The slides discuss AI-assisted analysis and include explicitly AI-generated illustrations/content in the introductory trend discussion. Explanation / application AI can speed feature extraction but may generalise poorly outside its training domain. Evaluate spatial/temporal transfer, training-data bias, uncertainty and reproducibility. Treat slide-generated forecasts and job statistics as claims to verify rather than settled evidence.", "19. Indigenous approaches and EO Slides 31–34 From the lecture The lecture highlights Martu art/knowledge, Winyama, Indigenous Mapping Collective, Living Knowledge Network, a cultural-burning project and UQ reconciliation planning. Explanation / application A responsible workflow begins with community priorities and governance, not just extracting geospatial observations. Discuss consent, local knowledge, attribution, data sovereignty, benefits and appropriate sharing restrictions. The slides introduce examples; they do not provide a universal consent protocol.", "20. Continuing study and research Slides 37–38 From the lecture The slides describe special-project opportunities and potential research directions: radar-derived vegetation structure/carbon dynamics, environmental change detection, coastal habitat/reef mapping and urban temperature. The special-project slide lists proposal 20%, project 70% and engagement 10% for that distinct offering. Explanation / application These percentages belong to the advertised special-project assessment, not the GEOM7001 final exam. Project opportunities require confirmation with staff and current enrolment information.", "21. Integrated mock exam A: mangrove early signal Supplementary exercise across course learning outcomes From the lecture Question: Compare NDVI, Sentinel-2 red-edge indices and SIF for detecting a 2015–2016 mangrove disturbance. Your data have differing resolutions, revisit frequencies and seasonal gaps. Design a defensible comparison. Explanation / application Answer: Define early signal relative to an independently mapped event; harmonise temporal support and spatial aggregation; build pre-event seasonal baselines; mask poor-quality data; calculate anomalies and lead times; compare affected/unaffected reference areas; assess false positives and uncertainty. SIF’s coarse spatial support and different physical meaning must be stated. An anomaly is not a demonstrated physiological mechanism.", "22. Integrated mock exam B: choose a sensor Supplementary exercise across LO1, LO4 and LO6 From the lecture Question: A manager needs (a) flood extent during persistent cloud, (b) detailed canopy height, and (c) shallow coral habitat. Which sensor types and limitations are relevant? Explanation / application Answer: (a) SAR is cloud-capable but affected by geometry, surface roughness and vegetation; (b) LiDAR measures 3D returns, subject to occlusion and sampling; (c) optical multispectral/hyperspectral imagery can map shallow benthos in clear water, subject to atmosphere, glint and depth attenuation. No sensor is universally best.", "23. Integrated mock exam C: small mapped change Supplementary exercise across LO2 and LO3 From the lecture Question: Two land-cover maps each report 88% overall accuracy, and their difference indicates 2% mangrove loss. Is the loss reliable? Explanation / application Answer: Not from those figures alone. Overall accuracy is not change-detection accuracy, and errors may compound. Use independent change-reference samples, class-specific errors, appropriate area estimators, matched-season images and uncertainty intervals. Report whether the inferred change exceeds plausible mapping error.", "24. Final revision checklist Slides 8–15; supplementary checklist From the lecture Can you explain all four resolutions; choose appropriate data; identify preprocessing errors; distinguish classification from regression; calculate and interpret accuracy; justify time-series methods; compare optical, radar, LiDAR and hyperspectral sensing; and explain field/reference matching? Explanation / application For each topic, practise: definition → physical principle → processing choice → assumption → failure mode → mitigation → validation. If you can only define an acronym but cannot defend its use, revise the workflow reasoning.", "25. Source boundaries and next steps Slides 15, 18, 37–38 From the lecture The provided final lecture is a wrap-up, careers and future-direction presentation. It refers students to the ECP and Blackboard exam guide, neither of which is reproduced here. The lecture contains some AI-generated forecasts and job-market assertions that are not independently verified in this chapter. Explanation / application Use this guide for synthesis. For exact exam rules, use the ECP and exam guide. For detailed technical derivations, revisit the earlier weekly lectures. The three integrated mock exams above are original practice questions, not leaked or official examination questions."]}, {"id": 11, "title": "SAR & LiDAR Processing", "category": "DEPTH MATERIAL", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Week11_Study_Guide_RETRY.pdf", "pages": 8, "sections": [{"title": "1. Lecture identity and learning objectives", "html": "<p>Slides 1–9; VTT 00:00:18–00:03:00</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Stuart Phinn describes a recorded lecture about processing two active remote sensing datasets: imaging radar (SAR) and LiDAR. The deck is labelled Lecture 12 even though this study sequence calls it Week 11. The stated structure is radar basics, preprocessing, mapping, modelling, multitemporal analysis, and LiDAR processing.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Do not treat this as a repeat of terrestrial LiDAR structural modelling. This session emphasises what raw observations contain and how correction and processing produce usable mapping variables.</p>", "text": "Slides 1–9; VTT 00:00:18–00:03:00 Lecture content Stuart Phinn describes a recorded lecture about processing two active remote sensing datasets: imaging radar (SAR) and LiDAR. The deck is labelled Lecture 12 even though this study sequence calls it Week 11. The stated structure is radar basics, preprocessing, mapping, modelling, multitemporal analysis, and LiDAR processing. Scientific explanation and exam application Do not treat this as a repeat of terrestrial LiDAR structural modelling. This session emphasises what raw observations contain and how correction and processing produce usable mapping variables."}, {"title": "2. Passive optical versus active microwave", "html": "<p>Slides 10–14; VTT ~00:03–00:12</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>SAR transmits microwave energy and records a portion of the returned signal. Optical reflective sensors depend on solar illumination; SAR supplies its own illumination and can observe in darkness. The lecture emphasises access to data under cloud conditions, although precipitation and environmental state can still affect measurements.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>SAR intensity depends on scattering geometry and dielectric properties, not visible colour. A bright radar pixel need not correspond to a bright optical pixel. State the physical interaction whenever interpreting a radar feature.</p>", "text": "Slides 10–14; VTT ~00:03–00:12 Lecture content SAR transmits microwave energy and records a portion of the returned signal. Optical reflective sensors depend on solar illumination; SAR supplies its own illumination and can observe in darkness. The lecture emphasises access to data under cloud conditions, although precipitation and environmental state can still affect measurements. Scientific explanation and exam application SAR intensity depends on scattering geometry and dielectric properties, not visible colour. A bright radar pixel need not correspond to a bright optical pixel. State the physical interaction whenever interpreting a radar feature."}, {"title": "3. Synthetic aperture and slant range", "html": "<p>Slides 11–15; supplementary physics</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The radar antenna illuminates ground targets from the side. The measured delay determines slant range. Platform motion and coherent processing synthesise a longer aperture to improve azimuth resolution. These principles explain why SAR imagery differs geometrically from a nadir-looking photograph.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Supplementary calculation: with two-way travel time Δt=20 microseconds, slant range R=cΔt/2≈3000 m using c≈3×10^8 m/s. This is an illustrative equation, not a numerical example quoted from the slides. Ground range is not identical to slant range.</p>", "text": "Slides 11–15; supplementary physics Lecture content The radar antenna illuminates ground targets from the side. The measured delay determines slant range. Platform motion and coherent processing synthesise a longer aperture to improve azimuth resolution. These principles explain why SAR imagery differs geometrically from a nadir-looking photograph. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary calculation: with two-way travel time Δt=20 microseconds, slant range R=cΔt/2≈3000 m using c≈3×10^8 m/s. This is an illustrative equation, not a numerical example quoted from the slides. Ground range is not identical to slant range."}, {"title": "4. Radar wavelength bands and penetration", "html": "<p>Slides 14–19; VTT ~00:08–00:13</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecture compares microwave bands and wavelengths, including common C-, L- and X-band radar applications. Different wavelengths interact with vegetation, soil and surface structures differently. Longer wavelengths can interact with larger canopy and woody components; shorter wavelengths often respond more strongly to smaller elements and surface roughness.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Avoid the oversimplification that longer wavelength always reaches the ground. Penetration depends on canopy density, moisture, incidence angle, polarisation and target structure. A cross-sensor comparison requires wavelength compatibility or explicit modelling.</p>", "text": "Slides 14–19; VTT ~00:08–00:13 Lecture content The lecture compares microwave bands and wavelengths, including common C-, L- and X-band radar applications. Different wavelengths interact with vegetation, soil and surface structures differently. Longer wavelengths can interact with larger canopy and woody components; shorter wavelengths often respond more strongly to smaller elements and surface roughness. Scientific explanation and exam application Avoid the oversimplification that longer wavelength always reaches the ground. Penetration depends on canopy density, moisture, incidence angle, polarisation and target structure. A cross-sensor comparison requires wavelength compatibility or explicit modelling."}, {"title": "5. Polarisation and scattering mechanisms", "html": "<p>Slides 20–24; VTT ~00:10:53–00:15:00</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The deck introduces HH, HV, VH and VV polarisations and scattering mechanisms. Co-polarised returns use the same transmit and receive orientation; cross-polarised returns switch orientation. Surface, volume and double-bounce interactions can produce distinct responses.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Smooth open water commonly returns little energy toward a side-looking sensor and may appear dark; vegetation volume scattering and building-ground double bounce can produce stronger returns. This is conditional, not a universal lookup table. The speaker uses a loose analogy between polarisations and wavelengths; physically, polarisation is orientation of the electric field, not a different wavelength.</p>", "text": "Slides 20–24; VTT ~00:10:53–00:15:00 Lecture content The deck introduces HH, HV, VH and VV polarisations and scattering mechanisms. Co-polarised returns use the same transmit and receive orientation; cross-polarised returns switch orientation. Surface, volume and double-bounce interactions can produce distinct responses. Scientific explanation and exam application Smooth open water commonly returns little energy toward a side-looking sensor and may appear dark; vegetation volume scattering and building-ground double bounce can produce stronger returns. This is conditional, not a universal lookup table. The speaker uses a loose analogy between polarisations and wavelengths; physically, polarisation is orientation of the electric field, not a different wavelength."}, {"title": "6. Why radar images have different textures", "html": "<p>Slides 17–24; VTT ~00:12:57–00:14:30</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecturer points to speckle and strong reflectors in example SAR imagery. Radar brightness can be controlled by local slope, moisture, roughness and scattering mechanism. Adjacent radar pixels often vary strongly even over relatively homogeneous cover.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Do not interpret all bright speckles as individual trees or all dark patches as water. Analyse acquisition geometry, spatial pattern, contextual information and polarisation before assigning ecological meaning.</p>", "text": "Slides 17–24; VTT ~00:12:57–00:14:30 Lecture content The lecturer points to speckle and strong reflectors in example SAR imagery. Radar brightness can be controlled by local slope, moisture, roughness and scattering mechanism. Adjacent radar pixels often vary strongly even over relatively homogeneous cover. Scientific explanation and exam application Do not interpret all bright speckles as individual trees or all dark patches as water. Analyse acquisition geometry, spatial pattern, contextual information and polarisation before assigning ecological meaning."}, {"title": "7. SAR geometry: layover", "html": "<p>Slides 28–36; VTT 00:34:40–00:35:46</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Layover occurs when the top of a tall or steep target is recorded at a shorter slant range than its base, reversing their apparent spatial order. The lecturer explicitly explains the mountaintop return arriving before the base return.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Layover is not a simple positional offset that can be perfectly recovered from one image. In severely overlapped returns, different ground locations contribute to the same image geometry; terrain correction can flag the problem but cannot reconstruct information never independently observed.</p>", "text": "Slides 28–36; VTT 00:34:40–00:35:46 Lecture content Layover occurs when the top of a tall or steep target is recorded at a shorter slant range than its base, reversing their apparent spatial order. The lecturer explicitly explains the mountaintop return arriving before the base return. Scientific explanation and exam application Layover is not a simple positional offset that can be perfectly recovered from one image. In severely overlapped returns, different ground locations contribute to the same image geometry; terrain correction can flag the problem but cannot reconstruct information never independently observed."}, {"title": "8. SAR geometry: foreshortening", "html": "<p>Slides 29–36; VTT 00:35:46–00:36:52</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>A slope facing the radar can occupy a compressed distance in slant-range imagery. The lecturer describes the slope being squashed or pushed toward the sensor. Unlike layover, the ordering of features can remain correct, but spacing is distorted.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Foreshortening changes spatial representation and can alter local backscatter intensity. Interpret slopes only after geometric/terrain processing and consider local incidence angle. Compare ascending and descending views when terrain visibility matters.</p>", "text": "Slides 29–36; VTT 00:35:46–00:36:52 Lecture content A slope facing the radar can occupy a compressed distance in slant-range imagery. The lecturer describes the slope being squashed or pushed toward the sensor. Unlike layover, the ordering of features can remain correct, but spacing is distorted. Scientific explanation and exam application Foreshortening changes spatial representation and can alter local backscatter intensity. Interpret slopes only after geometric/terrain processing and consider local incidence angle. Compare ascending and descending views when terrain visibility matters."}, {"title": "9. SAR geometry: shadow", "html": "<p>Slides 29–36; VTT 00:36:01–00:36:52</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Radar shadow occurs behind terrain or tall objects when the transmitted pulse cannot illuminate the surface, so little or no useful return is recorded. The lecture identifies these as no-data areas.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A radar-shadow pixel is not necessarily open water, cleared forest or low biomass. Mask shadow in thematic modelling and avoid interpreting it as a genuine environmental state. Alternate look directions may improve coverage.</p>", "text": "Slides 29–36; VTT 00:36:01–00:36:52 Lecture content Radar shadow occurs behind terrain or tall objects when the transmitted pulse cannot illuminate the surface, so little or no useful return is recorded. The lecture identifies these as no-data areas. Scientific explanation and exam application A radar-shadow pixel is not necessarily open water, cleared forest or low biomass. Mask shadow in thematic modelling and avoid interpreting it as a genuine environmental state. Alternate look directions may improve coverage."}, {"title": "10. Terrain correction and incidence angle", "html": "<p>Slides 28, 35–36; VTT ~00:37–00:38</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The slides explicitly list geometric correction requirements, including removal of incidence-angle variations and correction of geometric distortions. A DEM and sensor orbit geometry are typically required to geocode SAR data into a map projection.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Terrain correction improves map location and geometry but does not automatically remove all radiometric slope effects or recover layover/shadow information. Separate geocoding, radiometric terrain flattening and incidence-angle normalisation in a workflow defence.</p>", "text": "Slides 28, 35–36; VTT ~00:37–00:38 Lecture content The slides explicitly list geometric correction requirements, including removal of incidence-angle variations and correction of geometric distortions. A DEM and sensor orbit geometry are typically required to geocode SAR data into a map projection. Scientific explanation and exam application Terrain correction improves map location and geometry but does not automatically remove all radiometric slope effects or recover layover/shadow information. Separate geocoding, radiometric terrain flattening and incidence-angle normalisation in a workflow defence."}, {"title": "11. Speckle: why it exists", "html": "<p>Slide 37; VTT 00:38:23 onward</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Speckle is a characteristic granular variation in coherent SAR imagery arising from interference among returns from multiple scatterers within a resolution cell. The lecture explicitly names speckle reduction as a radiometric preprocessing challenge.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Speckle is not identical to additive camera noise. It can obscure boundaries and inflate variance, yet aggressive filtering can blur small features. Choose a filter/multilooking strategy matched to the mapping unit and validate that relevant features survive.</p>", "text": "Slide 37; VTT 00:38:23 onward Lecture content Speckle is a characteristic granular variation in coherent SAR imagery arising from interference among returns from multiple scatterers within a resolution cell. The lecture explicitly names speckle reduction as a radiometric preprocessing challenge. Scientific explanation and exam application Speckle is not identical to additive camera noise. It can obscure boundaries and inflate variance, yet aggressive filtering can blur small features. Choose a filter/multilooking strategy matched to the mapping unit and validate that relevant features survive."}, {"title": "12. Speckle filtering and trade-offs", "html": "<p>Slide 37; supplementary explanation</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Multilooking and spatial adaptive filters can reduce speckle at the cost of spatial detail or altered texture. Temporal averaging may help where repeated comparable acquisitions are available. These methods are common processing context beyond the slide outline.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>For a 10 m wide mangrove fringe, a large moving window may erase the very target you want to map. Report effective resolution after filtering, not merely nominal source pixel spacing. Preserve a copy of the original calibrated image for checking artefacts.</p>", "text": "Slide 37; supplementary explanation Lecture content Multilooking and spatial adaptive filters can reduce speckle at the cost of spatial detail or altered texture. Temporal averaging may help where repeated comparable acquisitions are available. These methods are common processing context beyond the slide outline. Scientific explanation and exam application For a 10 m wide mangrove fringe, a large moving window may erase the very target you want to map. Report effective resolution after filtering, not merely nominal source pixel spacing. Preserve a copy of the original calibrated image for checking artefacts."}, {"title": "13. Calibrating SAR backscatter", "html": "<p>Slides 35–40; supplementary terminology</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>SAR quantitative analysis requires calibrated measurements and a clear convention for backscatter, often sigma-nought (σ⁰), beta-nought (β⁰) or gamma-nought (γ⁰), depending on product and geometry. The slides discuss radiometric corrections and analysis-ready SAR data but do not specify one universal recipe for all sensors.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Supplementary conversion: dB=10 log10(linear power ratio). A linear backscatter value of 0.01 corresponds to −20 dB. Never subtract or average dB values as though they were linear powers without checking the intended operation. Keep calibration units and normalisation convention consistent across dates.</p>", "text": "Slides 35–40; supplementary terminology Lecture content SAR quantitative analysis requires calibrated measurements and a clear convention for backscatter, often sigma-nought (σ⁰), beta-nought (β⁰) or gamma-nought (γ⁰), depending on product and geometry. The slides discuss radiometric corrections and analysis-ready SAR data but do not specify one universal recipe for all sensors. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary conversion: dB=10 log10(linear power ratio). A linear backscatter value of 0.01 corresponds to −20 dB. Never subtract or average dB values as though they were linear powers without checking the intended operation. Keep calibration units and normalisation convention consistent across dates."}, {"title": "14. A defensible SAR preprocessing sequence", "html": "<p>Slides 25–41</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The deck lists radar processing software, geometric correction, speckle reduction and analysis-ready data resources. Processing choices depend on the sensor, level and task; the slides do not mandate one sequence for every mission.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A defensible Sentinel-1-style example is: choose compatible acquisitions/product levels → apply orbit and radiometric calibration where needed → address thermal/border noise if relevant → speckle handling → terrain correction/normalisation → common grid and masks → independent validation. Clearly mark this sequence as a general worked workflow, not a verbatim lecture algorithm.</p>", "text": "Slides 25–41 Lecture content The deck lists radar processing software, geometric correction, speckle reduction and analysis-ready data resources. Processing choices depend on the sensor, level and task; the slides do not mandate one sequence for every mission. Scientific explanation and exam application A defensible Sentinel-1-style example is: choose compatible acquisitions/product levels → apply orbit and radiometric calibration where needed → address thermal/border noise if relevant → speckle handling → terrain correction/normalisation → common grid and masks → independent validation. Clearly mark this sequence as a general worked workflow, not a verbatim lecture algorithm."}, {"title": "15. SAR classification and mapping", "html": "<p>Slides 42–47</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecture introduces grouping pixels by radar signatures and surveys applications/platforms including Sentinel-1, NovaSAR, Capella and TerraSAR-X. Radar mapping may use intensity, polarisation, texture, ancillary terrain and temporal features.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Radar class separation is not equivalent to optical spectral separation. Moisture and incidence angle can cause the same class to vary; different classes can share backscatter levels. A classifier needs representative training and independent validation across environmental conditions.</p>", "text": "Slides 42–47 Lecture content The lecture introduces grouping pixels by radar signatures and surveys applications/platforms including Sentinel-1, NovaSAR, Capella and TerraSAR-X. Radar mapping may use intensity, polarisation, texture, ancillary terrain and temporal features. Scientific explanation and exam application Radar class separation is not equivalent to optical spectral separation. Moisture and incidence angle can cause the same class to vary; different classes can share backscatter levels. A classifier needs representative training and independent validation across environmental conditions."}, {"title": "16. Empirical radar modelling", "html": "<p>Slides 48–49</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The slides identify empirical approaches for estimating environmental properties from radar observations. Such models relate backscatter and other radar-derived features to field-measured quantities, often with additional covariates.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A correlation between HV backscatter and biomass may saturate at high biomass and vary by forest type, moisture or radar band. Report units, calibration range, residuals and spatially independent validation. Avoid equating a backscatter increase with biomass growth without considering other mechanisms.</p>", "text": "Slides 48–49 Lecture content The slides identify empirical approaches for estimating environmental properties from radar observations. Such models relate backscatter and other radar-derived features to field-measured quantities, often with additional covariates. Scientific explanation and exam application A correlation between HV backscatter and biomass may saturate at high biomass and vary by forest type, moisture or radar band. Report units, calibration range, residuals and spatially independent validation. Avoid equating a backscatter increase with biomass growth without considering other mechanisms."}, {"title": "17. Multitemporal radar comparisons", "html": "<p>Slides 50–55</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecture emphasises correction requirements for multitemporal SAR analysis and shows forest clearing, illegal logging, oil-spill and agricultural applications. Temporal comparisons can exploit radar acquisitions during cloudy periods when optical data are limited.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Require compatible acquisition mode, orbit/look direction, polarisation, wavelength, incidence geometry, calibration and terrain correction. Rainfall-driven soil/canopy moisture changes can mimic structural change. Use controls, temporal context and independent evidence to interpret a backscatter change.</p>", "text": "Slides 50–55 Lecture content The lecture emphasises correction requirements for multitemporal SAR analysis and shows forest clearing, illegal logging, oil-spill and agricultural applications. Temporal comparisons can exploit radar acquisitions during cloudy periods when optical data are limited. Scientific explanation and exam application Require compatible acquisition mode, orbit/look direction, polarisation, wavelength, incidence geometry, calibration and terrain correction. Rainfall-driven soil/canopy moisture changes can mimic structural change. Use controls, temporal context and independent evidence to interpret a backscatter change."}, {"title": "18. Original worked radar change example", "html": "<p>Slides 50–55; supplementary calculation</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Suppose calibrated linear backscatter changes from 0.020 to 0.010 for a pixel. The linear ratio is 0.5 and the dB change is 10 log10(0.010/0.020)=−3.01 dB. This is a decrease in radar return, not automatically a 50% decline in canopy cover.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Before interpreting, check weather/moisture, geometry, calibration, speckle and land-cover context. If the pixel is near a steep slope, shadow and layover masks are essential. If the change is consistent over multiple dates and independently observed, a disturbance interpretation becomes stronger.</p>", "text": "Slides 50–55; supplementary calculation Lecture content Suppose calibrated linear backscatter changes from 0.020 to 0.010 for a pixel. The linear ratio is 0.5 and the dB change is 10 log10(0.010/0.020)=−3.01 dB. This is a decrease in radar return, not automatically a 50% decline in canopy cover. Scientific explanation and exam application Before interpreting, check weather/moisture, geometry, calibration, speckle and land-cover context. If the pixel is near a steep slope, shadow and layover masks are essential. If the change is consistent over multiple dates and independently observed, a disturbance interpretation becomes stronger."}, {"title": "19. Transition to LiDAR processing", "html": "<p>Slides 56–63; VTT ~00:46–00:52</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The second half of the lecture introduces LiDAR processing and discusses terrestrial, airborne and satellite observations. LiDAR transmits laser pulses and estimates ranges, building three-dimensional point clouds or vertically resolved return profiles.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>LiDAR and SAR are both active but record different interactions. LiDAR primarily resolves geometry and vertical structure; radar measures microwave scattering related to geometry and dielectric properties. Their complementary outputs can support vegetation structure analysis.</p>", "text": "Slides 56–63; VTT ~00:46–00:52 Lecture content The second half of the lecture introduces LiDAR processing and discusses terrestrial, airborne and satellite observations. LiDAR transmits laser pulses and estimates ranges, building three-dimensional point clouds or vertically resolved return profiles. Scientific explanation and exam application LiDAR and SAR are both active but record different interactions. LiDAR primarily resolves geometry and vertical structure; radar measures microwave scattering related to geometry and dielectric properties. Their complementary outputs can support vegetation structure analysis."}, {"title": "20. Discrete-return versus full-waveform LiDAR", "html": "<p>Slides 64–67; VTT 00:47:50–00:56:08</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The slides explicitly compare full-waveform and discrete-return LiDAR. Discrete-return systems record selected peaks, while full-waveform systems retain a more complete profile of returned energy along the pulse path. The lecturer discusses canopy, mid-storey and ground contributions.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A waveform is not simply a dense point cloud. Waveform processing can reveal vertical distributions and weak returns but depends on instrument response, pulse width, signal processing and occlusion. Discrete returns are convenient for point-cloud analysis but discard part of the signal shape.</p>", "text": "Slides 64–67; VTT 00:47:50–00:56:08 Lecture content The slides explicitly compare full-waveform and discrete-return LiDAR. Discrete-return systems record selected peaks, while full-waveform systems retain a more complete profile of returned energy along the pulse path. The lecturer discusses canopy, mid-storey and ground contributions. Scientific explanation and exam application A waveform is not simply a dense point cloud. Waveform processing can reveal vertical distributions and weak returns but depends on instrument response, pulse width, signal processing and occlusion. Discrete returns are convenient for point-cloud analysis but discard part of the signal shape."}, {"title": "21. LiDAR point-cloud data and LAS", "html": "<p>Slides 68–71</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecture introduces LAS file formats and LiDAR software. A point-cloud record can store coordinates and attributes such as intensity, return number, classification and scan information, depending on version and instrument.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Check coordinate reference system, vertical datum, point density, classification scheme, scan angle and return information before producing a raster. A LAS file containing XYZ coordinates is not automatically ground-classified or height-normalised.</p>", "text": "Slides 68–71 Lecture content The lecture introduces LAS file formats and LiDAR software. A point-cloud record can store coordinates and attributes such as intensity, return number, classification and scan information, depending on version and instrument. Scientific explanation and exam application Check coordinate reference system, vertical datum, point density, classification scheme, scan angle and return information before producing a raster. A LAS file containing XYZ coordinates is not automatically ground-classified or height-normalised."}, {"title": "22. Ground classification and DTM", "html": "<p>Slides 72–75</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>LiDAR processing distinguishes ground returns from vegetation and built structures to derive a digital terrain model (DTM). The deck presents DTM and terrain-slope outputs alongside imagery.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A DTM models bare-earth terrain and depends on ground-point availability and interpolation. Dense canopy or water can cause sparse/biased ground returns. Validate the terrain model with checkpoints and report interpolation uncertainty, particularly under forest.</p>", "text": "Slides 72–75 Lecture content LiDAR processing distinguishes ground returns from vegetation and built structures to derive a digital terrain model (DTM). The deck presents DTM and terrain-slope outputs alongside imagery. Scientific explanation and exam application A DTM models bare-earth terrain and depends on ground-point availability and interpolation. Dense canopy or water can cause sparse/biased ground returns. Validate the terrain model with checkpoints and report interpolation uncertainty, particularly under forest."}, {"title": "23. DSM, canopy height and normalisation", "html": "<p>Slides 73–77</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecture presents derived surfaces and maximum-height products. A DSM represents upper surfaces; a canopy height model is commonly formed by subtracting a co-registered DTM from a DSM. Height-normalised points subtract terrain elevation from each point.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Supplementary example: DSM 34.2 m minus DTM 11.7 m gives canopy height 22.5 m. If the two surfaces use different vertical datums or grids, the difference may be spurious. A CHM is not a direct measurement of biomass or species composition.</p>", "text": "Slides 73–77 Lecture content The lecture presents derived surfaces and maximum-height products. A DSM represents upper surfaces; a canopy height model is commonly formed by subtracting a co-registered DTM from a DSM. Height-normalised points subtract terrain elevation from each point. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary example: DSM 34.2 m minus DTM 11.7 m gives canopy height 22.5 m. If the two surfaces use different vertical datums or grids, the difference may be spurious. A CHM is not a direct measurement of biomass or species composition."}, {"title": "24. LiDAR intensity and fractional cover", "html": "<p>Slides 73–77</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The deck displays LiDAR-derived intensity, fractional-cover count and maximum-height products. Return counts and canopy-height distributions can help characterise canopy structure.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Intensity is affected by range, scan angle, wavelength and target properties; it should not be interpreted as calibrated reflectance without appropriate processing. Fractional cover from return counts requires explicit definitions and corrections for occlusion, sampling geometry and point density.</p>", "text": "Slides 73–77 Lecture content The deck displays LiDAR-derived intensity, fractional-cover count and maximum-height products. Return counts and canopy-height distributions can help characterise canopy structure. Scientific explanation and exam application Intensity is affected by range, scan angle, wavelength and target properties; it should not be interpreted as calibrated reflectance without appropriate processing. Fractional cover from return counts requires explicit definitions and corrections for occlusion, sampling geometry and point density."}, {"title": "25. Sampling density, scale and sensor choice", "html": "<p>Slides 60–63</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>The lecturer compares LiDAR platforms and the practical question of when LiDAR is appropriate. Ground, airborne and spaceborne systems differ in coverage, point density, cost and measurement geometry.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>A terrestrial scanner can capture fine stem structure but suffers occlusion; airborne LiDAR covers landscapes but may miss some understory; spaceborne sampling offers broad geographical reach but sparse footprints. Select the platform based on target structure, area, required uncertainty and budget.</p>", "text": "Slides 60–63 Lecture content The lecturer compares LiDAR platforms and the practical question of when LiDAR is appropriate. Ground, airborne and spaceborne systems differ in coverage, point density, cost and measurement geometry. Scientific explanation and exam application A terrestrial scanner can capture fine stem structure but suffers occlusion; airborne LiDAR covers landscapes but may miss some understory; spaceborne sampling offers broad geographical reach but sparse footprints. Select the platform based on target structure, area, required uncertainty and budget."}, {"title": "26. Integrated SAR workflow critique", "html": "<p>Original exam scenario based on slides 25–55</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>A team compares Sentinel-1 VV from ascending orbit in 2022 with VH from descending orbit in 2025, applies no terrain correction, labels all dark pixels as clearing and reports forest-loss area to the nearest square metre.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Critique: incompatible polarisation and look direction, terrain-induced geometry and shadow, moisture differences, speckle and no independent validation. Redesign using matched acquisitions, calibrated comparable backscatter, geometry masks, repeated observations, reference samples and uncertainty-aware area reporting.</p>", "text": "Original exam scenario based on slides 25–55 Lecture content A team compares Sentinel-1 VV from ascending orbit in 2022 with VH from descending orbit in 2025, applies no terrain correction, labels all dark pixels as clearing and reports forest-loss area to the nearest square metre. Scientific explanation and exam application Critique: incompatible polarisation and look direction, terrain-induced geometry and shadow, moisture differences, speckle and no independent validation. Redesign using matched acquisitions, calibrated comparable backscatter, geometry masks, repeated observations, reference samples and uncertainty-aware area reporting."}, {"title": "27. Integrated LiDAR workflow critique", "html": "<p>Original exam scenario based on slides 56–77</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>A team calculates canopy height by subtracting a DTM in ellipsoidal heights from a DSM in orthometric heights, assumes every first return is canopy, and converts height directly to tonnes of biomass without field calibration.</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Critique: inconsistent vertical datums, non-vegetation first returns, possible classification errors and missing allometric/empirical calibration. Harmonise vertical reference, classify points, derive reliable terrain/canopy surfaces, calibrate against field biomass and independently validate.</p>", "text": "Original exam scenario based on slides 56–77 Lecture content A team calculates canopy height by subtracting a DTM in ellipsoidal heights from a DSM in orthometric heights, assumes every first return is canopy, and converts height directly to tonnes of biomass without field calibration. Scientific explanation and exam application Critique: inconsistent vertical datums, non-vegetation first returns, possible classification errors and missing allometric/empirical calibration. Harmonise vertical reference, classify points, derive reliable terrain/canopy surfaces, calibrate against field biomass and independently validate."}, {"title": "28. Exam questions and answer anchors", "html": "<p>Slides 10–77</p><h3>Lecture content</h3><p>Q1 Why is SAR useful under clouds? Q2 What distinguishes polarisation from wavelength? Q3 How do layover, foreshortening and shadow differ? Q4 What causes speckle? Q5 Why is terrain correction not enough to recover shadow? Q6 What must match for SAR change detection? Q7 What is a LiDAR waveform? Q8 How do DTM, DSM and CHM differ?</p><h3>Scientific explanation and exam application</h3><p>Answers: active microwave transmission; electric-field orientation versus frequency; reordered/compressed/unilluminated geometry; coherent interference; absent observation; sensor geometry and calibration plus environmental conditions; return-energy profile; ground surface, upper surface and their difference. In an exam, connect each answer to an error source and mitigation.</p>", "text": "Slides 10–77 Lecture content Q1 Why is SAR useful under clouds? Q2 What distinguishes polarisation from wavelength? Q3 How do layover, foreshortening and shadow differ? Q4 What causes speckle? Q5 Why is terrain correction not enough to recover shadow? Q6 What must match for SAR change detection? Q7 What is a LiDAR waveform? Q8 How do DTM, DSM and CHM differ? Scientific explanation and exam application Answers: active microwave transmission; electric-field orientation versus frequency; reordered/compressed/unilluminated geometry; coherent interference; absent observation; sensor geometry and calibration plus environmental conditions; return-energy profile; ground surface, upper surface and their difference. In an exam, connect each answer to an error source and mitigation."}], "text": ["1. Lecture identity and learning objectives Slides 1–9; VTT 00:00:18–00:03:00 Lecture content Stuart Phinn describes a recorded lecture about processing two active remote sensing datasets: imaging radar (SAR) and LiDAR. The deck is labelled Lecture 12 even though this study sequence calls it Week 11. The stated structure is radar basics, preprocessing, mapping, modelling, multitemporal analysis, and LiDAR processing. Scientific explanation and exam application Do not treat this as a repeat of terrestrial LiDAR structural modelling. This session emphasises what raw observations contain and how correction and processing produce usable mapping variables.", "2. Passive optical versus active microwave Slides 10–14; VTT ~00:03–00:12 Lecture content SAR transmits microwave energy and records a portion of the returned signal. Optical reflective sensors depend on solar illumination; SAR supplies its own illumination and can observe in darkness. The lecture emphasises access to data under cloud conditions, although precipitation and environmental state can still affect measurements. Scientific explanation and exam application SAR intensity depends on scattering geometry and dielectric properties, not visible colour. A bright radar pixel need not correspond to a bright optical pixel. State the physical interaction whenever interpreting a radar feature.", "3. Synthetic aperture and slant range Slides 11–15; supplementary physics Lecture content The radar antenna illuminates ground targets from the side. The measured delay determines slant range. Platform motion and coherent processing synthesise a longer aperture to improve azimuth resolution. These principles explain why SAR imagery differs geometrically from a nadir-looking photograph. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary calculation: with two-way travel time Δt=20 microseconds, slant range R=cΔt/2≈3000 m using c≈3×10^8 m/s. This is an illustrative equation, not a numerical example quoted from the slides. Ground range is not identical to slant range.", "4. Radar wavelength bands and penetration Slides 14–19; VTT ~00:08–00:13 Lecture content The lecture compares microwave bands and wavelengths, including common C-, L- and X-band radar applications. Different wavelengths interact with vegetation, soil and surface structures differently. Longer wavelengths can interact with larger canopy and woody components; shorter wavelengths often respond more strongly to smaller elements and surface roughness. Scientific explanation and exam application Avoid the oversimplification that longer wavelength always reaches the ground. Penetration depends on canopy density, moisture, incidence angle, polarisation and target structure. A cross-sensor comparison requires wavelength compatibility or explicit modelling.", "5. Polarisation and scattering mechanisms Slides 20–24; VTT ~00:10:53–00:15:00 Lecture content The deck introduces HH, HV, VH and VV polarisations and scattering mechanisms. Co-polarised returns use the same transmit and receive orientation; cross-polarised returns switch orientation. Surface, volume and double-bounce interactions can produce distinct responses. Scientific explanation and exam application Smooth open water commonly returns little energy toward a side-looking sensor and may appear dark; vegetation volume scattering and building-ground double bounce can produce stronger returns. This is conditional, not a universal lookup table. The speaker uses a loose analogy between polarisations and wavelengths; physically, polarisation is orientation of the electric field, not a different wavelength.", "6. Why radar images have different textures Slides 17–24; VTT ~00:12:57–00:14:30 Lecture content The lecturer points to speckle and strong reflectors in example SAR imagery. Radar brightness can be controlled by local slope, moisture, roughness and scattering mechanism. Adjacent radar pixels often vary strongly even over relatively homogeneous cover. Scientific explanation and exam application Do not interpret all bright speckles as individual trees or all dark patches as water. Analyse acquisition geometry, spatial pattern, contextual information and polarisation before assigning ecological meaning.", "7. SAR geometry: layover Slides 28–36; VTT 00:34:40–00:35:46 Lecture content Layover occurs when the top of a tall or steep target is recorded at a shorter slant range than its base, reversing their apparent spatial order. The lecturer explicitly explains the mountaintop return arriving before the base return. Scientific explanation and exam application Layover is not a simple positional offset that can be perfectly recovered from one image. In severely overlapped returns, different ground locations contribute to the same image geometry; terrain correction can flag the problem but cannot reconstruct information never independently observed.", "8. SAR geometry: foreshortening Slides 29–36; VTT 00:35:46–00:36:52 Lecture content A slope facing the radar can occupy a compressed distance in slant-range imagery. The lecturer describes the slope being squashed or pushed toward the sensor. Unlike layover, the ordering of features can remain correct, but spacing is distorted. Scientific explanation and exam application Foreshortening changes spatial representation and can alter local backscatter intensity. Interpret slopes only after geometric/terrain processing and consider local incidence angle. Compare ascending and descending views when terrain visibility matters.", "9. SAR geometry: shadow Slides 29–36; VTT 00:36:01–00:36:52 Lecture content Radar shadow occurs behind terrain or tall objects when the transmitted pulse cannot illuminate the surface, so little or no useful return is recorded. The lecture identifies these as no-data areas. Scientific explanation and exam application A radar-shadow pixel is not necessarily open water, cleared forest or low biomass. Mask shadow in thematic modelling and avoid interpreting it as a genuine environmental state. Alternate look directions may improve coverage.", "10. Terrain correction and incidence angle Slides 28, 35–36; VTT ~00:37–00:38 Lecture content The slides explicitly list geometric correction requirements, including removal of incidence-angle variations and correction of geometric distortions. A DEM and sensor orbit geometry are typically required to geocode SAR data into a map projection. Scientific explanation and exam application Terrain correction improves map location and geometry but does not automatically remove all radiometric slope effects or recover layover/shadow information. Separate geocoding, radiometric terrain flattening and incidence-angle normalisation in a workflow defence.", "11. Speckle: why it exists Slide 37; VTT 00:38:23 onward Lecture content Speckle is a characteristic granular variation in coherent SAR imagery arising from interference among returns from multiple scatterers within a resolution cell. The lecture explicitly names speckle reduction as a radiometric preprocessing challenge. Scientific explanation and exam application Speckle is not identical to additive camera noise. It can obscure boundaries and inflate variance, yet aggressive filtering can blur small features. Choose a filter/multilooking strategy matched to the mapping unit and validate that relevant features survive.", "12. Speckle filtering and trade-offs Slide 37; supplementary explanation Lecture content Multilooking and spatial adaptive filters can reduce speckle at the cost of spatial detail or altered texture. Temporal averaging may help where repeated comparable acquisitions are available. These methods are common processing context beyond the slide outline. Scientific explanation and exam application For a 10 m wide mangrove fringe, a large moving window may erase the very target you want to map. Report effective resolution after filtering, not merely nominal source pixel spacing. Preserve a copy of the original calibrated image for checking artefacts.", "13. Calibrating SAR backscatter Slides 35–40; supplementary terminology Lecture content SAR quantitative analysis requires calibrated measurements and a clear convention for backscatter, often sigma-nought (σ⁰), beta-nought (β⁰) or gamma-nought (γ⁰), depending on product and geometry. The slides discuss radiometric corrections and analysis-ready SAR data but do not specify one universal recipe for all sensors. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary conversion: dB=10 log10(linear power ratio). A linear backscatter value of 0.01 corresponds to −20 dB. Never subtract or average dB values as though they were linear powers without checking the intended operation. Keep calibration units and normalisation convention consistent across dates.", "14. A defensible SAR preprocessing sequence Slides 25–41 Lecture content The deck lists radar processing software, geometric correction, speckle reduction and analysis-ready data resources. Processing choices depend on the sensor, level and task; the slides do not mandate one sequence for every mission. Scientific explanation and exam application A defensible Sentinel-1-style example is: choose compatible acquisitions/product levels → apply orbit and radiometric calibration where needed → address thermal/border noise if relevant → speckle handling → terrain correction/normalisation → common grid and masks → independent validation. Clearly mark this sequence as a general worked workflow, not a verbatim lecture algorithm.", "15. SAR classification and mapping Slides 42–47 Lecture content The lecture introduces grouping pixels by radar signatures and surveys applications/platforms including Sentinel-1, NovaSAR, Capella and TerraSAR-X. Radar mapping may use intensity, polarisation, texture, ancillary terrain and temporal features. Scientific explanation and exam application Radar class separation is not equivalent to optical spectral separation. Moisture and incidence angle can cause the same class to vary; different classes can share backscatter levels. A classifier needs representative training and independent validation across environmental conditions.", "16. Empirical radar modelling Slides 48–49 Lecture content The slides identify empirical approaches for estimating environmental properties from radar observations. Such models relate backscatter and other radar-derived features to field-measured quantities, often with additional covariates. Scientific explanation and exam application A correlation between HV backscatter and biomass may saturate at high biomass and vary by forest type, moisture or radar band. Report units, calibration range, residuals and spatially independent validation. Avoid equating a backscatter increase with biomass growth without considering other mechanisms.", "17. Multitemporal radar comparisons Slides 50–55 Lecture content The lecture emphasises correction requirements for multitemporal SAR analysis and shows forest clearing, illegal logging, oil-spill and agricultural applications. Temporal comparisons can exploit radar acquisitions during cloudy periods when optical data are limited. Scientific explanation and exam application Require compatible acquisition mode, orbit/look direction, polarisation, wavelength, incidence geometry, calibration and terrain correction. Rainfall-driven soil/canopy moisture changes can mimic structural change. Use controls, temporal context and independent evidence to interpret a backscatter change.", "18. Original worked radar change example Slides 50–55; supplementary calculation Lecture content Suppose calibrated linear backscatter changes from 0.020 to 0.010 for a pixel. The linear ratio is 0.5 and the dB change is 10 log10(0.010/0.020)=−3.01 dB. This is a decrease in radar return, not automatically a 50% decline in canopy cover. Scientific explanation and exam application Before interpreting, check weather/moisture, geometry, calibration, speckle and land-cover context. If the pixel is near a steep slope, shadow and layover masks are essential. If the change is consistent over multiple dates and independently observed, a disturbance interpretation becomes stronger.", "19. Transition to LiDAR processing Slides 56–63; VTT ~00:46–00:52 Lecture content The second half of the lecture introduces LiDAR processing and discusses terrestrial, airborne and satellite observations. LiDAR transmits laser pulses and estimates ranges, building three-dimensional point clouds or vertically resolved return profiles. Scientific explanation and exam application LiDAR and SAR are both active but record different interactions. LiDAR primarily resolves geometry and vertical structure; radar measures microwave scattering related to geometry and dielectric properties. Their complementary outputs can support vegetation structure analysis.", "20. Discrete-return versus full-waveform LiDAR Slides 64–67; VTT 00:47:50–00:56:08 Lecture content The slides explicitly compare full-waveform and discrete-return LiDAR. Discrete-return systems record selected peaks, while full-waveform systems retain a more complete profile of returned energy along the pulse path. The lecturer discusses canopy, mid-storey and ground contributions. Scientific explanation and exam application A waveform is not simply a dense point cloud. Waveform processing can reveal vertical distributions and weak returns but depends on instrument response, pulse width, signal processing and occlusion. Discrete returns are convenient for point-cloud analysis but discard part of the signal shape.", "21. LiDAR point-cloud data and LAS Slides 68–71 Lecture content The lecture introduces LAS file formats and LiDAR software. A point-cloud record can store coordinates and attributes such as intensity, return number, classification and scan information, depending on version and instrument. Scientific explanation and exam application Check coordinate reference system, vertical datum, point density, classification scheme, scan angle and return information before producing a raster. A LAS file containing XYZ coordinates is not automatically ground-classified or height-normalised.", "22. Ground classification and DTM Slides 72–75 Lecture content LiDAR processing distinguishes ground returns from vegetation and built structures to derive a digital terrain model (DTM). The deck presents DTM and terrain-slope outputs alongside imagery. Scientific explanation and exam application A DTM models bare-earth terrain and depends on ground-point availability and interpolation. Dense canopy or water can cause sparse/biased ground returns. Validate the terrain model with checkpoints and report interpolation uncertainty, particularly under forest.", "23. DSM, canopy height and normalisation Slides 73–77 Lecture content The lecture presents derived surfaces and maximum-height products. A DSM represents upper surfaces; a canopy height model is commonly formed by subtracting a co-registered DTM from a DSM. Height-normalised points subtract terrain elevation from each point. Scientific explanation and exam application Supplementary example: DSM 34.2 m minus DTM 11.7 m gives canopy height 22.5 m. If the two surfaces use different vertical datums or grids, the difference may be spurious. A CHM is not a direct measurement of biomass or species composition.", "24. LiDAR intensity and fractional cover Slides 73–77 Lecture content The deck displays LiDAR-derived intensity, fractional-cover count and maximum-height products. Return counts and canopy-height distributions can help characterise canopy structure. Scientific explanation and exam application Intensity is affected by range, scan angle, wavelength and target properties; it should not be interpreted as calibrated reflectance without appropriate processing. Fractional cover from return counts requires explicit definitions and corrections for occlusion, sampling geometry and point density.", "25. Sampling density, scale and sensor choice Slides 60–63 Lecture content The lecturer compares LiDAR platforms and the practical question of when LiDAR is appropriate. Ground, airborne and spaceborne systems differ in coverage, point density, cost and measurement geometry. Scientific explanation and exam application A terrestrial scanner can capture fine stem structure but suffers occlusion; airborne LiDAR covers landscapes but may miss some understory; spaceborne sampling offers broad geographical reach but sparse footprints. Select the platform based on target structure, area, required uncertainty and budget.", "26. Integrated SAR workflow critique Original exam scenario based on slides 25–55 Lecture content A team compares Sentinel-1 VV from ascending orbit in 2022 with VH from descending orbit in 2025, applies no terrain correction, labels all dark pixels as clearing and reports forest-loss area to the nearest square metre. Scientific explanation and exam application Critique: incompatible polarisation and look direction, terrain-induced geometry and shadow, moisture differences, speckle and no independent validation. Redesign using matched acquisitions, calibrated comparable backscatter, geometry masks, repeated observations, reference samples and uncertainty-aware area reporting.", "27. Integrated LiDAR workflow critique Original exam scenario based on slides 56–77 Lecture content A team calculates canopy height by subtracting a DTM in ellipsoidal heights from a DSM in orthometric heights, assumes every first return is canopy, and converts height directly to tonnes of biomass without field calibration. Scientific explanation and exam application Critique: inconsistent vertical datums, non-vegetation first returns, possible classification errors and missing allometric/empirical calibration. Harmonise vertical reference, classify points, derive reliable terrain/canopy surfaces, calibrate against field biomass and independently validate.", "28. Exam questions and answer anchors Slides 10–77 Lecture content Q1 Why is SAR useful under clouds? Q2 What distinguishes polarisation from wavelength? Q3 How do layover, foreshortening and shadow differ? Q4 What causes speckle? Q5 Why is terrain correction not enough to recover shadow? Q6 What must match for SAR change detection? Q7 What is a LiDAR waveform? Q8 How do DTM, DSM and CHM differ? Scientific explanation and exam application Answers: active microwave transmission; electric-field orientation versus frequency; reordered/compressed/unilluminated geometry; coherent interference; absent observation; sensor geometry and calibration plus environmental conditions; return-energy profile; ground surface, upper surface and their difference. In an exam, connect each answer to an error source and mitigation."]}, {"id": 12, "title": "Coral reef habitat mapping · Mock exam 2", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Mock_2_coral_reef_habitat_mapping.pdf", "pages": 25, "sections": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A coastal agency needs a 5 m map of benthic habitat (coral-dominated, algae-dominated, sand and seagrass) across shallow reefs, including turbid lagoons, for protected-area planning. Available: Planet Dove mosaics, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, bathymetry, 1 m2 underwater photoquadrats from two field seasons, tide records and a limited field budget. Deliverable: mapped classes, area estimates, class-level uncertainty and a clear statement of where bottom mapping is not possible.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst chooses the brightest Planet scene without checking tide, cloud or glint; applies a standard terrestrial atmospheric correction and treats the resulting pixel values as bottom reflectance; calculates NDVI for underwater vegetation; trains a four-class random forest using 40 photoquadrats from one clear-water reef, assigning each 1 m2 photo label directly to a 5 m pixel; randomly splits adjacent pixels 80:20; reports 96% overall accuracy; applies the classifier to deep and turbid lagoons; smooths the output until coral polygons look continuous; reports precise reef-wide coral area with no confidence interval.</p>", "text": "A coastal agency needs a 5 m map of benthic habitat (coral-dominated, algae-dominated, sand and seagrass) across shallow reefs, including turbid lagoons, for protected-area planning. Available: Planet Dove mosaics, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, bathymetry, 1 m2 underwater photoquadrats from two field seasons, tide records and a limited field budget. Deliverable: mapped classes, area estimates, class-level uncertainty and a clear statement of where bottom mapping is not possible. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst chooses the brightest Planet scene without checking tide, cloud or glint; applies a standard terrestrial atmospheric correction and treats the resulting pixel values as bottom reflectance; calculates NDVI for underwater vegetation; trains a four-class random forest using 40 photoquadrats from one clear-water reef, assigning each 1 m2 photo label directly to a 5 m pixel; randomly splits adjacent pixels 80:20; reports 96% overall accuracy; applies the classifier to deep and turbid lagoons; smooths the output until coral polygons look continuous; reports precise reef-wide coral area with no confidence interval."}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p><details><summary>Answer 1: Image choice and glint/tide (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Bright water can be surface glint; tide changes path length and exposure. Selecting on visual brightness biases the signal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Select low-cloud/low-glint scenes and comparable tides; inspect per-scene quality, use masking or tested glint correction.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Atmospheric versus water-column correction (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Atmospheric surface reflectance is not bottom reflectance. Water absorption/scattering and depth modify bottom signal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Assess optical depth, turbidity and bathymetry; apply justified water-column treatment or restrict to reliable shallow-water domain.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Index and target mismatch (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> NDVI is a land-vegetation index; NIR is strongly attenuated by water and is not a robust underwater seagrass indicator.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use visible-band and depth-aware predictors; justify bands and test them against reference data.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Training footprint and representativeness (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A 1 m2 photo does not necessarily represent a 25 m2 pixel; one clear-water reef cannot represent turbid/deep gradients.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Aggregate photo observations appropriately, record geolocation/footprint and stratify sampling by reef, depth, turbidity and class.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Leaky validation (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Random neighbouring-pixel split shares spatial structure and potentially training reference, inflating accuracy.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Hold out independent reef sites/field campaigns and use probability-based reference samples.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Accuracy and map area (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Overall accuracy hides coral commission/omission; smoothing can erase small true patches and generate false coral area.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report per-class user/producer accuracy, confusion matrix, sampling design, area-adjusted area/uncertainty where appropriate; preserve raw and cleaned outputs.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Extrapolation outside observability (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Deep/turbid water may contain no measurable bottom signal, making classifications unjustified.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Mask no-data/low-confidence zones; report mapped domain and do not label unobservable bottom.</p></details>", "text": "Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Image choice and glint/tide (5 marks)Problem: Bright water can be surface glint; tide changes path length and exposure. Selecting on visual brightness biases the signal.Correction: Select low-cloud/low-glint scenes and comparable tides; inspect per-scene quality, use masking or tested glint correction. Answer 2: Atmospheric versus water-column correction (5 marks)Problem: Atmospheric surface reflectance is not bottom reflectance. Water absorption/scattering and depth modify bottom signal.Correction: Assess optical depth, turbidity and bathymetry; apply justified water-column treatment or restrict to reliable shallow-water domain. Answer 3: Index and target mismatch (4 marks)Problem: NDVI is a land-vegetation index; NIR is strongly attenuated by water and is not a robust underwater seagrass indicator.Correction: Use visible-band and depth-aware predictors; justify bands and test them against reference data. Answer 4: Training footprint and representativeness (5 marks)Problem: A 1 m2 photo does not necessarily represent a 25 m2 pixel; one clear-water reef cannot represent turbid/deep gradients.Correction: Aggregate photo observations appropriately, record geolocation/footprint and stratify sampling by reef, depth, turbidity and class. Answer 5: Leaky validation (5 marks)Problem: Random neighbouring-pixel split shares spatial structure and potentially training reference, inflating accuracy.Correction: Hold out independent reef sites/field campaigns and use probability-based reference samples. Answer 6: Accuracy and map area (5 marks)Problem: Overall accuracy hides coral commission/omission; smoothing can erase small true patches and generate false coral area.Correction: Report per-class user/producer accuracy, confusion matrix, sampling design, area-adjusted area/uncertainty where appropriate; preserve raw and cleaned outputs. Answer 7: Extrapolation outside observability (6 marks)Problem: Deep/turbid water may contain no measurable bottom signal, making classifications unjustified.Correction: Mask no-data/low-confidence zones; report mapped domain and do not label unobservable bottom."}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p><details><summary>Stage 1: Target and sampling frame</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define benthic classes, mixed-pixel rule, depth/clarity domain, map date and minimum mapping unit.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A single dominant class is simpler than fractional cover but hides mixtures.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Data and sensor choice</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use suitable low-glint high-resolution optical scenes; Sentinel-2 provides spectral/contextual checks, bathymetry and tide data constrain interpretation.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Planet spatial detail is valuable, but limited spectral bands and cross-date mosaics complicate radiometry.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Cloud/shadow/glint masking; geometric coregistration; suitable atmospheric correction; quality control of water-column/depth effects; consistent grid.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Glint correction can introduce negative/biased values; masking severe glint may be safer.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Predictors and modelling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use visible spectral bands, ratios, bathymetry/depth, geomorphic zones, texture/context; test RF and object-based rules.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> RF handles nonlinear predictors; OBIA may help reef zones but segmentation scale can merge small patches.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Reference design</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent geolocated photoquadrats, footprint aggregation and stratification across reef/depth/turbidity/classes; reconcile labels.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Photo interpretation and automated labels both carry uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Validation and reporting</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent confusion matrix, coral user/producer accuracy, confidence intervals, class-wise and depth-wise performance; mask unreliable zones.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Area estimates from mapped labels alone are not unbiased if class errors are substantial.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Final communication</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Map class definitions, no-observation zones, acquisition dates, uncertainty, validation protocol and limitations.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A useful management map includes confidence and mapping domain, not only attractive colours.</p></details>", "text": "B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Target and sampling frameRecommended: Define benthic classes, mixed-pixel rule, depth/clarity domain, map date and minimum mapping unit.Defence and alternatives: A single dominant class is simpler than fractional cover but hides mixtures. Stage 2: Data and sensor choiceRecommended: Use suitable low-glint high-resolution optical scenes; Sentinel-2 provides spectral/contextual checks, bathymetry and tide data constrain interpretation.Defence and alternatives: Planet spatial detail is valuable, but limited spectral bands and cross-date mosaics complicate radiometry. Stage 3: PreprocessingRecommended: Cloud/shadow/glint masking; geometric coregistration; suitable atmospheric correction; quality control of water-column/depth effects; consistent grid.Defence and alternatives: Glint correction can introduce negative/biased values; masking severe glint may be safer. Stage 4: Predictors and modellingRecommended: Use visible spectral bands, ratios, bathymetry/depth, geomorphic zones, texture/context; test RF and object-based rules.Defence and alternatives: RF handles nonlinear predictors; OBIA may help reef zones but segmentation scale can merge small patches. Stage 5: Reference designRecommended: Independent geolocated photoquadrats, footprint aggregation and stratification across reef/depth/turbidity/classes; reconcile labels.Defence and alternatives: Photo interpretation and automated labels both carry uncertainty. Stage 6: Validation and reportingRecommended: Independent confusion matrix, coral user/producer accuracy, confidence intervals, class-wise and depth-wise performance; mask unreliable zones.Defence and alternatives: Area estimates from mapped labels alone are not unbiased if class errors are substantial. Stage 7: Final communicationRecommended: Map class definitions, no-observation zones, acquisition dates, uncertainty, validation protocol and limitations.Defence and alternatives: A useful management map includes confidence and mapping domain, not only attractive colours."}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Accuracy calculation (10 marks)</h3><p>Mapped coral/reference coral=36, mapped coral/reference non-coral=12, mapped non-coral/reference coral=9, mapped non-coral/reference non-coral=43. Compute OA, coral UA, coral PA and corresponding commission/omission rates; state matrix orientation.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Total=100; OA=(36+43)/100=79%; coral UA=36/(36+12)=75%, commission=25%; coral PA=36/(36+9)=80%, omission=20%. Rows mapped, columns reference. A single OA is insufficient for coral-specific decisions.</p></details><h3>C2 — Resolution and observation (5 marks)</h3><p>Explain why resampling Sentinel-2 to 5 m does not make it equivalent to Planet 5 m data.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Resampling creates more grid cells but not finer native spatial information or point-spread characteristics; the sensors also differ in band response, acquisition conditions and radiometry.</p></details><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (5 marks)</h3><p>Explain why training a benthic classifier on automated photo labels may create two coupled error sources.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>The photo-label model has its own domain shift and misclassification; the satellite model then learns noisy labels and can be evaluated against the same erroneous proxy. Independently validate photo annotation, audit ambiguous classes and propagate reference uncertainty.</p></details>", "text": "C1 — Accuracy calculation (10 marks) Mapped coral/reference coral=36, mapped coral/reference non-coral=12, mapped non-coral/reference coral=9, mapped non-coral/reference non-coral=43. Compute OA, coral UA, coral PA and corresponding commission/omission rates; state matrix orientation. Reveal worked answerTotal=100; OA=(36+43)/100=79%; coral UA=36/(36+12)=75%, commission=25%; coral PA=36/(36+9)=80%, omission=20%. Rows mapped, columns reference. A single OA is insufficient for coral-specific decisions. C2 — Resolution and observation (5 marks) Explain why resampling Sentinel-2 to 5 m does not make it equivalent to Planet 5 m data. Reveal worked answerResampling creates more grid cells but not finer native spatial information or point-spread characteristics; the sensors also differ in band response, acquisition conditions and radiometry. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (5 marks) Explain why training a benthic classifier on automated photo labels may create two coupled error sources. Reveal worked answerThe photo-label model has its own domain shift and misclassification; the satellite model then learns noisy labels and can be evaluated against the same erroneous proxy. Independently validate photo annotation, audit ambiguous classes and propagate reference uncertainty."}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}], "text": ["Scenario A coastal agency needs a 5 m map of benthic habitat (coral-dominated, algae-dominated, sand and seagrass) across shallow reefs, including turbid lagoons, for protected-area planning. Available: Planet Dove mosaics, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, bathymetry, 1 m2 underwater photoquadrats from two field seasons, tide records and a limited field budget. Deliverable: mapped classes, area estimates, class-level uncertainty and a clear statement of where bottom mapping is not possible. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst chooses the brightest Planet scene without checking tide, cloud or glint; applies a standard terrestrial atmospheric correction and treats the resulting pixel values as bottom reflectance; calculates NDVI for underwater vegetation; trains a four-class random forest using 40 photoquadrats from one clear-water reef, assigning each 1 m2 photo label directly to a 5 m pixel; randomly splits adjacent pixels 80:20; reports 96% overall accuracy; applies the classifier to deep and turbid lagoons; smooths the output until coral polygons look continuous; reports precise reef-wide coral area with no confidence interval.", "A — Critique (35 marks) Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Image choice and glint/tide (5 marks)Problem: Bright water can be surface glint; tide changes path length and exposure. Selecting on visual brightness biases the signal.Correction: Select low-cloud/low-glint scenes and comparable tides; inspect per-scene quality, use masking or tested glint correction. Answer 2: Atmospheric versus water-column correction (5 marks)Problem: Atmospheric surface reflectance is not bottom reflectance. Water absorption/scattering and depth modify bottom signal.Correction: Assess optical depth, turbidity and bathymetry; apply justified water-column treatment or restrict to reliable shallow-water domain. Answer 3: Index and target mismatch (4 marks)Problem: NDVI is a land-vegetation index; NIR is strongly attenuated by water and is not a robust underwater seagrass indicator.Correction: Use visible-band and depth-aware predictors; justify bands and test them against reference data. Answer 4: Training footprint and representativeness (5 marks)Problem: A 1 m2 photo does not necessarily represent a 25 m2 pixel; one clear-water reef cannot represent turbid/deep gradients.Correction: Aggregate photo observations appropriately, record geolocation/footprint and stratify sampling by reef, depth, turbidity and class. Answer 5: Leaky validation (5 marks)Problem: Random neighbouring-pixel split shares spatial structure and potentially training reference, inflating accuracy.Correction: Hold out independent reef sites/field campaigns and use probability-based reference samples. Answer 6: Accuracy and map area (5 marks)Problem: Overall accuracy hides coral commission/omission; smoothing can erase small true patches and generate false coral area.Correction: Report per-class user/producer accuracy, confusion matrix, sampling design, area-adjusted area/uncertainty where appropriate; preserve raw and cleaned outputs. Answer 7: Extrapolation outside observability (6 marks)Problem: Deep/turbid water may contain no measurable bottom signal, making classifications unjustified.Correction: Mask no-data/low-confidence zones; report mapped domain and do not label unobservable bottom.", "B — Design and defend (45 marks) B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Target and sampling frameRecommended: Define benthic classes, mixed-pixel rule, depth/clarity domain, map date and minimum mapping unit.Defence and alternatives: A single dominant class is simpler than fractional cover but hides mixtures. Stage 2: Data and sensor choiceRecommended: Use suitable low-glint high-resolution optical scenes; Sentinel-2 provides spectral/contextual checks, bathymetry and tide data constrain interpretation.Defence and alternatives: Planet spatial detail is valuable, but limited spectral bands and cross-date mosaics complicate radiometry. Stage 3: PreprocessingRecommended: Cloud/shadow/glint masking; geometric coregistration; suitable atmospheric correction; quality control of water-column/depth effects; consistent grid.Defence and alternatives: Glint correction can introduce negative/biased values; masking severe glint may be safer. Stage 4: Predictors and modellingRecommended: Use visible spectral bands, ratios, bathymetry/depth, geomorphic zones, texture/context; test RF and object-based rules.Defence and alternatives: RF handles nonlinear predictors; OBIA may help reef zones but segmentation scale can merge small patches. Stage 5: Reference designRecommended: Independent geolocated photoquadrats, footprint aggregation and stratification across reef/depth/turbidity/classes; reconcile labels.Defence and alternatives: Photo interpretation and automated labels both carry uncertainty. Stage 6: Validation and reportingRecommended: Independent confusion matrix, coral user/producer accuracy, confidence intervals, class-wise and depth-wise performance; mask unreliable zones.Defence and alternatives: Area estimates from mapped labels alone are not unbiased if class errors are substantial. Stage 7: Final communicationRecommended: Map class definitions, no-observation zones, acquisition dates, uncertainty, validation protocol and limitations.Defence and alternatives: A useful management map includes confidence and mapping domain, not only attractive colours.", "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks) C1 — Accuracy calculation (10 marks) Mapped coral/reference coral=36, mapped coral/reference non-coral=12, mapped non-coral/reference coral=9, mapped non-coral/reference non-coral=43. Compute OA, coral UA, coral PA and corresponding commission/omission rates; state matrix orientation. Reveal worked answerTotal=100; OA=(36+43)/100=79%; coral UA=36/(36+12)=75%, commission=25%; coral PA=36/(36+9)=80%, omission=20%. Rows mapped, columns reference. A single OA is insufficient for coral-specific decisions. C2 — Resolution and observation (5 marks) Explain why resampling Sentinel-2 to 5 m does not make it equivalent to Planet 5 m data. Reveal worked answerResampling creates more grid cells but not finer native spatial information or point-spread characteristics; the sensors also differ in band response, acquisition conditions and radiometry. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (5 marks) Explain why training a benthic classifier on automated photo labels may create two coupled error sources. Reveal worked answerThe photo-label model has its own domain shift and misclassification; the satellite model then learns noisy labels and can be evaluated against the same erroneous proxy. Independently validate photo annotation, audit ambiguous classes and propagate reference uncertainty.", "Self-marking guide 85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A coastal agency needs a 5 m map of benthic habitat (coral-dominated, algae-dominated, sand and seagrass) across shallow reefs, including turbid lagoons, for protected-area planning. Available: Planet Dove mosaics, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, bathymetry, 1 m2 underwater photoquadrats from two field seasons, tide records and a limited field budget. Deliverable: mapped classes, area estimates, class-level uncertainty and a clear statement of where bottom mapping is not possible.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst chooses the brightest Planet scene without checking tide, cloud or glint; applies a standard terrestrial atmospheric correction and treats the resulting pixel values as bottom reflectance; calculates NDVI for underwater vegetation; trains a four-class random forest using 40 photoquadrats from one clear-water reef, assigning each 1 m2 photo label directly to a 5 m pixel; randomly splits adjacent pixels 80:20; reports 96% overall accuracy; applies the classifier to deep and turbid lagoons; smooths the output until coral polygons look continuous; reports precise reef-wide coral area with no confidence interval.</p>"}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Accuracy calculation (10 marks)</h3><p>Mapped coral/reference coral=36, mapped coral/reference non-coral=12, mapped non-coral/reference coral=9, mapped non-coral/reference non-coral=43. Compute OA, coral UA, coral PA and corresponding commission/omission rates; state matrix orientation.</p><h3>C2 — Resolution and observation (5 marks)</h3><p>Explain why resampling Sentinel-2 to 5 m does not make it equivalent to Planet 5 m data.</p><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (5 marks)</h3><p>Explain why training a benthic classifier on automated photo labels may create two coupled error sources.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Answer 1: Image choice and glint/tide (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Bright water can be surface glint; tide changes path length and exposure. Selecting on visual brightness biases the signal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Select low-cloud/low-glint scenes and comparable tides; inspect per-scene quality, use masking or tested glint correction.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Atmospheric versus water-column correction (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Atmospheric surface reflectance is not bottom reflectance. Water absorption/scattering and depth modify bottom signal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Assess optical depth, turbidity and bathymetry; apply justified water-column treatment or restrict to reliable shallow-water domain.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Index and target mismatch (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> NDVI is a land-vegetation index; NIR is strongly attenuated by water and is not a robust underwater seagrass indicator.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use visible-band and depth-aware predictors; justify bands and test them against reference data.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Training footprint and representativeness (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A 1 m2 photo does not necessarily represent a 25 m2 pixel; one clear-water reef cannot represent turbid/deep gradients.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Aggregate photo observations appropriately, record geolocation/footprint and stratify sampling by reef, depth, turbidity and class.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Leaky validation (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Random neighbouring-pixel split shares spatial structure and potentially training reference, inflating accuracy.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Hold out independent reef sites/field campaigns and use probability-based reference samples.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Accuracy and map area (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Overall accuracy hides coral commission/omission; smoothing can erase small true patches and generate false coral area.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report per-class user/producer accuracy, confusion matrix, sampling design, area-adjusted area/uncertainty where appropriate; preserve raw and cleaned outputs.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Extrapolation outside observability (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Deep/turbid water may contain no measurable bottom signal, making classifications unjustified.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Mask no-data/low-confidence zones; report mapped domain and do not label unobservable bottom.</p></details>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Stage 1: Target and sampling frame</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define benthic classes, mixed-pixel rule, depth/clarity domain, map date and minimum mapping unit.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A single dominant class is simpler than fractional cover but hides mixtures.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Data and sensor choice</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use suitable low-glint high-resolution optical scenes; Sentinel-2 provides spectral/contextual checks, bathymetry and tide data constrain interpretation.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Planet spatial detail is valuable, but limited spectral bands and cross-date mosaics complicate radiometry.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Cloud/shadow/glint masking; geometric coregistration; suitable atmospheric correction; quality control of water-column/depth effects; consistent grid.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Glint correction can introduce negative/biased values; masking severe glint may be safer.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Predictors and modelling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use visible spectral bands, ratios, bathymetry/depth, geomorphic zones, texture/context; test RF and object-based rules.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> RF handles nonlinear predictors; OBIA may help reef zones but segmentation scale can merge small patches.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Reference design</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent geolocated photoquadrats, footprint aggregation and stratification across reef/depth/turbidity/classes; reconcile labels.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Photo interpretation and automated labels both carry uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Validation and reporting</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent confusion matrix, coral user/producer accuracy, confidence intervals, class-wise and depth-wise performance; mask unreliable zones.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Area estimates from mapped labels alone are not unbiased if class errors are substantial.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Final communication</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Map class definitions, no-observation zones, acquisition dates, uncertainty, validation protocol and limitations.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A useful management map includes confidence and mapping domain, not only attractive colours.</p></details>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Total=100; OA=(36+43)/100=79%; coral UA=36/(36+12)=75%, commission=25%; coral PA=36/(36+9)=80%, omission=20%. Rows mapped, columns reference. A single OA is insufficient for coral-specific decisions.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Resampling creates more grid cells but not finer native spatial information or point-spread characteristics; the sensors also differ in band response, acquisition conditions and radiometry.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>The photo-label model has its own domain shift and misclassification; the satellite model then learns noisy labels and can be evaluated against the same erroneous proxy. Independently validate photo annotation, audit ambiguous classes and propagate reference uncertainty.</p></details>"}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}]}, {"id": 13, "title": "Mangrove disturbance · Mock exam 1", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/14-GEOM7001_Mock_Exam_With_Full_Answers.pdf", "pages": 7, "sections": [{"title": "Instructions and scenario", "html": "<p>Attempt all questions. Prioritise correct reasoning, consequences, corrections and justified alternatives.</p><p>A coastal management agency asks you to assess whether a major 2015–2016 disturbance reduced mangrove canopy condition across a 180 km coastal region, and whether satellite indicators offered earlier warning than a conventional greenness index. It needs a spatial map, a defensible time series, and an uncertainty statement. Budget allows existing satellite products and a limited number of field visits. Historical field measurements are sparse. The region includes tidal flats, narrow mangrove fringes, seasonal cloud, and some pixels with mixed land and water.</p><h3>Proposed flawed workflow</h3><ol><li>The analyst selects one cloud-free image from August 2014 and one from February 2016 and calls the difference “disturbance impact”.</li><li>They mix Landsat 8 surface reflectance with Sentinel-2 top-of-atmosphere reflectance without harmonisation, because both products contain red and NIR bands.</li><li>They resample Landsat 30 m data to 10 m and claim this creates 10 m spatial detail.</li><li>They compute NDVI from every pixel, including tidal water and cloud shadows, and treat every decline of 0.08 as damaged mangrove.</li><li>They use the same field polygons to train and verify a random-forest classifier, reporting 97% overall accuracy.</li><li>They select reference sites only beside accessible roads and assume they represent the entire coastline.</li><li>They compare two classified maps and report a 4% mangrove-area loss without evaluating class-specific or change-map errors.</li><li>They use a red-edge index from Sentinel-2 for 2013, although the available Sentinel-2 mission record does not cover that date.</li><li>They call a low NDVI anomaly proof of physiological stress and identify drought as the cause without testing alternatives.</li><li>They deliver maps without dates, scale, units, valid-pixel counts, methods log or uncertainty bounds.</li></ol>", "text": "Attempt all questions. Prioritise correct reasoning, consequences, corrections and justified alternatives. A coastal management agency asks you to assess whether a major 2015–2016 disturbance reduced mangrove canopy condition across a 180 km coastal region, and whether satellite indicators offered earlier warning than a conventional greenness index. It needs a spatial map, a defensible time series, and an uncertainty statement. Budget allows existing satellite products and a limited number of field visits. Historical field measurements are sparse. The region includes tidal flats, narrow mangrove fringes, seasonal cloud, and some pixels with mixed land and water. Proposed flawed workflow The analyst selects one cloud-free image from August 2014 and one from February 2016 and calls the difference “disturbance impact”.They mix Landsat 8 surface reflectance with Sentinel-2 top-of-atmosphere reflectance without harmonisation, because both products contain red and NIR bands.They resample Landsat 30 m data to 10 m and claim this creates 10 m spatial detail.They compute NDVI from every pixel, including tidal water and cloud shadows, and treat every decline of 0.08 as damaged mangrove.They use the same field polygons to train and verify a random-forest classifier, reporting 97% overall accuracy.They select reference sites only beside accessible roads and assume they represent the entire coastline.They compare two classified maps and report a 4% mangrove-area loss without evaluating class-specific or change-map errors.They use a red-edge index from Sentinel-2 for 2013, although the available Sentinel-2 mission record does not cover that date.They call a low NDVI anomaly proof of physiological stress and identify drought as the cause without testing alternatives.They deliver maps without dates, scale, units, valid-pixel counts, methods log or uncertainty bounds."}, {"title": "Section A — Critique (40 marks)", "html": "<p>A1 (24 marks) Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.</p><p>A2 (8 marks) Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.</p><p>A3 (8 marks) The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.</p>", "text": "A1 (24 marks) Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices. A2 (8 marks) Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design. A3 (8 marks) The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change."}, {"title": "Section B — Design (40 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 (10 marks) Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.</p><p>B2 (14 marks) Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.</p><p>B3 (10 marks) Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.</p><p>B4 (6 marks) Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.</p>", "text": "B1 (10 marks) Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative. B2 (14 marks) Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls. B3 (10 marks) Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting. B4 (6 marks) Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality."}, {"title": "Section C — Applied concepts (20 marks)", "html": "<p>C1 (8 marks) Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.</p><p>Matrix (rows=map, columns=reference): [42,8] for mapped mangrove; [6,44] for mapped other.</p><p>C2 (6 marks) Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.</p><p>C3 (6 marks) A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.</p>", "text": "C1 (8 marks) Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors. Matrix (rows=map, columns=reference): [42,8] for mapped mangrove; [6,44] for mapped other. C2 (6 marks) Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour. C3 (6 marks) A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute."}, {"title": "Fully worked model answers", "html": "<p>Open each question to reveal the model response and mark guide.</p><details><summary>A1 — 24 marks: Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.</summary><h3>Priority 1 — invalid temporal comparison</h3><p>A single August-to-February difference confounds seasonal phenology, tidal conditions, clouds and an event with real long-term disturbance. Use seasonally matched multi-year observations, valid-pixel quality screening and pre-event variability to contextualise post-event anomalies. [4 marks]</p><h3>Priority 2 — radiometric/sensor inconsistency</h3><p>Surface reflectance and top-of-atmosphere reflectance are different physical processing levels; sensor bandpasses also differ. Artificial differences can be misread as vegetation change. Use consistently corrected products, sensor-specific indices or validated cross-sensor harmonisation; test stable reference sites. [4]</p><h3>Priority 3 — invalid mangrove domain and threshold</h3><p>Unmasked water, tidal inundation and shadow distort NDVI; a universal −0.08 threshold lacks evidence relative to noise and seasonal variability. Apply stable mangrove extent and water/cloud/shadow/tide masks, derive anomaly thresholds from baseline variability, and test sensitivity. [4]</p><h3>Priority 4 — non-independent validation</h3><p>Training and testing on the same polygons measures fit to seen labels and biases accuracy upward. Split by independent spatial sites or hold out entire field locations; ensure reference dates and class definitions match imagery. [4]</p><h3>Priority 5 — wrong historical sensor availability</h3><p>Sentinel-2 cannot supply a 2013 red-edge observation. This creates a non-existent time series and an invalid lead-time comparison. Use Landsat 8 for the historical period, begin Sentinel-2 red-edge analysis only after its acquisition record starts, and compare overlapping dates cautiously. [4]</p><h3>Priority 6 — unsupported interpretation and uncertainty</h3><p>NDVI anomaly is not a unique drought or physiological-stress signature, and a 4% mapped change may be smaller than classification uncertainty. Triangulate independent ecological/weather evidence, report alternative causes, class-specific errors and uncertainty intervals. [4]</p><h3>Other valid flaws</h3><p>Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not create new spatial information; roadside reference sites introduce selection bias; lack of provenance, legends and valid-pixel counts harms reproducibility. Full marks may be awarded for any six well-prioritised, mechanistically explained flaws and specific remedies.</p></details><details><summary>A2 — 8 marks: Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.</summary><h3>Why 97% is not defensible</h3><p>Reusing training polygons for testing is data leakage and produces optimistic accuracy. Spatially adjacent training/test samples may also share autocorrelation; road-accessible plots underrepresent remote tidal mangroves. Overall accuracy hides class-specific false positives and false negatives. [3]</p><h3>Better design</h3><p>Define target classes and mapping unit; use probability-based stratified random reference sampling across mangrove/non-mangrove, disturbed/undisturbed and key depth/tidal or geographic strata; obtain independent, temporally aligned high-quality field or imagery labels; separate entire sites or spatial blocks from training. [3]</p><h3>Report</h3><p>Provide confusion matrix with map/reference orientation, user’s and producer’s accuracies, sample sizes, uncertainty intervals, and area-adjusted estimates if the sampling design requires them. Record ambiguous reference labels and support mismatch. [2]</p></details><details><summary>A3 — 8 marks: The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.</summary><h3>Why the inference fails</h3><p>90% overall accuracy on each date is not 90% accuracy of change; errors can compound, and overall accuracy may be dominated by stable common classes. A 4% net difference could reflect misclassification, inconsistent class definitions, co-registration or seasonality. [4]</p><h3>Change validation</h3><p>Sample stable and changed transition strata independently; check reference conditions at both dates; calculate transition-specific omission/commission, confusion matrix for change/no-change or transitions, design-weighted area estimates and confidence intervals; assess sensitivity to registration, masks and thresholds. Do not call the 4% change statistically significant without an appropriate uncertainty comparison. [4]</p></details><details><summary>B1 — 10 marks: Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.</summary><h3>Core products</h3><p>Use Landsat 8 calibrated surface reflectance for a consistent 2013–2017 baseline and event analysis, with its 30 m spatial footprint honestly retained. Landsat time-series coverage supports the historical question; QA bands support cloud/shadow masking. Use Sentinel-2 surface reflectance/red-edge indices only for the period actually available and for complementary later or overlapping analyses. [4]</p><h3>Resolution trade-offs</h3><p>Spatial: 30 m can mix narrow mangrove strips with water; exclude or flag mixed pixels and compare with higher-resolution reference. Spectral: red/NIR enable NDVI, red-edge only where supported. Radiometric: consistent reflectance and band definitions. Temporal: revisit and cloud gaps require compositing, seasonal alignment and valid-observation counts. [4]</p><h3>Defended alternative</h3><p>DEA Sandbox is attractive for repeatable Landsat products and large-area time series; ENVI can provide detailed image inspection and classification. Higher-resolution commercial imagery may improve narrow fringe delineation but can lack historical consistency, cost more, and demand additional harmonisation. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B2 — 14 marks: Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.</summary><h3>1. Define target and hypotheses</h3><p>Specify mapped mangrove domain, disturbance period, target index/anomaly, spatial unit, lead-time definition and comparison with unaffected areas. Controls ambiguous interpretation. [2]</p><h3>2. Assemble imagery and ancillary data</h3><p>Select product level, band definitions, dates, tide/weather data, mangrove mask and independent reference sources; audit data availability. Controls unavailable bands and mismatched dates. [2]</p><h3>3. Quality and geometry</h3><p>Use cloud/cloud-shadow/saturation masks, co-registration checks, common CRS/grid, tidal water handling and valid-count masks. Controls false change at edges and invalid pixels. [2]</p><h3>4. Radiometric/seasonal consistency</h3><p>Use comparable surface-reflectance products, inspect stable targets, align phenological windows and avoid unjustified cross-sensor merges. Controls illumination/atmosphere/seasonal artefacts. [2]</p><h3>5. Construct time series</h3><p>Calculate NDVI and selected indicators with explicit formulas and consistent masks; aggregate seasonally or fit seasonal baselines; record sample support. Controls uneven sampling and noise. [2]</p><h3>6. Quantify anomaly and disturbance</h3><p>Calculate pre-event mean/dispersion or seasonal models, compare event anomalies and persistence, create categorical flags only using justified thresholds. Controls arbitrary cut-offs. [2]</p><h3>7. Validate and communicate</h3><p>Use independent spatially representative observations, test continuous and categorical accuracy, sensitivity and uncertainty; deliver readable maps, tables, plots and processing log. Controls unsupported claims. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B3 — 10 marks: Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.</summary><h3>Continuous estimates</h3><p>For vegetation indices or modelled cover, compare to independent contemporaneous reference quantities where physically comparable; report bias, MAE/RMSE and residuals by cover/depth/region. An NDVI anomaly cannot be validated directly as a field-measured “stress” unit without an explicit ecological relationship. [3]</p><h3>Categorical flags</h3><p>Use independent stratified probability samples across predicted disturbed/undisturbed and reference conditions; report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, false-alarm/miss rates and uncertainty intervals; account for unequal sampling fractions in area estimates. [3]</p><h3>Support and design</h3><p>Align spatial footprint, temporal date and class definition; use spatial blocks/sites for independent holdout; distinguish mixed pixels, tidal conditions and imperfect reference data. [2]</p><h3>Robustness</h3><p>Test alternative seasonal windows, thresholds, baseline lengths, valid-count criteria, registration offsets and masks; show confidence/uncertainty and where evidence is missing. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B4 — 6 marks: Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.</summary><h3>Operational definition</h3><p>Define event onset independently (e.g. mapped disturbance/reference evidence), anomaly detection threshold, false-alarm rule and earliest sustained crossing date; calculate lead time relative to NDVI using the same pixels, observation dates and baseline protocol. [3]</p><h3>Fair comparison</h3><p>Assess detection rate, false positives, sensitivity to seasonal sampling and observation gaps; stratify affected versus unaffected areas and report uncertainty. An index that changes earlier only because it has more valid observations is not necessarily biologically earlier. [2]</p><h3>Inference limit</h3><p>Earlier statistical anomaly is a candidate warning signal, not proof of physiological stress or causal drought mechanism; independent ecology and climate evidence is needed. [1]</p></details><details><summary>C1 — 8 marks: Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.</summary><h3>Given matrix</h3><p>Rows are MAP predictions; columns are REFERENCE. Map mangrove: reference mangrove 42, reference other 8. Map other: reference mangrove 6, reference other 44. N=100. [matrix supplied]</p><h3>Overall accuracy</h3><p>(42+44)/100 = 86%. [2]</p><h3>Mangrove user’s accuracy</h3><p>42/(42+8)=84%; commission error=8/50=16%. Interprets reliability of pixels mapped as mangrove. [2]</p><h3>Mangrove producer’s accuracy</h3><p>42/(42+6)=87.5%; omission error=6/48=12.5%. Interprets proportion of reference mangrove correctly mapped. [2]</p><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>User’s accuracy conditions on mapped class; producer’s on reference class. State orientation, sample design and limitations of overall accuracy. [2]</p></details><details><summary>C2 — 6 marks: Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.</summary><h3>DEA Sandbox strengths</h3><p>Cloud/data-cube access to analysis-ready EO products and scalable repeatable temporal compositing; useful for long Landsat series and large geographic extents. [2]</p><h3>ENVI strengths</h3><p>Detailed interactive image inspection, spectral processing and supervised/unsupervised classification tools; convenient for experimenting with per-scene corrections and visual diagnostics. [2]</p><h3>Limits and division of labour</h3><p>Sandbox analyses require coding and awareness of product/algorithm defaults, and do not make poor sampling valid. ENVI desktop workflows may be laborious for very large multiyear stacks and require careful logging. Use Sandbox for repeatable time-series assembly, masking and anomaly extraction; ENVI for targeted diagnostic/classification work, with consistent preprocessing and validation across both. [2]</p></details><details><summary>C3 — 6 marks: A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.</summary><h3>LiDAR</h3><p>Adds direct 3D canopy/terrain structural measurements (e.g. height, vertical distribution), useful for biomass and structural change, but acquisition coverage and revisit frequency may be insufficient for 2013–2017 early-warning trajectories. [2]</p><h3>SAR</h3><p>Active microwave observations can penetrate clouds and provide complementary sensitivity to structure, moisture and inundation; backscatter depends on incidence angle, polarisation, roughness and water level, so calibration/terrain/temporal harmonisation remain essential. [2]</p><h3>Hyperspectral</h3><p>Dense narrow spectral bands can help distinguish pigments/biochemical properties under suitable calibration and canopy conditions, but coverage, cost, atmosphere, mixed pixels and validation limit use. These datasets complement rather than automatically replace consistent historical optical observations. [2]</p></details>", "text": "Open each question to reveal the model response and mark guide. A1 — 24 marks: Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.Priority 1 — invalid temporal comparisonA single August-to-February difference confounds seasonal phenology, tidal conditions, clouds and an event with real long-term disturbance. Use seasonally matched multi-year observations, valid-pixel quality screening and pre-event variability to contextualise post-event anomalies. [4 marks]Priority 2 — radiometric/sensor inconsistencySurface reflectance and top-of-atmosphere reflectance are different physical processing levels; sensor bandpasses also differ. Artificial differences can be misread as vegetation change. Use consistently corrected products, sensor-specific indices or validated cross-sensor harmonisation; test stable reference sites. [4]Priority 3 — invalid mangrove domain and thresholdUnmasked water, tidal inundation and shadow distort NDVI; a universal −0.08 threshold lacks evidence relative to noise and seasonal variability. Apply stable mangrove extent and water/cloud/shadow/tide masks, derive anomaly thresholds from baseline variability, and test sensitivity. [4]Priority 4 — non-independent validationTraining and testing on the same polygons measures fit to seen labels and biases accuracy upward. Split by independent spatial sites or hold out entire field locations; ensure reference dates and class definitions match imagery. [4]Priority 5 — wrong historical sensor availabilitySentinel-2 cannot supply a 2013 red-edge observation. This creates a non-existent time series and an invalid lead-time comparison. Use Landsat 8 for the historical period, begin Sentinel-2 red-edge analysis only after its acquisition record starts, and compare overlapping dates cautiously. [4]Priority 6 — unsupported interpretation and uncertaintyNDVI anomaly is not a unique drought or physiological-stress signature, and a 4% mapped change may be smaller than classification uncertainty. Triangulate independent ecological/weather evidence, report alternative causes, class-specific errors and uncertainty intervals. [4]Other valid flawsResampling 30 m to 10 m does not create new spatial information; roadside reference sites introduce selection bias; lack of provenance, legends and valid-pixel counts harms reproducibility. Full marks may be awarded for any six well-prioritised, mechanistically explained flaws and specific remedies. A2 — 8 marks: Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.Why 97% is not defensibleReusing training polygons for testing is data leakage and produces optimistic accuracy. Spatially adjacent training/test samples may also share autocorrelation; road-accessible plots underrepresent remote tidal mangroves. Overall accuracy hides class-specific false positives and false negatives. [3]Better designDefine target classes and mapping unit; use probability-based stratified random reference sampling across mangrove/non-mangrove, disturbed/undisturbed and key depth/tidal or geographic strata; obtain independent, temporally aligned high-quality field or imagery labels; separate entire sites or spatial blocks from training. [3]ReportProvide confusion matrix with map/reference orientation, user’s and producer’s accuracies, sample sizes, uncertainty intervals, and area-adjusted estimates if the sampling design requires them. Record ambiguous reference labels and support mismatch. [2] A3 — 8 marks: The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.Why the inference fails90% overall accuracy on each date is not 90% accuracy of change; errors can compound, and overall accuracy may be dominated by stable common classes. A 4% net difference could reflect misclassification, inconsistent class definitions, co-registration or seasonality. [4]Change validationSample stable and changed transition strata independently; check reference conditions at both dates; calculate transition-specific omission/commission, confusion matrix for change/no-change or transitions, design-weighted area estimates and confidence intervals; assess sensitivity to registration, masks and thresholds. Do not call the 4% change statistically significant without an appropriate uncertainty comparison. [4] B1 — 10 marks: Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.Core productsUse Landsat 8 calibrated surface reflectance for a consistent 2013–2017 baseline and event analysis, with its 30 m spatial footprint honestly retained. Landsat time-series coverage supports the historical question; QA bands support cloud/shadow masking. Use Sentinel-2 surface reflectance/red-edge indices only for the period actually available and for complementary later or overlapping analyses. [4]Resolution trade-offsSpatial: 30 m can mix narrow mangrove strips with water; exclude or flag mixed pixels and compare with higher-resolution reference. Spectral: red/NIR enable NDVI, red-edge only where supported. Radiometric: consistent reflectance and band definitions. Temporal: revisit and cloud gaps require compositing, seasonal alignment and valid-observation counts. [4]Defended alternativeDEA Sandbox is attractive for repeatable Landsat products and large-area time series; ENVI can provide detailed image inspection and classification. Higher-resolution commercial imagery may improve narrow fringe delineation but can lack historical consistency, cost more, and demand additional harmonisation. [2] B2 — 14 marks: Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.1. Define target and hypothesesSpecify mapped mangrove domain, disturbance period, target index/anomaly, spatial unit, lead-time definition and comparison with unaffected areas. Controls ambiguous interpretation. [2]2. Assemble imagery and ancillary dataSelect product level, band definitions, dates, tide/weather data, mangrove mask and independent reference sources; audit data availability. Controls unavailable bands and mismatched dates. [2]3. Quality and geometryUse cloud/cloud-shadow/saturation masks, co-registration checks, common CRS/grid, tidal water handling and valid-count masks. Controls false change at edges and invalid pixels. [2]4. Radiometric/seasonal consistencyUse comparable surface-reflectance products, inspect stable targets, align phenological windows and avoid unjustified cross-sensor merges. Controls illumination/atmosphere/seasonal artefacts. [2]5. Construct time seriesCalculate NDVI and selected indicators with explicit formulas and consistent masks; aggregate seasonally or fit seasonal baselines; record sample support. Controls uneven sampling and noise. [2]6. Quantify anomaly and disturbanceCalculate pre-event mean/dispersion or seasonal models, compare event anomalies and persistence, create categorical flags only using justified thresholds. Controls arbitrary cut-offs. [2]7. Validate and communicateUse independent spatially representative observations, test continuous and categorical accuracy, sensitivity and uncertainty; deliver readable maps, tables, plots and processing log. Controls unsupported claims. [2] B3 — 10 marks: Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.Continuous estimatesFor vegetation indices or modelled cover, compare to independent contemporaneous reference quantities where physically comparable; report bias, MAE/RMSE and residuals by cover/depth/region. An NDVI anomaly cannot be validated directly as a field-measured “stress” unit without an explicit ecological relationship. [3]Categorical flagsUse independent stratified probability samples across predicted disturbed/undisturbed and reference conditions; report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, false-alarm/miss rates and uncertainty intervals; account for unequal sampling fractions in area estimates. [3]Support and designAlign spatial footprint, temporal date and class definition; use spatial blocks/sites for independent holdout; distinguish mixed pixels, tidal conditions and imperfect reference data. [2]RobustnessTest alternative seasonal windows, thresholds, baseline lengths, valid-count criteria, registration offsets and masks; show confidence/uncertainty and where evidence is missing. [2] B4 — 6 marks: Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.Operational definitionDefine event onset independently (e.g. mapped disturbance/reference evidence), anomaly detection threshold, false-alarm rule and earliest sustained crossing date; calculate lead time relative to NDVI using the same pixels, observation dates and baseline protocol. [3]Fair comparisonAssess detection rate, false positives, sensitivity to seasonal sampling and observation gaps; stratify affected versus unaffected areas and report uncertainty. An index that changes earlier only because it has more valid observations is not necessarily biologically earlier. [2]Inference limitEarlier statistical anomaly is a candidate warning signal, not proof of physiological stress or causal drought mechanism; independent ecology and climate evidence is needed. [1] C1 — 8 marks: Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.Given matrixRows are MAP predictions; columns are REFERENCE. Map mangrove: reference mangrove 42, reference other 8. Map other: reference mangrove 6, reference other 44. N=100. [matrix supplied]Overall accuracy(42+44)/100 = 86%. [2]Mangrove user’s accuracy42/(42+8)=84%; commission error=8/50=16%. Interprets reliability of pixels mapped as mangrove. [2]Mangrove producer’s accuracy42/(42+6)=87.5%; omission error=6/48=12.5%. Interprets proportion of reference mangrove correctly mapped. [2]InterpretationUser’s accuracy conditions on mapped class; producer’s on reference class. State orientation, sample design and limitations of overall accuracy. [2] C2 — 6 marks: Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.DEA Sandbox strengthsCloud/data-cube access to analysis-ready EO products and scalable repeatable temporal compositing; useful for long Landsat series and large geographic extents. [2]ENVI strengthsDetailed interactive image inspection, spectral processing and supervised/unsupervised classification tools; convenient for experimenting with per-scene corrections and visual diagnostics. [2]Limits and division of labourSandbox analyses require coding and awareness of product/algorithm defaults, and do not make poor sampling valid. ENVI desktop workflows may be laborious for very large multiyear stacks and require careful logging. Use Sandbox for repeatable time-series assembly, masking and anomaly extraction; ENVI for targeted diagnostic/classification work, with consistent preprocessing and validation across both. [2] C3 — 6 marks: A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.LiDARAdds direct 3D canopy/terrain structural measurements (e.g. height, vertical distribution), useful for biomass and structural change, but acquisition coverage and revisit frequency may be insufficient for 2013–2017 early-warning trajectories. [2]SARActive microwave observations can penetrate clouds and provide complementary sensitivity to structure, moisture and inundation; backscatter depends on incidence angle, polarisation, roughness and water level, so calibration/terrain/temporal harmonisation remain essential. [2]HyperspectralDense narrow spectral bands can help distinguish pigments/biochemical properties under suitable calibration and canopy conditions, but coverage, cost, atmosphere, mixed pixels and validation limit use. These datasets complement rather than automatically replace consistent historical optical observations. [2]"}, {"title": "Final checklist", "html": "<p>Critique: flaw → mechanism → consequence → fix. Design: question → data → preprocessing → analysis → validation → uncertainty → alternatives. Always state matrix orientation and independent sampling assumptions.</p><p>Based on user-provided official guidance and lecturer feedback. All questions, marks, timing and model answers are original practice material.</p>", "text": "Critique: flaw → mechanism → consequence → fix. Design: question → data → preprocessing → analysis → validation → uncertainty → alternatives. Always state matrix orientation and independent sampling assumptions. Based on user-provided official guidance and lecturer feedback. All questions, marks, timing and model answers are original practice material."}], "text": ["Instructions and scenario Attempt all questions. Prioritise correct reasoning, consequences, corrections and justified alternatives. A coastal management agency asks you to assess whether a major 2015–2016 disturbance reduced mangrove canopy condition across a 180 km coastal region, and whether satellite indicators offered earlier warning than a conventional greenness index. It needs a spatial map, a defensible time series, and an uncertainty statement. Budget allows existing satellite products and a limited number of field visits. Historical field measurements are sparse. The region includes tidal flats, narrow mangrove fringes, seasonal cloud, and some pixels with mixed land and water. Proposed flawed workflow The analyst selects one cloud-free image from August 2014 and one from February 2016 and calls the difference “disturbance impact”.They mix Landsat 8 surface reflectance with Sentinel-2 top-of-atmosphere reflectance without harmonisation, because both products contain red and NIR bands.They resample Landsat 30 m data to 10 m and claim this creates 10 m spatial detail.They compute NDVI from every pixel, including tidal water and cloud shadows, and treat every decline of 0.08 as damaged mangrove.They use the same field polygons to train and verify a random-forest classifier, reporting 97% overall accuracy.They select reference sites only beside accessible roads and assume they represent the entire coastline.They compare two classified maps and report a 4% mangrove-area loss without evaluating class-specific or change-map errors.They use a red-edge index from Sentinel-2 for 2013, although the available Sentinel-2 mission record does not cover that date.They call a low NDVI anomaly proof of physiological stress and identify drought as the cause without testing alternatives.They deliver maps without dates, scale, units, valid-pixel counts, methods log or uncertainty bounds.", "Section A — Critique (40 marks) A1 (24 marks) Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices. A2 (8 marks) Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design. A3 (8 marks) The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.", "Section B — Design (40 marks) B1 (10 marks) Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative. B2 (14 marks) Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls. B3 (10 marks) Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting. B4 (6 marks) Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.", "Section C — Applied concepts (20 marks) C1 (8 marks) Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors. Matrix (rows=map, columns=reference): [42,8] for mapped mangrove; [6,44] for mapped other. C2 (6 marks) Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour. C3 (6 marks) A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.", "Fully worked model answers Open each question to reveal the model response and mark guide. A1 — 24 marks: Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.Priority 1 — invalid temporal comparisonA single August-to-February difference confounds seasonal phenology, tidal conditions, clouds and an event with real long-term disturbance. Use seasonally matched multi-year observations, valid-pixel quality screening and pre-event variability to contextualise post-event anomalies. [4 marks]Priority 2 — radiometric/sensor inconsistencySurface reflectance and top-of-atmosphere reflectance are different physical processing levels; sensor bandpasses also differ. Artificial differences can be misread as vegetation change. Use consistently corrected products, sensor-specific indices or validated cross-sensor harmonisation; test stable reference sites. [4]Priority 3 — invalid mangrove domain and thresholdUnmasked water, tidal inundation and shadow distort NDVI; a universal −0.08 threshold lacks evidence relative to noise and seasonal variability. Apply stable mangrove extent and water/cloud/shadow/tide masks, derive anomaly thresholds from baseline variability, and test sensitivity. [4]Priority 4 — non-independent validationTraining and testing on the same polygons measures fit to seen labels and biases accuracy upward. Split by independent spatial sites or hold out entire field locations; ensure reference dates and class definitions match imagery. [4]Priority 5 — wrong historical sensor availabilitySentinel-2 cannot supply a 2013 red-edge observation. This creates a non-existent time series and an invalid lead-time comparison. Use Landsat 8 for the historical period, begin Sentinel-2 red-edge analysis only after its acquisition record starts, and compare overlapping dates cautiously. [4]Priority 6 — unsupported interpretation and uncertaintyNDVI anomaly is not a unique drought or physiological-stress signature, and a 4% mapped change may be smaller than classification uncertainty. Triangulate independent ecological/weather evidence, report alternative causes, class-specific errors and uncertainty intervals. [4]Other valid flawsResampling 30 m to 10 m does not create new spatial information; roadside reference sites introduce selection bias; lack of provenance, legends and valid-pixel counts harms reproducibility. Full marks may be awarded for any six well-prioritised, mechanistically explained flaws and specific remedies. A2 — 8 marks: Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.Why 97% is not defensibleReusing training polygons for testing is data leakage and produces optimistic accuracy. Spatially adjacent training/test samples may also share autocorrelation; road-accessible plots underrepresent remote tidal mangroves. Overall accuracy hides class-specific false positives and false negatives. [3]Better designDefine target classes and mapping unit; use probability-based stratified random reference sampling across mangrove/non-mangrove, disturbed/undisturbed and key depth/tidal or geographic strata; obtain independent, temporally aligned high-quality field or imagery labels; separate entire sites or spatial blocks from training. [3]ReportProvide confusion matrix with map/reference orientation, user’s and producer’s accuracies, sample sizes, uncertainty intervals, and area-adjusted estimates if the sampling design requires them. Record ambiguous reference labels and support mismatch. [2] A3 — 8 marks: The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.Why the inference fails90% overall accuracy on each date is not 90% accuracy of change; errors can compound, and overall accuracy may be dominated by stable common classes. A 4% net difference could reflect misclassification, inconsistent class definitions, co-registration or seasonality. [4]Change validationSample stable and changed transition strata independently; check reference conditions at both dates; calculate transition-specific omission/commission, confusion matrix for change/no-change or transitions, design-weighted area estimates and confidence intervals; assess sensitivity to registration, masks and thresholds. Do not call the 4% change statistically significant without an appropriate uncertainty comparison. [4] B1 — 10 marks: Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.Core productsUse Landsat 8 calibrated surface reflectance for a consistent 2013–2017 baseline and event analysis, with its 30 m spatial footprint honestly retained. Landsat time-series coverage supports the historical question; QA bands support cloud/shadow masking. Use Sentinel-2 surface reflectance/red-edge indices only for the period actually available and for complementary later or overlapping analyses. [4]Resolution trade-offsSpatial: 30 m can mix narrow mangrove strips with water; exclude or flag mixed pixels and compare with higher-resolution reference. Spectral: red/NIR enable NDVI, red-edge only where supported. Radiometric: consistent reflectance and band definitions. Temporal: revisit and cloud gaps require compositing, seasonal alignment and valid-observation counts. [4]Defended alternativeDEA Sandbox is attractive for repeatable Landsat products and large-area time series; ENVI can provide detailed image inspection and classification. Higher-resolution commercial imagery may improve narrow fringe delineation but can lack historical consistency, cost more, and demand additional harmonisation. [2] B2 — 14 marks: Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.1. Define target and hypothesesSpecify mapped mangrove domain, disturbance period, target index/anomaly, spatial unit, lead-time definition and comparison with unaffected areas. Controls ambiguous interpretation. [2]2. Assemble imagery and ancillary dataSelect product level, band definitions, dates, tide/weather data, mangrove mask and independent reference sources; audit data availability. Controls unavailable bands and mismatched dates. [2]3. Quality and geometryUse cloud/cloud-shadow/saturation masks, co-registration checks, common CRS/grid, tidal water handling and valid-count masks. Controls false change at edges and invalid pixels. [2]4. Radiometric/seasonal consistencyUse comparable surface-reflectance products, inspect stable targets, align phenological windows and avoid unjustified cross-sensor merges. Controls illumination/atmosphere/seasonal artefacts. [2]5. Construct time seriesCalculate NDVI and selected indicators with explicit formulas and consistent masks; aggregate seasonally or fit seasonal baselines; record sample support. Controls uneven sampling and noise. [2]6. Quantify anomaly and disturbanceCalculate pre-event mean/dispersion or seasonal models, compare event anomalies and persistence, create categorical flags only using justified thresholds. Controls arbitrary cut-offs. [2]7. Validate and communicateUse independent spatially representative observations, test continuous and categorical accuracy, sensitivity and uncertainty; deliver readable maps, tables, plots and processing log. Controls unsupported claims. [2] B3 — 10 marks: Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.Continuous estimatesFor vegetation indices or modelled cover, compare to independent contemporaneous reference quantities where physically comparable; report bias, MAE/RMSE and residuals by cover/depth/region. An NDVI anomaly cannot be validated directly as a field-measured “stress” unit without an explicit ecological relationship. [3]Categorical flagsUse independent stratified probability samples across predicted disturbed/undisturbed and reference conditions; report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, false-alarm/miss rates and uncertainty intervals; account for unequal sampling fractions in area estimates. [3]Support and designAlign spatial footprint, temporal date and class definition; use spatial blocks/sites for independent holdout; distinguish mixed pixels, tidal conditions and imperfect reference data. [2]RobustnessTest alternative seasonal windows, thresholds, baseline lengths, valid-count criteria, registration offsets and masks; show confidence/uncertainty and where evidence is missing. [2] B4 — 6 marks: Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.Operational definitionDefine event onset independently (e.g. mapped disturbance/reference evidence), anomaly detection threshold, false-alarm rule and earliest sustained crossing date; calculate lead time relative to NDVI using the same pixels, observation dates and baseline protocol. [3]Fair comparisonAssess detection rate, false positives, sensitivity to seasonal sampling and observation gaps; stratify affected versus unaffected areas and report uncertainty. An index that changes earlier only because it has more valid observations is not necessarily biologically earlier. [2]Inference limitEarlier statistical anomaly is a candidate warning signal, not proof of physiological stress or causal drought mechanism; independent ecology and climate evidence is needed. [1] C1 — 8 marks: Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.Given matrixRows are MAP predictions; columns are REFERENCE. Map mangrove: reference mangrove 42, reference other 8. Map other: reference mangrove 6, reference other 44. N=100. [matrix supplied]Overall accuracy(42+44)/100 = 86%. [2]Mangrove user’s accuracy42/(42+8)=84%; commission error=8/50=16%. Interprets reliability of pixels mapped as mangrove. [2]Mangrove producer’s accuracy42/(42+6)=87.5%; omission error=6/48=12.5%. Interprets proportion of reference mangrove correctly mapped. [2]InterpretationUser’s accuracy conditions on mapped class; producer’s on reference class. State orientation, sample design and limitations of overall accuracy. [2] C2 — 6 marks: Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.DEA Sandbox strengthsCloud/data-cube access to analysis-ready EO products and scalable repeatable temporal compositing; useful for long Landsat series and large geographic extents. [2]ENVI strengthsDetailed interactive image inspection, spectral processing and supervised/unsupervised classification tools; convenient for experimenting with per-scene corrections and visual diagnostics. [2]Limits and division of labourSandbox analyses require coding and awareness of product/algorithm defaults, and do not make poor sampling valid. ENVI desktop workflows may be laborious for very large multiyear stacks and require careful logging. Use Sandbox for repeatable time-series assembly, masking and anomaly extraction; ENVI for targeted diagnostic/classification work, with consistent preprocessing and validation across both. [2] C3 — 6 marks: A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.LiDARAdds direct 3D canopy/terrain structural measurements (e.g. height, vertical distribution), useful for biomass and structural change, but acquisition coverage and revisit frequency may be insufficient for 2013–2017 early-warning trajectories. [2]SARActive microwave observations can penetrate clouds and provide complementary sensitivity to structure, moisture and inundation; backscatter depends on incidence angle, polarisation, roughness and water level, so calibration/terrain/temporal harmonisation remain essential. [2]HyperspectralDense narrow spectral bands can help distinguish pigments/biochemical properties under suitable calibration and canopy conditions, but coverage, cost, atmosphere, mixed pixels and validation limit use. These datasets complement rather than automatically replace consistent historical optical observations. [2]", "Final checklist Critique: flaw → mechanism → consequence → fix. Design: question → data → preprocessing → analysis → validation → uncertainty → alternatives. Always state matrix orientation and independent sampling assumptions. Based on user-provided official guidance and lecturer feedback. All questions, marks, timing and model answers are original practice material."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Instructions and scenario", "html": "<p>Attempt all questions. Prioritise correct reasoning, consequences, corrections and justified alternatives.</p><p>A coastal management agency asks you to assess whether a major 2015–2016 disturbance reduced mangrove canopy condition across a 180 km coastal region, and whether satellite indicators offered earlier warning than a conventional greenness index. It needs a spatial map, a defensible time series, and an uncertainty statement. Budget allows existing satellite products and a limited number of field visits. Historical field measurements are sparse. The region includes tidal flats, narrow mangrove fringes, seasonal cloud, and some pixels with mixed land and water.</p><h3>Proposed flawed workflow</h3><ol><li>The analyst selects one cloud-free image from August 2014 and one from February 2016 and calls the difference “disturbance impact”.</li><li>They mix Landsat 8 surface reflectance with Sentinel-2 top-of-atmosphere reflectance without harmonisation, because both products contain red and NIR bands.</li><li>They resample Landsat 30 m data to 10 m and claim this creates 10 m spatial detail.</li><li>They compute NDVI from every pixel, including tidal water and cloud shadows, and treat every decline of 0.08 as damaged mangrove.</li><li>They use the same field polygons to train and verify a random-forest classifier, reporting 97% overall accuracy.</li><li>They select reference sites only beside accessible roads and assume they represent the entire coastline.</li><li>They compare two classified maps and report a 4% mangrove-area loss without evaluating class-specific or change-map errors.</li><li>They use a red-edge index from Sentinel-2 for 2013, although the available Sentinel-2 mission record does not cover that date.</li><li>They call a low NDVI anomaly proof of physiological stress and identify drought as the cause without testing alternatives.</li><li>They deliver maps without dates, scale, units, valid-pixel counts, methods log or uncertainty bounds.</li></ol>"}, {"title": "Section A — Critique (40 marks)", "html": "<p>A1 (24 marks) Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.</p><p>A2 (8 marks) Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.</p><p>A3 (8 marks) The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.</p>"}, {"title": "Section B — Design (40 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 (10 marks) Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.</p><p>B2 (14 marks) Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.</p><p>B3 (10 marks) Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.</p><p>B4 (6 marks) Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.</p>"}, {"title": "Section C — Applied concepts (20 marks)", "html": "<p>C1 (8 marks) Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.</p><p>Matrix (rows=map, columns=reference): [42,8] for mapped mangrove; [6,44] for mapped other.</p><p>C2 (6 marks) Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.</p><p>C3 (6 marks) A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "Fully worked model answers", "html": "<p>Open each question to reveal the model response and mark guide.</p><details><summary>A1 — 24 marks: Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.</summary><h3>Priority 1 — invalid temporal comparison</h3><p>A single August-to-February difference confounds seasonal phenology, tidal conditions, clouds and an event with real long-term disturbance. Use seasonally matched multi-year observations, valid-pixel quality screening and pre-event variability to contextualise post-event anomalies. [4 marks]</p><h3>Priority 2 — radiometric/sensor inconsistency</h3><p>Surface reflectance and top-of-atmosphere reflectance are different physical processing levels; sensor bandpasses also differ. Artificial differences can be misread as vegetation change. Use consistently corrected products, sensor-specific indices or validated cross-sensor harmonisation; test stable reference sites. [4]</p><h3>Priority 3 — invalid mangrove domain and threshold</h3><p>Unmasked water, tidal inundation and shadow distort NDVI; a universal −0.08 threshold lacks evidence relative to noise and seasonal variability. Apply stable mangrove extent and water/cloud/shadow/tide masks, derive anomaly thresholds from baseline variability, and test sensitivity. [4]</p><h3>Priority 4 — non-independent validation</h3><p>Training and testing on the same polygons measures fit to seen labels and biases accuracy upward. Split by independent spatial sites or hold out entire field locations; ensure reference dates and class definitions match imagery. [4]</p><h3>Priority 5 — wrong historical sensor availability</h3><p>Sentinel-2 cannot supply a 2013 red-edge observation. This creates a non-existent time series and an invalid lead-time comparison. Use Landsat 8 for the historical period, begin Sentinel-2 red-edge analysis only after its acquisition record starts, and compare overlapping dates cautiously. [4]</p><h3>Priority 6 — unsupported interpretation and uncertainty</h3><p>NDVI anomaly is not a unique drought or physiological-stress signature, and a 4% mapped change may be smaller than classification uncertainty. Triangulate independent ecological/weather evidence, report alternative causes, class-specific errors and uncertainty intervals. [4]</p><h3>Other valid flaws</h3><p>Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not create new spatial information; roadside reference sites introduce selection bias; lack of provenance, legends and valid-pixel counts harms reproducibility. Full marks may be awarded for any six well-prioritised, mechanistically explained flaws and specific remedies.</p></details><details><summary>A2 — 8 marks: Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.</summary><h3>Why 97% is not defensible</h3><p>Reusing training polygons for testing is data leakage and produces optimistic accuracy. Spatially adjacent training/test samples may also share autocorrelation; road-accessible plots underrepresent remote tidal mangroves. Overall accuracy hides class-specific false positives and false negatives. [3]</p><h3>Better design</h3><p>Define target classes and mapping unit; use probability-based stratified random reference sampling across mangrove/non-mangrove, disturbed/undisturbed and key depth/tidal or geographic strata; obtain independent, temporally aligned high-quality field or imagery labels; separate entire sites or spatial blocks from training. [3]</p><h3>Report</h3><p>Provide confusion matrix with map/reference orientation, user’s and producer’s accuracies, sample sizes, uncertainty intervals, and area-adjusted estimates if the sampling design requires them. Record ambiguous reference labels and support mismatch. [2]</p></details><details><summary>A3 — 8 marks: The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.</summary><h3>Why the inference fails</h3><p>90% overall accuracy on each date is not 90% accuracy of change; errors can compound, and overall accuracy may be dominated by stable common classes. A 4% net difference could reflect misclassification, inconsistent class definitions, co-registration or seasonality. [4]</p><h3>Change validation</h3><p>Sample stable and changed transition strata independently; check reference conditions at both dates; calculate transition-specific omission/commission, confusion matrix for change/no-change or transitions, design-weighted area estimates and confidence intervals; assess sensitivity to registration, masks and thresholds. Do not call the 4% change statistically significant without an appropriate uncertainty comparison. [4]</p></details><details><summary>B1 — 10 marks: Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.</summary><h3>Core products</h3><p>Use Landsat 8 calibrated surface reflectance for a consistent 2013–2017 baseline and event analysis, with its 30 m spatial footprint honestly retained. Landsat time-series coverage supports the historical question; QA bands support cloud/shadow masking. Use Sentinel-2 surface reflectance/red-edge indices only for the period actually available and for complementary later or overlapping analyses. [4]</p><h3>Resolution trade-offs</h3><p>Spatial: 30 m can mix narrow mangrove strips with water; exclude or flag mixed pixels and compare with higher-resolution reference. Spectral: red/NIR enable NDVI, red-edge only where supported. Radiometric: consistent reflectance and band definitions. Temporal: revisit and cloud gaps require compositing, seasonal alignment and valid-observation counts. [4]</p><h3>Defended alternative</h3><p>DEA Sandbox is attractive for repeatable Landsat products and large-area time series; ENVI can provide detailed image inspection and classification. Higher-resolution commercial imagery may improve narrow fringe delineation but can lack historical consistency, cost more, and demand additional harmonisation. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B2 — 14 marks: Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.</summary><h3>1. Define target and hypotheses</h3><p>Specify mapped mangrove domain, disturbance period, target index/anomaly, spatial unit, lead-time definition and comparison with unaffected areas. Controls ambiguous interpretation. [2]</p><h3>2. Assemble imagery and ancillary data</h3><p>Select product level, band definitions, dates, tide/weather data, mangrove mask and independent reference sources; audit data availability. Controls unavailable bands and mismatched dates. [2]</p><h3>3. Quality and geometry</h3><p>Use cloud/cloud-shadow/saturation masks, co-registration checks, common CRS/grid, tidal water handling and valid-count masks. Controls false change at edges and invalid pixels. [2]</p><h3>4. Radiometric/seasonal consistency</h3><p>Use comparable surface-reflectance products, inspect stable targets, align phenological windows and avoid unjustified cross-sensor merges. Controls illumination/atmosphere/seasonal artefacts. [2]</p><h3>5. Construct time series</h3><p>Calculate NDVI and selected indicators with explicit formulas and consistent masks; aggregate seasonally or fit seasonal baselines; record sample support. Controls uneven sampling and noise. [2]</p><h3>6. Quantify anomaly and disturbance</h3><p>Calculate pre-event mean/dispersion or seasonal models, compare event anomalies and persistence, create categorical flags only using justified thresholds. Controls arbitrary cut-offs. [2]</p><h3>7. Validate and communicate</h3><p>Use independent spatially representative observations, test continuous and categorical accuracy, sensitivity and uncertainty; deliver readable maps, tables, plots and processing log. Controls unsupported claims. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B3 — 10 marks: Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.</summary><h3>Continuous estimates</h3><p>For vegetation indices or modelled cover, compare to independent contemporaneous reference quantities where physically comparable; report bias, MAE/RMSE and residuals by cover/depth/region. An NDVI anomaly cannot be validated directly as a field-measured “stress” unit without an explicit ecological relationship. [3]</p><h3>Categorical flags</h3><p>Use independent stratified probability samples across predicted disturbed/undisturbed and reference conditions; report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, false-alarm/miss rates and uncertainty intervals; account for unequal sampling fractions in area estimates. [3]</p><h3>Support and design</h3><p>Align spatial footprint, temporal date and class definition; use spatial blocks/sites for independent holdout; distinguish mixed pixels, tidal conditions and imperfect reference data. [2]</p><h3>Robustness</h3><p>Test alternative seasonal windows, thresholds, baseline lengths, valid-count criteria, registration offsets and masks; show confidence/uncertainty and where evidence is missing. [2]</p></details><details><summary>B4 — 6 marks: Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.</summary><h3>Operational definition</h3><p>Define event onset independently (e.g. mapped disturbance/reference evidence), anomaly detection threshold, false-alarm rule and earliest sustained crossing date; calculate lead time relative to NDVI using the same pixels, observation dates and baseline protocol. [3]</p><h3>Fair comparison</h3><p>Assess detection rate, false positives, sensitivity to seasonal sampling and observation gaps; stratify affected versus unaffected areas and report uncertainty. An index that changes earlier only because it has more valid observations is not necessarily biologically earlier. [2]</p><h3>Inference limit</h3><p>Earlier statistical anomaly is a candidate warning signal, not proof of physiological stress or causal drought mechanism; independent ecology and climate evidence is needed. [1]</p></details><details><summary>C1 — 8 marks: Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.</summary><h3>Given matrix</h3><p>Rows are MAP predictions; columns are REFERENCE. Map mangrove: reference mangrove 42, reference other 8. Map other: reference mangrove 6, reference other 44. N=100. [matrix supplied]</p><h3>Overall accuracy</h3><p>(42+44)/100 = 86%. [2]</p><h3>Mangrove user’s accuracy</h3><p>42/(42+8)=84%; commission error=8/50=16%. Interprets reliability of pixels mapped as mangrove. [2]</p><h3>Mangrove producer’s accuracy</h3><p>42/(42+6)=87.5%; omission error=6/48=12.5%. Interprets proportion of reference mangrove correctly mapped. [2]</p><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>User’s accuracy conditions on mapped class; producer’s on reference class. State orientation, sample design and limitations of overall accuracy. [2]</p></details><details><summary>C2 — 6 marks: Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.</summary><h3>DEA Sandbox strengths</h3><p>Cloud/data-cube access to analysis-ready EO products and scalable repeatable temporal compositing; useful for long Landsat series and large geographic extents. [2]</p><h3>ENVI strengths</h3><p>Detailed interactive image inspection, spectral processing and supervised/unsupervised classification tools; convenient for experimenting with per-scene corrections and visual diagnostics. [2]</p><h3>Limits and division of labour</h3><p>Sandbox analyses require coding and awareness of product/algorithm defaults, and do not make poor sampling valid. ENVI desktop workflows may be laborious for very large multiyear stacks and require careful logging. Use Sandbox for repeatable time-series assembly, masking and anomaly extraction; ENVI for targeted diagnostic/classification work, with consistent preprocessing and validation across both. [2]</p></details><details><summary>C3 — 6 marks: A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.</summary><h3>LiDAR</h3><p>Adds direct 3D canopy/terrain structural measurements (e.g. height, vertical distribution), useful for biomass and structural change, but acquisition coverage and revisit frequency may be insufficient for 2013–2017 early-warning trajectories. [2]</p><h3>SAR</h3><p>Active microwave observations can penetrate clouds and provide complementary sensitivity to structure, moisture and inundation; backscatter depends on incidence angle, polarisation, roughness and water level, so calibration/terrain/temporal harmonisation remain essential. [2]</p><h3>Hyperspectral</h3><p>Dense narrow spectral bands can help distinguish pigments/biochemical properties under suitable calibration and canopy conditions, but coverage, cost, atmosphere, mixed pixels and validation limit use. These datasets complement rather than automatically replace consistent historical optical observations. [2]</p></details>", "text": "Open each question to reveal the model response and mark guide. A1 — 24 marks: Identify and prioritise the SIX most consequential methodological flaws. For each, explain the mechanism by which it threatens inference and propose a practical correction. Do not simply list generic best practices.Priority 1 — invalid temporal comparisonA single August-to-February difference confounds seasonal phenology, tidal conditions, clouds and an event with real long-term disturbance. Use seasonally matched multi-year observations, valid-pixel quality screening and pre-event variability to contextualise post-event anomalies. [4 marks]Priority 2 — radiometric/sensor inconsistencySurface reflectance and top-of-atmosphere reflectance are different physical processing levels; sensor bandpasses also differ. Artificial differences can be misread as vegetation change. Use consistently corrected products, sensor-specific indices or validated cross-sensor harmonisation; test stable reference sites. [4]Priority 3 — invalid mangrove domain and thresholdUnmasked water, tidal inundation and shadow distort NDVI; a universal −0.08 threshold lacks evidence relative to noise and seasonal variability. Apply stable mangrove extent and water/cloud/shadow/tide masks, derive anomaly thresholds from baseline variability, and test sensitivity. [4]Priority 4 — non-independent validationTraining and testing on the same polygons measures fit to seen labels and biases accuracy upward. Split by independent spatial sites or hold out entire field locations; ensure reference dates and class definitions match imagery. [4]Priority 5 — wrong historical sensor availabilitySentinel-2 cannot supply a 2013 red-edge observation. This creates a non-existent time series and an invalid lead-time comparison. Use Landsat 8 for the historical period, begin Sentinel-2 red-edge analysis only after its acquisition record starts, and compare overlapping dates cautiously. [4]Priority 6 — unsupported interpretation and uncertaintyNDVI anomaly is not a unique drought or physiological-stress signature, and a 4% mapped change may be smaller than classification uncertainty. Triangulate independent ecological/weather evidence, report alternative causes, class-specific errors and uncertainty intervals. [4]Other valid flawsResampling 30 m to 10 m does not create new spatial information; roadside reference sites introduce selection bias; lack of provenance, legends and valid-pixel counts harms reproducibility. Full marks may be awarded for any six well-prioritised, mechanistically explained flaws and specific remedies. A2 — 8 marks: Explain why the claim of 97% overall classification accuracy is unreliable, and propose a better independent sampling and verification design.Why 97% is not defensibleReusing training polygons for testing is data leakage and produces optimistic accuracy. Spatially adjacent training/test samples may also share autocorrelation; road-accessible plots underrepresent remote tidal mangroves. Overall accuracy hides class-specific false positives and false negatives. [3]Better designDefine target classes and mapping unit; use probability-based stratified random reference sampling across mangrove/non-mangrove, disturbed/undisturbed and key depth/tidal or geographic strata; obtain independent, temporally aligned high-quality field or imagery labels; separate entire sites or spatial blocks from training. [3]ReportProvide confusion matrix with map/reference orientation, user’s and producer’s accuracies, sample sizes, uncertainty intervals, and area-adjusted estimates if the sampling design requires them. Record ambiguous reference labels and support mismatch. [2] A3 — 8 marks: The analyst claims that a 4% mapped mangrove loss is significant because each individual land-cover map has 90% overall accuracy. Critique this inference and describe how to validate mapped change.Why the inference fails90% overall accuracy on each date is not 90% accuracy of change; errors can compound, and overall accuracy may be dominated by stable common classes. A 4% net difference could reflect misclassification, inconsistent class definitions, co-registration or seasonality. [4]Change validationSample stable and changed transition strata independently; check reference conditions at both dates; calculate transition-specific omission/commission, confusion matrix for change/no-change or transitions, design-weighted area estimates and confidence intervals; assess sensitivity to registration, masks and thresholds. Do not call the 4% change statistically significant without an appropriate uncertainty comparison. [4] B1 — 10 marks: Specify a feasible data strategy, including sensor/product choices, spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, plus a justified alternative.Core productsUse Landsat 8 calibrated surface reflectance for a consistent 2013–2017 baseline and event analysis, with its 30 m spatial footprint honestly retained. Landsat time-series coverage supports the historical question; QA bands support cloud/shadow masking. Use Sentinel-2 surface reflectance/red-edge indices only for the period actually available and for complementary later or overlapping analyses. [4]Resolution trade-offsSpatial: 30 m can mix narrow mangrove strips with water; exclude or flag mixed pixels and compare with higher-resolution reference. Spectral: red/NIR enable NDVI, red-edge only where supported. Radiometric: consistent reflectance and band definitions. Temporal: revisit and cloud gaps require compositing, seasonal alignment and valid-observation counts. [4]Defended alternativeDEA Sandbox is attractive for repeatable Landsat products and large-area time series; ENVI can provide detailed image inspection and classification. Higher-resolution commercial imagery may improve narrow fringe delineation but can lack historical consistency, cost more, and demand additional harmonisation. [2] B2 — 14 marks: Present an ordered end-to-end processing and analysis workflow. For each major step state its purpose and at least one error it controls.1. Define target and hypothesesSpecify mapped mangrove domain, disturbance period, target index/anomaly, spatial unit, lead-time definition and comparison with unaffected areas. Controls ambiguous interpretation. [2]2. Assemble imagery and ancillary dataSelect product level, band definitions, dates, tide/weather data, mangrove mask and independent reference sources; audit data availability. Controls unavailable bands and mismatched dates. [2]3. Quality and geometryUse cloud/cloud-shadow/saturation masks, co-registration checks, common CRS/grid, tidal water handling and valid-count masks. Controls false change at edges and invalid pixels. [2]4. Radiometric/seasonal consistencyUse comparable surface-reflectance products, inspect stable targets, align phenological windows and avoid unjustified cross-sensor merges. Controls illumination/atmosphere/seasonal artefacts. [2]5. Construct time seriesCalculate NDVI and selected indicators with explicit formulas and consistent masks; aggregate seasonally or fit seasonal baselines; record sample support. Controls uneven sampling and noise. [2]6. Quantify anomaly and disturbanceCalculate pre-event mean/dispersion or seasonal models, compare event anomalies and persistence, create categorical flags only using justified thresholds. Controls arbitrary cut-offs. [2]7. Validate and communicateUse independent spatially representative observations, test continuous and categorical accuracy, sensitivity and uncertainty; deliver readable maps, tables, plots and processing log. Controls unsupported claims. [2] B3 — 10 marks: Design the accuracy and uncertainty assessment for continuous anomalies and categorical disturbance maps. Address spatial/temporal support, independence, sampling and reporting.Continuous estimatesFor vegetation indices or modelled cover, compare to independent contemporaneous reference quantities where physically comparable; report bias, MAE/RMSE and residuals by cover/depth/region. An NDVI anomaly cannot be validated directly as a field-measured “stress” unit without an explicit ecological relationship. [3]Categorical flagsUse independent stratified probability samples across predicted disturbed/undisturbed and reference conditions; report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, false-alarm/miss rates and uncertainty intervals; account for unequal sampling fractions in area estimates. [3]Support and designAlign spatial footprint, temporal date and class definition; use spatial blocks/sites for independent holdout; distinguish mixed pixels, tidal conditions and imperfect reference data. [2]RobustnessTest alternative seasonal windows, thresholds, baseline lengths, valid-count criteria, registration offsets and masks; show confidence/uncertainty and where evidence is missing. [2] B4 — 6 marks: Explain how you would test whether an indicator provides an earlier warning than NDVI without overstating causality.Operational definitionDefine event onset independently (e.g. mapped disturbance/reference evidence), anomaly detection threshold, false-alarm rule and earliest sustained crossing date; calculate lead time relative to NDVI using the same pixels, observation dates and baseline protocol. [3]Fair comparisonAssess detection rate, false positives, sensitivity to seasonal sampling and observation gaps; stratify affected versus unaffected areas and report uncertainty. An index that changes earlier only because it has more valid observations is not necessarily biologically earlier. [2]Inference limitEarlier statistical anomaly is a candidate warning signal, not proof of physiological stress or causal drought mechanism; independent ecology and climate evidence is needed. [1] C1 — 8 marks: Using the confusion matrix provided, calculate overall, user’s and producer’s accuracy for mangrove and explain commission versus omission errors.Given matrixRows are MAP predictions; columns are REFERENCE. Map mangrove: reference mangrove 42, reference other 8. Map other: reference mangrove 6, reference other 44. N=100. [matrix supplied]Overall accuracy(42+44)/100 = 86%. [2]Mangrove user’s accuracy42/(42+8)=84%; commission error=8/50=16%. Interprets reliability of pixels mapped as mangrove. [2]Mangrove producer’s accuracy42/(42+6)=87.5%; omission error=6/48=12.5%. Interprets proportion of reference mangrove correctly mapped. [2]InterpretationUser’s accuracy conditions on mapped class; producer’s on reference class. State orientation, sample design and limitations of overall accuracy. [2] C2 — 6 marks: Compare ENVI and Digital Earth Australia Sandbox for this coastal multi-temporal study. Give two strengths, two limitations and a defensible division of labour.DEA Sandbox strengthsCloud/data-cube access to analysis-ready EO products and scalable repeatable temporal compositing; useful for long Landsat series and large geographic extents. [2]ENVI strengthsDetailed interactive image inspection, spectral processing and supervised/unsupervised classification tools; convenient for experimenting with per-scene corrections and visual diagnostics. [2]Limits and division of labourSandbox analyses require coding and awareness of product/algorithm defaults, and do not make poor sampling valid. ENVI desktop workflows may be laborious for very large multiyear stacks and require careful logging. Use Sandbox for repeatable time-series assembly, masking and anomaly extraction; ENVI for targeted diagnostic/classification work, with consistent preprocessing and validation across both. [2] C3 — 6 marks: A colleague suggests airborne LiDAR, SAR and hyperspectral imagery as replacements for the optical time series. Discuss what each adds and why none is automatically a substitute.LiDARAdds direct 3D canopy/terrain structural measurements (e.g. height, vertical distribution), useful for biomass and structural change, but acquisition coverage and revisit frequency may be insufficient for 2013–2017 early-warning trajectories. [2]SARActive microwave observations can penetrate clouds and provide complementary sensitivity to structure, moisture and inundation; backscatter depends on incidence angle, polarisation, roughness and water level, so calibration/terrain/temporal harmonisation remain essential. [2]HyperspectralDense narrow spectral bands can help distinguish pigments/biochemical properties under suitable calibration and canopy conditions, but coverage, cost, atmosphere, mixed pixels and validation limit use. These datasets complement rather than automatically replace consistent historical optical observations. [2]"}, {"title": "Final checklist", "html": "<p>Critique: flaw → mechanism → consequence → fix. Design: question → data → preprocessing → analysis → validation → uncertainty → alternatives. Always state matrix orientation and independent sampling assumptions.</p><p>Based on user-provided official guidance and lecturer feedback. All questions, marks, timing and model answers are original practice material.</p>", "text": "Critique: flaw → mechanism → consequence → fix. Design: question → data → preprocessing → analysis → validation → uncertainty → alternatives. Always state matrix orientation and independent sampling assumptions. Based on user-provided official guidance and lecturer feedback. All questions, marks, timing and model answers are original practice material."}]}, {"id": 14, "title": "Remote Sensing Foundations & Sensor Selection", "category": "SUMMARY-QUICK REVIEW", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Module1_Study_Guide.pdf", "pages": 6, "sections": [{"title": "Overview", "html": "<p class=\"note\">This is a focused first module, not the full course. Course-derived principles are cited in the source list; illustrative scenarios and model answers are supplementary exam practice.</p>", "text": "This is a focused first module, not the full course. Course-derived principles are cited in the source list; illustrative scenarios and model answers are supplementary exam practice."}, {"title": "1 | What the examiner is assessing", "html": "<h3>Concept</h3><p>A defensible Earth observation study starts with a measurable target and a question. Work backwards from the output: what object or process must be detected, at what spatial and temporal scale, and with what acceptable uncertainty?</p><h3>Course workflow</h3><p>Problem statement → image acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → thematic/quantitative extraction → error assessment → change detection → GIS integration → problem solving. This sequence appears in Lecture 2, slide 5, and Lecture 8, slide 3. In real projects, quality control and validation are iterative.</p><h3>Critical reasoning</h3><p>For each proposed choice, ask: what assumption is required? What physical or statistical process could violate it? What bias or uncertainty would result? How can the problem be tested or mitigated?</p><h3>Example</h3><p>A student chooses the highest-resolution image available. This is not sufficient justification: it may lack suitable spectral bands, cloud-free acquisitions, consistent historical coverage, or an affordable licensing arrangement.</p>", "text": "Concept A defensible Earth observation study starts with a measurable target and a question. Work backwards from the output: what object or process must be detected, at what spatial and temporal scale, and with what acceptable uncertainty? Course workflow Problem statement → image acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → thematic/quantitative extraction → error assessment → change detection → GIS integration → problem solving. This sequence appears in Lecture 2, slide 5, and Lecture 8, slide 3. In real projects, quality control and validation are iterative. Critical reasoning For each proposed choice, ask: what assumption is required? What physical or statistical process could violate it? What bias or uncertainty would result? How can the problem be tested or mitigated? Example A student chooses the highest-resolution image available. This is not sufficient justification: it may lack suitable spectral bands, cloud-free acquisitions, consistent historical coverage, or an affordable licensing arrangement."}, {"title": "2 | Radiation, targets and measurements", "html": "<h3>Physical chain</h3><p>For reflected-solar imagery, solar radiation travels through the atmosphere, interacts with the target, and returns through the atmosphere to the sensor. Recorded radiance is not identical to target surface reflectance. Scattering, absorption, illumination geometry and neighbouring targets affect the observation. Lecture 7 introduces the source–target–observer geometry (slides 6–7); Lecture 5 covers atmospheric correction.</p><h3>Spectral behaviour</h3><p>Vegetation typically absorbs red light through pigments and reflects strongly in the near infrared because of leaf structure; water strongly absorbs NIR and SWIR, while sediment, depth and bottom reflectance complicate visible-water signals. These are useful physical tendencies, not universal signatures.</p><h3>Equation</h3><p>NDVI = (NIR − Red) / (NIR + Red). It is a relative spectral contrast, not a direct measurement of biomass, health or canopy mortality. Its interpretation depends on background, saturation, atmosphere, illumination and target conditions.</p><h3>Critical application</h3><p>If NDVI decreases after a cyclone, plausible explanations include leaf loss, sediment/water background changes, cloud shadow, phenology or tidal inundation. Evidence of a negative difference alone cannot establish the cause.</p><h3>Source note</h3><p>Lecture 7: radiative transfer fundamentals; Lecture 5: atmospheric correction; Lecture 8: aquatic-interface effects.</p>", "text": "Physical chain For reflected-solar imagery, solar radiation travels through the atmosphere, interacts with the target, and returns through the atmosphere to the sensor. Recorded radiance is not identical to target surface reflectance. Scattering, absorption, illumination geometry and neighbouring targets affect the observation. Lecture 7 introduces the source–target–observer geometry (slides 6–7); Lecture 5 covers atmospheric correction. Spectral behaviour Vegetation typically absorbs red light through pigments and reflects strongly in the near infrared because of leaf structure; water strongly absorbs NIR and SWIR, while sediment, depth and bottom reflectance complicate visible-water signals. These are useful physical tendencies, not universal signatures. Equation NDVI = (NIR − Red) / (NIR + Red). It is a relative spectral contrast, not a direct measurement of biomass, health or canopy mortality. Its interpretation depends on background, saturation, atmosphere, illumination and target conditions. Critical application If NDVI decreases after a cyclone, plausible explanations include leaf loss, sediment/water background changes, cloud shadow, phenology or tidal inundation. Evidence of a negative difference alone cannot establish the cause. Source note Lecture 7: radiative transfer fundamentals; Lecture 5: atmospheric correction; Lecture 8: aquatic-interface effects."}, {"title": "3 | Spatial resolution and mixed pixels", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Ground resolution element (GRE) describes the ground support of image measurements. A 30 m pixel covers 900 m²; a 10 m pixel covers 100 m². Objects smaller than a pixel can influence its signal but cannot generally be delineated reliably as separate objects.</p><h3>Mixed pixels</h3><p>A coastal pixel may contain mangrove canopy, water, exposed sediment and shadow. Its spectrum is a mixture, so a threshold-based classification may depend on tide and viewing geometry even without ecological change.</p><h3>Resampling</h3><p>Nearest neighbour copies original values and is appropriate for categorical classes. Bilinear and cubic interpolation produce new continuous pixel values and may be useful for imagery, but can modify spectra and boundaries. Resampling 30 m data to 1 m does not create 1 m information.</p><h3>Trade-off</h3><p>Very-high-resolution imagery can resolve narrow features, but may have fewer spectral bands, shorter archives, higher cost and greater sensitivity to shadows. Choose resolution from the target size and decision requirements, not from a blanket “higher is better” rule.</p>", "text": "Foundation Ground resolution element (GRE) describes the ground support of image measurements. A 30 m pixel covers 900 m²; a 10 m pixel covers 100 m². Objects smaller than a pixel can influence its signal but cannot generally be delineated reliably as separate objects. Mixed pixels A coastal pixel may contain mangrove canopy, water, exposed sediment and shadow. Its spectrum is a mixture, so a threshold-based classification may depend on tide and viewing geometry even without ecological change. Resampling Nearest neighbour copies original values and is appropriate for categorical classes. Bilinear and cubic interpolation produce new continuous pixel values and may be useful for imagery, but can modify spectra and boundaries. Resampling 30 m data to 1 m does not create 1 m information. Trade-off Very-high-resolution imagery can resolve narrow features, but may have fewer spectral bands, shorter archives, higher cost and greater sensitivity to shadows. Choose resolution from the target size and decision requirements, not from a blanket “higher is better” rule."}, {"title": "4 | Spectral and radiometric resolution", "html": "<h3>Spectral</h3><p>Spectral resolution concerns band placement and width as well as band count. Broad multispectral bands can identify major classes; narrow hyperspectral bands may resolve subtle absorption features, at the cost of more complex calibration, noise management and dimensionality reduction.</p><h3>Radiometric</h3><p>An n-bit quantiser has 2^n possible codes (8-bit = 256; 12-bit = 4096). More digital levels do not automatically imply a higher signal-to-noise ratio or physically meaningful discrimination.</p><h3>Cross-sensor caution</h3><p>A “red” band on two sensors need not have identical wavelength boundaries or response functions. Differences in measured indices can arise from sensor spectral response even if the ground target is unchanged.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>Saving an 8-bit raster as 16-bit changes the storage type, not the information originally measured. Likewise, applying a display contrast stretch does not improve physical radiometric calibration.</p>", "text": "Spectral Spectral resolution concerns band placement and width as well as band count. Broad multispectral bands can identify major classes; narrow hyperspectral bands may resolve subtle absorption features, at the cost of more complex calibration, noise management and dimensionality reduction. Radiometric An n-bit quantiser has 2^n possible codes (8-bit = 256; 12-bit = 4096). More digital levels do not automatically imply a higher signal-to-noise ratio or physically meaningful discrimination. Cross-sensor caution A “red” band on two sensors need not have identical wavelength boundaries or response functions. Differences in measured indices can arise from sensor spectral response even if the ground target is unchanged. Exam trap Saving an 8-bit raster as 16-bit changes the storage type, not the information originally measured. Likewise, applying a display contrast stretch does not improve physical radiometric calibration."}, {"title": "5 | Temporal resolution and change", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Revisit frequency is not equivalent to the number of usable observations. Clouds, shadows, acquisition geometry and missing scenes can reduce effective temporal coverage.</p><h3>Comparability</h3><p>Lecture 6 slides 20–21 specify equal GRE, matching datum/projection/coordinates, co-registration RMSE &lt; 0.5 pixel, comparable look angles, matching spectral band characteristics, calibrated radiance/reflectance and known processing history.</p><h3>Seasonality</h3><p>A wet-season versus dry-season comparison may conflate phenology or moisture with disturbance. Prefer matched seasonal windows or model seasonal cycles using a multi-date series.</p><h3>Robust design</h3><p>A two-date comparison is simple but vulnerable to anomalies. A baseline and post-event trajectory, with enough clear observations, supports assessment of persistence and departures from natural variability.</p><h3>Caution</h3><p>The lecture’s &lt;0.5 pixel co-registration benchmark is a useful course-specific target, not a guarantee that narrow-boundary change is error-free.</p>", "text": "Foundation Revisit frequency is not equivalent to the number of usable observations. Clouds, shadows, acquisition geometry and missing scenes can reduce effective temporal coverage. Comparability Lecture 6 slides 20–21 specify equal GRE, matching datum/projection/coordinates, co-registration RMSE < 0.5 pixel, comparable look angles, matching spectral band characteristics, calibrated radiance/reflectance and known processing history. Seasonality A wet-season versus dry-season comparison may conflate phenology or moisture with disturbance. Prefer matched seasonal windows or model seasonal cycles using a multi-date series. Robust design A two-date comparison is simple but vulnerable to anomalies. A baseline and post-event trajectory, with enough clear observations, supports assessment of persistence and departures from natural variability. Caution The lecture’s <0.5 pixel co-registration benchmark is a useful course-specific target, not a guarantee that narrow-boundary change is error-free."}, {"title": "6 | Passive and active sensing: choosing evidence", "html": "<h3>Passive optical</h3><p>Useful for reflectance-based land cover, vegetation indices, coastal habitats and water colour where visible. Constraints include cloud, illumination, atmospheric effects, shadows and water-column attenuation.</p><h3>SAR</h3><p>Active microwave radar supplies its own illumination and often observes through cloud and at night. Its response depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, roughness, moisture and structure. Speckle, geometric distortions and wet-surface ambiguities require care. Lecture 12 addresses radar correction, classification and multi-date analysis.</p><h3>LiDAR</h3><p>Active laser ranging can measure three-dimensional structure and elevation. Useful for canopy height and structural attributes, but occlusion, sampling density, scan geometry, georeferencing and classification affect estimates. Lecture 12 and the terrestrial laser-scanning guest lecture provide applications.</p><h3>Complementarity</h3><p>A cloudy mangrove-monitoring study might combine optical indices for canopy spectral condition, SAR for flood or structure-related information, and LiDAR or field plots for height/structure. Each measures different properties; agreement is informative but not guaranteed.</p>", "text": "Passive optical Useful for reflectance-based land cover, vegetation indices, coastal habitats and water colour where visible. Constraints include cloud, illumination, atmospheric effects, shadows and water-column attenuation. SAR Active microwave radar supplies its own illumination and often observes through cloud and at night. Its response depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, roughness, moisture and structure. Speckle, geometric distortions and wet-surface ambiguities require care. Lecture 12 addresses radar correction, classification and multi-date analysis. LiDAR Active laser ranging can measure three-dimensional structure and elevation. Useful for canopy height and structural attributes, but occlusion, sampling density, scan geometry, georeferencing and classification affect estimates. Lecture 12 and the terrestrial laser-scanning guest lecture provide applications. Complementarity A cloudy mangrove-monitoring study might combine optical indices for canopy spectral condition, SAR for flood or structure-related information, and LiDAR or field plots for height/structure. Each measures different properties; agreement is informative but not guaranteed."}, {"title": "7 | Selecting analysis-ready data and ancillary information", "html": "<h3>Data selection checklist</h3><p>Target definition; spatial footprint; spectral bands; radiometric units; revisit and clear-scene availability; processing level; geolocation; sensor continuity; licence/cost; reference-data availability.</p><h3>ARD is not “no checks”</h3><p>Analysis-ready products reduce repetitive pre-processing, but you still must inspect metadata, quality masks, projection, scaling factors, valid observations and suitability for the target. Lecture 5 explicitly emphasises checking correction levels and measurement units.</p><h3>Ancillary datasets</h3><p>Field observations, high-resolution imagery, DEMs, tides, land-cover masks and disturbance records can improve interpretation and validation. Their dates, resolution and independence must be checked.</p><h3>Design choice</h3><p>For a 10-year mangrove trend, a consistent Landsat surface-reflectance series may be preferable to two unmatched high-resolution images; Sentinel-2 may complement recent years. For individual 5 m buildings, Landsat alone is inadequate.</p>", "text": "Data selection checklist Target definition; spatial footprint; spectral bands; radiometric units; revisit and clear-scene availability; processing level; geolocation; sensor continuity; licence/cost; reference-data availability. ARD is not “no checks” Analysis-ready products reduce repetitive pre-processing, but you still must inspect metadata, quality masks, projection, scaling factors, valid observations and suitability for the target. Lecture 5 explicitly emphasises checking correction levels and measurement units. Ancillary datasets Field observations, high-resolution imagery, DEMs, tides, land-cover masks and disturbance records can improve interpretation and validation. Their dates, resolution and independence must be checked. Design choice For a 10-year mangrove trend, a consistent Landsat surface-reflectance series may be preferable to two unmatched high-resolution images; Sentinel-2 may complement recent years. For individual 5 m buildings, Landsat alone is inadequate."}, {"title": "8 | Worked critique: four high-value errors", "html": "<h3>Scenario</h3><p>A researcher maps 2016–2026 mangrove degradation using one Landsat and one Sentinel-2 scene. They resample both to 1 m, compute NDVI from raw digital numbers, mark every negative difference as degradation, and visually validate with the same scenes.</p><h3>Error 1: unsupported spatial detail</h3><p>Resampling cannot recover individual narrow mangrove strips. Correct by analysing at justified common support, using higher-resolution reference data where required and discussing mixed pixels.</p><h3>Error 2: inconsistent physical measurements</h3><p>Uncorrected digital numbers and different band response functions can introduce artificial change. Correct by using calibrated surface reflectance and checking cross-sensor harmonisation.</p><h3>Error 3: invalid causal interpretation</h3><p>Negative NDVI can reflect seasonal, tidal or atmospheric effects. Correct using masks, comparable seasons, multi-date baselines, threshold sensitivity and corroborating indicators.</p><h3>Error 4: circular validation</h3><p>Interpreting the same scenes used to produce the map is not independent ground truth. Correct using independent field or suitable high-resolution reference data and a defensible sample design.</p><h3>What earns marks</h3><p>State the flaw, explain the mechanism, describe its consequence, and propose a feasible correction. Rank errors by their effect on the research question.</p>", "text": "Scenario A researcher maps 2016–2026 mangrove degradation using one Landsat and one Sentinel-2 scene. They resample both to 1 m, compute NDVI from raw digital numbers, mark every negative difference as degradation, and visually validate with the same scenes. Error 1: unsupported spatial detail Resampling cannot recover individual narrow mangrove strips. Correct by analysing at justified common support, using higher-resolution reference data where required and discussing mixed pixels. Error 2: inconsistent physical measurements Uncorrected digital numbers and different band response functions can introduce artificial change. Correct by using calibrated surface reflectance and checking cross-sensor harmonisation. Error 3: invalid causal interpretation Negative NDVI can reflect seasonal, tidal or atmospheric effects. Correct using masks, comparable seasons, multi-date baselines, threshold sensitivity and corroborating indicators. Error 4: circular validation Interpreting the same scenes used to produce the map is not independent ground truth. Correct using independent field or suitable high-resolution reference data and a defensible sample design. What earns marks State the flaw, explain the mechanism, describe its consequence, and propose a feasible correction. Rank errors by their effect on the research question."}, {"title": "9 | Exam practice with model answers", "html": "<details><summary>Q1 (8 marks): 5 m houses — A researcher chooses 30 m Landsat to map individual 5 m over-water houses. Critique.</summary><p>individual houses are sub-pixel, yielding mixed roof/water responses and unreliable object boundaries. Use suitable very-high-resolution imagery, align dates and tides, apply object-based or other appropriate detection, and validate against independent high-resolution or field references. Landsat may provide regional context.</p></details><details><summary>Q2 (8 marks): radiometric claim — An analyst exports an 8-bit image as 16-bit and claims improved detection of subtle vegetation stress.</summary><p>bit-depth conversion cannot create new signal information. Distinguish file encoding from sensor quantisation and SNR; select calibrated imagery with appropriate bands and noise characteristics and test sensitivity against field observations.</p></details><details><summary>Q3 (10 marks): false change — Two optical dates differ in season, atmosphere and co-registration. An NDVI subtraction shows extensive loss.</summary><p>seasonality, atmospheric effects and misalignment can all cause false change. Use comparable surface reflectance, quality masks, matched phenological windows, robust co-registration, multiple dates and independent change validation.</p></details><details><summary>Q4 (GEOM7001 extension; 10 marks) — Choose optical, SAR or LiDAR to monitor mangrove damage during a cloudy monsoon.</summary><p>optical supports canopy spectral indicators but suffers cloud and tide limitations; SAR improves temporal coverage through cloud but requires speckle/geometry controls and careful interpretation of moisture/structure; LiDAR measures structure and height but may have sparse temporal availability and high cost. Combine according to the target variable and available reference data, rather than assuming any sensor directly measures “health”.</p></details><details><summary>Q5 (12 marks): defend a design — Design a 10-year narrow-coastline change study.</summary><p>define detectable change and minimum mapping unit; select spatial/spectral/temporal support; use corrected, quality-screened observations; harmonise and register dates; build comparable seasonal composites or time series; extract indicators and changes; independently validate across change/no-change strata; report omission, commission and area uncertainty; explain unresolved mixed pixels and observation gaps.</p></details>", "text": "Q1 (8 marks): 5 m houses — A researcher chooses 30 m Landsat to map individual 5 m over-water houses. Critique.individual houses are sub-pixel, yielding mixed roof/water responses and unreliable object boundaries. Use suitable very-high-resolution imagery, align dates and tides, apply object-based or other appropriate detection, and validate against independent high-resolution or field references. Landsat may provide regional context. Q2 (8 marks): radiometric claim — An analyst exports an 8-bit image as 16-bit and claims improved detection of subtle vegetation stress.bit-depth conversion cannot create new signal information. Distinguish file encoding from sensor quantisation and SNR; select calibrated imagery with appropriate bands and noise characteristics and test sensitivity against field observations. Q3 (10 marks): false change — Two optical dates differ in season, atmosphere and co-registration. An NDVI subtraction shows extensive loss.seasonality, atmospheric effects and misalignment can all cause false change. Use comparable surface reflectance, quality masks, matched phenological windows, robust co-registration, multiple dates and independent change validation. Q4 (GEOM7001 extension; 10 marks) — Choose optical, SAR or LiDAR to monitor mangrove damage during a cloudy monsoon.optical supports canopy spectral indicators but suffers cloud and tide limitations; SAR improves temporal coverage through cloud but requires speckle/geometry controls and careful interpretation of moisture/structure; LiDAR measures structure and height but may have sparse temporal availability and high cost. Combine according to the target variable and available reference data, rather than assuming any sensor directly measures “health”. Q5 (12 marks): defend a design — Design a 10-year narrow-coastline change study.define detectable change and minimum mapping unit; select spatial/spectral/temporal support; use corrected, quality-screened observations; harmonise and register dates; build comparable seasonal composites or time series; extract indicators and changes; independently validate across change/no-change strata; report omission, commission and area uncertainty; explain unresolved mixed pixels and observation gaps."}, {"title": "10 | Self-test and rapid revision", "html": "<h3>Recall</h3><p>Explain, without notes: why is surface reflectance preferable to raw DN for multi-date NDVI? Why does resampling not increase true spatial detail? Why is a nominal revisit not the same as a usable revisit?</p><h3>Application</h3><p>For each study objective, write a one-sentence justification for spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, then identify one unavoidable limitation.</p><h3>Exam writing formula</h3><p>CHOICE → PHYSICAL/STATISTICAL REASON → LIKELY ERROR IF IGNORED → MITIGATION → REMAINING LIMITATION.</p><h3>Preparation target</h3><p>Aim to answer each 8–12-mark critique in concise, causally linked points. A list of method names without justification is weaker than fewer well-explained, high-impact corrections.</p>", "text": "Recall Explain, without notes: why is surface reflectance preferable to raw DN for multi-date NDVI? Why does resampling not increase true spatial detail? Why is a nominal revisit not the same as a usable revisit? Application For each study objective, write a one-sentence justification for spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, then identify one unavoidable limitation. Exam writing formula CHOICE → PHYSICAL/STATISTICAL REASON → LIKELY ERROR IF IGNORED → MITIGATION → REMAINING LIMITATION. Preparation target Aim to answer each 8–12-mark critique in concise, causally linked points. A list of method names without justification is weaker than fewer well-explained, high-impact corrections."}, {"title": "Course source guide", "html": "<p>Lecture numbering follows filenames and slide labels; slide numbering can differ from PDF page numbering.</p><ul><li>Lecture 2: geom3001_7001_L2_DigitalEarths_EO_2026.pdf (slide 5 and multi-temporal requirements).</li><li>Lecture 5: geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf (geometric, radiometric and atmospheric corrections; ARD metadata).</li><li>Lecture 6: geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (slides 20–21, multi-date comparability).</li><li>Lecture 7: geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (slides 6–7, source–target–observer geometry).</li><li>Lecture 8: geom3001_7001_L8_AquaticRemoteSensing_2026.pdf (slide 7, water-column and interface effects).</li><li>Lecture 12: geom3001_7001_L12_Radar_Lidar_Processing_2026(1).pdf (SAR and LiDAR processing).</li></ul>", "text": "Lecture numbering follows filenames and slide labels; slide numbering can differ from PDF page numbering. Lecture 2: geom3001_7001_L2_DigitalEarths_EO_2026.pdf (slide 5 and multi-temporal requirements).Lecture 5: geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf (geometric, radiometric and atmospheric corrections; ARD metadata).Lecture 6: geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (slides 20–21, multi-date comparability).Lecture 7: geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (slides 6–7, source–target–observer geometry).Lecture 8: geom3001_7001_L8_AquaticRemoteSensing_2026.pdf (slide 7, water-column and interface effects).Lecture 12: geom3001_7001_L12_Radar_Lidar_Processing_2026(1).pdf (SAR and LiDAR processing)."}], "text": ["Overview This is a focused first module, not the full course. Course-derived principles are cited in the source list; illustrative scenarios and model answers are supplementary exam practice.", "1 | What the examiner is assessing Concept A defensible Earth observation study starts with a measurable target and a question. Work backwards from the output: what object or process must be detected, at what spatial and temporal scale, and with what acceptable uncertainty? Course workflow Problem statement → image acquisition → pre-processing → image enhancement → thematic/quantitative extraction → error assessment → change detection → GIS integration → problem solving. This sequence appears in Lecture 2, slide 5, and Lecture 8, slide 3. In real projects, quality control and validation are iterative. Critical reasoning For each proposed choice, ask: what assumption is required? What physical or statistical process could violate it? What bias or uncertainty would result? How can the problem be tested or mitigated? Example A student chooses the highest-resolution image available. This is not sufficient justification: it may lack suitable spectral bands, cloud-free acquisitions, consistent historical coverage, or an affordable licensing arrangement.", "2 | Radiation, targets and measurements Physical chain For reflected-solar imagery, solar radiation travels through the atmosphere, interacts with the target, and returns through the atmosphere to the sensor. Recorded radiance is not identical to target surface reflectance. Scattering, absorption, illumination geometry and neighbouring targets affect the observation. Lecture 7 introduces the source–target–observer geometry (slides 6–7); Lecture 5 covers atmospheric correction. Spectral behaviour Vegetation typically absorbs red light through pigments and reflects strongly in the near infrared because of leaf structure; water strongly absorbs NIR and SWIR, while sediment, depth and bottom reflectance complicate visible-water signals. These are useful physical tendencies, not universal signatures. Equation NDVI = (NIR − Red) / (NIR + Red). It is a relative spectral contrast, not a direct measurement of biomass, health or canopy mortality. Its interpretation depends on background, saturation, atmosphere, illumination and target conditions. Critical application If NDVI decreases after a cyclone, plausible explanations include leaf loss, sediment/water background changes, cloud shadow, phenology or tidal inundation. Evidence of a negative difference alone cannot establish the cause. Source note Lecture 7: radiative transfer fundamentals; Lecture 5: atmospheric correction; Lecture 8: aquatic-interface effects.", "3 | Spatial resolution and mixed pixels Foundation Ground resolution element (GRE) describes the ground support of image measurements. A 30 m pixel covers 900 m²; a 10 m pixel covers 100 m². Objects smaller than a pixel can influence its signal but cannot generally be delineated reliably as separate objects. Mixed pixels A coastal pixel may contain mangrove canopy, water, exposed sediment and shadow. Its spectrum is a mixture, so a threshold-based classification may depend on tide and viewing geometry even without ecological change. Resampling Nearest neighbour copies original values and is appropriate for categorical classes. Bilinear and cubic interpolation produce new continuous pixel values and may be useful for imagery, but can modify spectra and boundaries. Resampling 30 m data to 1 m does not create 1 m information. Trade-off Very-high-resolution imagery can resolve narrow features, but may have fewer spectral bands, shorter archives, higher cost and greater sensitivity to shadows. Choose resolution from the target size and decision requirements, not from a blanket “higher is better” rule.", "4 | Spectral and radiometric resolution Spectral Spectral resolution concerns band placement and width as well as band count. Broad multispectral bands can identify major classes; narrow hyperspectral bands may resolve subtle absorption features, at the cost of more complex calibration, noise management and dimensionality reduction. Radiometric An n-bit quantiser has 2^n possible codes (8-bit = 256; 12-bit = 4096). More digital levels do not automatically imply a higher signal-to-noise ratio or physically meaningful discrimination. Cross-sensor caution A “red” band on two sensors need not have identical wavelength boundaries or response functions. Differences in measured indices can arise from sensor spectral response even if the ground target is unchanged. Exam trap Saving an 8-bit raster as 16-bit changes the storage type, not the information originally measured. Likewise, applying a display contrast stretch does not improve physical radiometric calibration.", "5 | Temporal resolution and change Foundation Revisit frequency is not equivalent to the number of usable observations. Clouds, shadows, acquisition geometry and missing scenes can reduce effective temporal coverage. Comparability Lecture 6 slides 20–21 specify equal GRE, matching datum/projection/coordinates, co-registration RMSE < 0.5 pixel, comparable look angles, matching spectral band characteristics, calibrated radiance/reflectance and known processing history. Seasonality A wet-season versus dry-season comparison may conflate phenology or moisture with disturbance. Prefer matched seasonal windows or model seasonal cycles using a multi-date series. Robust design A two-date comparison is simple but vulnerable to anomalies. A baseline and post-event trajectory, with enough clear observations, supports assessment of persistence and departures from natural variability. Caution The lecture’s <0.5 pixel co-registration benchmark is a useful course-specific target, not a guarantee that narrow-boundary change is error-free.", "6 | Passive and active sensing: choosing evidence Passive optical Useful for reflectance-based land cover, vegetation indices, coastal habitats and water colour where visible. Constraints include cloud, illumination, atmospheric effects, shadows and water-column attenuation. SAR Active microwave radar supplies its own illumination and often observes through cloud and at night. Its response depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, roughness, moisture and structure. Speckle, geometric distortions and wet-surface ambiguities require care. Lecture 12 addresses radar correction, classification and multi-date analysis. LiDAR Active laser ranging can measure three-dimensional structure and elevation. Useful for canopy height and structural attributes, but occlusion, sampling density, scan geometry, georeferencing and classification affect estimates. Lecture 12 and the terrestrial laser-scanning guest lecture provide applications. Complementarity A cloudy mangrove-monitoring study might combine optical indices for canopy spectral condition, SAR for flood or structure-related information, and LiDAR or field plots for height/structure. Each measures different properties; agreement is informative but not guaranteed.", "7 | Selecting analysis-ready data and ancillary information Data selection checklist Target definition; spatial footprint; spectral bands; radiometric units; revisit and clear-scene availability; processing level; geolocation; sensor continuity; licence/cost; reference-data availability. ARD is not “no checks” Analysis-ready products reduce repetitive pre-processing, but you still must inspect metadata, quality masks, projection, scaling factors, valid observations and suitability for the target. Lecture 5 explicitly emphasises checking correction levels and measurement units. Ancillary datasets Field observations, high-resolution imagery, DEMs, tides, land-cover masks and disturbance records can improve interpretation and validation. Their dates, resolution and independence must be checked. Design choice For a 10-year mangrove trend, a consistent Landsat surface-reflectance series may be preferable to two unmatched high-resolution images; Sentinel-2 may complement recent years. For individual 5 m buildings, Landsat alone is inadequate.", "8 | Worked critique: four high-value errors Scenario A researcher maps 2016–2026 mangrove degradation using one Landsat and one Sentinel-2 scene. They resample both to 1 m, compute NDVI from raw digital numbers, mark every negative difference as degradation, and visually validate with the same scenes. Error 1: unsupported spatial detail Resampling cannot recover individual narrow mangrove strips. Correct by analysing at justified common support, using higher-resolution reference data where required and discussing mixed pixels. Error 2: inconsistent physical measurements Uncorrected digital numbers and different band response functions can introduce artificial change. Correct by using calibrated surface reflectance and checking cross-sensor harmonisation. Error 3: invalid causal interpretation Negative NDVI can reflect seasonal, tidal or atmospheric effects. Correct using masks, comparable seasons, multi-date baselines, threshold sensitivity and corroborating indicators. Error 4: circular validation Interpreting the same scenes used to produce the map is not independent ground truth. Correct using independent field or suitable high-resolution reference data and a defensible sample design. What earns marks State the flaw, explain the mechanism, describe its consequence, and propose a feasible correction. Rank errors by their effect on the research question.", "9 | Exam practice with model answers Q1 (8 marks): 5 m houses — A researcher chooses 30 m Landsat to map individual 5 m over-water houses. Critique.individual houses are sub-pixel, yielding mixed roof/water responses and unreliable object boundaries. Use suitable very-high-resolution imagery, align dates and tides, apply object-based or other appropriate detection, and validate against independent high-resolution or field references. Landsat may provide regional context. Q2 (8 marks): radiometric claim — An analyst exports an 8-bit image as 16-bit and claims improved detection of subtle vegetation stress.bit-depth conversion cannot create new signal information. Distinguish file encoding from sensor quantisation and SNR; select calibrated imagery with appropriate bands and noise characteristics and test sensitivity against field observations. Q3 (10 marks): false change — Two optical dates differ in season, atmosphere and co-registration. An NDVI subtraction shows extensive loss.seasonality, atmospheric effects and misalignment can all cause false change. Use comparable surface reflectance, quality masks, matched phenological windows, robust co-registration, multiple dates and independent change validation. Q4 (GEOM7001 extension; 10 marks) — Choose optical, SAR or LiDAR to monitor mangrove damage during a cloudy monsoon.optical supports canopy spectral indicators but suffers cloud and tide limitations; SAR improves temporal coverage through cloud but requires speckle/geometry controls and careful interpretation of moisture/structure; LiDAR measures structure and height but may have sparse temporal availability and high cost. Combine according to the target variable and available reference data, rather than assuming any sensor directly measures “health”. Q5 (12 marks): defend a design — Design a 10-year narrow-coastline change study.define detectable change and minimum mapping unit; select spatial/spectral/temporal support; use corrected, quality-screened observations; harmonise and register dates; build comparable seasonal composites or time series; extract indicators and changes; independently validate across change/no-change strata; report omission, commission and area uncertainty; explain unresolved mixed pixels and observation gaps.", "10 | Self-test and rapid revision Recall Explain, without notes: why is surface reflectance preferable to raw DN for multi-date NDVI? Why does resampling not increase true spatial detail? Why is a nominal revisit not the same as a usable revisit? Application For each study objective, write a one-sentence justification for spatial, spectral, radiometric and temporal requirements, then identify one unavoidable limitation. Exam writing formula CHOICE → PHYSICAL/STATISTICAL REASON → LIKELY ERROR IF IGNORED → MITIGATION → REMAINING LIMITATION. Preparation target Aim to answer each 8–12-mark critique in concise, causally linked points. A list of method names without justification is weaker than fewer well-explained, high-impact corrections.", "Course source guide Lecture numbering follows filenames and slide labels; slide numbering can differ from PDF page numbering. Lecture 2: geom3001_7001_L2_DigitalEarths_EO_2026.pdf (slide 5 and multi-temporal requirements).Lecture 5: geom3001_7001_L5_Pre-Processing_2026.pdf (geometric, radiometric and atmospheric corrections; ARD metadata).Lecture 6: geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (slides 20–21, multi-date comparability).Lecture 7: geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (slides 6–7, source–target–observer geometry).Lecture 8: geom3001_7001_L8_AquaticRemoteSensing_2026.pdf (slide 7, water-column and interface effects).Lecture 12: geom3001_7001_L12_Radar_Lidar_Processing_2026(1).pdf (SAR and LiDAR processing)."]}, {"id": 15, "title": "Preprocessing & Corrections", "category": "SUMMARY-QUICK REVIEW", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Module2_Preprocessing.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "Overview", "html": "<p>Primary course sources: Lecture 4 (PDF pp. 9–58), Lecture 6, and Lecture 8. Course concepts are identified with slide references; worked examples and mock questions are supplementary.</p>", "text": "Primary course sources: Lecture 4 (PDF pp. 9–58), Lecture 6, and Lecture 8. Course concepts are identified with slide references; worked examples and mock questions are supplementary."}, {"title": "1 | Why pre-processing matters", "html": "<h3>Concept</h3><p>The course frames pre-processing as the work needed to make imagery geometrically meaningful and radiometrically comparable before information extraction. Raw image values contain both target information and effects of the sensor, atmosphere, viewing geometry and environment. A visually convincing image is not necessarily suitable for quantitative comparison.</p><h3>Course anchors</h3><p>Lecture 4, PDF pp. 9–16: accessible format and metadata; geometric distortions; radiometric calibration; analysis-ready data (ARD). The course distinguishes standard geometric/radiometric corrections from additional terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections.</p><h3>Decision rule</h3><p>First identify the product level, units, scale factors, coordinate reference system, quality flags and corrections already applied. Do not blindly repeat a correction on ARD. Verify whether the product is fit for your exact application.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>A student says “I downloaded analysis-ready data, so no checks are needed.” ARD reduces routine processing, but does not guarantee that cloud masks, co-registration, spectral compatibility, tidal state or application-specific corrections are sufficient.</p>", "text": "Concept The course frames pre-processing as the work needed to make imagery geometrically meaningful and radiometrically comparable before information extraction. Raw image values contain both target information and effects of the sensor, atmosphere, viewing geometry and environment. A visually convincing image is not necessarily suitable for quantitative comparison. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF pp. 9–16: accessible format and metadata; geometric distortions; radiometric calibration; analysis-ready data (ARD). The course distinguishes standard geometric/radiometric corrections from additional terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections. Decision rule First identify the product level, units, scale factors, coordinate reference system, quality flags and corrections already applied. Do not blindly repeat a correction on ARD. Verify whether the product is fit for your exact application. Exam trap A student says “I downloaded analysis-ready data, so no checks are needed.” ARD reduces routine processing, but does not guarantee that cloud masks, co-registration, spectral compatibility, tidal state or application-specific corrections are sufficient."}, {"title": "2 | Geometric correction, rectification and registration", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Geometric distortions displace pixels relative to their actual ground locations. Datum, map projection and coordinate system describe how the image is positioned in geographic space. Rectification places imagery in map coordinates; image-to-image registration aligns observations with one another. Orthorectification additionally addresses terrain/relief displacement using geometry and elevation information.</p><h3>How it works</h3><p>Select ground control points (GCPs) matching identifiable image and reference features; fit a coordinate transformation; inspect residuals and spatial distribution; then assign output pixel values using a resampling method. A first-order polynomial can model translation, rotation, scale and skew: x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y; y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y.</p><h3>Quality control</h3><p>Lecture 4, PDF pp. 25–30, teaches checking transformation accuracy mathematically and visually and uses a standard RMSE tolerance &lt;0.5 pixel. Crucially, the lecture warns that GCP RMSE assesses the control points, not the entire image. Independent check points and inspection across the scene strengthen evaluation.</p><h3>Resampling</h3><p>Nearest neighbour retains original pixel values and is often preferred for categorical classes or when preserving measured values is critical. Bilinear interpolation smooths continuous imagery using neighbouring pixels. Cubic convolution can produce smoother results but changes values and can introduce overshoot. A 30 m image resampled to 1 m does not gain true 1 m detail.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>“Both images have 10 m pixels, therefore they are aligned.” Equal grid size does not imply matching geolocation. Sub-pixel shifts near shorelines or forest edges can create false change.</p>", "text": "Foundation Geometric distortions displace pixels relative to their actual ground locations. Datum, map projection and coordinate system describe how the image is positioned in geographic space. Rectification places imagery in map coordinates; image-to-image registration aligns observations with one another. Orthorectification additionally addresses terrain/relief displacement using geometry and elevation information. How it works Select ground control points (GCPs) matching identifiable image and reference features; fit a coordinate transformation; inspect residuals and spatial distribution; then assign output pixel values using a resampling method. A first-order polynomial can model translation, rotation, scale and skew: x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y; y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y. Quality control Lecture 4, PDF pp. 25–30, teaches checking transformation accuracy mathematically and visually and uses a standard RMSE tolerance <0.5 pixel. Crucially, the lecture warns that GCP RMSE assesses the control points, not the entire image. Independent check points and inspection across the scene strengthen evaluation. Resampling Nearest neighbour retains original pixel values and is often preferred for categorical classes or when preserving measured values is critical. Bilinear interpolation smooths continuous imagery using neighbouring pixels. Cubic convolution can produce smoother results but changes values and can introduce overshoot. A 30 m image resampled to 1 m does not gain true 1 m detail. Exam trap “Both images have 10 m pixels, therefore they are aligned.” Equal grid size does not imply matching geolocation. Sub-pixel shifts near shorelines or forest edges can create false change."}, {"title": "3 | Radiometric calibration: DN to physical measurements", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Digital numbers (DN) are quantised sensor outputs. Radiometric calibration converts them to physically interpretable at-sensor quantities. The lecture gives Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ, with coefficients specific to band and sensor. Reflectance is dimensionless; radiance is not. Sensor-specific scale factors and offsets matter.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>For an illustrative band with DN = 500, gain = 0.02 and offset = 1.5, at-sensor radiance is 11.5 in the product’s radiance units. This is a teaching example, not a calibration for any real satellite. To compute surface reflectance, atmospheric effects must also be addressed.</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>Comparing raw DN from different sensors or dates may confound changes in gain, solar illumination and atmosphere with target change. Quantitative biophysical modelling and spectral-library matching demand appropriate physical units.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>“I converted the image to 16-bit and improved radiometric sensitivity.” Increasing storage bit depth cannot recreate signal precision absent from the original sensor measurement.</p>", "text": "Foundation Digital numbers (DN) are quantised sensor outputs. Radiometric calibration converts them to physically interpretable at-sensor quantities. The lecture gives Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ, with coefficients specific to band and sensor. Reflectance is dimensionless; radiance is not. Sensor-specific scale factors and offsets matter. Worked example For an illustrative band with DN = 500, gain = 0.02 and offset = 1.5, at-sensor radiance is 11.5 in the product’s radiance units. This is a teaching example, not a calibration for any real satellite. To compute surface reflectance, atmospheric effects must also be addressed. Why it matters Comparing raw DN from different sensors or dates may confound changes in gain, solar illumination and atmosphere with target change. Quantitative biophysical modelling and spectral-library matching demand appropriate physical units. Exam trap “I converted the image to 16-bit and improved radiometric sensitivity.” Increasing storage bit depth cannot recreate signal precision absent from the original sensor measurement."}, {"title": "4 | Atmospheric correction: sensor versus surface", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Incoming and reflected radiation can be scattered and absorbed by atmospheric gases and aerosols. At-sensor radiance can include path radiance and a surface signal modified by atmospheric transmission. At-sensor reflectance is not equivalent to at-surface reflectance.</p><h3>Lecture sequence</h3><p>Lecture 4, PDF pp. 39–47: raw DN → calibrated at-sensor radiance (optionally at-sensor reflectance) → atmospheric correction to surface units → topographic/BRDF or other corrections as needed. The lecture describes absolute correction using radiative-transfer modelling and relative correction using empirical relationships.</p><h3>Absolute versus relative</h3><p>Absolute atmospheric correction aims for physically meaningful surface reflectance using atmospheric state, sensor and illumination geometry. Relative radiometric normalisation makes one scene comparable with a reference, often through stable targets or regression, but does not necessarily recover absolute surface reflectance.</p><h3>Worked judgement</h3><p>A study comparing the same vegetation index across 12 years should favour consistently processed surface-reflectance data, quality masking and checks for residual cross-date bias. If reliable surface reflectance is unavailable, relative normalisation may help, but its assumptions and limitations must be explicit.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>“A histogram stretch is atmospheric correction.” A stretch improves visual contrast; it does not model scattering, absorption or surface reflectance.</p>", "text": "Foundation Incoming and reflected radiation can be scattered and absorbed by atmospheric gases and aerosols. At-sensor radiance can include path radiance and a surface signal modified by atmospheric transmission. At-sensor reflectance is not equivalent to at-surface reflectance. Lecture sequence Lecture 4, PDF pp. 39–47: raw DN → calibrated at-sensor radiance (optionally at-sensor reflectance) → atmospheric correction to surface units → topographic/BRDF or other corrections as needed. The lecture describes absolute correction using radiative-transfer modelling and relative correction using empirical relationships. Absolute versus relative Absolute atmospheric correction aims for physically meaningful surface reflectance using atmospheric state, sensor and illumination geometry. Relative radiometric normalisation makes one scene comparable with a reference, often through stable targets or regression, but does not necessarily recover absolute surface reflectance. Worked judgement A study comparing the same vegetation index across 12 years should favour consistently processed surface-reflectance data, quality masking and checks for residual cross-date bias. If reliable surface reflectance is unavailable, relative normalisation may help, but its assumptions and limitations must be explicit. Exam trap “A histogram stretch is atmospheric correction.” A stretch improves visual contrast; it does not model scattering, absorption or surface reflectance."}, {"title": "5 | Topographic and directional effects", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Slope and aspect alter illumination, so the same vegetation can appear darker on a shaded slope than a sun-facing slope. BRDF describes the directional dependence of reflected radiation on illumination and viewing geometry; even flat targets can show directional effects.</p><h3>Course anchors</h3><p>Lecture 4, PDF pp. 49–56 covers terrain effects, band ratios and BRDF. Band ratios may reduce some multiplicative illumination variation, but do not universally remove shadows or all BRDF effects. Terrain normalisation may require DEM, slope/aspect and solar geometry.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>A researcher interprets lower reflectance on south-facing slopes as lower biomass without accounting for illumination. The signal may be a geometry effect, not vegetation condition.</p>", "text": "Foundation Slope and aspect alter illumination, so the same vegetation can appear darker on a shaded slope than a sun-facing slope. BRDF describes the directional dependence of reflected radiation on illumination and viewing geometry; even flat targets can show directional effects. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF pp. 49–56 covers terrain effects, band ratios and BRDF. Band ratios may reduce some multiplicative illumination variation, but do not universally remove shadows or all BRDF effects. Terrain normalisation may require DEM, slope/aspect and solar geometry. Exam trap A researcher interprets lower reflectance on south-facing slopes as lower biomass without accounting for illumination. The signal may be a geometry effect, not vegetation condition."}, {"title": "6 | Environment-specific corrections: coastal and aquatic scenes", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Water adds a surface and a water-column pathway. Waves create sun glint; air–water refraction changes ray direction; water absorbs and scatters light; depth and clarity change the apparent bottom signal. Tides may change exposed area and the water column above the target.</p><h3>Course anchors</h3><p>Lecture 4, PDF p. 58 identifies sun glint. Lecture 8, PDF pp. 7–9 emphasises changing depth, water clarity, tides, waves, currents, refraction, absorption, scattering and reflection. For submerged mapping, ordinary land surface reflectance processing is not enough.</p><h3>Decision rule</h3><p>For seagrass or reef mapping, assess cloud and glint masking, tidal and depth conditions, water clarity, possible water-column correction, and whether the bottom is optically visible. Choose appropriate bands and independent benthic reference observations.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>“A dark patch in shallow water must be seagrass.” It could instead be deeper water, shadow, sediment, substrate or water-column attenuation.</p>", "text": "Foundation Water adds a surface and a water-column pathway. Waves create sun glint; air–water refraction changes ray direction; water absorbs and scatters light; depth and clarity change the apparent bottom signal. Tides may change exposed area and the water column above the target. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF p. 58 identifies sun glint. Lecture 8, PDF pp. 7–9 emphasises changing depth, water clarity, tides, waves, currents, refraction, absorption, scattering and reflection. For submerged mapping, ordinary land surface reflectance processing is not enough. Decision rule For seagrass or reef mapping, assess cloud and glint masking, tidal and depth conditions, water clarity, possible water-column correction, and whether the bottom is optically visible. Choose appropriate bands and independent benthic reference observations. Exam trap “A dark patch in shallow water must be seagrass.” It could instead be deeper water, shadow, sediment, substrate or water-column attenuation."}, {"title": "7 | Enhancement is not correction", "html": "<h3>Foundation</h3><p>Image enhancement improves visual interpretability or emphasises patterns. Contrast stretching, band combinations, principal components and spatial filtering can be useful for exploration or feature extraction. They do not by themselves establish physically comparable reflectance.</p><h3>Practical choice</h3><p>Apply enhancement for interpretation or as a documented analytical transformation, but retain calibrated data for quantitative indices where needed. Distinguish a display stretch from changing numerical pixel values used by a classifier.</p><h3>Exam trap</h3><p>“I applied a separate histogram equalisation to each date and then subtracted the images.” Independent enhancement changes image value distributions and can manufacture apparent differences.</p>", "text": "Foundation Image enhancement improves visual interpretability or emphasises patterns. Contrast stretching, band combinations, principal components and spatial filtering can be useful for exploration or feature extraction. They do not by themselves establish physically comparable reflectance. Practical choice Apply enhancement for interpretation or as a documented analytical transformation, but retain calibrated data for quantitative indices where needed. Distinguish a display stretch from changing numerical pixel values used by a classifier. Exam trap “I applied a separate histogram equalisation to each date and then subtracted the images.” Independent enhancement changes image value distributions and can manufacture apparent differences."}, {"title": "8 | Defensible processing order and audit trail", "html": "<h3>Workflow</h3><p>1. Define target, scale and required outputs. 2. Inspect metadata/product level and sensor suitability. 3. Apply/check geometric positioning and co-registration. 4. Apply/check radiometric calibration and atmospheric correction. 5. Mask invalid pixels. 6. Address terrain, BRDF, glint, water-column or other application-specific effects. 7. Harmonise grids, bands and observation conditions. 8. Extract information. 9. Validate independently and quantify uncertainty. 10. Interpret and report limitations.</p><h3>Important nuance</h3><p>The exact order depends on the sensor, product and algorithms; some operations are bundled in ARD processing. Avoid treating this checklist as a universal software command sequence. Document every input, parameter, exclusion and assumption.</p><h3>GEOM7001 reasoning</h3><p>For each choice, state: the scientific need; the assumption; what can go wrong; the alternative; and the independent evidence that would reveal error.</p>", "text": "Workflow 1. Define target, scale and required outputs. 2. Inspect metadata/product level and sensor suitability. 3. Apply/check geometric positioning and co-registration. 4. Apply/check radiometric calibration and atmospheric correction. 5. Mask invalid pixels. 6. Address terrain, BRDF, glint, water-column or other application-specific effects. 7. Harmonise grids, bands and observation conditions. 8. Extract information. 9. Validate independently and quantify uncertainty. 10. Interpret and report limitations. Important nuance The exact order depends on the sensor, product and algorithms; some operations are bundled in ARD processing. Avoid treating this checklist as a universal software command sequence. Document every input, parameter, exclusion and assumption. GEOM7001 reasoning For each choice, state: the scientific need; the assumption; what can go wrong; the alternative; and the independent evidence that would reveal error."}, {"title": "Practice exam — reveal model answers", "html": "<h3>Q1 | Flawed forest-change workflow (12 marks)</h3><p>A student downloads one 2017 Level-1 image and one 2026 surface-reflectance image, applies separate histogram stretches, resamples both to 2 m, subtracts red-band pixel values, and labels all negative changes as forest loss. Identify four serious flaws and corrections.</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>The input products use incompatible radiometric levels: convert or obtain both as consistently processed surface reflectance. Independent histogram stretches distort quantitative comparability: use physical units and consistent scaling. Resampling does not create 2 m information: choose a defensible common spatial support and verify co-registration. Red-band change alone is not uniquely forest loss: use a vegetation-specific measure, multi-date/seasonal context, land-cover mask and independent reference validation. Discuss clouds, shadows, phenology and uncertainty.</p></details><h3>Q2 | Co-registration (8 marks)</h3><p>Two images have the same projection and 10 m pixels, but their coastlines differ by 7 m. A student claims they are ready for pixel-based change detection. Evaluate.</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>Same CRS and pixel size do not guarantee co-registration. A 7 m displacement is 0.7 pixel, exceeding the course’s &lt;0.5-pixel benchmark. Coastline differences may reflect tides as well as registration error. Check stable landmarks and independent points, correct alignment, and control tidal stage; otherwise narrow boundary zones produce false change.</p></details><h3>Q3 | Absolute or relative atmospheric correction? (10 marks)</h3><p>You need to compare vegetation greenness across years and match reflectance against field spectra. Would a per-image histogram match be sufficient?</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>No. Histogram matching may improve relative appearance but does not guarantee physical surface reflectance and can distort genuine change. Prefer calibrated surface reflectance with consistent atmospheric processing; verify band response and acquisition conditions. Relative normalisation may be a contingency for cross-date consistency if assumptions are met, but it is not a substitute for absolute physical reflectance when matching field spectra.</p></details><h3>Q4 | Aquatic mapping (10 marks)</h3><p>A researcher uses atmospherically corrected Sentinel-2 scenes to map seagrass and concludes all remaining spectral differences represent seagrass cover. Critique.</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>Atmospheric correction addresses the air pathway but not all water-specific effects. Bottom signal varies with depth, turbidity, absorption, scattering, glint and tide. Control observation conditions, assess optical depth and water clarity, remove glint where needed, consider water-column correction and validate with independent benthic observations. Deep or turbid areas may be unmappable with passive optical data.</p></details><h3>Q5 | GEOM7001 extended design (15 marks)</h3><p>Design a preprocessing strategy for mapping drought-related vegetation condition across mountainous terrain from multiple optical dates and two sensors. Explain your trade-offs.</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>Define target and period; choose compatible bands and common spatial support; inspect metadata and ARD status; use consistent surface reflectance, cloud/shadow masks, co-registration and seasonally matched dates; assess terrain illumination and BRDF, using DEM and sensor geometry as needed; harmonise cross-sensor spectral response rather than assuming bands are identical; use robust time-series indicators; test stable surfaces, independent reference samples and sensitivity to correction choices. Trade-off: aggressive normalisation can remove true ecological signal, whereas insufficient correction creates spurious differences.</p></details><h3>Q6 | Applied calibration (6 marks)</h3><p>A fictional band has gain 0.02, offset 1.5 and DN 500. Calculate at-sensor radiance. Has atmospheric correction been completed?</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p>Radiance = 0.02 × 500 + 1.5 = 11.5 (in the calibration’s radiance units). No: radiometric calibration alone does not remove atmospheric scattering and absorption or establish at-surface reflectance.</p></details>", "text": "Q1 | Flawed forest-change workflow (12 marks) A student downloads one 2017 Level-1 image and one 2026 surface-reflectance image, applies separate histogram stretches, resamples both to 2 m, subtracts red-band pixel values, and labels all negative changes as forest loss. Identify four serious flaws and corrections. Reveal model answerThe input products use incompatible radiometric levels: convert or obtain both as consistently processed surface reflectance. Independent histogram stretches distort quantitative comparability: use physical units and consistent scaling. Resampling does not create 2 m information: choose a defensible common spatial support and verify co-registration. Red-band change alone is not uniquely forest loss: use a vegetation-specific measure, multi-date/seasonal context, land-cover mask and independent reference validation. Discuss clouds, shadows, phenology and uncertainty. Q2 | Co-registration (8 marks) Two images have the same projection and 10 m pixels, but their coastlines differ by 7 m. A student claims they are ready for pixel-based change detection. Evaluate. Reveal model answerSame CRS and pixel size do not guarantee co-registration. A 7 m displacement is 0.7 pixel, exceeding the course’s <0.5-pixel benchmark. Coastline differences may reflect tides as well as registration error. Check stable landmarks and independent points, correct alignment, and control tidal stage; otherwise narrow boundary zones produce false change. Q3 | Absolute or relative atmospheric correction? (10 marks) You need to compare vegetation greenness across years and match reflectance against field spectra. Would a per-image histogram match be sufficient? Reveal model answerNo. Histogram matching may improve relative appearance but does not guarantee physical surface reflectance and can distort genuine change. Prefer calibrated surface reflectance with consistent atmospheric processing; verify band response and acquisition conditions. Relative normalisation may be a contingency for cross-date consistency if assumptions are met, but it is not a substitute for absolute physical reflectance when matching field spectra. Q4 | Aquatic mapping (10 marks) A researcher uses atmospherically corrected Sentinel-2 scenes to map seagrass and concludes all remaining spectral differences represent seagrass cover. Critique. Reveal model answerAtmospheric correction addresses the air pathway but not all water-specific effects. Bottom signal varies with depth, turbidity, absorption, scattering, glint and tide. Control observation conditions, assess optical depth and water clarity, remove glint where needed, consider water-column correction and validate with independent benthic observations. Deep or turbid areas may be unmappable with passive optical data. Q5 | GEOM7001 extended design (15 marks) Design a preprocessing strategy for mapping drought-related vegetation condition across mountainous terrain from multiple optical dates and two sensors. Explain your trade-offs. Reveal model answerDefine target and period; choose compatible bands and common spatial support; inspect metadata and ARD status; use consistent surface reflectance, cloud/shadow masks, co-registration and seasonally matched dates; assess terrain illumination and BRDF, using DEM and sensor geometry as needed; harmonise cross-sensor spectral response rather than assuming bands are identical; use robust time-series indicators; test stable surfaces, independent reference samples and sensitivity to correction choices. Trade-off: aggressive normalisation can remove true ecological signal, whereas insufficient correction creates spurious differences. Q6 | Applied calibration (6 marks) A fictional band has gain 0.02, offset 1.5 and DN 500. Calculate at-sensor radiance. Has atmospheric correction been completed? Reveal model answerRadiance = 0.02 × 500 + 1.5 = 11.5 (in the calibration’s radiance units). No: radiometric calibration alone does not remove atmospheric scattering and absorption or establish at-surface reflectance."}, {"title": "Five-point exam checklist", "html": "<ol><li>Define the target, scale and physical units</li><li>Verify geometric, radiometric, spectral and temporal comparability</li><li>Identify confounding effects and residual errors</li><li>Justify correction choices and their assumptions</li><li>Validate independently and report uncertainty</li></ol>", "text": "Define the target, scale and physical unitsVerify geometric, radiometric, spectral and temporal comparabilityIdentify confounding effects and residual errorsJustify correction choices and their assumptionsValidate independently and report uncertainty"}], "text": ["Overview Primary course sources: Lecture 4 (PDF pp. 9–58), Lecture 6, and Lecture 8. Course concepts are identified with slide references; worked examples and mock questions are supplementary.", "1 | Why pre-processing matters Concept The course frames pre-processing as the work needed to make imagery geometrically meaningful and radiometrically comparable before information extraction. Raw image values contain both target information and effects of the sensor, atmosphere, viewing geometry and environment. A visually convincing image is not necessarily suitable for quantitative comparison. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF pp. 9–16: accessible format and metadata; geometric distortions; radiometric calibration; analysis-ready data (ARD). The course distinguishes standard geometric/radiometric corrections from additional terrain, viewing/illumination and environment-specific corrections. Decision rule First identify the product level, units, scale factors, coordinate reference system, quality flags and corrections already applied. Do not blindly repeat a correction on ARD. Verify whether the product is fit for your exact application. Exam trap A student says “I downloaded analysis-ready data, so no checks are needed.” ARD reduces routine processing, but does not guarantee that cloud masks, co-registration, spectral compatibility, tidal state or application-specific corrections are sufficient.", "2 | Geometric correction, rectification and registration Foundation Geometric distortions displace pixels relative to their actual ground locations. Datum, map projection and coordinate system describe how the image is positioned in geographic space. Rectification places imagery in map coordinates; image-to-image registration aligns observations with one another. Orthorectification additionally addresses terrain/relief displacement using geometry and elevation information. How it works Select ground control points (GCPs) matching identifiable image and reference features; fit a coordinate transformation; inspect residuals and spatial distribution; then assign output pixel values using a resampling method. A first-order polynomial can model translation, rotation, scale and skew: x′ = a0 + a1x + a2y; y′ = b0 + b1x + b2y. Quality control Lecture 4, PDF pp. 25–30, teaches checking transformation accuracy mathematically and visually and uses a standard RMSE tolerance <0.5 pixel. Crucially, the lecture warns that GCP RMSE assesses the control points, not the entire image. Independent check points and inspection across the scene strengthen evaluation. Resampling Nearest neighbour retains original pixel values and is often preferred for categorical classes or when preserving measured values is critical. Bilinear interpolation smooths continuous imagery using neighbouring pixels. Cubic convolution can produce smoother results but changes values and can introduce overshoot. A 30 m image resampled to 1 m does not gain true 1 m detail. Exam trap “Both images have 10 m pixels, therefore they are aligned.” Equal grid size does not imply matching geolocation. Sub-pixel shifts near shorelines or forest edges can create false change.", "3 | Radiometric calibration: DN to physical measurements Foundation Digital numbers (DN) are quantised sensor outputs. Radiometric calibration converts them to physically interpretable at-sensor quantities. The lecture gives Lλ = gainλ × DNλ + offsetλ, with coefficients specific to band and sensor. Reflectance is dimensionless; radiance is not. Sensor-specific scale factors and offsets matter. Worked example For an illustrative band with DN = 500, gain = 0.02 and offset = 1.5, at-sensor radiance is 11.5 in the product’s radiance units. This is a teaching example, not a calibration for any real satellite. To compute surface reflectance, atmospheric effects must also be addressed. Why it matters Comparing raw DN from different sensors or dates may confound changes in gain, solar illumination and atmosphere with target change. Quantitative biophysical modelling and spectral-library matching demand appropriate physical units. Exam trap “I converted the image to 16-bit and improved radiometric sensitivity.” Increasing storage bit depth cannot recreate signal precision absent from the original sensor measurement.", "4 | Atmospheric correction: sensor versus surface Foundation Incoming and reflected radiation can be scattered and absorbed by atmospheric gases and aerosols. At-sensor radiance can include path radiance and a surface signal modified by atmospheric transmission. At-sensor reflectance is not equivalent to at-surface reflectance. Lecture sequence Lecture 4, PDF pp. 39–47: raw DN → calibrated at-sensor radiance (optionally at-sensor reflectance) → atmospheric correction to surface units → topographic/BRDF or other corrections as needed. The lecture describes absolute correction using radiative-transfer modelling and relative correction using empirical relationships. Absolute versus relative Absolute atmospheric correction aims for physically meaningful surface reflectance using atmospheric state, sensor and illumination geometry. Relative radiometric normalisation makes one scene comparable with a reference, often through stable targets or regression, but does not necessarily recover absolute surface reflectance. Worked judgement A study comparing the same vegetation index across 12 years should favour consistently processed surface-reflectance data, quality masking and checks for residual cross-date bias. If reliable surface reflectance is unavailable, relative normalisation may help, but its assumptions and limitations must be explicit. Exam trap “A histogram stretch is atmospheric correction.” A stretch improves visual contrast; it does not model scattering, absorption or surface reflectance.", "5 | Topographic and directional effects Foundation Slope and aspect alter illumination, so the same vegetation can appear darker on a shaded slope than a sun-facing slope. BRDF describes the directional dependence of reflected radiation on illumination and viewing geometry; even flat targets can show directional effects. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF pp. 49–56 covers terrain effects, band ratios and BRDF. Band ratios may reduce some multiplicative illumination variation, but do not universally remove shadows or all BRDF effects. Terrain normalisation may require DEM, slope/aspect and solar geometry. Exam trap A researcher interprets lower reflectance on south-facing slopes as lower biomass without accounting for illumination. The signal may be a geometry effect, not vegetation condition.", "6 | Environment-specific corrections: coastal and aquatic scenes Foundation Water adds a surface and a water-column pathway. Waves create sun glint; air–water refraction changes ray direction; water absorbs and scatters light; depth and clarity change the apparent bottom signal. Tides may change exposed area and the water column above the target. Course anchors Lecture 4, PDF p. 58 identifies sun glint. Lecture 8, PDF pp. 7–9 emphasises changing depth, water clarity, tides, waves, currents, refraction, absorption, scattering and reflection. For submerged mapping, ordinary land surface reflectance processing is not enough. Decision rule For seagrass or reef mapping, assess cloud and glint masking, tidal and depth conditions, water clarity, possible water-column correction, and whether the bottom is optically visible. Choose appropriate bands and independent benthic reference observations. Exam trap “A dark patch in shallow water must be seagrass.” It could instead be deeper water, shadow, sediment, substrate or water-column attenuation.", "7 | Enhancement is not correction Foundation Image enhancement improves visual interpretability or emphasises patterns. Contrast stretching, band combinations, principal components and spatial filtering can be useful for exploration or feature extraction. They do not by themselves establish physically comparable reflectance. Practical choice Apply enhancement for interpretation or as a documented analytical transformation, but retain calibrated data for quantitative indices where needed. Distinguish a display stretch from changing numerical pixel values used by a classifier. Exam trap “I applied a separate histogram equalisation to each date and then subtracted the images.” Independent enhancement changes image value distributions and can manufacture apparent differences.", "8 | Defensible processing order and audit trail Workflow 1. Define target, scale and required outputs. 2. Inspect metadata/product level and sensor suitability. 3. Apply/check geometric positioning and co-registration. 4. Apply/check radiometric calibration and atmospheric correction. 5. Mask invalid pixels. 6. Address terrain, BRDF, glint, water-column or other application-specific effects. 7. Harmonise grids, bands and observation conditions. 8. Extract information. 9. Validate independently and quantify uncertainty. 10. Interpret and report limitations. Important nuance The exact order depends on the sensor, product and algorithms; some operations are bundled in ARD processing. Avoid treating this checklist as a universal software command sequence. Document every input, parameter, exclusion and assumption. GEOM7001 reasoning For each choice, state: the scientific need; the assumption; what can go wrong; the alternative; and the independent evidence that would reveal error.", "Practice exam — reveal model answers Q1 | Flawed forest-change workflow (12 marks) A student downloads one 2017 Level-1 image and one 2026 surface-reflectance image, applies separate histogram stretches, resamples both to 2 m, subtracts red-band pixel values, and labels all negative changes as forest loss. Identify four serious flaws and corrections. Reveal model answerThe input products use incompatible radiometric levels: convert or obtain both as consistently processed surface reflectance. Independent histogram stretches distort quantitative comparability: use physical units and consistent scaling. Resampling does not create 2 m information: choose a defensible common spatial support and verify co-registration. Red-band change alone is not uniquely forest loss: use a vegetation-specific measure, multi-date/seasonal context, land-cover mask and independent reference validation. Discuss clouds, shadows, phenology and uncertainty. Q2 | Co-registration (8 marks) Two images have the same projection and 10 m pixels, but their coastlines differ by 7 m. A student claims they are ready for pixel-based change detection. Evaluate. Reveal model answerSame CRS and pixel size do not guarantee co-registration. A 7 m displacement is 0.7 pixel, exceeding the course’s <0.5-pixel benchmark. Coastline differences may reflect tides as well as registration error. Check stable landmarks and independent points, correct alignment, and control tidal stage; otherwise narrow boundary zones produce false change. Q3 | Absolute or relative atmospheric correction? (10 marks) You need to compare vegetation greenness across years and match reflectance against field spectra. Would a per-image histogram match be sufficient? Reveal model answerNo. Histogram matching may improve relative appearance but does not guarantee physical surface reflectance and can distort genuine change. Prefer calibrated surface reflectance with consistent atmospheric processing; verify band response and acquisition conditions. Relative normalisation may be a contingency for cross-date consistency if assumptions are met, but it is not a substitute for absolute physical reflectance when matching field spectra. Q4 | Aquatic mapping (10 marks) A researcher uses atmospherically corrected Sentinel-2 scenes to map seagrass and concludes all remaining spectral differences represent seagrass cover. Critique. Reveal model answerAtmospheric correction addresses the air pathway but not all water-specific effects. Bottom signal varies with depth, turbidity, absorption, scattering, glint and tide. Control observation conditions, assess optical depth and water clarity, remove glint where needed, consider water-column correction and validate with independent benthic observations. Deep or turbid areas may be unmappable with passive optical data. Q5 | GEOM7001 extended design (15 marks) Design a preprocessing strategy for mapping drought-related vegetation condition across mountainous terrain from multiple optical dates and two sensors. Explain your trade-offs. Reveal model answerDefine target and period; choose compatible bands and common spatial support; inspect metadata and ARD status; use consistent surface reflectance, cloud/shadow masks, co-registration and seasonally matched dates; assess terrain illumination and BRDF, using DEM and sensor geometry as needed; harmonise cross-sensor spectral response rather than assuming bands are identical; use robust time-series indicators; test stable surfaces, independent reference samples and sensitivity to correction choices. Trade-off: aggressive normalisation can remove true ecological signal, whereas insufficient correction creates spurious differences. Q6 | Applied calibration (6 marks) A fictional band has gain 0.02, offset 1.5 and DN 500. Calculate at-sensor radiance. Has atmospheric correction been completed? Reveal model answerRadiance = 0.02 × 500 + 1.5 = 11.5 (in the calibration’s radiance units). No: radiometric calibration alone does not remove atmospheric scattering and absorption or establish at-surface reflectance.", "Five-point exam checklist Define the target, scale and physical unitsVerify geometric, radiometric, spectral and temporal comparabilityIdentify confounding effects and residual errorsJustify correction choices and their assumptionsValidate independently and report uncertainty"]}, {"id": 16, "title": "Classification & Biophysical Modelling", "category": "SUMMARY-QUICK REVIEW", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Module3_Classification_Biophysical.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "1. What the examiner expects", "html": "<p>The critical decision is whether the output should be a thematic map (discrete categories), a quantitative biophysical surface (continuous measurements), or a change product. This determines your reference data, model, assessment metrics and interpretation. A classifier cannot directly prove biomass or ecological health merely by assigning a vegetation label. Begin with the scientific question and define the measurand or class scheme before choosing an algorithm.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: image classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative mapping.</small>", "text": "The critical decision is whether the output should be a thematic map (discrete categories), a quantitative biophysical surface (continuous measurements), or a change product. This determines your reference data, model, assessment metrics and interpretation. A classifier cannot directly prove biomass or ecological health merely by assigning a vegetation label. Begin with the scientific question and define the measurand or class scheme before choosing an algorithm. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative mapping."}, {"title": "2. Image statistics: why they matter", "html": "<p>Each band is a variable and each pixel has a multivariate spectral vector. Within a training class inspect means, medians, spread, histograms, skew and covariance. Two classes may have similar means but different covariance; conversely overlapping histograms may indicate poor separability. Correlation between bands can create redundant predictors and unstable covariance estimates. Training data should represent the variability of each class across illumination, moisture, geography and phenology. A small homogeneous sample can look beautifully separable but generalise poorly.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: image statistics; training-site evaluation.</small>", "text": "Each band is a variable and each pixel has a multivariate spectral vector. Within a training class inspect means, medians, spread, histograms, skew and covariance. Two classes may have similar means but different covariance; conversely overlapping histograms may indicate poor separability. Correlation between bands can create redundant predictors and unstable covariance estimates. Training data should represent the variability of each class across illumination, moisture, geography and phenology. A small homogeneous sample can look beautifully separable but generalise poorly. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image statistics; training-site evaluation."}, {"title": "3. Design the classification scheme before the training data", "html": "<p>Classes must be operationally defined, mutually interpretable and detectable with the available spatial/spectral information. “Healthy mangrove” and “degraded mangrove” require explicit measurable thresholds or defensible reference labels; these are not automatically observable from a single image. Distinguish land cover (what physically exists) from land use (human purpose). A class scheme too detailed for the sensor produces systematic confusion; too broad a scheme may fail the decision need. Include an unknown/unclassified option where evidence is insufficient.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: classification scheme and image classification sequence.</small>", "text": "Classes must be operationally defined, mutually interpretable and detectable with the available spatial/spectral information. “Healthy mangrove” and “degraded mangrove” require explicit measurable thresholds or defensible reference labels; these are not automatically observable from a single image. Distinguish land cover (what physically exists) from land use (human purpose). A class scheme too detailed for the sensor produces systematic confusion; too broad a scheme may fail the decision need. Include an unknown/unclassified option where evidence is insufficient. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: classification scheme and image classification sequence."}, {"title": "4. Training-site design and separability", "html": "<p>Supervised classification requires labelled representative training sites. Inspect site quality using band histograms, feature-space plots, standard deviations, class boundaries and covariance/correlation. Sample across the study area and environmental gradients; avoid using adjacent pixels as if independent observations. Keep training and validation samples separate, ideally at spatial-block or site level where autocorrelation is strong. If rare classes are underrepresented, targeted training can improve learning, but accuracy estimates must account for the reference sampling design.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: image class generation and evaluation.</small>", "text": "Supervised classification requires labelled representative training sites. Inspect site quality using band histograms, feature-space plots, standard deviations, class boundaries and covariance/correlation. Sample across the study area and environmental gradients; avoid using adjacent pixels as if independent observations. Keep training and validation samples separate, ideally at spatial-block or site level where autocorrelation is strong. If rare classes are underrepresented, targeted training can improve learning, but accuracy estimates must account for the reference sampling design. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image class generation and evaluation."}, {"title": "5. Supervised classifiers: assumptions and trade-offs", "html": "<p>Maximum likelihood classification models class-conditional spectral distributions, typically multivariate normal, and uses mean and covariance to assign probabilities. It can fail with non-normal, multimodal classes or unstable covariance from small samples. Random Forest combines many decision trees and handles nonlinear relationships and interacting predictors, but still requires representative labels, tuning and independent evaluation; it does not remove bias in training data. Simple threshold rules are transparent but sensitive to site, season and illumination. Compare algorithms with the same independent test design rather than selecting by training accuracy.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: Maximum Likelihood (PDF p.42), Random Forest (PDF p.43).</small>", "text": "Maximum likelihood classification models class-conditional spectral distributions, typically multivariate normal, and uses mean and covariance to assign probabilities. It can fail with non-normal, multimodal classes or unstable covariance from small samples. Random Forest combines many decision trees and handles nonlinear relationships and interacting predictors, but still requires representative labels, tuning and independent evaluation; it does not remove bias in training data. Simple threshold rules are transparent but sensitive to site, season and illumination. Compare algorithms with the same independent test design rather than selecting by training accuracy. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: Maximum Likelihood (PDF p.42), Random Forest (PDF p.43)."}, {"title": "6. Unsupervised clustering and its limits", "html": "<p>Unsupervised methods group pixels by spectral similarity without pre-labelled classes. Clusters are not automatically ecological or land-cover categories; interpretation and merging require external evidence. A single land-cover class may split into many spectral clusters (e.g., shaded versus sunlit forest), while distinct covers may occupy the same cluster. Unsupervised clustering can be useful for exploration or when labels are scarce, but it does not bypass accuracy assessment.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: unsupervised clustering section.</small>", "text": "Unsupervised methods group pixels by spectral similarity without pre-labelled classes. Clusters are not automatically ecological or land-cover categories; interpretation and merging require external evidence. A single land-cover class may split into many spectral clusters (e.g., shaded versus sunlit forest), while distinct covers may occupy the same cluster. Unsupervised clustering can be useful for exploration or when labels are scarce, but it does not bypass accuracy assessment. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: unsupervised clustering section."}, {"title": "7. Pixel-based, object-based and subpixel analysis", "html": "<p>Pixel classification uses individual pixel vectors and is practical when targets are broad relative to the pixel. Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) first segments images into candidate objects, then uses spectral, texture, shape, neighbourhood and context information. It is attractive for very-high-resolution building, canopy or habitat mapping, but segmentation scale can merge distinct objects or fragment single objects. Subpixel methods estimate mixtures or fractions when pixels contain multiple covers; fractions are constrained by endmember choice, spectral variability and model assumptions. Choose the unit of analysis to match the phenomenon.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: sub-pixel approaches and object-based analysis (PDF p.53 and subsequent).</small>", "text": "Pixel classification uses individual pixel vectors and is practical when targets are broad relative to the pixel. Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) first segments images into candidate objects, then uses spectral, texture, shape, neighbourhood and context information. It is attractive for very-high-resolution building, canopy or habitat mapping, but segmentation scale can merge distinct objects or fragment single objects. Subpixel methods estimate mixtures or fractions when pixels contain multiple covers; fractions are constrained by endmember choice, spectral variability and model assumptions. Choose the unit of analysis to match the phenomenon. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: sub-pixel approaches and object-based analysis (PDF p.53 and subsequent)."}, {"title": "8. Classification workflow: defensible order", "html": "<p>(1) Specify decision and map legend. (2) Select sensor and dates appropriate to object size and spectral separability. (3) Check analysis-ready product level and required corrections. (4) Mask clouds, shadows and non-target surfaces; align imagery and ancillary layers. (5) Construct representative training data and a separately designed reference sample. (6) Inspect spectral statistics and feature separability. (7) Fit and tune the classifier without leaking validation labels. (8) Map predictions and class uncertainty where possible. (9) Apply justified post-processing without hiding real small features. (10) Evaluate independent accuracy and report limitations.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Classification: classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification refinement and error assessment.</small>", "text": "(1) Specify decision and map legend. (2) Select sensor and dates appropriate to object size and spectral separability. (3) Check analysis-ready product level and required corrections. (4) Mask clouds, shadows and non-target surfaces; align imagery and ancillary layers. (5) Construct representative training data and a separately designed reference sample. (6) Inspect spectral statistics and feature separability. (7) Fit and tune the classifier without leaking validation labels. (8) Map predictions and class uncertainty where possible. (9) Apply justified post-processing without hiding real small features. (10) Evaluate independent accuracy and report limitations. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification refinement and error assessment."}, {"title": "9. Biophysical modelling: a different output", "html": "<p>A biophysical model estimates a continuous variable such as canopy cover, biomass, leaf area index, temperature or water depth. The target must have a clear definition, measurement units, scale and field reference protocol. A regression trained on field canopy cover predicts cover, not automatically biomass or carbon. Spectral indices may correlate with a property but can saturate or be confounded by soil background, water, shadow and canopy architecture. Match field plot support to pixel footprints and acquisition timing.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Biophysical: transforming images to biophysical map products and quantitative map definition.</small>", "text": "A biophysical model estimates a continuous variable such as canopy cover, biomass, leaf area index, temperature or water depth. The target must have a clear definition, measurement units, scale and field reference protocol. A regression trained on field canopy cover predicts cover, not automatically biomass or carbon. Spectral indices may correlate with a property but can saturate or be confounded by soil background, water, shadow and canopy architecture. Match field plot support to pixel footprints and acquisition timing. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: transforming images to biophysical map products and quantitative map definition."}, {"title": "10. Empirical, physical and hybrid models", "html": "<p>Empirical models fit relationships between EO predictors and observed field quantities; their validity is limited by training range and transferability. Physical/radiative-transfer models represent energy interactions with leaves, canopies and atmosphere; they can simulate responses under specified structural and biochemical parameters but may have non-unique inverse solutions. Hybrid approaches generate simulated training sets using physical models and fit efficient statistical surrogates. Model complexity is justified only if the required parameters, assumptions and uncertainty can be supported.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 7 Radiative Transfer Models: PROSPECT (PDF p.32), canopy RTM (pp.36–39), simulation applications.</small>", "text": "Empirical models fit relationships between EO predictors and observed field quantities; their validity is limited by training range and transferability. Physical/radiative-transfer models represent energy interactions with leaves, canopies and atmosphere; they can simulate responses under specified structural and biochemical parameters but may have non-unique inverse solutions. Hybrid approaches generate simulated training sets using physical models and fit efficient statistical surrogates. Model complexity is justified only if the required parameters, assumptions and uncertainty can be supported. Source: Lecture 7 Radiative Transfer Models: PROSPECT (PDF p.32), canopy RTM (pp.36–39), simulation applications."}, {"title": "11. Why radiative transfer matters", "html": "<p>A measured reflectance is influenced by illumination and viewing geometry, leaf optical properties, canopy structure, shadows and background. Thus the same vegetation condition can produce different reflectance under different acquisition geometries, and different canopy conditions can produce similar reflectance. This is why model transfer between dates or sensors needs calibration, angular/seasonal consistency and validation. In aquatic settings, the water column and air-water interface add further complexity.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 7 RTM; Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing.</small>", "text": "A measured reflectance is influenced by illumination and viewing geometry, leaf optical properties, canopy structure, shadows and background. Thus the same vegetation condition can produce different reflectance under different acquisition geometries, and different canopy conditions can produce similar reflectance. This is why model transfer between dates or sensors needs calibration, angular/seasonal consistency and validation. In aquatic settings, the water column and air-water interface add further complexity. Source: Lecture 7 RTM; Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing."}, {"title": "12. Validation of thematic maps", "html": "<p>A confusion matrix compares map labels with independent reference labels. Overall accuracy = correctly classified reference samples / total samples, but can hide poor performance in rare classes. Producer’s accuracy = correctly mapped reference samples of a class / all reference samples belonging to that class (sensitivity; omission perspective). User’s accuracy = correctly mapped samples assigned to a mapped class / all samples mapped to that class (reliability; commission perspective). State clearly which axis is map versus reference. When sample allocation is disproportionate, raw counts do not directly represent landscape-wide accuracy; use design-based area weights.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Biophysical: thematic error matrix and user/producer accuracy (PDF pp.75–76).</small>", "text": "A confusion matrix compares map labels with independent reference labels. Overall accuracy = correctly classified reference samples / total samples, but can hide poor performance in rare classes. Producer’s accuracy = correctly mapped reference samples of a class / all reference samples belonging to that class (sensitivity; omission perspective). User’s accuracy = correctly mapped samples assigned to a mapped class / all samples mapped to that class (reliability; commission perspective). State clearly which axis is map versus reference. When sample allocation is disproportionate, raw counts do not directly represent landscape-wide accuracy; use design-based area weights. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: thematic error matrix and user/producer accuracy (PDF pp.75–76)."}, {"title": "13. Validation of quantitative products", "html": "<p>For continuous predictions use residual = predicted minus observed; bias = mean residual; RMSE = sqrt(mean squared residual), and MAE = mean absolute residual. RMSE penalises large errors more strongly than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure unbiased accuracy and can be high despite systematic offset. Report units, sample size, uncertainty, spatial distribution of residuals and whether validation covers extremes. Avoid random pixel splitting of spatially autocorrelated observations; use independent sites or spatial blocks where feasible.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative image product error assessment and RMSE.</small>", "text": "For continuous predictions use residual = predicted minus observed; bias = mean residual; RMSE = sqrt(mean squared residual), and MAE = mean absolute residual. RMSE penalises large errors more strongly than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure unbiased accuracy and can be high despite systematic offset. Report units, sample size, uncertainty, spatial distribution of residuals and whether validation covers extremes. Avoid random pixel splitting of spatially autocorrelated observations; use independent sites or spatial blocks where feasible. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative image product error assessment and RMSE."}, {"title": "14. Worked calculation: classification accuracy", "html": "<p>Suppose reference samples for mangrove are 40, of which 32 are mapped mangrove and 8 mapped other. Another 10 reference non-mangrove samples are wrongly mapped mangrove. Producer’s accuracy for mangrove = 32/40 = 80%; omission = 20%. User’s accuracy for mapped mangrove = 32/(32+10) = 76.2%; commission = 23.8%. These are sample-based illustrative values; representative landscape accuracy depends on the sampling design.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 accuracy concepts.</small>", "text": "Suppose reference samples for mangrove are 40, of which 32 are mapped mangrove and 8 mapped other. Another 10 reference non-mangrove samples are wrongly mapped mangrove. Producer’s accuracy for mangrove = 32/40 = 80%; omission = 20%. User’s accuracy for mapped mangrove = 32/(32+10) = 76.2%; commission = 23.8%. These are sample-based illustrative values; representative landscape accuracy depends on the sampling design. Source: Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 accuracy concepts."}, {"title": "15. Worked calculation: regression error", "html": "<p>Field canopy-cover percentages are 30, 50, 70 and 90. Predicted values are 40, 45, 75 and 80. Residuals are +10, -5, +5 and -10 percentage points. Bias = 0 percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((100+25+25+100)/4) = 7.91 percentage points. Zero bias does not mean zero error: positive and negative residuals can cancel.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 quantitative accuracy concepts.</small>", "text": "Field canopy-cover percentages are 30, 50, 70 and 90. Predicted values are 40, 45, 75 and 80. Residuals are +10, -5, +5 and -10 percentage points. Bias = 0 percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((100+25+25+100)/4) = 7.91 percentage points. Zero bias does not mean zero error: positive and negative residuals can cancel. Source: Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 quantitative accuracy concepts."}, {"title": "16. Multi-date mapping: error compounds", "html": "<p>Post-classification comparison can identify transitions between classes, but change estimates inherit errors from both dates. Misregistration and differing class definitions create false transitions. Two individually accurate maps do not guarantee an accurate change map. A robust change reference sample should independently label conditions or transitions at both times, using appropriately dated evidence. Alternatively, direct spectral change analysis avoids some classification steps but requires rigorous radiometric and seasonal comparability.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification change comparison and multi-temporal requirements.</small>", "text": "Post-classification comparison can identify transitions between classes, but change estimates inherit errors from both dates. Misregistration and differing class definitions create false transitions. Two individually accurate maps do not guarantee an accurate change map. A robust change reference sample should independently label conditions or transitions at both times, using appropriately dated evidence. Alternatively, direct spectral change analysis avoids some classification steps but requires rigorous radiometric and seasonal comparability. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification change comparison and multi-temporal requirements."}, {"title": "17. Exam scenario A: flawed mangrove classification", "html": "<p>SCENARIO: A researcher maps “healthy”, “stressed” and “dead” mangrove from one cloud-free Sentinel-2 NDVI image, trains a Random Forest on 50 pixels labelled by inspecting that same image, reports 99% training accuracy, and infers a 20% biomass decline. QUESTION: Identify five major weaknesses. </p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p> (1) Health labels lack independent ecological evidence; (2) NDVI alone is confounded by canopy density, tide, soil and phenology; (3) visual labels from the same imagery risk circular evidence; (4) training accuracy is not independent generalisation accuracy; (5) thematic class proportions do not directly measure biomass. Correct by defining health indicators, adding field or independent high-resolution reference observations, using suitable bands and multi-date information, spatially independent validation, and separate biomass calibration if biomass is the target.</p></details><small><strong>Source:</strong> Original exam-style scenario informed by Lectures 6–8.</small>", "text": "SCENARIO: A researcher maps “healthy”, “stressed” and “dead” mangrove from one cloud-free Sentinel-2 NDVI image, trains a Random Forest on 50 pixels labelled by inspecting that same image, reports 99% training accuracy, and infers a 20% biomass decline. QUESTION: Identify five major weaknesses.  Reveal model answer (1) Health labels lack independent ecological evidence; (2) NDVI alone is confounded by canopy density, tide, soil and phenology; (3) visual labels from the same imagery risk circular evidence; (4) training accuracy is not independent generalisation accuracy; (5) thematic class proportions do not directly measure biomass. Correct by defining health indicators, adding field or independent high-resolution reference observations, using suitable bands and multi-date information, spatially independent validation, and separate biomass calibration if biomass is the target. Source: Original exam-style scenario informed by Lectures 6–8."}, {"title": "18. Exam scenario B: defend an OBIA choice", "html": "<p>SCENARIO: Map 3–6 m over-water buildings in 0.5 m imagery. QUESTION: Why choose OBIA instead of 10 m pixel classification, and what can go wrong? </p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p> Buildings are smaller than 10 m pixels, producing mixed spectra. Very-high-resolution OBIA can use roof shape, size, texture and neighbourhood to distinguish structures from water and boats. But segmentation scale, shadows, occlusion, roof variability and temporally inconsistent imagery cause omission and commission. Tune segmentation on training areas, validate building objects independently and report object-level precision/recall, not merely pixel accuracy.</p></details><small><strong>Source:</strong> Original exam-style scenario informed by Lecture 6 GEOBIA section.</small>", "text": "SCENARIO: Map 3–6 m over-water buildings in 0.5 m imagery. QUESTION: Why choose OBIA instead of 10 m pixel classification, and what can go wrong?  Reveal model answer Buildings are smaller than 10 m pixels, producing mixed spectra. Very-high-resolution OBIA can use roof shape, size, texture and neighbourhood to distinguish structures from water and boats. But segmentation scale, shadows, occlusion, roof variability and temporally inconsistent imagery cause omission and commission. Tune segmentation on training areas, validate building objects independently and report object-level precision/recall, not merely pixel accuracy. Source: Original exam-style scenario informed by Lecture 6 GEOBIA section."}, {"title": "19. Exam scenario C: choose a biophysical model", "html": "<p>SCENARIO: Estimate woody canopy cover using Sentinel-2 reflectance and 60 field plots. QUESTION: Compare index thresholding, Random Forest regression and RTM inversion. </p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p> An index threshold is transparent but may saturate and cannot establish calibrated cover without reference data. RF regression can model nonlinear relationships but risks overfit and poor extrapolation, especially with 60 spatially clustered plots. RTM inversion has physical interpretation but requires canopy/leaf parameters and may suffer non-unique solutions. Choose a parsimonious calibrated model with spatially independent validation; assess plot-pixel support, bias, RMSE and uncertainty, and test transfer across canopy densities.</p></details><small><strong>Source:</strong> Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lectures 6–7.</small>", "text": "SCENARIO: Estimate woody canopy cover using Sentinel-2 reflectance and 60 field plots. QUESTION: Compare index thresholding, Random Forest regression and RTM inversion.  Reveal model answer An index threshold is transparent but may saturate and cannot establish calibrated cover without reference data. RF regression can model nonlinear relationships but risks overfit and poor extrapolation, especially with 60 spatially clustered plots. RTM inversion has physical interpretation but requires canopy/leaf parameters and may suffer non-unique solutions. Choose a parsimonious calibrated model with spatially independent validation; assess plot-pixel support, bias, RMSE and uncertainty, and test transfer across canopy densities. Source: Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lectures 6–7."}, {"title": "20. Exam scenario D: critique a change map", "html": "<p>SCENARIO: Two separately classified maps have 90% overall accuracy. The researcher concludes that every mapped transition is 90% accurate and reports a precise changed area. </p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p> Overall accuracy for each date does not equal change accuracy. Errors may combine, and unchanged areas can be falsely labelled as transitions. Need co-registration, consistent legend, independently labelled change-reference samples, transition-specific accuracy and uncertainty-aware area estimates. Evaluate class-specific omission/commission and area-adjustment if probability sampling is used.</p></details><small><strong>Source:</strong> Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lecture 6 change and accuracy sections.</small>", "text": "SCENARIO: Two separately classified maps have 90% overall accuracy. The researcher concludes that every mapped transition is 90% accurate and reports a precise changed area.  Reveal model answer Overall accuracy for each date does not equal change accuracy. Errors may combine, and unchanged areas can be falsely labelled as transitions. Need co-registration, consistent legend, independently labelled change-reference samples, transition-specific accuracy and uncertainty-aware area estimates. Evaluate class-specific omission/commission and area-adjustment if probability sampling is used. Source: Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lecture 6 change and accuracy sections."}, {"title": "21. Short-answer practice with model reasoning", "html": "<p>Q1 Why is random pixel-level cross-validation optimistic? A: nearby pixels share spectra and environmental context, so train/test observations are not independent; split by site or spatial blocks. Q2 Does RF remove the need for spectral understanding? A: no; labels, bands, scale and acquisition artefacts determine validity. Q3 Can a confusion matrix validate biomass? A: no; biomass is continuous, so use quantitative residual-based assessment. Q4 Why can smoothing increase apparent accuracy but harm the product? A: it removes isolated errors but also legitimate narrow or rare features. Q5 Why can a model with high R-squared still be unsuitable? A: systematic bias, extrapolation failure or large absolute errors may remain.</p><details><summary>Reveal model answer</summary><p></p></details><small><strong>Source:</strong> Supplementary practice applying Lecture 6 concepts.</small>", "text": "Q1 Why is random pixel-level cross-validation optimistic? A: nearby pixels share spectra and environmental context, so train/test observations are not independent; split by site or spatial blocks. Q2 Does RF remove the need for spectral understanding? A: no; labels, bands, scale and acquisition artefacts determine validity. Q3 Can a confusion matrix validate biomass? A: no; biomass is continuous, so use quantitative residual-based assessment. Q4 Why can smoothing increase apparent accuracy but harm the product? A: it removes isolated errors but also legitimate narrow or rare features. Q5 Why can a model with high R-squared still be unsuitable? A: systematic bias, extrapolation failure or large absolute errors may remain. Reveal model answer Source: Supplementary practice applying Lecture 6 concepts."}, {"title": "22. Exam response template", "html": "<p>For critique: identify the flaw → explain the physical/statistical mechanism → state how it biases results → propose a feasible correction. For design: define output → select data and scale → state corrections → justify algorithm against alternatives → describe independent validation → discuss uncertainty and constraints. Allocate time according to marks; prioritise fundamental flaws over cosmetic issues.</p><small><strong>Source:</strong> Study strategy derived from the exam instructions supplied by the student.</small>", "text": "For critique: identify the flaw → explain the physical/statistical mechanism → state how it biases results → propose a feasible correction. For design: define output → select data and scale → state corrections → justify algorithm against alternatives → describe independent validation → discuss uncertainty and constraints. Allocate time according to marks; prioritise fundamental flaws over cosmetic issues. Source: Study strategy derived from the exam instructions supplied by the student."}, {"title": "23. Primary lecture references and scope", "html": "<p>Core: geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf (105 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (81 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (53 PDF pages). Supplementary: Lecture 5 Pre-processing and Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing. The exam scenarios and numerical examples are authored for practice, not copied from an official exam. This module synthesises major concepts; it does not reproduce every slide or transcript.</p>", "text": "Core: geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf (105 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (81 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (53 PDF pages). Supplementary: Lecture 5 Pre-processing and Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing. The exam scenarios and numerical examples are authored for practice, not copied from an official exam. This module synthesises major concepts; it does not reproduce every slide or transcript."}], "text": ["1. What the examiner expects The critical decision is whether the output should be a thematic map (discrete categories), a quantitative biophysical surface (continuous measurements), or a change product. This determines your reference data, model, assessment metrics and interpretation. A classifier cannot directly prove biomass or ecological health merely by assigning a vegetation label. Begin with the scientific question and define the measurand or class scheme before choosing an algorithm. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative mapping.", "2. Image statistics: why they matter Each band is a variable and each pixel has a multivariate spectral vector. Within a training class inspect means, medians, spread, histograms, skew and covariance. Two classes may have similar means but different covariance; conversely overlapping histograms may indicate poor separability. Correlation between bands can create redundant predictors and unstable covariance estimates. Training data should represent the variability of each class across illumination, moisture, geography and phenology. A small homogeneous sample can look beautifully separable but generalise poorly. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image statistics; training-site evaluation.", "3. Design the classification scheme before the training data Classes must be operationally defined, mutually interpretable and detectable with the available spatial/spectral information. “Healthy mangrove” and “degraded mangrove” require explicit measurable thresholds or defensible reference labels; these are not automatically observable from a single image. Distinguish land cover (what physically exists) from land use (human purpose). A class scheme too detailed for the sensor produces systematic confusion; too broad a scheme may fail the decision need. Include an unknown/unclassified option where evidence is insufficient. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: classification scheme and image classification sequence.", "4. Training-site design and separability Supervised classification requires labelled representative training sites. Inspect site quality using band histograms, feature-space plots, standard deviations, class boundaries and covariance/correlation. Sample across the study area and environmental gradients; avoid using adjacent pixels as if independent observations. Keep training and validation samples separate, ideally at spatial-block or site level where autocorrelation is strong. If rare classes are underrepresented, targeted training can improve learning, but accuracy estimates must account for the reference sampling design. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: image class generation and evaluation.", "5. Supervised classifiers: assumptions and trade-offs Maximum likelihood classification models class-conditional spectral distributions, typically multivariate normal, and uses mean and covariance to assign probabilities. It can fail with non-normal, multimodal classes or unstable covariance from small samples. Random Forest combines many decision trees and handles nonlinear relationships and interacting predictors, but still requires representative labels, tuning and independent evaluation; it does not remove bias in training data. Simple threshold rules are transparent but sensitive to site, season and illumination. Compare algorithms with the same independent test design rather than selecting by training accuracy. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: Maximum Likelihood (PDF p.42), Random Forest (PDF p.43).", "6. Unsupervised clustering and its limits Unsupervised methods group pixels by spectral similarity without pre-labelled classes. Clusters are not automatically ecological or land-cover categories; interpretation and merging require external evidence. A single land-cover class may split into many spectral clusters (e.g., shaded versus sunlit forest), while distinct covers may occupy the same cluster. Unsupervised clustering can be useful for exploration or when labels are scarce, but it does not bypass accuracy assessment. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: unsupervised clustering section.", "7. Pixel-based, object-based and subpixel analysis Pixel classification uses individual pixel vectors and is practical when targets are broad relative to the pixel. Geographic Object-Based Image Analysis (GEOBIA/OBIA) first segments images into candidate objects, then uses spectral, texture, shape, neighbourhood and context information. It is attractive for very-high-resolution building, canopy or habitat mapping, but segmentation scale can merge distinct objects or fragment single objects. Subpixel methods estimate mixtures or fractions when pixels contain multiple covers; fractions are constrained by endmember choice, spectral variability and model assumptions. Choose the unit of analysis to match the phenomenon. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: sub-pixel approaches and object-based analysis (PDF p.53 and subsequent).", "8. Classification workflow: defensible order (1) Specify decision and map legend. (2) Select sensor and dates appropriate to object size and spectral separability. (3) Check analysis-ready product level and required corrections. (4) Mask clouds, shadows and non-target surfaces; align imagery and ancillary layers. (5) Construct representative training data and a separately designed reference sample. (6) Inspect spectral statistics and feature separability. (7) Fit and tune the classifier without leaking validation labels. (8) Map predictions and class uncertainty where possible. (9) Apply justified post-processing without hiding real small features. (10) Evaluate independent accuracy and report limitations. Source: Lecture 6 Classification: classification sequence; Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification refinement and error assessment.", "9. Biophysical modelling: a different output A biophysical model estimates a continuous variable such as canopy cover, biomass, leaf area index, temperature or water depth. The target must have a clear definition, measurement units, scale and field reference protocol. A regression trained on field canopy cover predicts cover, not automatically biomass or carbon. Spectral indices may correlate with a property but can saturate or be confounded by soil background, water, shadow and canopy architecture. Match field plot support to pixel footprints and acquisition timing. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: transforming images to biophysical map products and quantitative map definition.", "10. Empirical, physical and hybrid models Empirical models fit relationships between EO predictors and observed field quantities; their validity is limited by training range and transferability. Physical/radiative-transfer models represent energy interactions with leaves, canopies and atmosphere; they can simulate responses under specified structural and biochemical parameters but may have non-unique inverse solutions. Hybrid approaches generate simulated training sets using physical models and fit efficient statistical surrogates. Model complexity is justified only if the required parameters, assumptions and uncertainty can be supported. Source: Lecture 7 Radiative Transfer Models: PROSPECT (PDF p.32), canopy RTM (pp.36–39), simulation applications.", "11. Why radiative transfer matters A measured reflectance is influenced by illumination and viewing geometry, leaf optical properties, canopy structure, shadows and background. Thus the same vegetation condition can produce different reflectance under different acquisition geometries, and different canopy conditions can produce similar reflectance. This is why model transfer between dates or sensors needs calibration, angular/seasonal consistency and validation. In aquatic settings, the water column and air-water interface add further complexity. Source: Lecture 7 RTM; Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing.", "12. Validation of thematic maps A confusion matrix compares map labels with independent reference labels. Overall accuracy = correctly classified reference samples / total samples, but can hide poor performance in rare classes. Producer’s accuracy = correctly mapped reference samples of a class / all reference samples belonging to that class (sensitivity; omission perspective). User’s accuracy = correctly mapped samples assigned to a mapped class / all samples mapped to that class (reliability; commission perspective). State clearly which axis is map versus reference. When sample allocation is disproportionate, raw counts do not directly represent landscape-wide accuracy; use design-based area weights. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: thematic error matrix and user/producer accuracy (PDF pp.75–76).", "13. Validation of quantitative products For continuous predictions use residual = predicted minus observed; bias = mean residual; RMSE = sqrt(mean squared residual), and MAE = mean absolute residual. RMSE penalises large errors more strongly than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure unbiased accuracy and can be high despite systematic offset. Report units, sample size, uncertainty, spatial distribution of residuals and whether validation covers extremes. Avoid random pixel splitting of spatially autocorrelated observations; use independent sites or spatial blocks where feasible. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: quantitative image product error assessment and RMSE.", "14. Worked calculation: classification accuracy Suppose reference samples for mangrove are 40, of which 32 are mapped mangrove and 8 mapped other. Another 10 reference non-mangrove samples are wrongly mapped mangrove. Producer’s accuracy for mangrove = 32/40 = 80%; omission = 20%. User’s accuracy for mapped mangrove = 32/(32+10) = 76.2%; commission = 23.8%. These are sample-based illustrative values; representative landscape accuracy depends on the sampling design. Source: Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 accuracy concepts.", "15. Worked calculation: regression error Field canopy-cover percentages are 30, 50, 70 and 90. Predicted values are 40, 45, 75 and 80. Residuals are +10, -5, +5 and -10 percentage points. Bias = 0 percentage points. RMSE = sqrt((100+25+25+100)/4) = 7.91 percentage points. Zero bias does not mean zero error: positive and negative residuals can cancel. Source: Supplementary worked example based on Lecture 6 quantitative accuracy concepts.", "16. Multi-date mapping: error compounds Post-classification comparison can identify transitions between classes, but change estimates inherit errors from both dates. Misregistration and differing class definitions create false transitions. Two individually accurate maps do not guarantee an accurate change map. A robust change reference sample should independently label conditions or transitions at both times, using appropriately dated evidence. Alternatively, direct spectral change analysis avoids some classification steps but requires rigorous radiometric and seasonal comparability. Source: Lecture 6 Biophysical: post-classification change comparison and multi-temporal requirements.", "17. Exam scenario A: flawed mangrove classification SCENARIO: A researcher maps “healthy”, “stressed” and “dead” mangrove from one cloud-free Sentinel-2 NDVI image, trains a Random Forest on 50 pixels labelled by inspecting that same image, reports 99% training accuracy, and infers a 20% biomass decline. QUESTION: Identify five major weaknesses.  Reveal model answer (1) Health labels lack independent ecological evidence; (2) NDVI alone is confounded by canopy density, tide, soil and phenology; (3) visual labels from the same imagery risk circular evidence; (4) training accuracy is not independent generalisation accuracy; (5) thematic class proportions do not directly measure biomass. Correct by defining health indicators, adding field or independent high-resolution reference observations, using suitable bands and multi-date information, spatially independent validation, and separate biomass calibration if biomass is the target. Source: Original exam-style scenario informed by Lectures 6–8.", "18. Exam scenario B: defend an OBIA choice SCENARIO: Map 3–6 m over-water buildings in 0.5 m imagery. QUESTION: Why choose OBIA instead of 10 m pixel classification, and what can go wrong?  Reveal model answer Buildings are smaller than 10 m pixels, producing mixed spectra. Very-high-resolution OBIA can use roof shape, size, texture and neighbourhood to distinguish structures from water and boats. But segmentation scale, shadows, occlusion, roof variability and temporally inconsistent imagery cause omission and commission. Tune segmentation on training areas, validate building objects independently and report object-level precision/recall, not merely pixel accuracy. Source: Original exam-style scenario informed by Lecture 6 GEOBIA section.", "19. Exam scenario C: choose a biophysical model SCENARIO: Estimate woody canopy cover using Sentinel-2 reflectance and 60 field plots. QUESTION: Compare index thresholding, Random Forest regression and RTM inversion.  Reveal model answer An index threshold is transparent but may saturate and cannot establish calibrated cover without reference data. RF regression can model nonlinear relationships but risks overfit and poor extrapolation, especially with 60 spatially clustered plots. RTM inversion has physical interpretation but requires canopy/leaf parameters and may suffer non-unique solutions. Choose a parsimonious calibrated model with spatially independent validation; assess plot-pixel support, bias, RMSE and uncertainty, and test transfer across canopy densities. Source: Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lectures 6–7.", "20. Exam scenario D: critique a change map SCENARIO: Two separately classified maps have 90% overall accuracy. The researcher concludes that every mapped transition is 90% accurate and reports a precise changed area.  Reveal model answer Overall accuracy for each date does not equal change accuracy. Errors may combine, and unchanged areas can be falsely labelled as transitions. Need co-registration, consistent legend, independently labelled change-reference samples, transition-specific accuracy and uncertainty-aware area estimates. Evaluate class-specific omission/commission and area-adjustment if probability sampling is used. Source: Original GEOM7001-level scenario based on Lecture 6 change and accuracy sections.", "21. Short-answer practice with model reasoning Q1 Why is random pixel-level cross-validation optimistic? A: nearby pixels share spectra and environmental context, so train/test observations are not independent; split by site or spatial blocks. Q2 Does RF remove the need for spectral understanding? A: no; labels, bands, scale and acquisition artefacts determine validity. Q3 Can a confusion matrix validate biomass? A: no; biomass is continuous, so use quantitative residual-based assessment. Q4 Why can smoothing increase apparent accuracy but harm the product? A: it removes isolated errors but also legitimate narrow or rare features. Q5 Why can a model with high R-squared still be unsuitable? A: systematic bias, extrapolation failure or large absolute errors may remain. Reveal model answer Source: Supplementary practice applying Lecture 6 concepts.", "22. Exam response template For critique: identify the flaw → explain the physical/statistical mechanism → state how it biases results → propose a feasible correction. For design: define output → select data and scale → state corrections → justify algorithm against alternatives → describe independent validation → discuss uncertainty and constraints. Allocate time according to marks; prioritise fundamental flaws over cosmetic issues. Source: Study strategy derived from the exam instructions supplied by the student.", "23. Primary lecture references and scope Core: geom3001_7001_L6_classification_OBIA_2026.pdf (105 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L6_BiophysicalModelling_Error_2026 Full slides.pdf (81 PDF pages); geom3001_7001_L7_P2_RTM_2026.pdf (53 PDF pages). Supplementary: Lecture 5 Pre-processing and Lecture 8 Aquatic Remote Sensing. The exam scenarios and numerical examples are authored for practice, not copied from an official exam. This module synthesises major concepts; it does not reproduce every slide or transcript."]}, {"id": 17, "title": "Module 4: Accuracy, Change Detection, SAR & LiDAR", "category": "SUMMARY-QUICK REVIEW", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Module4_Accuracy_Change_SAR_LiDAR.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "1. The exam's central distinction: map accuracy vs measurement error", "html": "<p>Your lecture separates thematic products (categories) from quantitative products (continuous estimates such as temperature, depth or biomass). This distinction determines the validation design. A land-cover map needs an error matrix and class-specific errors; a biomass map needs residuals, bias and prediction error. Reporting overall accuracy for a continuous prediction is a category mistake.</p><p>Ask four questions before choosing a metric: What is the response variable? What counts as an independent reference? At what spatial and temporal support is the comparison made? What inference (pixel prediction, mapped area, regional trend) is intended?</p><p>Critique example: &#x27;We used R-squared of 0.95 to show a wetland classification is 95% accurate.&#x27; Incorrect: R-squared describes explained variation in a quantitative relationship, not classification accuracy.</p>", "text": "Your lecture separates thematic products (categories) from quantitative products (continuous estimates such as temperature, depth or biomass). This distinction determines the validation design. A land-cover map needs an error matrix and class-specific errors; a biomass map needs residuals, bias and prediction error. Reporting overall accuracy for a continuous prediction is a category mistake. Ask four questions before choosing a metric: What is the response variable? What counts as an independent reference? At what spatial and temporal support is the comparison made? What inference (pixel prediction, mapped area, regional trend) is intended? Critique example: 'We used R-squared of 0.95 to show a wetland classification is 95% accurate.' Incorrect: R-squared describes explained variation in a quantitative relationship, not classification accuracy."}, {"title": "2. Confusion matrices: understand the denominators", "html": "<p>Rows here are mapped classes (predictions), columns are reference classes (truth). The diagonal counts correct labels. Overall accuracy = diagonal sum / all validation samples. User&#x27;s accuracy for a mapped class = diagonal / its row total; commission error = 1 - user&#x27;s accuracy. Producer&#x27;s accuracy = diagonal / its column total; omission error = 1 - producer&#x27;s accuracy.</p><p>Worked example (mapped rows x reference columns): mangrove [40,10], other [5,45]. Overall = (40+45)/100 = 85%; mangrove user&#x27;s accuracy = 40/50 = 80%; mangrove producer&#x27;s accuracy = 40/45 = 88.9%. A map user asks &#x27;How often is a pixel mapped mangrove truly mangrove?&#x27; A reference producer asks &#x27;How many actual mangrove pixels did the map capture?&#x27;</p><p>An 85% overall score can mask a rare class with very poor detection. Always inspect class-specific error and sampling design. The row/column convention varies; state it before computing.</p>", "text": "Rows here are mapped classes (predictions), columns are reference classes (truth). The diagonal counts correct labels. Overall accuracy = diagonal sum / all validation samples. User's accuracy for a mapped class = diagonal / its row total; commission error = 1 - user's accuracy. Producer's accuracy = diagonal / its column total; omission error = 1 - producer's accuracy. Worked example (mapped rows x reference columns): mangrove [40,10], other [5,45]. Overall = (40+45)/100 = 85%; mangrove user's accuracy = 40/50 = 80%; mangrove producer's accuracy = 40/45 = 88.9%. A map user asks 'How often is a pixel mapped mangrove truly mangrove?' A reference producer asks 'How many actual mangrove pixels did the map capture?' An 85% overall score can mask a rare class with very poor detection. Always inspect class-specific error and sampling design. The row/column convention varies; state it before computing."}, {"title": "3. Sampling, independence and area estimation", "html": "<p>Validation must be separate from training and model tuning. Reusing training points produces optimistically biased accuracy. Random pixel splitting can still leak information when adjacent pixels or observations from the same field plot are strongly correlated. For spatially autocorrelated data, consider spatial block or geographically independent validation.</p><p>Stratified sampling ensures rare or important classes receive enough reference observations, but the raw sample proportion is not necessarily the landscape proportion. Use sampling-design weights for unbiased area estimates and uncertainty where required. For change maps, sample stable and changed strata, with adequate representation of rare change.</p><p>Reference data have their own error: uncertain interpretation, GPS offsets, date mismatch, mixed pixels and label disagreement. Report provenance, timing and uncertainty rather than calling them perfect &#x27;ground truth&#x27;.</p>", "text": "Validation must be separate from training and model tuning. Reusing training points produces optimistically biased accuracy. Random pixel splitting can still leak information when adjacent pixels or observations from the same field plot are strongly correlated. For spatially autocorrelated data, consider spatial block or geographically independent validation. Stratified sampling ensures rare or important classes receive enough reference observations, but the raw sample proportion is not necessarily the landscape proportion. Use sampling-design weights for unbiased area estimates and uncertainty where required. For change maps, sample stable and changed strata, with adequate representation of rare change. Reference data have their own error: uncertain interpretation, GPS offsets, date mismatch, mixed pixels and label disagreement. Report provenance, timing and uncertainty rather than calling them perfect 'ground truth'."}, {"title": "4. Quantitative modelling error: RMSE, MAE, bias, R-squared", "html": "<p>For n reference pairs, residual e_i = prediction_i - reference_i. Bias = mean(e_i); MAE = mean(|e_i|); RMSE = sqrt(mean(e_i^2)). RMSE penalises large errors more heavily than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure absolute error or bias.</p><p>Worked example: reference biomass [100,120,140] t/ha, predictions [110,115,155]. Residuals [+10,-5,+15]. Bias = +6.67 t/ha; MAE = 10 t/ha; RMSE = sqrt((100+25+225)/3) = 10.80 t/ha. A positive bias means systematic overestimation on average.</p><p>Report units and uncertainty, evaluate error by relevant conditions (low/high biomass, forest type, sensor geometry), and assess extrapolation beyond the training range. A high R-squared can coexist with systematic overprediction.</p>", "text": "For n reference pairs, residual e_i = prediction_i - reference_i. Bias = mean(e_i); MAE = mean(|e_i|); RMSE = sqrt(mean(e_i^2)). RMSE penalises large errors more heavily than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure absolute error or bias. Worked example: reference biomass [100,120,140] t/ha, predictions [110,115,155]. Residuals [+10,-5,+15]. Bias = +6.67 t/ha; MAE = 10 t/ha; RMSE = sqrt((100+25+225)/3) = 10.80 t/ha. A positive bias means systematic overestimation on average. Report units and uncertainty, evaluate error by relevant conditions (low/high biomass, forest type, sensor geometry), and assess extrapolation beyond the training range. A high R-squared can coexist with systematic overprediction."}, {"title": "5. Multi-temporal change detection: comparability first", "html": "<p>Your Lecture 6 stresses equal ground resolution elements, common projection/datum, co-registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel, comparable view angles, matched spectral response and calibrated radiometric units. Misregistration at narrow boundaries can create apparent change without any real disturbance.</p><p>Before differencing, check processing level (prefer consistent surface reflectance for optical time series), cloud/shadow masks, phenological season, tidal stage for coastal habitats, atmospheric conditions, spatial support and observation dates. Annual composites can reduce cloud effects, but median compositing can hide short-lived events.</p><p>Methods differ: direct band/index differencing captures magnitude; ratios highlight relative change but become unstable near zero; change-vector analysis uses multidimensional spectral movement; post-classification comparison gives from-to transitions but compounds classification errors; dense time-series modelling can distinguish seasonal variability from persistent change.</p>", "text": "Your Lecture 6 stresses equal ground resolution elements, common projection/datum, co-registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel, comparable view angles, matched spectral response and calibrated radiometric units. Misregistration at narrow boundaries can create apparent change without any real disturbance. Before differencing, check processing level (prefer consistent surface reflectance for optical time series), cloud/shadow masks, phenological season, tidal stage for coastal habitats, atmospheric conditions, spatial support and observation dates. Annual composites can reduce cloud effects, but median compositing can hide short-lived events. Methods differ: direct band/index differencing captures magnitude; ratios highlight relative change but become unstable near zero; change-vector analysis uses multidimensional spectral movement; post-classification comparison gives from-to transitions but compounds classification errors; dense time-series modelling can distinguish seasonal variability from persistent change."}, {"title": "6. Change detection: thresholds, trends and causal claims", "html": "<p>A negative NDVI difference is evidence of changed spectral greenness, not automatically evidence of mortality, deforestation or climate-driven dieback. A fixed threshold such as delta NDVI &lt; -0.1 must be justified against baseline variability, sensor noise and validation. Avoid selecting the threshold solely to maximise accuracy on the final test data.</p><p>For disturbance monitoring, build a multi-year baseline, model seasonality, flag anomalies and check persistence. Use spatial controls or unaffected reference sites when possible. Validate both change and no-change classes. If the question is about &#x27;early warning&#x27;, evaluate lead time against independently dated events, not merely whether the index drops eventually.</p><p>Post-classification comparison may be interpretable (forest to non-forest) but two independently imperfect maps create transition errors. Direct spectral differencing avoids class-label propagation but requires rigorous radiometric harmonisation and a defensible threshold.</p>", "text": "A negative NDVI difference is evidence of changed spectral greenness, not automatically evidence of mortality, deforestation or climate-driven dieback. A fixed threshold such as delta NDVI < -0.1 must be justified against baseline variability, sensor noise and validation. Avoid selecting the threshold solely to maximise accuracy on the final test data. For disturbance monitoring, build a multi-year baseline, model seasonality, flag anomalies and check persistence. Use spatial controls or unaffected reference sites when possible. Validate both change and no-change classes. If the question is about 'early warning', evaluate lead time against independently dated events, not merely whether the index drops eventually. Post-classification comparison may be interpretable (forest to non-forest) but two independently imperfect maps create transition errors. Direct spectral differencing avoids class-label propagation but requires rigorous radiometric harmonisation and a defensible threshold."}, {"title": "7. SAR fundamentals and interpretation", "html": "<p>Synthetic aperture radar is active microwave sensing. It supplies its own illumination and can observe through cloud, making it valuable in cloudy tropical regions. Backscatter depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, surface roughness, dielectric properties (including moisture), vegetation structure and scattering geometry.</p><p>Do not interpret every bright SAR pixel as vegetation or every backscatter increase as biomass gain. Water often appears dark under calm conditions because of specular reflection, but wind-roughened water and double-bounce in flooded vegetation can be bright. SAR does not simply &#x27;see through everything&#x27;.</p><p>Typical radar processing includes orbit information, calibration to a normalised backscatter coefficient (e.g. sigma-nought), speckle management, terrain/geometric correction and consistent multi-date acquisition geometry. Convert linear backscatter to dB when appropriate, but never average dB values as though they were linear powers.</p>", "text": "Synthetic aperture radar is active microwave sensing. It supplies its own illumination and can observe through cloud, making it valuable in cloudy tropical regions. Backscatter depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, surface roughness, dielectric properties (including moisture), vegetation structure and scattering geometry. Do not interpret every bright SAR pixel as vegetation or every backscatter increase as biomass gain. Water often appears dark under calm conditions because of specular reflection, but wind-roughened water and double-bounce in flooded vegetation can be bright. SAR does not simply 'see through everything'. Typical radar processing includes orbit information, calibration to a normalised backscatter coefficient (e.g. sigma-nought), speckle management, terrain/geometric correction and consistent multi-date acquisition geometry. Convert linear backscatter to dB when appropriate, but never average dB values as though they were linear powers."}, {"title": "8. SAR-specific pitfalls and corrections", "html": "<p>Speckle is a coherent interference phenomenon, not the same as cloud noise. Multilooking or filtering reduces speckle at a cost in spatial detail; overly aggressive smoothing erases small features and boundaries. Compare images with consistent filtering and resolution.</p><p>Layover, foreshortening and radar shadow are geometry-driven distortions, particularly in steep terrain. Orthorectification and terrain correction improve location, but cannot recover information missing in radar shadow. Changes in incidence angle or soil moisture can mimic land-cover change.</p><p>Useful choice: Sentinel-1 SAR for frequent cloud-independent inundation/structural monitoring, combined with optical imagery for spectral discrimination. Justify the fusion rather than claiming either modality alone measures every ecological variable.</p>", "text": "Speckle is a coherent interference phenomenon, not the same as cloud noise. Multilooking or filtering reduces speckle at a cost in spatial detail; overly aggressive smoothing erases small features and boundaries. Compare images with consistent filtering and resolution. Layover, foreshortening and radar shadow are geometry-driven distortions, particularly in steep terrain. Orthorectification and terrain correction improve location, but cannot recover information missing in radar shadow. Changes in incidence angle or soil moisture can mimic land-cover change. Useful choice: Sentinel-1 SAR for frequent cloud-independent inundation/structural monitoring, combined with optical imagery for spectral discrimination. Justify the fusion rather than claiming either modality alone measures every ecological variable."}, {"title": "9. LiDAR: measurements, products and limits", "html": "<p>LiDAR measures laser ranges and produces 3D point clouds. Ground and non-ground classification supports a digital terrain model (DTM), while a digital surface model (DSM) includes upper surfaces; a canopy height model (CHM) is often DSM minus DTM. Point density, scan angle, occlusion and georeferencing influence what can be measured.</p><p>Airborne laser scanning covers landscapes; terrestrial laser scanning can provide detailed tree structure but has occlusion and plot-sampling constraints. Height and structural metrics can support biomass models, yet biomass is not directly measured without appropriate modelling and calibration.</p><p>Common flaw: treating a DSM as bare-earth elevation in forest. The elevation includes canopy and buildings. Another flaw: assuming a single LiDAR scan sees every branch; shadowing and occlusion create incomplete point clouds.</p>", "text": "LiDAR measures laser ranges and produces 3D point clouds. Ground and non-ground classification supports a digital terrain model (DTM), while a digital surface model (DSM) includes upper surfaces; a canopy height model (CHM) is often DSM minus DTM. Point density, scan angle, occlusion and georeferencing influence what can be measured. Airborne laser scanning covers landscapes; terrestrial laser scanning can provide detailed tree structure but has occlusion and plot-sampling constraints. Height and structural metrics can support biomass models, yet biomass is not directly measured without appropriate modelling and calibration. Common flaw: treating a DSM as bare-earth elevation in forest. The elevation includes canopy and buildings. Another flaw: assuming a single LiDAR scan sees every branch; shadowing and occlusion create incomplete point clouds."}, {"title": "10. Integrated sensor and validation decisions", "html": "<p>Optical imagery: useful for spectral properties, vegetation indices and habitat types; constrained by cloud, illumination and (for underwater targets) water clarity, depth, waves and bottom visibility. SAR: cloud-independent structural/moisture-sensitive backscatter, but complicated scattering geometry and speckle. LiDAR: strong 3D structural and terrain measurements, but cost, coverage and occlusion can be limiting.</p><p>Choose the dataset by the target variable and scale. For narrow mangrove fringes, 30 m pixels may mix land, water and canopy; finer imagery can help. For broad regional inundation under persistent clouds, SAR may be preferable. For canopy height, LiDAR is generally more direct than NDVI.</p><p>Your answer should always include: target, sensor and resolution, preprocessing, algorithm/model, independent reference, performance metrics, main uncertainty and why the alternative is less suitable.</p>", "text": "Optical imagery: useful for spectral properties, vegetation indices and habitat types; constrained by cloud, illumination and (for underwater targets) water clarity, depth, waves and bottom visibility. SAR: cloud-independent structural/moisture-sensitive backscatter, but complicated scattering geometry and speckle. LiDAR: strong 3D structural and terrain measurements, but cost, coverage and occlusion can be limiting. Choose the dataset by the target variable and scale. For narrow mangrove fringes, 30 m pixels may mix land, water and canopy; finer imagery can help. For broad regional inundation under persistent clouds, SAR may be preferable. For canopy height, LiDAR is generally more direct than NDVI. Your answer should always include: target, sensor and resolution, preprocessing, algorithm/model, independent reference, performance metrics, main uncertainty and why the alternative is less suitable."}, {"title": "Exam-style questions with expandable model answers", "html": "<details><summary>Q1 — 12 marks: Accuracy audit</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> A map has 90% overall accuracy but only 35% producer&#x27;s accuracy for a rare wetland class. The author concludes the map is suitable for estimating wetland loss.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> The conclusion is unsupported. Overall accuracy is dominated by common classes. Producer&#x27;s accuracy of 35% means 65% of reference wetland cases were omitted. For area-change inference, inspect the confusion matrix, commission error, sampling weights and independent change validation; consider redesigning training and class sampling. A rare target class needs class-specific performance, not just an impressive global score.</p></details><details><summary>Q2 — 12 marks: Quantitative model audit</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> A biomass model has R-squared = 0.91. Predictions are consistently 25 t/ha too high. The author calls it accurate.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> R-squared alone does not establish accuracy. Systematic +25 t/ha bias is important, especially for carbon accounting. Report RMSE, MAE, bias, residual patterns and uncertainty from independent spatial validation. Investigate calibration, allometry, saturation and sampling representativeness; consider recalibration and out-of-sample testing.</p></details><details><summary>Q3 — 14 marks: Two-date coastal change</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> Two NDVI images taken in different seasons and tidal conditions are subtracted. Every negative pixel is labelled degraded mangrove.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Check consistent surface reflectance, masking, co-registration, common pixel support and tidal/seasonal comparability. Restrict to independently mapped mangrove extent. Model baseline variability or use multiple matched observations, justify change thresholds, and independently validate stable and changed samples. NDVI decline alone cannot identify the cause or permanence of degradation.</p></details><details><summary>Q4 — 12 marks: SAR choice</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> A researcher proposes radar because it works through clouds, then interprets all high backscatter as forest biomass.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Cloud independence is a legitimate benefit, but backscatter also responds to moisture, roughness, geometry and scattering mechanisms. Calibrate, correct geometry, control incidence angle, manage speckle, use suitable polarisation and validate with forest structure/biomass reference data. Distinguish correlation from direct measurement.</p></details><details><summary>Q5 — 10 marks: LiDAR product confusion</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> A researcher subtracts a DSM from GPS-measured tree-top elevations to derive canopy height, without a DTM.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Canopy height is relative to ground elevation, so a DTM is needed (commonly CHM = DSM - DTM). A DSM includes canopy/buildings; incorrect reference surfaces distort heights. Check point-cloud ground classification, point density, occlusion, vertical datum and field measurements.</p></details><details><summary>Q6 — GEOM7001 extended, 20 marks: Design and defend</summary><p><strong>Question:</strong> Design an approach to identify mangrove disturbance over 2016–2026 in a cloudy tropical estuary, where narrow mangrove fringes and changing tides complicate optical imagery.</p><p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Define disturbance operationally and the minimum detectable patch. Use harmonised optical surface-reflectance time series with cloud/shadow masking and season/tide controls, plus SAR for cloud-independent temporal coverage; use higher-resolution imagery or LiDAR for narrow fringes/structural corroboration if available. Co-register and match spatial support. Compare index trends, SAR metrics and independently mapped mangrove extent; do not equate any single signal with mortality. Validate using independent stratified spatial samples and field/high-resolution references, estimate class-specific change errors and area uncertainty, and discuss optical mixed pixels, SAR moisture sensitivity and date mismatch. Defend each data source against cost, resolution and physical limitations.</p></details>", "text": "Q1 — 12 marks: Accuracy auditQuestion: A map has 90% overall accuracy but only 35% producer's accuracy for a rare wetland class. The author concludes the map is suitable for estimating wetland loss.Model answer: The conclusion is unsupported. Overall accuracy is dominated by common classes. Producer's accuracy of 35% means 65% of reference wetland cases were omitted. For area-change inference, inspect the confusion matrix, commission error, sampling weights and independent change validation; consider redesigning training and class sampling. A rare target class needs class-specific performance, not just an impressive global score. Q2 — 12 marks: Quantitative model auditQuestion: A biomass model has R-squared = 0.91. Predictions are consistently 25 t/ha too high. The author calls it accurate.Model answer: R-squared alone does not establish accuracy. Systematic +25 t/ha bias is important, especially for carbon accounting. Report RMSE, MAE, bias, residual patterns and uncertainty from independent spatial validation. Investigate calibration, allometry, saturation and sampling representativeness; consider recalibration and out-of-sample testing. Q3 — 14 marks: Two-date coastal changeQuestion: Two NDVI images taken in different seasons and tidal conditions are subtracted. Every negative pixel is labelled degraded mangrove.Model answer: Check consistent surface reflectance, masking, co-registration, common pixel support and tidal/seasonal comparability. Restrict to independently mapped mangrove extent. Model baseline variability or use multiple matched observations, justify change thresholds, and independently validate stable and changed samples. NDVI decline alone cannot identify the cause or permanence of degradation. Q4 — 12 marks: SAR choiceQuestion: A researcher proposes radar because it works through clouds, then interprets all high backscatter as forest biomass.Model answer: Cloud independence is a legitimate benefit, but backscatter also responds to moisture, roughness, geometry and scattering mechanisms. Calibrate, correct geometry, control incidence angle, manage speckle, use suitable polarisation and validate with forest structure/biomass reference data. Distinguish correlation from direct measurement. Q5 — 10 marks: LiDAR product confusionQuestion: A researcher subtracts a DSM from GPS-measured tree-top elevations to derive canopy height, without a DTM.Model answer: Canopy height is relative to ground elevation, so a DTM is needed (commonly CHM = DSM - DTM). A DSM includes canopy/buildings; incorrect reference surfaces distort heights. Check point-cloud ground classification, point density, occlusion, vertical datum and field measurements. Q6 — GEOM7001 extended, 20 marks: Design and defendQuestion: Design an approach to identify mangrove disturbance over 2016–2026 in a cloudy tropical estuary, where narrow mangrove fringes and changing tides complicate optical imagery.Model answer: Define disturbance operationally and the minimum detectable patch. Use harmonised optical surface-reflectance time series with cloud/shadow masking and season/tide controls, plus SAR for cloud-independent temporal coverage; use higher-resolution imagery or LiDAR for narrow fringes/structural corroboration if available. Co-register and match spatial support. Compare index trends, SAR metrics and independently mapped mangrove extent; do not equate any single signal with mortality. Validate using independent stratified spatial samples and field/high-resolution references, estimate class-specific change errors and area uncertainty, and discuss optical mixed pixels, SAR moisture sensitivity and date mismatch. Defend each data source against cost, resolution and physical limitations."}, {"title": "Source notes", "html": "<ul><li>GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Biophysical Modelling, Multi-date Image Processing, Error &amp; Accuracy Assessment (uploaded PDF).</li><li>GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 12: Image Processing for Active Data — Imaging Radar (and LiDAR) (uploaded PDF).</li><li>GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 8: Aquatic Remote Sensing &amp; Allen Coral Atlas (uploaded PDF).</li><li>GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Classification and Object-Based Image Analysis (uploaded PDF).</li><li>Additional worked numbers and hypothetical exam scenarios are original study examples, not verbatim examination questions.</li></ul>", "text": "GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Biophysical Modelling, Multi-date Image Processing, Error & Accuracy Assessment (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 12: Image Processing for Active Data — Imaging Radar (and LiDAR) (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 8: Aquatic Remote Sensing & Allen Coral Atlas (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Classification and Object-Based Image Analysis (uploaded PDF).Additional worked numbers and hypothetical exam scenarios are original study examples, not verbatim examination questions."}], "text": ["1. The exam's central distinction: map accuracy vs measurement error Your lecture separates thematic products (categories) from quantitative products (continuous estimates such as temperature, depth or biomass). This distinction determines the validation design. A land-cover map needs an error matrix and class-specific errors; a biomass map needs residuals, bias and prediction error. Reporting overall accuracy for a continuous prediction is a category mistake. Ask four questions before choosing a metric: What is the response variable? What counts as an independent reference? At what spatial and temporal support is the comparison made? What inference (pixel prediction, mapped area, regional trend) is intended? Critique example: 'We used R-squared of 0.95 to show a wetland classification is 95% accurate.' Incorrect: R-squared describes explained variation in a quantitative relationship, not classification accuracy.", "2. Confusion matrices: understand the denominators Rows here are mapped classes (predictions), columns are reference classes (truth). The diagonal counts correct labels. Overall accuracy = diagonal sum / all validation samples. User's accuracy for a mapped class = diagonal / its row total; commission error = 1 - user's accuracy. Producer's accuracy = diagonal / its column total; omission error = 1 - producer's accuracy. Worked example (mapped rows x reference columns): mangrove [40,10], other [5,45]. Overall = (40+45)/100 = 85%; mangrove user's accuracy = 40/50 = 80%; mangrove producer's accuracy = 40/45 = 88.9%. A map user asks 'How often is a pixel mapped mangrove truly mangrove?' A reference producer asks 'How many actual mangrove pixels did the map capture?' An 85% overall score can mask a rare class with very poor detection. Always inspect class-specific error and sampling design. The row/column convention varies; state it before computing.", "3. Sampling, independence and area estimation Validation must be separate from training and model tuning. Reusing training points produces optimistically biased accuracy. Random pixel splitting can still leak information when adjacent pixels or observations from the same field plot are strongly correlated. For spatially autocorrelated data, consider spatial block or geographically independent validation. Stratified sampling ensures rare or important classes receive enough reference observations, but the raw sample proportion is not necessarily the landscape proportion. Use sampling-design weights for unbiased area estimates and uncertainty where required. For change maps, sample stable and changed strata, with adequate representation of rare change. Reference data have their own error: uncertain interpretation, GPS offsets, date mismatch, mixed pixels and label disagreement. Report provenance, timing and uncertainty rather than calling them perfect 'ground truth'.", "4. Quantitative modelling error: RMSE, MAE, bias, R-squared For n reference pairs, residual e_i = prediction_i - reference_i. Bias = mean(e_i); MAE = mean(|e_i|); RMSE = sqrt(mean(e_i^2)). RMSE penalises large errors more heavily than MAE. R-squared alone does not measure absolute error or bias. Worked example: reference biomass [100,120,140] t/ha, predictions [110,115,155]. Residuals [+10,-5,+15]. Bias = +6.67 t/ha; MAE = 10 t/ha; RMSE = sqrt((100+25+225)/3) = 10.80 t/ha. A positive bias means systematic overestimation on average. Report units and uncertainty, evaluate error by relevant conditions (low/high biomass, forest type, sensor geometry), and assess extrapolation beyond the training range. A high R-squared can coexist with systematic overprediction.", "5. Multi-temporal change detection: comparability first Your Lecture 6 stresses equal ground resolution elements, common projection/datum, co-registration RMSE below 0.5 pixel, comparable view angles, matched spectral response and calibrated radiometric units. Misregistration at narrow boundaries can create apparent change without any real disturbance. Before differencing, check processing level (prefer consistent surface reflectance for optical time series), cloud/shadow masks, phenological season, tidal stage for coastal habitats, atmospheric conditions, spatial support and observation dates. Annual composites can reduce cloud effects, but median compositing can hide short-lived events. Methods differ: direct band/index differencing captures magnitude; ratios highlight relative change but become unstable near zero; change-vector analysis uses multidimensional spectral movement; post-classification comparison gives from-to transitions but compounds classification errors; dense time-series modelling can distinguish seasonal variability from persistent change.", "6. Change detection: thresholds, trends and causal claims A negative NDVI difference is evidence of changed spectral greenness, not automatically evidence of mortality, deforestation or climate-driven dieback. A fixed threshold such as delta NDVI < -0.1 must be justified against baseline variability, sensor noise and validation. Avoid selecting the threshold solely to maximise accuracy on the final test data. For disturbance monitoring, build a multi-year baseline, model seasonality, flag anomalies and check persistence. Use spatial controls or unaffected reference sites when possible. Validate both change and no-change classes. If the question is about 'early warning', evaluate lead time against independently dated events, not merely whether the index drops eventually. Post-classification comparison may be interpretable (forest to non-forest) but two independently imperfect maps create transition errors. Direct spectral differencing avoids class-label propagation but requires rigorous radiometric harmonisation and a defensible threshold.", "7. SAR fundamentals and interpretation Synthetic aperture radar is active microwave sensing. It supplies its own illumination and can observe through cloud, making it valuable in cloudy tropical regions. Backscatter depends on wavelength, polarisation, incidence angle, surface roughness, dielectric properties (including moisture), vegetation structure and scattering geometry. Do not interpret every bright SAR pixel as vegetation or every backscatter increase as biomass gain. Water often appears dark under calm conditions because of specular reflection, but wind-roughened water and double-bounce in flooded vegetation can be bright. SAR does not simply 'see through everything'. Typical radar processing includes orbit information, calibration to a normalised backscatter coefficient (e.g. sigma-nought), speckle management, terrain/geometric correction and consistent multi-date acquisition geometry. Convert linear backscatter to dB when appropriate, but never average dB values as though they were linear powers.", "8. SAR-specific pitfalls and corrections Speckle is a coherent interference phenomenon, not the same as cloud noise. Multilooking or filtering reduces speckle at a cost in spatial detail; overly aggressive smoothing erases small features and boundaries. Compare images with consistent filtering and resolution. Layover, foreshortening and radar shadow are geometry-driven distortions, particularly in steep terrain. Orthorectification and terrain correction improve location, but cannot recover information missing in radar shadow. Changes in incidence angle or soil moisture can mimic land-cover change. Useful choice: Sentinel-1 SAR for frequent cloud-independent inundation/structural monitoring, combined with optical imagery for spectral discrimination. Justify the fusion rather than claiming either modality alone measures every ecological variable.", "9. LiDAR: measurements, products and limits LiDAR measures laser ranges and produces 3D point clouds. Ground and non-ground classification supports a digital terrain model (DTM), while a digital surface model (DSM) includes upper surfaces; a canopy height model (CHM) is often DSM minus DTM. Point density, scan angle, occlusion and georeferencing influence what can be measured. Airborne laser scanning covers landscapes; terrestrial laser scanning can provide detailed tree structure but has occlusion and plot-sampling constraints. Height and structural metrics can support biomass models, yet biomass is not directly measured without appropriate modelling and calibration. Common flaw: treating a DSM as bare-earth elevation in forest. The elevation includes canopy and buildings. Another flaw: assuming a single LiDAR scan sees every branch; shadowing and occlusion create incomplete point clouds.", "10. Integrated sensor and validation decisions Optical imagery: useful for spectral properties, vegetation indices and habitat types; constrained by cloud, illumination and (for underwater targets) water clarity, depth, waves and bottom visibility. SAR: cloud-independent structural/moisture-sensitive backscatter, but complicated scattering geometry and speckle. LiDAR: strong 3D structural and terrain measurements, but cost, coverage and occlusion can be limiting. Choose the dataset by the target variable and scale. For narrow mangrove fringes, 30 m pixels may mix land, water and canopy; finer imagery can help. For broad regional inundation under persistent clouds, SAR may be preferable. For canopy height, LiDAR is generally more direct than NDVI. Your answer should always include: target, sensor and resolution, preprocessing, algorithm/model, independent reference, performance metrics, main uncertainty and why the alternative is less suitable.", "Exam-style questions with expandable model answers Q1 — 12 marks: Accuracy auditQuestion: A map has 90% overall accuracy but only 35% producer's accuracy for a rare wetland class. The author concludes the map is suitable for estimating wetland loss.Model answer: The conclusion is unsupported. Overall accuracy is dominated by common classes. Producer's accuracy of 35% means 65% of reference wetland cases were omitted. For area-change inference, inspect the confusion matrix, commission error, sampling weights and independent change validation; consider redesigning training and class sampling. A rare target class needs class-specific performance, not just an impressive global score. Q2 — 12 marks: Quantitative model auditQuestion: A biomass model has R-squared = 0.91. Predictions are consistently 25 t/ha too high. The author calls it accurate.Model answer: R-squared alone does not establish accuracy. Systematic +25 t/ha bias is important, especially for carbon accounting. Report RMSE, MAE, bias, residual patterns and uncertainty from independent spatial validation. Investigate calibration, allometry, saturation and sampling representativeness; consider recalibration and out-of-sample testing. Q3 — 14 marks: Two-date coastal changeQuestion: Two NDVI images taken in different seasons and tidal conditions are subtracted. Every negative pixel is labelled degraded mangrove.Model answer: Check consistent surface reflectance, masking, co-registration, common pixel support and tidal/seasonal comparability. Restrict to independently mapped mangrove extent. Model baseline variability or use multiple matched observations, justify change thresholds, and independently validate stable and changed samples. NDVI decline alone cannot identify the cause or permanence of degradation. Q4 — 12 marks: SAR choiceQuestion: A researcher proposes radar because it works through clouds, then interprets all high backscatter as forest biomass.Model answer: Cloud independence is a legitimate benefit, but backscatter also responds to moisture, roughness, geometry and scattering mechanisms. Calibrate, correct geometry, control incidence angle, manage speckle, use suitable polarisation and validate with forest structure/biomass reference data. Distinguish correlation from direct measurement. Q5 — 10 marks: LiDAR product confusionQuestion: A researcher subtracts a DSM from GPS-measured tree-top elevations to derive canopy height, without a DTM.Model answer: Canopy height is relative to ground elevation, so a DTM is needed (commonly CHM = DSM - DTM). A DSM includes canopy/buildings; incorrect reference surfaces distort heights. Check point-cloud ground classification, point density, occlusion, vertical datum and field measurements. Q6 — GEOM7001 extended, 20 marks: Design and defendQuestion: Design an approach to identify mangrove disturbance over 2016–2026 in a cloudy tropical estuary, where narrow mangrove fringes and changing tides complicate optical imagery.Model answer: Define disturbance operationally and the minimum detectable patch. Use harmonised optical surface-reflectance time series with cloud/shadow masking and season/tide controls, plus SAR for cloud-independent temporal coverage; use higher-resolution imagery or LiDAR for narrow fringes/structural corroboration if available. Co-register and match spatial support. Compare index trends, SAR metrics and independently mapped mangrove extent; do not equate any single signal with mortality. Validate using independent stratified spatial samples and field/high-resolution references, estimate class-specific change errors and area uncertainty, and discuss optical mixed pixels, SAR moisture sensitivity and date mismatch. Defend each data source against cost, resolution and physical limitations.", "Source notes GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Biophysical Modelling, Multi-date Image Processing, Error & Accuracy Assessment (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 12: Image Processing for Active Data — Imaging Radar (and LiDAR) (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 8: Aquatic Remote Sensing & Allen Coral Atlas (uploaded PDF).GEOM3001/7001 Lecture 6: Classification and Object-Based Image Analysis (uploaded PDF).Additional worked numbers and hypothetical exam scenarios are original study examples, not verbatim examination questions."]}, {"id": 18, "title": "End-to-End Workflow & Reference Data", "category": "SUMMARY-QUICK REVIEW", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Revision_5_RETRY.pdf", "pages": 4, "sections": [{"title": "Overview", "html": "<p>End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox &amp; Reference Data Selection</p><h3>End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox &amp; Reference Data Selection</h3><p><small>Compact revision companion • Exam-style answers can be expanded below.</small></p>", "text": "End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox & Reference Data Selection End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox & Reference Data Selection Compact revision companion • Exam-style answers can be expanded below."}, {"title": "1 | What this module adds", "html": "<p><strong>Purpose.</strong> This compact companion addresses three priorities from the lecturer’s class feedback: complete image-processing sequences; strengths and limitations of ENVI versus DEA Sandbox; and training/verification data suitable for categorical accuracy assessment. It supports the exam’s two-part task: critique a flawed workflow, then design and defend an alternative.</p><p><strong>Exam principle.</strong> A strong answer is a chain of justified decisions, not a list of software commands. For each stage: state the objective, why the step is necessary, what assumption it makes, what error it prevents, and how success is checked.</p>", "text": "Purpose. This compact companion addresses three priorities from the lecturer’s class feedback: complete image-processing sequences; strengths and limitations of ENVI versus DEA Sandbox; and training/verification data suitable for categorical accuracy assessment. It supports the exam’s two-part task: critique a flawed workflow, then design and defend an alternative. Exam principle. A strong answer is a chain of justified decisions, not a list of software commands. For each stage: state the objective, why the step is necessary, what assumption it makes, what error it prevents, and how success is checked."}, {"title": "2 | A defensible end-to-end workflow", "html": "<p><strong>Stage 1 — Define the decision and output.</strong> Specify the phenomenon, area, time period, minimum mapping unit, classes or continuous variable, intended use, and acceptable uncertainty. “Map change” is underspecified: distinguish land-cover transition, spectral anomaly, and quantitative biophysical change.</p><p><strong>Stage 2 — Select data and ancillary evidence.</strong> Choose sensors using spatial resolution (feature size/mixed pixels), spectral bands (physical separability), radiometric quality (noise/calibration), temporal sampling (phenology/event timing), acquisition geometry, processing level, access and cost. Select field, high-resolution, DEM, tide or other ancillary data for specific purposes.</p><p><strong>Stage 3 — Audit inputs and preprocessing level.</strong> Inspect metadata, units, scale factors, band definitions, projection, acquisition dates, viewing angles and quality flags. Determine which corrections were already performed in an analysis-ready product. Never apply the same correction twice without justification.</p><p><strong>Stage 4 — Geometric preparation.</strong> Check geolocation, common coordinate reference system, spatial support, image-to-image co-registration and independent check points. Course materials use &lt;0.5 pixel registration RMSE as a useful target, not a universal guarantee. Select resampling according to data type; upsampling does not create spatial information.</p><p><strong>Stage 5 — Radiometric and environmental preparation.</strong> Use appropriate calibration and consistent surface reflectance for optical comparison; mask cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Address terrain illumination/BRDF where material; for water consider glint, depth, water-column attenuation and tide; for SAR address calibration, speckle and terrain geometry. Processing is sensor- and product-dependent.</p><p><strong>Stage 6 — Feature generation and analysis.</strong> Generate physically interpretable predictors (bands, indices, textures, structural metrics) and choose classification, regression, change detection or segmentation according to the output. Use training data only for fitting/tuning. Do not confuse image enhancement for visual display with physical correction.</p><p><strong>Stage 7 — Independent validation and uncertainty.</strong> Reserve independently sourced verification samples; define the reference protocol and spatial/temporal match. For categorical outputs report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, omission/commission and, where appropriate, area-adjusted estimates. For continuous outputs report bias, MAE, RMSE and residual patterns.</p><p><strong>Stage 8 — Interpretation, reproducibility and delivery.</strong> Evaluate failure modes, sensitivity to thresholds/corrections, representativeness, unresolved mixed pixels and extrapolation. Document inputs, code/settings, processing versions, sampling design, uncertainties and limits of causal interpretation.</p><p><strong>Important nuance.</strong> This is a logical scientific sequence, not a fixed menu-click order. For example, ARD may bundle geometric, radiometric and atmospheric processing; image registration and quality masking may be iterated; and field data collection should be planned before classification.</p>", "text": "Stage 1 — Define the decision and output. Specify the phenomenon, area, time period, minimum mapping unit, classes or continuous variable, intended use, and acceptable uncertainty. “Map change” is underspecified: distinguish land-cover transition, spectral anomaly, and quantitative biophysical change. Stage 2 — Select data and ancillary evidence. Choose sensors using spatial resolution (feature size/mixed pixels), spectral bands (physical separability), radiometric quality (noise/calibration), temporal sampling (phenology/event timing), acquisition geometry, processing level, access and cost. Select field, high-resolution, DEM, tide or other ancillary data for specific purposes. Stage 3 — Audit inputs and preprocessing level. Inspect metadata, units, scale factors, band definitions, projection, acquisition dates, viewing angles and quality flags. Determine which corrections were already performed in an analysis-ready product. Never apply the same correction twice without justification. Stage 4 — Geometric preparation. Check geolocation, common coordinate reference system, spatial support, image-to-image co-registration and independent check points. Course materials use <0.5 pixel registration RMSE as a useful target, not a universal guarantee. Select resampling according to data type; upsampling does not create spatial information. Stage 5 — Radiometric and environmental preparation. Use appropriate calibration and consistent surface reflectance for optical comparison; mask cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Address terrain illumination/BRDF where material; for water consider glint, depth, water-column attenuation and tide; for SAR address calibration, speckle and terrain geometry. Processing is sensor- and product-dependent. Stage 6 — Feature generation and analysis. Generate physically interpretable predictors (bands, indices, textures, structural metrics) and choose classification, regression, change detection or segmentation according to the output. Use training data only for fitting/tuning. Do not confuse image enhancement for visual display with physical correction. Stage 7 — Independent validation and uncertainty. Reserve independently sourced verification samples; define the reference protocol and spatial/temporal match. For categorical outputs report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, omission/commission and, where appropriate, area-adjusted estimates. For continuous outputs report bias, MAE, RMSE and residual patterns. Stage 8 — Interpretation, reproducibility and delivery. Evaluate failure modes, sensitivity to thresholds/corrections, representativeness, unresolved mixed pixels and extrapolation. Document inputs, code/settings, processing versions, sampling design, uncertainties and limits of causal interpretation. Important nuance. This is a logical scientific sequence, not a fixed menu-click order. For example, ARD may bundle geometric, radiometric and atmospheric processing; image registration and quality masking may be iterated; and field data collection should be planned before classification."}, {"title": "3 | ENVI versus DEA Sandbox: choose by the task", "html": "<p><strong>ENVI — general strengths.</strong> A desktop image-analysis environment with interactive visual inspection, fine-grained image-processing tools and workflows for spectral analysis, supervised classification and specialized imagery. It can be useful for teaching individual corrections and testing small-area, custom processing choices.</p><p><strong>ENVI — constraints.</strong> Large multi-year archives may demand local storage, computation and manual scene management; licensing/access can constrain reproducibility or collaboration. Available features depend on installation, extensions and version. A polished ENVI classification is not automatically validated.</p><p><strong>DEA Sandbox — general strengths.</strong> A cloud/notebook environment oriented to accessing and analysing Digital Earth Australia products, often using analysis-ready satellite data and Python-based reproducible, time-series workflows. It is attractive for regional, multi-date analysis and transparent code-based processing.</p><p><strong>DEA Sandbox — constraints.</strong> Data availability, product definitions, cloud-compute limits and notebook/coding proficiency constrain work. Preprocessed data still need quality masking, units checks, registration checks and application-specific decisions. The platform does not guarantee a correct algorithm or independent reference data.</p><p><strong>Defensible comparison.</strong> For a single UAV hyperspectral scene requiring interactive spectral inspection or specialist tools, ENVI may be convenient if the relevant modules are available. For a decade of Australian satellite surface-reflectance observations, DEA Sandbox may reduce data acquisition and preprocessing overhead. Either can be scientifically sound; justify the workflow, data and validation rather than claiming one platform is inherently more accurate.</p><p><strong>Exam trap.</strong> “DEA is analysis-ready, so no preprocessing or accuracy assessment is necessary” is false. “ENVI produces more accurate maps because it has more tools” is also false. Accuracy depends on sensor suitability, processing decisions, training/reference data, algorithm assumptions and independent testing.</p>", "text": "ENVI — general strengths. A desktop image-analysis environment with interactive visual inspection, fine-grained image-processing tools and workflows for spectral analysis, supervised classification and specialized imagery. It can be useful for teaching individual corrections and testing small-area, custom processing choices. ENVI — constraints. Large multi-year archives may demand local storage, computation and manual scene management; licensing/access can constrain reproducibility or collaboration. Available features depend on installation, extensions and version. A polished ENVI classification is not automatically validated. DEA Sandbox — general strengths. A cloud/notebook environment oriented to accessing and analysing Digital Earth Australia products, often using analysis-ready satellite data and Python-based reproducible, time-series workflows. It is attractive for regional, multi-date analysis and transparent code-based processing. DEA Sandbox — constraints. Data availability, product definitions, cloud-compute limits and notebook/coding proficiency constrain work. Preprocessed data still need quality masking, units checks, registration checks and application-specific decisions. The platform does not guarantee a correct algorithm or independent reference data. Defensible comparison. For a single UAV hyperspectral scene requiring interactive spectral inspection or specialist tools, ENVI may be convenient if the relevant modules are available. For a decade of Australian satellite surface-reflectance observations, DEA Sandbox may reduce data acquisition and preprocessing overhead. Either can be scientifically sound; justify the workflow, data and validation rather than claiming one platform is inherently more accurate. Exam trap. “DEA is analysis-ready, so no preprocessing or accuracy assessment is necessary” is false. “ENVI produces more accurate maps because it has more tools” is also false. Accuracy depends on sensor suitability, processing decisions, training/reference data, algorithm assumptions and independent testing."}, {"title": "4 | Training versus verification: what makes evidence suitable?", "html": "<p><strong>Training data.</strong> Used to teach a supervised classifier or fit a regression. Labels must be operationally defined, representative of within-class variability and spread across relevant geography, seasons and illumination conditions. Rare classes may need deliberate oversampling for model fitting.</p><p><strong>Verification/reference data.</strong> Used to assess predictions independently of training and tuning. Design a probability-based or otherwise defensible sampling plan, with adequate rare-class representation. Spatially adjacent pixels, repeated observations from the same plot or labels interpreted solely from the classified image can create leakage or circular validation.</p><p><strong>Spatial characteristics.</strong> Reference observations must match the mapped object and support: a 1 m point label may not represent a mixed 30 m pixel; a plot mean should align with the model pixel/footprint; boundaries need careful treatment. GPS accuracy, positional uncertainty and object-level versus pixel-level evaluation matter.</p><p><strong>Spectral/thematic characteristics.</strong> The reference source must actually distinguish the target classes. A high-resolution RGB photograph may locate a canopy but not reliably establish species, biomass or physiological stress. Field measurements, expert interpretation, spectroscopy or other independent evidence may be necessary for difficult labels.</p><p><strong>Temporal characteristics.</strong> Reference acquisition should be close enough to image dates to reflect the same state. For change validation, reference information must support both dates or the transition. Season, tides, disturbance timing and temporary conditions can invalidate otherwise precise labels.</p><p><strong>Categorical versus continuous.</strong> For class maps: confusion matrices, class-specific user/producer accuracy and design-based area uncertainty. For continuous maps: matched measured quantities, residuals, RMSE/MAE/bias, and spatially independent test sites. A high R² is not equivalent to accurate classification or unbiased prediction.</p><p><strong>Worked categorical example.</strong> Mapped rows × reference columns: Reef [36, 9], Non-reef [4, 51]. Overall accuracy = (36+51)/100 = 87%. Reef user’s accuracy = 36/45 = 80%; reef producer’s accuracy = 36/40 = 90%. Commission error = 20%; omission error = 10%. If the validation sample is disproportionally stratified, raw totals need design-based weighting for population inference.</p>", "text": "Training data. Used to teach a supervised classifier or fit a regression. Labels must be operationally defined, representative of within-class variability and spread across relevant geography, seasons and illumination conditions. Rare classes may need deliberate oversampling for model fitting. Verification/reference data. Used to assess predictions independently of training and tuning. Design a probability-based or otherwise defensible sampling plan, with adequate rare-class representation. Spatially adjacent pixels, repeated observations from the same plot or labels interpreted solely from the classified image can create leakage or circular validation. Spatial characteristics. Reference observations must match the mapped object and support: a 1 m point label may not represent a mixed 30 m pixel; a plot mean should align with the model pixel/footprint; boundaries need careful treatment. GPS accuracy, positional uncertainty and object-level versus pixel-level evaluation matter. Spectral/thematic characteristics. The reference source must actually distinguish the target classes. A high-resolution RGB photograph may locate a canopy but not reliably establish species, biomass or physiological stress. Field measurements, expert interpretation, spectroscopy or other independent evidence may be necessary for difficult labels. Temporal characteristics. Reference acquisition should be close enough to image dates to reflect the same state. For change validation, reference information must support both dates or the transition. Season, tides, disturbance timing and temporary conditions can invalidate otherwise precise labels. Categorical versus continuous. For class maps: confusion matrices, class-specific user/producer accuracy and design-based area uncertainty. For continuous maps: matched measured quantities, residuals, RMSE/MAE/bias, and spatially independent test sites. A high R² is not equivalent to accurate classification or unbiased prediction. Worked categorical example. Mapped rows × reference columns: Reef [36, 9], Non-reef [4, 51]. Overall accuracy = (36+51)/100 = 87%. Reef user’s accuracy = 36/45 = 80%; reef producer’s accuracy = 36/40 = 90%. Commission error = 20%; omission error = 10%. If the validation sample is disproportionally stratified, raw totals need design-based weighting for population inference."}, {"title": "5 | Fast decision table: match question to method", "html": "<p><strong>Map discrete habitat types.</strong> Multispectral or suitably high-resolution imagery; atmospheric and habitat-specific correction; supervised/OBIA classification; independent class labels; confusion matrix and class-specific errors.</p><p><strong>Estimate biomass or canopy height.</strong> LiDAR structural metrics and/or suitable spectral/SAR predictors; field plot calibration; continuous regression/physical model; spatially independent plots; RMSE, bias and uncertainty. NDVI is not a direct biomass measurement.</p><p><strong>Map change across years.</strong> Consistent sensors/ARD, seasons, co-registration, masks and spectral support; direct index/time-series or post-classification change; independent change/no-change reference; transition errors and area uncertainty.</p><p><strong>Map water quality.</strong> Water-suitable optical bands, aquatic atmospheric/glint treatment and in situ sampling near overpass; calibrated turbidity/chlorophyll model; independent water samples; residual-based error. Spectral indices are not automatically concentrations.</p><p><strong>Map inundation under clouds.</strong> Calibrated SAR with geometry and speckle management; distinguish rough water, vegetation double-bounce and soil moisture; independent flood extent/time-matched reference; omission and commission.</p>", "text": "Map discrete habitat types. Multispectral or suitably high-resolution imagery; atmospheric and habitat-specific correction; supervised/OBIA classification; independent class labels; confusion matrix and class-specific errors. Estimate biomass or canopy height. LiDAR structural metrics and/or suitable spectral/SAR predictors; field plot calibration; continuous regression/physical model; spatially independent plots; RMSE, bias and uncertainty. NDVI is not a direct biomass measurement. Map change across years. Consistent sensors/ARD, seasons, co-registration, masks and spectral support; direct index/time-series or post-classification change; independent change/no-change reference; transition errors and area uncertainty. Map water quality. Water-suitable optical bands, aquatic atmospheric/glint treatment and in situ sampling near overpass; calibrated turbidity/chlorophyll model; independent water samples; residual-based error. Spectral indices are not automatically concentrations. Map inundation under clouds. Calibrated SAR with geometry and speckle management; distinguish rough water, vegetation double-bounce and soil moisture; independent flood extent/time-matched reference; omission and commission."}, {"title": "6 | Exam technique: two-part scenario", "html": "<p><strong>Part A — Critique.</strong> Rank flaws by damage to validity. Use: FLAW → MECHANISM → CONSEQUENCE → CORRECTION. E.g., “They compare raw digital numbers from different sensors. DN depends on sensor calibration, so an apparent difference may be instrumental rather than environmental. Use compatible calibrated surface reflectance, harmonise band response and inspect stable targets.”</p><p><strong>Part B — Design and defend.</strong> State target/output, minimum mapping unit and constraints. Select sensor/date/product with trade-offs. Give a logically ordered processing sequence. Defend algorithm versus a plausible alternative. Specify independent reference sampling, metrics and uncertainty. Finish with limitations that matter for the decision.</p><p><strong>GEOM7001 standard.</strong> Go beyond naming a correct method: discuss competing error sources, independence, scale mismatch, transferability, validation design and why a simpler or more complex method may be preferable under constraints. Answer in proportion to marks; quality of reasoning matters more than length.</p>", "text": "Part A — Critique. Rank flaws by damage to validity. Use: FLAW → MECHANISM → CONSEQUENCE → CORRECTION. E.g., “They compare raw digital numbers from different sensors. DN depends on sensor calibration, so an apparent difference may be instrumental rather than environmental. Use compatible calibrated surface reflectance, harmonise band response and inspect stable targets.” Part B — Design and defend. State target/output, minimum mapping unit and constraints. Select sensor/date/product with trade-offs. Give a logically ordered processing sequence. Defend algorithm versus a plausible alternative. Specify independent reference sampling, metrics and uncertainty. Finish with limitations that matter for the decision. GEOM7001 standard. Go beyond naming a correct method: discuss competing error sources, independence, scale mismatch, transferability, validation design and why a simpler or more complex method may be preferable under constraints. Answer in proportion to marks; quality of reasoning matters more than length."}, {"title": "7 | Practice questions with model answers", "html": "<p><strong>Q1 — Critique (12 marks).</strong> A student classifies 2018 and 2026 coastal habitats using different optical products. They resample both to 1 m, apply independent contrast stretches, train on visually interpreted pixels from the same scenes, report training accuracy and subtract the class maps to estimate habitat loss. Identify four priority problems and fixes.</p><details><summary>MODEL ANSWER 1 — reveal answer</summary><p>(1) Upsampling invents no detail: analyse at real common support or acquire genuinely finer imagery. (2) Independent stretches destroy radiometric comparability: use compatible calibrated surface reflectance and environmental corrections. (3) Same-image labels and training accuracy are not independent validation: use separately collected, time-matched reference samples and a held-out spatial design. (4) Post-classification change compounds errors: check co-registration, consistent legend and season/tide; validate change transitions directly and report area uncertainty. Award marks for causal explanations, not just naming fixes.</p></details><p><strong>Q2 — Platform decision (10 marks).</strong> You need annual land-cover estimates over a large Australian region from 2014–2026. Compare ENVI and DEA Sandbox and defend a choice.</p><details><summary>MODEL ANSWER 2 — reveal answer</summary><p>DEA Sandbox is a defensible primary environment because accessible Australian ARD archives, Python notebooks and time-series processing can reduce manual scene handling and improve reproducibility. Verify product availability, observation density, cloud masks, scaling, cross-sensor consistency and computational constraints. ENVI remains useful for detailed visual checking, custom classification or specialized processing, but local storage, licensing and scene-by-scene management may be less efficient at this scale. Platform selection does not replace independent validation.</p></details><p><strong>Q3 — Reference-data audit (12 marks).</strong> A 10 m wetland map is trained on 200 labelled pixels. Validation uses 100 adjacent pixels from the same training polygons, with labels copied from a two-year-old RGB basemap. Why might 94% overall accuracy be misleading?</p><details><summary>MODEL ANSWER 3 — reveal answer</summary><p>Adjacent samples share spatial autocorrelation with training, so reported accuracy may be optimistic. Copied RGB labels may be wrong for wetland classes that require ecological evidence and are temporally mismatched. Overall accuracy can hide omission of rare wetlands. Redesign independent, spatially separated verification with current field or suitable high-resolution evidence, representative sampling, class-specific confusion matrix, and weighting if using disproportionate stratification. Match 10 m pixel support and report uncertain boundaries.</p></details><p><strong>Q4 — Calculation (8 marks).</strong> Mapped rows × reference columns: Target [24, 6]; Other [12, 58]. Compute overall accuracy, target user’s accuracy, target producer’s accuracy and omission error.</p><details><summary>MODEL ANSWER 4 — reveal answer</summary><p>Total = 100. Overall = (24+58)/100 = 82%. Target user’s = 24/(24+6) = 80%. Target producer’s = 24/(24+12) = 66.7%. Target omission error = 1 − 0.667 = 33.3%. The target is missed in one-third of reference target cases even though overall accuracy is 82%.</p></details><p><strong>Q5 — GEOM7001 only: integrated design (18 marks).</strong> Design an EO workflow to monitor cyclone damage to coastal forest across a cloudy region with limited field access. Defend the role of optical, SAR and LiDAR; state validation and remaining uncertainty.</p><details><summary>MODEL ANSWER 5 — reveal answer</summary><p>Define damage (e.g., canopy loss versus structural change), mapping unit, pre/post period and required decision. Use matched optical surface-reflectance time series where clear observations exist for canopy spectral change; SAR can fill cloud-driven observation gaps but backscatter depends on moisture, geometry and structure; LiDAR, if available at appropriate dates/coverage, can independently support height/structural change. Check orbit/calibration/speckle/terrain for SAR, cloud/shadow/season/BRDF for optical, ground classification/DTM/CHM for LiDAR, and co-registration across sensors. Analyse persistent anomalies against a pre-event baseline and unaffected controls, not a single threshold. Validate damaged and unchanged strata with independent field, dated high-resolution or structural observations, and assess class-specific errors and area uncertainty; if modelling continuous height loss, add RMSE/bias. State limits: cloud gaps, SAR moisture confounding, LiDAR cost/temporal gaps, spatial mismatch and uncertain damage dates. A multisensor fusion is justified only when each source contributes identifiable evidence.</p></details>", "text": "Q1 — Critique (12 marks). A student classifies 2018 and 2026 coastal habitats using different optical products. They resample both to 1 m, apply independent contrast stretches, train on visually interpreted pixels from the same scenes, report training accuracy and subtract the class maps to estimate habitat loss. Identify four priority problems and fixes. MODEL ANSWER 1 — reveal answer(1) Upsampling invents no detail: analyse at real common support or acquire genuinely finer imagery. (2) Independent stretches destroy radiometric comparability: use compatible calibrated surface reflectance and environmental corrections. (3) Same-image labels and training accuracy are not independent validation: use separately collected, time-matched reference samples and a held-out spatial design. (4) Post-classification change compounds errors: check co-registration, consistent legend and season/tide; validate change transitions directly and report area uncertainty. Award marks for causal explanations, not just naming fixes. Q2 — Platform decision (10 marks). You need annual land-cover estimates over a large Australian region from 2014–2026. Compare ENVI and DEA Sandbox and defend a choice. MODEL ANSWER 2 — reveal answerDEA Sandbox is a defensible primary environment because accessible Australian ARD archives, Python notebooks and time-series processing can reduce manual scene handling and improve reproducibility. Verify product availability, observation density, cloud masks, scaling, cross-sensor consistency and computational constraints. ENVI remains useful for detailed visual checking, custom classification or specialized processing, but local storage, licensing and scene-by-scene management may be less efficient at this scale. Platform selection does not replace independent validation. Q3 — Reference-data audit (12 marks). A 10 m wetland map is trained on 200 labelled pixels. Validation uses 100 adjacent pixels from the same training polygons, with labels copied from a two-year-old RGB basemap. Why might 94% overall accuracy be misleading? MODEL ANSWER 3 — reveal answerAdjacent samples share spatial autocorrelation with training, so reported accuracy may be optimistic. Copied RGB labels may be wrong for wetland classes that require ecological evidence and are temporally mismatched. Overall accuracy can hide omission of rare wetlands. Redesign independent, spatially separated verification with current field or suitable high-resolution evidence, representative sampling, class-specific confusion matrix, and weighting if using disproportionate stratification. Match 10 m pixel support and report uncertain boundaries. Q4 — Calculation (8 marks). Mapped rows × reference columns: Target [24, 6]; Other [12, 58]. Compute overall accuracy, target user’s accuracy, target producer’s accuracy and omission error. MODEL ANSWER 4 — reveal answerTotal = 100. Overall = (24+58)/100 = 82%. Target user’s = 24/(24+6) = 80%. Target producer’s = 24/(24+12) = 66.7%. Target omission error = 1 − 0.667 = 33.3%. The target is missed in one-third of reference target cases even though overall accuracy is 82%. Q5 — GEOM7001 only: integrated design (18 marks). Design an EO workflow to monitor cyclone damage to coastal forest across a cloudy region with limited field access. Defend the role of optical, SAR and LiDAR; state validation and remaining uncertainty. MODEL ANSWER 5 — reveal answerDefine damage (e.g., canopy loss versus structural change), mapping unit, pre/post period and required decision. Use matched optical surface-reflectance time series where clear observations exist for canopy spectral change; SAR can fill cloud-driven observation gaps but backscatter depends on moisture, geometry and structure; LiDAR, if available at appropriate dates/coverage, can independently support height/structural change. Check orbit/calibration/speckle/terrain for SAR, cloud/shadow/season/BRDF for optical, ground classification/DTM/CHM for LiDAR, and co-registration across sensors. Analyse persistent anomalies against a pre-event baseline and unaffected controls, not a single threshold. Validate damaged and unchanged strata with independent field, dated high-resolution or structural observations, and assess class-specific errors and area uncertainty; if modelling continuous height loss, add RMSE/bias. State limits: cloud gaps, SAR moisture confounding, LiDAR cost/temporal gaps, spatial mismatch and uncertain damage dates. A multisensor fusion is justified only when each source contributes identifiable evidence."}, {"title": "8 | Source basis and final checklist", "html": "<p><strong>Course basis.</strong> Synthesis of the four uploaded GEOM7001 short modules: Module 1 (sensor selection); Module 2 (preprocessing); Module 3 (classification and modelling); Module 4 (accuracy, change, SAR and LiDAR), plus the exam guidance and lecturer feedback supplied in this conversation. Platform comparison is an applied synthesis rather than a verified current software feature inventory.</p><p><strong>Before the exam.</strong> Can you (1) define the output; (2) justify spatial/spectral/radiometric/temporal requirements; (3) audit ARD and preprocessing; (4) select and defend the algorithm; (5) distinguish training from independent verification; (6) calculate user’s/producer’s accuracy; (7) discuss error and alternative approaches; (8) explain ENVI versus DEA Sandbox without claiming either is automatically more accurate?</p>", "text": "Course basis. Synthesis of the four uploaded GEOM7001 short modules: Module 1 (sensor selection); Module 2 (preprocessing); Module 3 (classification and modelling); Module 4 (accuracy, change, SAR and LiDAR), plus the exam guidance and lecturer feedback supplied in this conversation. Platform comparison is an applied synthesis rather than a verified current software feature inventory. Before the exam. Can you (1) define the output; (2) justify spatial/spectral/radiometric/temporal requirements; (3) audit ARD and preprocessing; (4) select and defend the algorithm; (5) distinguish training from independent verification; (6) calculate user’s/producer’s accuracy; (7) discuss error and alternative approaches; (8) explain ENVI versus DEA Sandbox without claiming either is automatically more accurate?"}], "text": ["Overview End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox & Reference Data Selection End-to-End Workflow Design, ENVI vs DEA Sandbox & Reference Data Selection Compact revision companion • Exam-style answers can be expanded below.", "1 | What this module adds Purpose. This compact companion addresses three priorities from the lecturer’s class feedback: complete image-processing sequences; strengths and limitations of ENVI versus DEA Sandbox; and training/verification data suitable for categorical accuracy assessment. It supports the exam’s two-part task: critique a flawed workflow, then design and defend an alternative. Exam principle. A strong answer is a chain of justified decisions, not a list of software commands. For each stage: state the objective, why the step is necessary, what assumption it makes, what error it prevents, and how success is checked.", "2 | A defensible end-to-end workflow Stage 1 — Define the decision and output. Specify the phenomenon, area, time period, minimum mapping unit, classes or continuous variable, intended use, and acceptable uncertainty. “Map change” is underspecified: distinguish land-cover transition, spectral anomaly, and quantitative biophysical change. Stage 2 — Select data and ancillary evidence. Choose sensors using spatial resolution (feature size/mixed pixels), spectral bands (physical separability), radiometric quality (noise/calibration), temporal sampling (phenology/event timing), acquisition geometry, processing level, access and cost. Select field, high-resolution, DEM, tide or other ancillary data for specific purposes. Stage 3 — Audit inputs and preprocessing level. Inspect metadata, units, scale factors, band definitions, projection, acquisition dates, viewing angles and quality flags. Determine which corrections were already performed in an analysis-ready product. Never apply the same correction twice without justification. Stage 4 — Geometric preparation. Check geolocation, common coordinate reference system, spatial support, image-to-image co-registration and independent check points. Course materials use <0.5 pixel registration RMSE as a useful target, not a universal guarantee. Select resampling according to data type; upsampling does not create spatial information. Stage 5 — Radiometric and environmental preparation. Use appropriate calibration and consistent surface reflectance for optical comparison; mask cloud, shadow and invalid observations. Address terrain illumination/BRDF where material; for water consider glint, depth, water-column attenuation and tide; for SAR address calibration, speckle and terrain geometry. Processing is sensor- and product-dependent. Stage 6 — Feature generation and analysis. Generate physically interpretable predictors (bands, indices, textures, structural metrics) and choose classification, regression, change detection or segmentation according to the output. Use training data only for fitting/tuning. Do not confuse image enhancement for visual display with physical correction. Stage 7 — Independent validation and uncertainty. Reserve independently sourced verification samples; define the reference protocol and spatial/temporal match. For categorical outputs report confusion matrix, user’s and producer’s accuracy, omission/commission and, where appropriate, area-adjusted estimates. For continuous outputs report bias, MAE, RMSE and residual patterns. Stage 8 — Interpretation, reproducibility and delivery. Evaluate failure modes, sensitivity to thresholds/corrections, representativeness, unresolved mixed pixels and extrapolation. Document inputs, code/settings, processing versions, sampling design, uncertainties and limits of causal interpretation. Important nuance. This is a logical scientific sequence, not a fixed menu-click order. For example, ARD may bundle geometric, radiometric and atmospheric processing; image registration and quality masking may be iterated; and field data collection should be planned before classification.", "3 | ENVI versus DEA Sandbox: choose by the task ENVI — general strengths. A desktop image-analysis environment with interactive visual inspection, fine-grained image-processing tools and workflows for spectral analysis, supervised classification and specialized imagery. It can be useful for teaching individual corrections and testing small-area, custom processing choices. ENVI — constraints. Large multi-year archives may demand local storage, computation and manual scene management; licensing/access can constrain reproducibility or collaboration. Available features depend on installation, extensions and version. A polished ENVI classification is not automatically validated. DEA Sandbox — general strengths. A cloud/notebook environment oriented to accessing and analysing Digital Earth Australia products, often using analysis-ready satellite data and Python-based reproducible, time-series workflows. It is attractive for regional, multi-date analysis and transparent code-based processing. DEA Sandbox — constraints. Data availability, product definitions, cloud-compute limits and notebook/coding proficiency constrain work. Preprocessed data still need quality masking, units checks, registration checks and application-specific decisions. The platform does not guarantee a correct algorithm or independent reference data. Defensible comparison. For a single UAV hyperspectral scene requiring interactive spectral inspection or specialist tools, ENVI may be convenient if the relevant modules are available. For a decade of Australian satellite surface-reflectance observations, DEA Sandbox may reduce data acquisition and preprocessing overhead. Either can be scientifically sound; justify the workflow, data and validation rather than claiming one platform is inherently more accurate. Exam trap. “DEA is analysis-ready, so no preprocessing or accuracy assessment is necessary” is false. “ENVI produces more accurate maps because it has more tools” is also false. Accuracy depends on sensor suitability, processing decisions, training/reference data, algorithm assumptions and independent testing.", "4 | Training versus verification: what makes evidence suitable? Training data. Used to teach a supervised classifier or fit a regression. Labels must be operationally defined, representative of within-class variability and spread across relevant geography, seasons and illumination conditions. Rare classes may need deliberate oversampling for model fitting. Verification/reference data. Used to assess predictions independently of training and tuning. Design a probability-based or otherwise defensible sampling plan, with adequate rare-class representation. Spatially adjacent pixels, repeated observations from the same plot or labels interpreted solely from the classified image can create leakage or circular validation. Spatial characteristics. Reference observations must match the mapped object and support: a 1 m point label may not represent a mixed 30 m pixel; a plot mean should align with the model pixel/footprint; boundaries need careful treatment. GPS accuracy, positional uncertainty and object-level versus pixel-level evaluation matter. Spectral/thematic characteristics. The reference source must actually distinguish the target classes. A high-resolution RGB photograph may locate a canopy but not reliably establish species, biomass or physiological stress. Field measurements, expert interpretation, spectroscopy or other independent evidence may be necessary for difficult labels. Temporal characteristics. Reference acquisition should be close enough to image dates to reflect the same state. For change validation, reference information must support both dates or the transition. Season, tides, disturbance timing and temporary conditions can invalidate otherwise precise labels. Categorical versus continuous. For class maps: confusion matrices, class-specific user/producer accuracy and design-based area uncertainty. For continuous maps: matched measured quantities, residuals, RMSE/MAE/bias, and spatially independent test sites. A high R² is not equivalent to accurate classification or unbiased prediction. Worked categorical example. Mapped rows × reference columns: Reef [36, 9], Non-reef [4, 51]. Overall accuracy = (36+51)/100 = 87%. Reef user’s accuracy = 36/45 = 80%; reef producer’s accuracy = 36/40 = 90%. Commission error = 20%; omission error = 10%. If the validation sample is disproportionally stratified, raw totals need design-based weighting for population inference.", "5 | Fast decision table: match question to method Map discrete habitat types. Multispectral or suitably high-resolution imagery; atmospheric and habitat-specific correction; supervised/OBIA classification; independent class labels; confusion matrix and class-specific errors. Estimate biomass or canopy height. LiDAR structural metrics and/or suitable spectral/SAR predictors; field plot calibration; continuous regression/physical model; spatially independent plots; RMSE, bias and uncertainty. NDVI is not a direct biomass measurement. Map change across years. Consistent sensors/ARD, seasons, co-registration, masks and spectral support; direct index/time-series or post-classification change; independent change/no-change reference; transition errors and area uncertainty. Map water quality. Water-suitable optical bands, aquatic atmospheric/glint treatment and in situ sampling near overpass; calibrated turbidity/chlorophyll model; independent water samples; residual-based error. Spectral indices are not automatically concentrations. Map inundation under clouds. Calibrated SAR with geometry and speckle management; distinguish rough water, vegetation double-bounce and soil moisture; independent flood extent/time-matched reference; omission and commission.", "6 | Exam technique: two-part scenario Part A — Critique. Rank flaws by damage to validity. Use: FLAW → MECHANISM → CONSEQUENCE → CORRECTION. E.g., “They compare raw digital numbers from different sensors. DN depends on sensor calibration, so an apparent difference may be instrumental rather than environmental. Use compatible calibrated surface reflectance, harmonise band response and inspect stable targets.” Part B — Design and defend. State target/output, minimum mapping unit and constraints. Select sensor/date/product with trade-offs. Give a logically ordered processing sequence. Defend algorithm versus a plausible alternative. Specify independent reference sampling, metrics and uncertainty. Finish with limitations that matter for the decision. GEOM7001 standard. Go beyond naming a correct method: discuss competing error sources, independence, scale mismatch, transferability, validation design and why a simpler or more complex method may be preferable under constraints. Answer in proportion to marks; quality of reasoning matters more than length.", "7 | Practice questions with model answers Q1 — Critique (12 marks). A student classifies 2018 and 2026 coastal habitats using different optical products. They resample both to 1 m, apply independent contrast stretches, train on visually interpreted pixels from the same scenes, report training accuracy and subtract the class maps to estimate habitat loss. Identify four priority problems and fixes. MODEL ANSWER 1 — reveal answer(1) Upsampling invents no detail: analyse at real common support or acquire genuinely finer imagery. (2) Independent stretches destroy radiometric comparability: use compatible calibrated surface reflectance and environmental corrections. (3) Same-image labels and training accuracy are not independent validation: use separately collected, time-matched reference samples and a held-out spatial design. (4) Post-classification change compounds errors: check co-registration, consistent legend and season/tide; validate change transitions directly and report area uncertainty. Award marks for causal explanations, not just naming fixes. Q2 — Platform decision (10 marks). You need annual land-cover estimates over a large Australian region from 2014–2026. Compare ENVI and DEA Sandbox and defend a choice. MODEL ANSWER 2 — reveal answerDEA Sandbox is a defensible primary environment because accessible Australian ARD archives, Python notebooks and time-series processing can reduce manual scene handling and improve reproducibility. Verify product availability, observation density, cloud masks, scaling, cross-sensor consistency and computational constraints. ENVI remains useful for detailed visual checking, custom classification or specialized processing, but local storage, licensing and scene-by-scene management may be less efficient at this scale. Platform selection does not replace independent validation. Q3 — Reference-data audit (12 marks). A 10 m wetland map is trained on 200 labelled pixels. Validation uses 100 adjacent pixels from the same training polygons, with labels copied from a two-year-old RGB basemap. Why might 94% overall accuracy be misleading? MODEL ANSWER 3 — reveal answerAdjacent samples share spatial autocorrelation with training, so reported accuracy may be optimistic. Copied RGB labels may be wrong for wetland classes that require ecological evidence and are temporally mismatched. Overall accuracy can hide omission of rare wetlands. Redesign independent, spatially separated verification with current field or suitable high-resolution evidence, representative sampling, class-specific confusion matrix, and weighting if using disproportionate stratification. Match 10 m pixel support and report uncertain boundaries. Q4 — Calculation (8 marks). Mapped rows × reference columns: Target [24, 6]; Other [12, 58]. Compute overall accuracy, target user’s accuracy, target producer’s accuracy and omission error. MODEL ANSWER 4 — reveal answerTotal = 100. Overall = (24+58)/100 = 82%. Target user’s = 24/(24+6) = 80%. Target producer’s = 24/(24+12) = 66.7%. Target omission error = 1 − 0.667 = 33.3%. The target is missed in one-third of reference target cases even though overall accuracy is 82%. Q5 — GEOM7001 only: integrated design (18 marks). Design an EO workflow to monitor cyclone damage to coastal forest across a cloudy region with limited field access. Defend the role of optical, SAR and LiDAR; state validation and remaining uncertainty. MODEL ANSWER 5 — reveal answerDefine damage (e.g., canopy loss versus structural change), mapping unit, pre/post period and required decision. Use matched optical surface-reflectance time series where clear observations exist for canopy spectral change; SAR can fill cloud-driven observation gaps but backscatter depends on moisture, geometry and structure; LiDAR, if available at appropriate dates/coverage, can independently support height/structural change. Check orbit/calibration/speckle/terrain for SAR, cloud/shadow/season/BRDF for optical, ground classification/DTM/CHM for LiDAR, and co-registration across sensors. Analyse persistent anomalies against a pre-event baseline and unaffected controls, not a single threshold. Validate damaged and unchanged strata with independent field, dated high-resolution or structural observations, and assess class-specific errors and area uncertainty; if modelling continuous height loss, add RMSE/bias. State limits: cloud gaps, SAR moisture confounding, LiDAR cost/temporal gaps, spatial mismatch and uncertain damage dates. A multisensor fusion is justified only when each source contributes identifiable evidence.", "8 | Source basis and final checklist Course basis. Synthesis of the four uploaded GEOM7001 short modules: Module 1 (sensor selection); Module 2 (preprocessing); Module 3 (classification and modelling); Module 4 (accuracy, change, SAR and LiDAR), plus the exam guidance and lecturer feedback supplied in this conversation. Platform comparison is an applied synthesis rather than a verified current software feature inventory. Before the exam. Can you (1) define the output; (2) justify spatial/spectral/radiometric/temporal requirements; (3) audit ARD and preprocessing; (4) select and defend the algorithm; (5) distinguish training from independent verification; (6) calculate user’s/producer’s accuracy; (7) discuss error and alternative approaches; (8) explain ENVI versus DEA Sandbox without claiming either is automatically more accurate?"]}, {"id": 19, "title": "Precision agriculture and pasture quality", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Mock_3_precision_agriculture_and_pasture_quality.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>An agricultural research group wants maps of pasture nitrogen status and biomass to guide paddock management during a growing season. Available: UAV hyperspectral imagery (high spatial/spectral detail), Sentinel-2 time series, destructive quadrat measurements of biomass/nitrogen, UAV calibration-panel observations, and limited flights. Outputs: calibrated continuous estimates, uncertainty, and transferability to later dates and neighbouring paddocks.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The team flies a hyperspectral UAV at noon on a clear day, exports raw digital numbers as reflectance, ignores illumination changes across flight lines, keeps all 200 bands including noisy water-absorption regions, chooses the single band with the highest correlation to nitrogen across the whole dataset, randomly splits 1 m pixels into training/testing despite multiple pixels belonging to the same quadrat, fits a complex model with 99% R2, predicts nitrogen on a different day and paddock, then thresholds the prediction to prescribe fertiliser without reporting prediction error or ground-truth representativeness.</p>", "text": "An agricultural research group wants maps of pasture nitrogen status and biomass to guide paddock management during a growing season. Available: UAV hyperspectral imagery (high spatial/spectral detail), Sentinel-2 time series, destructive quadrat measurements of biomass/nitrogen, UAV calibration-panel observations, and limited flights. Outputs: calibrated continuous estimates, uncertainty, and transferability to later dates and neighbouring paddocks. Flawed proposed workflow The team flies a hyperspectral UAV at noon on a clear day, exports raw digital numbers as reflectance, ignores illumination changes across flight lines, keeps all 200 bands including noisy water-absorption regions, chooses the single band with the highest correlation to nitrogen across the whole dataset, randomly splits 1 m pixels into training/testing despite multiple pixels belonging to the same quadrat, fits a complex model with 99% R2, predicts nitrogen on a different day and paddock, then thresholds the prediction to prescribe fertiliser without reporting prediction error or ground-truth representativeness."}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p><details><summary>Answer 1: Raw DN versus reflectance (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> DN is instrument-dependent and affected by illumination, exposure and sensor characteristics.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use dark/white reference and radiometric calibration, panel-based empirical-line where appropriate, consistent acquisition settings and quality checks.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Illumination/BRDF/mosaics (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Sun angle, changing clouds, viewing geometry and flight-line seams can mimic biochemical differences.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Record geometry and irradiance; correct/normalise where defensible and inspect overlap/flight-line residuals.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Spectral noise and multiple testing (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Noisy bands and searching 200 bands on all data risks spurious correlation and overfitting.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Remove low-SNR/artifact bands, use nested feature selection or dimension reduction inside training folds.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Scale mismatch and leakage (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Many pixels from one quadrat are not independent; random split inflates generalisation estimates.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Aggregate spectra to quadrat footprint and hold out plots/paddocks/dates as independent groups.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Model target confounding (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Spectra reflect canopy water, structure, soil and species as well as nitrogen; correlation is not uniquely causal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Include biomass/phenology/moisture covariates or stratification and examine residuals by conditions.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Transfer and uncertainty (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A model fit on one date/paddock may fail under changed growth stage, soil background or sensor settings.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Test spatial and temporal transfer, report bias/RMSE and uncertainty intervals, and restrict extrapolation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Decision threshold without validation (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A precise-looking fertiliser map can cause costly management errors.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Validate at management-unit scale; quantify false prescriptions and use agronomic thresholds with uncertainty.</p></details>", "text": "Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Raw DN versus reflectance (5 marks)Problem: DN is instrument-dependent and affected by illumination, exposure and sensor characteristics.Correction: Use dark/white reference and radiometric calibration, panel-based empirical-line where appropriate, consistent acquisition settings and quality checks. Answer 2: Illumination/BRDF/mosaics (5 marks)Problem: Sun angle, changing clouds, viewing geometry and flight-line seams can mimic biochemical differences.Correction: Record geometry and irradiance; correct/normalise where defensible and inspect overlap/flight-line residuals. Answer 3: Spectral noise and multiple testing (5 marks)Problem: Noisy bands and searching 200 bands on all data risks spurious correlation and overfitting.Correction: Remove low-SNR/artifact bands, use nested feature selection or dimension reduction inside training folds. Answer 4: Scale mismatch and leakage (6 marks)Problem: Many pixels from one quadrat are not independent; random split inflates generalisation estimates.Correction: Aggregate spectra to quadrat footprint and hold out plots/paddocks/dates as independent groups. Answer 5: Model target confounding (4 marks)Problem: Spectra reflect canopy water, structure, soil and species as well as nitrogen; correlation is not uniquely causal.Correction: Include biomass/phenology/moisture covariates or stratification and examine residuals by conditions. Answer 6: Transfer and uncertainty (6 marks)Problem: A model fit on one date/paddock may fail under changed growth stage, soil background or sensor settings.Correction: Test spatial and temporal transfer, report bias/RMSE and uncertainty intervals, and restrict extrapolation. Answer 7: Decision threshold without validation (4 marks)Problem: A precise-looking fertiliser map can cause costly management errors.Correction: Validate at management-unit scale; quantify false prescriptions and use agronomic thresholds with uncertainty."}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p><details><summary>Stage 1: Question and measurement</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Specify nitrogen concentration (% dry matter) versus nitrogen per area (kg/ha) versus biomass (kg/ha); these are different targets.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Choose the quantity that actually drives management decisions.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Sampling and flights</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Stratify plots by species, biomass, soil and management; acquire contemporaneous destructive quadrats and UAV imagery.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Few well-distributed plots may be more useful than many clustered pixels.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Sensor calibration</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Radiometric correction, panel/irradiance calibration, geometric alignment, orthomosaic quality control and shadow masks.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Hyperspectral detail improves feature sensitivity but increases noise/calibration burden.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Feature extraction</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Remove bad bands; calculate physically plausible red-edge and spectral features; consider vegetation masking and canopy structure.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Compare simple index regression to multivariate methods; complexity must earn predictive gain.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Modelling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Fit regression with grouped cross-validation; tune and select features inside training folds; quantify RMSE, MAE, bias and residual patterns.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Random forest/PLSR may outperform simple regression, but are less interpretable and require more samples.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Scaling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Compare UAV predictions aggregated to Sentinel-2 support; evaluate cross-date transfer and spectral response mismatch.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Do not assume a 10 m pixel equals a mean of pure 1 m canopy pixels if soil/mixed backgrounds differ.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Operational product</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Map predictions, uncertainty and excluded pixels; test management threshold sensitivity.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A calibrated but uncertain model may still support coarse prioritisation, not exact fertiliser prescriptions.</p></details>", "text": "B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Question and measurementRecommended: Specify nitrogen concentration (% dry matter) versus nitrogen per area (kg/ha) versus biomass (kg/ha); these are different targets.Defence and alternatives: Choose the quantity that actually drives management decisions. Stage 2: Sampling and flightsRecommended: Stratify plots by species, biomass, soil and management; acquire contemporaneous destructive quadrats and UAV imagery.Defence and alternatives: Few well-distributed plots may be more useful than many clustered pixels. Stage 3: Sensor calibrationRecommended: Radiometric correction, panel/irradiance calibration, geometric alignment, orthomosaic quality control and shadow masks.Defence and alternatives: Hyperspectral detail improves feature sensitivity but increases noise/calibration burden. Stage 4: Feature extractionRecommended: Remove bad bands; calculate physically plausible red-edge and spectral features; consider vegetation masking and canopy structure.Defence and alternatives: Compare simple index regression to multivariate methods; complexity must earn predictive gain. Stage 5: ModellingRecommended: Fit regression with grouped cross-validation; tune and select features inside training folds; quantify RMSE, MAE, bias and residual patterns.Defence and alternatives: Random forest/PLSR may outperform simple regression, but are less interpretable and require more samples. Stage 6: ScalingRecommended: Compare UAV predictions aggregated to Sentinel-2 support; evaluate cross-date transfer and spectral response mismatch.Defence and alternatives: Do not assume a 10 m pixel equals a mean of pure 1 m canopy pixels if soil/mixed backgrounds differ. Stage 7: Operational productRecommended: Map predictions, uncertainty and excluded pixels; test management threshold sensitivity.Defence and alternatives: A calibrated but uncertain model may still support coarse prioritisation, not exact fertiliser prescriptions."}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Continuous accuracy (8 marks)</h3><p>Reference nitrogen (%)=[2.0,3.0,4.0,5.0]; predictions=[2.4,2.6,4.2,5.4]. Compute bias, MAE and RMSE.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Residuals prediction−reference=[0.4,−0.4,0.2,0.4]; bias=0.15 percentage points; MAE=0.35; RMSE=sqrt((.16+.16+.04+.16)/4)=sqrt(.13)=0.361 percentage points.</p></details><h3>C2 — Spatial versus spectral resolution (6 marks)</h3><p>Defend UAV hyperspectral versus Sentinel-2 for plot-level nitrogen estimation and regional monitoring.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>UAV hyperspectral resolves narrow absorption/red-edge features and fine plots but has limited coverage, costly calibration and infrequent flights. Sentinel-2 has repeat coverage and consistent products but broader bands and coarser pixels; calibrate/validate any transfer.</p></details><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain a nested, grouped cross-validation design for selecting spectral bands without information leakage.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Outer folds hold out entire paddocks or dates to assess transfer. Within each outer training fold, inner grouped folds choose bands/hyperparameters; fit on the outer training set, test once on the held-out group. Report distribution of errors and unstable selected bands.</p></details>", "text": "C1 — Continuous accuracy (8 marks) Reference nitrogen (%)=[2.0,3.0,4.0,5.0]; predictions=[2.4,2.6,4.2,5.4]. Compute bias, MAE and RMSE. Reveal worked answerResiduals prediction−reference=[0.4,−0.4,0.2,0.4]; bias=0.15 percentage points; MAE=0.35; RMSE=sqrt((.16+.16+.04+.16)/4)=sqrt(.13)=0.361 percentage points. C2 — Spatial versus spectral resolution (6 marks) Defend UAV hyperspectral versus Sentinel-2 for plot-level nitrogen estimation and regional monitoring. Reveal worked answerUAV hyperspectral resolves narrow absorption/red-edge features and fine plots but has limited coverage, costly calibration and infrequent flights. Sentinel-2 has repeat coverage and consistent products but broader bands and coarser pixels; calibrate/validate any transfer. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain a nested, grouped cross-validation design for selecting spectral bands without information leakage. Reveal worked answerOuter folds hold out entire paddocks or dates to assess transfer. Within each outer training fold, inner grouped folds choose bands/hyperparameters; fit on the outer training set, test once on the held-out group. Report distribution of errors and unstable selected bands."}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}], "text": ["Scenario An agricultural research group wants maps of pasture nitrogen status and biomass to guide paddock management during a growing season. Available: UAV hyperspectral imagery (high spatial/spectral detail), Sentinel-2 time series, destructive quadrat measurements of biomass/nitrogen, UAV calibration-panel observations, and limited flights. Outputs: calibrated continuous estimates, uncertainty, and transferability to later dates and neighbouring paddocks. Flawed proposed workflow The team flies a hyperspectral UAV at noon on a clear day, exports raw digital numbers as reflectance, ignores illumination changes across flight lines, keeps all 200 bands including noisy water-absorption regions, chooses the single band with the highest correlation to nitrogen across the whole dataset, randomly splits 1 m pixels into training/testing despite multiple pixels belonging to the same quadrat, fits a complex model with 99% R2, predicts nitrogen on a different day and paddock, then thresholds the prediction to prescribe fertiliser without reporting prediction error or ground-truth representativeness.", "A — Critique (35 marks) Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Raw DN versus reflectance (5 marks)Problem: DN is instrument-dependent and affected by illumination, exposure and sensor characteristics.Correction: Use dark/white reference and radiometric calibration, panel-based empirical-line where appropriate, consistent acquisition settings and quality checks. Answer 2: Illumination/BRDF/mosaics (5 marks)Problem: Sun angle, changing clouds, viewing geometry and flight-line seams can mimic biochemical differences.Correction: Record geometry and irradiance; correct/normalise where defensible and inspect overlap/flight-line residuals. Answer 3: Spectral noise and multiple testing (5 marks)Problem: Noisy bands and searching 200 bands on all data risks spurious correlation and overfitting.Correction: Remove low-SNR/artifact bands, use nested feature selection or dimension reduction inside training folds. Answer 4: Scale mismatch and leakage (6 marks)Problem: Many pixels from one quadrat are not independent; random split inflates generalisation estimates.Correction: Aggregate spectra to quadrat footprint and hold out plots/paddocks/dates as independent groups. Answer 5: Model target confounding (4 marks)Problem: Spectra reflect canopy water, structure, soil and species as well as nitrogen; correlation is not uniquely causal.Correction: Include biomass/phenology/moisture covariates or stratification and examine residuals by conditions. Answer 6: Transfer and uncertainty (6 marks)Problem: A model fit on one date/paddock may fail under changed growth stage, soil background or sensor settings.Correction: Test spatial and temporal transfer, report bias/RMSE and uncertainty intervals, and restrict extrapolation. Answer 7: Decision threshold without validation (4 marks)Problem: A precise-looking fertiliser map can cause costly management errors.Correction: Validate at management-unit scale; quantify false prescriptions and use agronomic thresholds with uncertainty.", "B — Design and defend (45 marks) B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Question and measurementRecommended: Specify nitrogen concentration (% dry matter) versus nitrogen per area (kg/ha) versus biomass (kg/ha); these are different targets.Defence and alternatives: Choose the quantity that actually drives management decisions. Stage 2: Sampling and flightsRecommended: Stratify plots by species, biomass, soil and management; acquire contemporaneous destructive quadrats and UAV imagery.Defence and alternatives: Few well-distributed plots may be more useful than many clustered pixels. Stage 3: Sensor calibrationRecommended: Radiometric correction, panel/irradiance calibration, geometric alignment, orthomosaic quality control and shadow masks.Defence and alternatives: Hyperspectral detail improves feature sensitivity but increases noise/calibration burden. Stage 4: Feature extractionRecommended: Remove bad bands; calculate physically plausible red-edge and spectral features; consider vegetation masking and canopy structure.Defence and alternatives: Compare simple index regression to multivariate methods; complexity must earn predictive gain. Stage 5: ModellingRecommended: Fit regression with grouped cross-validation; tune and select features inside training folds; quantify RMSE, MAE, bias and residual patterns.Defence and alternatives: Random forest/PLSR may outperform simple regression, but are less interpretable and require more samples. Stage 6: ScalingRecommended: Compare UAV predictions aggregated to Sentinel-2 support; evaluate cross-date transfer and spectral response mismatch.Defence and alternatives: Do not assume a 10 m pixel equals a mean of pure 1 m canopy pixels if soil/mixed backgrounds differ. Stage 7: Operational productRecommended: Map predictions, uncertainty and excluded pixels; test management threshold sensitivity.Defence and alternatives: A calibrated but uncertain model may still support coarse prioritisation, not exact fertiliser prescriptions.", "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks) C1 — Continuous accuracy (8 marks) Reference nitrogen (%)=[2.0,3.0,4.0,5.0]; predictions=[2.4,2.6,4.2,5.4]. Compute bias, MAE and RMSE. Reveal worked answerResiduals prediction−reference=[0.4,−0.4,0.2,0.4]; bias=0.15 percentage points; MAE=0.35; RMSE=sqrt((.16+.16+.04+.16)/4)=sqrt(.13)=0.361 percentage points. C2 — Spatial versus spectral resolution (6 marks) Defend UAV hyperspectral versus Sentinel-2 for plot-level nitrogen estimation and regional monitoring. Reveal worked answerUAV hyperspectral resolves narrow absorption/red-edge features and fine plots but has limited coverage, costly calibration and infrequent flights. Sentinel-2 has repeat coverage and consistent products but broader bands and coarser pixels; calibrate/validate any transfer. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain a nested, grouped cross-validation design for selecting spectral bands without information leakage. Reveal worked answerOuter folds hold out entire paddocks or dates to assess transfer. Within each outer training fold, inner grouped folds choose bands/hyperparameters; fit on the outer training set, test once on the held-out group. Report distribution of errors and unstable selected bands.", "Self-marking guide 85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>An agricultural research group wants maps of pasture nitrogen status and biomass to guide paddock management during a growing season. Available: UAV hyperspectral imagery (high spatial/spectral detail), Sentinel-2 time series, destructive quadrat measurements of biomass/nitrogen, UAV calibration-panel observations, and limited flights. Outputs: calibrated continuous estimates, uncertainty, and transferability to later dates and neighbouring paddocks.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The team flies a hyperspectral UAV at noon on a clear day, exports raw digital numbers as reflectance, ignores illumination changes across flight lines, keeps all 200 bands including noisy water-absorption regions, chooses the single band with the highest correlation to nitrogen across the whole dataset, randomly splits 1 m pixels into training/testing despite multiple pixels belonging to the same quadrat, fits a complex model with 99% R2, predicts nitrogen on a different day and paddock, then thresholds the prediction to prescribe fertiliser without reporting prediction error or ground-truth representativeness.</p>"}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Continuous accuracy (8 marks)</h3><p>Reference nitrogen (%)=[2.0,3.0,4.0,5.0]; predictions=[2.4,2.6,4.2,5.4]. Compute bias, MAE and RMSE.</p><h3>C2 — Spatial versus spectral resolution (6 marks)</h3><p>Defend UAV hyperspectral versus Sentinel-2 for plot-level nitrogen estimation and regional monitoring.</p><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain a nested, grouped cross-validation design for selecting spectral bands without information leakage.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Answer 1: Raw DN versus reflectance (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> DN is instrument-dependent and affected by illumination, exposure and sensor characteristics.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use dark/white reference and radiometric calibration, panel-based empirical-line where appropriate, consistent acquisition settings and quality checks.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Illumination/BRDF/mosaics (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Sun angle, changing clouds, viewing geometry and flight-line seams can mimic biochemical differences.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Record geometry and irradiance; correct/normalise where defensible and inspect overlap/flight-line residuals.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Spectral noise and multiple testing (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Noisy bands and searching 200 bands on all data risks spurious correlation and overfitting.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Remove low-SNR/artifact bands, use nested feature selection or dimension reduction inside training folds.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Scale mismatch and leakage (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Many pixels from one quadrat are not independent; random split inflates generalisation estimates.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Aggregate spectra to quadrat footprint and hold out plots/paddocks/dates as independent groups.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Model target confounding (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Spectra reflect canopy water, structure, soil and species as well as nitrogen; correlation is not uniquely causal.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Include biomass/phenology/moisture covariates or stratification and examine residuals by conditions.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Transfer and uncertainty (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A model fit on one date/paddock may fail under changed growth stage, soil background or sensor settings.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Test spatial and temporal transfer, report bias/RMSE and uncertainty intervals, and restrict extrapolation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Decision threshold without validation (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A precise-looking fertiliser map can cause costly management errors.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Validate at management-unit scale; quantify false prescriptions and use agronomic thresholds with uncertainty.</p></details>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Stage 1: Question and measurement</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Specify nitrogen concentration (% dry matter) versus nitrogen per area (kg/ha) versus biomass (kg/ha); these are different targets.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Choose the quantity that actually drives management decisions.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Sampling and flights</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Stratify plots by species, biomass, soil and management; acquire contemporaneous destructive quadrats and UAV imagery.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Few well-distributed plots may be more useful than many clustered pixels.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Sensor calibration</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Radiometric correction, panel/irradiance calibration, geometric alignment, orthomosaic quality control and shadow masks.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Hyperspectral detail improves feature sensitivity but increases noise/calibration burden.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Feature extraction</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Remove bad bands; calculate physically plausible red-edge and spectral features; consider vegetation masking and canopy structure.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Compare simple index regression to multivariate methods; complexity must earn predictive gain.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Modelling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Fit regression with grouped cross-validation; tune and select features inside training folds; quantify RMSE, MAE, bias and residual patterns.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Random forest/PLSR may outperform simple regression, but are less interpretable and require more samples.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Scaling</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Compare UAV predictions aggregated to Sentinel-2 support; evaluate cross-date transfer and spectral response mismatch.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Do not assume a 10 m pixel equals a mean of pure 1 m canopy pixels if soil/mixed backgrounds differ.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Operational product</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Map predictions, uncertainty and excluded pixels; test management threshold sensitivity.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A calibrated but uncertain model may still support coarse prioritisation, not exact fertiliser prescriptions.</p></details>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Residuals prediction−reference=[0.4,−0.4,0.2,0.4]; bias=0.15 percentage points; MAE=0.35; RMSE=sqrt((.16+.16+.04+.16)/4)=sqrt(.13)=0.361 percentage points.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>UAV hyperspectral resolves narrow absorption/red-edge features and fine plots but has limited coverage, costly calibration and infrequent flights. Sentinel-2 has repeat coverage and consistent products but broader bands and coarser pixels; calibrate/validate any transfer.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Outer folds hold out entire paddocks or dates to assess transfer. Within each outer training fold, inner grouped folds choose bands/hyperparameters; fit on the outer training set, test once on the held-out group. Report distribution of errors and unstable selected bands.</p></details>"}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}]}, {"id": 20, "title": "Forest structure and biomass estimation", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Mock_4_forest_structure_and_biomass_estimation.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A forest agency needs canopy-height and aboveground biomass estimates across mixed eucalyptus forest with steep slopes and occasional dense understory. Available: airborne LiDAR strips from one year, terrestrial LiDAR plots, field stem measurements and allometric equations, Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 imagery from multiple seasons. The agency requests a biomass map and a defensible estimate of its uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst takes the highest LiDAR return in each 10 m cell as tree height, subtracts a global constant elevation instead of a terrain model, accepts ground points from only one scan strip, fits biomass against canopy height using the same 20 plots used for validation, ignores allometric uncertainty and occlusion, converts SAR VV backscatter in dB directly into linear biomass by multiplying by 10, merges all scenes regardless of incidence angle and soil moisture, and reports 99% accurate biomass with no units or confidence interval.</p>", "text": "A forest agency needs canopy-height and aboveground biomass estimates across mixed eucalyptus forest with steep slopes and occasional dense understory. Available: airborne LiDAR strips from one year, terrestrial LiDAR plots, field stem measurements and allometric equations, Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 imagery from multiple seasons. The agency requests a biomass map and a defensible estimate of its uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst takes the highest LiDAR return in each 10 m cell as tree height, subtracts a global constant elevation instead of a terrain model, accepts ground points from only one scan strip, fits biomass against canopy height using the same 20 plots used for validation, ignores allometric uncertainty and occlusion, converts SAR VV backscatter in dB directly into linear biomass by multiplying by 10, merges all scenes regardless of incidence angle and soil moisture, and reports 99% accurate biomass with no units or confidence interval."}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p><details><summary>Answer 1: Terrain normalisation (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Raw LiDAR elevation is not canopy height; slopes and terrain elevation dominate without a DTM.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Classify ground points, generate DTM/DSM and compute height above local ground; inspect steep-slope errors.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Point-cloud coverage (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Occlusion, variable density, strip alignment and scan angle affect detected canopy and ground.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Check strip registration, density, returns and gap distribution; use multiple scan positions where needed.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Height metric (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Single maximum return is noise-sensitive and not equal to stand height or biomass.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use robust canopy-height percentiles, cover, vertical density and plot-matched metrics.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Training/validation leakage (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Same 20 plots for calibration and testing yields optimistic accuracy.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use independent plot or spatial-block validation and quantify sampling uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Allometry and support (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Field biomass depends on tree measurements, species-specific allometry and plot-to-pixel matching.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Document equations, plot size, GPS, allometric errors and propagate uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: SAR misuse (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Backscatter dB is logarithmic, depends on structure, moisture and geometry, and may saturate for dense biomass.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Calibrate/terrain-correct SAR, convert units correctly and model relationships empirically with independent validation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Uncertainty claim (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> 99% accurate has no defined metric for a continuous variable.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report RMSE, MAE, bias in Mg/ha, interval coverage, errors by forest type and spatial extrapolation limits.</p></details>", "text": "Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Terrain normalisation (6 marks)Problem: Raw LiDAR elevation is not canopy height; slopes and terrain elevation dominate without a DTM.Correction: Classify ground points, generate DTM/DSM and compute height above local ground; inspect steep-slope errors. Answer 2: Point-cloud coverage (5 marks)Problem: Occlusion, variable density, strip alignment and scan angle affect detected canopy and ground.Correction: Check strip registration, density, returns and gap distribution; use multiple scan positions where needed. Answer 3: Height metric (4 marks)Problem: Single maximum return is noise-sensitive and not equal to stand height or biomass.Correction: Use robust canopy-height percentiles, cover, vertical density and plot-matched metrics. Answer 4: Training/validation leakage (5 marks)Problem: Same 20 plots for calibration and testing yields optimistic accuracy.Correction: Use independent plot or spatial-block validation and quantify sampling uncertainty. Answer 5: Allometry and support (5 marks)Problem: Field biomass depends on tree measurements, species-specific allometry and plot-to-pixel matching.Correction: Document equations, plot size, GPS, allometric errors and propagate uncertainty. Answer 6: SAR misuse (6 marks)Problem: Backscatter dB is logarithmic, depends on structure, moisture and geometry, and may saturate for dense biomass.Correction: Calibrate/terrain-correct SAR, convert units correctly and model relationships empirically with independent validation. Answer 7: Uncertainty claim (4 marks)Problem: 99% accurate has no defined metric for a continuous variable.Correction: Report RMSE, MAE, bias in Mg/ha, interval coverage, errors by forest type and spatial extrapolation limits."}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p><details><summary>Stage 1: Biophysical target</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define AGB dry mass (Mg/ha), plot area, forest types and reference year.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Biomass is not a directly observed laser or radar quantity.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: LiDAR preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Register strips, classify ground/non-ground, build DTM, normalise point heights, QC point density and canopy occlusion.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Airborne LiDAR covers large areas; TLS captures detailed stem structure but is locally limited.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Plot linkage</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Measure stems/DBH/heights and apply appropriate allometry; co-register plots and compute matching LiDAR metrics.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A few badly located plots can dominate model error.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Multisensor features</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> LiDAR height percentiles/cover; optionally Sentinel-1 calibrated backscatter and Sentinel-2 canopy condition/land-cover covariates.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> SAR can add moisture/structure information but is not a direct linear biomass meter.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Regression</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Fit interpretable baseline then more flexible models; assess saturation, ecological transfer and covariate importance.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Complex models may improve fit but increase extrapolation risk.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Independent assessment</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Spatially separated plots; report RMSE/bias by stand type, model and allometric uncertainty, map intervals.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Uncertainty can arise in both reference biomass and satellite predictors.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Decision output</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Provide biomass maps, units, date, coverage and uncertainty; do not imply LiDAR alone measures carbon.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Carbon conversion requires explicit factors/assumptions and further uncertainty.</p></details>", "text": "B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Biophysical targetRecommended: Define AGB dry mass (Mg/ha), plot area, forest types and reference year.Defence and alternatives: Biomass is not a directly observed laser or radar quantity. Stage 2: LiDAR preprocessingRecommended: Register strips, classify ground/non-ground, build DTM, normalise point heights, QC point density and canopy occlusion.Defence and alternatives: Airborne LiDAR covers large areas; TLS captures detailed stem structure but is locally limited. Stage 3: Plot linkageRecommended: Measure stems/DBH/heights and apply appropriate allometry; co-register plots and compute matching LiDAR metrics.Defence and alternatives: A few badly located plots can dominate model error. Stage 4: Multisensor featuresRecommended: LiDAR height percentiles/cover; optionally Sentinel-1 calibrated backscatter and Sentinel-2 canopy condition/land-cover covariates.Defence and alternatives: SAR can add moisture/structure information but is not a direct linear biomass meter. Stage 5: RegressionRecommended: Fit interpretable baseline then more flexible models; assess saturation, ecological transfer and covariate importance.Defence and alternatives: Complex models may improve fit but increase extrapolation risk. Stage 6: Independent assessmentRecommended: Spatially separated plots; report RMSE/bias by stand type, model and allometric uncertainty, map intervals.Defence and alternatives: Uncertainty can arise in both reference biomass and satellite predictors. Stage 7: Decision outputRecommended: Provide biomass maps, units, date, coverage and uncertainty; do not imply LiDAR alone measures carbon.Defence and alternatives: Carbon conversion requires explicit factors/assumptions and further uncertainty."}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — LiDAR height calculation (7 marks)</h3><p>At one location DSM=127.4 m and DTM=108.9 m. Calculate canopy height and explain one possible error.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>CHM=DSM−DTM=18.5 m. Misclassified ground points or horizontal misregistration on a slope bias the DTM and therefore the height estimate.</p></details><h3>C2 — Radar dB and physical interpretation (7 marks)</h3><p>Convert sigma0=−10 dB to linear power ratio and explain why it is not biomass.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Linear sigma0=10^(−10/10)=0.1. Radar backscatter also depends on moisture, roughness, structure, polarisation and incidence angle; biomass requires calibrated modelling and independent plots.</p></details><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain how a LiDAR-derived 3D canopy model can support radiative transfer modelling, and why the inverse problem is uncertain.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>LiDAR supplies canopy geometry/gap structure for a forward RTM predicting reflectance. Inversion for leaf/biophysical properties is non-unique because geometry, leaf optics, soil background and atmosphere can produce similar signals; use priors, multi-angle/spectral observations and independent validation.</p></details>", "text": "C1 — LiDAR height calculation (7 marks) At one location DSM=127.4 m and DTM=108.9 m. Calculate canopy height and explain one possible error. Reveal worked answerCHM=DSM−DTM=18.5 m. Misclassified ground points or horizontal misregistration on a slope bias the DTM and therefore the height estimate. C2 — Radar dB and physical interpretation (7 marks) Convert sigma0=−10 dB to linear power ratio and explain why it is not biomass. Reveal worked answerLinear sigma0=10^(−10/10)=0.1. Radar backscatter also depends on moisture, roughness, structure, polarisation and incidence angle; biomass requires calibrated modelling and independent plots. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain how a LiDAR-derived 3D canopy model can support radiative transfer modelling, and why the inverse problem is uncertain. Reveal worked answerLiDAR supplies canopy geometry/gap structure for a forward RTM predicting reflectance. Inversion for leaf/biophysical properties is non-unique because geometry, leaf optics, soil background and atmosphere can produce similar signals; use priors, multi-angle/spectral observations and independent validation."}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}], "text": ["Scenario A forest agency needs canopy-height and aboveground biomass estimates across mixed eucalyptus forest with steep slopes and occasional dense understory. Available: airborne LiDAR strips from one year, terrestrial LiDAR plots, field stem measurements and allometric equations, Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 imagery from multiple seasons. The agency requests a biomass map and a defensible estimate of its uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst takes the highest LiDAR return in each 10 m cell as tree height, subtracts a global constant elevation instead of a terrain model, accepts ground points from only one scan strip, fits biomass against canopy height using the same 20 plots used for validation, ignores allometric uncertainty and occlusion, converts SAR VV backscatter in dB directly into linear biomass by multiplying by 10, merges all scenes regardless of incidence angle and soil moisture, and reports 99% accurate biomass with no units or confidence interval.", "A — Critique (35 marks) Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Terrain normalisation (6 marks)Problem: Raw LiDAR elevation is not canopy height; slopes and terrain elevation dominate without a DTM.Correction: Classify ground points, generate DTM/DSM and compute height above local ground; inspect steep-slope errors. Answer 2: Point-cloud coverage (5 marks)Problem: Occlusion, variable density, strip alignment and scan angle affect detected canopy and ground.Correction: Check strip registration, density, returns and gap distribution; use multiple scan positions where needed. Answer 3: Height metric (4 marks)Problem: Single maximum return is noise-sensitive and not equal to stand height or biomass.Correction: Use robust canopy-height percentiles, cover, vertical density and plot-matched metrics. Answer 4: Training/validation leakage (5 marks)Problem: Same 20 plots for calibration and testing yields optimistic accuracy.Correction: Use independent plot or spatial-block validation and quantify sampling uncertainty. Answer 5: Allometry and support (5 marks)Problem: Field biomass depends on tree measurements, species-specific allometry and plot-to-pixel matching.Correction: Document equations, plot size, GPS, allometric errors and propagate uncertainty. Answer 6: SAR misuse (6 marks)Problem: Backscatter dB is logarithmic, depends on structure, moisture and geometry, and may saturate for dense biomass.Correction: Calibrate/terrain-correct SAR, convert units correctly and model relationships empirically with independent validation. Answer 7: Uncertainty claim (4 marks)Problem: 99% accurate has no defined metric for a continuous variable.Correction: Report RMSE, MAE, bias in Mg/ha, interval coverage, errors by forest type and spatial extrapolation limits.", "B — Design and defend (45 marks) B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Biophysical targetRecommended: Define AGB dry mass (Mg/ha), plot area, forest types and reference year.Defence and alternatives: Biomass is not a directly observed laser or radar quantity. Stage 2: LiDAR preprocessingRecommended: Register strips, classify ground/non-ground, build DTM, normalise point heights, QC point density and canopy occlusion.Defence and alternatives: Airborne LiDAR covers large areas; TLS captures detailed stem structure but is locally limited. Stage 3: Plot linkageRecommended: Measure stems/DBH/heights and apply appropriate allometry; co-register plots and compute matching LiDAR metrics.Defence and alternatives: A few badly located plots can dominate model error. Stage 4: Multisensor featuresRecommended: LiDAR height percentiles/cover; optionally Sentinel-1 calibrated backscatter and Sentinel-2 canopy condition/land-cover covariates.Defence and alternatives: SAR can add moisture/structure information but is not a direct linear biomass meter. Stage 5: RegressionRecommended: Fit interpretable baseline then more flexible models; assess saturation, ecological transfer and covariate importance.Defence and alternatives: Complex models may improve fit but increase extrapolation risk. Stage 6: Independent assessmentRecommended: Spatially separated plots; report RMSE/bias by stand type, model and allometric uncertainty, map intervals.Defence and alternatives: Uncertainty can arise in both reference biomass and satellite predictors. Stage 7: Decision outputRecommended: Provide biomass maps, units, date, coverage and uncertainty; do not imply LiDAR alone measures carbon.Defence and alternatives: Carbon conversion requires explicit factors/assumptions and further uncertainty.", "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks) C1 — LiDAR height calculation (7 marks) At one location DSM=127.4 m and DTM=108.9 m. Calculate canopy height and explain one possible error. Reveal worked answerCHM=DSM−DTM=18.5 m. Misclassified ground points or horizontal misregistration on a slope bias the DTM and therefore the height estimate. C2 — Radar dB and physical interpretation (7 marks) Convert sigma0=−10 dB to linear power ratio and explain why it is not biomass. Reveal worked answerLinear sigma0=10^(−10/10)=0.1. Radar backscatter also depends on moisture, roughness, structure, polarisation and incidence angle; biomass requires calibrated modelling and independent plots. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain how a LiDAR-derived 3D canopy model can support radiative transfer modelling, and why the inverse problem is uncertain. Reveal worked answerLiDAR supplies canopy geometry/gap structure for a forward RTM predicting reflectance. Inversion for leaf/biophysical properties is non-unique because geometry, leaf optics, soil background and atmosphere can produce similar signals; use priors, multi-angle/spectral observations and independent validation.", "Self-marking guide 85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A forest agency needs canopy-height and aboveground biomass estimates across mixed eucalyptus forest with steep slopes and occasional dense understory. Available: airborne LiDAR strips from one year, terrestrial LiDAR plots, field stem measurements and allometric equations, Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 imagery from multiple seasons. The agency requests a biomass map and a defensible estimate of its uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst takes the highest LiDAR return in each 10 m cell as tree height, subtracts a global constant elevation instead of a terrain model, accepts ground points from only one scan strip, fits biomass against canopy height using the same 20 plots used for validation, ignores allometric uncertainty and occlusion, converts SAR VV backscatter in dB directly into linear biomass by multiplying by 10, merges all scenes regardless of incidence angle and soil moisture, and reports 99% accurate biomass with no units or confidence interval.</p>"}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — LiDAR height calculation (7 marks)</h3><p>At one location DSM=127.4 m and DTM=108.9 m. Calculate canopy height and explain one possible error.</p><h3>C2 — Radar dB and physical interpretation (7 marks)</h3><p>Convert sigma0=−10 dB to linear power ratio and explain why it is not biomass.</p><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain how a LiDAR-derived 3D canopy model can support radiative transfer modelling, and why the inverse problem is uncertain.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Answer 1: Terrain normalisation (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Raw LiDAR elevation is not canopy height; slopes and terrain elevation dominate without a DTM.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Classify ground points, generate DTM/DSM and compute height above local ground; inspect steep-slope errors.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Point-cloud coverage (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Occlusion, variable density, strip alignment and scan angle affect detected canopy and ground.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Check strip registration, density, returns and gap distribution; use multiple scan positions where needed.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Height metric (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Single maximum return is noise-sensitive and not equal to stand height or biomass.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use robust canopy-height percentiles, cover, vertical density and plot-matched metrics.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Training/validation leakage (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Same 20 plots for calibration and testing yields optimistic accuracy.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use independent plot or spatial-block validation and quantify sampling uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Allometry and support (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Field biomass depends on tree measurements, species-specific allometry and plot-to-pixel matching.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Document equations, plot size, GPS, allometric errors and propagate uncertainty.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: SAR misuse (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Backscatter dB is logarithmic, depends on structure, moisture and geometry, and may saturate for dense biomass.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Calibrate/terrain-correct SAR, convert units correctly and model relationships empirically with independent validation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Uncertainty claim (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> 99% accurate has no defined metric for a continuous variable.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report RMSE, MAE, bias in Mg/ha, interval coverage, errors by forest type and spatial extrapolation limits.</p></details>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Stage 1: Biophysical target</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define AGB dry mass (Mg/ha), plot area, forest types and reference year.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Biomass is not a directly observed laser or radar quantity.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: LiDAR preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Register strips, classify ground/non-ground, build DTM, normalise point heights, QC point density and canopy occlusion.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Airborne LiDAR covers large areas; TLS captures detailed stem structure but is locally limited.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Plot linkage</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Measure stems/DBH/heights and apply appropriate allometry; co-register plots and compute matching LiDAR metrics.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A few badly located plots can dominate model error.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Multisensor features</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> LiDAR height percentiles/cover; optionally Sentinel-1 calibrated backscatter and Sentinel-2 canopy condition/land-cover covariates.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> SAR can add moisture/structure information but is not a direct linear biomass meter.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Regression</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Fit interpretable baseline then more flexible models; assess saturation, ecological transfer and covariate importance.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Complex models may improve fit but increase extrapolation risk.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Independent assessment</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Spatially separated plots; report RMSE/bias by stand type, model and allometric uncertainty, map intervals.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Uncertainty can arise in both reference biomass and satellite predictors.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Decision output</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Provide biomass maps, units, date, coverage and uncertainty; do not imply LiDAR alone measures carbon.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Carbon conversion requires explicit factors/assumptions and further uncertainty.</p></details>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>CHM=DSM−DTM=18.5 m. Misclassified ground points or horizontal misregistration on a slope bias the DTM and therefore the height estimate.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Linear sigma0=10^(−10/10)=0.1. Radar backscatter also depends on moisture, roughness, structure, polarisation and incidence angle; biomass requires calibrated modelling and independent plots.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>LiDAR supplies canopy geometry/gap structure for a forward RTM predicting reflectance. Inversion for leaf/biophysical properties is non-unique because geometry, leaf optics, soil background and atmosphere can produce similar signals; use priors, multi-angle/spectral observations and independent validation.</p></details>"}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}]}, {"id": 21, "title": "Urban expansion and impervious-surface change", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Mock_5_urban_expansion_and_impervious_surface_change.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A city council wants annual estimates of impervious-surface expansion and the conversion of vegetation to built land between 2016 and 2026. Available: Landsat surface reflectance, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, aerial orthophotos for selected years, cadastral roads/buildings, and field visits in accessible neighbourhoods. The council requires a reproducible change map and area uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst compares a 30 m Landsat image from wet season 2016 to a 10 m Sentinel-2 image from dry season 2026, resamples Landsat to 10 m and calls both products equally detailed; classifies each image with different class definitions; applies unsupervised clustering in 2016 and a supervised RF in 2026; validates only the 2026 map using easy roadside points; computes change by subtracting class-code numbers; interprets all red-to-grey transitions as urbanisation; and reports 2.3% growth to one decimal place despite 15% class error.</p>", "text": "A city council wants annual estimates of impervious-surface expansion and the conversion of vegetation to built land between 2016 and 2026. Available: Landsat surface reflectance, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, aerial orthophotos for selected years, cadastral roads/buildings, and field visits in accessible neighbourhoods. The council requires a reproducible change map and area uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst compares a 30 m Landsat image from wet season 2016 to a 10 m Sentinel-2 image from dry season 2026, resamples Landsat to 10 m and calls both products equally detailed; classifies each image with different class definitions; applies unsupervised clustering in 2016 and a supervised RF in 2026; validates only the 2026 map using easy roadside points; computes change by subtracting class-code numbers; interprets all red-to-grey transitions as urbanisation; and reports 2.3% growth to one decimal place despite 15% class error."}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p><details><summary>Answer 1: Resolution mismatch (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not recover 10 m information; mixed pixels and PSF differ.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use consistent support or aggregate higher-resolution data to common units; assess minimum mapping unit.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Season/phenology (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Wet/dry vegetation, soil moisture and shadow can mimic impervious change.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use comparable seasonal composites and robust cloud/shadow masks.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Inconsistent taxonomy (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Different class definitions prevent meaningful transition comparison.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Harmonise class labels and classification protocols across dates.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Algorithm and temporal inconsistency (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Unsupervised clusters do not automatically map to the same semantic classes as RF outputs.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use consistent reference-guided labels or explicitly crosswalk and validate each date.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Biased reference (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Roadside samples miss rooftops, informal developments and interior green spaces.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use probability sampling stratified by class/change and independent high-quality orthophotos.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Class-code subtraction (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Codes are nominal; numeric differences have no physical interpretation.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use categorical cross-tabulation/transition matrices and distinguish persistent/changed labels.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: False precision (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A small mapped growth may be within classification/change uncertainty.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Estimate change area using independent change-reference samples and confidence intervals; report sensitivity.</p></details>", "text": "Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Resolution mismatch (6 marks)Problem: Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not recover 10 m information; mixed pixels and PSF differ.Correction: Use consistent support or aggregate higher-resolution data to common units; assess minimum mapping unit. Answer 2: Season/phenology (4 marks)Problem: Wet/dry vegetation, soil moisture and shadow can mimic impervious change.Correction: Use comparable seasonal composites and robust cloud/shadow masks. Answer 3: Inconsistent taxonomy (5 marks)Problem: Different class definitions prevent meaningful transition comparison.Correction: Harmonise class labels and classification protocols across dates. Answer 4: Algorithm and temporal inconsistency (4 marks)Problem: Unsupervised clusters do not automatically map to the same semantic classes as RF outputs.Correction: Use consistent reference-guided labels or explicitly crosswalk and validate each date. Answer 5: Biased reference (5 marks)Problem: Roadside samples miss rooftops, informal developments and interior green spaces.Correction: Use probability sampling stratified by class/change and independent high-quality orthophotos. Answer 6: Class-code subtraction (5 marks)Problem: Codes are nominal; numeric differences have no physical interpretation.Correction: Use categorical cross-tabulation/transition matrices and distinguish persistent/changed labels. Answer 7: False precision (6 marks)Problem: A small mapped growth may be within classification/change uncertainty.Correction: Estimate change area using independent change-reference samples and confidence intervals; report sensitivity."}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p><details><summary>Stage 1: Question and categories</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define impervious, pervious vegetation, bare soil, water and mixed categories; specify whether expansion is gross or net.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Bare construction soil may be transitional, not permanent impervious cover.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Image harmonisation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Choose comparable seasons, surface-reflectance products, consistent grid/support, cloud/shadow and topographic corrections where relevant.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Landsat has long history; Sentinel-2 finer pixels but shorter record and different spectral response.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Ancillary evidence</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use orthophotos, building footprints and roads as supporting reference/predictors, respecting their date and incompleteness.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Ancillary maps may lag construction and cannot be assumed error-free.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Classification</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Train consistent supervised models or calibrated fractional imperviousness; check urban shadow and soil confusion.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Fractional cover may better handle mixed pixels than hard classes.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Change estimation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Create class transition matrix, inspect stable/changed pixels, compare annual trajectories and control for temporary bare soil.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Post-classification change compounds errors; direct spectral change can be more sensitive but less semantic.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Validation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent probability-based reference for both dates and transitions; user/producer accuracies and area-adjusted estimates.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Oversample rare change classes but weight results to population area.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Delivery</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Report gross gain, loss, net gain, intervals and locations; explain resolution and temporal limitations.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Precision in hectares must be justified by sampling error and map scale.</p></details>", "text": "B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Question and categoriesRecommended: Define impervious, pervious vegetation, bare soil, water and mixed categories; specify whether expansion is gross or net.Defence and alternatives: Bare construction soil may be transitional, not permanent impervious cover. Stage 2: Image harmonisationRecommended: Choose comparable seasons, surface-reflectance products, consistent grid/support, cloud/shadow and topographic corrections where relevant.Defence and alternatives: Landsat has long history; Sentinel-2 finer pixels but shorter record and different spectral response. Stage 3: Ancillary evidenceRecommended: Use orthophotos, building footprints and roads as supporting reference/predictors, respecting their date and incompleteness.Defence and alternatives: Ancillary maps may lag construction and cannot be assumed error-free. Stage 4: ClassificationRecommended: Train consistent supervised models or calibrated fractional imperviousness; check urban shadow and soil confusion.Defence and alternatives: Fractional cover may better handle mixed pixels than hard classes. Stage 5: Change estimationRecommended: Create class transition matrix, inspect stable/changed pixels, compare annual trajectories and control for temporary bare soil.Defence and alternatives: Post-classification change compounds errors; direct spectral change can be more sensitive but less semantic. Stage 6: ValidationRecommended: Independent probability-based reference for both dates and transitions; user/producer accuracies and area-adjusted estimates.Defence and alternatives: Oversample rare change classes but weight results to population area. Stage 7: DeliveryRecommended: Report gross gain, loss, net gain, intervals and locations; explain resolution and temporal limitations.Defence and alternatives: Precision in hectares must be justified by sampling error and map scale."}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Transition matrix (8 marks)</h3><p>Of 100 ha classified vegetation in 2016, 24 ha is classified impervious in 2026; 10 ha originally impervious is now classified vegetation. What are gross vegetation-to-impervious transition and net impervious gain from these two transitions?</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Gross mapped vegetation→impervious=24 ha; reverse=10 ha; net from these transitions=14 ha. These are mapped changes, not necessarily true changes; other class transitions and classification error may affect total city net gain.</p></details><h3>C2 — ENVI versus DEA Sandbox (6 marks)</h3><p>Defend which platform is better for a ten-year annual Landsat/Sentinel analysis and when the alternative is preferable.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>DEA Sandbox/data-cube tools facilitate analysis-ready time series, reproducible Python workflows and cloud-scale composites. ENVI provides interactive visual inspection, specialist image processing, ROI tools and manual diagnostics; local data/compute management is harder for long time series. Both require independent validation and careful product/algorithm choice.</p></details><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain why stratified oversampling of rare change pixels requires weighting to estimate area-wide accuracy.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>A 50:50 sample of changed/stable pixels may not match population proportions. Raw OA and mapped change proportions are biased for the whole area. Use design weights/stratum areas, reference-based area estimates and variance/confidence intervals; preserve inclusion probabilities.</p></details>", "text": "C1 — Transition matrix (8 marks) Of 100 ha classified vegetation in 2016, 24 ha is classified impervious in 2026; 10 ha originally impervious is now classified vegetation. What are gross vegetation-to-impervious transition and net impervious gain from these two transitions? Reveal worked answerGross mapped vegetation→impervious=24 ha; reverse=10 ha; net from these transitions=14 ha. These are mapped changes, not necessarily true changes; other class transitions and classification error may affect total city net gain. C2 — ENVI versus DEA Sandbox (6 marks) Defend which platform is better for a ten-year annual Landsat/Sentinel analysis and when the alternative is preferable. Reveal worked answerDEA Sandbox/data-cube tools facilitate analysis-ready time series, reproducible Python workflows and cloud-scale composites. ENVI provides interactive visual inspection, specialist image processing, ROI tools and manual diagnostics; local data/compute management is harder for long time series. Both require independent validation and careful product/algorithm choice. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain why stratified oversampling of rare change pixels requires weighting to estimate area-wide accuracy. Reveal worked answerA 50:50 sample of changed/stable pixels may not match population proportions. Raw OA and mapped change proportions are biased for the whole area. Use design weights/stratum areas, reference-based area estimates and variance/confidence intervals; preserve inclusion probabilities."}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}], "text": ["Scenario A city council wants annual estimates of impervious-surface expansion and the conversion of vegetation to built land between 2016 and 2026. Available: Landsat surface reflectance, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, aerial orthophotos for selected years, cadastral roads/buildings, and field visits in accessible neighbourhoods. The council requires a reproducible change map and area uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst compares a 30 m Landsat image from wet season 2016 to a 10 m Sentinel-2 image from dry season 2026, resamples Landsat to 10 m and calls both products equally detailed; classifies each image with different class definitions; applies unsupervised clustering in 2016 and a supervised RF in 2026; validates only the 2026 map using easy roadside points; computes change by subtracting class-code numbers; interprets all red-to-grey transitions as urbanisation; and reports 2.3% growth to one decimal place despite 15% class error.", "A — Critique (35 marks) Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Resolution mismatch (6 marks)Problem: Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not recover 10 m information; mixed pixels and PSF differ.Correction: Use consistent support or aggregate higher-resolution data to common units; assess minimum mapping unit. Answer 2: Season/phenology (4 marks)Problem: Wet/dry vegetation, soil moisture and shadow can mimic impervious change.Correction: Use comparable seasonal composites and robust cloud/shadow masks. Answer 3: Inconsistent taxonomy (5 marks)Problem: Different class definitions prevent meaningful transition comparison.Correction: Harmonise class labels and classification protocols across dates. Answer 4: Algorithm and temporal inconsistency (4 marks)Problem: Unsupervised clusters do not automatically map to the same semantic classes as RF outputs.Correction: Use consistent reference-guided labels or explicitly crosswalk and validate each date. Answer 5: Biased reference (5 marks)Problem: Roadside samples miss rooftops, informal developments and interior green spaces.Correction: Use probability sampling stratified by class/change and independent high-quality orthophotos. Answer 6: Class-code subtraction (5 marks)Problem: Codes are nominal; numeric differences have no physical interpretation.Correction: Use categorical cross-tabulation/transition matrices and distinguish persistent/changed labels. Answer 7: False precision (6 marks)Problem: A small mapped growth may be within classification/change uncertainty.Correction: Estimate change area using independent change-reference samples and confidence intervals; report sensitivity.", "B — Design and defend (45 marks) B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Question and categoriesRecommended: Define impervious, pervious vegetation, bare soil, water and mixed categories; specify whether expansion is gross or net.Defence and alternatives: Bare construction soil may be transitional, not permanent impervious cover. Stage 2: Image harmonisationRecommended: Choose comparable seasons, surface-reflectance products, consistent grid/support, cloud/shadow and topographic corrections where relevant.Defence and alternatives: Landsat has long history; Sentinel-2 finer pixels but shorter record and different spectral response. Stage 3: Ancillary evidenceRecommended: Use orthophotos, building footprints and roads as supporting reference/predictors, respecting their date and incompleteness.Defence and alternatives: Ancillary maps may lag construction and cannot be assumed error-free. Stage 4: ClassificationRecommended: Train consistent supervised models or calibrated fractional imperviousness; check urban shadow and soil confusion.Defence and alternatives: Fractional cover may better handle mixed pixels than hard classes. Stage 5: Change estimationRecommended: Create class transition matrix, inspect stable/changed pixels, compare annual trajectories and control for temporary bare soil.Defence and alternatives: Post-classification change compounds errors; direct spectral change can be more sensitive but less semantic. Stage 6: ValidationRecommended: Independent probability-based reference for both dates and transitions; user/producer accuracies and area-adjusted estimates.Defence and alternatives: Oversample rare change classes but weight results to population area. Stage 7: DeliveryRecommended: Report gross gain, loss, net gain, intervals and locations; explain resolution and temporal limitations.Defence and alternatives: Precision in hectares must be justified by sampling error and map scale.", "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks) C1 — Transition matrix (8 marks) Of 100 ha classified vegetation in 2016, 24 ha is classified impervious in 2026; 10 ha originally impervious is now classified vegetation. What are gross vegetation-to-impervious transition and net impervious gain from these two transitions? Reveal worked answerGross mapped vegetation→impervious=24 ha; reverse=10 ha; net from these transitions=14 ha. These are mapped changes, not necessarily true changes; other class transitions and classification error may affect total city net gain. C2 — ENVI versus DEA Sandbox (6 marks) Defend which platform is better for a ten-year annual Landsat/Sentinel analysis and when the alternative is preferable. Reveal worked answerDEA Sandbox/data-cube tools facilitate analysis-ready time series, reproducible Python workflows and cloud-scale composites. ENVI provides interactive visual inspection, specialist image processing, ROI tools and manual diagnostics; local data/compute management is harder for long time series. Both require independent validation and careful product/algorithm choice. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Explain why stratified oversampling of rare change pixels requires weighting to estimate area-wide accuracy. Reveal worked answerA 50:50 sample of changed/stable pixels may not match population proportions. Raw OA and mapped change proportions are biased for the whole area. Use design weights/stratum areas, reference-based area estimates and variance/confidence intervals; preserve inclusion probabilities.", "Self-marking guide 85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A city council wants annual estimates of impervious-surface expansion and the conversion of vegetation to built land between 2016 and 2026. Available: Landsat surface reflectance, Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, aerial orthophotos for selected years, cadastral roads/buildings, and field visits in accessible neighbourhoods. The council requires a reproducible change map and area uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst compares a 30 m Landsat image from wet season 2016 to a 10 m Sentinel-2 image from dry season 2026, resamples Landsat to 10 m and calls both products equally detailed; classifies each image with different class definitions; applies unsupervised clustering in 2016 and a supervised RF in 2026; validates only the 2026 map using easy roadside points; computes change by subtracting class-code numbers; interprets all red-to-grey transitions as urbanisation; and reports 2.3% growth to one decimal place despite 15% class error.</p>"}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Transition matrix (8 marks)</h3><p>Of 100 ha classified vegetation in 2016, 24 ha is classified impervious in 2026; 10 ha originally impervious is now classified vegetation. What are gross vegetation-to-impervious transition and net impervious gain from these two transitions?</p><h3>C2 — ENVI versus DEA Sandbox (6 marks)</h3><p>Defend which platform is better for a ten-year annual Landsat/Sentinel analysis and when the alternative is preferable.</p><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Explain why stratified oversampling of rare change pixels requires weighting to estimate area-wide accuracy.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Answer 1: Resolution mismatch (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Resampling 30 m to 10 m does not recover 10 m information; mixed pixels and PSF differ.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use consistent support or aggregate higher-resolution data to common units; assess minimum mapping unit.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Season/phenology (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Wet/dry vegetation, soil moisture and shadow can mimic impervious change.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use comparable seasonal composites and robust cloud/shadow masks.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Inconsistent taxonomy (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Different class definitions prevent meaningful transition comparison.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Harmonise class labels and classification protocols across dates.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Algorithm and temporal inconsistency (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Unsupervised clusters do not automatically map to the same semantic classes as RF outputs.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use consistent reference-guided labels or explicitly crosswalk and validate each date.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Biased reference (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Roadside samples miss rooftops, informal developments and interior green spaces.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use probability sampling stratified by class/change and independent high-quality orthophotos.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Class-code subtraction (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Codes are nominal; numeric differences have no physical interpretation.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use categorical cross-tabulation/transition matrices and distinguish persistent/changed labels.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: False precision (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A small mapped growth may be within classification/change uncertainty.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Estimate change area using independent change-reference samples and confidence intervals; report sensitivity.</p></details>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Stage 1: Question and categories</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Define impervious, pervious vegetation, bare soil, water and mixed categories; specify whether expansion is gross or net.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Bare construction soil may be transitional, not permanent impervious cover.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Image harmonisation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Choose comparable seasons, surface-reflectance products, consistent grid/support, cloud/shadow and topographic corrections where relevant.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Landsat has long history; Sentinel-2 finer pixels but shorter record and different spectral response.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Ancillary evidence</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use orthophotos, building footprints and roads as supporting reference/predictors, respecting their date and incompleteness.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Ancillary maps may lag construction and cannot be assumed error-free.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Classification</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Train consistent supervised models or calibrated fractional imperviousness; check urban shadow and soil confusion.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Fractional cover may better handle mixed pixels than hard classes.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Change estimation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Create class transition matrix, inspect stable/changed pixels, compare annual trajectories and control for temporary bare soil.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Post-classification change compounds errors; direct spectral change can be more sensitive but less semantic.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Validation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Independent probability-based reference for both dates and transitions; user/producer accuracies and area-adjusted estimates.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Oversample rare change classes but weight results to population area.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Delivery</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Report gross gain, loss, net gain, intervals and locations; explain resolution and temporal limitations.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Precision in hectares must be justified by sampling error and map scale.</p></details>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Gross mapped vegetation→impervious=24 ha; reverse=10 ha; net from these transitions=14 ha. These are mapped changes, not necessarily true changes; other class transitions and classification error may affect total city net gain.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>DEA Sandbox/data-cube tools facilitate analysis-ready time series, reproducible Python workflows and cloud-scale composites. ENVI provides interactive visual inspection, specialist image processing, ROI tools and manual diagnostics; local data/compute management is harder for long time series. Both require independent validation and careful product/algorithm choice.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>A 50:50 sample of changed/stable pixels may not match population proportions. Raw OA and mapped change proportions are biased for the whole area. Use design weights/stratum areas, reference-based area estimates and variance/confidence intervals; preserve inclusion probabilities.</p></details>"}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}]}, {"id": 22, "title": "Coastal water quality and sediment-plume monitoring", "category": "MOCK EXAM", "file": "materials/GEOM7001_Mock_6_coastal_water_quality_and_sediment_plume_monitoring.pdf", "pages": 5, "sections": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A coastal authority wants to track suspended-sediment plumes after major rain events and estimate how often sensitive seagrass habitat is exposed to high turbidity. Available: Sentinel-2 and Landsat surface-reflectance time series, river discharge and rainfall, a few in-situ turbidity measurements in NTU, bathymetry, tide data and a seagrass habitat map. Deliverables: plume extent through time, a defensible turbidity proxy or calibrated estimate, and uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst selects one cloud-free image two months after each storm; treats all visible-band reflectance increases as sediment; uses NDTI and labels its values as NTU; applies a single deep-water algorithm to shallow seagrass banks without checking bottom contribution; ignores sunglint and atmospheric adjacency near bright beaches; stacks all dates with no tide or discharge metadata; calibrates a linear model on five field samples taken several days away from satellite overpasses; extrapolates beyond the sampled range; and claims that every seagrass pixel inside a plume polygon suffered ecological damage.</p>", "text": "A coastal authority wants to track suspended-sediment plumes after major rain events and estimate how often sensitive seagrass habitat is exposed to high turbidity. Available: Sentinel-2 and Landsat surface-reflectance time series, river discharge and rainfall, a few in-situ turbidity measurements in NTU, bathymetry, tide data and a seagrass habitat map. Deliverables: plume extent through time, a defensible turbidity proxy or calibrated estimate, and uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst selects one cloud-free image two months after each storm; treats all visible-band reflectance increases as sediment; uses NDTI and labels its values as NTU; applies a single deep-water algorithm to shallow seagrass banks without checking bottom contribution; ignores sunglint and atmospheric adjacency near bright beaches; stacks all dates with no tide or discharge metadata; calibrates a linear model on five field samples taken several days away from satellite overpasses; extrapolates beyond the sampled range; and claims that every seagrass pixel inside a plume polygon suffered ecological damage."}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p><details><summary>Answer 1: Temporal aliasing (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A two-month-delayed image may miss short-lived plume peaks; cloud-free availability biases event capture.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use event-window time series, rainfall/discharge records, acquisition-time matching and explicit observation gaps.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Proxy/unit error (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> NDTI is dimensionless and cannot be labelled NTU without validated calibration.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Distinguish index from turbidity units; fit/test appropriate empirical or physical relationship.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Shallow-bottom contamination (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Reflectance includes bottom contributions, varying with depth/tide, not only suspended sediment.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Mask optically shallow zones or use depth-aware correction and validate by depth.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Atmosphere, adjacency and glint (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Coastal bright land and glint contaminate water-leaving reflectance; surface reflectance products may be imperfect over water.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use water-appropriate atmospheric correction and quality masks; inspect shoreline artefacts.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Insufficient field calibration (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Five temporally mismatched samples cannot establish robust transfer or uncertainty.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Collect contemporaneous stratified samples spanning turbidity, tide and locations; hold out independent observations.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Extrapolation (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Index saturation and nonlinear optics may invalidate linear model at high turbidity.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Test model range, residuals, uncertainty and alternative band combinations; flag extrapolation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Ecological overclaim (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Exposure to turbidity is not equivalent to demonstrated seagrass damage.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report exposure duration/intensity as a risk proxy; corroborate with ecological monitoring and baseline trends.</p></details>", "text": "Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Temporal aliasing (5 marks)Problem: A two-month-delayed image may miss short-lived plume peaks; cloud-free availability biases event capture.Correction: Use event-window time series, rainfall/discharge records, acquisition-time matching and explicit observation gaps. Answer 2: Proxy/unit error (5 marks)Problem: NDTI is dimensionless and cannot be labelled NTU without validated calibration.Correction: Distinguish index from turbidity units; fit/test appropriate empirical or physical relationship. Answer 3: Shallow-bottom contamination (5 marks)Problem: Reflectance includes bottom contributions, varying with depth/tide, not only suspended sediment.Correction: Mask optically shallow zones or use depth-aware correction and validate by depth. Answer 4: Atmosphere, adjacency and glint (5 marks)Problem: Coastal bright land and glint contaminate water-leaving reflectance; surface reflectance products may be imperfect over water.Correction: Use water-appropriate atmospheric correction and quality masks; inspect shoreline artefacts. Answer 5: Insufficient field calibration (5 marks)Problem: Five temporally mismatched samples cannot establish robust transfer or uncertainty.Correction: Collect contemporaneous stratified samples spanning turbidity, tide and locations; hold out independent observations. Answer 6: Extrapolation (4 marks)Problem: Index saturation and nonlinear optics may invalidate linear model at high turbidity.Correction: Test model range, residuals, uncertainty and alternative band combinations; flag extrapolation. Answer 7: Ecological overclaim (6 marks)Problem: Exposure to turbidity is not equivalent to demonstrated seagrass damage.Correction: Report exposure duration/intensity as a risk proxy; corroborate with ecological monitoring and baseline trends."}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p><details><summary>Stage 1: Define exposure metric</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Specify plume threshold, recurrence, duration and whether reporting NDTI, reflectance-derived TSS or calibrated NTU.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> TSS (mg/L) and turbidity (NTU) are distinct physical/optical quantities.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Data strategy</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use frequent Sentinel-2 and Landsat scenes around rain events; discharge/rainfall to contextualise gaps; habitat and depth/tide as ancillary layers.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Spatial resolution versus revisit/cloud trade-offs require explicit justification.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Optical preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Cloud/shadow, sunglint, adjacency and water-appropriate atmospheric corrections; check co-registration and valid water mask.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Terrestrial surface-reflectance correction is not always adequate for coastal water.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Depth and algorithms</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Limit to optically deep water where appropriate; compare visible/red/red-edge band algorithms and depth effects.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A single index is interpretable but may saturate or respond to CDOM/bottom.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Calibration and validation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Match field samples to overpass time/location; evaluate bias/RMSE and uncertainty by turbidity/depth; independent holdout.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> NTU requires empirical calibration; otherwise report a relative index without false units.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Temporal analysis</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Calculate per-event maps, recurrence and exposure with observation counts and missingness flags; consider cloud-induced bias.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Absence of a detected plume is not proof of no plume between acquisitions.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Interpretation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Overlay habitat only for exposure/risk screening; validate ecological response separately.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Remote sensing of sediment is not direct proof of seagrass mortality.</p></details>", "text": "B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Define exposure metricRecommended: Specify plume threshold, recurrence, duration and whether reporting NDTI, reflectance-derived TSS or calibrated NTU.Defence and alternatives: TSS (mg/L) and turbidity (NTU) are distinct physical/optical quantities. Stage 2: Data strategyRecommended: Use frequent Sentinel-2 and Landsat scenes around rain events; discharge/rainfall to contextualise gaps; habitat and depth/tide as ancillary layers.Defence and alternatives: Spatial resolution versus revisit/cloud trade-offs require explicit justification. Stage 3: Optical preprocessingRecommended: Cloud/shadow, sunglint, adjacency and water-appropriate atmospheric corrections; check co-registration and valid water mask.Defence and alternatives: Terrestrial surface-reflectance correction is not always adequate for coastal water. Stage 4: Depth and algorithmsRecommended: Limit to optically deep water where appropriate; compare visible/red/red-edge band algorithms and depth effects.Defence and alternatives: A single index is interpretable but may saturate or respond to CDOM/bottom. Stage 5: Calibration and validationRecommended: Match field samples to overpass time/location; evaluate bias/RMSE and uncertainty by turbidity/depth; independent holdout.Defence and alternatives: NTU requires empirical calibration; otherwise report a relative index without false units. Stage 6: Temporal analysisRecommended: Calculate per-event maps, recurrence and exposure with observation counts and missingness flags; consider cloud-induced bias.Defence and alternatives: Absence of a detected plume is not proof of no plume between acquisitions. Stage 7: InterpretationRecommended: Overlay habitat only for exposure/risk screening; validate ecological response separately.Defence and alternatives: Remote sensing of sediment is not direct proof of seagrass mortality."}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Index calculation (7 marks)</h3><p>Using NDTI=(Red−Green)/(Red+Green), calculate for red=0.08 and green=0.05. Can the result be called 0.23 NTU?</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>NDTI=(0.08−0.05)/(0.08+0.05)=0.03/0.13=0.2308 (dimensionless). No: NTU requires instrument measurement or calibrated relationship.</p></details><h3>C2 — Temporal resolution (7 marks)</h3><p>Explain why a daily revisit with 80% cloud contamination may be less informative than a 5-day revisit with several clear event observations.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Nominal revisit is not effective valid-observation frequency. Cloud gaps, event duration and acquisition timing determine plume detectability. Report valid observations and uncertainty from unobserved intervals.</p></details><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Design a test to distinguish bottom reflectance from suspended sediment as causes of an apparent plume signal.</p><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Stratify by bathymetry/tide and optical depth; compare shallow versus deep water, spectral responses and temporal persistence; use matched in-situ turbidity and water-level data; mask bottom-visible pixels or model bottom contribution. An observed correlation with depth alone is not causal proof.</p></details>", "text": "C1 — Index calculation (7 marks) Using NDTI=(Red−Green)/(Red+Green), calculate for red=0.08 and green=0.05. Can the result be called 0.23 NTU? Reveal worked answerNDTI=(0.08−0.05)/(0.08+0.05)=0.03/0.13=0.2308 (dimensionless). No: NTU requires instrument measurement or calibrated relationship. C2 — Temporal resolution (7 marks) Explain why a daily revisit with 80% cloud contamination may be less informative than a 5-day revisit with several clear event observations. Reveal worked answerNominal revisit is not effective valid-observation frequency. Cloud gaps, event duration and acquisition timing determine plume detectability. Report valid observations and uncertainty from unobserved intervals. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Design a test to distinguish bottom reflectance from suspended sediment as causes of an apparent plume signal. Reveal worked answerStratify by bathymetry/tide and optical depth; compare shallow versus deep water, spectral responses and temporal persistence; use matched in-situ turbidity and water-level data; mask bottom-visible pixels or model bottom contribution. An observed correlation with depth alone is not causal proof."}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}], "text": ["Scenario A coastal authority wants to track suspended-sediment plumes after major rain events and estimate how often sensitive seagrass habitat is exposed to high turbidity. Available: Sentinel-2 and Landsat surface-reflectance time series, river discharge and rainfall, a few in-situ turbidity measurements in NTU, bathymetry, tide data and a seagrass habitat map. Deliverables: plume extent through time, a defensible turbidity proxy or calibrated estimate, and uncertainty. Flawed proposed workflow The analyst selects one cloud-free image two months after each storm; treats all visible-band reflectance increases as sediment; uses NDTI and labels its values as NTU; applies a single deep-water algorithm to shallow seagrass banks without checking bottom contribution; ignores sunglint and atmospheric adjacency near bright beaches; stacks all dates with no tide or discharge metadata; calibrates a linear model on five field samples taken several days away from satellite overpasses; extrapolates beyond the sampled range; and claims that every seagrass pixel inside a plume polygon suffered ecological damage.", "A — Critique (35 marks) Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it. Answer 1: Temporal aliasing (5 marks)Problem: A two-month-delayed image may miss short-lived plume peaks; cloud-free availability biases event capture.Correction: Use event-window time series, rainfall/discharge records, acquisition-time matching and explicit observation gaps. Answer 2: Proxy/unit error (5 marks)Problem: NDTI is dimensionless and cannot be labelled NTU without validated calibration.Correction: Distinguish index from turbidity units; fit/test appropriate empirical or physical relationship. Answer 3: Shallow-bottom contamination (5 marks)Problem: Reflectance includes bottom contributions, varying with depth/tide, not only suspended sediment.Correction: Mask optically shallow zones or use depth-aware correction and validate by depth. Answer 4: Atmosphere, adjacency and glint (5 marks)Problem: Coastal bright land and glint contaminate water-leaving reflectance; surface reflectance products may be imperfect over water.Correction: Use water-appropriate atmospheric correction and quality masks; inspect shoreline artefacts. Answer 5: Insufficient field calibration (5 marks)Problem: Five temporally mismatched samples cannot establish robust transfer or uncertainty.Correction: Collect contemporaneous stratified samples spanning turbidity, tide and locations; hold out independent observations. Answer 6: Extrapolation (4 marks)Problem: Index saturation and nonlinear optics may invalidate linear model at high turbidity.Correction: Test model range, residuals, uncertainty and alternative band combinations; flag extrapolation. Answer 7: Ecological overclaim (6 marks)Problem: Exposure to turbidity is not equivalent to demonstrated seagrass damage.Correction: Report exposure duration/intensity as a risk proxy; corroborate with ecological monitoring and baseline trends.", "B — Design and defend (45 marks) B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers. Stage 1: Define exposure metricRecommended: Specify plume threshold, recurrence, duration and whether reporting NDTI, reflectance-derived TSS or calibrated NTU.Defence and alternatives: TSS (mg/L) and turbidity (NTU) are distinct physical/optical quantities. Stage 2: Data strategyRecommended: Use frequent Sentinel-2 and Landsat scenes around rain events; discharge/rainfall to contextualise gaps; habitat and depth/tide as ancillary layers.Defence and alternatives: Spatial resolution versus revisit/cloud trade-offs require explicit justification. Stage 3: Optical preprocessingRecommended: Cloud/shadow, sunglint, adjacency and water-appropriate atmospheric corrections; check co-registration and valid water mask.Defence and alternatives: Terrestrial surface-reflectance correction is not always adequate for coastal water. Stage 4: Depth and algorithmsRecommended: Limit to optically deep water where appropriate; compare visible/red/red-edge band algorithms and depth effects.Defence and alternatives: A single index is interpretable but may saturate or respond to CDOM/bottom. Stage 5: Calibration and validationRecommended: Match field samples to overpass time/location; evaluate bias/RMSE and uncertainty by turbidity/depth; independent holdout.Defence and alternatives: NTU requires empirical calibration; otherwise report a relative index without false units. Stage 6: Temporal analysisRecommended: Calculate per-event maps, recurrence and exposure with observation counts and missingness flags; consider cloud-induced bias.Defence and alternatives: Absence of a detected plume is not proof of no plume between acquisitions. Stage 7: InterpretationRecommended: Overlay habitat only for exposure/risk screening; validate ecological response separately.Defence and alternatives: Remote sensing of sediment is not direct proof of seagrass mortality.", "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks) C1 — Index calculation (7 marks) Using NDTI=(Red−Green)/(Red+Green), calculate for red=0.08 and green=0.05. Can the result be called 0.23 NTU? Reveal worked answerNDTI=(0.08−0.05)/(0.08+0.05)=0.03/0.13=0.2308 (dimensionless). No: NTU requires instrument measurement or calibrated relationship. C2 — Temporal resolution (7 marks) Explain why a daily revisit with 80% cloud contamination may be less informative than a 5-day revisit with several clear event observations. Reveal worked answerNominal revisit is not effective valid-observation frequency. Cloud gaps, event duration and acquisition timing determine plume detectability. Report valid observations and uncertainty from unobserved intervals. C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks) Design a test to distinguish bottom reflectance from suspended sediment as causes of an apparent plume signal. Reveal worked answerStratify by bathymetry/tide and optical depth; compare shallow versus deep water, spectral responses and temporal persistence; use matched in-situ turbidity and water-level data; mask bottom-visible pixels or model bottom contribution. An observed correlation with depth alone is not causal proof.", "Self-marking guide 85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."], "examQuestions": [{"title": "Scenario", "html": "<p>A coastal authority wants to track suspended-sediment plumes after major rain events and estimate how often sensitive seagrass habitat is exposed to high turbidity. Available: Sentinel-2 and Landsat surface-reflectance time series, river discharge and rainfall, a few in-situ turbidity measurements in NTU, bathymetry, tide data and a seagrass habitat map. Deliverables: plume extent through time, a defensible turbidity proxy or calibrated estimate, and uncertainty.</p><h3>Flawed proposed workflow</h3><p>The analyst selects one cloud-free image two months after each storm; treats all visible-band reflectance increases as sediment; uses NDTI and labels its values as NTU; applies a single deep-water algorithm to shallow seagrass banks without checking bottom contribution; ignores sunglint and atmospheric adjacency near bright beaches; stacks all dates with no tide or discharge metadata; calibrates a linear model on five field samples taken several days away from satellite overpasses; extrapolates beyond the sampled range; and claims that every seagrass pixel inside a plume polygon suffered ecological damage.</p>"}, {"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<p>Identify the seven most consequential flaws. Explain why each matters and how to correct it.</p>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<p>B1 target (5); B2 data (10); B3 preprocessing (10); B4 modelling (10); B5 independent verification (10). Explain your complete workflow before expanding the answers.</p>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<h3>C1 — Index calculation (7 marks)</h3><p>Using NDTI=(Red−Green)/(Red+Green), calculate for red=0.08 and green=0.05. Can the result be called 0.23 NTU?</p><h3>C2 — Temporal resolution (7 marks)</h3><p>Explain why a daily revisit with 80% cloud contamination may be less informative than a 5-day revisit with several clear event observations.</p><h3>C3 — GEOM7001-only extension (6 marks)</h3><p>Design a test to distinguish bottom reflectance from suspended sediment as causes of an apparent plume signal.</p>"}], "examAnswers": [{"title": "A — Critique (35 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Answer 1: Temporal aliasing (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> A two-month-delayed image may miss short-lived plume peaks; cloud-free availability biases event capture.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use event-window time series, rainfall/discharge records, acquisition-time matching and explicit observation gaps.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 2: Proxy/unit error (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> NDTI is dimensionless and cannot be labelled NTU without validated calibration.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Distinguish index from turbidity units; fit/test appropriate empirical or physical relationship.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 3: Shallow-bottom contamination (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Reflectance includes bottom contributions, varying with depth/tide, not only suspended sediment.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Mask optically shallow zones or use depth-aware correction and validate by depth.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 4: Atmosphere, adjacency and glint (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Coastal bright land and glint contaminate water-leaving reflectance; surface reflectance products may be imperfect over water.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Use water-appropriate atmospheric correction and quality masks; inspect shoreline artefacts.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 5: Insufficient field calibration (5 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Five temporally mismatched samples cannot establish robust transfer or uncertainty.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Collect contemporaneous stratified samples spanning turbidity, tide and locations; hold out independent observations.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 6: Extrapolation (4 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Index saturation and nonlinear optics may invalidate linear model at high turbidity.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Test model range, residuals, uncertainty and alternative band combinations; flag extrapolation.</p></details><details><summary>Answer 7: Ecological overclaim (6 marks)</summary><p><b>Problem:</b> Exposure to turbidity is not equivalent to demonstrated seagrass damage.</p><p><b>Correction:</b> Report exposure duration/intensity as a risk proxy; corroborate with ecological monitoring and baseline trends.</p></details>"}, {"title": "B — Design and defend (45 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Stage 1: Define exposure metric</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Specify plume threshold, recurrence, duration and whether reporting NDTI, reflectance-derived TSS or calibrated NTU.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> TSS (mg/L) and turbidity (NTU) are distinct physical/optical quantities.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 2: Data strategy</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Use frequent Sentinel-2 and Landsat scenes around rain events; discharge/rainfall to contextualise gaps; habitat and depth/tide as ancillary layers.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Spatial resolution versus revisit/cloud trade-offs require explicit justification.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 3: Optical preprocessing</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Cloud/shadow, sunglint, adjacency and water-appropriate atmospheric corrections; check co-registration and valid water mask.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Terrestrial surface-reflectance correction is not always adequate for coastal water.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 4: Depth and algorithms</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Limit to optically deep water where appropriate; compare visible/red/red-edge band algorithms and depth effects.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> A single index is interpretable but may saturate or respond to CDOM/bottom.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 5: Calibration and validation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Match field samples to overpass time/location; evaluate bias/RMSE and uncertainty by turbidity/depth; independent holdout.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> NTU requires empirical calibration; otherwise report a relative index without false units.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 6: Temporal analysis</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Calculate per-event maps, recurrence and exposure with observation counts and missingness flags; consider cloud-induced bias.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Absence of a detected plume is not proof of no plume between acquisitions.</p></details><details><summary>Stage 7: Interpretation</summary><p><b>Recommended:</b> Overlay habitat only for exposure/risk screening; validate ecological response separately.</p><p><b>Defence and alternatives:</b> Remote sensing of sediment is not direct proof of seagrass mortality.</p></details>"}, {"title": "C — Applied technical questions (20 marks)", "html": "<details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>NDTI=(0.08−0.05)/(0.08+0.05)=0.03/0.13=0.2308 (dimensionless). No: NTU requires instrument measurement or calibrated relationship.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Nominal revisit is not effective valid-observation frequency. Cloud gaps, event duration and acquisition timing determine plume detectability. Report valid observations and uncertainty from unobserved intervals.</p></details><details><summary>Reveal worked answer</summary><p>Stratify by bathymetry/tide and optical depth; compare shallow versus deep water, spectral responses and temporal persistence; use matched in-situ turbidity and water-level data; mask bottom-visible pixels or model bottom contribution. An observed correlation with depth alone is not causal proof.</p></details>"}, {"title": "Self-marking guide", "html": "<p>85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification.</p><p>Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions.</p>", "text": "85–100: coherent, physically justified critique and design with independent validation and trade-offs. 70–84: strong, some omissions. 55–69: basic sequence, weak justification. Below 55: generic steps, major conceptual errors or no valid verification. Original practice scenarios applying GEOM7001 lecture and practical concepts. Not an official exam or a reproduction of lecturer questions."}]}];